window.__SCI_INDEX__={"v":1,"pages":[[7,1,"Cells","y7sci_c1_cells"],[7,2,"Materials and their structure","y7sci_c2_materials_structure"],[7,3,"Forces and energy","y7sci_c3_forces_energy"],[7,4,"Grouping and identifying organisms","y7sci_c4_organisms"],[7,5,"Properties of materials","y7sci_c5_properties_materials"],[7,6,"Earth physics","y7sci_c6_earth_physics"],[7,7,"Microorganisms in the environment","y7sci_c7_microorganisms"],[7,8,"Changes to materials","y7sci_c8_changes_materials"],[7,9,"Electricity","y7sci_c9_electricity"],[8,1,"Respiration","y8sci_c1_respiration"],[8,2,"Properties of materials","y8sci_c2_solubility"],[8,3,"Forces and energy","y8sci_c3_forces_energy"],[8,4,"Ecosystems","y8sci_c4_ecosystems"],[8,5,"Materials and their structure","y8sci_c5_materials_cycles"],[8,6,"Light","y8sci_c6_light"],[8,7,"Diet and growth","y8sci_c7_diet_growth"],[8,8,"Chemical reactions","y8sci_c8_chemical_reactions"],[8,9,"Magnetism","y8sci_c9_magnetism"],[9,1,"Photosynthesis and the carbon cycle","y9sci_c1_photosynthesis"],[9,2,"Properties of materials","y9sci_c2_materials"],[9,3,"Forces and energy","y9sci_c3_forces"],[9,4,"Maintaining life","y9sci_c4_maintaining_life"],[9,5,"Reactivity","y9sci_c5_reactivity"],[9,6,"Sound and space","y9sci_c6_sound_space"],[9,7,"Genes and inheritance","y9sci_c7_genes"],[9,8,"Rates of reaction","y9sci_c8_rates"],[9,9,"Electricity","y9sci_c9_electricity"]],"notes":[[0,"1-1-plant-cells","1.1 Plant cells","Key words: cell · cell membrane · cell wall · cellulose · chlorophyll · chloroplast · cytoplasm · magnify · mitochondria · nucleus · sap vacuole"],[0,"what-cells-are","What cells are","- If you look at part of a plant through a microscope, you see that it is made of a very large number of tiny \"boxes\". These are called cells . - All living organisms are made of cells. - Cells are far too small to see with your eyes alone. A microscope magnifies the view, so the cells look much bigger than they really are."],[0,"the-parts-of-a-plant-cell","The parts of a plant cell","Part What it is like What it does cell wall strong and stiff, made of cellulose ; every plant cell has one holds the plant cell in shape cell membrane very thin and flexible, like the skin of a soap bubble; lies just inside the cell wall controls what goes in and out of the cell cytoplasm like clear jelly; all cells have it the chemical reactions that keep the cell alive happen here nucleus most cells have one controls the activities of the cell sap vacuole a large space filled with cell sap — a solution of sugars and other substances in water stores the cell sap chloroplast green, because it contains chlorophyll ; found in plant cells that get sunlight where the plant makes its food mitochondrion (plural: mitochondria ) all plant cells have them energy is released from food inside them Cell wall or cell membrane? This is the pair most people mix up. Cell wall Cell membrane on the outside of the cell further in, just inside the wall made of cellulose not made of cellulose strong and stiff thin and flexible much thicker much thinner holds the cell in shape controls what goes in and out only plant cells have one every cell has one"],[0,"using-a-microscope","Using a microscope","- The eyepiece is the lens you look down. - The three objective lenses (low-power, medium-power, high-power) sit on a turntable. Always start with the smallest one over the hole in the stage. - The stage is the flat platform the slide sits on; the slide goes with the specimen over the hole. - The mirror reflects light up through the hole, so light passes through the specimen. - The coarse focussing knob moves the lens a long way; the fine focussing knob moves it a very small amount, for the last sharp adjustment."],[0,"experiment-looking-at-onion-cells","Experiment: looking at onion cells","You will need — a microscope, a slide, a cover slip, a piece of onion bulb, tweezers, a small sharp knife, a dropper pipette, water. Method 1. Cut a piece of onion about 1 cm square. 2. Put a small drop of water in the middle of a clean slide. 3. Very carefully peel the thin layer from the inside of the piece of onion. 4. Push the layer into the drop of water and spread it as flat as you can. 5. Gently lower a cover slip over it, trying not to trap air bubbles. (Cover slips break very easily.) 6. Turn the smallest objective lens over the hole in the stage. 7. Put the slide on the stage with the onion over the hole. 8. Look down the eyepiece and slowly turn the focussing knob to move the lens away from the slide until the onion comes into focus. Why it is done this way - The layer must be thin so light can pass through it — a thick piece would look black. - The water stops the cells drying out and helps the cover slip lie flat. - Focussing by moving the lens away from the slide means you cannot crash the lens into the cover slip and break it. What you see — neat, brick-shaped cells with clear walls. They are not green , because cells inside an onion bulb get no light, so they have no chloroplasts and no chlorophyll. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.1</b </summary 1 Look at the photograph of the plant cells. What do you think the little green circles inside the cells are? Why are they green? What happens inside them? They are chloroplasts. They are green because they contain chlorophyll. Photosynthesis happens inside them — this is where the plant makes its food. 2 Describe four differences between a cell wall and a cell membrane. Any four of: the cell wall is on the outside and the membrane is further in; the wall is made of cellulose and the membrane is not; the wall is strong and stiff while the membrane is flexible; the wall is much thicker; the wall holds the cell in shape while the membrane does not; the membrane controls what goes in and out while the wall does not. Think like a scientist — 1 Suggest why the cells from the onion do not look green. They contain no chloroplasts and no chlorophyll. The cells are inside the onion where no light reaches them, so there would be no point in having chloroplasts. </details"],[0,"1-2-animal-cells","1.2 Animal cells","Key words: stain"],[0,"the-parts-of-an-animal-cell","The parts of an animal cell","- You are an animal, and your body is made of cells. One estimate is that a person has about 100 trillion of them. - Animal cells are like plant cells in three ways: they have a cell membrane , cytoplasm and a nucleus . They also contain mitochondria . What animal cells do not have: Part Plant cell Animal cell cell wall yes no large sap vacuole yes no chloroplasts in cells that get light no cell membrane yes yes cytoplasm yes yes nucleus usually usually mitochondria yes yes How to tell the two apart in a picture: look for a cell wall . A cell wall gives the cell a regular, straight-sided shape. Animal cells have no wall, so their edges look irregular and rounded."],[0,"experiment-looking-at-cheek-cells","Experiment: looking at cheek cells","You will need — a microscope, a slide, a cover slip, a cotton bud, methylene blue, a dropper pipette. Method 1. Gently rub the cotton bud along the inside of your cheek to collect some loose cells. 2. Rub the cotton bud on a clean slide. 3. Add a drop of methylene blue . This is a stain — a dye that colours the cells so they are easier to see. 4. Carefully lower a cover slip over the drop. 5. Put the smallest objective lens over the stage, then put the slide on the stage. 6. Watching from the side , turn the focussing knob until the lens is close to the slide. 7. Now look down the eyepiece and slowly move the lens upwards until the cells come into focus. 8. Turn to a larger lens for a more magnified view, and draw one or two cells. Why a stain is used — cheek cells are almost colourless and very thin, so without a stain there is too little contrast to see anything. The dye makes the nucleus stand out most strongly. Why you watch from the side in step 6 — so that you can see how close the lens is and stop before it touches the slide. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.2</b </summary 1 The photographs show some cells seen through a microscope. For each photograph, decide whether the cells are plant cells or animal cells. Explain your decision. A: plant cells. B: plant cells. C: animal cells. A and B have cell walls and regular shapes; C has no cell walls and the cells are irregular in shape. 2 Think about the model of a plant cell that you made. What would you change to make it into a model of an animal cell? Remove the part representing the cell wall (keeping the cell membrane), remove the parts representing the chloroplasts, and remove the part representing the sap vacuole. </details"],[0,"1-3-specialised-cells","1.3 Specialised cells","Key words: absorb · adapted · axon · capillary · cilia · ciliated cell · dendrite · function · haemoglobin · mucus · neurone · palisade cell · pigment · red blood cell · root hair cell · specialised"],[0,"structure-and-function","Structure and function","- The function of a cell is the job it does. - Not all cells are the same. Each kind of cell is specialised : it has a structure that helps it do its function really well. We say the cell is adapted to carry out its function. - When you are asked to explain an adaptation, always join the two halves: this structure → so it can do this job ."],[0,"three-specialised-animal-cells","Three specialised animal cells","Red blood cell — transports oxygen around the body. Specialised structure How it helps cytoplasm packed with a red pigment called haemoglobin haemoglobin carries the oxygen no nucleus leaves more room for haemoglobin, so it carries more oxygen smaller than most other cells it can squeeze through the tiny blood vessels called capillaries and reach every part of the body Neurone — carries electrical signals from one part of the body to another , so that different parts of the body can communicate. For example, a neurone can carry a signal from the brain to a muscle to make it move. Specialised structure How it helps a very long axon — a long strand of cytoplasm electrical signals travel a long distance very quickly dendrites — short strands of cytoplasm they collect electrical signals from other nearby nerve cells Ciliated cell — stops dust and bacteria getting into the lungs. - Cilia are tiny threads, like microscopic hairs, along one edge of the cell. They can move. - Ciliated cells line the tubes leading from your mouth down to your lungs. Other cells in that lining make a sticky substance called mucus . - When you breathe in, the mucus traps dust and bacteria. The cilia all wave together and sweep the mucus up to the back of your mouth, and you swallow it."],[0,"two-specialised-plant-cells","Two specialised plant cells","Root hair cell — absorbs water and mineral ions from the soil. It is found on the outside of a plant root. It has a long, thin extension from one side, which lets water move easily from the soil into the cell. Root hair cells contain no chloroplasts , because roots are underground where there is no light, so they cannot photosynthesise anyway. Palisade cell — makes food by photosynthesis. It is found in the leaves. It contains a lot of chloroplasts , which hold chlorophyll . The chlorophyll absorbs energy from sunlight, and the plant uses that energy to make food. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.3</b </summary 1 List two things that red blood cells, neurones and ciliated cells have in common. They all have a cell membrane and cytoplasm. 2 How can you tell that all of these three cells are animal cells, not plant cells? None of them has a cell wall, and none has a large sap vacuole. 3 Suggest why root hair cells do not contain chloroplasts. They are underground, where they get no sunlight, so they cannot photosynthesise. There is no point in having chloroplasts. 4 Water moves through several parts of the root hair cell as it goes from the soil into the sap vacuole. List these parts, in order. Cell wall, then cell membrane, then cytoplasm. </details"],[0,"1-4-cells-tissues-and-organs","1.4 Cells, tissues and organs","Key words: ciliated epithelium · lower epidermis · onion epidermis · organ · organ system · organism · palisade layer · spongy layer · tissue · upper epidermis"],[0,"from-cells-to-organisms","From cells to organisms","Living things are called organisms . Their bodies are built up in levels: cell → tissue → organ → organ system → organism Word Meaning Example cell the smallest unit a living thing is made of a ciliated cell tissue a group of similar cells that all work together to carry out a particular function ciliated epithelium organ a structure made of several different kinds of tissue, working together the leaf, the brain, the stomach organ system a set of organs that all work together to carry out the same function the digestive system organism a living thing; it may contain many organ systems, organs and tissues a human, an onion plant"],[0,"two-tissues","Two tissues","- Onion epidermis — a plant tissue that covers the surface of the layers inside an onion. - Ciliated epithelium — the animal tissue that lines the tubes leading down to the lungs. All the cilia wave together, like grass in the wind, sweeping mucus (with the dust and bacteria trapped in it) away from the lungs."],[0,"a-leaf-is-an-organ","A leaf is an organ","A leaf is a plant organ , because it is made of several different tissues: - upper epidermis — the covering layer on top - palisade layer — tightly packed palisade cells, full of chloroplasts, where most photosynthesis happens - spongy layer — loosely packed cells with air spaces between them - lower epidermis — the covering layer underneath"],[0,"human-organ-systems","Human organ systems","Function Organ system Some organs in it transporting substances around the body circulatory system heart, arteries, veins, capillaries breaking down food and absorbing it into the blood digestive system stomach, intestines, liver taking oxygen into the body and getting rid of carbon dioxide respiratory system lungs, trachea helping different parts of the body to communicate with one another nervous system brain, spinal cord, nerves"],[0,"hydra-a-two-layer-animal","Hydra — a two-layer animal","Hydra is a tiny animal that lives in freshwater ponds. It catches even smaller animals with its tentacles and pushes them into its mouth . The mouth opens into a digestive cavity , where digestion happens. Its whole body is only two layers of cells thick: an outer layer of cells and an inner layer of cells . - The inner and outer layers are tissues — many similar cells working together. - A tentacle is an organ — it is made of more than one kind of tissue working together. - Hydra has no digestive system . Digestion happens in a single cavity, not in a set of several organs working together, so there is nothing for a system to be made of. Hydra has specialised sting cells that help it catch food. Each one contains a tiny coiled thread . When a prey animal touches the trigger , the thread shoots out and wraps around the prey. Some threads carry poisonous chemicals that kill the prey. - A sting cell is a single cell , not a tissue or an organ. - It has the parts you would expect in most animal cells: a cell membrane , cytoplasm and a nucleus . - You can tell it is an animal cell because it has no cell wall, no chloroplasts and no large sap vacuole. - It is adapted to its function: the thread is coiled tightly so it can shoot out a long way very quickly, and the trigger on the outside means the cell only fires when prey actually touches it. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.4</b </summary 1 What is the function of the ciliated epithelium tissue? It sweeps mucus, containing trapped dust particles and bacteria, away from the lungs. 2 The word \"tissue\" has an everyday meaning and a different scientific meaning. Write two sentences, one using each meaning. Everyday: \"I used a tissue to wipe my nose.\" Scientific: \"Ciliated epithelium is a tissue that lines the tubes leading to the lungs.\" 3 Copy and complete each sentence, using words from the list. A group of similar cells is called a tissue . An organ is a structure made of many different tissues. An organ system is a group of organs that carry out a particular function. An organism is a living thing. Workbook 1.4C — 1 For each part of Hydra , decide whether it is a cell, a tissue or an organ. a inner layer of cells — tissue. b outer layer of cells — tissue. c sting cell — cell. d tentacle — organ. Workbook 1.4C — 2 Does Hydra have a digestive system? Explain your answer. No. A system needs several different organs working together, but Hydra digests its food in one single cavity. Workbook 1.4C — 3 List three features of a sting cell that you would expect to find in most animal cells. A cell membrane, cytoplasm and a nucleus. Workbook 1.4C — 4 Explain how you can tell from the diagram that the sting cell is an animal cell and not a plant cell. It has no cell wall, no chloroplasts and no large sap vacuole. </details"],[0,"the-story-of-the-cell","The story of the cell","Scientific knowledge builds up slowly, each discovery making the next one possible. Year Who What happened 1625 Galileo Galilei builds the first microscope 1665 Robert Hooke looks at cork through a microscope and describes little compartments he calls cells 1670 Anton van Leeuwenhoek improves the microscope and sees living cells in a drop of pond water 1833 Robert Brown discovers the nucleus in plant cells 1838 Matthias Schleiden and Theodor Schwann propose that all plant tissues, and all animal tissues, are made of cells 1845 Carl Heinrich Braun proposes that cells are the basic unit of all life 1855 Rudolf Virchow says that all cells arise only from other cells"],[1,"2-1-solids-liquids-and-gases","2.1 Solids, liquids and gases","Key words: compressed · flow · hypothesis · matter · particle · pour · property · states of matter · theory · vacuum · vibrate · volume"],[1,"matter-and-the-three-states","Matter and the three states","- Everything you can see and feel is called matter . - Scientists sort matter into three groups, called the states of matter : solids , liquids and gases . - The three states behave in different ways. The ways they behave are called their properties ."],[1,"the-properties-of-each-state","The properties of each state","Solid Liquid Gas Shape keeps the same shape takes the shape of its container fills any closed container it is in Volume keeps the same volume keeps the same volume, whatever the shape of the container the volume can change Can it be poured? no yes it flows, like a liquid Can it be compressed (squashed)? no no yes, very easily Weight — — gases weigh very little You usually cannot see or feel a gas, but you can sometimes smell one, and you can feel air moving on your face."],[1,"from-hypothesis-to-theory","From hypothesis to theory","Scientists try to explain what they see. For example: - you can smell food cooking in another room - some substances get bigger when you heat them - liquids such as water change to a gas when you heat them - substances change from liquid to solid if you cool them. A suggestion for an explanation is called a hypothesis . A hypothesis can be tested by doing more investigations. When a hypothesis has been tested and is widely accepted as valid by other scientists, it is called a theory . The best theory we have for explaining how matter behaves is particle theory ."],[1,"particle-theory","Particle theory","All matter is made up of tiny particles that are far too small to see. What makes the three states different is how those particles are arranged. Solid Liquid Gas Arrangement a fixed, regular pattern touching each other, but with no pattern far apart, spread out How close? tightly packed, touching touching a long way apart Forces between particles strong weak none Movement vibrate on the spot, stay in the same place move past one another, but stay in touch move about freely in all directions"],[1,"explaining-the-properties-with-particle-theory","Explaining the properties with particle theory","Two rules explain almost everything in this topic: 1. Matter can only flow (be poured) if its particles can move past one another. 2. Matter can only change volume if its particles can spread out or move closer together. - Solids cannot flow. Attractive forces hold the particles in a fixed pattern, so they can only vibrate — they cannot move past one another. - Solids and liquids cannot be compressed. Their particles are already touching, so there is no space to squeeze them into. - Liquids can flow. The forces between the particles are weak enough to let them move past one another, but still strong enough to hold them together. - Gases flow and spread out. There are no attractive forces between the particles, so they move quickly in all directions. - Gases can be compressed. The particles are a long way apart, so squashing a gas just pushes them closer together and the gas takes up less space."],[1,"no-particles-at-all","No particles at all","A space with no particles in it at all is called a vacuum . A vacuum contains nothing. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.1</b </summary 1. What are the three states of matter? Solid, liquid and gas. 2. Which state of matter can be compressed easily? Gas. 3. Which state of matter cannot be poured? Solid. 4. List the properties of solids. They keep the same shape; they take up the same amount of space; they keep the same volume; they cannot be compressed or poured. 5. Name a property of liquids that they do not share with solids. They can be poured, and they take the shape of their container. 6. Name a property of gases that they share with liquids. They can flow and be poured. 7. Name a property of gases that they do not share with solids or liquids. They can be compressed. Think like a scientist — is a sponge a problem for particle theory? A sponge can be squashed, but the solid part of the sponge is not being compressed. A sponge is full of gaps filled with air, and it is the air — a gas, whose particles are far apart — that is being compressed. </details"],[1,"2-2-changes-of-state","2.2 Changes of state","Key words: boil · boiling point · change of state · condensation · condense · evaporation · freeze · measuring cylinder · melt · melting point · meniscus · steam · thermometer · water vapour"],[1,"the-changes-of-state","The changes of state","Change From → to What it means melting solid → liquid ice left in a warm place melts and becomes liquid water freezing liquid → solid water put in the freezer freezes and becomes ice evaporation liquid → gas water on the ground slowly disappears as it changes to water vapour , an invisible gas boiling liquid → gas at 100 °C all the water rapidly changes to steam condensation gas → liquid water vapour or steam touching something cold changes back to liquid water - The temperature at which a solid melts is its melting point . - The temperature at which a liquid boils is its boiling point . For water this is 100 °C . - The warmer the water, the more quickly it evaporates. - Evaporation and boiling are not the same thing. Evaporation happens slowly, at any temperature, from the surface only. Boiling happens at one particular temperature, and all through the liquid."],[1,"measuring-volume","Measuring volume","- Use a measuring cylinder . - The surface of the liquid curves. That curve is called the meniscus . - Read the volume from the bottom of the meniscus, with your eye level with it. If your eye is above or below, you will read the wrong number."],[1,"measuring-temperature","Measuring temperature","- Use a thermometer . - The liquid inside expands as it gets hotter, so it rises up the tube. Read the temperature from the scale, with your eye level with the top of the liquid."],[1,"experiment-measuring-the-temperature-as-water-is-heated","Experiment: measuring the temperature as water is heated","You will need — a beaker, a Bunsen burner, a clamp stand, a gauze, a measuring cylinder, a thermometer, a tripod, a heat-proof mat, a stopwatch. Safety — wear safety spectacles; take care with hot water. Method 1. Measure a known volume of water into the beaker. 2. Clamp the thermometer with its bulb in the water, not touching the bottom of the beaker . 3. Measure the starting temperature and record it in a table. 4. Heat the water. 5. Measure the temperature every minute until the water is boiling vigorously. 6. Plot a graph with time on the horizontal axis and temperature on the vertical axis. Why the thermometer must not rest on the bottom. The bottom of the beaker is in much closer contact with the Bunsen flame than the water is, so it is hotter. Resting the bulb there would measure the temperature of the glass, not the temperature of the water. What the graph shows. The temperature rises, but not by the same amount each minute — it rises quickly at first and then more slowly. Once the water is boiling, the temperature stops rising and stays at 100 °C , even though heating continues. This is because the energy going in is being used to turn liquid water into a gas, not to make the water hotter. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.2</b </summary - Activity 2.2.1 — which change of state? Melt: from solid to liquid. Freeze: from liquid to solid. Condense: from gas to liquid. Boil: from liquid to gas. - Reading the measuring cylinders (A, B, C): 28 cm³, 54 cm³, 82 cm³. - Reading the thermometers (A, B, C): 24 °C, 15 °C, 29 °C. </details"],[1,"2-3-explaining-changes-of-state","2.3 Explaining changes of state","Key words: attractive force · expand · heat · energy transferred"],[1,"heating-a-solid-why-it-expands","Heating a solid — why it expands","- The particles in a solid sit in a fixed pattern and vibrate . - When the solid is heated, heat energy is transferred to the particles . - The more energy the particles have, the more they vibrate; the more they vibrate, the more space they take up. So the solid expands . - The particles are still held in position by the attractive forces between them, so the solid keeps its shape."],[1,"melting-solid-to-liquid","Melting — solid to liquid","- Heat the solid more strongly and even more energy is transferred to the particles. - The particles vibrate so much that the attractive forces can no longer hold them in a fixed pattern. - The particles can now slide past one another instead of only vibrating — but the forces are still strong enough to keep them touching. The solid has melted."],[1,"evaporating-and-boiling-liquid-to-gas","Evaporating and boiling — liquid to gas","- In a liquid, the particles touch each other and are held together weakly. - Heat energy makes them move more. Some particles gain enough energy to break the weak attractive forces holding them in. - Those particles escape as gas particles, moving freely."],[1,"condensing-gas-to-liquid","Condensing — gas to liquid","- Gas particles move freely and spread out; no forces hold them. - When gas particles reach a cold surface , some of their heat energy is transferred to that surface. - With less energy the particles move less and get closer together, and they form a liquid."],[1,"freezing-liquid-to-solid","Freezing — liquid to solid","- Heat energy is transferred from the particles to the surroundings, so the liquid cools. - The less energy the particles have, the less able they are to slide past one another. - Eventually they have so little energy that they can only vibrate, and they settle into a fixed pattern. The liquid has become a solid. The one idea behind all of it: a change of state is always a change in how much energy the particles have, and therefore in whether the attractive forces between them can still hold them in place. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.3</b </summary 1. Why does a solid expand when it is heated? Energy is transferred to the particles, so they vibrate more. Vibrating more takes up more space, so the solid gets bigger. 2. Why can solids and liquids not be compressed? Their particles are already touching one another with no spaces between them, so they cannot be squeezed into a smaller space. 3. Why can liquids and gases flow? Their particles are free to move past each other, so the substance can flow when it is poured. 4. How does a liquid change to a gas? Heat energy is transferred to the particles so they move more. Some gain enough energy to escape the weak forces holding them together and leave as a gas. 5. How does a liquid change to a solid? Heat energy is transferred from the particles to the surroundings, so they have less energy, can no longer move past one another, and settle into a fixed pattern. 6. Steam in a bathroom hits a cold mirror. What happens? Some steam particles hit the cold surface and transfer heat energy to it. With less energy they slow down, move closer together and condense into liquid water on the mirror. 7. Name the processes A–D on the flow chart. A melting, B boiling (evaporating is also accepted), C freezing, D condensing. </details"],[1,"2-4-the-water-cycle","2.4 The water cycle","Key words: atmosphere · groundwater · open water · precipitation · surface run-off · transpiration · water cycle · water vapour"],[1,"why-it-is-a-cycle","Why it is a cycle","- Water is vital for all living things; our bodies are made up of at least 60% water . - Water on Earth is constantly moving between rivers, lakes, oceans, the atmosphere and the land. It is recycled over and over again in a continuous system called the water cycle . - The Earth has been recycling the same water for more than four billion years — you are still using the water that the ancient Greeks and Romans used."],[1,"the-stages","The stages","Stage What happens Particle explanation Evaporation energy from the Sun heats the water in rivers, lakes and oceans, and some liquid water becomes water vapour particles in the liquid gain enough energy to break free of the forces holding them and change to a gas Transpiration water also evaporates into the atmosphere from plants same as evaporation, but the water leaves through the leaves Condensation water vapour rises, cools, and turns back into tiny droplets, forming clouds the particles lose energy and can no longer move quickly, so they come back together Precipitation the droplets grow too heavy for the air to hold, and fall as rain — or as snow, hail or sleet if it is cold enough — Collection the water lands on rivers, lakes, oceans, plants or ground — Evapotranspiration is a single word for the two ways water goes up together: evaporation from open water plus transpiration from plants."],[1,"where-the-fallen-water-goes","Where the fallen water goes","- Some falls straight onto rivers, lakes and oceans, and evaporates again — the cycle restarts. - Some falls on plants, and either evaporates from the leaves or trickles down to the ground, where roots may take it up. - Some soaks into the soil and rocks as groundwater . Shallow groundwater moves sideways towards streams and rivers; deeper groundwater refills underground stores. - In cold climates the precipitation may build up as snow, ice or glaciers, and melts when temperatures rise. - Water that flows straight across the surface of the land into rivers is surface run-off . Heavy run-off carries soil off the land into the rivers, which can silt them up and block them. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.4</b </summary 1. What are the different types of precipitation? Rain, hail and snow (sleet too). 2. How does rain form? Water vapour in the clouds cools and condenses into liquid water. The droplets grow too heavy for the air to hold them, so they fall as rain. 3. Use particle theory to explain how a pool of water on the road disappears. The particles in the pool touch each other but can move past one another. Energy is transferred to them from the surroundings as the Sun comes out or the temperature rises, so they move more. Some gain enough energy to overcome the weak forces holding them and escape into the atmosphere as water vapour — the pool evaporates. 4. Where does drinking water come from? It depends where you live: a well, a reservoir, a river or a desalination plant. 5. What have people done to make sure of a water supply? Boreholes and wells, reservoirs, desalination plants, water treatment plants, and moving water from one area to another. 6. What do we use water for in our bodies? In the blood, in the digestive system, in the respiratory system, and in every cell. </details"],[1,"2-5-atoms-elements-and-the-periodic-table","2.5 Atoms, elements and the Periodic Table","Key words: atom · element · group · metals · nanotube · non-metal · period · symbol · The Periodic Table"],[1,"what-atoms-are","What atoms are","- Over 2000 years ago the Greek philosopher Democritus suggested that if you kept cutting a substance into smaller and smaller pieces, you would reach a piece that could not be cut up any more. He called these pieces atoms ; atom means \"cannot be divided\". - We now know atoms really do exist. Some of the larger kinds can even be seen using a scanning tunnelling microscope . - Scientists have found 94 kinds of atom that occur naturally in the universe. Another 24 kinds have been made in laboratories, which makes 118 in all."],[1,"what-an-element-is","What an element is","- A substance made of just one kind of atom is an element . - Carbon is made only of carbon atoms; gold only of gold atoms; silver only of silver atoms. - Because each type of atom has different properties, different elements have different properties. - Since there are 94 naturally occurring kinds of atom, there are 94 naturally occurring elements ."],[1,"how-atoms-sit-together-in-an-element","How atoms sit together in an element","Element How its atoms are arranged neon (and five other elements) single atoms moving around freely, not attached to one another gold and other metals atoms packed closely together oxygen two atoms joined to make one particle sulfur eight atoms joined to make one particle"],[1,"the-periodic-table","The Periodic Table","- The Periodic Table is the way scientists arrange the elements. The full table holds all 118 known elements; here you only need the first 20. - The rows are called periods . The columns are called groups . - Reading across a period from left to right, the atoms increase in mass . Hydrogen atoms have the smallest mass, then helium, then lithium, and so on. - Elements with similar properties are close together. In this diagram the metals are in yellow boxes and the non-metals are in blue boxes."],[1,"chemical-symbols","Chemical symbols","Every element has a symbol — a shorthand way of writing its name. There are three patterns: Pattern Example the first letter of the English name oxygen → O the first letter plus another letter from the name helium → He taken from another language sodium → Na , from the old Latin name natrium The rule that catches people out: the first letter is always upper case and the second letter, if there is one, is always lower case. So carbon is C and calcium is Ca — not CA. Element Symbol Element Symbol hydrogen H sodium Na helium He magnesium Mg lithium Li aluminium Al beryllium Be silicon Si boron B phosphorus P carbon C sulfur S nitrogen N chlorine Cl oxygen O argon Ar fluorine F potassium K neon Ne calcium Ca <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.5</b </summary 1. What are atoms? Tiny pieces, or particles, of matter. 2. If there are 94 naturally occurring kinds of atom, how many naturally occurring elements are there? 94. 3. Name the elements with the symbols Mg, Be, Li and N. Magnesium, beryllium, lithium and nitrogen. 4. Give the symbols for aluminium, boron, fluorine and potassium. Al, B, F and K. 5. Which element has atoms with the smallest mass? Hydrogen. 6. Which of the first 20 elements has atoms with the greatest mass? Calcium. 7. Name two elements in the same period as magnesium. Any two of: sodium, aluminium, silicon, phosphorus, sulfur, chlorine, argon. 8. Give the symbols of two elements in the same group as helium. Ne and Ar. </details"],[1,"2-6-compounds-and-formulae","2.6 Compounds and formulae","Key words: bonding · compound · formula · sodium chloride"],[1,"element-or-compound","Element or compound?","- An element is made of only one type of atom. - If two or more different types of atom are joined tightly together, the substance is a compound . - The chemical term for atoms joining tightly together is bonding ."],[1,"a-compound-is-nothing-like-the-elements-it-is-made-from","A compound is nothing like the elements it is made from","When two elements bond, they completely lose the properties of the individual elements and the compound has totally new properties. Sodium Chlorine Sodium chloride a soft grey metal, a solid a greenish-yellow gas a white solid you could not eat it you could not eat it it is common salt — you may have eaten some today"],[1,"naming-compounds","Naming compounds","- If the compound contains a metal , the metal comes first in the name. - The name of the non-metal is usually changed; the ending often becomes -ide . Sodium and chlorine make sodium chloride , not \"sodium chlorine\". - If the compound contains two elements plus oxygen , the name often ends in -ate . A compound of calcium, carbon and oxygen is calcium carbon ate . - Di- means two and mon-/mono- means one: carbon di oxide has one carbon atom joined to two oxygen atoms, and carbon mon oxide has one carbon atom joined to one oxygen atom."],[1,"formulae","Formulae","Every compound also has a formula , made from the symbols of the elements bonded in it. The small number written below and to the right of a symbol tells you how many atoms of that element are in the particle. No number means one atom. Chemical name Formula What it contains calcium oxide CaO one calcium atom bonded with one oxygen atom carbon dioxide CO₂ one carbon atom bonded with two oxygen atoms carbon monoxide CO one carbon atom bonded with one oxygen atom hydrogen sulfide H₂S two hydrogen atoms bonded with one sulfur atom calcium carbonate CaCO₃ one calcium, one carbon and three oxygen atoms sodium hydroxide NaOH one sodium, one oxygen and one hydrogen atom Read symbols carefully. C is carbon; Ca is calcium. CO is carbon monoxide; Co would be the metal cobalt."],[1,"particle-diagrams","Particle diagrams","A particle diagram shows which atoms of which elements make up a particle. It is an easy way to tell an element from a compound: if there are different kinds of atom bonded together, it is a compound. - CO₂ (carbon dioxide), H₂O (water) and CH₄ (methane) are compounds — each particle contains more than one kind of atom. - O₂ (oxygen) is an element — both atoms in the particle are oxygen atoms. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.6</b </summary 1. Two ways sodium chloride differs from sodium. Sodium chloride is white and sodium metal is grey; sodium chloride is edible and sodium metal is not. 2. Two ways sodium chloride differs from chlorine. Sodium chloride is a solid and chlorine is a gas; sodium chloride is white and chlorine is greenish-yellow. 3. Which two elements are combined in sodium chloride? Sodium and chlorine. 4. In hydrogen sulfide? Hydrogen and sulfur. 5. In magnesium oxide? Magnesium and oxygen. 6. A student wrote \"sulfur calcium\". What is wrong? The non-metal has been written first. The metal comes first and the non-metal's name changes, so the correct name is calcium sulfide . 7. Which three elements are in calcium nitrate? Calcium, nitrogen and oxygen. 8. In magnesium carbonate? Magnesium, carbon and oxygen. 9. In lithium sulfate? Lithium, sulfur and oxygen. 10. Which of K, O₂, NaCl, Al, Ca, CaCl₂, H₂ are elements and which are compounds? K, O₂, Al, Ca and H₂ are elements — each contains only one type of atom. NaCl and CaCl₂ are compounds — each contains more than one type of atom. 11. SO₂ — (a) how many different elements? (b) how many oxygen atoms per sulfur atom? (a) 2. (b) 2. 12. H₂O — (a) which two elements? (b) what do the numbers tell you? (a) Hydrogen and oxygen. (b) Two atoms of hydrogen are combined with one atom of oxygen. 13. Why is CO called carbon monoxide rather than just carbon oxide? To prevent confusion with the other gas made from carbon and oxygen, carbon dioxide (CO₂). 14. Name MgO, NaCl and CaCl₂. Magnesium oxide, sodium chloride, calcium chloride. 15. NaOH and KOH — which two elements are in all hydroxides? Oxygen and hydrogen. 16. What is LiOH? Lithium hydroxide. 17. How many different elements are in LiOH? Three. </details"],[1,"2-7-compounds-and-mixtures","2.7 Compounds and mixtures","Key words: composition · evaporating basin · filings · mineral · mixture · natural emissions · pipe-clay triangle · pure"],[1,"iron-and-sulfur-the-classic-comparison","Iron and sulfur: the classic comparison","Iron Sulfur Iron sulfide (the compound) metal or non-metal metal non-metal — appearance hard, grey, strong yellow, brittle a dark solid conducts heat and electricity? yes no no magnetic? yes no no - Mix iron filings and sulfur powder and you get a mixture . Both elements keep all their own properties, so a magnet can pull the iron straight back out. - Heat that mixture and the atoms bond to form the compound iron sulfide . It has completely new properties — a magnet will not separate the iron out, because there is no longer any iron in the mixture; the iron atoms are bonded inside the compound."],[1,"mixture-or-compound","Mixture or compound?","Mixture Compound the substances are just mixed together, not bonded the atoms are bonded tightly together each substance keeps its own properties the properties are completely new can usually be separated easily, e.g. with a magnet cannot be separated by physical means the amounts can vary the formula is always the same Pure means containing only a single substance. Pure water contains only water. A mixture is not pure: it is made of different kinds of particle mixed together, and it may be a mixture of elements, compounds, or both. There are solid, liquid and gas mixtures."],[1,"air-is-a-mixture","Air is a mixture","Gas Roughly how much of the air nitrogen 78% oxygen 21% carbon dioxide, argon, water vapour and other gases 1% - The composition of air varies. The amount of water vapour changes all the time, depending on the weather. - The amount of carbon dioxide changes too. Natural emissions add it — animals and plants produce carbon dioxide when they respire. Plants also remove it when they make food. Human activity, especially burning fossil fuels, increases it. - Over millions of years the composition of air has changed a great deal; at one time there was much less oxygen in the atmosphere. In this diagram, red circles are oxygen atoms, black circles are carbon atoms, blue circles are nitrogen atoms and white circles are hydrogen atoms. Carbon dioxide, nitrogen, oxygen and water are all present — so air contains elements (nitrogen, oxygen) and compounds (carbon dioxide, water) mixed together."],[1,"mineral-water-is-a-mixture","Mineral water is a mixture","- The label on a bottle of mineral water lists many minerals . There is more than just water in the bottle. - The minerals are dissolved in the water, so the mineral water is a solution — and a solution is a kind of mixture. - On the label shown, the three most abundant minerals are bicarbonate (248 mg/l) , calcium (55 mg/l) and chloride (37 mg/l) ."],[1,"experiment-is-drinking-water-really-a-mixture","Experiment: is drinking water really a mixture?","You will need — safety glasses, an evaporating basin, a tripod, a pipe-clay triangle, a Bunsen burner, tongs, water. Method 1. Put some water in the evaporating basin and heat it until it boils. 2. Turn the heat down and keep heating gently. 3. Remove it from the heat when some of the water has evaporated, or as soon as the solution starts to spit. 4. Leave it to cool. The rest of the water may take a day or two to evaporate. What you see, and why. A solid residue is left in the basin. It was dissolved in the water all along — the water evaporated away and left it behind. That proves the water was not pure : it was a mixture of water and dissolved substances. (Safety glasses matter here because boiling solutions spit.) <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.7</b </summary - Describe the appearance of (a) a mixture of iron and sulfur, (b) iron sulfide. (a) You can still see separate grey iron filings and yellow sulfur powder. (b) A single dark solid, with no separate yellow and grey parts. - Can you remove the iron from iron sulfide with a magnet? No. In the compound the iron atoms are bonded to sulfur atoms, so there is no iron left with its own magnetic property. - Which is the most common element in air? Nitrogen. - How many different kinds of substance are shown in the particle diagram? Four — nitrogen, oxygen, carbon dioxide and water. - Which is the least common compound in that sample of air? Carbon dioxide. - List the three most abundant minerals on the label. Bicarbonate, calcium and chloride. </details"],[2,"3-1-gravity-weight-and-mass","3.1 Gravity, weight and mass","Key words: accurate · contact force · force of gravity · formula triangle · gravity · kilogram · mass · newton · quantity · weight"],[2,"gravity","Gravity","- When you let go of an object, it falls to the ground. The pull that makes it fall is the force of gravity . - Every object causes a force of gravity. Even a pen or a pencil does. - The larger the mass of an object, the stronger the force of gravity it causes. The Earth has an enormous mass, so its gravity is strong enough to hold you on the ground. A pencil has a tiny mass, so its gravity is far too weak to notice. - The force of gravity caused by an object always acts towards the centre of that object . Imagine the Earth as a giant ball: wherever you stand on it, gravity pulls you towards the middle. That is why people at the South Pole do not fall off. - The strength of gravity decreases as you move further away from a large object. Travel away from Earth in a spacecraft and the Earth's pull on you gets weaker and weaker."],[2,"weight","Weight","Weight is the force of gravity acting on an object. - Weight is a force , so it is measured in newtons (N) . - The weight of an apple is about 1 N. That means the Earth's gravity pulls on the apple with a force of 1 N, and you need a force of 1 N to hold it up. - Heavy objects are hard to lift because you are pulling against gravity."],[2,"the-contact-force","The contact force","A book with a weight of 5 N rests on a desk. Gravity is still pulling the book down with a force of 5 N — so why does the book not sink through the desk? Because the desk pushes back up on the book with an equal force of 5 N. This upward push from a surface is the contact force . - The contact force acts upwards from any surface that is supporting an object. - When the surface is not moving, contact force = weight . The two forces are equal and opposite, so the object stays still. - If the weight is larger than the contact force the surface can supply, the object sinks into the surface or the surface breaks — like a heavy car sinking into soft sand."],[2,"weight-and-mass-are-not-the-same-thing","Weight and mass are not the same thing","Weight Mass What it is the force of gravity on an object the quantity of matter in an object Unit newton, N kilogram, kg Does it change on another planet? yes — it depends on the strength of gravity no — it is the same everywhere People often say \"the weight of my bag is 10 kg\". That sentence is wrong: it says weight but gives a mass . The correct sentence is \"the mass of my bag is 10 kg\"."],[2,"calculating-weight","Calculating weight","On Earth, gravity pulls with a force of 10 N on every 1 kg of mass. We say the strength of gravity on Earth is 10 N/kg (\"10 newtons per kilogram\"). weight (N) = mass (kg) × strength of gravity (N/kg) On Earth: W = m × 10 You can show this in a formula triangle with W on top and m and 10 underneath. Cover the quantity you want to find, and the uncovered part tells you the calculation. Worked examples - A person has a mass of 45 kg. Weight = 45 × 10 = 450 N . - A games console has a weight of 28 N. Mass = 28 ÷ 10 = 2.8 kg ."],[2,"gravity-elsewhere-in-the-solar-system","Gravity elsewhere in the Solar System","The strength of gravity is not 10 N/kg everywhere. To find weight somewhere else, use the same equation but replace the 10 with the local strength of gravity. Place Strength of gravity (N/kg) Jupiter 25 Neptune 11 Earth 10 Saturn 10 Venus 8.9 Uranus 8.7 Mercury 3.7 Mars 3.7 Moon 1.6 Your weight would be greatest on Jupiter and smallest on the Moon — but your mass would be exactly the same in every one of these places. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.1</b </summary 1. Draw the Earth and add arrows showing the direction of the force of gravity. Draw a circle, then draw at least four arrows that all point towards the centre of the circle. (Arrows may be drawn inside or outside the circle, as long as their lines would pass through the centre.) 2. Explain why people at the South Pole do not fall off the Earth. Gravity pulls them towards the centre of the Earth, so they are pulled \"down\" onto the ground — exactly as you are. 3. A ball is dropped at four places A, B, C and D around the Earth. Draw the direction each ball falls. Every arrow points towards the centre of the Earth. 4. Some people think there is no gravity on the Moon. Are they correct? No. Any object with mass causes gravity, and the Moon has a large mass, so it does exert a force of gravity — just a weaker one than the Earth's. 5. A box rests on a desk. Add and label the weight (W) and the contact force (C). W is an arrow pointing down from the box; C is an arrow pointing up from the desk into the box. 6. A rock resting on the ground has a weight of 8000 N. What is the contact force from the ground? 8000 N. 7. An elephant of weight 40 000 N stands on four feet. Calculate the contact force on each foot. 40 000 ÷ 4 = 10 000 N . 8. A car drives into soft mud. Each wheel needs a contact force of 24 000 N. a Why do the wheels start to sink when the contact force is only 2000 N? The weight is greater than the contact force, so the downward force is bigger than the upward force and the wheel moves down. b The contact force increases with depth. What stops the sinking? When the contact force has grown until it equals the weight, the up and down forces balance, so there is no more movement. 9. Gravity on Earth is 10 N/kg. a Weight of an adult of mass 75 kg? 75 × 10 = 750 N . b Mass of a car of weight 8500 N? 8500 ÷ 10 = 850 kg . 10. Use the diagram of gravity strengths. a Where would your weight be greatest? Jupiter. b Which planet gives you the same weight as on Earth? Saturn. c Weight of a 25 kg mass on Mars? 25 × 3.7 = 92.5 N . d How would your mass on Earth compare with your mass on Mercury? Exactly the same. Mass is the quantity of matter in an object, so it does not depend on the strength of gravity. 11. Standing on scales shows your mass in kg. Is it your mass or your weight that makes the scales work? Weight — it is the force of gravity on your body pushing down on the scales. (Your mass is what causes that weight, so both are involved.) 12. Why is a larger force needed to travel from Earth to the Moon than from the Moon back to Earth? Gravity on Earth (10 N/kg) is much stronger than gravity on the Moon (1.6 N/kg). Leaving Earth means overcoming a much greater weight, so a much larger force is needed. Activity — mass or weight? Mass … Weight … is measured in kilograms, kg of an object is affected by the strength of gravity on a planet is not affected by gravity is measured in newtons, N is the quantity of matter in an object of an object decreases as the object goes further from Earth can be measured in grams, g is the force needed to lift an object is the property of a planet that makes the planet have gravity is equal to the contact force on a level surface that is not moving Think like a scientist — linking weight and mass 1. As the mass gets bigger, the weight gets bigger . When the mass doubles, the weight doubles . 2. Is 1.0 kg exactly 10 N? No — a real force meter gives something in the range 9.5–9.9 N. 3. Why use 10 N/kg instead of the accurate values (9.7639–9.8337 N/kg)? The differences are very small, both values round to 10, and 10 is far easier to multiply by. </details"],[2,"3-2-formation-of-the-solar-system","3.2 Formation of the Solar System","Key words: axis · contradict · evidence · formed · hypothesis · model · nebula · observe · orbit · plane · spin · support"],[2,"how-can-we-know","How can we know?","Nobody was there to observe the Solar System forming. Scientists work on a problem like this in two ways: - look for evidence — facts from observations or experiments — and then try to explain what they have found; or - think of a testable idea, called a hypothesis , and then look for evidence that supports it. Evidence that goes against an idea contradicts it."],[2,"facts-about-the-solar-system","Facts about the Solar System","- All the planets orbit the Sun in the same direction . - The Sun and all the planets except Venus and Uranus spin on their axes in the same direction . - Most moons orbit their planets in the same direction as the planets go round the Sun. - The direction of spin of the Sun and most planets is the same as the direction of orbit. - All the planets orbit the Sun in the same plane — objects in the same plane could all be laid on one flat surface, so the Solar System looks flat."],[2,"watching-stars-being-born","Watching stars being born","Telescopes show distant stars forming out of clouds of dust and gas. A cloud of dust and gas in space is called a nebula — the Orion Nebula is one. Some young stars are seen with a flat disc of dust around them. Scientists think our own Solar System formed the same way."],[2,"using-models","Using models","Scientists cannot make a star form in a laboratory, so they use computers to build a model — a way of representing something that is difficult to observe directly. A model can compress millions of years into a short calculation. But models are not real, so they always have limitations ."],[2,"how-stars-and-planets-form","How stars and planets form","1. A nebula contains countless particles of dust and gas. Every particle has its own very weak gravity . 2. The particles pull on each other and stick together . Their total mass increases. 3. As the mass increases, the strength of their gravity increases , so they attract even more material. 4. A ball forms and grows. If it grows beyond a certain size it gets hot enough to become a star ; if not, it becomes a planet . Jupiter is thought to have almost reached the size needed to become a star. Forming a star or planet takes millions of years. Evidence that fits: if a flat, spinning cloud collapsed, everything would end up going round in the same direction and in the same plane — which is what we see. Evidence that does not fit: Venus and Uranus spin the \"wrong\" way. This seems to contradict the hypothesis. So the honest statement is that most, but not all , of the evidence supports it. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.2</b </summary 1. All the planets in the Solar System orbit the Sun in … the same direction. 2. Explain what \"the same plane\" means. All the orbits are on the same level and are not tilted relative to one another — they could all be laid on one flat surface. 3. What is the name given to a cloud of dust and gas in space? A nebula. 4. Name the force that pulls particles of dust and gas together in space. Gravity. Think like a scientist — using models 1. Another reason for using a model? It is not possible to do an experiment that re-creates the formation of the Solar System; a real experiment would need far too much space; it would be far too difficult to set up. 2. Models are not real, so may not be accurate. Which term describes this? A limitation . 3. a Two facts that seem to contradict the hypothesis. Venus and Uranus both spin on their axes in the opposite direction to the other planets. b Why do they contradict it? If everything formed from one flat, spinning cloud, everything should be turning the same way. Two planets turning the other way do not fit that picture. 4. Which two statements describe this hypothesis? Most, but not all, of the evidence supports it , and the model that is used has limitations . </details"],[2,"3-3-movement-in-space","3.3 Movement in space","Key words: air resistance · circular · speed · vacuum"],[2,"the-sun-and-its-gravity","The Sun and its gravity","- The Sun has by far the largest mass in the Solar System — greater than the mass of all the planets added together. - Because of that mass, the Sun's gravity is 27 times stronger than the Earth's. Where the Earth pulls with 10 N/kg, the Sun pulls with about 270 N/kg. - The Sun's gravity is what holds every planet in its orbit . - The Sun's gravity gets weaker as distance increases — but it is still strong enough to hold Neptune, which is 30 times further from the Sun than the Earth is. Safety: never look directly at the Sun, and never try to photograph it yourself."],[2,"orbits-of-planets","Orbits of planets","The orbits of the planets are almost circular . - To keep any object moving in a circle, there must be a force causing it to turn . - For a planet, that force is the gravity of the Sun , and it always acts towards the Sun . - Without that force, the planet would simply travel off in a straight line into space."],[2,"why-the-inner-planets-move-faster","Why the inner planets move faster","Mercury is closest to the Sun, so it feels the strongest pull of the Sun's gravity — and it orbits at the highest speed of all the planets, about 170 000 km/h. The Earth's average orbital speed is about 100 000 km/h. Planet Orbital speed (km/s) Mercury 48 Venus 35 Earth 30 Mars 24 Jupiter 13 Saturn 10 Uranus 7 Neptune 5 The pattern: as the distance from the Sun increases, the speed of orbit decreases. The orbits are not exact circles, so a planet's distance from the Sun changes slightly as it goes round. When it is closer to the Sun, the pull is stronger and the planet moves faster ."],[2,"speed-in-space","Speed in space","On Earth, moving objects are always being slowed down. Air resistance is one of the forces that does this. - Air resistance is caused by a moving object having to push against the particles in the air . - It always acts in the opposite direction to the movement . - The faster an object moves, the greater the air resistance on it. - Aeroplanes use this deliberately: changing the shape of the wing after landing produces extra air resistance and helps the plane slow down. In space there is almost no air. A space with no particles in it is called a vacuum . With no air resistance, spacecraft can reach and keep speeds that would be impossible in the Earth's atmosphere. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.3</b </summary 1. State the direction in which the force of gravity from the Sun pulls on a planet. Towards the (centre of the) Sun. 2. What keeps comets and asteroids in orbit around the Sun? Gravity from the Sun. 3. Which object has the second strongest gravity in the Solar System? Jupiter, because it has the second largest mass. 4. What word describes a space that has no particles in it? A vacuum. 5. Why could Voyager 1 not travel at its current speed on Earth? In space there is no air resistance acting on it. On Earth the air resistance at that speed would be far too great. 6. Which forces act on the Earth as it orbits the Sun? Gravity only. 7. How does the speed of a planet change with distance from the Sun during its orbit? The closer the planet gets to the Sun, the stronger the force of gravity on it, so the faster it moves. Activity — planet speeds 1. As the distance from the Sun increases, the speed of orbit of the planets decreases . 2. Why a graph rather than a table? It is easier to see a trend or pattern, and easier to spot a result that does not fit. 3. Explain the trend. As distance from the Sun increases, the force of gravity from the Sun on the planet decreases, so the planet's speed decreases. 4. Why a bar graph rather than a line graph? The planets are discrete (not continuous) data — there are no values in between, so there is nothing to read off between the bars. Think like a scientist — discovering planets 1. Calculations of where Uranus should be were predictions . 2. The recorded results of where Uranus actually was were observations . 3. \"Another source of gravity was pulling Uranus\" is a conclusion . 4. Uranus moving further from the Sun contradicted the original prediction about its orbit. Scientists found Neptune using careful observations . </details"],[2,"3-4-tides","3.4 Tides","Key words: coastal · depth · earth tide · earthquake · force of attraction · harbour · tidal force · tidal range · tide"],[2,"what-is-a-tide","What is a tide?","- The depth of the ocean is the distance from the surface of the water down to the bottom. - In many parts of the world that depth changes by several metres during the day. This regular change in depth is a tide . - The difference in depth between high tide and low tide is the tidal range . - The largest tidal range in the world is 16.3 m , in the Bay of Fundy in Canada. Some of the smallest, in the Caribbean and Mediterranean seas, are less than a metre. - High tides are about 12 hours apart. Low tides are also about 12 hours apart. A high tide and the next low tide are 6 hours apart. - The land rises and falls too. This is called an earth tide , and its tidal range is about 30 cm ."],[2,"what-causes-tides","What causes tides?","The Moon orbits the Earth, held there by the Earth's gravity — but the Moon has gravity too , and it pulls back on the Earth. - Water can be pulled more easily than solid land, so the effect shows up most in the oceans. - The Moon's pull on the Earth is called a tidal force . - The side of the Earth closest to the Moon has a high tide. - The side furthest from the Moon also has a high tide. - The Earth takes 24 hours to spin once on its axis. So 12 hours after a place is closest to the Moon, that place is furthest away — and it gets a high tide again. That is why high tides are 12 hours apart."],[2,"the-sun-s-part","The Sun's part","The Sun also produces a tidal force on the Earth, but a weaker one, because the Sun is so much further away. When the Sun, Earth and Moon line up — which happens at a full Moon and at a new Moon — the two tidal forces pull together and produce the largest tidal ranges ."],[2,"effects-of-tides","Effects of tides","- Harbours are places where boats load and unload. Some can only be used at certain times of day, because at low tide the water is not deep enough for boats to move safely. - Coastal areas — land close to the ocean — are more likely to flood at high tide when winds are strong. - The flow of water in and out of coastal areas can be dangerous for small boats. - Tides affect food chains : at low tide birds can reach shellfish that are usually under water, and some fish move with the tide to find food. - Earth tides have been linked with volcanic eruptions and earthquakes . Studying them may help scientists predict when a volcano will become dangerous. - The movement of water with the tides can be used to generate electricity . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.4</b </summary 1. Which causes the force of gravity for tides on Earth? The Sun and the Moon. 2. What is the name given to the pull of gravity that causes tides? The tidal force. 3. State the time between: a one high tide and the next — 12 hours; b one low tide and the next — 12 hours; c a high tide and the next low tide — 6 hours. 4. Explain why some harbours cannot be used at low tide. At low tide the water is at its lowest level, and it may not be deep enough for boats to move safely. 5. Why are the largest tidal ranges at full Moon and new Moon? At those times the Sun, Moon and Earth are in line, so the tidal forces from the Sun and the Moon pull together. The combined force on the water is stronger. 6. Average depth 5.0 m, largest tidal range 2.0 m. Calculate the maximum depth. A range of 2.0 m means 1.0 m above and 1.0 m below the average, so the maximum depth is 5.0 + 1.0 = 6.0 m . Activity — investigating tides 1. Why are the deepest parts of the oceans in line with the Moon? The tidal force from the Moon pulls the water towards the Moon on the near side of the Earth. 2. High tides in one full rotation? Two. 3. Low tides in one full rotation? Two. 4. a The Earth takes 24 hours to rotate once and there are two high tides in that time, so they are 12 hours apart. b Low tide is midway between two high tides — six hours after a high tide, and therefore 12 hours after the previous low tide. 5. a High tides do not happen at exactly the same time each day: the Moon is itself moving round the Earth, in the same direction as the Earth spins. b The Moon needs about 29 days to come back over the same point on Earth, so each day the tide is roughly 1/29 of a day — about 50 minutes — later. 6. Why is set 2 better in part 3? The ocean facing the Moon is deeper in set 2. When the Sun and Moon pull together the force on the water is greater than from the Moon alone. 7. Earth–Moon–Sun in a line with the Moon between: new Moon . 8. Moon on the far side: full Moon . 9. a The highest tides happen at new Moon and full Moon. b Two per lunar month. Think like a scientist — discovering the causes of tides 1. The sailor noticing the depth change was an observation . Thinking it was caused by the Moon was a prediction . Not knowing about gravity, he could not give an explanation . 2. How to show there is no magnetic force between the Moon and the oceans? Either show that water is not magnetic — pour some sea water out and try to attract it with a magnet — or show that there is no detectable magnetic effect from the Moon, for example that a compass points the same way whatever the Moon's position. 3. Why were Newton's ideas accepted? Newton provided evidence to support a hypothesis, and Newton made predictions that were shown to be accurate. </details"],[2,"3-5-energy","3.5 Energy","Key words: chemical · elastic potential · electrical · energy · fuel · gravitational potential · joule · kinetic · light · luminous · sound · stored · thermal · transferred"],[2,"what-is-energy","What is energy?","Energy is something that must be changed or transferred in order to make something happen. - Energy is measured in joules (J) . - Energy can be stored or transferred in many different ways."],[2,"energy-stores-and-transfers","Energy stores and transfers","Energy Description Store or transfer? kinetic energy stored because an object is moving store chemical energy stored in food, batteries and fuels such as wood, oil and coal store thermal heat energy stored in hot objects and transferred to colder objects store or transfer elastic potential energy stored when something is stretched or squeezed out of shape store gravitational potential energy stored when an object is lifted away from a source of gravity store electrical the flow of current in a circuit transfers electrical energy transfer sound energy transferred from vibrating objects transfer light visible energy from luminous objects (objects that give out their own light) transfer One object can have many of these at once: - chemical — the bus carries fuel - kinetic — the bus is moving - electrical — for the lights - thermal — the engine is hot - sound — the engine makes noise - gravitational potential — the bus is going uphill - elastic — the tyres get squeezed"],[2,"which-stores-last","Which stores last?","Energy can be stored much more easily in some ways than in others. Easy to store, lasts a long time: - Chemical — uncooked rice keeps for a long time; a battery holds its charge; coal and crude oil are chemical stores that formed millions of years ago. - Gravitational potential — water pumped up into a tank stays there, storing gravitational potential energy until it is let out. - Elastic potential — a stretched or squeezed object holds its store until it is released. Hard to store, lasts a short time: - Thermal — hot objects always cool down, losing their store to the surroundings. - Kinetic — a moving object such as a tennis ball only has its store while it is still moving. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.5</b </summary 1. Complete the sentences about the circuit. a Chemical energy is stored in the battery. b Electrical energy is transferred in the wires. 2. Name the energy store in each of these. a food — chemical; b gasoline (petrol) — chemical; c a falling rock — gravitational potential and kinetic; d a book lifted onto a shelf — gravitational potential. 3. a Name two energy stores that will last for a long time. Chemical, elastic potential or gravitational potential. b Name one store, apart from thermal, that will not last long. Kinetic. 4. Describe an example showing that thermal energy cannot be stored for long. Any hot object that cools down over time — a cup of tea, a cooking pot taken off the fire, a hot road after sunset. 5. How is a growing, developing world population affecting the amount of energy used? Demand keeps increasing: more people need more food, so more chemical energy; more people want electrical goods, so more electrical energy has to be generated; more people want cars and transport, so more fuel is burned. </details"],[2,"3-6-changes-in-energy","3.6 Changes in energy","Key words: change · event · process"],[2,"how-does-energy-change","How does energy change?","Energy must be changed or transferred before anything can happen. While energy is simply stored , it is not doing anything. When something happens — an event or a process — energy changes from one form to another. Cooking on a wood fire 1. Wood is a store of chemical energy. 2. Burning the wood changes chemical energy to thermal energy. 3. The thermal energy is transferred to the pot and the food inside. Walking up stairs 1. Food is a store of chemical energy. 2. The body changes it to kinetic energy for movement. 3. Because the movement takes you higher, the kinetic energy is changed to gravitational potential energy. A gas power station chemical (natural gas) → thermal (burning) → kinetic (spinning generators) → electrical (transferred through wires to homes)"],[2,"showing-energy-changes-in-a-diagram","Showing energy changes in a diagram","You can write an energy change as a chain, with an arrow for each step: - a candle: chemical → light - a television: electrical → sound + light - a book falling off a shelf: gravitational potential → kinetic These are all useful energy changes — the energy is changed in a way that we want. Not every energy change is useful, and not every one is helpful: typhoons, earthquakes and tsunamis are all energy being changed in damaging ways. You will meet wasted energy in Section 3.7. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.6</b </summary 1. When something happens, energy is … or … . changed or transferred. 2. Write the useful energy change in each of these. a an electric lamp — electrical → light; b a bus — chemical → kinetic (electrical → kinetic for an electric bus); c a radio — electrical → sound (chemical → electrical → sound if it runs on a battery). 3. Draw diagrams to show the energy changes in: a a motorcycle using petrol — chemical → kinetic; b a wood-burning cooking fire — chemical → thermal; c a bird flying higher — kinetic → gravitational potential; d a ball rolling down a hill — gravitational potential → kinetic. Activity — freezing water 1. The temperature of the water decreases . 2. You can tell without a thermometer because the water turns from liquid to solid — freezing only happens if the temperature has fallen. 3. Energy is transferred as thermal energy . 4. It comes from the water . 5. It goes out of the water into the freezer, and then out of the back of the freezer. Think like a scientist — candle energy 1. Why not stir with the thermometer? It risks breaking the thermometer, and a thermometer is measuring equipment, not stirring equipment. 2. Where did the energy come from? The wax is a store of chemical energy; burning changes that chemical energy to thermal energy in the flame. 3. What happened to the candle? The wax was used up, so the candle decreased in mass. 4. Two other things that get heated. Any two of: the container; the tripod, gauze or support; the air around the equipment; the heat-proof mat or desk. 5. How to transfer more thermal energy into the oil? Move the flame closer to the beaker; shield the apparatus from draughts; put a lid on the beaker; use a thinner gauze; insulate the sides of the beaker. 6. Why a line graph rather than a bar chart? The data are continuous — there are real temperature values in between the readings, and you can read intermediate values off the line. </details"],[2,"3-7-where-does-energy-go","3.7 Where does energy go?","Key words: dissipated · recovered · useful · wasted energy"],[2,"useful-and-wasted-energy","Useful and wasted energy","Every time energy is transferred or changed, some of it is useful and some of it is wasted . - A motorcycle engine changes the chemical energy in petrol into kinetic energy to move the bike — that part is useful. But a great deal of the chemical energy also becomes thermal energy in the engine and sound from the exhaust. That part is wasted. - Two lamps can give the same brightness while wasting very different amounts: - Lamp A changes about 15% of the electrical energy into light; the other 85% is wasted as thermal energy. - Lamp B changes about 50% into light; the other 50% is wasted as thermal energy."],[2,"dissipation","Dissipation","Wasted energy spreads out into the surroundings and becomes too spread out to be useful. We say it is dissipated . Energy that has been dissipated cannot be recovered. Every time energy is changed or transferred, some thermal energy is dissipated — there is no exception. Even when thermal energy is exactly what you want, some of it still escapes: Water is heated over a wood fire. Chemical energy in the wood is changed to thermal energy by burning. Some of the thermal energy heats the water — the useful part. But thermal energy also heats the rocks, the metal container and the surrounding air, some escapes in the steam, and the fire also gives out light. All of that is wasted energy, dissipated and gone for good. As you move further from a hot object you feel less heat: the temperature falls as distance increases, and eventually levels off at the temperature of the surroundings. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.7</b </summary 1. Which term describes energy that is dissipated? Energy that spreads out and becomes less useful. 2. Which of these can be dissipated? Thermal, light and sound. 3. List all the energy changes, saying whether each is useful or wasted. a Electricity in a lamp: electrical → light (useful), thermal (wasted). b Petrol in a car engine: chemical → kinetic (useful), thermal and sound (both wasted). c Electricity in a motor: electrical → kinetic (useful), thermal and sound (both wasted). 4. Which graph shows how temperature changes with increasing distance from a hot object? Graph 3 — the temperature falls as you move away and then levels off, because eventually the temperature becomes the same as the surroundings. Activity — ripple tanks 1. A variable that must stay the same when you change the direction of the wave. The depth of the water (and the height the tray is dropped from). 2. Which way does the wave travel the longest total distance? Along the tray — it makes about the same number of passes each way, but each pass along the tray is longer. 3. Name the energy stored by the moving wave. Kinetic. 4. As the wave travels, the energy in the wave … dissipates. Think like a scientist — energy dissipation 1. Plot time on the horizontal axis and temperature on the vertical axis, with labelled axes, units and sensible linear scales, and draw a smooth curve through the points. 2. Describe the pattern. As time increases, the temperature decreases — quickly at first, then more and more slowly. 3. Why stir before measuring? So all the liquid is at the same temperature and the thermometer is not sitting in a hot or cold spot. 4. What do the results show? The thermal energy in the tea decreases with time — it is being transferred out of the cup and dissipated. 5. Where could the thermal energy have gone? Into the cup, the air around the cup, the table, and away in the steam. 6. a To cool the tea faster: pour it into a wider, shallower container; blow across it; stir it; stand the cup somewhere cold or in a draught. b To cool it more slowly: put a lid on the cup; wrap the cup in an insulating material; stand it on an insulating mat. 7. Will the tea keep cooling? No. It will stop cooling once it reaches the temperature of its surroundings. </details"],[3,"4-1-characteristics-of-living-organisms","4.1 Characteristics of living organisms","Key words: excretion · growth · movement · nutrition · organism · reproduction · respiration · sensitivity"],[3,"living-and-non-living","Living and non-living","- A living thing is called an organism . - Deciding whether something is alive is not always as easy as it sounds. A person is obviously alive — you can check for breathing and a heartbeat. But a plant does not breathe and has no heart, and a plant is certainly alive. - So biologists do not use one single test. They use a set of seven characteristics . Something that shows all seven is a living organism."],[3,"the-seven-characteristics","The seven characteristics","Characteristic What it means Example Nutrition feeding — taking in materials from the environment for energy and growth a bear eats meat; a plant makes food by photosynthesis Growth a permanent increase in size a cub grows into an adult bear Movement changing the position or shape of part or all of the body a bear walks; a plant turns its leaves towards the light Excretion getting rid of the waste materials made by the chemical reactions inside the body animals excrete carbon dioxide when they breathe out Reproduction making new individuals of the same kind of organism bears produce cubs Respiration breaking down food inside cells to release energy every living cell respires, all the time Sensitivity detecting and responding to changes in the environment a bear smells meat from far away A useful memory trick: the first letters spell MRS NERG — M ovement, R espiration, S ensitivity, N utrition, E xcretion, R eproduction, G rowth."],[3,"two-things-that-are-easy-to-mix-up","Two things that are easy to mix up","Respiration is not breathing. Breathing moves air in and out of the lungs. Respiration is a chemical reaction that happens inside cells , in every living organism, including plants and organisms that have no lungs at all. Excretion is not the same as getting rid of undigested food. Excretion means getting rid of waste made by the body's own chemical reactions — carbon dioxide, for example."],[3,"is-a-car-alive","Is a car alive?","A car is a good test of the seven characteristics, because it seems to show several of them: Like a living organism Not like a living organism it uses fuel — a bit like nutrition it cannot move by itself; a driver has to make it move it uses fuel and oxygen to release energy — a bit like respiration it cannot reproduce its exhaust gives off waste gases, including carbon dioxide — a bit like excretion it cannot grow some cars have sensors, so they respond to darkness — a bit like sensitivity A car shows some of the characteristics, but not all seven. That is why a car is not alive."],[3,"does-every-organism-show-all-seven-all-of-the-time","Does every organism show all seven all of the time?","Not at every moment. An elephant is alive but is not reproducing all of the time. An apple does not show sensitivity — but it is respiring, and it contains seeds that can grow into a whole plant. A seed does not move — but it will grow into a plant that can move parts of its body. The characteristics describe what an organism can do over its life, not what it happens to be doing this second. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.1</b </summary Copy and complete, using: carbon dioxide, chewing, feeding, growth, movement, oxygen, reproduce, respiration, sight, smell 1. Another word for taking in nutrition is …. feeding 2. Polar bears can sense things in their environment. With their nose they can sense the … of meat. smell 3. Animals excrete … when they breathe out. carbon dioxide 4. Living organisms … to make more of the same kind of organism. reproduce 5. Young plants and animals get bigger. This is called …. growth 6. All living organisms break down some of the food they eat, to provide them with energy. This happens in a process called …. respiration 7. Most living organisms can change the shape and position of their bodies. This is called …. movement Activity 4.1.1 — Is a car alive? 1. Similarities between a car and living organisms. It moves; it uses fuel, which is like taking in food (nutrition); it uses fuel and oxygen to release energy, which is like respiration; it gives off waste gases including carbon dioxide, which is like excretion; some cars have sensors, which is like sensitivity. 2. Differences. A car cannot move by itself — someone has to drive it. It cannot reproduce and it cannot grow. Workbook 4.1B — the seven characteristics from their clues 1 sensitivity · 2 respiration · 3 movement · 4 excretion · 5 nutrition · 6 reproduction · 7 growth Workbook 4.1C — Living or not? Not all living things show every characteristic all of the time. An elephant does not reproduce all the time. An apple shows no sensitivity, but it does respire and it holds seeds that can grow into a complete plant. A seed does not move, but it will grow into a plant that can. Something is living if it shows all seven characteristics at some stage of its life . </details"],[3,"4-2-viruses","4.2 Viruses","Key words: electron microscope · influenza · protein · replicate · RNA · virus"],[3,"what-is-a-virus","What is a virus?","- A virus is very, very small — much smaller than one of your cells. A school microscope cannot show you one. To see a virus you need an electron microscope , which is a large, expensive machine that produces its picture on a screen. - In an electron-microscope photograph, viruses may be shown about 100 000 times bigger than they really are."],[3,"the-structure-of-a-virus","The structure of a virus","Viruses are not made of cells. They have no cell membrane and no cytoplasm. A virus has only two parts: Part What it is protein coat the outer layer, made of protein, with little pegs sticking out of it RNA inside the coat: threads carrying a set of coded instructions for making more viruses The pegs matter: they are what lets the virus stick to a cell and get inside it. In SARS-CoV-2, the pegs are called spike proteins . They help the virus attach to a cell and get inside."],[3,"how-viruses-replicate","How viruses replicate","A virus can do nothing at all on its own. It does not respire, feed, excrete or grow; it is not sensitive and it cannot move. It has to get inside a living cell before it can make copies of itself. 1. The virus reaches a cell it can infect. For H3N2 influenza, the viruses go up your nose when you breathe in. 2. The pegs stick the virus onto the cell, and the virus gets inside. 3. Once inside, the virus bursts open . 4. The virus forces the cell to copy the instructions on its RNA and make many new viruses. This is called replication . 5. Replication kills the cell . The new viruses burst out, ready to infect more cells. That is why the animal whose cells are infected feels ill. H3N2 viruses cause a very unpleasant and dangerous kind of influenza (flu) ; in 1968–1969 they killed approximately one million people. There are thousands of different kinds of virus, and each kind infects one particular kind of cell . Some viruses infect plant cells."],[3,"sars-cov-2","SARS-CoV-2","- A new virus appeared in 2019 . Scientists think it developed in a wild animal and then spread to humans. - Its official name is SARS-CoV-2 . The illness it causes is called Covid-19 , which stands for coronavirus disease 2019 . - It is similar to the viruses that cause flu and colds, and it spread all over the world very quickly. - Many people get it without being ill at all, or have mild symptoms; in some people it causes dangerous illness and even death. Ways of dealing with it include vaccination and drugs to treat the illness."],[3,"are-viruses-alive","Are viruses alive?","Scientists do not all agree. Set a virus against the seven characteristics: Argument that viruses ARE living Argument that viruses are NOT living they replicate — they make more of themselves, which is like reproduction they can only replicate inside a living cell ; on their own they can do nothing they carry coded instructions (RNA), as living cells do they are not made of cells they show none of the other six characteristics: no respiration, nutrition, excretion, growth, movement or sensitivity On balance, most biologists decide that viruses are not living organisms — but it is a genuine argument, not a settled fact, and either answer can be defended if you give the evidence. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.2</b </summary Getting started — the other six characteristics of living organisms are nutrition, excretion, growth, movement, sensitivity and reproduction. Their meanings are in the table in 4.1. Think like a scientist — making a model of a virus. The model needs three things: an outer coat of protein, little threads of RNA inside the coat, and pegs on the outside of the coat. Activity 4.2.1 — Are viruses alive? Viruses can replicate themselves, which is a characteristic of living organisms. But they can only do it inside a living cell, and they show none of the other characteristics. On balance, most people decide that viruses are not living organisms. Workbook 4.2 1. Viruses are very small . Viruses are not made of cells . Viruses can only replicate when they are inside a living cell. 2. a Evidence for Scientist A (viruses are not living): they can only replicate inside a living cell, and they cannot carry out any of the other characteristics of living organisms. b Evidence for Scientist B (viruses are living): they can replicate; they carry coded instructions (RNA) for making copies of themselves. 3. How viruses were discovered. The first virus found was tobacco mosaic virus . In 1892 the Russian scientist Ivanovsky passed an extract from infected leaves through a filter fine enough to hold back bacteria. The filtered extract could still infect other leaves, so he concluded there was something smaller than a bacterium in it — though he thought it was a chemical. In 1898 the Dutch scientist Beijerinck worked on the same problem and decided 'viruses' were a liquid. Years later the American scientist Stanley proved that they are very small particles. Progress was slow because microscopes could not yet 'see' anything that small. </details"],[3,"4-3-what-is-a-species","4.3 What is a species?","Key words: fertile · identical · infertile · offspring · species · specimen · variation"],[3,"species","Species","- Scientists group living organisms into different kinds. Each kind of organism is called a species. - Organisms in the same species usually look similar to one another, and look different from organisms of other species. Comparing two species of elephant Similarities Differences both have four legs the African elephant has larger ears both have tusks the African elephant has longer tusks both have a trunk the African elephant has a more domed head both have large ears that lie back against the neck the African elephant is grey ; the Indian elephant is brown"],[3,"variation","Variation","All the organisms in a species share the same characteristics, but they are not identical . Some Indian elephants have straighter tusks than others; the pink markings on their skin are in different places. Differences between individuals of the same species are called variation . Variation is exactly what makes identification hard. Two organisms that look slightly different might be two species — or they might be two individuals of one species."],[3,"the-species-test-fertile-offspring","The species test: fertile offspring","Because looks are not enough, scientists use reproduction as the test. - Each species reproduces only with other members of its own species , and the offspring belong to the same species as their parents. - Those offspring are fertile : they can go on to produce offspring of their own. A species is a group of organisms that can reproduce together to produce fertile offspring."],[3,"ligers-and-mules-why-the-test-needs-the-word-fertile","Ligers and mules — why the test needs the word *fertile*","Very rarely, two organisms from different species do reproduce together. It happens in zoos, when an animal has no member of its own species to breed with. - A male lion and a female tiger can produce a liger . Ligers are healthy animals, but they cannot reproduce — they are infertile . - A female horse and a male donkey can produce a mule . Mules are big and strong like horses, quiet and easy to handle like donkeys — and infertile . So lions and tigers, and horses and donkeys, belong to different species even though they can sometimes breed together. The offspring are not fertile, so the test is not passed."],[3,"how-scientists-decide-about-a-new-organism","How scientists decide about a new organism","If a scientist finds an organism that might be a new species, they: - collect several specimens , because of variation within a species — but not so many that a rare species is put in danger; - compare observable features carefully with similar known species: colour, measurements of body parts, behaviour, calls, and sometimes genes; - try to find out whether it can breed with the similar species to produce fertile offspring ; - publish the evidence so that other scientists can check it and repeat the work. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.3</b </summary Activity 4.3.1 — Comparing two species of elephant. Similarities: four legs, tusks, a trunk, large ears lying back against the neck. Differences: African elephants have larger ears, longer tusks and a more domed head, and they are grey rather than brown. (Some of what you can see in two photographs is variation between those two individuals, not a difference between the species — you cannot tell which is which from pictures alone.) Questions 1. Copy and complete, choosing from: bigger, different, identical, similar. Organisms that belong to the same species usually look similar to one another. They look different from organisms belonging to other species. 2. Explain why biologists say lions and tigers belong to different species, even though they can sometimes reproduce together. To be the same species, two organisms must reproduce together to produce fertile offspring. A liger is not fertile, so the test fails and lions and tigers are different species. Think like a scientist — comparing organisms of different species. 3. Suggest what a scientist would do to be sure the organisms really are two different species. Find out whether the two can reproduce with each other. If they have offspring, find out whether those offspring can themselves reproduce. If the offspring are fertile, the organisms are the same species; if not, they are different species. Workbook 4.3A — rhinoceroses. White rhinoceroses and Indian rhinoceroses belong to different species . This means they cannot reproduce with each other to produce fertile offspring. They do not look exactly the same as one another. Indian rhinoceroses have one horn , but white rhinoceroses have two horns . Workbook 4.3B — horses, donkeys and mules. 1. Three similarities between a horse and a donkey: four legs; two ears; hair on the body. (Other correct answers are allowed.) 2. Two differences: the donkey is smaller; the donkey has longer hair along its neck. 3. Two pieces of evidence that they are different species: they have different Latin names; they cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring — their offspring, the mule, is infertile. Workbook 4.3C — a new frog species. The researchers should collect enough specimens to allow for variation within a species, but not so many that they endanger a possibly rare frog. They should record observable features — colour, measurements, behaviour, the male's call — and compare them with the eight known Microhyla species in India; they could also compare genes, and even the tadpoles. Best evidence of all would be whether the new frog can breed with a known species to produce fertile offspring. Finally, other scientists should be able to check the data and repeat the findings. </details"],[3,"4-4-using-keys","4.4 Using keys","Key words: dichotomous key"],[3,"identifying-organisms","Identifying organisms","A biologist who finds an unfamiliar organism can start by looking at pictures in a reference book or on the internet, and hope to find a picture with the name on it. That does not always work — many species look alike, and there may be no picture of the one in front of you. So biologists use a key . A key is a set of questions about the organism you want to identify . The answer to each question sends you to another question. You work through the questions until you arrive at the name of the organism."],[3,"dichotomous-keys","Dichotomous keys","Dichotomous means 'branching into two'. At every step there are exactly two choices, so you always know where to go next. How to use it - Choose one organism you want to identify. - Start at the top of the key and answer the first question — yes or no. - Follow the line to the next question, and keep going until you reach a name. Take the dragonfly: Does it have legs? Yes → Does it have more than six legs? No → Does it have four wings? Yes → dragonfly ."],[3,"the-same-key-written-as-pairs-of-statements","The same key, written as pairs of statements","A key can be set out in a second style. Instead of a question there is a pair of statements to choose between, and instead of an arrow there is a number telling you which pair to go to next. Step Statement Where it takes you 1 a It has legs. go to 2 b It does not have legs. go to 3 2 a It has exactly six legs. go to 4 b It has more than six legs. crab 3 a Its body is made up of rings. earthworm b Its body is not made up of rings. slug 4 a It has four wings. dragonfly b It has two wings. housefly Working through this style for the dragonfly gives the steps 1a, 2a, 4a . For the earthworm: 1b, 3a . Both styles contain exactly the same information. Which is easier is a matter of taste — the branching diagram shows the whole structure at a glance, while the numbered statements fit into a book and are easier to print."],[3,"a-worked-example-four-species-of-fish","A worked example: four species of fish","The key asks: Does it have stripes? → Are the stripes vertical? → Do the fins have spines? Fish Stripes? Stripes vertical? Fins have spines? Name A no — — horn shark B yes yes yes dragon fish C yes no — zebra fish D yes yes no clown fish Written as pairs of statements: Step Statement Where it takes you 1 a The fish has stripes. go to 2 b The fish does not have stripes. horn shark 2 a The stripes are vertical. go to 3 b The stripes are not vertical. zebra fish 3 a The fins have spines. dragon fish b The fins do not have spines. clown fish <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.4</b </summary Questions 1. Using the key, which steps would you go through to identify the earthworm? 1b, 3a — it does not have legs, and its body is made up of rings. 2. Explain why the key is called a dichotomous key. Dichotomous means 'branching into two'. At every step there are exactly two statements or answers to choose between. Think like a scientist — using a key to identify species of fish 1. A is a horn shark · B is a dragon fish · C is a zebra fish · D is a clown fish. 2. The same key in the pairs-of-statements style: 1 a Fish has stripes … go to 2 · b Fish does not have stripes … horn shark 2 a Stripes are vertical … go to 3 · b Stripes are not vertical … zebra fish 3 a Fins have spines … dragon fish · b Fins do not have spines … clown fish Workbook 4.4A — identifying a fruit. Fruit B is a sycamore . Workbook 4.4B — four fish. 1. a Description 1a fits fish A (it has a row of gill slits), so go to step 2. b Fish A is a basking shark — its eye is above the front end of its mouth. 2. a Fish B also fits 1a . b Fish B is a Greenland shark — its eye is above the back end of its mouth. 3. Fish C and D show no gill slits, so go to step 3. The one with long spines on its top fin is the John Dory ; the one with short spines is the sea bream . Workbook 4.4C — five leaves. Work down the key one statement at a time and write out the route: for example 1b (not made of many small leaflets) → 2a (oval) → 3a (at least twice as long as wide) → 4a (shiny surface) gives willow ; 4b (dull surface) gives cherry . A leaf made of many small leaflets is rowan ; an unlobed leaf that is less than twice as long as wide is hazel ; a leaf with five lobes is maple . </details"],[3,"4-5-constructing-keys","4.5 Constructing keys",""],[3,"building-your-own-key","Building your own key","To make a key, you keep splitting the group in two until every organism is on its own. Step What you do 1 Find a feature that splits everything into two groups — for example, Is the learner female? 2 Take one of those groups and find a feature that splits it in two — for example, hair colour. 3 Repeat until each organism is separated from all the others."],[3,"what-makes-a-good-question","What makes a good question","This is the part that goes wrong most often. Every statement in a key must give a clear, certain yes-or-no answer for someone holding one specimen with nothing to compare it with. Bad question Why it fails Is the plant tall? 'Tall' compared with what? Two people would disagree. Does the plant have dark green leaves? 'Dark green' is a matter of opinion. Are its leaves bigger than the other plant's? You may only have one plant in front of you. Good question Why it works Do the flowers have five or more petals? You can count petals; the answer is the same for everyone. Are the leaves darker on the upper surface than on the lower surface? Both surfaces are on the same leaf, so it is a comparison you can always make. Does it have exactly six legs? Countable, and certain. Two more rules of thumb: keep the key short (three pairs of statements is usually enough for four organisms), and test it on somebody else — if they can identify every organism with it, it works. If they cannot, ask which step confused them and rewrite that step. Flowers are a good group to practise on, because the features you need — the number of petals, whether the petals are joined, the shape of the petal edge — are all countable or clear-cut. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.5</b </summary Getting started — which two questions would not be good to use in a key? Is the plant tall? and Does the plant have dark green leaves? Different people would disagree about what counts as 'tall' or 'dark green'. A statement in a key must always give a clear yes/no answer. Think like a scientist — a key for four learners. Any working key is acceptable. For example: 1 a The learner is male … go to 2 · b The learner is female … go to 3 2 a The learner wears glasses … (one of the boys) · b The learner does not wear glasses … (the other boy) 3 a The learner has red hair … (one of the girls) · b The learner does not have red hair … (the other girl) Peer assessment — what a good key looks like. It is made of pairs of statements; each pair is easy to choose between; there are no more than three pairs; and someone else can actually use it to identify all four people. Think like a scientist — a key for four species of cat. For example: 1 a The cat has stripes … tiger · b The cat does not have stripes … go to 2 2 a The cat has spots … leopard · b The cat does not have spots … go to 3 3 a The ears have long black tufts … lynx · b The ears have no tufts … Pallas's cat Then swap keys with a partner, ask them to try it, and change any step that they found unclear. </details"],[3,"check-your-progress","Check your progress","<details <summary <b Check your progress — worked answers</b </summary 4.1 The list describes some features of a horse: it can move · it has a heart · it has hair · it feeds · it respires · it can sense changes in its environment · it has a brain. a Which of these are characteristics of all living things? [4] It can move · it feeds · it respires · it can sense changes in its environment. (Having a heart, hair or a brain is not true of all living things — a plant has none of them.) b Two more characteristics of living things not in part a. [2] Excretion and reproduction. (Growth is also correct.) 4.2 Use the key to classify each animal into the correct class. [5] 1 a has three pairs of legs → insects ; b has more than three pairs → go to 2 2 a a pair of legs on every segment → myriapods ; b not a pair on every segment → go to 3 3 a has eight legs → arachnids ; b has more than eight legs → crustacea So: count the legs first. Six legs (three pairs) is an insect; eight legs is an arachnid; more than eight legs with a pair on every ringed segment is a myriapod; more than eight legs without that arrangement is a crustacean. 4.3 Two kinds of parakeet in New Zealand. The scientist never finds a yellow-crowned parakeet paired up with a red-crowned parakeet. a What evidence does she have for concluding they are different species? [2] They never breed with each other — she has searched suitable habitats and found no mixed pairs making a nest. Members of one species reproduce only with members of that same species. b What should she do to be more certain? [2] Check more pairs of parakeets in the wild. More observations of the same kind make the conclusion more reliable. Stuffed museum specimens cannot show her which birds pair up, and looking at other species of parakeet tells her nothing about these two. 4.4 Six flowers, A to F. Complete the key. [4] 1 a The flower has exactly four petals … Lunaria b The flower has more than four petals … go to 2 2 a The flower is yellow … go to 3 b The flower is not yellow … go to 4 3 a The petals are broad and rounded … Limnanthes · b The petals are narrow … Potentilla 4 a The flower is purple with dark markings … Viola · b The flower has no dark markings … Silene or Erodium (Any key works as long as each statement gives a clear yes/no answer and it separates all six flowers.) </details"],[4,"5-1-metals-and-non-metals","5.1 Metals and non-metals","Key words: brittle · conduct · ductile · insulators · magnetic · malleable · materials · shatter · shiny · sonorous"],[4,"materials","Materials","Materials are the substances from which objects are made. Wood, glass, plastic, copper and steel are all materials. Chemists sort materials into two big families: metals and non-metals ."],[4,"the-properties-of-metals","The properties of metals","There are many different metals, and they are used for many different jobs. The jobs they are chosen for come straight from their properties . Property What it means Where it is useful strong and tough they do not shatter when dropped and do not crack easily; they can hold large weights without breaking iron is used for bridges shiny they reflect light well when freshly cut or polished gold is used for jewellery malleable they can be hammered into shape iron is malleable ductile they can be drawn out into wires copper is ductile sonorous they make a ringing sound like a bell when hit cymbals high melting and boiling points most metals do not melt easily metals for cooking pans good conductors of heat heat energy travels through them easily steel cooking pans good conductors of electricity an electric current can flow through them copper electrical wiring some are magnetic they are attracted by a magnet iron, steel, nickel and cobalt Mercury is the odd one out: it is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature . Why metals feel cold. A metal at room temperature is not actually colder than the table next to it. It is a good conductor, so when you touch it, it conducts heat energy away from your hand quickly. Your hand cools down, and that is what \"cold\" feels like."],[4,"three-things-to-be-careful-about","Three things to be careful about","- The surface of most metals goes dull after a while, so \"shiny\" is only a fair test on a freshly cut or polished surface. - Big lumps of metal are hard to test for flexibility — you cannot bend a girder by hand, but it is still malleable. - Bottles and cups also make a ringing sound when they are hit, and they are not metals. One property on its own is never enough."],[4,"the-properties-of-non-metals","The properties of non-metals","Non-metals are often useful because of the chemical reactions they have with other substances. There is a lot of variation between non-metals, so their properties come in two groups. Properties shared by almost all non-metals Property Why it matters dull they do not reflect light well and the surface is not as smooth as a metal brittle (the solid ones) if you drop them they may shatter poor conductors of heat some are used for handles on cooking pans poor conductors of electricity they are used as insulators — coverings for plugs and cables An insulator is a material that does not conduct heat or electricity. Properties shared by many non-metals - Non-metals are not as hardwearing as metals. - Many non-metals are gases at room temperature. - The non-metals that are not gases have low melting points and low boiling points ."],[4,"some-useful-non-metals","Some useful non-metals","Non-metal Use sulfur added to rubber to make it hard oxygen pure oxygen is used in hospitals for people with breathing difficulties helium fills balloons and airships, because it is very light chlorine kills bacteria — it is dissolved in water and added to swimming pools carbon purifies water and treats indigestion silicon makes computer chips"],[4,"where-they-are-in-the-periodic-table","Where they are in the Periodic Table","Metals are found on the left side of the Periodic Table. Non-metals are found on the right side. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.1</b </summary 1. List ten metals. Any ten, for example: iron, copper, gold, silver, aluminium, zinc, tin, lead, nickel, mercury. 2. Why are gold and silver used for jewellery? Because they are shiny and quite rare, which increases their value. 3. Why is copper so useful? It can be made into wires because it is ductile, and it is a good conductor of electricity, so it is used for electrical wires. 4. What do 'malleable' and 'ductile' mean? Malleable: able to be hammered or shaped. Ductile: able to be drawn out into a wire. 5. What are Olympic medals made from? Gold, silver and bronze. 6. Where are metals found in the Periodic Table? On the left side. 7. Name five non-metals, other than sulfur and helium. Any five, for example: oxygen, nitrogen, carbon, chlorine, silicon. 8. What is sulfur used for? Sulfur is used to harden rubber. 9. What property of helium makes it useful in balloons? It is very light. 10. Where would you find the non-metals in the Periodic Table? On the right side. </details"],[4,"5-2-comparing-metals-and-non-metals","5.2 Comparing metals and non-metals","Key words: contact · distinguish · examine"],[4,"the-comparison-table","The comparison table","Metals Non-metals Most are solid at room temperature. Many are gases at room temperature. They are shiny. They are dull. They do not shatter. They are brittle. They conduct heat energy well. They do not conduct heat energy well. They conduct electricity. Most do not conduct electricity. They are malleable. They are ductile. They are sonorous."],[4,"testing-a-material","Testing a material","To distinguish (identify) whether a material is a metal or a non-metal, examine it and then test it: - What does it look like — shiny or dull? - Does it make a ringing sound when you hit it? - Is it brittle? - Can you bend it? - Does it feel hot or cold? - Does it conduct electricity? The electrical test. Build a circuit with a cell, a lamp, wires and two crocodile clips, and put the test material in the gap. Before you start, check the lamp works by touching the crocodile clips together with nothing between them. When you test, make sure you have good contact between the clips and the material. If the lamp lights, the material conducts electricity. The conductivity test is usually the most useful single test, because almost all metals pass it and almost all non-metals fail it. But carbon in its black, soft form conducts electricity even though it is a non-metal — which is why you never rely on one test alone. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.2</b </summary 1. List five objects in the photograph of a market that are made of metal and five that are made of non-metals. Any five correct named objects of each. 2. A material is dull, brittle and does not conduct electricity. Is it a metal or non-metal? A non-metal. 3. Mercury is a metal. What unusual property does it have? It is a liquid at room temperature. 4. Write down two things that a metal can do better than a non-metal. Conduct electrical energy; conduct heat energy. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — identifying six substances (Exercise 5.2C)</b </summary Substance Metal or non-metal? Reason What it is A use A: shiny liquid, m.p. −39 °C, b.p. 357 °C, conducts, toxic metal it conducts electricity mercury thermometers and barometers (no longer used, because it is toxic) B: one black dull brittle form that conducts; one transparent very hard shiny form, m.p. 3730 °C non-metal it is dull and brittle, and the shiny form does not conduct carbon the soft form in pencils; the hard form (diamond) in jewellery and drills C: transparent gas, m.p. −219 °C, b.p. −183 °C, needed for respiration non-metal it is a gas at room temperature and does not conduct oxygen respiration; burning D: unreactive gas, m.p. −272 °C, b.p. −269 °C, very light non-metal gas at room temperature, does not conduct helium balloons and airships E: reddish shiny solid, m.p. 1082 °C, b.p. 2580 °C metal conducts heat and electricity well copper electrical wiring; cooking pans F: yellow brittle solid, m.p. 119 °C, b.p. 445 °C non-metal does not conduct electricity and is brittle sulfur hardening rubber True or false? a All metals are magnetic. — False (only iron, steel, nickel and cobalt). b Solid non-metals are brittle. — True . c All metals are solids. — False (mercury is a liquid). d Metals conduct heat energy. — True . e Non-metals are good conductors of electricity. — False . </details"],[4,"5-3-metal-mixtures","5.3 Metal mixtures","Key words: alloy · bronze · disrupt · steel"],[4,"what-an-alloy-is","What an alloy is","Metal mixtures are called alloys . Alloys are made by mixing different metals together and melting them. The atoms of the different metals mix but do not bond together — that is why an alloy is a mixture, not a compound. The properties of an alloy are different from the properties of the metals it contains. Alloy Made from Note bronze copper + tin harder than either copper or tin brass copper + zinc sometimes lead, aluminium, manganese or silicon too steel iron + carbon unusual, because carbon is not a metal stainless steel iron + carbon + chromium + nickel does not rust; used for cutlery duralumin aluminium + magnesium + copper about five times stronger than pure aluminium Nitinol nickel + titanium a shape memory alloy cupro-nickel copper + nickel \"silver\" coins"],[4,"why-alloys-are-harder-the-particle-explanation","Why alloys are harder — the particle explanation","This is the most important idea in the whole topic. In a pure metal , the atoms are all the same size and arranged in regular rows . When a force is applied, the layers can slide over one another easily . That sliding is what makes a metal malleable when it is hammered, and ductile when it is stretched out. In an alloy , atoms of a different size are mixed in. A different sized atom disrupts the regular pattern, so the rows are no longer neat. The layers get stuck in place and cannot slip past one another easily. So: pure metal → regular rows → layers slide → soft. Alloy → disrupted pattern → layers stick → harder and stronger. The bubble raft model. Blow small, equal-sized bubbles onto diluted washing-up liquid in a Petri dish with a syringe. They line up in rows like the atoms of a pure metal, and the rows slide past each other easily. When a bubble bursts, the others move in to fill the space. Now push harder to inject one much larger bubble into the middle — it disrupts the rows, and the bubbles can no longer move freely. That is a model of an alloy."],[4,"alloys-in-everyday-life","Alloys in everyday life","Coins. Pure metals are too soft to withstand all the wear coins get. The coins that look silver are not silver — silver is too soft and far too expensive; they are alloys containing copper and nickel. Copper coins contain copper, zinc and tin. Coins must be hardwearing but still malleable enough to be stamped with complex patterns. Jewellery. Most gold jewellery is an alloy of gold and copper, because pure gold is soft and a pure gold wedding ring would wear away. Pure gold is 24 carat — 24 parts out of 24 are gold. 18 carat gold has 18 parts out of 24 gold and six parts of other metals such as copper, silver or zinc. Aeroplanes. A plane needs a metal that is light but very strong. Planes are mainly aluminium, but pure aluminium is not strong enough for the stresses of flight. Adding magnesium and copper makes duralumin . Artificial joints. Replacement hip and knee joints are made of plastic and alloys, often alloys of titanium . An alloy used inside the body must be strong, light, and must not react with the body's tissues. Modern alloys. Nitinol is a shape memory alloy made of nickel and titanium. Some glasses frames are made from it: if they get bent, heating them returns them to their original shape."],[4,"copper-zinc-and-brass","Copper, zinc and brass","copper zinc brass Element or mixture? element element mixture Appearance reddish brown soft metal silvery grey soft metal golden yellow, reddish gold or silver soft alloy Melting point 1085 °C 419.5 °C 900–1000 °C Properties very ductile and malleable; conducts heat and electricity well less ductile and malleable than copper; conducts less well than copper less ductile than copper, more malleable than zinc and copper; conducts less well than zinc; resistant to corrosion Uses electrical wiring; central heating pipes coating iron in a thin layer to stop it rusting musical instruments, plumbing"],[4,"copper-tin-and-bronze","Copper, tin and bronze","copper tin bronze Element or mixture? element element mixture Appearance reddish brown soft metal white metal reddish brown, not as bright as brass Melting point 1085 °C 232 °C 860–1150 °C Properties very ductile and malleable; good conductor soft, ductile and malleable; conducts less well than copper; does not corrode readily less ductile than copper; hard; brittle; good conductor; corrosion resistant Uses electrical wiring; central heating pipes coating the inside of food cans; many alloys sculptures; bells and cymbals; ship fittings; electrical connectors Why does brass or bronze have a range of melting points? Because there is not one brass or one bronze. Different amounts of copper and zinc (or copper and tin) give different mixtures, and each mixture has its own melting point. Copper and zinc are elements, so each has one fixed melting point. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.3</b </summary 1. What is an alloy? A mixture of metals. 2. Which properties of aluminium make it useful for building planes? It is light and easily malleable. 3. Why is an alloy of aluminium used for making planes instead of pure aluminium? Because aluminium is not strong enough on its own to withstand the stresses involved in flight. 4. Pure gold is 24 carat gold. What does this mean? 24 parts out of 24 are gold — there is no other metal present, so it is pure gold. 5. Explain the difference between the purity of 18 carat gold and 24 carat gold. 18 carat gold is an alloy of 18 parts gold to 6 parts of another metal, such as silver or copper; 24 carat gold is pure gold. 6. Why do we not use pure silver for our coins? It is too expensive, and it is not hard enough to stand up to the damage caused when coins rub against each other in pockets, purses and machines. 7. Why do we not use pure copper for our coins? It is too expensive and too soft. Copper also reacts with the air and turns green. 8. What properties must an alloy used inside the body have? It must be strong and light, and must not react with the body tissues. 9. Why are some glasses frames made from shape memory alloy? Because if they get bent they can easily be returned to their original shape by heating. 10. Why is a range of temperatures given for the melting point of bronze? Because there is more than one type of bronze — different proportions of copper and tin give mixtures with different melting points. 11. Give one property that brass and bronze share. Any correct one, for example: both are alloys; both contain copper; both are resistant to corrosion; both have a range of melting points. 12. Give one property that copper and zinc share. Any correct one, for example: both are elements; both are metals; both conduct heat and electricity. 13. Give one difference in properties between copper and tin. Any correct one, for example: copper is reddish and tin is white; tin is softer; tin is not as good a conductor; copper melts at 1085 °C but tin melts at 232 °C. </details"],[4,"5-4-using-the-properties-of-materials-to-separate-mixtures","5.4 Using the properties of materials to separate mixtures","Key words: condenser · conical flask · filter funnel · filter paper"],[4,"the-big-idea","The big idea","Mixtures contain different substances that are not combined together chemically . Because the substances keep their own properties, you can use a difference between those properties to pull the mixture apart. Mixture Property used Method iron filings + sulfur iron is magnetic, sulfur is not a magnet copper sulfate + water water evaporates, copper sulfate does not evaporation food dye + water they have different boiling points distillation with a condenser sand + salty water sand does not dissolve, salt does filtration, then evaporation glass + copper sulfate crystals copper sulfate dissolves in water, glass does not dissolve, filter, then evaporate"],[4,"evaporation","Evaporation","An evaporating dish holds a mixture of water and copper sulfate. Left in a warm room, the water evaporates and leaves the copper sulfate behind in the dish."],[4,"distillation-with-a-condenser","Distillation with a condenser","A mixture of two liquids is separated with a condenser . 1. The mixture is heated and boils. The liquid water reaches the temperature where it changes state and becomes a gas. Water in the gas state, formed by boiling, is called steam . 2. The gas travels along the tube into the condenser. 3. Cold water circulating around the outside of the condenser cools the gas down, so it condenses back into liquid water. 4. The liquid water collects in the beaker. The food dye stays behind in the heated flask, because it has a much higher boiling point. Notice the direction of the labels: cold water in at the bottom, water out at the top, and pure water collecting at the far end. In particle language. Heating the water transfers energy to its particles, so they move faster. Eventually some have enough energy to overcome the weak forces holding them in place, and the liquid changes state to a gas. In the condenser the opposite happens: the steam particles transfer energy to the cold water in the outer tube, lose energy, can no longer move freely, and condense back to liquid."],[4,"filtration","Filtration","To separate sandy, salty water : 1. Fold a piece of filter paper and place it in a filter funnel . Stand the funnel in a conical flask . 2. Pour the mixture into the funnel slowly, so it does not run down the outside of the paper. Do not poke the wet filter paper — it tears easily. 3. The sand stays in the filter paper because the grains are too large to pass through the small holes . The clear salty water runs through into the conical flask. 4. Pour the clear liquid into an evaporating basin , stand it on a pipe clay triangle on a tripod, and heat gently with a Bunsen burner. 5. When the liquid starts to spit , remove it from the heat and leave it in a warm place to evaporate. The salt is left behind. Safety: wear safety glasses, because the hot solution may spit; turn the Bunsen burner off as soon as spitting starts; move the hot evaporating basin with tongs . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.4</b </summary 1. Explain how the water in the flask changes to a gas. The liquid water is heated, so its particles have more energy transferred to them and move more. Eventually some can overcome the weak forces holding them in place, and the water changes state to a gas, which escapes into the condenser. 2. Explain how the steam changes back into a liquid in the condenser. The steam moves along the central tube. Cold tap water flows through the outer tube and cools the condenser. Heat energy is transferred from the steam to that water; as the particles lose energy they can no longer move freely, and they condense into liquid water. 3. Which different properties of the water and the food dye are used to separate them? Their different boiling temperatures. 4. What safety precautions should you take? Wear safety glasses; take care while the solution is heating because it may spit; move the evaporating basin with tongs. 5. Why does the sand remain in the filter paper? Because the grains of sand are too large to pass through the small holes in the filter paper. 6. One group stirred the mixture in the filter paper with a pencil. Why is this not a good idea? The wet filter paper is easily torn. Stirring with a pencil is likely to damage it and let the contents flow straight through. 7. How could you obtain the water from your mixture? Heat the salty water in a condenser and collect the distilled water. 8. The salt left in the evaporating basin is a little dirty. How could you get cleaner salt? Wash the salt with clean water, filter using finer filter paper, then evaporate again — repeating several times if needed. </details <details <summary <b Worked problem — Marcus and the broken bottle</b </summary Marcus drops a glass bottle of copper sulfate crystals. He has swept the glass and crystals into a container. How can he separate them? - Equipment: dustpan and brush, thick rubber gloves, safety glasses, beaker, stirring rod, filter paper, filter funnel, conical flask, evaporating basin, pipe clay triangle, tripod, Bunsen burner, tongs. - Pick up: wearing gloves and safety glasses, pick out the larger pieces of glass by hand and sweep the rest up with the dustpan and brush. - Separate: add the mixture to a beaker of water and stir. The copper sulfate dissolves; the glass does not. Filter the mixture — the glass stays in the filter paper, the blue solution runs into the conical flask. Handle the glass in the paper carefully and dispose of it safely. - Recover the crystals: pour the blue solution into an evaporating basin and heat it. Water boils at 100 °C and changes to a gas; copper sulfate does not. Turn the burner down once it boils and off once it spits, then leave the basin for a few days so the rest of the water evaporates. - Stay safe: gloves for the glass, safety glasses for the heating, tongs for the hot basin. </details"],[4,"5-5-acids-and-alkalis","5.5 Acids and alkalis","Key words: acid · alkalis · corrosive · flammable · harmful · irritate · oxidising · toxic"],[4,"acids-are-everywhere","Acids are everywhere","Many foods contain acid, and those foods have a sour, sharp, tangy taste . Lemons and limes taste sour because they contain citric acid , a weak acid. The common acids in the laboratory are hydrochloric acid , sulfuric acid and nitric acid ."],[4,"some-acids-are-dangerous","Some acids are dangerous","Strong acids are corrosive . If a strong acid gets onto your skin it will dissolve the skin and you will get a chemical burn . Always use eye protection when using acids. Acids can be diluted with water, which makes them less dangerous. But dilute acids are still harmful — they can irritate your skin and eyes, and their bottles still carry hazard warning labels. If you spill acid, wash the area with lots of water. The water dilutes the acid."],[4,"alkalis-are-everywhere","Alkalis are everywhere","Many cleaning products contain alkalis such as sodium hydroxide , a compound of sodium, hydrogen and oxygen. Sodium hydroxide is a strong alkali, and strong alkalis are corrosive too. If a strong alkali gets on your skin it dissolves the skin, your skin feels soapy , and you get a chemical burn. Alkalis are harmful in the eyes, so always wear eye protection. The common alkalis in the laboratory are sodium hydroxide , potassium hydroxide and calcium hydroxide . Acids and alkalis are chemical opposites. They cancel each other out when they are mixed together. How acidic or alkaline a substance is, is a chemical property of that substance."],[4,"working-safely-with-acids-and-alkalis","Working safely with acids and alkalis","What you do Why stand up to work if you spill something it does not spill onto you wear safety glasses so nothing gets into your eyes put the bottle top upside down on the bench so the acid does not get onto the surface, and dirt does not get into the acid replace the top as soon as you have finished prevents spills and stops you putting the wrong top on the wrong bottle work in an orderly way you are less likely to have an accident or use the wrong chemical"],[4,"hazard-warning-symbols","Hazard warning symbols","Symbol What it warns you about Explosive a substance that can explode if it comes into contact with a flame or heat Flammable a substance that can catch fire easily Oxidising a substance that gives off a large amount of heat when in contact with other substances Corrosive a substance that can destroy living tissue; it can cause burns Toxic a substance that can poison you Hazardous to the environment a substance that can kill or damage living things in the environment Health hazard a substance that can cause harm such as irritating your skin and eyes Serious health hazard a substance that can cause a serious problem to your health <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.5</b </summary 1. Name a food that contains acid. Citrus fruits or tomatoes; drinks such as cola and coffee are also acidic. 2. Describe the taste of lemons and limes. They taste sour. 3. What does corrosive mean? It means the substance will dissolve your clothing and skin and cause chemical burns. 4. What should you do if you spill acid? Wash the area with lots of water. Sorting words (workbook): Acid Alkali citric acid, nitric acid, lemon juice, cola, vinegar, sour, sharp, corrosive, harmful sodium hydroxide, washing powder, washing soda, soap, corrosive, harmful Notice that corrosive and harmful belong in both columns — strong acids and strong alkalis are both corrosive. </details"],[4,"5-6-indicators-and-the-ph-scale","5.6 Indicators and the pH scale","Key words: indicator · litmus · neutral · pH scale · universal indicator"],[4,"which-is-which","Which is which?","Three containers can look identical — one holding water, one acid, one alkali. You tell them apart by adding a few drops of an indicator . An indicator turns one colour in an acid and a different colour in an alkali . Red cabbage juice works as an indicator: it is red in acids, blue in water and yellow in alkalis . Indicators can be made from brightly coloured berries, flowers and other plant parts, including red cabbage, blackcurrant and beetroot. Making your own indicator: cut up the plant material, crush it in a pestle and mortar, add a little ethanol with a pipette, crush again until the colour comes out, then transfer some liquid to a test tube with a clean pipette. (Ethanol is flammable — keep it away from flames.)"],[4,"litmus","Litmus","Litmus is a very common indicator. It is a dye, and you usually use litmus paper , made by soaking absorbent paper in litmus solution. Substance Litmus colour Type of substance hydrochloric acid red acid lemon juice red acid water purple neutral sodium hydroxide blue alkali calcium hydroxide blue alkali Litmus turns red in acids, blue in alkalis, and purple in a neutral substance. A neutral substance is one that is neither acid nor alkali. Litmus is purple in water, which tells you water is neutral."],[4,"universal-indicator-and-the-ph-scale","Universal indicator and the pH scale","Litmus and other simple indicators only tell you whether a substance is an acid or an alkali. Universal indicator tells you how acidic or alkaline it is. It is made from a mixture of different indicators , so it can change to many different colours. Type of substance Colour of universal indicator strongly acid red weakly acid yellow neutral green weakly alkaline blue strongly alkaline purple The strength of acids and alkalis is measured on the pH scale , which runs from 0 to 14 . pH Type Universal indicator 1 strongly acidic red 4 weakly acidic yellow / orange 7 neutral green 10 weakly alkaline blue 13 strongly alkaline purple Below 7 is more acidic ; above 7 is more alkaline ; exactly 7 is neutral ."],[4,"other-indicators","Other indicators","Different indicators change at different points on the pH scale, so two of them together can pin a pH down more tightly than either alone. Indicator pH 1 pH 4 pH 7 pH 10 pH 13 universal indicator red orange green blue purple phenolphthalein colourless colourless colourless pink pink methyl orange red yellow yellow yellow yellow If a liquid leaves phenolphthalein colourless and turns methyl orange red , it must be around pH 1 — methyl orange is only red at the very acidic end, and phenolphthalein rules out pH 10 and above. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.6</b </summary 1. How does an indicator show whether a substance is an acid or an alkali? The indicator turns one colour in an acid and a different colour in an alkali. 2. What is the colour change when red cabbage juice is added to lemon juice? It turns red. 3. What does litmus do when it is added to sodium hydroxide? It turns blue. 4. What colour does litmus change to in an acid? Red. 5. Is water an acid, an alkali or neutral? Give the reason. Water is neutral, because litmus in water is neither red nor blue — it is purple. 6. What does the pH scale measure? How acidic or alkaline a substance is. 7. What is the pH of a neutral solution? pH 7. 8. A liquid has a pH of 1. What type of liquid is it? A strong acid. 9. What range of pH do strong alkalis have? pH 12 to 14. 10. What colour does universal indicator turn in a liquid with a pH of 9? Blue. 11. Which colours does universal indicator turn in acids? Red, orange or yellow. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — finding the mistakes in Marcus's table (Exercise 5.6A)</b </summary Liquid Colour with universal indicator pH Marcus's conclusion Correction lemon juice yellow 4 weakly alkaline weakly acid soap solution blue/green 8 weakly alkaline correct water green 5 neutral pH 7 hydrochloric acid blue 2 strongly acid red sodium hydroxide blue/purple 11 strongly alkaline correct Why universal indicator is more useful than litmus: litmus only tells you whether something is an acid or an alkali, but universal indicator tells you how strong it is, by giving you the pH. </details"],[4,"check-your-progress","Check your progress","5.1 Complete the paragraph ( brittle, conduct, cut, ductile, electricity, malleable, metal, ring ) Metals are shiny when freshly cut or polished. They are strong and if you tap them they ring like a bell. Metals conduct heat energy and electricity . Metals are malleable , which means they can be beaten into shape. They are ductile , which means they can be drawn out into wires. 5.2 a Why aren't 'silver' coins made of pure silver? Because pure silver would be too expensive, and it would wear away quickly because it is so soft. 5.2 b Explain, using particle theory, why alloys are harder than the metals they are made from. In a pure metal the atoms line up in regular rows, side by side and one on top of another, so when the metal is hit the rows can move across one another. In an alloy, particles of a different size are mixed with the pure metal, so the particles in the rows are not all the same and the pattern is disrupted. When the alloy is hit the rows cannot slide across one another in the same way. 5.3 a Which property of metals is most useful when: - i copper is used for electrical wiring — it conducts electricity (and it is ductile). - ii gold is used for jewellery — it is shiny (and rare, so expensive). - iii iron is used to build bridges — it is strong. - iv stainless steel is used for cooking pans — it conducts heat well, it is strong, and it does not rust. 5.3 b State three differences between metals and non-metals. Any three, each naming the feature of both : - Metals are solid at room temperature, but many non-metals are gases. - Metals are shiny, but non-metals are dull. - Metals are malleable and do not shatter, but non-metals are brittle. - Metals are ductile, but non-metals are not. - Metals conduct heat energy well, but non-metals do not. - Metals conduct electricity well, but non-metals do not. 5.4 Marcus and the broken bottle of copper sulfate. Equipment: dustpan and brush, thick rubber gloves, safety glasses, filter paper, filter funnel, beaker, conical flask, evaporating basin, tripod, pipe clay triangle, Bunsen burner, tongs. Method: pick out the large pieces of glass and sweep up the rest; add the mixture to water and stir, so the copper sulfate dissolves and the glass does not; filter, leaving the glass in the filter paper; heat the blue solution in an evaporating basin, turning the burner down once it boils and off once it spits; leave it for a few days for the last of the water to evaporate; move the hot basin with tongs. 5.5 Litmus is red in acid, blue in alkali and purple in a neutral solution. - a What is the correct scientific term for a substance that changes colour in this way? An indicator . - b What colour is litmus in a liquid of pH 4? Red. - c What colour is litmus in water? Purple. 5.6 A truck is loading acid at a factory. - a Why is the orange warning notice important? So that everyone knows what is being delivered, which means they will know at once how to treat it if there is an accident. - b What could be done if some acid is spilt on the ground? Cover the area of the spill with lots of water to dilute the acid so that it causes less harm. 5.7 Melting and boiling points Substance Melting point / °C Boiling point / °C gold 1064 2850 lead 328 1750 copper 1082 2580 helium −270 −269 oxygen −219 −183 mercury −39 357 aluminium 660 2400 nickel 1455 2150 sulfur 119 445 sodium 98 900 - a Tally the melting points: Melting point / °C Tally Which ones up to 0 3 helium, oxygen, mercury 0 to 499 3 sodium, sulfur, lead 500 to 999 1 aluminium 1000 to 1499 3 gold, copper, nickel Boiling point / °C Tally Which ones up to 0 2 helium, oxygen 0 to 999 3 mercury, sulfur, sodium 1000 to 1999 1 lead 2000 to 2999 4 gold, copper, aluminium, nickel - b Plot the tallied figures on two frequency diagrams. Use a sharp pencil and a ruler, label both axes, and keep the bars the same width with equal gaps. - c Which are gases at a room temperature of 25 °C? Oxygen and helium — both boil far below 25 °C. - d Which are liquid at 25 °C? Mercury — it melts at −39 °C and boils at 357 °C. - e Which are solid at 25 °C? Gold, lead, copper, aluminium, nickel, sulfur and sodium. - f Which has the smallest difference between its melting and boiling points? Helium (−270 °C to −269 °C, a difference of just 1 °C). - g Which has the largest difference? Gold (1064 °C to 2850 °C, a difference of 1786 °C)."],[5,"6-1-sound-waves","6.1 Sound waves","Key words: backwards and forwards · loudness · medium · particles · pitch · sound wave · speed of sound · vacuum · vibrate"],[5,"where-does-sound-come-from","Where does sound come from?","Things that vibrate make sounds. To vibrate means to move backwards and forwards very quickly. - Hitting a drum makes the drum skin vibrate, and that vibration makes the sound. - Hit the drum with more force and it vibrates more, which makes a louder sound. - Touch the front of your neck while you speak and you can feel a vibration. It comes from your vocal cords . - Put small objects on the paper cone of a loudspeaker and they bounce around. That shows the cone is vibrating. Sounds vary in two ways: Feature What it means Example loudness how loud or quiet the sound is thunder is loud, whispering is quiet pitch how high or low the sound is a baby crying is high-pitched, thunder is low-pitched"],[5,"what-is-a-sound-wave","What is a sound wave?","Sound travels from a vibrating object to your ears. This is called a sound wave . When an object vibrates it moves backwards and forwards . Every time it moves forward, the air in front of it is pushed forward. The particles in the air are made to vibrate backwards and forwards in time with the object. Those particles then make the particles in front of them vibrate, and so on. That chain of vibrating particles is the sound wave. Two things to be clear about: - The particles vibrate backwards and forwards along the same line as the direction the wave travels. They do not move up and down, and they do not travel along with the wave. - The particles stay roughly where they are. It is the wave — the energy — that travels. The speed of sound in air is about 343 metres per second . Sound waves transfer sound energy . When the vibration reaches another object, that object starts to vibrate too: - Very loud sounds make the air particles vibrate so much that they can damage your ears . - Very quiet sounds make vibrations too small for your ears to detect. - A sound at a certain pitch can cause damaging vibrations even when it is not very loud — this is how a glass can be broken by a high-pitched sound."],[5,"sound-waves-on-the-move","Sound waves on the move","A sound wave travels by making particles vibrate, so it will travel through anything that has particles : a gas, a liquid or a solid. You can show this by tapping on a table. Listen normally — the sound reaches you through the air , which is a gas. Now put your ear on the table and listen again — the sound reaches you through the table , which is a solid. Sound also travels through liquids . Whales and dolphins communicate with sound under water. The substance a sound wave travels through is called the medium . Solids, liquids and gases can all be a medium for sound. Sound travels most easily through solids, because the particles in a solid are closer together , so vibrations pass from particle to particle more easily."],[5,"vibrations-in-a-vacuum","Vibrations in a vacuum","To hear a sound there must be: - a vibration to make the sound, and - a medium containing particles for the sound wave to travel through. A vacuum is a space where there are no particles . With no particles there is nothing to vibrate, so sound cannot travel in a vacuum . The bell jar demonstration. Put an electric bell inside a glass jar and switch it on: you can see it vibrate and you can hear it. Now pump the air out of the jar to make a vacuum. You can still see the hammer vibrating, but you can no longer hear it. Space is a vacuum, so: - Astronauts on the Moon cannot hear each other's hammering and digging through the outside air — there is none. They talk by radio. - Films that show a loud bang when a spaceship explodes are wrong. There would be no sound at all. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.1</b </summary 1. Which one of these is needed to make a sound from a guitar? B — the guitar strings vibrate. 2. How can Zara make the sound of the plucked string louder? A — pluck the string with more force. 3. Some flying insects make a buzzing sound. What causes this sound? The vibration of their wings. 4. Sound travels through air as a sound ……… wave. 5. Which arrow shows how the particles in air vibrate? C — the double-headed arrow along the direction the sound is travelling. 6. Thunder makes objects in a room vibrate. Explain what causes this. Vibration of the particles in the air, caused by the thunder, is passed on to the objects. 7. Why can people not hear a fly walking up a window? The vibrations it makes are very small, so the sound is too quiet to hear. 8. Which of these can sound travel through? A, B and C — solid, liquid and gas. Not a vacuum. 9. Why could people working on the Moon not hear their own hammering? There is a vacuum on the Moon. With no particles, a sound wave cannot travel. 10. Would you really hear an explosion in space? No. Space is a vacuum, and sound waves cannot travel in a vacuum. </details <details <summary <b Activity — modelling sound waves with a slinky spring</b </summary Stretch the spring along a bench, mark places on it with chalk, hold one end still and move the other end backwards and forwards. 1. In which direction does the wave move? Away from the moving hand. 2. Does the whole spring move in that direction? No — only the wave (the vibrations) travels. 3. In which direction do the marks move? Backwards and forwards along the line of the spring, the same line as the wave direction. 4. What did the person at the fixed end feel? Movement — the energy of the wave arriving. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 6.1 The movement of sound</b </summary 1. How does sound travel through air? The particles in the air vibrate. 2. True or false? True, true, false. 3. Why does sound not travel through a vacuum? There are no particles to vibrate. 4. How does sound travel through water? The particles in the water vibrate. 5. Explain why sound cannot travel in a vacuum. Sound waves need particles to vibrate, and there are no particles in a vacuum. 6. What makes a window vibrate when loud music is played? The vibration of the air, caused by the music. 8. Why does sound travel better through a solid than a gas? The particles in a solid are closer together (touching), and vibrations pass between particles more easily when the particles are closer together. 9. Why can very loud sounds damage hearing? Vibrations that are too large can damage parts of the ear. </details"],[5,"6-2-reflections-of-sound","6.2 Reflections of sound","Key words: echo · effect on the sound · property · reflected · unwanted"],[5,"reflections","Reflections","One property of all waves is that they can be reflected from surfaces, so sound waves can be reflected too. Reflection is like bouncing a ball off a wall: the wave hits the wall and comes back. The only difference is that a wave is not affected by gravity. Sound reflects best from large, smooth, flat, hard surfaces — glass, tiles, flat metal, smooth concrete. A reflection of a sound wave is called an echo . If you clap once in an empty room between two flat walls, the clap seems to last longer than usual and then fade away. That is the sound wave bouncing back and forth off the walls."],[5,"useful-echoes","Useful echoes","Who uses it How it works bats the bat makes a sound; the sound reflects off an insect; from the time the echo takes to come back and the direction it comes from, the bat works out where the insect is boats and ships a sound is sent down from the bottom of the boat; it reflects off the solid ground; the time for the echo to return is used to work out the depth of the water medical scans sound sent into a mother's body echoes back out, and the echoes are used to make an image of the unborn baby Watch the distance. The sound travels to the reflecting surface and back again , so the distance travelled by the sound is double the distance from the object to the surface. To find the depth of the water you work out the total distance the sound travelled and then halve it."],[5,"unwanted-echoes","Unwanted echoes","Sometimes echoes are a nuisance: - When music is being recorded, a note played once repeats with an echo and spoils the recording. Recording studios have shaped, soft walls instead of large flat ones. - In a theatre the audience needs to hear the voices on stage clearly. Echoes would blur the voices, so theatres are designed with no large flat surfaces and with curved and soft surfaces that absorb sound. Soft, thick, rough materials — curtains, carpet, wool — absorb sound and give poor reflections. Hard, smooth, flat materials — glass, metal, tile, wood — give good reflections. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.2</b </summary 1. What happens to a sound to make an echo? C — the sound gets reflected. 2. Which material gives the best echo? B — a glass window (hard, smooth and flat). 3. Why did the music sound better in theatre B? There are fewer echoes in B, because B has curved surfaces rather than large flat ones. 4. What could Zara put on the walls to stop echoes? C — soft thick curtains. 5. Extension: a sound travels for 0.2 s at 1500 m/s before the echo returns. How far away are the fish? Total distance = 1500 × 0.2 = 300 m. The sound went there and back, so the distance to the fish = 300 ÷ 2 = 150 m . </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 6.2 Reflections of sound</b </summary 1. What is the name for a reflected sound? An echo. 2. What gives the best reflection? A flat wall. 3. Why does he hear his own shout again in the tunnel? The sound wave is reflected off the walls, floor and ceiling of the tunnel, so he hears the echo. 4. Which place gives the louder echo, and why? The tunnel, because the walls, floor and ceiling all reflect the sound. 5. Bar chart of echo loudness. Letters A–D on the horizontal axis; loudness 0–65 dB on the vertical axis; bars of equal width, correct height, not touching. Loudest echo: B. Best sound absorber: C. 6. Time for the echo. Time = 85 ÷ (340 × 2) = 0.5 seconds . 7. How does a submarine use an echo to find the distance to a rock? The submarine emits a sound; the sound wave travels through the water and reflects off the rock; the wave arrives back at the submarine; the time between sending and receiving is used to work out the distance. </details"],[5,"6-3-structure-of-the-earth","6.3 Structure of the Earth","Key words: continental · core · crust · drift · magma · mantle · molten · tectonic plates"],[5,"what-is-inside-the-earth","What is inside the Earth?","The Earth is about 4500 million years old . Scientists have worked out that it is made of layers. Layer What it is crust the thin, solid outer layer of rock mantle molten (hot liquid) rock below the crust, which can flow outer core molten metals — mostly iron and nickel inner core solid metals — mostly iron and nickel — right at the centre The rocks in the crust contain both metals and non-metals. - The most common non-metal in the crust is oxygen . - The most common metal in the crust is aluminium ."],[5,"continental-drift","Continental drift","People used to think the Earth was only a few thousand years old and had never changed. In 1912 a German scientist called Alfred Wegener suggested that millions of years ago all the land was one large continent . Over millions of years it broke up and drifted apart. This idea is called continental drift . Wegener's evidence: - the shapes of the continents fit together - the types of rock on different continents match up where they fit together - the fossils on different continents match up where they fit together. Why people did not believe him: Wegener could not explain how continental drift happened. He had evidence for the idea but no mechanism, so not everyone accepted it."],[5,"tectonic-plates","Tectonic plates","We now know that the Earth's crust is made up of large tectonic plates . - Plates under the oceans are oceanic plates . - Plates that form the continents are continental plates . The plates move slowly on the liquid rock, called magma , beneath them. That is what causes continental drift — the mechanism Wegener was missing. The plates move only about 4 cm each year , which is about the speed your fingernails grow. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.3</b </summary 1. Name the part of the Earth that forms the centre. The core. 2. Name the metals found in this part. Iron and nickel. 3. Name the most common non-metal in the Earth's crust. Oxygen. 4. Name the most common metal in the Earth's crust. Aluminium. 5. What evidence did Wegener have? The shapes of the continents fit together; the types of rock on different continents match up where they fit together; the fossils on different continents match up where they fit together. 6. Why did some people reject his idea? Because he could not explain how continental drift occurred. 7. Which tectonic plate do you live on? Read it off the plate map. 8. What causes the tectonic plates to move? They move on the liquid magma beneath them. </details <details <summary <b Activity 6.3.1 — drifting plates</b </summary Pieces of polystyrene are floated on water in a heat-proof dish, and the water is heated gently. 1. What does each part represent? a the polystyrene = the tectonic plates; b the heat source = heat from the Earth's core; c the water = the magma, or the mantle. 2. Strengths and weaknesses. Strengths: the movement of the polystyrene is caused by heat, as in the real Earth, and the movement can be seen in a short time. Weaknesses: real tectonic plates are not small and light; they do not float on water; and they move very much more slowly. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 6.3 Continental drift</b </summary 1. Label the layers. crust · mantle · outer core · inner core. 2. Which layer is described? a the core; b the crust; c the mantle; d the inner core. 3. What is continental drift? The slow movement of the continents. 4. What was Wegener's evidence? The shapes of the continents fit together; the rock types match up; the fossils match up. 5. What explains how continental drift happens? The theory of tectonic plates. 6. Describe tectonic plates. The Earth's crust is made of parts called tectonic plates; they are made of solid rock and move on the liquid magma beneath them. 7. Comparing two websites. Website 1 is relevant and uses accepted science supported by evidence. Website 2 claims the Earth is getting bigger and getting cooler, and neither claim is supported by evidence; the claim that all plates are moving apart is contradicted by fold mountains and earthquakes, which happen where plates move towards each other or slide past each other. Website 2 may be biased and may use facts that are not relevant. </details"],[5,"6-4-changes-in-the-earth","6.4 Changes in the Earth","Key words: active · dormant · earthquake · extinct · fold mountains · geological change · inactive · lava · magnitude · plate boundary · subduction · volcano"],[5,"geological-change","Geological change","The places where tectonic plates meet are called plate boundaries . Geological change happens at plate boundaries, because the plates are always moving. - Some geological change is very slow — mountains built over millions of years. - Some is very sudden and violent — earthquakes and eruptions. The plate boundaries around the edge of the Pacific Ocean have so many volcanoes and earthquakes that the area is called the Pacific Ring of Fire ."],[5,"the-three-types-of-plate-boundary","The three types of plate boundary","Plates are… What happens What it makes moving together one plate slides underneath the other — this is called subduction . The rocks of that plate melt as they move down into the mantle and become part of the mantle deep trenches; fold mountains moving apart the plates become too thin, so they break and crack. Lava (liquid rock) erupts from the mantle and hardens into new crust a volcano sliding past the plates are large and heavy, so there is a lot of friction and they stick together. Force keeps building until the pressure causes a sudden violent movement an earthquake"],[5,"fold-mountains","Fold mountains","When tectonic plates move together , the rocks crumple and fold upwards . The mountains this produces are called fold mountains . It can happen under the ocean or on land. Age Examples newest — 10 to 25 million years old the Himalayas in Asia, the Rocky Mountains in North America oldest — more than 200 million years old the Ural Mountains in Russia"],[5,"volcanoes","Volcanoes","Volcanoes are usually formed at plate boundaries , when magma from the mantle rises up through cracks in the Earth's crust. Magma and lava are the same rock with different names: - magma = liquid rock when it is underground - lava = liquid rock when it is on the surface . At the surface the magma erupts to form lava flows and ash deposits . The lava and ash harden as they cool to form new rock, so each time the volcano erupts it gets bigger . If the magma is very thick and contains dissolved gas, the pressure builds up and the eruption is violent . Gases and rock shoot up through the opening. Violent eruptions can cause avalanches and earthquakes — and a tsunami if the volcano is close to the sea. Word What it means active the volcano may erupt at any time inactive or dormant it has not erupted for a very long time extinct it will not erupt again"],[5,"earthquakes","Earthquakes","Some earthquakes are extremely violent and cause a lot of damage. Others are so slight that they only show up on scientific instruments. The size, or magnitude , of an earthquake depends on: - the size of the faults at the plate boundary, and - how far the rocks move when the earthquake happens. In the largest earthquakes the rocks can move tens of metres in seconds . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.4</b </summary 1. Which statement is true about tectonic plates? B — they move in different ways. 2. Explain how tectonic plates cause fold mountains to form. The plates push against each other, and the rocks are pushed upward at the boundary. 3. Which word describes the strength of an earthquake? C — magnitude. 4. Explain what causes an earthquake. There are forces on the tectonic plates; force or pressure builds up while the plates are stuck together; then there is a sudden movement. </details <details <summary <b Activity — models for moving tectonic plates</b </summary Model What happened What it models A cloth pushed together the cloth wrinkled and was pushed up into folds formation of fold mountains B two sheets of paper pushed together one sheet slid over or under the other; edge-to-edge they bent into a curve and lifted off the surface subduction, then formation of fold mountains C two flat pieces of clay pushed together the clay was compressed and folded upward formation of fold mountains D two soft-centred chocolate bars pushed together the outer chocolate cracked, the soft centre came out, and the cracked parts were pushed upward earthquake (the cracking), volcanoes (the soft centre escaping) and fold mountains Strengths and limitations. A: the cloth folds easily, but one piece of cloth has to stand for two plates. B: the two sheets really are two separate plates, but paper is too stiff to fold like cloth. C: the clay is nicely flexible. D: it shows both a solid crust (the chocolate) and liquid magma (the soft centre). Model D is the best , because it models all three — fold mountains, earthquakes and volcanoes. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 6.4 How the Earth changes</b </summary 1. What is the place where two plates meet called? A plate boundary. 2. What is the sudden violent movement of plates called? An earthquake. 4. Magnitude scale. a 10 b 1000. 5. a Where do most earthquakes happen? At plate boundaries. b What else can a large earthquake in the ocean cause? A tsunami — a large-magnitude earthquake under the ocean sends shocks to the nearest land. </details"],[5,"6-5-solar-and-lunar-eclipses","6.5 Solar and lunar eclipses","Key words: lunar eclipse · opaque · partial · ray · shadow · solar eclipse · total"],[5,"shadows","Shadows","Light travels in straight lines called rays . An opaque object is one that will not let light pass through it. When an opaque object passes in front of a source of light, a shadow forms. The shadow is dark because the light has been blocked from reaching that area; the ground around it is brighter because light is still reaching those parts. If you stood on the grass and the shadow of an aeroplane passed over you, it would seem to go dark, then get brighter again once the shadow had gone."],[5,"solar-eclipse","Solar eclipse","A solar eclipse happens when the Moon comes between the Sun and the Earth . The Moon is made of rock, so it is opaque . It blocks the rays of light coming from the Sun, and the Moon's shadow falls on the Earth. Where you are What you see Why in the middle of the shadow a total solar eclipse all the light rays from the Sun are blocked away from the middle of the shadow a partial solar eclipse some of the light rays from the Sun can still reach you Safety. Never look directly at the Sun, even during an eclipse. The light is very bright and can cause permanent damage to your eyes. A solar eclipse can only be seen in the daytime , because it happens when the Moon passes in front of the Sun, and the Sun can only be seen in the daytime."],[5,"lunar-eclipse","Lunar eclipse","A lunar eclipse happens when the Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon . The Earth is also opaque, so the Earth blocks the light from the Sun, and the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon. A lunar eclipse can only be seen at night . To see the Earth's shadow crossing the Moon you must be on the side of the Earth facing away from the Sun — and that side is in night."],[5,"why-eclipses-are-not-monthly","Why eclipses are not monthly","You might expect an eclipse every month, because the Moon takes 28 days to orbit the Earth. But the Moon's orbit is tilted slightly , so it is not in exactly the same plane as the Earth's orbit around the Sun. An eclipse can only happen when the Sun, Earth and Moon are in the same straight line . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.5</b </summary 1. Which describes how a solar eclipse happens? C — the Moon comes between the Earth and the Sun. 2. Which describes how a lunar eclipse happens? B — the Earth comes between the Moon and the Sun. 3. Why can a solar eclipse only be seen in the daytime? A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes in front of the Sun, and the Sun can only be seen in the daytime. 4. True or false: a total lunar eclipse can only be seen in the daytime. False. To see the Earth's shadow pass across the Moon you must be at the point on Earth furthest from the Sun, and it is night time there. </details <details <summary <b Activity 6.5.1 — classroom eclipses</b </summary A lamp is the Sun, a white soccer ball is the Earth, and a tennis ball on a string is the Moon. 1. Which side of the Earth is in day and which in night? The side facing the lamp is in day; the side facing away is in night. 2. Moon between lamp and soccer ball — which eclipse? A solar eclipse. 3. Where is it total and where partial? Total where the shadow on the Earth is darkest; partial where the shadow is lightest. 4. Moon moved into the shade of the soccer ball — which eclipse? A lunar eclipse. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — predicting eclipses</b </summary 1. What has to be given in an eclipse prediction? The date, the time, and the place from which it can be seen. 2. How many days in 223 lunar months? 223 × 29.5 = 6578.5 days . 3. What must happen to the Sun, Moon and Earth every 223 lunar months? They must be in line with each other. 4. How can a pattern of observations be used to predict? Working out the time between events in the past lets you predict the times of events in the future. 5. How can the accuracy of a prediction be tested? Observe whether the eclipse actually happens when it was predicted. 6. Why do predictions get less accurate further ahead? The times are not exact, so small differences build up the further into the future you go. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 6.5A How eclipses happen</b </summary 1. Which of these give out their own light? The Sun does. The Moon does not. The Earth does not. 2. What is the dark area formed behind an opaque object called? A shadow. 3. Complete the sentences. a A solar eclipse happens when the Moon comes between the Sun and the Earth . b A lunar eclipse happens when the Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon . </details"],[5,"check-your-progress","Check your progress","<details <summary <b Answers</b </summary 6.1 Vibration. 6.2 A sound wave travels from the pen to Sofia's ears . The sound wave travels through the air . 6.3 Draw a straight arrow in any direction for the sound wave. Then draw a double-headed arrow (or two opposing arrows) parallel to that direction to show how the particles move. 6.4 Modelling. 6.5 a W–B, X–D, Y–C, Z–A. b Tectonic plates are separate parts of the Earth's crust that move on the mantle. 6.6 Earthquakes. 6.7 Tectonic plates move together in opposite directions, and the rocks get pushed upward at the boundary of the plates. 6.8 A solar eclipse happens when the shadow of the Moon forms on the Earth. 6.9 a For a lunar eclipse , draw the Moon on the far side of the Earth from the Sun, in line with the Sun and the Earth: Sun — Earth — Moon. b For a solar eclipse , draw the Moon between the Sun and the Earth, in line with both: Sun — Moon — Earth. </details"],[6,"7-1-microorganisms","7.1 Microorganisms","Key words: agar jelly · algae · bacteria · colony · fungi · microorganism · mushroom · Petri dish · protozoa · single-celled · sterile · toadstool · yeast"],[6,"what-is-a-microorganism","What is a microorganism?","A microorganism is a living organism that is so small that you can only see it clearly by using a microscope . Like all living organisms, microorganisms are made of cells . Many microorganisms are made of only one cell — they are single-celled . There are several different kinds of microorganism: Kind What it is like bacteria single-celled; much smaller than animal or plant cells microscopic fungi some are single cells (yeast), some are tangles of very thin threads protozoa animal-like microorganisms, common in pond water algae plant-like microorganisms, common in pond water"],[6,"bacteria","Bacteria","Bacteria are everywhere. ( Bacteria is a plural word. The singular word, for just one of them, is bacterium .) Each bacterium is made of a single cell . Cells of bacteria are much smaller than animal cells or plant cells. You could fit 1000 bacteria, lined up end to end, between two of the millimetre marks on your ruler. Most bacteria are harmless , but there are a few kinds that can make you ill."],[6,"fungi","Fungi","Fungi (singular: fungus ) are not always microorganisms. Many fungi, including mushrooms and toadstools , are large and easy to see. But be careful with what a mushroom actually is: - A mushroom or toadstool is only part of the fungus's body, and it only grows at certain times of year. - Most of the time the fungus is just a tangle of very thin threads, often growing under the ground or inside a dead log. Those threads are so thin that they are difficult to see without a microscope. - Some kinds of fungi never produce mushrooms or toadstools at all. They are made of single cells, not threads, so they are definitely microorganisms. Yeast is one of these. The powdery white substance you sometimes see on the surface of grapes is made up of millions of yeast cells. Yeast is a microscopic, single-celled fungus , and it feeds on the sugar in the grapes. Yeast reproduces by growing little buds out of its cells. You can see the buds if you look closely through a microscope."],[6,"growing-microorganisms","Growing microorganisms","A single microorganism is too small to see without a microscope. But when it is left to grow, a single cell of a bacterium or a fungus divides repeatedly to make a collection of many cells. This collection of cells is called a colony , and a colony is big enough to see without a microscope. Scientists grow microorganisms safely in the laboratory in a Petri dish containing a special kind of jelly called agar jelly . The dish and the jelly have to be sterile . Sterile means that any living organisms on them have been killed . If the dish were not sterile, you would not know whether the colonies that grew came from the air or were already there."],[6,"microscopic-algae-and-protozoa","Microscopic algae and protozoa","If you look at some pond water through a microscope, you will see many tiny living organisms in the water: - Some are tiny plant-like organisms, called algae (singular: alga ). Their cells have cell walls and green colouring inside them. - Some are animal-like organisms, called protozoa (singular: protozoan ). Their cells have no cell wall and no green colouring. Not all of them are single-celled. Some have cells arranged end to end in long threads , and some are arranged in a sphere . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.1</b </summary 1. Viruses are even smaller than bacteria. Suggest why they are not usually said to be microorganisms. Viruses are not generally considered to be living organisms. They show none of the characteristics of living organisms apart from reproduction, and they can only do that inside a cell. 2. We can see yeast on the surface of fruit. Why is yeast classed as a microorganism? What we see on the fruit is a very large number of individual cells — a colony. Each individual cell is a complete organism, and a single cell can only be seen with a microscope. 3. Some of the microorganisms in the photograph are not single-celled. How are their cells arranged? The cells are arranged end to end in long threads. Some are also arranged in a sphere. 4. Drawing microorganisms with plant-like and animal-like cells. a Draw one or two of the bluish-white cells (animal-like). b Draw one of the cells or threads with green colouring (plant-like). c The labels should show that the plant-like cells have cell walls and green colouring inside them (chlorophyll — the chloroplasts themselves are not visible). Animal cells do not have these features. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — growing microorganisms from the air</b </summary You leave the lid off a sterile Petri dish of sterile agar jelly, then tape it shut and leave it to grow. 1. What are nutrients? Nutrients are materials that organisms need for growth and to provide energy. 2. Why must the dish and jelly be sterile? The purpose of the investigation is to grow microorganisms that have come from the air . You do not want to grow any microorganisms that were on the dish before you took the lid off. 3. Drawings of what grew. These will depend on what has grown on your agar jelly. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 7.1 Microorganisms experiment</b </summary 1. Reading the results. Read the number of colonies from each dish in the diagram. 2. Which variables were kept the same? The length of time he leaves the lid off, and the type of jelly in the dish. 3. Conclusion about the two classrooms. There are more microorganisms in the air in Classroom 203 than in Classroom 204. 4. Conclusion about temperature. Bacteria grow faster at a temperature of 30 °C than at 10 °C. 5. Plan an experiment on temperature and bacterial growth. Set up at least three Petri dishes with agar jelly exactly as before. Put all three in the same place and take off the lids for exactly the same time. Then put one in a cold place (a fridge), one in the laboratory at room temperature and one in a warmer place (an incubator), measuring each temperature with a thermometer. Leave the dishes for a set time and measure the sizes of the colonies in each dish — or measure them each day for several days. Keep the other variables the same, for example how long the agar is exposed. If the hypothesis is correct, the colonies in the warm dishes will be larger than those in the cold dishes. </details"],[6,"7-2-food-chains-and-webs","7.2 Food chains and webs","Key words: carnivore · consumer · ecology · food chain · food web · herbivore · predator · prey · producer"],[6,"microorganisms-in-the-environment","Microorganisms in the environment","The study of organisms in their environment is called ecology . All the different organisms that live together affect one another in some way — one species of animal may eat another, or a plant may provide shelter for an animal. Microorganisms have important roles to play in the environment. In this topic you will look at how food chains and food webs describe how energy, in the form of food, is transferred between plants and animals. In the next topics you will see how microorganisms fit into them."],[6,"food-chains","Food chains","The energy in food begins in the Sun . 1. Energy from the Sun reaches the Earth in sunlight . 2. Plants use the energy in sunlight to make their own food. Some of that energy goes into the food the plant stores in its roots, stems, fruits and leaves. 3. When an animal eats part of the plant, it eats the food the plant made, and that is how the animal gets its energy. Passing energy on like this is called energy transfer , and you show it by drawing a food chain . The arrows in a food chain show the direction in which the energy is passed on. Every organism in a food chain has a name for its role: Term What it means producer the first organism in the chain — a plant, which uses energy from the Sun to produce food consumer every other organism in the chain; animals are always consumers, because they must eat ready-made food herbivore a consumer that eats only plants carnivore a consumer that eats other animals predator an animal that catches, kills and eats other animals prey the animals that a predator eats"],[6,"food-webs","Food webs","Here are two food chains from the African plains: - acacia tree → springbok → cheetah - grass → termite → aardvark → leopard In real life the same organisms appear in several food chains at once. A diagram that joins them all up is a food web . To trace a food chain inside a food web, put your finger on a producer and follow the arrows: acacia tree → springbok → cheetah. Then try another, starting at the grass and ending at the leopard. In this food web: - The producers are the acacia tree and the grass . - There are 7 consumers . - There are 3 herbivores (springbok, zebra and termite). - The carnivores are the cheetah, leopard, hyena and aardvark. - Predator–prey pairs include cheetah and springbok, leopard and hyena, aardvark and termites, hyena and springbok. A food chain or food web is a model . It is useful because it shows the feeding relationships in a simple, visual way. But it is not perfect: many more organisms would really be part of the web, and the diagram does not tell you how important each relationship is — for example, whether leopards eat mostly hyenas or mostly zebras. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.2</b </summary 1. The chicken Arun ate had eaten wheat. Draw the food chain. Sunlight → wheat → chicken → Arun, with an arrow at each step. 2. Draw a food chain for something you ate. This depends on your choice of food, but it must begin with the Sun and a plant. 3. Write down two more food chains from the food web. Accept any food chain that can be traced along the arrows of the food web. 4. Name the two producers. Acacia tree and grass. 5. How many consumers are there? 7. 6. How many herbivores are there? 3. 7. Name two carnivores. Any two from cheetah, leopard, hyena and aardvark. 8. Name two predators and their prey. Any two from: cheetah (predator) and springbok, zebra or aardvark (prey); leopard (predator) and hyena, springbok, zebra or aardvark (prey); aardvark (predator) and termites (prey); hyena (predator) and springbok (prey). </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — using a food web as a model</b </summary How well does the African food web show what really happens? A food web is useful because it shows the feeding relationships between organisms in a simple and visual way, and you can see how energy passes from one organism to another. It is far from a complete picture, though: in reality many more organisms are part of the web, and the model does not tell you how important each feeding relationship is. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercises 7.2A, 7.2B and 7.2C</b </summary Exercise 7.2A Arctic hares. The web runs from the producers — Arctic willow, grasses and purple saxifrage — to the Arctic hare, and on to the Arctic wolf, Arctic fox, snowy owl and gyrfalcon. Exercise 7.2B Building up a food web. Producers: sea grass and phytoplankton. Herbivores: any two of sea urchins, pen shells and queen conch. Top consumers: any two of helmet snails, stingrays and humans. Exercise 7.2C Constructing a food web. Producers (green circles): the acacia trees and the grass. Herbivores (blue circles): giraffes, impala, grass rats and locusts. Carnivores (red circles): wild dogs, cheetahs, lions, birds and eagles. </details"],[6,"7-3-microorganisms-and-decay","7.3 Microorganisms and decay","Key words: decay · decomposer · mould · organic matter · rot"],[6,"decomposers-and-decay","Decomposers and decay","The food chains and food webs in the last topic did not include microorganisms. But microorganisms are everywhere — in the air, in the soil, in water, on our skin and inside our bodies. Look at a mouldy apple. Each spot on the apple is a colony made up of millions of cells of microscopic fungi. This kind of fungus is often called mould , and the apple is said to be mouldy . The microorganisms have changed the apple — they have made it decay . Organisms that make things decay are called decomposers . Many different kinds of microorganism are decomposers, including some kinds of bacteria and some microscopic fungi . Apples come from plants, which are living organisms. Any substance that has been made by a living organism — by a plant or an animal — is called organic matter . So an apple is organic matter. Some microorganisms can break down organic matter when they feed on it. That is what has happened to the apple: the microscopic fungi have broken down the crisp, fresh apple and made it brown and soft. They have made it rot . Things made of organic matter — bread, leather, wood, fruit — can be broken down by microorganisms. Things that are not organic matter — water, rock, glass, metal, plastic — cannot . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.3</b </summary 1. Some microorganisms are decomposers. Explain what this means. Decomposers break down organic material, making it decay. 2. Describe one way in which decay by microorganisms is not useful. For example, they make food go bad. 3. Suggest one way in which decay by microorganisms is useful. For example, they change food from one form into another, such as milk into yoghurt; or they get rid of dead bodies and waste material. </details <details <summary <b Activity 7.3.1 — what can microorganisms decay?</b </summary 1. Which of these are made of organic matter? bread · water · leather · rock · wood · fruit Bread, leather, wood and fruit. 2. Two more of each. Any two more things made of organic matter, and any two things that are not. 3. Which can be broken down by microorganisms? Bread, leather, wood, fruit, and the two organic items you named. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — temperature, moisture and decay</b </summary Temperature. Two similar pieces of moist bread are kept in different places and watched each day. 1. Results. The bread goes mouldy more quickly in the warm place than in the fridge. If you collect quantitative results you can make a quantitative comparison. 2. Differences between groups. These could come from using different bread, covering it differently, making the bread wetter, or how many microorganisms fell onto the bread while it was exposed to the air. 3. Conclusion. Higher temperatures speed up decay by microorganisms. Moisture. Plan an experiment to test the hypothesis moist bread decays more quickly than dry bread . The plan should say that the amount of water will be varied and how; how decay will be measured , for example by estimating the area of bread covered by mould; which variables are kept the same (temperature, type of bread, time exposed to the air, type of covering); and a prediction — that decay will be faster on moist bread. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercises 7.3A, 7.3B and 7.3C</b </summary Exercise 7.3A Decomposers in a compost heap. 1 Circle the glass, metal and plastic. 2 These things are not organic and cannot be broken down by microorganisms. 3 To show that microorganisms are present, mix the material with water, spread a little of the mixture onto sterile agar jelly in a Petri dish and leave it for some days; microorganisms will form colonies on the agar. A second dish with only water added is a useful control. Exercise 7.3B Investigating leaf decay. 1 The greatest loss is the October result for the leaves in the 1 cm mesh bag. 2 The bag with the 1 cm mesh. 3 Microorganisms, earthworms and other small animals. 4 Parts of the leaves disappeared because they decayed; decay was greatest when earthworms and other small animals, as well as microorganisms, could reach the leaves. Exercise 7.3C Analysing data about mould on bread. 1 Place a mesh, or transparent paper marked in squares of known size, over the mould and count the squares it covers. 2 Circle the value 5 for bread sample 2 in dish 4 (the anomalous result). 3 Mean = 16 mm² (strictly 16.0 mm², to match the other means). 4 Mean = 11.7 mm² . 6 Variables the girls could not control include differences in moisture, the number of mould spores landing on each piece, small differences in nutrients in the bread, and the difficulty of estimating an irregular area. 7 A good conclusion: mould grows best on bread at about 40 °C ; growth increases as temperature increases up to 40 °C, and falls again above it. </details"],[6,"7-4-microorganisms-in-food-webs","7.4 Microorganisms in food webs","Key words: dung · nutrients"],[6,"roles-of-decomposers","Roles of decomposers","No-one would want to eat a rotten apple — there, the microorganisms have spoiled the food. But most of the time decay is useful . Microorganisms break down dead bodies and animal waste; they decompose this material. Almost all decomposers are microorganisms, and fungi and bacteria are the most important decomposers . Two reasons decomposers matter: - If dead bodies and waste were not broken down they would simply build up — there would be heaps of dead plants, dead animals and animal dung everywhere. - More importantly, dead bodies and waste contain substances that living organisms need for energy and growth. These substances are called nutrients . When microorganisms decay organic matter they return the nutrients to the soil , where plants can use them to grow. That helps animals too, because there are then more plants to eat."],[6,"decomposers-in-food-webs","Decomposers in food webs","Decomposers feed on almost every organism after it dies, and on waste from animals. That is how decay microorganisms get their energy : energy from the dead organisms and their waste is transferred to the decomposers . You can show this by adding decomposers to a food chain or food web. It is not usually done, because you have to draw an arrow from every organism to the decomposers, which makes the diagram look very complicated. Decomposers are consumers , not producers — they do not make their own food."],[6,"ddt-in-food-webs","DDT in food webs","DDT is a chemical used to kill the mosquitoes that transmit malaria . In many countries where malaria is present, DDT is sprayed inside houses; it sticks to the walls, keeps working for up to six months, and needs much less DDT than spraying outdoors. DDT is persistent — it lasts for a long time. If DDT gets into a lake or river, more than half of it will still be there 150 years later. That is why DDT turns up in animals far from where it was used, such as albatrosses on Midway Atoll. DDT in birds of prey makes the shells of their eggs very thin , so the eggs break before the young birds hatch. In the USA, numbers of bald eagles fell while DDT was used. DDT was banned in 1972 , and the number of young raised then rose again. Malaria is still a very large problem: in 2015 there were 212 million cases and 429 000 deaths; in 2016 there were 216 million cases and 445 000 deaths. More than two-thirds of those who die from malaria are children under the age of 5. DDT has been completely banned in 34 countries, but many countries — including India and many African countries — still use it. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.4</b </summary 1. Write a food chain of your own and add decomposers to it. The food chain should begin with a producer, with arrows pointing from the producer to a consumer and then to another consumer. There should be an arrow from each of these organisms to the decomposers. 2. Are decomposers producers or consumers? Explain. Decomposers are consumers . They do not make their own food. </details <details <summary <b Activity 7.4.3 — are all decomposers microorganisms?</b </summary Look at the photograph of the fungus growing on a dead log. 1. Is this fungus a decomposer? Yes. It is breaking down the wood in the log as it feeds on it. 2. Is this fungus a microorganism? It is not a microorganism when it is producing toadstools. At other times, when there are no toadstools, the fungus is a microorganism — microscopic threads growing through the wood. 3. A slug eats the fungus. Where did the slug's energy originally come from? From sunlight . The tree used energy in sunlight to make food; some of that energy passed to the fungus as it fed on the wood, and then to the slug as it fed on the fungus. 4. Draw that food chain. tree → fungus → slug. 5. What other microorganisms act as decomposers? Bacteria. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 7.4 Microorganisms in food webs</b </summary 1. Name the terms. a Microorganism. b Food web. c Decomposer. 2. Complete the food web. Grass is the producer; grasshoppers and caterpillars feed on it; lizards and small birds feed on them; hawks are at the top. An arrow runs from every organism to the decomposers . 3. Why are decomposers important in this food web? They break down waste material from living organisms, and also their dead bodies. This returns nutrients to the soil, which plants can use to grow. That then supplies food for all the other organisms in the food web. </details"],[6,"check-your-progress","Check your progress","<details <summary <b Answers</b </summary 7.1 B and C — yeast is a decomposer and yeast is a microorganism. (It is not a producer, because it does not make its own food, and it is not a virus.) 7.2 Draw a food chain that begins with a producer and runs through two consumers, then add decomposers with an arrow from every organism in the chain to them. The arrows must point in the direction the energy travels. 7.3 a A suitable table has one column headed Next to cow dung (11, 13, 9, 12, 8) and one headed No cow dung (9, 10, 6, 7, 9), with the heading Length of grass leaf in cm . b Next to cow dung: mean = 10.6 cm . No cow dung: mean = 8.2 cm . Add a mean row or column to the table. c The conclusion does match the data, but only five grass leaves were measured in each area. The sample size is not big enough to be representative. The farmer should measure many more grass leaves from many more areas with and without cow dung. d The fungi break down (decompose) the cow dung. This releases nutrients into the soil, which the grass uses to grow faster and taller. 7.4 a Washing the container with boiling water kills any microorganisms already present , which might otherwise make the milk go bad rather than turn it into yoghurt. b The yoghurt she bought contains the kind of microorganisms ( bacteria ) that change milk into yoghurt. c She could put the milk in a warm place . Microorganisms grow faster at warmer temperatures, so they would change the milk into yoghurt faster. d The pH becomes lower . The microorganisms change sugars in the milk to lactic acid , and acids have a pH below 7. </details"],[7,"8-1-simple-chemical-reactions","8.1 Simple chemical reactions","Key words: chemical reaction · combine · product · react · reactant"],[7,"physical-properties-and-chemical-properties","Physical properties and chemical properties","The physical properties of a substance are features such as: - what colour it is - whether it is a solid, a liquid or a gas - what its boiling or melting temperature is - whether it is heavy or light. For example, some of the physical properties of iron are that it is a grey, heavy solid with a melting point of 1538 °C. One of the physical properties of hydrochloric acid is that it is a colourless liquid. The chemical properties of a substance are features such as: - how acidic or alkaline it is - how it reacts with water, acids or metals - how readily it reacts. Some of the chemical properties of iron are that it combines with sulfur when heated to form iron sulfide, and that it combines with oxygen to form iron oxide (rust). One of the chemical properties of hydrochloric acid is that it has a pH of 2."],[7,"physical-changes-and-chemical-changes","Physical changes and chemical changes","In a physical change , no new substances are formed. When liquid water freezes, the water has changed state but it is still the same substance after the change. In a chemical change , new substances are formed. When iron and sulfur are heated together, they form a new substance — a compound called iron sulfide. iron + sulfur → iron sulfide The iron and the sulfur have reacted together to form a new substance, so a chemical reaction has taken place. The iron atoms have combined and bonded with the sulfur atoms. - The reactants are the substances that react together — here, iron and sulfur. - The products are the new substances made in the reaction — here, there is only one product, iron sulfide. In some chemical reactions a substance breaks apart to make new substances. Water, for example, can be split apart to form oxygen and hydrogen. water → oxygen + hydrogen Chemical reactions happen everywhere: inside plants when they grow and when they decay, and inside your body to keep you alive — for example when you digest food."],[7,"burning","Burning","Burning is a chemical reaction. When a substance burns, it reacts with the oxygen in the air. Sometimes ashes are formed, and the new substances in the ashes are oxides . Charcoal is made of the element carbon . When carbon burns it combines with oxygen in the air to make the gas carbon dioxide . carbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide When magnesium metal is burnt, a white powder is formed. This powder is magnesium oxide . Magnesium and oxygen are the reactants; magnesium oxide is the product. A chemical property of magnesium is that it burns in air to form magnesium oxide by combining with oxygen. magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide"],[7,"comparing-the-properties-of-reactants-and-products","Comparing the properties of reactants and products","The properties of a product are different from the properties of the reactants it was made from. Reactant: magnesium Reactant: oxygen Product: magnesium oxide Element or compound? element element compound State at room temperature solid gas solid Appearance soft, shiny, malleable colourless, no smell white, powdery Conducts electricity? yes no no Melting point in °C 651 −214 2800"],[7,"reactions-with-water","Reactions with water","Some substances react very violently with water; some do not react with water at all. Potassium is a metal that is so soft it can be cut with a knife — that is a physical property. Potassium is so reactive that it has to be stored under oil to stop it reacting with the water vapour in the air — that is a chemical property. When a very small piece of potassium is placed in a large trough of water, hydrogen gas is given off. The reaction produces so much heat that the gas burns. Safety: you cannot carry out the potassium and water reaction yourself. If your teacher shows it as a demonstration, you must wear safety glasses and there must be a safety screen in front of the trough."],[7,"reactions-with-acid","Reactions with acid","When magnesium is placed in hydrochloric acid, bubbles of gas are given off. The magnesium has reacted with the acid and formed new substances: the gas is hydrogen, and magnesium chloride is also formed. magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen When you see bubbles forming in a reaction you know a gas is being produced — but you cannot tell which gas it is without a test."],[7,"testing-for-hydrogen","Testing for hydrogen","Hydrogen gas burns with a squeaky pop . To carry out the test you light a splint and place it in the mouth of the test tube. Keep your finger over the end of the test tube until the last moment, or there will be no hydrogen left to test, because hydrogen gas is lighter than air. When the hydrogen pops, it is reacting with oxygen in the air to form water."],[7,"collecting-hydrogen-over-water","Collecting hydrogen over water","Hydrogen can be produced on a larger scale by collecting the gas over water. The acid is poured in through the thistle funnel onto the metal in the conical flask. The gas travels along the delivery tube, bubbles up under the bee-hive shelf and pushes the water out of the measuring cylinder, so the volume of gas can be measured. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.1</b </summary 1. Compare the melting points of magnesium, oxygen and magnesium oxide. The melting point of magnesium is 651 °C and that of oxygen is −214 °C, whereas the melting point of the product, magnesium oxide, is much higher at 2800 °C. 2. Find one similarity between magnesium oxide and one of the reactants. Both magnesium and magnesium oxide are solids. 3. Physical change or chemical reaction? a Making toast — chemical reaction. b Melting chocolate — physical change. c Fireworks going off — chemical reaction. d Ice melting — physical change. e Coal burning — chemical reaction. f Copper roof turning green — chemical reaction. 4. Give two chemical properties of magnesium. Magnesium reacts with oxygen to produce magnesium oxide, and it reacts with hydrochloric acid to produce magnesium chloride and hydrogen. 5. Give two physical properties of magnesium. Magnesium is a light grey/silver coloured solid which is readily malleable. (Other properties, such as being very light or soft, are also acceptable.) 6. What are the products when zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid? Zinc chloride and hydrogen. </details <details <summary <b Getting started — hazard symbols</b </summary - A substance that can poison you: toxic - A substance that catches fire easily: flammable - A substance that can dissolve your skin: corrosive </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — burning magnesium</b </summary You hold a piece of magnesium ribbon in tongs and put it into a Bunsen flame. 1. Describe what happens to the magnesium ribbon. It burns very brightly, and the ribbon disappears (changes). 2. Describe what has been formed. A white powder is formed. 3. Name the reactants. The reactants are magnesium and oxygen. 4. Safety precautions. Wear safety glasses; do not look directly at the burning magnesium; hold the magnesium at arm's length; take care with the hot tongs. Not all metals have the same chemical properties as magnesium — they may not burn in the same way. You could try holding pieces of copper, zinc or iron in the Bunsen flame and record what happens. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — reactions with water and acid</b </summary Part 1: put a small piece of each metal into a different test tube, add water until the tube is half full, and record your observations in a table. Part 2: repeat with hydrochloric acid instead of water; if bubbles are given off, test for hydrogen. 5. Write the reactants and products for each reaction with acid. Each reaction should show the relevant reactants and products — for example, reactants: zinc and hydrochloric acid; products: zinc chloride and hydrogen. Where there was no reaction, write only the names of the reactants, because no new products were made. 6. Safety precautions. Wear safety glasses; handle acid with care; do not overfill the test tubes, to avoid fizzing and spilling. 7. How did you test for hydrogen? A lighted splint placed in the mouth of a test tube containing hydrogen will produce a pop sound. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.1A Physical and chemical reactions</b </summary 1. The correct statements are: in a physical change, no new substances are formed; when iron atoms bond with sulfur atoms, it is a chemical change; when you cook an egg, it is a chemical change. 2. Splitting water. a The reactant is water . b The products are oxygen and hydrogen . c The new substances formed are oxygen and hydrogen . </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.1B Atoms in chemical reactions</b </summary 1. a and b — the magnesium diagram. Two magnesium atoms and two oxygen atoms combine to give two units of magnesium oxide: magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide. 2. Which gas is given off when a metal reacts with an acid? Hydrogen. 3. How do you test for it? If a lighted splint is placed in the gas, there will be a 'pop' sound. 4. Complete the word equations. a zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen. b magnesium + sulfuric acid → magnesium sulfate + hydrogen. 5. Name the elements in the reaction of magnesium with hydrochloric acid. Magnesium, hydrogen and chlorine. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.1C Metal and acid</b </summary Arun and Marcus measured the volume of hydrogen given off when different masses of a metal reacted completely with acid. Mass of metal that reacted in g 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5 3.0 3.5 4.0 4.5 5.0 Volume of hydrogen given off in cm³ 70 120 150 220 260 310 350 410 450 510 1. Plot a graph. Use a sharp pencil and a ruler. Mass of metal goes on the x -axis and volume of hydrogen on the y -axis. Choose a suitable scale, plot the points accurately and draw a line of best fit. 2. Write a conclusion. The more metal used, the larger the volume of hydrogen produced. However, the increase is not consistent: it varies between about 30 cm³ and 70 cm³ for each extra 0.5 g of metal. 3. Why not use 100 cm³ measuring cylinders? The results would not be very accurate, because you would have to change cylinders during the reaction. Some of the gas would be lost each time you changed. 4. Products when zinc reacts with sulfuric acid. Zinc sulfate and hydrogen. 5. Products when magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid. Magnesium chloride and hydrogen. </details"],[7,"8-2-neutralisation","8.2 Neutralisation","Key words: burette · decay · digest · filtrate · indigestion · neutralisation · neutralised"],[7,"mixing-acids-and-alkalis","Mixing acids and alkalis","Acids and alkalis can cancel each other out. When you mix them together they react and make a neutral solution. This is called neutralisation , and it is another chemical change. Neutrality is also a chemical property of a substance. - Add too much acid to an alkali and you make an acidic liquid. - Add too little acid and the liquid stays alkaline. Adding the acid very slowly, a few drops at a time, makes it easier to judge exactly when the mixture becomes neutral."],[7,"making-a-neutral-solution-with-a-burette","Making a neutral solution with a burette","You can use a piece of equipment called a burette to neutralise an alkali very accurately. Universal indicator is added to the alkali in the conical flask, and acid is run in from the burette. Stage Volume of acid added pH in the flask What it means At the start 0 cm³ about 13 strongly alkaline Part way 25 cm³ 7 neutral — the acid has neutralised the alkali A little more acid more than 25 cm³ about 6 weakly acidic The flask is shaken each time some acid is added. A chemical reaction takes place and new substances are formed. With hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide (an alkali) as the reactants, the products are sodium chloride and water. hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium chloride + water"],[7,"neutralisation-in-everyday-life","Neutralisation in everyday life","Indigestion. Your stomach produces hydrochloric acid, which gives the stomach the right conditions to digest your food. When your stomach produces too much acid you get indigestion , which can be very uncomfortable. The medicines that help are all alkalis that neutralise the acid; they are sometimes called antacids . Toothpaste. There are millions of bacteria in your mouth. They feed on pieces of food left on your teeth, and they produce acid when they feed. This acid damages your teeth and makes them decay . Toothpaste contains alkali, which helps to neutralise the acid. Neutralising lakes. In some parts of the world, harmful chemicals in the air make the rain acidic. This acid rain damages trees and changes the pH of lakes, rivers and ponds. The plants and animals that live there cannot survive in acid conditions, so some countries drop alkalis into the lakes to neutralise the acid. Growing crops. In some areas the soil is very acidic and plants do not grow well. Farmers spread lime on the soil to neutralise the acid so the plants grow better. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.2</b </summary 1. What colour is universal indicator when the solution is neutral? Green. 2. What sort of reaction happens when an acid and an alkali are mixed? A neutralisation reaction. 3. Why is toothpaste alkaline? So that it can neutralise the acid in your mouth and help to stop your teeth decaying. 4. Where does the acid in your mouth come from? From the millions of bacteria there. When the bacteria feed, they produce acid. 5. Why is an alkaline substance dropped into lakes in some countries? The lakes have become very acidic as a result of acid rain. This changes the pH so that plants and animals can no longer live there, and the alkali overcomes this. 6. What do farmers spread onto acidic soil, and why? Lime. It neutralises the acid, so that crops which prefer less acidic soil will grow well. </details <details <summary <b Activity 8.2.1 — Rainbow neutralisation</b </summary A crystal of washing soda is put in the bottom of a clamped test tube, water is added until the tube is two-thirds full, universal indicator is added, and acid is poured carefully on top. The tube is left to stand for a few days without being moved. 1. pH at the top of the tube? About pH 2 (it depends on how well the practical was carried out). 2. pH at the bottom of the tube? About pH 13 . 3. The most alkaline part of the tube? The bottom of the test tube. 4. Why must the tube be kept still? So that the liquids do not mix too much. 5. Which hazard symbols were on the equipment? This depends on the acid and alkali used. Most likely corrosive (can destroy living tissue and cause burns) or health hazard (can cause harm and irritate skin and eyes). How the rainbow appears. At the top , the acid turns the indicator red — strongly acidic; as the acid particles move down and mix with more water it turns yellow — weakly acidic. In the middle , the acid and washing soda solution meet and react, neutralising each other. At the bottom , the washing soda dissolves and the indicator turns purple or dark blue — strongly alkaline; further up it becomes lighter blue — weakly alkaline. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — testing the pH of soil</b </summary Take a sample of soil in a test tube, add water and shake. Filter the mixture, then add a few drops of universal indicator to the filtrate (the liquid that comes through the filter paper), and record the result. The answer depends on your own soil sample. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.2A Measuring</b </summary 1. Read the measuring cylinders. B 25 cm³, C 36 cm³, D 12 cm³, E 47 cm³. 2. Draw the liquid levels at: B 20 cm³, C 35 cm³, D 15 cm³, E 5 cm³. The lines should be slightly curved (a meniscus), with the bottom of the curve at the level of the scale reading. 3. Read the burettes. A 16 cm³, B 23 cm³, C 40 cm³, D 49 cm³, E 62 cm³. (Remember that a burette scale is numbered from the top downwards.) </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.2B Neutralising acid</b </summary A model answer, written as a method: I put on my safety glasses to protect my eyes in case of spills or splashes. I collected the chemicals from the teacher and used a beaker to take a small amount of acid back to my workspace. I used 0.5 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid and carefully filled a burette. I made sure the burette was secure in the clamp stand and that the tap was closed, so that no acid was spilt. I collected some sodium hydroxide from the teacher in a beaker. I measured 25 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide using a measuring cylinder and put it into a conical flask. I added a few drops of universal indicator solution; the solution turned blue because sodium hydroxide is an alkali. I placed the conical flask under the burette. I slowly added acid from the burette to the conical flask, gently shaking the flask each time I added acid, and watching the colour of the solution. When the solution was green, I knew I had added enough acid to neutralise the sodium hydroxide. The important points are: the acid goes in the burette; the sodium hydroxide is measured with a measuring cylinder or pipette; and the safety measures are given together with the reasons for them. </details"],[7,"8-3-investigating-acids-and-alkalis","8.3 Investigating acids and alkalis","Key words: remedy · variable"],[7,"asking-questions-you-can-answer","Asking questions you can answer","Scientists ask questions, then devise tests to look for the answers. Here are some questions about neutralisation that a scientist might try to answer. - How much lime should be added to an acid lake to neutralise it? - Which is the best indigestion remedy (treatment for an illness or injury)? - How much toothpaste is needed to neutralise the acid in your mouth? 'Which is the best indigestion remedy?' is not a precise question. What does best mean — the most pleasant tasting, the cheapest, the most effective, or the most cost-effective? A scientist would rewrite it so that it can be tested, for example: Which indigestion powder neutralises the acid using the least amount of powder?"],[7,"planning-an-investigation","Planning an investigation","When you plan an investigation you have to design an experiment. If you are investigating the effect of indigestion powders on stomach acid, you cannot use your own stomach acid — you have to use a model instead, such as a beaker of acid. There is a lot to think about: - What will I change, and what will I keep the same? The things that can change are called variables . - How will I make my test fair? - What will I measure, and how will I know when the powder has neutralised the acid? - What equipment do I need? - What will I do to stay safe? - How will I record the results?"],[7,"which-powder-is-best-at-neutralising-acid","Which powder is best at neutralising acid?","Marcus and Arun put 20 cm³ of hydrochloric acid into each of three beakers. The acid has a pH of 1, like the strong acid in your stomach. They put a few drops of universal indicator in each beaker, then added indigestion powder spatula by spatula until the indicator turned green, recording the number of spatulas used. Their first set of results: Powder Number of spatulas used to neutralise the acid A 10 B 6 C 24 They then repeated the whole investigation twice more: Powder First try Second try Third try Mean A 10 9 11 10 B 6 17 16 13 C 24 23 25 24 With only one set of results, powder B looked the most effective. With three sets, it is clear that B's first result was an anomaly , and powder A is really the most effective. This is why an investigation is repeated — and why, when you look at evidence from someone else's investigation, you should always ask how many times the measurement was repeated. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.3</b </summary 1. Marcus and Arun are using acid of pH 1. What should they do to stay safe? Wear safety glasses and take care when handling strong acids. If they spill the acid they should dilute it with water; if they get it on their skin, they should wash it off at once and seek medical help. 2. Which variables are being kept the same? The volume of acid; the type and strength of the acid; the volume of universal indicator; and the volume of powder added with each spatula. 3. Which variable is being changed? The powder being tested. 4. What is being measured? The number of spatulas of powder used to neutralise the acid. 5. Which is the most effective powder (first set of results)? Powder B. 6. Which is the least effective powder? Powder C. 7. Is there enough evidence to be certain? No. There is only one set of results, so there could be mistakes. 8. After repeating, which powder is the most effective? Powder A. 9. Which result looks 'wrong'? The first result for powder B. 10. Suggest why they got this 'wrong' result. They may have miscounted the number of spatulas, or heaped them up so that they were adding more powder each time; or they may have measured the volume of acid incorrectly. 11. What should they have done about it? Ignored the 'wrong' result and repeated the experiment. 12. Should the 'wrong' result be included in the mean? No — it should be ignored when calculating the mean. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — asking questions and planning</b </summary In a group, write down four questions about acids and alkalis that you could investigate, then check that each one really can be investigated. Choose one of them and plan how to carry out the investigation, dividing up the tasks — equipment list, method, safety, results table — and share the plan with the class. Self-assessment: was your plan safe, would it work, and was the test fair? </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.3A Indigestion investigation</b </summary 1. What is being measured? The number of spatulas used to neutralise the acid. 2. Which tests are fair? - Arun's test: not fair, because when he tested powder B he used less hydrochloric acid (25 cm³) than when he tested powders A and C (50 cm³). - Sofia's test: not fair, because a different acid (sulfuric acid) was used with powder A than with powders B and C (hydrochloric acid). - Zara's test: not fair, because no universal indicator was used with powder C. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.3B Planning investigations</b </summary 1. What is a 'variable'? A variable is something that can change, such as the type of acid or the type of indigestion powder used. 2. The lake scientist. a To make step 2 a fair test, she must use the same volume of lake water in each of her tests. b She tests each sample three times so that she can check the results are consistent . c Her results: Lake First try Second try Third try Mean A 4 6 5 5 B 10 9 11 10 C 1 1 1 1 Lake C is the least acidic and lake B is the most acidic — the more acidic the water, the more sodium hydroxide is needed to neutralise it. There is a very large range in the acidity of the three lakes. </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.3C Investigating antacids</b </summary 1. The results table. Antacid powder after 0 g after 1 g after 2 g after 3 g after 4 g A 2 3 4 5 6 B 2 5 7 7 7 C 2 2 2 3 3 D 2 4 6 7 7 E 2 2 3 3 4 2. The graph. Plot mass of antacid added in g on the x -axis and pH of the acid on the y -axis, with a separate labelled line for each powder A–E. 3. Which variables were kept the same, and why? The volume of acid and the type of acid, to make it a fair test. 4. Which powders neutralised the acid? B and D (they reach pH 7). 5. Which powder neutralised the acid fastest? B. 6. Which powder was the least effective? C. 7. What pH shows the acid has been neutralised? pH 7. 8. How much of powder D is needed to reach pH 7? About 3.5 g. 9. Which powder would you recommend? D, because it reaches pH 7 but does not neutralise the acid too fast, so it may not produce a lot of uncomfortable gas quickly. (B is acceptable if you comment on the gas being produced quickly.) </details"],[7,"8-4-detecting-chemical-reactions","8.4 Detecting chemical reactions","Key words: cloudy · glowing · precipitate"],[7,"how-can-you-tell-a-chemical-reaction-has-happened","How can you tell a chemical reaction has happened?","In a chemical reaction, new products are formed from the reactants. There are several clues that a reaction has taken place: - a gas is given off - a reactant seems to 'disappear' - there is a colour change - heat is produced - there is a change in pH - a precipitate is formed."],[7,"clue-1-a-gas-is-given-off","Clue 1: a gas is given off","Reaction 1 — hydrogen. Magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid and bubbles of hydrogen are given off. Hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop when a lighted splint is placed in the mouth of the tube. magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen Reaction 2 — carbon dioxide. When baking powder and vinegar react, carbon dioxide is given off. You test for carbon dioxide using limewater : when carbon dioxide mixes with limewater, the limewater turns cloudy . Reaction 3 — oxygen. When a piece of apple is placed in hydrogen peroxide, it bubbles and oxygen is given off. You test for oxygen with a glowing splint: if the gas is oxygen, the glowing splint relights. Gas Test Positive result hydrogen lighted splint in the mouth of the tube squeaky pop oxygen glowing splint in the mouth of the tube splint relights carbon dioxide bubble the gas through limewater limewater turns cloudy"],[7,"clue-2-a-reactant-disappears","Clue 2: a reactant 'disappears'","When magnesium ribbon reacts with acid, hydrogen is produced and the magnesium ribbon 'disappears'. The magnesium is used up in the reaction: it combines with the chlorine from the hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride."],[7,"clue-3-a-colour-change","Clue 3: a colour change","Gently heating black copper oxide with sulfuric acid produces a blue solution of copper sulfate. copper oxide (black) + sulfuric acid → copper sulfate (blue) + water Safety: do not boil the blue liquid, as harmful fumes will be given off."],[7,"clue-4-heat-is-produced","Clue 4: heat is produced","When potassium is placed in water, hydrogen is given off and the reaction produces so much heat that the hydrogen burns. (This reaction must only be done by a teacher.) When zinc is added to hydrochloric acid, hydrogen is given off and the test tube feels hot."],[7,"clue-5-a-change-in-ph","Clue 5: a change in pH","When you neutralise an alkali there is a change in pH. This is a neutralisation reaction. sodium hydroxide + hydrochloric acid → sodium chloride + water"],[7,"clue-6-a-precipitate-is-formed","Clue 6: a precipitate is formed","If you mix solutions of silver nitrate and calcium chloride, a solid is formed when the two liquids are mixed. This solid is called a precipitate — here it is silver chloride. silver nitrate + calcium chloride → silver chloride + calcium nitrate Limewater is a solution of calcium hydroxide. The limewater turns cloudy when carbon dioxide is bubbled in because a precipitate of calcium carbonate forms — you added a gas to a liquid and a solid was formed. calcium hydroxide + carbon dioxide → calcium carbonate + water <details <summary <b Getting started — 8.4</b </summary - Reactants and products. Reactants are the chemicals you start with in a chemical reaction; products are the chemicals you end up with. - Chemical and physical changes. In a chemical change new chemicals are made, as in the reaction between magnesium and hydrochloric acid. In a physical change no new chemicals are formed, but there may be a change of state — for example from a solid to a liquid. - Acids and alkalis. An acid has a pH below 7; an alkali has a pH above 7. </details <details <summary <b Activity 8.4.1 — Testing gases</b </summary Testing for carbon dioxide: half-fill a test tube with limewater. Put a small amount of calcium carbonate into another test tube and add hydrochloric acid. Quickly fit the rubber bung and delivery tube, making sure the delivery tube reaches into the limewater. Testing for oxygen: half-fill a test tube with hydrogen peroxide, add a spatula of manganese dioxide, and place a glowing splint into the neck of the test tube. 1. What did you see with the limewater? The limewater was colourless to start with. As the carbon dioxide bubbled into the tube, it started to go cloudy; the more gas that was bubbled through, the whiter and cloudier the limewater became. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — chemical reactions or not?</b </summary Before trying any of these, carry out a risk assessment and wear safety glasses. For each experiment, decide whether a chemical reaction has taken place and say what evidence you have. Experiment Reaction? Evidence A black copper oxide + dilute sulfuric acid yes colour change — the solution turns blue as copper sulfate forms B zinc + dilute hydrochloric acid yes bubbles of gas (hydrogen), the zinc disappears, and the tube feels hot C blowing through a straw into limewater yes the limewater turns cloudy — a precipitate of calcium carbonate D chocolate in a test tube in hot water no melting is a physical change; the chocolate sets again when it cools E heating copper sulfate solution in an evaporating dish no the water evaporates and blue crystals form again — a physical change F sodium hydroxide + universal indicator + acid from a burette yes the pH changes and the indicator turns green — neutralisation G baking powder + vinegar yes fizzing; a gas (carbon dioxide) is given off H silver nitrate + calcium chloride solution yes a white precipitate of silver chloride forms </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.4A Key words for material changes</b </summary Word Meaning neutralisation the reaction where an acid is cancelled out by an alkali; a neutral solution has a pH of 7 precipitate what is formed when two liquids react and produce a solid pH scale this shows the strength of an acid or an alkali corrosive able to dissolve or eat away other materials reactants what you start with in a chemical reaction products what is made in a chemical reaction alkali pH of more than 7 acid pH of less than 7 </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.4B Has a reaction taken place?</b </summary One way to tell that a reaction has taken place is that a gas is given off — you see bubbles or fizzing (for example vinegar and baking powder). Three other things to look for: 1. A colour change. Example: black copper oxide heated with sulfuric acid gives a blue copper sulfate solution. 2. A change in temperature — heat is produced. Example: zinc added to hydrochloric acid makes the test tube feel hot. 3. A precipitate forms — a solid appears when two solutions are mixed. Example: silver nitrate mixed with calcium chloride gives a white precipitate of silver chloride. (A change in pH, or a reactant 'disappearing', would also be accepted.) </details <details <summary <b Workbook — Exercise 8.4C Testing for gases</b </summary You have four test tubes: one contains air, one oxygen, one hydrogen and one carbon dioxide. Take each tube in turn, keeping your thumb over the mouth until the moment you test it. 1. Lighted splint. Place a lighted splint in the mouth of the tube. The tube that gives a squeaky pop contains hydrogen . 2. Glowing splint. Place a glowing splint in the mouth of each of the remaining tubes. The tube in which the splint relights contains oxygen . A splint put into carbon dioxide goes out; in air it keeps glowing but does not relight. 3. Limewater. To separate carbon dioxide from air, pour a little limewater into each of the last two tubes, put your thumb over the mouth and shake. The limewater that turns cloudy shows the tube contained carbon dioxide ; the other tube contained air . Wear safety glasses throughout. </details <details <summary <b Project — moving dangerous chemicals</b </summary Working in a group of three, write a report as a team of reporters covering a serious road accident involving a truck carrying concentrated acid. Points you could consider: - Why is an acid spill so dangerous, and what would happen if people drove through it? - How do the emergency services know which chemical they are dealing with (hazard labels and transport documents)? - What must they do to the spilt acid, and how will they know when the task is complete (neutralise it, then check the pH)? - What safety measures must the emergency services take? - What would be the effect of the acid reaching the soil alongside the road? - What regulations apply in your area to the transport of dangerous materials, and what extra considerations apply at sea or in the air? Present your report as a newspaper or magazine article, a radio or television interview, or a class presentation — choose a form you have not used in another science project. </details"],[7,"check-your-progress","Check your progress","<details <summary <b Answers</b </summary 8.1 Sort the list into two columns. Physical change: melting chocolate; heating glass and bending it. Chemical reaction: burning a piece of wood; cooking an egg; baking a cake. (In the physical changes no new substance is made and the change can be reversed; in the chemical reactions new substances are formed.) [5] 8.2 Zinc added to sulfuric acid. a The gas given off is hydrogen . [1] b Place a lighted splint in the mouth of the test tube; hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop . [2] c The products are zinc sulfate and hydrogen . [2] d The bubbling (fizzing) stops, and some zinc is left over unreacted. [1] 8.3 Magnesium ribbon burning in air. a The chemical symbol for magnesium is Mg . [1] b The element in the air that reacts with magnesium is oxygen . [1] c The compound formed is magnesium oxide . [1] d With chlorine, magnesium would form magnesium chloride . [1] 8.4 a Produces a metal oxide: D (copper carbonate → copper oxide + carbon dioxide). [1] b Is a neutralisation reaction: B (sodium hydroxide + hydrochloric acid → sodium chloride + water). [1] c Is a burning reaction: A (carbon combines with oxygen to form carbon dioxide). [1] 8.5 Zara and Sofia added acid 10 cm³ at a time to 50 cm³ of alkali. Volume of acid added in cm³ 0 10 20 30 40 50 pH of solution 12 11 10 9 8 7 a At the start the solution was blue or purple (pH 12 — alkaline). [1] b At the end the solution was green (pH 7 — neutral). [1] c The correct statement is 'the acid and the alkali were equal in strength' . 50 cm³ of acid exactly neutralised 50 cm³ of alkali, so equal volumes cancelled each other out. [1] d Plot volume of acid added on the horizontal axis and pH on the vertical axis. Use a sensible scale, plot the six points accurately and join them with a straight line of best fit — the pH falls steadily by 1 for every 10 cm³ of acid added. [4] </details"],[8,"9-1-flow-of-electricity","9.1 Flow of electricity","Key words: attract · battery · cell · components · current · electrons · free to move · negative charge · repel · terminals"],[8,"current","Current","Look at the circuit in the picture. When you close the switch, the lamp lights. A cell stores chemical energy that can be changed to electrical energy in a circuit. A battery contains two or more cells joined together. The cell in the circuit has two connections, called terminals . All cells, batteries, power supplies and many other components have two terminals. The terminals are labelled with + and − symbols, meaning positive (+) and negative (−). You can see these symbols in the photograph of the three cells below. When connected into a circuit, the negative terminal of a cell, battery or power supply pushes electrons around in the wires. All materials contain atoms. Atoms contain smaller particles. An electron is one type of smaller particle in an atom. The flow of electrons in the circuit is called current . Remember: the flow of current in a circuit is the movement of electrons around the circuit."],[8,"more-about-electrons","More about electrons","Some of the electrons in a metal are free to move . That means they can move through the metal. In a metal, these electrons move randomly. Electrons are very small, so diagrams of them are never to scale. When the metal is placed into a circuit, the electrons move in the same direction. Electrons have a negative charge . Opposite charges attract , and like charges repel . To attract means to pull together; to repel means to push apart. That means: - positive and negative attract - positive and positive repel - negative and negative repel. Therefore, electrons are attracted towards the positive terminal of the power supply and repelled from the negative terminal. If there is a break in the circuit, all the electrons stop flowing. Electrons can only flow in a complete circuit."],[8,"modelling-electron-flow","Modelling electron flow","A loop of string held by a circle of people is a useful model of a circuit. One person is the cell and pulls the string around through everyone else's hands. Coloured marks on the string represent the electrons. The model shows two important ideas: - the whole loop starts to move at the same moment, so the current is the same everywhere - the string moves at the same speed all the way round, just as electrons do in a series circuit. The model is not perfect: the string is a loop of one material, while a real circuit contains different components, and nothing in the model stands for the energy stored in the cell."],[8,"9-2-electrical-circuits","9.2 Electrical circuits","Key words: ammeter · circuit diagram · circuit symbols"],[8,"circuit-diagrams","Circuit diagrams","Circuits can be shown in a drawing or in a circuit diagram . Advantages of circuit diagrams compared with pictures: - circuit diagrams are easier to draw - the components have standard symbols - wires are drawn with straight lines, which is easy to interpret."],[8,"circuit-symbols","Circuit symbols","Components in circuits can look very different. The picture shows how different some lamps can look. Standard circuit symbols are used in circuit diagrams so there is no confusion. The same symbols are used in all countries. Name Function Ammeter measures current in the circuit Cell provides energy to make current flow Lamp gives out light Switch (open) stops the flow of current when opened Switch (closed) starts the flow of current when closed Buzzer makes a buzzing sound In the circuit symbol for a cell, the positive terminal is the longer of the two lines. The negative terminal is the shorter line. Take care when drawing circuit diagrams. Make sure: - there are no gaps in the lines, especially at the corners and where wires meet components - wires are not drawn through components."],[8,"comparing-circuit-diagrams","Comparing circuit diagrams","Different circuits are used to do different jobs. This circuit could be used in a doorbell. If the switch is pushed outside a door, it makes the buzzer sound and attracts attention inside. The ammeter measures the electric current. This circuit could be used to light a room and measure the current flowing through the circuit. By measuring the current, you could tell if the cell needs to be replaced — a smaller current means the cell is low on stored energy. The lamp would also become less bright, but you might not notice until too late."],[8,"9-3-measuring-the-flow-of-current","9.3 Measuring the flow of current","Key words: amps · in series"],[8,"measuring-current","Measuring current","In Topic 9.1 you saw that electrons move when current flows. - When electrons move more quickly, the current increases . - When electrons move more slowly, the current decreases . In Topic 9.2 you saw that you can use an ammeter to measure current. Current is measured in units called amps . Amps have the symbol A. Ammeters have two terminals, for wires to be attached. One terminal is red and the other is black. In a circuit, the red terminal of the ammeter must be connected to the positive terminal of the power supply. The red terminal may be connected directly, or through other components, to the positive terminal. Both ends of the ammeter are the same in a circuit diagram. You do not need to show the red and black terminals on the circuit symbol for an ammeter."],[8,"connecting-an-ammeter","Connecting an ammeter","Ammeters are always connected in series with the other components in a circuit. If the components are connected in series they are all connected end to end, one after another, and there are no branches in the circuit."],[8,"reading-an-ammeter-scale","Reading an ammeter scale","Different ammeters have different scales. Always look at the numbers printed on the scale and work out what one small division is worth before you read the pointer."],[8,"current-in-a-series-circuit","Current in a series circuit","If you move an ammeter to different positions in the same series circuit, the reading does not change. The current is the same at every position in a series circuit."],[8,"9-4-conductors-and-insulators","9.4 Conductors and insulators","Key words: allow current to flow · conduct electricity · conductor · inhibit · insulator"],[8,"conductors","Conductors","Conductors are materials that conduct electricity . That means conductors allow current to flow . Electric current can flow through a conductor. You saw in Topic 9.1 that electrons move when current flows. Therefore, a conductor is a material in which electrons are free to move. Conductors allow electrons to flow. All metals are conductors. Wires that carry electric current are made from metal. Most wires in houses and schools are made from copper, with a plastic coating. Wires that cover long distances are usually made from aluminium or steel. These metals are cheaper than copper. Some circuits, such as those in phones and laptops, do not use wires. A printed circuit board has copper tracks instead of wires. The tracks are green because the copper is coated with another material."],[8,"insulators","Insulators","Electrical insulators are materials that do not allow current to flow through them. Most non-metals, such as plastic, wood, air and cotton, are insulators. In an insulator, the electrons are not free to move. Because the electrons are not free to move, current cannot flow. Insulators inhibit electron flow."],[8,"using-conductors-and-insulators","Using conductors and insulators","Insulators are used to keep people safe from electricity. The body of a plug is made from plastic so that people can touch the plug safely. The pins are made from metal so that current can flow into the appliance. The wire coming from the plug is also coated in plastic to protect people from electric shocks. Plastic insulation on wires is also useful because the plastic can be coloured differently to identify each wire. The insulators on power lines stop the current flowing from the wires into the metal pylon. If the current flowed to the pylon, electrical energy would be dissipated into the ground. Also, people and animals touching the pylon would get electric shocks."],[8,"testing-materials","Testing materials","You can find out whether an object is a conductor or an insulator by connecting it into a circuit with a cell and a lamp, using crocodile clips. - Touch the crocodile clips together first. The lamp should light. This shows that the circuit itself works. - Then connect the object between the clips. If the lamp lights, the object is a conductor. If the lamp does not light, the object is an insulator. Some metal objects can appear to be insulators in this test. This may be because the metal is covered with paint, plastic or a layer of oxide, or because the clips are not touching bare metal. Scraping the surface clean, or clipping onto a bare part, shows that the metal really is a conductor."],[8,"9-5-adding-or-removing-components","9.5 Adding or removing components","Key words: adding components · dimmer · position · removing components"],[8,"current-in-a-series-circuit","Current in a series circuit","Current is the same all the way around a series circuit. You can think of the electrons in a series circuit like a train. All parts of the train move at the same speed on the track. The back of the train cannot go faster than the front. As soon as one part of the train moves, all of the train moves. Electrons flow at the same speed in the wires of a series circuit. When electrons start to flow in one part of the circuit, they all start to flow. That means: - you can put an ammeter at any position in a series circuit and it will give the same result - you can put the same components of a series circuit in a different order and the current will be the same. Adding components or removing components will affect the current. The effect depends on which components are changed."],[8,"adding-or-removing-cells","Adding or removing cells","You saw in Topic 3.5 that cells and batteries are stores of chemical energy. In a complete circuit, the chemical energy gets changed to electrical energy. If you add more cells to a circuit, you have more chemical energy to change into electrical energy in the circuit. Each component is identical in both circuits. The circuit with two cells has double the electrical energy of the circuit with one cell. The lamp will be brighter. The ammeter in the circuit with two cells shows that the current is doubled. You need to be careful when adding more cells to a circuit. The lamps can be damaged and the wires can get hot. - Adding more cells increases the current when the other components are kept the same. - Removing cells decreases the current when the other components are kept the same."],[8,"adding-or-removing-lamps","Adding or removing lamps","You saw in Topic 3.5 that lamps change electrical energy to light energy and thermal energy. Electrons carry electrical energy around a circuit. When the electrons pass through a lamp, the electrons transfer some of their electrical energy to the lamp. The lamp changes the electrical energy into light energy and thermal energy. Remember that the current is the same at all positions in a series circuit. Putting a lamp in a circuit will make the electrons move more slowly, but they move more slowly the whole way around the circuit. The circuit with two lamps transfers twice as much energy as the circuit with one lamp. The lamps will be dimmer — they do not shine as brightly. This makes the electrons move at half the speed, so the reading on the ammeter is halved. - Adding more lamps decreases the current when the other components are kept the same. - Removing lamps increases the current when the other components are kept the same."],[8,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can describe how electrons move in circuits. - I can work out the direction in which electrons move. - I can recognise and draw the circuit symbols for a cell, a switch, a lamp, a buzzer and an ammeter. - I can draw a circuit diagram containing any of these components. - I can compare the jobs of different circuits from their circuit diagrams. - I can recall the unit of current and its symbol. - I can take accurate readings from an ammeter. - I can explain how to connect an ammeter in a circuit. - I can describe what is meant by 'electrical conductor' and 'insulator'. - I can explain conduction and insulation in terms of electrons. - I can recall some examples of conductors and insulators, and describe some of their uses. - I can explain that the current is the same all around a series circuit. - I can predict what happens to the current when cells or lamps are added to or removed from a series circuit."],[9,"1-1-the-human-respiratory-system","1.1 The human respiratory system","Key words: aerobic respiration · air sac · bronchiole · bronchus · cartilage · larynx · respiration · respiratory system · trachea · vocal cords · voicebox · windpipe"],[9,"why-we-need-oxygen","Why we need oxygen","Respiration is one of the characteristics shared by all living things. It is a series of chemical reactions that happens inside every living cell . The kind of respiration that usually happens inside our cells is called aerobic respiration . Aerobic respiration uses oxygen . The cells produce carbon dioxide as a waste product. The air around you contains oxygen. When you breathe, you take air into your lungs. Some of the oxygen from the air goes into your blood. The blood delivers the oxygen to every cell in the body, so that the cells can use it for respiration. The blood collects the waste carbon dioxide from the cells and takes it back to the lungs. The organs that help you to take oxygen out of the air, and get rid of carbon dioxide, make up the respiratory system ."],[9,"the-structure-of-the-human-respiratory-system","The structure of the human respiratory system","In the diagram below, the white spaces are the 'tubes' that air moves through as it goes into and out of your lungs. Air gets into your body through your mouth or nose. Your mouth and nose both connect to your trachea , which is sometimes called the windpipe . - The trachea has strong rings of cartilage around it. These rings keep the trachea open and prevent it collapsing, so air can keep moving in and out of your body. If you put your fingers on the front of your neck and move them downwards, you can feel the rings of cartilage. - The trachea branches into two bronchi (singular: bronchus ). The bronchi also have cartilage to support them. One bronchus goes to each lung, and carries air deep into the lung. - Each bronchus divides into several smaller tubes called bronchioles . These branches allow air to reach deeper into the lungs. - The bronchioles end by branching into many tiny structures called air sacs . This is where oxygen goes into the blood and carbon dioxide comes out. Order of structures, breathing in: nose or mouth → trachea → bronchus → bronchiole → air sac. Breathing out, the order is reversed."],[9,"the-larynx","The larynx","Your larynx ( voicebox ) sits at the top of the trachea. It contains your vocal cords — bands of muscle stretched across the larynx, rather like guitar strings. When these cords vibrate, they make a sound. Changing the cords changes the pitch of the sound."],[9,"1-2-gas-exchange","1.2 Gas exchange","Key words: alveoli · analogy · capillaries · diffusion · expired air · gas exchange · haemoglobin · inspired air · limewater"],[9,"air-sacs","Air sacs","Seen through a powerful microscope, the lungs are mostly holes. These holes are the air sacs. Another name for them is alveoli . There are also lots of very tiny blood vessels wrapped around the air sacs. These blood vessels are capillaries ."],[9,"the-structure-of-an-air-sac","The structure of an air sac","The wall of an air sac is made of one layer of very thin cells . A blood capillary is pressed tightly against the outside of the alveolus, and the wall of the capillary is also a single layer of very thin cells."],[9,"gas-exchange-in-the-air-sacs","Gas exchange in the air sacs","Inside the air sacs, oxygen from the air goes into the blood and carbon dioxide from the blood goes into the air. This is called gas exchange . - The blood arriving at the lungs has come from the organs of the body. The cells there have been respiring, so this blood contains only a small amount of oxygen and a lot of carbon dioxide . - The air inside the air sac has come from outside the body, so it contains a lot of oxygen and only a small amount of carbon dioxide . - Only two very thin cells separate the air from the blood. The oxygen particles in the air are a gas, so they move freely. They move easily from the air, through the thin walls, and into the blood. This is called diffusion : the oxygen molecules move from where there are a lot of them (in the air) to where there are fewer of them (in the blood). When the oxygen gets into the blood it dissolves, then goes into the red blood cells where it combines with haemoglobin . Carbon dioxide does the opposite. There is a lot of it in the blood and only a small quantity in the air inside the air sac, so carbon dioxide diffuses out of the blood into the air sac. Why the air sacs are small: many small air sacs give a much larger total surface area than a few large ones of the same total volume, so gases diffuse in and out much faster."],[9,"comparing-inspired-and-expired-air","Comparing inspired and expired air","The air you breathe in is called inspired air . The air you breathe out is called expired air . Limewater turns cloudy when carbon dioxide bubbles through it. In the apparatus below, expired air bubbles through the limewater in one test tube and inspired air bubbles through the limewater in the other. The limewater that expired air bubbles through goes cloudy first . This shows that expired air contains more carbon dioxide than inspired air ."],[9,"1-3-breathing","1.3 Breathing","Key words: breathing · contract · relax"],[9,"measuring-the-air-you-breathe-out","Measuring the air you breathe out","You can measure the volume of air you push out in one breath by filling a bottle with water, turning it upside down in a bowl of water, and breathing out through a tube into the bottle. The expired air pushes water out of the bottle, and a scale marked on the bottle gives the volume."],[9,"how-breathing-works","How breathing works","Air is a gas. The pressure of a gas increases when the volume of its container decreases. Breathing works by changing the volume of the chest cavity. When you breathe in: - The intercostal muscles between the ribs contract (get shorter). This pulls the ribs upwards and outwards. - The muscles in the diaphragm contract . This pulls the diaphragm downwards. - These two movements make more space inside the chest cavity — they increase its volume. - When the volume increases, the pressure inside the chest cavity and lungs decreases . - Air moves down through the trachea into the lungs to fill the extra space. When you breathe out: - The intercostal muscles between the ribs relax (return to normal size). This allows the ribs to drop down into their natural position. - The muscles in the diaphragm relax . This allows the diaphragm to return to its normal, domed shape. - These two movements make less space inside the chest cavity — they decrease its volume. - When the volume decreases, the pressure increases, so air is squeezed out of the lungs. Action Diaphragm muscles Intercostal muscles Volume of chest Air moves Breathing in contract contract increases into the lungs Breathing out relax relax decreases out of the lungs"],[9,"a-model-of-breathing","A model of breathing","A syringe with the end cut off, a rubber bung and a balloon can be used to model breathing movements. Pulling the plunger out increases the volume inside the syringe, so the pressure drops and the balloon inflates. In the model, the balloon represents the lungs, the small hole in the bung represents the trachea, the plunger represents the diaphragm and the syringe barrel represents the rib cage. The model is not perfect — for example, nothing in it represents the rib movements caused by the intercostal muscles."],[9,"1-4-respiration","1.4 Respiration","Key words: glucose · mitochondria"],[9,"using-energy-to-stay-alive","Using energy to stay alive","Our bodies need energy for many different reasons — to move around, to send electrical impulses along neurones, and to keep our bodies warm when it is cold. All of our energy comes from the food that we eat. Carbohydrates are especially good for giving us energy. When we eat food containing carbohydrates, our digestive system breaks the carbohydrates down to a kind of sugar called glucose . The glucose goes into our blood, and the blood delivers glucose to every cell in the body."],[9,"releasing-energy-from-glucose","Releasing energy from glucose","The energy in glucose is locked up inside it — glucose is an energy store. Before your cells can use that energy, it has to be released from the glucose. This is done by tiny structures called mitochondria that are found inside cells. Most cells have many mitochondria inside them. The mitochondria carry out a chemical reaction called aerobic respiration . Aerobic means that it uses oxygen from the air. Word equation for aerobic respiration: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water In this reaction, some of the energy inside the glucose is released. This is done in a very controlled way — just a little energy at a time, just enough for the cell's needs. - The reactants are glucose and oxygen. - The products are carbon dioxide and water. - Cells that use a lot of energy, such as neurones, contain more mitochondria than cells that use little energy, such as cheek cells."],[9,"respiration-and-heat-production","Respiration and heat production","Every time energy is transferred or transformed, some of it is changed to heat energy. In respiration, chemical energy stored in glucose is transferred to other substances so that cells can use it, and some of the energy is changed to heat energy. So respiring cells get a little warmer than their surroundings . Germinating seeds respire especially quickly, because germinating needs a lot of energy. In the experiment below, a flask of live, germinating peas becomes warmer than an identical flask of dead peas. - The variable that is changed is whether the peas are alive or dead. - The variables kept the same include the number of peas, the size of the flask, the insulation and the starting temperature. - The variable that is measured is the temperature inside each flask. A thermogram shows how warm different objects are. A living person shows up much warmer than the chair they are sitting on, because their cells are respiring. Breathing is not respiration. Breathing is the movement of air into and out of the lungs. Respiration is a series of chemical reactions inside every living cell that releases useful energy from glucose."],[9,"1-5-blood","1.5 Blood","Key words: antibodies · blood plasma · oxyhaemoglobin · pathogens · red blood cells · white blood cells"],[9,"delivering-the-requirements-for-respiration","Delivering the requirements for respiration","Every cell in your body needs a good supply of glucose and oxygen, and the carbon dioxide and water the cell makes must be taken away. The delivery and removal is done by the blood . The blood moves around the body inside blood vessels, and the heart pumps constantly to keep it moving."],[9,"what-is-blood","What is blood?","Seen through a microscope, blood is not simply a red liquid. - The liquid part of blood is called blood plasma . It is not red at all — it is a very pale yellow. - Blood looks red because it contains a great many red blood cells floating in the plasma. An adult has at least 20 trillion red blood cells, about five million in every 1 cm³ of blood. - There are also white blood cells . There are far fewer of them, and some kinds are quite a lot bigger than red blood cells. They always have nuclei — red blood cells do not."],[9,"plasma","Plasma","Plasma is mostly water. The red and white blood cells are transported around the body in the plasma, and many substances are dissolved in it. For example: - glucose , carried from the digestive system to every cell - carbon dioxide , produced by respiration in every cell and carried to the lungs, where it diffuses out and is breathed out in expired air."],[9,"red-blood-cells","Red blood cells","Red blood cells are very unusual cells. They have no nucleus and no mitochondria . They are full of a red pigment called haemoglobin , which is what makes blood look red. How the structure of a red blood cell fits its function: - There is no nucleus , to make more room for haemoglobin. - The cytoplasm contains the red pigment haemoglobin , which carries oxygen. - There are no mitochondria , so the cell does not use up the oxygen it is carrying. - Red blood cells are small , so they can squeeze through very tiny capillaries and get close to the alveoli in the lungs and to the respiring cells in the body. Haemoglobin transports oxygen like this: - As blood flows through the tiny capillaries next to the alveoli, oxygen diffuses into the blood and into the red blood cells. - Inside the red blood cell, the oxygen combines with haemoglobin to form a very bright red compound called oxyhaemoglobin . - As the blood travels around the body, it passes cells that are respiring. The oxyhaemoglobin lets go of its oxygen and gives it to those cells. - The blood, which has given away most of its oxygen, travels back to the lungs to collect more."],[9,"white-blood-cells","White blood cells","White blood cells always have nuclei, and some kinds are larger than red blood cells. Some bacteria and viruses cause illness when they get into the body. These bacteria and viruses are called pathogens . White blood cells defend us against pathogens in two different ways. Phagocytosis. Some kinds of white blood cell change their shape and push their cytoplasm out to make 'fingers' that capture a bacterium. The white blood cell then produces chemicals that kill and digest the bacterium. Antibodies. Other types of white blood cell produce chemicals called antibodies that kill pathogens. A different kind of antibody is needed for each different kind of pathogen. The antibodies stick onto the pathogen. Sometimes they kill it directly; sometimes they glue lots of pathogens together so that they cannot move, which makes it easy for other white blood cells to capture and kill them. Component of blood Appearance Function red blood cell red, no nucleus, no mitochondria, small carries oxygen using haemoglobin white blood cell has a nucleus, often larger than a red blood cell destroys pathogens by phagocytosis or by making antibodies plasma pale yellow liquid transports blood cells, glucose, carbon dioxide and other dissolved substances"],[9,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can name the parts of the respiratory system and identify them on a diagram. - I can list the organs that air passes through as it moves into and out of the lungs. - I can describe how oxygen gets into the blood from the air, and how carbon dioxide goes the other way. - I can explain why it is better to have lots of very small air sacs in the lungs rather than a few big ones. - I can describe how to compare the carbon dioxide in inspired air and expired air using limewater. - I can describe how the diaphragm and intercostal muscles cause breathing movements, and explain how these movements make air move into and out of the lungs. - I can explain that respiration is a chemical reaction that releases useful energy from glucose in a controlled way. - I can write the word equation for aerobic respiration. - I can state that respiration happens inside every living cell, and that aerobic respiration uses oxygen and happens inside mitochondria. - I can explain the difference between breathing and respiration. - I can describe what blood plasma is and what it does. - I can explain how red blood cells, containing haemoglobin, transport oxygen. - I can explain how white blood cells help to protect us against pathogens."],[10,"2-1-dissolving","2.1 Dissolving","Key words: conserved · dissolving · opaque · solute · solution · solvent · transparent"],[10,"what-is-a-solution","What is a solution?","When you place a lump of sugar in water, the sugar seems gradually to disappear. The sugar is dissolving . You are left with a colourless solution . - The substance that dissolves is called the solute . - The substance that it dissolves into is called the solvent . A solution is a mixture. In this example the colourless solution is a mixture of sugar and water. Although the sugar seems to disappear, it is still there. The sugar particles have simply spread out among the water particles."],[10,"what-the-particles-do","What the particles do","1. The sugar crystal is visible because it is made of lots of groups of vibrating particles that are tightly packed together. 2. As the water particles vibrate and slide past one another they bump into the vibrating sugar particles. The movement helps to separate the sugar particles and they get mixed up with the water particles. 3. Eventually, the water particles separate all the sugar particles. The sugar particles are no longer in groups and are too small to be seen."],[10,"transparent-and-opaque","Transparent and opaque","All solutions are transparent . This means you can see through them. Transparent does not mean colourless. If you dissolve a coloured salt, such as copper sulfate, the solution formed is blue — but you can still see through it, so it is still transparent. A liquid such as milk is not transparent. You cannot see through it, so it is opaque . Because of this, you can tell that milk is not a solution."],[10,"dissolving-is-not-melting","Dissolving is not melting","It is easy to confuse melting with dissolving. Remember: dissolving needs two substances, a solute and a solvent. Examples of dissolving Examples of melting Sugar (solute) in black tea (solvent) Butter in a frying pan Instant coffee (solute) in hot water (solvent) Ice cream on a warm day Nail polish (solute) in nail polish remover (solvent) Candle wax as the candle burns"],[10,"dissolving-and-mass","Dissolving and mass","In this investigation you measure the mass of the filter paper, then add about 20–25 g of salt and measure the mass of the salt. You then place a beaker containing at least 100 cm³ of water on the top pan balance and measure its mass. Finally you add the salt to the water, stir until all the salt has dissolved, and measure the mass of the beaker and salt solution. When salt is added to water and it dissolves, it has not disappeared. The salt particles are still in the water. The mass of the solution equals the total mass of the solute and solvent. This is true for any solution. mass of solute + mass of solvent = mass of solution No mass has been lost. The mass has been conserved ."],[10,"2-2-solutions-and-solubility","2.2 Solutions and solubility","Key words: concentrated · dilute · insoluble · saturated · solubility · soluble · solution"],[10,"concentrated-and-dilute-solutions","Concentrated and dilute solutions","A solution is made when a solute is dissolved in a solvent. More particles of the solute are dissolved in a concentrated solution than in a dilute solution. You can make solutions of different concentrations by mixing a strong solution with water. For example, 10 cm³ of strong food dye solution makes solution A, while 8 cm³ of dye plus 2 cm³ of water makes solution B. Each solution has the same total volume of 10 cm³, but fewer dye particles each time. Solution Volume of strong food dye solution in cm³ Volume of water in cm³ Total volume in cm³ A 10 0 10 B 8 2 10 C 6 4 10 D 4 6 10 E 2 8 10"],[10,"soluble-insoluble-and-saturated","Soluble, insoluble and saturated","- A solid that dissolves in a solvent such as water is said to be soluble . Sodium chloride (common salt) and sugar are soluble. - A solid that will not dissolve in water is insoluble . Iron filings are insoluble in water. - If you keep adding a soluble solid to a beaker of water, there comes a point where no more of the solid will dissolve. You have made a saturated solution. Some soluble substances are more soluble than others. If you have 100 cm³ of water, you would be able to dissolve a lot of sodium chloride in it, but only a tiny amount of lead chloride. Sodium chloride has greater solubility than lead chloride."],[10,"comparing-solubility","Comparing solubility","To compare the solubility of different solutes you must measure how much of each solute will dissolve in a known amount of the solvent. Solute Solubility in grams of solute in 100 g of water at 20 °C sodium chloride 36 copper sulfate 32 calcium chloride 74 potassium chlorate 7 lead chloride 1"],[10,"temperature-and-solubility","Temperature and solubility","Most solutes will dissolve more quickly and easily in hot water than in cold water. The more energy the particles have, the more they vibrate and move. You can dissolve a greater mass of the solute in hot water than in the same volume of cold water. In other words, as the temperature increases, the solubility of most solutes also increases . For example, in 100 g of water at 20 °C you can dissolve 204 g of sugar. If you heat the water to 80 °C, you can dissolve 362 g of sugar. Worked example: 204 g of sugar dissolves in 100 g of water at 20 °C. - In 200 g of water, twice as much dissolves: 204 × 2 = 408 g. - In 50 g of water: 204 ÷ 100 = 2.04 g per gram of water, so 2.04 × 50 = 102 g. Temperature in °C Potassium nitrate in g per 100 g water Sodium nitrate in g per 100 g water Copper sulfate in g per 100 g water 0 14 73 13 10 21 81 15 20 32 88 17 30 45 96 20 40 63 105 30 50 84 114 35 60 108 124 40 70 136 134 47 80 168 148 56"],[10,"other-solvents","Other solvents","Water is not the only solvent. Some substances that are insoluble in water will dissolve in other solvents. For example, some types of oil paint are not soluble in water, so brushes are cleaned with a solvent that the paint will dissolve in, such as methanol (methylated spirits). Most nail polish remover contains the solvent propanone (acetone)."],[10,"2-3-planning-a-solubility-investigation","2.3 Planning a solubility investigation","Key words: control variables · dependent variable · independent variable · interval · range · variables"],[10,"dissolving-salt-in-water","Dissolving salt in water","Students investigating how temperature affects the amount of salt that will dissolve in water have to think of all the different things that could affect the results. These are the variables . They decide to count the number of spatulas of salt (sodium chloride) that will dissolve in 50 cm³ of water, and to repeat the experiment at different temperatures from 20 °C to 80 °C. - The volume of water is kept the same. - The temperature is changed. - The number of spatulas used is measured."],[10,"naming-the-variables","Naming the variables","- The variable you change is called the independent variable . Here it is the temperature of the water. - The variable you measure is called the dependent variable . Here it is the number of spatulas of salt that dissolve. - The variables you keep the same are the control variables . Here the volume of water is kept the same, to make sure the test is fair. When you plot a graph of your results, the independent variable always goes along the horizontal axis. The dependent variable always goes up the vertical axis."],[10,"range-and-interval","Range and interval","- The range is the highest and lowest values you will use — in this investigation, 20 °C to 80 °C. - The interval is the size of the gap between the values — for example, gaps of 10 degrees or of 5 degrees. Before carrying out an investigation you should plan the variables, the risk assessment, the equipment and a step-by-step method, and prepare a results table with sensible headings and units."],[10,"2-4-paper-chromatography","2.4 Paper chromatography","Key words: chromatogram · paper chromatography · permanent · solvent front"],[10,"colours-in-ink","Colours in ink","Black coloured ink looks as if it is just one colour, but in fact it is a mixture of different coloured inks. You can separate out the coloured inks by using a technique called paper chromatography . Special paper, a bit like filter paper, is used. A small drop of ink is placed on the paper. The water in the beaker soaks up into the paper. As the water moves up the paper, the different coloured inks separate out. The resulting image on the paper is called a chromatogram . The coloured inks separate because the water dissolves them — water is the solvent. As the water moves up the paper, it carries the ink particles with it. The different kinds of ink particles are carried different distances before they are left behind on the paper, because not all the ink particles have the same solubility. The more soluble the ink, the further its particles are carried. Some ink is not soluble in water, such as the ink in permanent marker pens. To separate out the colours in these inks, you would need to use a different solvent, such as alcohol."],[10,"separating-the-colours-in-ink","Separating the colours in ink","1. Take a strip of chromatography paper. Draw a pencil line about 1 cm from the end. 2. Place a spot of ink on the pencil line. The spot should be as small as possible. 3. Dry the spot and then add a little more ink. 4. Place about 2 cm depth of water in a beaker. 5. Hang the paper over a glass rod, pencil or wooden spill so that the end with the ink spot is just in the water. Make sure the ink spot stays above the level of the water. 6. Watch what happens as the water moves up the strip of paper. 7. Remove the strip of paper before the water reaches the top. The wet paper tears easily, so be careful. 8. Allow the strip to dry and then stick it in your book. This is your chromatogram. A pencil line is used because pencil is insoluble, so the line does not run with the solvent."],[10,"comparing-several-substances","Comparing several substances","When comparing different substances, a scientist may use a large piece of chromatography paper and place spots of different items alongside each other, so the solvent moves up through all the samples at the same time. To do this, the paper needs to be placed in a large chromatography tank. Scientists use chromatography to study the dyes used in food. Some food dyes contain only one substance; they are a pure substance. Other dyes contain a mixture of substances. It is important to know exactly what is being used when our food is processed, because some substances could be a health risk — they could be toxic or cause allergic reactions. A dye that gives only one spot is a pure substance. A dye that gives more than one spot is a mixture."],[10,"checking-that-dyes-are-permitted","Checking that dyes are permitted","Public health scientists also use chromatography to check that the colourings used in products such as hair dye or the ink in pens are not harmful. To do this they compare a chromatogram taken from a solution of the food, hair dye or ink with a chromatogram of the colourings that are permitted. Any spot on the sample chromatogram that does not line up with a spot on the chromatogram of permitted dyes is not permitted."],[10,"is-the-green-colour-in-plant-leaves-pure","Is the green colour in plant leaves pure?","Ethanol is used instead of water because the green pigment in leaves dissolves in ethanol but not in water. When using ethanol, make sure you are in a well-ventilated room and there are no heat sources close to you. 1. Add a handful of spinach and a few drops of ethanol into the mortar. Use the pestle to crush the spinach and ethanol together. Let it stand for about 10 minutes so that the green pigment dissolves in the ethanol. 2. Prepare the chromatography paper with a pencil line and a cross, about 1 cm from the end. 3. Use a pipette to load some of the green liquid from the mortar onto the cross. Allow the spot to dry before adding more of the liquid. 4. Place the chromatography paper over a glass rod or pencil. Hang it in a beaker containing some ethanol, so that the pencil line is just above the ethanol. 5. Watch carefully and remove the chromatography paper before the ethanol reaches the top of the paper. The point that the solvent reaches is called the solvent front ."],[10,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can use the terms 'solvent', 'solute' and 'solution' appropriately. - I can use particle theory to explain some of the properties of solutions. - I can measure mass and volume of liquids accurately. - I can describe how to make solutions of different concentrations. - I can compare the number of solute particles in solutions of different concentrations. - I can compare the solubility of various solutes. - I can identify different types of variables. - I can plan and carry out an investigation safely. - I can describe how to use chromatography to separate dissolved substances. - I can explain what the results of a chromatogram show. - I can use scientific language accurately."],[11,"3-1-forces-and-motion","3.1 Forces and motion","Key words: balanced · change direction · direction · force · opposite · slow down · speed up · unbalanced"],[11,"balanced-or-unbalanced","Balanced or unbalanced?","Look at any object that is not moving. You may think that if an object is not moving, no forces are acting on it. This is not true. The force of gravity pulls the rock towards the centre of the Earth. This force is called its weight . The rock does not move downwards because the ground pushes up on the rock. This upward push is the contact force . These two forces are balanced . Balanced means the forces are equal in size and opposite in direction . There can be more than two forces acting on the rock. Imagine the wind is blowing. The wind pushes the rock from one side, but the rock does not move sideways because the push from the wind is balanced by friction between the rock and the ground. Forces are shown on a force diagram . In a force diagram, the arrows show the size and direction of each force. The longer the arrow, the bigger the force. So when you draw balanced forces, make the arrows the same length and point them in opposite directions."],[11,"starting-to-move","Starting to move","Now imagine the rock is pushed with a much larger force than the wind, such as a push from a large vehicle. The pushing force is now larger than friction. The sideways forces are unbalanced , so the rock starts to move in the direction of the larger sideways force. The rock still does not move up or down, because the up and down forces are still balanced."],[11,"slowing-down","Slowing down","Unbalanced forces can also make a moving object slow down. A parachute makes a falling object slow down. When the object is falling quickly, the parachute causes a force of air resistance that is larger than the weight of the object. The forces are unbalanced, so the object slows down. As the object slows, the air resistance decreases. Eventually air resistance equals weight, the forces are balanced again, and the object falls at a constant speed ."],[11,"changing-direction","Changing direction","Unbalanced forces can also make an object change direction. When the ball hits the racket, the ball pushes on the racket and the racket pushes on the ball. To send the ball back in the opposite direction, the hitting force on the ball must be larger than the force from the ball on the racket. Planets orbit the Sun because of the force of gravity. The force of gravity on a planet is a constant, unbalanced force . When an object moves in a circle, its direction is always changing. A constant unbalanced force is needed to keep an object moving in a circle. An aeroplane flying at a constant speed in a straight line has four forces acting on it: lift and weight are balanced, and thrust and drag are balanced."],[11,"summary","Summary","- When forces are equal in size and opposite in direction , the forces are balanced . - Balanced forces cause no change in movement. - When forces are not equal in size, and/or act in directions that are not opposite, the forces are unbalanced . - Unbalanced forces cause a change in movement : speeding up, slowing down, or changing direction. Size of forces Direction of forces Balanced or unbalanced Change in movement Equal Opposite Balanced None Equal Not opposite Unbalanced Change of direction Not equal Opposite Unbalanced Increase or decrease speed Not equal Not opposite Unbalanced Change of speed and of direction Remember: an object that is not moving still has forces acting on it. They are simply balanced."],[11,"3-2-speed","3.2 Speed","Key words: average speed · calculate · constant · m/s · metres per second · speed"],[11,"units-of-speed","Units of speed","There are many different units of speed. The speed of ships is often measured in knots, and aeroplanes often use Mach. Some countries set road speed limits in kilometres per hour, others in miles per hour. To avoid confusion, scientists use standard units in all countries. The standard unit of speed is the metre per second , written m/s . The word per means 'in each'. So metres per second means the number of metres travelled in each second. A horse running at a speed of 15 m/s travels 15 metres in each second."],[11,"calculating-speed","Calculating speed","Think of a bus that travels 100 m in 20 s. How many metres does it travel in each 1 second? number of metres travelled in each second = total distance travelled ÷ total time The number of metres travelled in each second is the speed, so: speed = distance ÷ time For the bus: speed = 100 m ÷ 20 s = 5 m/s The speed of an object during a journey is not always constant . Constant means not changing. When the speed changes, we use the term average speed . Average speed is calculated in exactly the same way."],[11,"using-the-formula-triangle","Using the formula triangle","The same equation can be rearranged to find distance or time: - distance = speed × time - time = distance ÷ speed Worked example 1. Marcus rides his bicycle at 4 m/s for 60 s. How far does he go? distance = speed × time = 4 × 60 = 240 m Worked example 2. Sofia is in a car travelling at 35 m/s. How long will the car take to travel 2100 m? time = distance ÷ speed = 2100 ÷ 35 = 60 s Worked example 3. An aeroplane travels 2500 km in 5 hours. What is its speed? speed = distance ÷ time = 2500 ÷ 5 = 500 km/h The units you put in decide the units you get out. If the distance is in km and the time is in hours, the speed comes out in km/h. Unless you are told otherwise, always work in metres, seconds and metres per second. Remember: in every calculation, show the steps of your working and include the correct unit with your answer."],[11,"3-3-describing-movement","3.3 Describing movement","Key words: at rest · distance–time graph · sketch · stationary"],[11,"distance-time-graphs","Distance–time graphs","Scientists use graphs to describe how two variables are related. To describe the movement of an object, plot the distance travelled on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis . A graph like this is called a distance–time graph . Graphs are more useful than words for describing movement because: - it is easier to see trends and patterns - you can read any value of distance or time during the journey - other values, such as speed, can be calculated from the graph - information about the whole journey can be seen at once. This graph shows the journey of a car from a starting position A to a destination C, and then back to A. Look at each of the four sections in turn. 1. A to B — constant speed away from the start. The car travels the same distance in each second, so the distance from the start increases steadily with time. The line is straight and sloping upwards . 2. At B — stationary. The car stops. Stationary means not moving, with a speed of zero; you can also say the car is at rest . The distance from the start does not change, but time still passes. The line is straight and horizontal . 3. B to C — faster constant speed. The car travels a greater distance in each second than it did between A and B, so the line is straight, sloping upwards and steeper . 4. C back to A — returning. The distance from the start decreases with time. The line is straight and sloping downwards . A sketch graph does not need numbers on its axes. It only has to show the correct shape."],[11,"reading-values-from-a-graph","Reading values from a graph","When a distance–time graph does have values on the axes, you can use it to calculate speed. Read the distance from the vertical axis and the time from the horizontal axis, then use speed = distance ÷ time. A train travels from station P to station R, a distance of 2000 m, in 200 s. It waits at R for 140 s, then returns to P in 100 s. - Speed from P to R = 2000 ÷ 200 = 10 m/s - Speed from R back to P = 2000 ÷ 100 = 20 m/s The return line is steeper because the train is travelling faster. Remember: on a distance–time graph, a steeper line means a faster speed , and a horizontal line means the object is stationary ."],[11,"3-4-turning-forces","3.4 Turning forces","Key words: lever · moment · newton metre · pivot · turn"],[11,"turning-effects-of-forces","Turning effects of forces","When you push down on a door handle, the handle turns. When you push down on a bicycle pedal, the pedal turns. When you pull a door, the door turns towards you. These are all forces that cause an object to turn . The object that turns is called a lever . The point around which the lever turns is called the pivot . When you bend your arm, the arm acts as a lever and your elbow is the pivot. The human body has many levers and pivots."],[11,"calculating-moments","Calculating moments","The moment of a force describes the turning effect of that force. The moment depends on two things: - the size of the force — the bigger the force, the bigger the moment - the distance from the pivot to where the force acts — the greater the distance, the greater the moment. moment = force × distance Here, distance means the distance from the pivot to the position where the force acts. The unit of force is the newton (N) and the unit of distance is the metre (m), so the unit of moment is the newton metre , written N m . Use an upper case N and a lower case m. Worked example 1. A pulling force of 35 N is needed to open a door. The distance from the handle to the hinges (the pivot) is 0.8 m. What is the moment? moment = force × distance = 35 × 0.8 = 28 N m Worked example 2. A weight of 20 N is held 0.25 m from the pivot at the elbow. What moment does the weight cause? moment = force × distance = 20 × 0.25 = 5 N m"],[11,"balancing","Balancing","A seesaw is a type of lever. People sit on either side of the pivot and make the lever turn one way and then the other. A seesaw is balanced when the moments on both sides of the pivot are equal and opposite . Worked example 3. Marcus has a weight of 600 N and sits 2 m from the pivot. Arun has a weight of 800 N. Where should Arun sit so the seesaw balances? Marcus's moment = 600 × 2 = 1200 N m For balance, Arun's moment must also be 1200 N m. distance = moment ÷ force = 1200 ÷ 800 = 1.5 m from the pivot Remember: a force pushing or pulling straight towards the pivot has no turning effect. The force must act at a distance from the pivot to produce a moment."],[11,"3-5-pressure-between-solids","3.5 Pressure between solids","Key words: newtons per square metre · point · pressure · sharp · surface area"],[11,"the-pushing-effect-of-a-force","The pushing effect of a force","A knife works because the force used to push down on the clay causes pressure on the clay. You can think of pressure as the pushing effect of a force . Suppose the clay is difficult to cut. You could: - increase the force on the knife — as the force increases, the pressure increases - use a sharper knife — a sharp knife has less surface area in contact with the clay, and as the area decreases, the pressure increases. pressure = force ÷ area The unit of force is the newton and the unit of area is the metre squared, so the unit of pressure is newtons per metre squared, N/m² . Smaller areas are sometimes measured in cm² or mm². If the area is in cm², the pressure is in N/cm². If the area is in mm², the pressure is in N/mm²."],[11,"large-areas-and-small-areas","Large areas and small areas","Some things have large areas to decrease pressure ; others have small areas to increase pressure . A camel has large feet, so the force from its weight acts over a large area. The pressure on the sand is decreased, so the camel does not sink. On a bed of nails, the weight of the person acts on many hundreds of sharp points , so the pressure from each nail is very small. The end of a pin has a sharp point with a very small area. The pressure is increased, so the pin goes easily into wood or card. Scissors have sharp blades. The area along each cutting edge is small, which increases the pressure and makes things easier to cut."],[11,"calculating-pressure","Calculating pressure","Worked example 1. An elephant has a weight of 50 000 N. The total area of all four feet is 0.4 m². What pressure does it exert on the ground? pressure = force ÷ area = 50 000 ÷ 0.4 = 125 000 N/m² The unit is N/m² because the area was given in m². Worked example 2. A bicycle and rider together weigh 1000 N, supported equally on two tyres. The area of each tyre in contact with the ground is 5 cm². What pressure does each tyre exert? weight on each tyre = 1000 ÷ 2 = 500 N pressure = force ÷ area = 500 ÷ 5 = 100 N/cm² The unit is N/cm² because the area was given in cm². Worked example 3. A nail has a point of area 1.5 mm². A pressure of 50 N/mm² is needed for the nail to go into wood. What force is needed from the hammer? force = pressure × area = 50 × 1.5 = 75 N Remember: the unit of pressure comes from the units you put in. Force in N with area in cm² gives pressure in N/cm²."],[11,"3-6-pressure-in-liquids-and-gases","3.6 Pressure in liquids and gases","Key words: altitude · atmospheric pressure · collide · container · depth · sea level"],[11,"pressure-in-liquids","Pressure in liquids","Look at the dam. Water comes out from two levels. The water coming out near the top comes out with less force. The water coming out from further down comes out with more force. This is because pressure in a liquid increases with depth . The wall of the dam is wider at the bottom to make it stronger where the pressure from the water is greatest."],[11,"pressure-and-depth-in-liquids","Pressure and depth in liquids","Why does pressure increase with depth ? As you go deeper in a liquid, there is more liquid above your position. The weight of this liquid, caused by gravity, pushes on the particles of the liquid below it. When the particles of the liquid are pushed, they move with more force. The particles in a liquid move randomly in all directions, so the pressure in a liquid is equal in all directions at a given depth."],[11,"pressure-in-gases","Pressure in gases","Before you blow up a balloon there is only a small volume of air inside it. The balloon is open at one end, so the pressure inside is the same as the pressure outside. As you blow air in, you are adding more gas particles. Particles in a gas move randomly and collide with the walls of the container . Every time a particle collides with the wall, it exerts a small force on the wall. The more particles there are in the gas, the more collisions happen with the walls, and so the total force on the walls increases. That force acts on an area, so it causes pressure. The pressure inside the balloon gets bigger as you blow in more air, pushing the walls of the balloon outwards. The air inside this tyre is at low pressure — not enough to support the weight of the vehicle. If more air is put inside, more collisions happen with the inside walls, the pressure increases, and the tyre is pushed outwards so that it supports the vehicle."],[11,"pressure-and-depth-in-gases","Pressure and depth in gases","As with liquids, the pressure in a gas increases with depth. Altitude is height above sea level . The Earth's atmosphere is made of gases, and sea level is where the atmosphere is at its deepest. So atmospheric pressure is highest at sea level . As you go higher in the atmosphere, two things change: - the number of particles in each cubic metre of air decreases, so the concentration of gas particles decreases - the weight of air above your position decreases. Both changes make atmospheric pressure smaller at high altitude. Atmospheric pressure at sea level is about 100 000 N/m². That is like the weight of two large elephants pushing on every square metre of surface. We are not aware of it because we live in the atmosphere all the time, and the pressure inside our bodies is the same."],[11,"effects-of-atmospheric-pressure","Effects of atmospheric pressure","The effect of atmospheric pressure can be shown by pumping the air out of a metal container. - Before the air is pumped out, the pressure inside the container equals the pressure outside. - When the air is pumped out, the pressure inside becomes close to zero, but the pressure outside does not change. - The container is crushed by the pressure of the air outside."],[11,"pressure-and-temperature-in-gases","Pressure and temperature in gases","As the temperature of a gas increases, the speed of the particles increases . Particles travelling faster exert more force in each collision, and collide more often. So increasing the temperature of a gas in a sealed container increases its pressure . A pressure cooker uses this effect. Water and food are put inside and the lid is sealed. When the water boils the steam cannot escape, so the pressure of the gas inside increases and the food cooks faster. A weight on top controls the pressure, and valves let steam escape once the required pressure is reached. Remember: pressure in a gas can be increased in three ways — add more particles, make the container smaller, or raise the temperature."],[11,"3-7-particles-on-the-move","3.7 Particles on the move","Key words: concentration · diffusion"],[11,"mixing-gases","Mixing gases","The particles in a gas move randomly. Both the speed of the particles and the direction of the particles are random. If two gases are mixed in one container, the particles of each gas move randomly, so each gas spreads out to fill the container . This movement of the particles of each gas is called diffusion . Diffusion is the overall random movement of particles from an area where they are in higher concentration to an area where they are in lower concentration . Concentration is the number of particles in a particular volume. 1. At the start, the tap is closed. One gas is at high concentration on the left and zero concentration on the right. The other gas is at high concentration on the right and zero concentration on the left. 2. The tap is opened and the gases start to spread out. At random, some particles of each gas pass through the space where the tap opens. Each gas is now at quite high concentration on its own side and low concentration on the other side. 3. After some time the gases have completely diffused. There is equal concentration of both gases on both sides. Diffusion stops when the concentrations are equal. However, the movement of individual particles does not stop when diffusion stops. Particles still cross from side to side — but now equal numbers cross each way, so there is no overall change."],[11,"smelling-food","Smelling food","Diffusion explains how you can smell food cooking. When food is heated, some particles in the food change state and become a gas. These gas particles move randomly and so spread out through the air by diffusion. The smell gets stronger as you move closer to the food , because the concentration of the particles is higher the closer you get to the source of the particles. Many animals use this change in the strength of a smell to find food."],[11,"mixing-liquids","Mixing liquids","Diffusion also happens in liquids, because particles in a liquid also move at random. When blue ink is added to water, the ink moves from the area of high concentration to the areas of low concentration , until it is completely diffused throughout the water."],[11,"speed-of-diffusion","Speed of diffusion","The speed of diffusion depends on two things: - the difference in concentration of the particles — the bigger the difference in the concentrations, the faster the diffusion - temperature — the higher the temperature, the faster the diffusion. Higher temperature makes particles move faster, so the particles can spread out faster. For example, when you make a cup of tea the tea diffuses through the water. It diffuses faster in hot water than in cold water. Remember: diffusion needs nothing to push the particles along. The particles were already moving at random; there are simply more of them starting in the crowded region, so more of them move out than move in."],[11,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can describe what is meant by balanced forces in terms of size and direction. - I can understand that forces can be acting on an object that is not moving. - I can recognise when forces are unbalanced. - I can list some of the effects of unbalanced forces. - I know and can use the equation that links speed, distance and time. - I can use the equation to calculate speed, distance or time when I know the other two quantities. - I know that the standard units are metres, seconds and metres per second. - I can calculate speeds using different units. - I can sketch a distance–time graph for an object moving at a constant speed away from a starting position. - I can sketch a distance–time graph for a stationary object. - I can sketch a distance–time graph for an object moving back towards a starting position. - I can tell whether an object is moving quickly, slowly or is stationary from a distance–time graph. - I can read values from a distance–time graph and use them to calculate speed. - I can understand that forces can cause turning effects. - I can understand what is meant by the term moment. - I know and can use the equation that links moment, force and distance. - I know the unit of moment. - I can understand that pressure is the pushing effect of a force. - I know and can use the equation that links pressure, force and area. - I can work out the unit of pressure from the units of force and area. - I can understand what causes pressure in a liquid and how it changes with depth. - I can understand what causes pressure in a gas. - I can understand how altitude affects atmospheric pressure. - I can understand how the quantity of gas in a container affects the pressure. - I can understand how the temperature of a gas in a closed container affects the pressure. - I know how to describe diffusion. - I can understand how diffusion happens in gases. - I can understand how diffusion happens in liquids. - I can understand that the speed of diffusion is affected by the difference in concentration and by the temperature."],[12,"4-1-the-sonoran-desert","4.1 The Sonoran Desert","Key words: adaptations · ecology · ecosystem · environment · food web · habitat · interact · nectar · nocturnal · pollen · pollinating"],[12,"a-desert-in-arizona","A desert in Arizona","Deserts are not easy places for animals and plants to live. Deserts do not get much rainfall, so the organisms that live there must have adaptations that help them to survive with little water. The tall plants in the photograph are saguaro cacti. They grow very slowly, and some are more than 100 years old. Their roots spread out widely just underneath the soil, ready to absorb any rain that falls. Many animals live among the cacti and other desert plants. - Gila woodpeckers make holes in the cacti to make their nests. Other birds also visit these holes. - Cactus wrens nest in a different kind of cactus, the teddy bear cholla. It is so spiky that very few other animals will get close to it, so the wren's eggs and young are protected from predators. During the hot days, lizards, tortoises and other animals rest in the shade of the plants, or burrow into the soil where it is cooler. At night, when the temperature falls, kangaroo rats come out to feed, wary of predators such as rattlesnakes and coyotes. In the Sonoran Desert it usually rains heavily at least once a year. When the rains come, the desert is transformed. Many plants quickly produce flowers. Insects feed on the nectar and pollen in the flowers, pollinating them and helping the plants to reproduce. At night, bats feed on nectar from the flowers of agave plants. Seeds fall to the ground and are collected by ants, to take into their nests as a food store. Many months or years later, some of the uneaten seeds may germinate to produce new plants."],[12,"interactions-in-the-sonoran-desert","Interactions in the Sonoran Desert","All the different animals and plants in the desert depend on each other. They interact with each other: the actions of one organism affect another."],[12,"non-living-things-in-the-desert","Non-living things in the desert","It is not only other organisms that affect the plants and animals. There are also interactions between the organisms and the non-living parts of their environment . Non-living factor How it affects the organisms Light Bright sunlight helps the plants to photosynthesise, producing food that other organisms can eat. Temperature Often very high by day but much lower at night. Some animals are nocturnal , which helps them avoid overheating or drying out. Soil Rocks and soil provide minerals for plants to grow, and building material for ground-nesting birds. It is cooler under the soil, so some animals dig burrows for shelter during the day. Water All organisms need water to keep their cells alive. Rain, when it comes, allows them to become more active and to reproduce. Air The desert air provides carbon dioxide for photosynthesis, and oxygen for all the organisms to use in respiration. The organisms also affect their environment. For example, droppings from the kangaroo rats become part of the soil, and the gases the animals take in and give out affect the composition of the air."],[12,"the-desert-ecosystem","The desert ecosystem","Everything in the desert interacts with everything else. All of these interactions make up the desert ecosystem. Remember: an ecosystem is a network of interactions between all the living organisms and the non-living things around them. Some of the interactions in an ecosystem involve food webs . Plants are the producers in a food web — they use energy in sunlight to make food. As animals eat the desert plants and each other, the energy is passed through the food web. Food webs are a very important part of the interactions in an ecosystem, but they are not the only ones. For example: - plants provide places for some animals to make nests - plant roots help to hold the soil together, so it is not washed away when it rains - animals help plants to reproduce by pollinating their flowers or spreading their seeds. The interactions in an ecosystem are usually very complicated. The study of ecosystems is called ecology . No ecologist would ever claim to have discovered all of the different interactions in an ecosystem."],[12,"habitats-in-a-desert-ecosystem","Habitats in a desert ecosystem","The place where an organism naturally lives is called its habitat . There are many different places to live in a desert. Organism Habitat Saguaro cactus the open desert Gila woodpecker a saguaro cactus (nest), and the air and ground in the open desert (food) Desert ant underneath rocks and soil, and on the soil surface Termite the base of the saguaro stems Sap beetle inside the saguaro flowers Kangaroo rat burrows, coming out to look for food at night Remember: a habitat is a place where an organism lives. An ecosystem is the whole network of interactions between the organisms and their non-living surroundings. One ecosystem contains many habitats."],[12,"4-2-different-ecosystems","4.2 Different ecosystems","Key words: ecosystem · habitat"],[12,"more-about-ecosystems","More about ecosystems","The ecosystem in the Sonoran Desert is just one of many different ecosystems on Earth. Here are three more examples."],[12,"mangrove-forest","Mangrove forest","- Mangroves are trees that can grow with their roots in sea water. They form forests along the coasts of many tropical countries. - Young fish live among the mangrove roots, safe from larger fish that might eat them. Mud skippers climb out onto the mud when the tide is out, feeding on whatever they can find. - As the mangrove leaves fall onto the mud, they are decomposed by bacteria. Prawns and crabs eat the partly decomposed leaves. - Crab-eating macaques, a type of monkey, climb through the trees and catch crabs on the tree roots and mud."],[12,"sea-ice-in-the-arctic-ocean","Sea ice in the Arctic Ocean","- During the winter in the Arctic Ocean, it is so cold that some of the sea water freezes. - Seals hunt for fish in the water, but have to come to the surface to breathe air. - Polar bears patrol the ice, looking for seals to kill and eat. Polar bears are good swimmers and can move from one ice floe to another. - Arctic foxes also look for food on the ice. - Enough light passes through the ice to allow tiny algae (single-celled plants) to grow on the underside of the ice floes. Tiny shrimp-like organisms eat the algae, and fish eat the shrimps."],[12,"rice-paddy","Rice paddy","- Not all ecosystems are natural. This area of rice paddies in Malaysia is farmed by people. - At some times of year the paddy fields are flooded with water. Algae grow in this shallow water, and on the mud at the sides of the flooded areas. - Fish swim into the flooded paddies from the irrigation canals. Frogs and dragonflies breed in the water. - Because the water is shallow, it heats up quickly during the day and cools down quickly at night. - Farmers often add fertiliser to the paddy fields. This makes not only the rice but also the algae grow faster, providing more food for the animals. - Many birds feed in and around the paddy fields."],[12,"investigating-a-local-ecosystem","Investigating a local ecosystem","You can study an ecosystem near your school — a garden area, part of a playing field, a group of trees or a pond. Apparatus What it is used for Pitfall trap A plastic cup set into the ground. Small animals that crawl or run over the surface, such as beetles, fall in and cannot get out. Check it regularly, or you may find just one extremely fat carnivorous beetle and nothing else. Sweep net A large net on a long pole. Sweep it through long grass to catch insects, or use it in a pond or small stream. Beating tray Gently hit a branch with a stick while others hold a large sheet underneath, to find insects living in the lower branches of trees. Hand lens Looking at very small organisms. Camera Photographing organisms you cannot name, so you can identify them later. Keys and books Identifying the animals and plants that you find. A good method is: 1. Describe the ecosystem — how big it is, what plants grow there, whether it is light or shady, damp or dry. 2. Find the different habitats in it, such as underneath stones, in the soil, on the soil surface, on leaves, in flowers, on tree trunks and in the air. 3. List the organisms living in each habitat, and note what the animals are doing. 4. If possible, visit at different times of day — or at night — to see how the ecosystem changes. 5. Draw a diagram of the interactions you saw, between organisms and between organisms and the non-living parts of the environment. Remember: if you catch any animals you must take great care of them. Do not take them away from their habitat — release them exactly where you found them."],[12,"4-3-intruders-in-an-ecosystem","4.3 Intruders in an ecosystem","Key words: eradicate · extinct · native species"],[12,"new-species-in-an-ecosystem","New species in an ecosystem","All the organisms in an ecosystem interact with each other and with their environment. So what happens if a new species suddenly arrives? How does it fit into the network of interactions, and how does that affect the species already there? A native species is a species that belongs naturally in that place. A species brought in from somewhere else is an introduced species. An introduced species that spreads and causes harm is called an invasive species."],[12,"introduced-species-in-new-zealand","Introduced species in New Zealand","New Zealand became separated from all the other areas of land in the world about 66 million years ago. Because of this separation, the species that developed there were different from those elsewhere on Earth. Before humans arrived, there were no predatory mammals in New Zealand. Many of the native birds nest on the ground, because there were no predators to eat their eggs. Several native birds, such as the kiwi, cannot even fly. Humans probably arrived about 700 years ago, and brought other species with them. Species Why it arrived What happened Rats Stowed away on boats Rats now live in most of the country. They eat birds' eggs and defenceless young birds. Sheep Brought by farmers for wool and meat Farmed in pastures. Rabbits Brought on sailing ships as food They escaped and began to eat the grass in the sheep pastures. Stoats Brought from Europe to control the rabbits Stoats spread all over New Zealand. Stoats are fierce hunters and breed rapidly. They can kill and eat birds much larger than themselves. Stoats have made several species of native bird extinct , including the laughing owl and the New Zealand thrush, and they eat almost 60% of kiwi chicks. People in New Zealand are now trying to eradicate stoats — that is, to get rid of them completely — but this is very difficult to do. The best that can be done is to control their numbers. Scientists think that 53 species of native bird in New Zealand have become extinct since humans arrived. The extinctions were partly caused by people hunting the birds, but mostly by introduced invasive species. Remember: a species is often harmless in its native ecosystem because predators, diseases and competitors keep its numbers down. In a new ecosystem those controls may be missing, so its numbers can grow very quickly."],[12,"buffelgrass-in-the-sonoran-desert","Buffelgrass in the Sonoran Desert","Buffelgrass is native to Africa, Asia and the Middle East. It was planted in Arizona in the 1930s as food for cattle, and it is now spreading rapidly through the Sonoran Desert. It grows in dense patches and takes water and nutrients from the soil, so the native desert plants — including saguaro cacti — have less water and fewer nutrients. If the native plants suffer, so do the animals that depend on them."],[12,"4-4-bioaccumulation","4.4 Bioaccumulation","Key words: accumulate · bioaccumulation · biomagnification · insecticide · persistent · toxic"],[12,"ddt","DDT","DDT is an insecticide . This means that it kills insects. DDT was first produced in the 1940s. It was used to kill insects that transmit diseases — especially mosquitoes that transmit malaria, and fleas that transmit a disease called typhus. It was also used to kill insects that eat crops. No one thought that DDT could harm organisms other than insects. But gradually people began to realise that it was also harming animals that no one wanted to kill. In 1962 an American author called Rachel Carson wrote a book called Silent Spring , describing how DDT was killing not only mosquitoes but also birds. Her book made many people realise that some insecticides are very harmful to the environment."],[12,"ddt-in-food-chains","DDT in food chains","We now know that DDT does not break down. It is a persistent chemical: - it stays in the environment for many years - it is not broken down by decomposers - when it is sprayed, some of it is carried high into the air and can be blown very long distances, far away from where it was used - when it gets into an animal's body it stays there for the whole life of the organism. DDT is toxic (poisonous) to many kinds of animal. For example, it makes the shells of birds' eggs very thin and easy to break."],[12,"bioaccumulation-and-biomagnification","Bioaccumulation and biomagnification","Imagine that DDT has been sprayed onto some water. Tiny protoctists take up some of the DDT. Shrimps eat the protoctists, fish eat the shrimps, and cormorants (fish-eating birds) eat the fish. All the DDT in all of the protoctists that a shrimp eats over its lifetime accumulates , or builds up, in its body. The longer the organism lives, and the more DDT it takes in, the more DDT it gets in its body. This process is called bioaccumulation . All of the DDT in all of the shrimps that a fish eats accumulates in the fish's body. Eventually, all the DDT in all of the fish that a cormorant eats in its lifetime accumulates in the cormorant's body. This means that the concentration of DDT in an animal's body increases as you go up the food chain. This is called biomagnification . The concentration is measured in parts per million (ppm). This is the number of grams of DDT in one million grams of the organism. Part of the food chain Concentration of DDT / ppm water 0.000 05 protoctists 0.04 shrimps 0.16 minnows 0.50 cormorants 26.40 Remember: bioaccumulation is the build-up of a substance inside one organism over its lifetime. Biomagnification is the increase in concentration along a food chain , from one level to the next."],[12,"biodegradable-insecticides","Biodegradable insecticides","Some insecticides are biodegradable, which means they can be broken down by microorganisms in the environment or inside an animal's body. A biodegradable insecticide does not stay in an animal's body for its whole life, so it does not build up along a food chain in the same way. Other substances that do not break down, such as microplastics, can build up in the environment in a similar way to DDT."],[12,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can describe some of the interactions between the organisms in a desert ecosystem. - I can describe some of the interactions between the organisms and the non-living parts of the environment in a desert ecosystem. - I can name some of the different habitats in a desert ecosystem. - I can explain the difference between a habitat and an ecosystem. - I can describe some of the habitats and interactions in other ecosystems, such as a mangrove forest, Arctic sea ice and a rice paddy. - I can use different methods to find out about habitats and interactions in an ecosystem near my school. - I can explain how new or invasive species can affect an ecosystem. - I can describe examples of invasive species and their effects. - I can explain what is meant by bioaccumulation. - I can explain why DDT shows bioaccumulation. - I can explain why organisms at the top of a food chain have higher concentrations of DDT in their bodies than organisms at the base of the food chain."],[13,"5-1-the-structure-of-the-atom","5.1 The structure of the atom","Key words: electron · electrostatic attraction · neutron · nucleus · proton · sub-atomic particles"],[13,"atoms","Atoms","In Stage 7 you learnt that atoms are so small that you cannot see them without using the most powerful microscopes yet invented. The word 'atom' comes from a Greek word that means 'cannot be split'. All the atoms of a particular element are the same. Different elements have different atoms."],[13,"what-is-an-atom-like","What is an atom like?","Scientists have discovered that atoms are made up of even smaller particles, called sub-atomic particles . Atoms are made up of three kinds of particle: protons , neutrons and electrons . The particles are arranged in a similar way in all atoms. The protons and neutrons are grouped closely together in the centre of the atom. They form the nucleus of the atom. Be careful not to confuse the nucleus of a cell with the nucleus of an atom. The electrons move around the nucleus. The three different particles in an atom have different properties. Particle Where it is Relative mass Electrical charge Proton in the nucleus same as a neutron positive (+) Neutron in the nucleus same as a proton none Electron moving around the nucleus almost no mass negative (−) - Protons and neutrons have much more mass than the electrons. In fact, electrons have almost no mass. - Protons and neutrons have the same mass. - Protons have a positive electrical charge. - Neutrons have no electrical charge. - Electrons have a negative electrical charge. There is a lot of empty space between the parts of the atom. This space really is completely empty — there is nothing in it at all. There is an attraction between the positive and negative charges. This electrostatic attraction between the positive charge on the protons and the negative charge on the electrons is what holds individual atoms together. Remember: in a neutral atom the number of protons equals the number of electrons, so the positive and negative charges cancel and the overall charge is zero."],[13,"how-did-scientists-come-up-with-this-model","How did scientists come up with this model?","Scientists from many different parts of the world have worked on a number of different ideas that have led to the model of the atom that they use today. In the late 1890s a British scientist called J. J. Thompson discovered the electron. His model for the atom was that the electrons were scattered throughout the structure of the atom. It is sometimes called the 'plum pudding model' because the particles are arranged randomly throughout the model, like fruit in a cake or pudding. One of Thompson's students was originally from New Zealand. His name was Ernest Rutherford. Rutherford discovered the proton in 1909 and the nucleus in 1911. Rutherford's most famous experiment was the gold foil experiment. In this experiment Rutherford fired fast moving particles, smaller than an atom, at very thin gold foil. Most of the particles passed straight through the foil. Only a few of the particles (about 1 in 8000) were deflected in various directions. Deflected means that the direction of the particle was changed. The results of this experiment led Rutherford to have the idea that the gold atoms must be mostly empty space, with their particles packed into a dense nucleus at the centre. This helped to move towards the model of the atom that scientists use today. James Chadwick also worked with Rutherford and Thompson. In 1932, he proved that neutrons exist. This discovery moved the model of the atom closer to the one scientists use today. Scientist Discovery When J. J. Thompson the electron late 1890s Ernest Rutherford the proton 1909 Ernest Rutherford the nucleus 1911 James Chadwick the neutron 1932 The stories of these scientists show how people from all over the world work together to develop their ideas. Each scientist builds on the ideas and the discoveries of others. Chadwick, Rutherford and Thompson won Nobel prizes for their work. There is still a lot we do not know about atoms. Scientists continue to work to improve our understanding of the structure of the atom. For example, scientists from all over the world are using the Large Hadron Collider in Switzerland to further understand the structure of matter."],[13,"5-2-purity","5.2 Purity","Key words: carat · purity · suggest · translucent"],[13,"pure-elements","Pure elements","If an element is pure , it means that every one of its atoms is exactly the same and made of just one type of atom. For example, pure gold is made of gold atoms. However, an alloy of gold may contain other metals such as copper or silver. When you buy gold it is usually marked to state if it is pure gold (24 carat ) or an alloy such as 18 carat or 9 carat. This is a measure of its purity. The more gold it has, the higher its purity. 18 carat gold has 18 parts out of 24 that are gold; the rest (6 parts out of the 24) is made up of other metals such as silver or copper. $$\\frac{18}{24} \\times 100 = 75\\%$$ So 18 carat gold has a purity of 75%. You can see that the colour changes from slightly coppery to yellow-gold as the purity increases. When silver is sold, it is usually marked with the number of parts per thousand that are silver. So, silver marked 925 has 925 atoms out of 1000 that are silver and 75 atoms of some other metal. Silver marked 900 is of lower purity than that marked 925. $$\\frac{925}{1000} \\times 100 = 92.5\\%$$ So the ring is 92.5% pure silver. Remember: percentage purity = (parts that are the pure substance ÷ total parts) × 100."],[13,"diamonds","Diamonds","Diamonds are made from the element carbon. The carbon atoms are arranged in a particular way. If they are pure, diamonds contain no other elements. Pure diamonds are colourless and translucent (they let the light through). If diamonds have other elements mixed in with the carbon atoms, they can be different colours. Colour What causes it Colourless pure carbon only Yellow a few carbon atoms per million replaced by nitrogen Blue some carbon atoms replaced by boron Green about one carbon atom per 1000 replaced by nitrogen (the rarest of all)"],[13,"seawater","Seawater","Seawater is made up of water and various salts such as sodium chloride. If you want to obtain sodium chloride from seawater you can evaporate off the water. In some countries this is done by letting seawater fill flat and shallow areas called beds, and allowing the water to evaporate in the heat from the Sun. If you are trying to obtain pure sodium chloride this will be a problem, because sodium chloride is not the only salt that is dissolved in seawater. If you take 1000 g of seawater, about 35 g of it is made up of salts. Of this salt, about 68% is sodium chloride; the rest is made up of magnesium chloride, sodium sulfate, calcium chloride and some other salts. Part of seawater Percentage Water 96.5% Salt 3.5% Salt in the dissolved solids Percentage Sodium chloride 68% Magnesium chloride 14.6% Sodium sulfate 11.4% Calcium chloride 3.1% Other salts 2.9% The sodium chloride that is obtained from this seawater is only 68% pure. The mass of sodium chloride in 1000 g of seawater is: $$\\frac{35}{100} \\times 68 = 23.8\\ \\text{g}$$ Salts are compounds made from acids. The names tell you which acid has been used to form them. For example, sodium chloride comes from hydrochloric acid and sodium sulfate comes from sulfuric acid."],[13,"pure-products","Pure products","When a chemical reaction takes place and a product is formed from the reactants, it is often very important that the product is pure. For example, when medicines are being made, the product needs to be free from any impurities that may upset the way the medicine works or that do the patient harm. When there is a simple reaction there is only one product. magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide In other reactions there may be more than one product. When there is more than one product they are mixed up together. These products will need to be separated and purified to produce whichever pure product you want. The products may also be mixed up with some of the reactants if these have not all been used up in the reaction. silver nitrate + sodium chloride → sodium nitrate + silver chloride sulfuric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium sulfate + water barium chloride + sodium sulfate → barium sulfate + sodium chloride lead nitrate + potassium iodide → potassium nitrate + lead iodide copper carbonate + hydrochloric acid → copper chloride + water + carbon dioxide In a neutralisation reaction the reactants must be measured carefully, using a burette and a conical flask, so that exactly the right amounts react and no reactant is left over. When copper carbonate reacts with hydrochloric acid, one of the products is carbon dioxide. The gas travels through the delivery tube into the limewater, which turns milky."],[13,"5-3-weather-and-climate","5.3 Weather and climate","Key words: atmosphere · climate · climatology · humidity · meteorology · statistics · visibility · weather"],[13,"what-is-weather","What is weather?","When you look out of the window, what is the weather like? What people mean by weather is the state of the atmosphere and its changes from minute to minute, hour to hour, day to day, or week to week. In some places, the weather is very similar each day but in others the weather changes frequently. When people talk about the weather they say things such as 'What is the weather like today?', 'How hot is it today?' or 'Will it rain tomorrow?' They are thinking in the short term. People generally think about weather as the combination of temperature, humidity , precipitation, cloudiness, visibility and wind. Countries all over the world take careful measurements of the weather to help predict what will happen next, to see patterns in the weather and to provide information about the weather over time. For example, it is important to be prepared for snowstorms or heat waves, as they can affect the transport systems, production of food, how much power people need to run their homes, and even what they want to buy in the shops."],[13,"weather-or-climate","Weather or climate?","What is the difference between weather and climate? When people talk about climate , they mean the weather of a place over a much longer time, usually more than 30 years. Weather can change in a few hours or even in minutes. Climate is the average weather in an area and takes several years to change. Climate information includes the statistics of weather information that tell us about the normal weather as well as the range of extreme weather at that place. Weather Climate Time scale minutes to weeks more than 30 years How fast it changes in hours or minutes takes several years Affected by temperature, humidity, cloudiness, precipitation temperature and precipitation Name of the study meteorology climatology Remember: weather is what the atmosphere is doing right now; climate is the average of that weather over a very long time."],[13,"climate-zones","Climate zones","The map shows the main climate zones on Earth. The key shows the names of these zones. Each zone has a characteristic climate. Climate zone Description of climate Polar very cold and dry all year Temperate cold winters and mild summers Arid hot and dry all year Tropical hot and wet all year Mediterranean mild winters and hot, dry summers Mountains / tundra / taiga very cold all year"],[13,"5-4-climate-and-ice-ages","5.4 Climate and ice ages","Key words: boulder · cycled · glacial period · glaciers · ice ages · interglacial period · peat bog"],[13,"ice-sheets","Ice sheets","The body of a baby mammoth, named Yuka, was discovered in 1977 in eastern Siberia. In that part of the world it is so cold that the lower layers of the soil stay frozen solid all year round. Scientists think that Yuka lived and died about 39 000 years ago, when the temperature was even colder than it is now. When Yuka was alive, and for thousands of years afterwards, the Earth was much colder than it is now. The map shows the parts of the world that were covered by ice sheets 25 000 years ago."],[13,"glacial-and-interglacial-periods","Glacial and interglacial periods","The very cold period when Yuka lived lasted until about 10 000 years ago. Because so much of the Earth was frozen, it is called a glacial period . 'Glacial' means 'frozen'. Today, the Earth is in an interglacial period . 'Inter' means 'between'. Over the last 450 000 years, the Earth's climate has cycled , or swung, between glacial and interglacial periods. - In an interglacial period, there is permanent ice close to the North and South Poles. - In a glacial period, the ice spreads much further south from the North Pole and further north from the South Pole."],[13,"ice-ages","Ice ages","Looking even further back in time, scientists have found that this cycle of glacial periods and interglacial periods did not always happen. There were long periods of time when the Earth was so warm that there was no permanent ice on its surface, not even at the North Pole or South Pole. In between these warm periods, there were cold periods, with glacials and interglacials. These cold periods are called ice ages . Scientists think the second ice age, which began about 850 million years ago, was the coldest. Some scientists think that the whole Earth was covered with ice and snow then. The Earth was like a giant snowball. Remember: an ice age is a long cold period that contains many glacial and interglacial periods inside it. The Earth is in an ice age now, but in an interglacial period within it."],[13,"evidence-from-boulders-and-glaciers","Evidence from boulders and glaciers","The photograph shows some boulders (big rocks) in California, USA. People have often wondered how rocks like this got into their strange positions. The best explanation is that they were carried there by ancient glaciers . Glaciers are rivers of ice that move slowly downhill. They are formed from snow that over many years becomes compressed into thick masses of ice. The glaciers carry rocks with them. If the glacier melts, the rocks are left behind. Boulders like this were the first clue that glaciers used to be present in parts of the Earth that are much warmer now. Later, other evidence was found that supports this idea. For example, in places that are now quite warm, it is possible to find fossils of animals and plants that were adapted to live in very cold places."],[13,"pollen-evidence","Pollen evidence","Scientists can use pollen to find out what kind of plants lived long ago. In New Zealand, a scientist extracted a core of soil from a peat bog . When plants die they normally decay. In some conditions, without oxygen and slightly acid, the decay is very slow and a peat bog may be formed. The different layers of peat represent different periods of history: the deeper the peat, the older it is. Scientists can take samples of the peat bog by using an instrument called an auger to remove a core. They must be careful to remove the core and keep it in the correct order, so that they know which part is oldest. The deepest level of the soil in this core was formed 237 000 years ago. The scientist collected pollen from different parts of the core and identified the plants from which the pollen came. Because he knew the type of climate that each kind of plant can live in, he was able to work out what the climate was like from 237 000 years ago to now."],[13,"5-5-atmosphere-and-climate","5.5 Atmosphere and climate","Key words: analogy · biodegradable · bioplastics · deforestation · emissions · fossil fuels · global warming · greenhouse effect · locked up · photosynthesis · recycled · renewable resources"],[13,"the-atmosphere","The atmosphere","The atmosphere is a layer of gas above the Earth's surface. It is very different today from the atmosphere when the Earth formed billions of years ago. Scientists think that the Earth was formed about 4600 million years ago. The Earth was very hot and it was molten for millions of years. Then, as the Earth cooled, a solid crust formed. There was a lot of volcanic activity, much more than there is now. The volcanoes produced gases, which formed the early atmosphere. Water vapour was produced by the volcanoes and, as the Earth cooled, this water vapour condensed into liquid water. The water fell as rain and formed the first lakes and oceans. About 4000 million years ago, scientists think that the atmosphere contained mainly carbon dioxide, little or no oxygen, small amounts of the gases methane and ammonia, and some water vapour. This early atmosphere on Earth was like the atmosphere on Venus is today. The temperature on Venus is very high — on the surface it is above 400 °C, which is hot enough to melt lead. Early atmosphere Atmosphere today Carbon dioxide 95% traces Nitrogen traces 78% Oxygen little or none 21% Water vapour 4% traces Average surface temperature above 400 °C 20 °C"],[13,"changes-to-the-atmosphere","Changes to the atmosphere","About 3500 million years ago, micro-organisms developed on Earth. They lived in the oceans and used the carbon dioxide in the atmosphere to make food. They produced oxygen as a waste product of this process. As plants developed over millions of years, they began to grow on land. Plants use carbon dioxide to produce food (glucose, a sugar) by the process of photosynthesis . carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen The levels of oxygen in the atmosphere continued to rise. Scientists know this because there was enough oxygen to combine with iron in the rocks to form iron oxide. By 200 million years ago there was very little carbon dioxide left in the atmosphere. Most of the carbon had been used to make the chemicals that are part of all living things. When organisms die and rot, the carbon in them is released back into the environment. It is recycled . Some organisms do not rot when they die and are turned into fossil fuels such as oil or coal. The carbon is locked up in the fossil fuels until they are burned. Many organisms with shells evolved around 600 to 400 million years ago. The shells are made from calcium carbonate, CaCO₃. When these shelled animals died and fell to the bottom of the oceans as sediment, the many layers of shells pressing down on each other formed rocks, such as limestone. So carbon is also locked up in these rocks."],[13,"atmospheric-changes-today","Atmospheric changes today","The amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere fell until about 200 years ago. Then the levels of carbon dioxide began to rise. Where is the carbon dioxide coming from? Humans caused this rise, because they started to release the carbon that had been locked up in the Earth for millions of years. They burn fossil fuels, such as oil and coal, to keep themselves warm. carbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide As humans developed industry and transport, they have burned more and more oil and coal. So there are greater emissions of carbon dioxide. Humans also use a lot of fossil fuels to generate electricity in power stations. Humans also make the situation worse as they cut down forests of large established trees that use a lot of carbon dioxide in photosynthesis. This deforestation is done for many reasons: to use the wood for building or to make things, to get to resources such as minerals which are mined, to produce crops for profit such as palm oil, to grow more food crops, or to provide pastures for animals such as cattle. Because there are fewer trees, less of the carbon dioxide is being used up, so the level in the atmosphere increases. Cattle eat the grass and produce carbon dioxide and gases such as methane in their intestines. This also changes the atmosphere. When limestone, which consists of calcium carbonate, is used to make other products such as building cement, the carbon in the calcium carbonate is released into the atmosphere. calcium carbonate → calcium oxide + carbon dioxide All these things lead to a change in the atmosphere and an increase in carbon dioxide levels."],[13,"atmospheric-changes-and-climate","Atmospheric changes and climate","There is evidence that carbon dioxide and other gases, such as methane, act like a blanket around the Earth. This is an analogy . The 'blanket' represents the gases that keep the Earth warm. Another analogy is that the gases are like putting the Earth in a greenhouse. A greenhouse lets in light and heat from the Sun, but heat energy is trapped inside. The layers of gases produce the greenhouse effect . This is a natural effect; without it, the Earth would be much colder. However, increasing the levels of greenhouse gases, such as carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour, increases the greenhouse effect. So, as the levels of carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour increase, the amount of heat escaping decreases, and so the Earth's climate becomes warmer. This is known as global warming . You can model the greenhouse effect with three sealed plastic bottles, each with a thermometer held in the lid with modelling clay. One bottle contains normal air, so that it acts as a control, and another contains extra carbon dioxide. The bottles are left in the same warm place and the temperatures are recorded over time. Remember: the greenhouse effect itself is natural and necessary. It is the increase in greenhouse gases caused by humans that leads to global warming."],[13,"reducing-global-warming","Reducing global warming","Humans contribute to global warming by burning fossil fuels. One way to reduce this impact is to use renewable resources . A renewable resource is one that does not deplete (run out) or can be replenished within a human lifetime. Examples include wind, tidal and solar power. A non-renewable resource is a resource that cannot be replaced after it is used. Examples include coal, petroleum and natural gas. When fossil fuels are burned to generate electricity, carbon dioxide is produced. This adds to the problem of global warming. To reduce global warming, people need to generate more of their electricity from renewable energy resources. These energy resources will become more important as the supplies of fossil fuels run out. Wind. People have been using the power of the wind for hundreds of years. They used windmills to grind wheat into flour and to pump water. Now they use wind turbines to generate electricity. You need a lot of wind turbines to generate as much electricity as a power station, so wind turbines are grouped together to form wind farms. No harmful waste product, such as carbon dioxide, is produced when electricity is generated in this way. The problem with wind-generated power is that electricity is only produced when the wind blows. Tidal. Tides make water rise and fall twice every day. This happens as a result of the pull of the Moon's gravity as the Earth spins. It is possible to use this movement to generate electricity. To do this you need to trap the water, in river estuaries, behind a barrier and then generate electricity by releasing the water to flow through electricity generator turbines as the tide goes out. The problem is that you can only generate electricity at certain times each day, and the barrier may interfere with wildlife habitats. Solar. The energy from the Sun can be used to generate electricity. Photovoltaic cells can convert solar energy to electrical energy. This can only happen when the Sun is shining."],[13,"bioplastics","Bioplastics","The use of plastics has developed over the past hundred years. Plastics are very useful materials but they have one very big problem: they do not break down easily and cause many problems for wildlife when they are thrown away. The waste builds up on land and in the oceans. Humans produce a lot of plastic waste, especially from plastics that are only used once. Bioplastic is a biodegradable material that comes from renewable sources (biomass, such as vegetable oils, sawdust or food waste), unlike conventional plastics that are made from petroleum. The use of bioplastics from renewable sources will help to reduce the use of fossil fuels and to prevent damage to the environment caused by disposal of single-use plastics."],[13,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can describe the structure of the atom. - I can list the particles found in an atom. - I can describe some of the properties of the particles found in an atom. - I can describe some of the discoveries that have helped to create the model of the atom that is used today. - I can explain what is meant by purity. - I can calculate percentage purity. - I can explain why it is difficult to obtain a pure product. - I can explain the difference between weather and climate. - I can make observations and take measurements of the weather. - I can describe how the Earth's climate has changed in the past. - I can explain the difference between ice ages, glacial periods and interglacial periods. - I can give some evidence that the Earth's climate cycles between colder and warmer periods. - I can describe the Earth's early atmosphere and how it has changed. - I can explain the greenhouse effect and how human activity increases it. - I can name some renewable resources and describe their advantages and problems."],[14,"6-1-reflection","6.1 Reflection","Key words: angle of incidence · angle of reflection · incident ray · law of reflection · normal · perpendicular · plane mirror · protractor · ray diagram · rays · reflection · set square"],[14,"reflection-from-a-plane-mirror","Reflection from a plane mirror","Most of the mirrors you use are plane mirrors . 'Plane' means flat. A reflection from a surface that is not plane — the back of a spoon, for example — is distorted . Light travels in straight lines called rays . When you draw a light ray, always use a ruler and put an arrowhead on the ray to show its direction. A light ray arriving at a mirror is called the incident ray . The incident ray makes an angle with the mirror, but you do not measure that angle from the mirror itself. You measure it from a line that is perpendicular to the mirror. That line is called the normal . In physics and maths, 'normal' means perpendicular, or at right angles, to something."],[14,"drawing-a-ray-diagram","Drawing a ray diagram","1. Draw the incident ray and the mirror. Sometimes this is done for you. 2. Use a ruler to make the incident ray meet the mirror. 3. Use a protractor or set square to draw the normal , perpendicular to the mirror at the point where the incident ray meets the surface. The normal is usually drawn as a dashed line so it is not confused with a light ray. 4. Use a protractor to measure the angle between the incident ray and the normal. This is the angle of incidence , i . 5. Measure an equal angle on the other side of the normal. This is the angle of reflection , r . Draw the reflected ray coming away from the mirror at this angle, with an arrowhead on it. These diagrams are called ray diagrams because they show what happens to the light rays during reflection. Remember: the law of reflection — the angle of reflection is equal to the angle of incidence. In physics, a law is something that always applies."],[14,"using-reflection","Using reflection","Mirrors can be used to see behind us. Light from the Sun is reflected from the cyclist. That is the incident ray on the mirror. The reflected ray from the mirror goes to the driver's eye."],[14,"measuring-angles-of-incidence-and-reflection","Measuring angles of incidence and reflection","You will need: a ray box with power supply · a plane mirror and a support to hold it vertical · plain paper · pencil · ruler · protractor. Make the room as dark as possible. Method 1. Place the plane mirror vertically at one side of the white paper and mark the position of the front of the mirror with a pencil. 2. Use the protractor to draw a normal line at the surface of the mirror. 3. Use the ray box to direct a ray of light onto the mirror, where the normal meets the mirror surface. 4. Mark the positions of the incident and reflected rays on the paper with a pencil. 5. Turn the ray box off, remove the mirror, and use the pencil marks to construct the ray diagram. Draw the normal with the protractor. 6. Measure and record the angle of incidence, i , and the angle of reflection, r . 7. Repeat for at least four more different angles. In this experiment the independent variable is the angle of incidence and the dependent variable is the angle of reflection. A graph of r against i gives a straight line through the origin."],[14,"6-2-refraction","6.2 Refraction","Key words: angle of refraction · away from the normal · bent · distorted · lenses · medium · refraction · towards the normal"],[14,"what-refraction-is","What refraction is","When you look through a glass of water or a wet window, you cannot see clearly. The background looks distorted — changed to be less clear. This happens because of refraction . The material that light passes through is called a medium . Air, glass and water are all media for light. Medium Speed of light in km/s air 300 000 water 225 000 glass 200 000 Light travels more slowly in water or glass than in air, and this change of speed can make the light change direction. Imagine you are moving at a constant speed on roller skates and the skate on one foot runs onto grass. You change direction, because one skate is now moving more slowly than the other. The same thing happens to a ray of light: one side of the ray slows down first, so the ray changes direction. Remember: refraction of light is defined as the change in direction of light on passing from one medium into another because of a change in speed."],[14,"air-into-glass-or-water","Air into glass or water","The light slows down , so it is bent towards the normal . The refracted ray is closer to the normal than the incident ray would have been if it had carried straight on. The angle of incidence is greater than the angle of refraction. Both angles are measured from the normal."],[14,"glass-or-water-into-air","Glass or water into air","The light speeds up , so it is bent away from the normal . Here the angle of refraction is greater than the angle of incidence."],[14,"refraction-in-everyday-life","Refraction in everyday life","Each drop of water on a window refracts light in a different direction, which makes it very hard to see through. Windscreen wipers remove the drops so that all the refraction from the glass is in the same direction. Refraction is also useful. A lens is a curved piece of glass designed to refract light in a known way. Lenses are used in our eyes, in cameras and in the glasses that people wear."],[14,"refraction-effects","Refraction effects","Effect 1 — the appearing coin. Put a coin at the bottom of an opaque cup and position yourself so the coin is just out of view behind the edge. Pour water in carefully. The coin comes into view, because light from the coin is refracted as it leaves the water. Effect 2 — the broken pencil. Rest a pencil at an angle in a glass about three-quarters full of water and look from the side. The pencil appears broken at the water surface. Effect 3 — water and oil. Half fill the glass with water, then gently pour cooking oil on top. The pencil now appears to bend twice, which tells you the speed of light in oil is different again."],[14,"drawing-accurate-ray-diagrams","Drawing accurate ray diagrams","You will need: a ray box and power supply · a glass block · plain paper · pencil · ruler · protractor. Make the room as dark as possible. Draw around the glass block, shine the ray in at an angle, mark the incident ray in two places and the emerging ray in two places, then lift the block off and join the marks. Draw a normal at both surfaces and measure the angles. Include an angle of incidence of zero: the ray then passes straight through without bending."],[14,"6-3-making-rainbows","6.3 Making rainbows","Key words: dispersion · prism · spectrum · triangular"],[14,"newton-s-discovery-about-light","Newton's discovery about light","In 1666, Isaac Newton showed that white light could be split into different colours. He let sunlight through a hole, passed it through a glass block shaped like a triangle, and shone the colours onto a screen."],[14,"the-spectrum","The spectrum","The range of colours seen in white light is called a spectrum . In the spectrum the colours are not separate; they merge from one to another. Most people now agree there are seven colours. In order: red · orange · yellow · green · blue · indigo · violet You can remember the order with the made-up name 'ROY G. BIV' ."],[14,"dispersion","Dispersion","Dispersion means splitting light into different colours. It happens because each colour in white light is refracted through a slightly different angle. A triangular prism shows this well. Remember: violet light is refracted through the largest angle and red light through the smallest angle."],[14,"rainbows","Rainbows","A rainbow is formed when drops of water in the air cause dispersion of light, and the light is also reflected inside the drops. For you to see a rainbow: - the Sun must be shining, to provide bright light - there must be rain or small drops of water in the air, to cause dispersion - the Sun must be behind you, because the water drops reflect the light inside them."],[14,"making-a-rainbow","Making a rainbow","You will need: a ray box and suitable power supply · a triangular glass prism · a piece of white paper or card to use as a screen. Safety: do not put your eye closer to the prism than about half a metre. The light becomes very bright and could damage your eye. Adjust the ray box and the screen until you see the colours on the screen, then move the screen closer and further away and describe what changes."],[14,"6-4-colours-of-light","6.4 Colours of light","Key words: absorbed · coloured filters · cyan · magenta · non-luminous · primary colours · subtraction · transmit"],[14,"primary-colours","Primary colours","There are three colours of light from which all other colours of light can be made. These are the primary colours , and they cannot be made by mixing any other colours of light: - red · green · blue The primary colours of light are different from the primary colours in paints, because light mixes differently from paint. - red + green = yellow - red + blue = magenta - blue + green = cyan - red + green + blue = white Every colour on a phone, computer or television screen is made from combinations of these three. Most screens can display 256 different colours, made by changing the brightness of each primary colour. For example, orange is red added to green with the red brighter than the green."],[14,"subtracting-colours-of-light","Subtracting colours of light","A coloured filter is a transparent piece of coloured glass or plastic. Put in front of a white light, it transmits (lets through) only light of its own colour and absorbs all the others. Traffic lights use three identical white lamps with a red, a yellow and a green filter in front of them. White light contains seven colours; at the red filter only red is transmitted and the other six are absorbed. This is an example of subtraction of light. Stage lights work the same way: a white lamp inside a black case, with a coloured filter in front. Photographers sometimes use coloured filters on a camera to get different effects. You can subtract colours until nothing is left. White light through a green filter gives green light. If that green light then meets a red filter, no light gets through at all — green is one of the colours a red filter absorbs."],[14,"seeing-colours","Seeing colours","When you look at a non-luminous object — one that does not emit its own light — you see the light reflected from it. The flower looks yellow because it reflects only yellow light and absorbs the other six colours of white light. - A white object reflects all the colours in white light equally. - A black object absorbs all the colours and reflects none. A red cube appears black in green light, because red objects reflect only red and absorb everything else. A blue cube appears black in red light for the same reason."],[14,"6-5-galaxies","6.5 Galaxies","Key words: elliptical · galaxy · irregular · spiral · stellar dust · universe"],[14,"the-milky-way","The Milky Way","On a clear night, far from any lights, you can see a milky band across the sky. That band is part of the galaxy we live in, called the Milky Way . The word 'galaxy' comes from a Greek word for 'milky'. The Milky Way is a spiral galaxy. Because we live inside it, we can only see one 'arm' of the spiral — the band across the sky. Most of the stars we see at night are in our own galaxy; there are an estimated 250 000 000 stars in the Milky Way, including our Sun."],[14,"shapes-of-galaxies","Shapes of galaxies","The universe is all of space and everything in it. Other galaxies are classified by shape: - spiral - elliptical - irregular The closest known galaxy to the Milky Way is the Canis Major Dwarf Galaxy , which is elliptical."],[14,"what-galaxies-are-made-of","What galaxies are made of","Galaxies are made from stellar dust , gas, stars and solar systems, held together by gravity . Stellar dust is the dust found in space; the Earth is travelling through a cloud of it containing on average about one dust particle in every million cubic metres. The gravity holding a galaxy together is very strong because galaxies have such large mass. The Milky Way is estimated to have 1 500 000 000 000 times the mass of the Sun, and even travelling at the speed of light it would take more than 100 000 years to cross from one side to the other. Scientists counted the galaxies in one part of space and multiplied up to estimate about 100 000 000 000 galaxies in the universe. Estimates like this may not be accurate, because that part of space may not be typical and the total volume of the universe is not known accurately."],[14,"6-6-rocks-in-space","6.6 Rocks in space","Key words: asteroid belt · asteroids · craters · impacts"],[14,"asteroids","Asteroids","Asteroids are objects made from rock that orbit the Sun. They range in size up to 975 km across; the smallest one studied is 2 m across. Most are not regular shapes — scientists often describe them as being shaped like potatoes. Most asteroids orbit the Sun between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter. This part of the Solar System is called the asteroid belt . There are millions of asteroids, and some of those that have been studied have been given names. Ceres is the largest asteroid. It was discovered in 1801, and at first scientists thought it was a new planet; because it looked much smaller than a planet, the term 'asteroid' was introduced. The photograph was taken by a spacecraft in 2015. Ceres is round, 975 km in diameter, covered with craters , and has a core, a mantle and a crust like some planets. Scientists think Ceres would have become a planet if it had kept growing while the Solar System formed. Itokawa is about 530 m long and 250 m wide. In 2005 a spacecraft collected samples from its surface — at that time the smallest asteroid to be visited. Itokawa appears to be made from lumps of rock broken off other small planets or moons by impacts . The gravity holding those lumps together is weak because the asteroid is so small, so tidal forces can change its shape when it passes a planet. Some asteroids are made from a single piece of rock. Scientists know this because those asteroids are small and spin quickly, and their gravity would be too weak to hold separate pieces together."],[14,"asteroids-and-earth","Asteroids and Earth","Scientists think a large asteroid impacts the Earth on average every 130 000 years . Smaller asteroids hit much more often — the ones on this map broke apart in the atmosphere and never reached the surface. There are two reasons why asteroids impact with Earth: - the Earth exerts a strong force of gravity on passing objects such as asteroids - many asteroids have orbits that pass relatively close to Earth."],[14,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can understand and describe how light is reflected from a plane mirror. - I can recall the law of reflection. - I can draw ray diagrams to show reflection of light from a plane mirror. - I can describe how light changes speed between air and either water or glass. - I can recall that a change in speed can make a light ray change direction. - I can recall which way light changes direction when it passes from air into glass or water, and from glass or water into air. - I can recall that white light is made from different colours of light. - I can describe how to use a prism to produce dispersion of white light. - I can list the seven colours of the spectrum in order starting from red. - I can recall the three primary colours of light and what they make when added. - I can explain how a coloured filter subtracts colours from white light. - I can explain why a non-luminous object appears to be a particular colour. - I can understand what galaxies are and recall the three shapes of galaxy. - I can recall that galaxies contain stellar dust and gas, stars and solar systems, held together by gravity. - I can describe what is meant by the term asteroid and recall where most asteroids are found. - I can know that some asteroids pass close by Earth and can, from time to time, impact Earth."],[15,"7-1-nutrients","7.1 Nutrients","Key words: anaemia · carbohydrate · fat · minerals · nutrients · oil · protein · starch · vitamin A · vitamin C · vitamin D · vitamins Nutrients are substances found in food that you need in order to stay healthy. There are six kinds of nutrient: carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins, minerals and water."],[15,"carbohydrates-fats-and-proteins","Carbohydrates, fats and proteins","Carbohydrates give you energy. Rice contains a lot of starch , which is a type of carbohydrate. After you eat starch, the body breaks it down into a sugar called glucose. Glucose is the fuel that your cells use for respiration, to release energy. Protein is needed for making new cells. So you need protein to grow, and to repair the body if it is damaged. Protein is also needed to make haemoglobin and antibodies. Chicken and beans contain a lot of protein. Fats and oils also give you energy, and they are needed to make cell membranes. Fats and oils are very similar, but fats are solid at normal temperatures and oils are liquid. Avocado contains fats and oils. Nutrient Good sources Why the body needs it Carbohydrate rice, bread, pasta, potatoes, sugar releases energy in respiration Protein meat, fish, eggs, beans, pulses, dairy growth and repair; haemoglobin and antibodies Fat and oil avocado, nuts, oils, butter energy store, cell membranes, heat insulation"],[15,"energy-stores","Energy stores","You do not eat all the time, but you need energy all the time. You get almost all of your energy from the carbohydrates and fats that you eat. You can also get energy from protein if you run out of carbohydrates and fats. - A short-term store: a small amount of carbohydrate is stored in your cells, especially in the liver and muscles. - A long-term store: fat is stored in special cells underneath the skin and around some of the body organs. Fat stores under the skin also work as heat insulation. Remember: you can test a food for starch with iodine solution. Iodine solution is orange-brown, and turns very dark blue-black when it mixes with starch."],[15,"vitamins","Vitamins","Vitamins are nutrients that are needed in only small amounts, but if you do not eat them you can become ill. Each kind of vitamin is given a letter. Vitamin A helps your eyes to work well, and is especially important for seeing when it is quite dark. People who do not get enough vitamin A may not be able to see anything at night. Vitamin A also helps your white blood cells to fight pathogens. Good sources are green vegetables, carrots, squash, fruit, foods made from milk, and some kinds of fish. Vitamin C helps the skin to stay strong and to heal quickly, and keeps blood vessels and bones healthy. Too little vitamin C causes scurvy : the person feels weak and may have swollen, bleeding gums. Good sources are fresh fruit and vegetables — citrus fruits especially, but also potatoes and colourful berries. Vitamin D is needed for strong bones and teeth, because it helps the body to absorb calcium from food. Oily fish is the best food source, but most of your vitamin D is made in your own skin when sunlight falls onto it. Too little vitamin D in children stops their bones growing normally; this illness is called rickets ."],[15,"minerals","Minerals","Minerals are nutrients needed in small amounts. Two of the most important are calcium and iron. Calcium is found in bones and teeth, so you need plenty of it to make them strong. Foods made from milk are excellent sources. Seeds and some types of nut, such as almonds, also contain a lot of calcium. Iron is needed to make haemoglobin. Without enough iron you cannot make enough haemoglobin, so not enough oxygen is transported around the body. This causes anaemia , which makes a person feel very tired. Good sources of iron are red meat, dark green vegetables, many kinds of fish and shellfish, and some nuts and seeds."],[15,"water","Water","Water is the sixth nutrient. Almost 90 % of a person's body weight is water. Water inside cells lets all the different chemicals dissolve so that they can react together, and these reactions keep you alive. Water in the blood lets the blood flow easily, so that substances can be transported all over the body."],[15,"7-2-a-balanced-diet","7.2 A balanced diet","Key words: balanced diet · constipation · fibre"],[15,"diet","Diet","Your diet is the food that you eat each day. A balanced diet is one that provides all the different kinds of nutrient, and the right amount of energy."],[15,"how-much-energy","How much energy?","Different people need different amounts of energy each day. - Someone who does a lot of exercise, or has an active job, needs more energy. - Someone who sits down for most of the day needs less energy. - A person who is still growing needs extra energy, because energy is needed to make cells divide. - Tall people usually need more energy than small people."],[15,"different-diets","Different diets","Everyone needs plenty of minerals and vitamins, but people vary in how much protein and carbohydrate they need. Young people who are still growing need a lot of protein to make new cells, enough carbohydrate for the energy they use, and a little fat for energy and for making the membranes of the new cells. People who sit down for a lot of the day do not use up as much energy, so they do not need to eat as much carbohydrate or fat as someone who moves around all day or does a lot of sport. A pregnant woman needs plenty of protein to build her baby's new cells, lots of iron to make haemoglobin for her own blood and her baby's blood, and plenty of calcium for building her baby's bones."],[15,"fibre","Fibre","You also need to eat plenty of fibre . Fibre is not actually a nutrient, because you cannot digest it — it does not go into the blood or to your cells. Instead it travels all the way through the digestive system and leaves the body in the faeces. Fibre is still very important. It keeps the digestive system healthy and helps to prevent constipation , when the digestive system slows down and faeces collect inside it instead of being passed out. Fibre is mostly cellulose. Plant cell walls are made of cellulose, so foods made from plants are good sources: cereal grains, seeds, and fresh fruit and vegetables. These foods usually contain lots of different minerals and vitamins as well."],[15,"food-groups","Food groups","It can be difficult to think about which nutrients are in each kind of food. The food triangle makes this easier. The bigger the area in the triangle, the greater the proportion of your diet that kind of food should make up. Position in the triangle Foods What they provide Bottom (largest) rice, bread, pasta — preferably whole grain starch and fibre Next fresh fruit and vegetables minerals, vitamins and fibre Next meat, fish, eggs, pulses, dairy products protein Top (smallest) sweets and fried foods eat only a little"],[15,"not-too-much","Not too much","Some things should not be eaten in large amounts. - Too much sugar can make your teeth decay, and increases the risk of an illness called diabetes. - Too much fat, oil or carbohydrate can make you put on weight, which puts a strain on your joints, heart and other body organs. - Too much animal fat increases the risk of developing heart disease. Remember: a balanced diet is not just \"enough food\". It must contain every kind of nutrient, in roughly the right proportions, and supply the right amount of energy for that particular person."],[15,"7-3-growth-development-and-health","7.3 Growth, development and health","Key words: carbon monoxide · development · embryo · nicotine · particulates · tar"],[15,"growth","Growth","Every person on Earth began life as a single cell. That cell divided to produce two cells; each of those cells grew, then divided again. Growth happens by this repeated pattern: cells divide, then the new cells grow . At first all the cells are the same. They form a little ball of cells called an embryo , and eventually a baby. This all happens inside the mother's body. A baby continues to grow until it is about 18–20 years old. Cells contain a lot of protein, and energy is needed to make cells divide. That is why a pregnant woman and a growing child need plenty of protein in their diet, as well as enough energy."],[15,"development","Development","Becoming an adult involves more than just getting bigger. As the embryo grows into a baby, all its different tissues and organs are formed. As the baby grows into a child, its leg muscles and bones become stronger so that it can walk and then run, and its brain develops as it learns to talk and to play. These changes are called development . Everyone grows and develops at different rates, but everyone goes through the same stages: baby, toddler, child, adolescent, adult. Each stage blends gradually into the next one — there are no sharp divisions between them. Remember: growth means getting bigger; development means becoming more complex and gaining new abilities."],[15,"exercise-and-health","Exercise and health","Taking regular exercise: - uses up some of the energy in the food you eat, so less is stored as fat - makes the heart and muscles work hard, so they become strong - can make people feel more cheerful and positive about life."],[15,"smoking","Smoking","Smoking cigarettes damages the smoker's health. It also damages the health of people around them, who accidentally breathe in the smoke. Tobacco contains many different harmful substances. Substance What it does Nicotine addictive, so smokers find it very difficult to stop; narrows blood vessels so blood cannot get through easily, increasing the risk of heart disease Tar a mixture of dark, sticky substances; some of its chemicals cause cancer, including lung cancer Carbon monoxide a poisonous gas that combines with haemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen and cells cannot release enough energy by respiration Particulates tiny particles of carbon and other materials that get trapped in the lungs and break down the walls of the alveoli, leaving big spaces instead of millions of tiny alveoli Cancer happens when cells start dividing out of control and spread to other parts of the body."],[15,"7-4-moving-the-body","7.4 Moving the body","Key words: antagonistic muscles · ball-and-socket joints · biceps · contraction · exoskeleton · hinge joints · joints · muscles · relax · skeleton · tendons · triceps"],[15,"the-skeleton","The skeleton","Animals' bodies are supported by a skeleton . Insects and other arthropods have their skeleton on the outside of the body; this is called an exoskeleton . Your skeleton is inside your body and is made of bones. Bones are hard and strong, and contain a lot of calcium. If you do not have enough calcium in your diet, your bones may not grow properly. Bones also contain living cells, so you need protein in the diet to build strong bones."],[15,"joints","Joints","Bones cannot bend. Movement in the skeleton can only take place where two bones meet one another. These places are called joints . - Hinge joints work like the hinges on a door. They let the bones move back and forth in one direction only — for example the elbow, the knee and the joints in the fingers. - Ball-and-socket joints let the bones move in a complete circle. One bone has an end shaped like a ball, and the other has a cup, or socket, that the ball fits into — for example the shoulder and the hip."],[15,"joints-in-the-arm","Joints in the arm","The arm contains the shoulder joint, the elbow joint, the wrist joint and all the joints in the fingers. Joint Type Bones that meet Shoulder ball-and-socket scapula and humerus Elbow hinge humerus, radius and ulna Finger joints hinge finger bones"],[15,"muscles","Muscles","Bones and joints cannot move themselves. You use muscles to move bones at joints. Muscles are made of specialised cells that are able to make themselves shorter. This is called contraction . Muscles use energy to contract, and they get that energy from glucose by respiration. The more your muscles contract, the more glucose they use. Muscles can produce a strong pulling force when they contract. Many muscles are attached to bones by tough cords called tendons . When the muscle contracts it pulls on the tendon, which pulls on the bone, and the bone moves at a joint. The biceps is the bigger muscle in the diagram. 'Bi-' means two: the biceps is named after the two tendons that attach it to the scapula. Its other end is attached to the radius. The triceps is the longer, thinner muscle. 'Tri-' means three, and the triceps has three tendons. It is attached to the scapula and the humerus at one end, and to the ulna at the other. Remember: tendons are not stretchy. If they stretched, the pull of the muscle would not be passed on to the bone."],[15,"bending-and-straightening-the-elbow","Bending and straightening the elbow","To bend the arm, the biceps contracts and the triceps relaxes. Your brain sends an electrical impulse along a neurone to the biceps. The muscle cells respond by contracting, so the whole muscle gets shorter. The contracting biceps pulls on its tendon, so the radius moves upwards and the elbow bends. Muscles can only pull — they cannot push. A muscle can produce a force by contracting, but not by getting longer. So the biceps cannot push the arm straight again; another muscle is needed to pull it straight. To straighten the arm, the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes. The contracting triceps pulls on its tendon, so the ulna moves downwards."],[15,"antagonistic-muscles","Antagonistic muscles","The biceps and the triceps work as a team. - To bend the arm, the biceps contracts and the triceps relaxes. - To straighten the arm, the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes. Two muscles that work together like this are called antagonistic muscles . When one of them contracts it moves the bones at a joint in one direction; when the other contracts it moves them back the other way. In a model arm, a piece of wood represents the humerus and another represents the radius, joined by a freely moving pivot at the 'elbow'. A newton meter attached to hooks at different distances from the pivot represents the biceps. The further the newton meter is from the elbow, the smaller the force needed to hold the 'radius' horizontal — because the turning effect of a force depends on its distance from the pivot."],[15,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can name the six nutrients and say why the body needs each of them. - I can name good food sources of protein, carbohydrate, fat, vitamin A, vitamin C, vitamin D, calcium and iron. - I can describe the illnesses caused by a lack of vitamin C, vitamin D and iron. - I can explain what is meant by a balanced diet. - I can explain why different people need different diets. - I can explain why fibre is important even though it is not a nutrient. - I can explain why no one should eat too much sugar or fat. - I can describe how organisms grow. - I can explain what is meant by development. - I can explain why exercise is good for a person's health. - I can explain how nicotine, tar, carbon monoxide and particulates damage health. - I can name the bones in the arm. - I can identify some hinge joints and ball-and-socket joints in the body. - I can describe how muscles produce a force when they contract. - I can explain how the biceps and triceps work as antagonistic muscles to move the arm at the elbow."],[16,"8-1-exothermic-reactions","8.1 Exothermic reactions","Key words: combustion · dissipate · exothermic reaction · fuel · oxidation reaction · preliminary work"],[16,"burning","Burning","When something burns, a chemical reaction takes place. Burning is a chemical reaction in which a substance combines with oxygen. In a burning reaction, there are energy changes. The substance that reacts with oxygen is called a fuel . Fuels have a store of chemical energy. Charcoal, wood, coal, natural gas and oil are examples of fuels. When the fuel burns, the chemical energy is changed to thermal, light and sound energy. The thermal, light and sound energy dissipate (spread out) into the surroundings. Burning needs three things together. If any one of them is missing, the fuel will not burn. Combustion is another term for burning. When carbon burns, the atoms of carbon and oxygen join together in new ways. When this happens, chemical energy is changed to thermal energy and the temperature rises. A chemical reaction in which thermal energy is given out is called an exothermic reaction . Remember: in an exothermic reaction the temperature of the surroundings goes up , because energy is given out."],[16,"burning-other-substances","Burning other substances","Hydrogen can be used as a fuel in a model rocket. The combustion of hydrogen is an exothermic reaction. The hydrogen and the oxygen combine to form water. When the atoms of hydrogen and oxygen rearrange themselves and combine together, energy is given out. This chemical energy is changed into kinetic, thermal, sound and light energy. In this experiment, a large plastic soda bottle filled with hydrogen and air is attached to a string across the room. The stopper in the bottle has wires that allow a spark to be generated. The hot spark provides the energy to start the reaction. The hydrogen and oxygen react together. The reaction gives out a lot of energy and the stopper is pushed out, so the bottle shoots along the string. The reactions of other substances burning in air are also exothermic reactions. An example is burning magnesium, which produces magnesium oxide. Energy is given out as heat and light as the magnesium and oxygen atoms rearrange themselves. When a substance burns, it combines with oxygen and a new substance called an oxide is formed. Any reaction in which a substance combines with oxygen is an oxidation reaction ."],[16,"exothermic-reactions-without-burning","Exothermic reactions without burning","Not every exothermic reaction is a burning reaction. Potassium reacts with water without any flame being applied. Water is made up of particles containing atoms of hydrogen and oxygen. In the potassium and water reaction, the bonds between the atoms of oxygen and hydrogen in the water break. The atoms rearrange to form the products potassium hydroxide and hydrogen. Stored chemical energy is changed to thermal energy, which dissipates into the environment. If you add magnesium to dilute hydrochloric acid the test tube gets hot. This reaction is an exothermic one."],[16,"measuring-the-rise-in-temperature-during-a-reaction","Measuring the rise in temperature during a reaction","Sofia and Marcus each measured 10 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid into a test tube and measured the temperature. Then they each added an identical piece of magnesium ribbon to their test tube of acid. When the reaction stopped, they each measured the temperature again. The bigger the temperature rise, the more chemical energy has been changed to thermal energy. Before a full investigation it is useful to do some preliminary work — a quick trial run that shows whether the quantities and the method will give results you can use."],[16,"8-2-endothermic-reactions","8.2 Endothermic reactions","Key words: endothermic process · endothermic reaction"],[16,"reactions-that-take-energy-in","Reactions that take energy in","Some chemical reactions absorb thermal energy from their surroundings and change it to chemical energy stored in the chemical bonds. These are called endothermic reactions . When an endothermic reaction takes place, the temperature at the end of the reaction is lower than that at the start of the reaction. You can show this with citric acid (or lemon juice) and sodium hydrogen carbonate: 1. Place some citric acid or lemon juice in a test tube so that it is about half full. 2. Measure and record the temperature. 3. Add three spatulas of sodium hydrogen carbonate and stir. Do not use the thermometer to do this. 4. Measure and record the temperature. This is the word equation for the reaction: sodium hydrogen carbonate + citric acid → sodium citrate + water + carbon dioxide During this reaction, thermal energy is absorbed from the surroundings and stored in the form of chemical bonds. So the surroundings have a lower temperature and the test tube feels cooler. If you eat sherbet sweets, this reaction takes place in your mouth. The sherbet is a mixture of dry citric acid and sodium hydrogen carbonate. When you eat the sweets, these substances dissolve in your saliva and react together. This gives a cool 'fizzy' feeling in your mouth (the surroundings), which is refreshing."],[16,"endothermic-processes","Endothermic processes","Not every change that takes energy in is a reaction. If you place some water in a beaker and then stir in three spatulas of potassium chloride, you will find that the beaker gets cold. In this case, no chemical reaction has taken place. No new products are formed — the potassium chloride has just dissolved. A solution of potassium chloride has been formed, in which potassium chloride is the solute and water is the solvent. When potassium chloride dissolves in water, thermal energy is absorbed from the surroundings. This is why the beaker feels cold. This is an endothermic process . Ice melting is another endothermic process. Thermal energy is absorbed from the surroundings as the solid ice changes to liquid water. The particles in the ice are lined up in rows and can only vibrate about fixed positions — they cannot move around inside the ice. The forces between the particles are strong. As the particles absorb thermal energy from the surroundings, they vibrate more and more. When the particles have enough energy, they can move and overcome the forces holding them in place. The particles can now slide past one another, and the water is in a liquid state. Remember: a reaction makes new substances; a process such as dissolving or melting does not. Both can be exothermic or endothermic."],[16,"exothermic-or-endothermic","Exothermic or endothermic?","Exothermic Endothermic Energy given out to the surroundings taken in from the surroundings Temperature of surroundings rises falls Examples burning, metal + acid, calcium + water citric acid + sodium hydrogen carbonate, dissolving potassium chloride, ice melting A polystyrene cup is a good container for this kind of experiment, because it is a poor conductor and keeps the energy change inside the cup. When you plan an experiment like this, you also need to think about how accurate and how precise your measurements are."],[16,"using-exothermic-reactions","Using exothermic reactions","Some exothermic reactions are used to produce self-heating cans of food or drink. A can of self-heating food contains two chemicals, which are in separate compartments. When you open the can, the two chemicals mix with each other and react, and the reaction warms up the food or drink. The chemicals used are calcium oxide and water: calcium oxide + water → calcium hydroxide These cans can be very useful if you are in a remote area, or in an emergency when there is no power, or when you are camping. They are expensive to produce, because the compartments must be sealed from one another and from the food so that it does not become contaminated. There have also been problems with the food not being heated evenly."],[16,"using-endothermic-processes","Using endothermic processes","People sometimes use ice packs when they injure themselves. These packs are stored in a fridge or freezer until they are needed. When the ice pack is placed on the injured area, heat from this area is transferred to the ice pack and the ice melts. The injured area is cooled, and this often prevents it from swelling up. After it has been used, the ice pack can go back into the freezer to be used again. Some 'ice' packs are made from substances that undergo an endothermic process when they mix together. These packs can be used even when you don't have a fridge or freezer. The pack has two compartments inside, each with a different substance — usually ammonium nitrate and water. When you push on the pack and break the compartment containing ammonium nitrate, the water mixes with it and the ammonium nitrate begins to dissolve. This is an endothermic process, so the temperature drops."],[16,"8-3-metals-and-their-reactions-with-oxygen","8.3 Metals and their reactions with oxygen","Key words: collapses · inert · prevent · reactive · rusts"],[16,"metals-and-oxygen","Metals and oxygen","Many metals react with oxygen if they get hot enough. When you look carefully at the reactions of metals with oxygen, it is possible to identify which metals are more reactive . For example, magnesium is more reactive than iron because magnesium reacts more quickly than iron. This reaction between metals and oxygen is an oxidation reaction: metal + oxygen → metal oxide You can compare metals by heating a small piece of each one in a Bunsen flame and recording what you see. Some metals react very quickly with oxygen without even being heated. When pieces of sodium, potassium or calcium are taken from their containers, they appear dull. When the pieces are cut, the surface is shiny. The shiny surface soon becomes dull because the metal reacts with the oxygen in the air, and the surface becomes covered with a new substance — the oxide of the metal. These metals are so reactive that they have to be stored under oil to prevent them reacting. The layer of metal oxide on the surface prevents any more of the metal from reacting with the air or water vapour. Some metals, such as gold, do not react with oxygen. They are generally unreactive and are described as inert . Silver reacts slowly with the air: if a silver object is not cleaned it goes black over time, as silver oxide is formed."],[16,"the-reaction-between-iron-and-oxygen","The reaction between iron and oxygen","When iron is left in damp air it rusts . The iron reacts with oxygen to form an orange-brown solid, called iron oxide, otherwise known as rust. This is not a very useful reaction, because it means that the iron changes and no longer has the same properties. A strong iron girder can become rusted and fall apart, which could mean that a building collapses ."],[16,"what-causes-iron-to-rust","What causes iron to rust?","A new iron nail is placed in each of four test tubes, as in the diagram. Tube What is in it Air? Water? 1 nail only, open to the air yes no 2 water, nail half in the water, open to the air yes yes 3 calcium chloride, which absorbs water, so the air is dry yes no 4 boiled water with a layer of oil on top, which stops air entering no yes After a few weeks the experiment looks like this. Only the nail in tube 2 rusts badly. The reaction between iron and oxygen only takes place when both water and oxygen are present. The water is not part of the equation, but it is needed for the reaction to happen. The reaction takes a long time to happen — iron is not very reactive with oxygen."],[16,"how-can-iron-be-protected","How can iron be protected?","Rusting can be slowed down or stopped by keeping air and water away from the iron surface."],[16,"8-4-reactions-of-metals-with-water","8.4 Reactions of metals with water","Key words: reactivity · sandpaper"],[16,"reactions-of-sodium-and-potassium-with-water","Reactions of sodium and potassium with water","Sodium reacts vigorously with water. Potassium is even more reactive than sodium. So much thermal energy is generated that the hydrogen gas produced in the reaction catches fire. In these two reactions the metal reacted with water to produce hydrogen and the metal hydroxide: metal + water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen"],[16,"reactions-of-other-metals-with-water","Reactions of other metals with water","Some other metals react less vigorously with water — for example, calcium and magnesium. To compare metals fairly, take a small piece of each metal, use sandpaper to clean the surface, and place it into a test tube of water. You may need to leave the metal to react for some time. If nothing happens, you could try testing the metal again, this time using hot water. In the experiment shown in the diagram, a piece of calcium has been placed at the bottom of a beaker and covered with water. A filter funnel has been placed upside down over the metal. The gas given off is collected in a test tube by the displacement of water."],[16,"metals-and-steam","Metals and steam","Some of the metals that do not react with water may react with steam. Even magnesium will react more rapidly with steam than with water. In the reaction shown here, magnesium is heated. From time to time, the heat is directed at the ceramic wool. The ceramic wool has been soaked in water, which when heated produces steam. In this reaction the magnesium reacts with water, which is in the form of a gas. Magnesium oxide and hydrogen are formed: magnesium + water (g) → magnesium oxide + hydrogen The (g) after water indicates it is water in the form of a gas, in this case steam. Steam is formed by boiling water and is very hot, whereas water vapour is made up of water particles in the air at lower temperatures. Some metals, such as gold, do not react with water at all. Putting the metals in order of how strongly they react gives their order of reactivity ."],[16,"8-5-reactions-of-metals-with-dilute-acids","8.5 Reactions of metals with dilute acids","Key words: reagents · salt"],[16,"a-familiar-reaction","A familiar reaction","You will probably remember the reaction of magnesium with dilute hydrochloric acid. This is the word equation for this reaction: magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen Magnesium chloride is an example of a salt . When a metal reacts with an acid, the products are a salt and hydrogen: metal + acid → salt + hydrogen"],[16,"planning-an-investigation","Planning an investigation","Sofia and Marcus have been asked to investigate the reactions of metals with acids. They need to decide which of the equipment and reagents in the laboratory they need, to enable them to carry out the investigation safely. The more reactive the metal, the faster the bubbles of hydrogen are produced. Comparing the bubbles in each tube puts the metals in order of reactivity."],[16,"comparing-fuels","Comparing fuels","The same idea of a fair test is used when comparing how much thermal energy different fuels give out. Each fuel heats the same volume of water, and the rise in temperature is measured."],[16,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can describe what happens in an exothermic reaction. - I can explain that burning (combustion) is a reaction between a fuel and oxygen. - I can state what is needed for combustion to take place. - I can explain what an oxidation reaction is. - I can list some chemical reactions that are endothermic. - I can explain the difference between an endothermic reaction and an endothermic process. - I can describe some uses of exothermic and endothermic reactions and processes. - I can plan an investigation and carry it out safely. - I can describe the reactions of some metals with oxygen. - I can compare how reactive some metals are with oxygen. - I can describe the conditions needed for iron to rust and how rusting can be prevented. - I can describe the reactions of some metals with water and with steam. - I can compare how reactive some metals are with water. - I can describe the reactions of some metals with dilute acid. - I can write word equations for the reactions of metals with oxygen, water and acid."],[17,"9-1-magnetic-fields","9.1 Magnetic fields","Key words: compass · like poles · magnet · magnetic · magnetic field · magnetic field lines"],[17,"the-magnetic-field","The magnetic field","A magnet is something that will attract magnetic materials. Magnetic materials include the metals iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. You have probably used a magnet to pick up paperclips. Magnets have two poles , north and south. On diagrams they are shown with the letters N and S . When a paperclip is close to one of the poles of a magnet, the paperclip is attracted to the magnet. As you move the paperclip further away, it stops being attracted. The paperclip is attracted to a magnet only when it is inside the magnetic field of that magnet. Remember: a magnetic field is the area around a magnet where the effects of the magnet can be detected. A magnetic field surrounds every magnet. The magnetic field of a magnet is strongest at the poles ."],[17,"detecting-a-magnetic-field","Detecting a magnetic field","You can detect a magnetic field in two ways. - See whether a magnetic object moves because it is attracted. - Use a compass . A compass contains a magnetised needle that is free to turn. The needle turns and points along the magnetic field. Some mobile devices, such as phones, have compass apps."],[17,"magnetic-field-lines","Magnetic field lines","You can draw magnetic field lines around a magnet to represent the magnetic field. The rules are: - magnetic field lines join opposite poles - the field lines have arrows that point from N to S - field lines must not touch each other - field lines must not cross each other. Field lines also tell you how strong the field is. The magnetic field is strongest where the lines are closest together. If one magnet is stronger than another, the field of the stronger magnet is different in two ways: - all the field lines are closer together - the field lines extend further away from the magnet. A compass placed in a magnetic field points in the direction of the field lines."],[17,"magnetic-fields-interacting","Magnetic fields interacting","You already know about the forces between two magnets. Poles brought together Force Two north poles repel Two south poles repel A north pole and a south pole attract This force is caused by the magnetic fields of the two magnets. The field lines between these two magnets all point in the same direction, so there is a force of attraction between the magnets. The field lines in the space directly between these two magnets point in opposite directions, so the magnets repel and try to move away from each other."],[17,"measuring-the-strength-of-a-magnetic-field","Measuring the strength of a magnetic field","One way to compare magnets is to find how far away a magnet can still attract a paperclip. The further away the paperclip is picked up, the stronger the magnetic field. Sprinkling iron filings on a sheet of paper over a magnet shows the field pattern. The filings crowd together at the poles, where the field is strongest."],[17,"9-2-the-earth-as-a-giant-magnet","9.2 The Earth as a giant magnet","Key words: geographic north pole · magnetic north · naturally occurring · navigate"],[17,"lodestone-and-the-first-compasses","Lodestone and the first compasses","Around 4000 years ago a Greek shepherd called Magnes noticed that the iron nails in his shoes stuck to one particular type of rock. The rock was called lodestone. It contains a substance later named magnetite, which is a naturally occurring magnet — one that is not made by people. The Chinese discovered that a small needle of lodestone could be floated on water. When it was free to float, the needle always turned to point the same way: one end towards the north, the other towards the south. This was the invention of the magnetic compass. The compass needle points to a place close to the Earth's north pole, but not exactly to it. That place is called magnetic north . Magnetic north moves very slowly and is currently in the Arctic Ocean, north of Alaska. The compass was important because it let people navigate across oceans and deserts with less chance of getting lost. Even today, with satellite navigation (satnav), ships and aeroplanes still carry magnetic compasses. Satnav systems do not use the Earth's magnetic field. Some animals use the Earth's magnetic field to navigate over long distances."],[17,"the-earth-s-magnetic-field","The Earth's magnetic field","Notice that the field lines around the Earth point towards the Earth's north pole. Field lines always point from north to south, so the north pole of the Earth is actually a magnetic south pole. Remember: magnetic north means the magnetic pole close to the geographic north pole; magnetic south means the magnetic pole close to the geographic south pole. The geographic north and south poles are the parts of the Earth through which the spin axis passes. The Earth's magnetic field also causes natural lights in the night sky close to the north and south poles. They appear when particles arriving from the Sun reach the stronger parts of the Earth's magnetic field."],[17,"origin-of-the-earth-s-magnetic-field","Origin of the Earth's magnetic field","People once thought the Earth was made almost entirely from magnetic rocks. It is now known that the high temperatures deep inside the Earth would make rocks lose any magnetism they had. Scientists also know that the Earth's magnetic field has reversed in the past. The last change was around 500 000 years ago, when south really was north. The origin of the field is the Earth's core , although scientists have still to discover the exact reason. - The core is mostly made from iron, and the heat generated there keeps creating a magnetic field. - The core also contains some nickel, which is another magnetic metal. - Movement in the liquid outer core explains why the magnetic poles move slowly and have occasionally reversed. Magnetic north moves at a speed of about 60 km per year. Runway numbers sometimes have to be repainted because magnetic north has moved."],[17,"9-3-electromagnets","9.3 Electromagnets","Key words: coil · core · electromagnet · magnetic · magnetised · permanent magnets"],[17,"properties-of-magnetic-materials","Properties of magnetic materials","A material is described as magnetic if it is attracted to a magnet. Magnetic materials include iron, nickel and cobalt. Steel is another common magnetic metal; steel is a mixture that contains a large proportion of iron. Magnetic materials can be magnetised , which means turned into a magnet. Every magnet you have used was made by magnetising a magnetic material. Those magnets are called permanent magnets because their magnetic field is always there. You cannot switch it off and on again. One way to magnetise a magnetic material is by using electricity. A magnet made this way is called an electromagnet ."],[17,"making-an-electromagnet","Making an electromagnet","An electromagnet is made by wrapping a wire around a magnetic material such as iron. - The wire that is wrapped around is called the coil . - The material in the middle of the coil is called the core . When current passes through the coil, the magnetic material becomes magnetised. When the current is switched off, the magnetic material loses most of its magnetism. You can build the same electromagnet from a large iron nail, a coil of insulated wire, a cell and a switch. Only the plastic coating of the wire should touch the nail, and the electromagnet should be switched on for the shortest possible time so the cell lasts."],[17,"poles-of-an-electromagnet","Poles of an electromagnet","An electromagnet has two poles, just like a bar magnet. There are two simple ways to find out which pole is which. - Use a magnetic compass. A compass points along magnetic field lines, so it points towards the south pole. - Use a bar magnet with known poles. Opposite poles attract and like poles repel, so bringing the bar magnet close shows which pole is which. You can reverse the poles of an electromagnet in one of two ways: - wrap the coil around in the opposite direction - reverse the connections on the cell or power supply."],[17,"applications-of-electromagnets","Applications of electromagnets","Electromagnets are used wherever a permanent magnet would not be useful, because an electromagnet can be switched off . A fire door is held open by an electromagnet connected to the fire alarm. When the alarm goes off the magnet is switched off and the door closes. An MRI scanner produces images from inside the human body. MRI stands for magnetic resonance imaging. An electromagnet sorting scrap attracts iron and steel and leaves the non-magnetic metals behind. Common non-magnetic metals include copper, aluminium and zinc. Once the magnetic metals have been lifted and moved, switching off the magnet drops them. In a toaster, pushing the handle down makes an electromagnet hold a metal basket down. A timer turns the electromagnet off and the basket pops up with the toast. An electric bell uses an electromagnet to move the hammer. When the electromagnet is on, the hammer is pulled onto the bell. That movement breaks the circuit, so the hammer moves away, the circuit becomes complete again and the hammer is pulled back. This repeats until the power supply is turned off. Electric bells are used in schools, as fire alarms and as door bells. Electric motors use electromagnets to change electrical energy into kinetic energy."],[17,"9-4-investigating-electromagnets","9.4 Investigating electromagnets","Key words: factors · soft iron"],[17,"strength-of-electromagnets","Strength of electromagnets","Topic 9.3 listed the three things needed to make an electromagnet: - a coil of wire - a magnetic core inside the coil - an electric current flowing in the coil. These three things are also the factors that affect how strong the electromagnet is. A factor is another word for a variable that will affect something. Factor Effect on strength Number of turns in the coil The more turns, the stronger the electromagnet Material of the core Iron and some types of steel make the strongest electromagnets Current in the coil The greater the current, the stronger the electromagnet"],[17,"three-ways-to-make-an-electromagnet-stronger","Three ways to make an electromagnet stronger","Increase the number of turns on the coil. Keep the current and the core the same. Use more cells to increase the current. Keep the number of turns and the core the same. Use a soft iron core in place of a steel core. Keep the number of turns and the current the same. Remember: each of these is a fair test — only one factor is changed and the other two are kept the same."],[17,"soft-iron","Soft iron","Soft iron is not soft in the way modelling clay is soft. Soft iron is the term for iron that is easily magnetised and also easily demagnetised . Demagnetised means it has lost its magnetism. In 2019, scientists broke the world record for the strongest electromagnet. They made one 4500 times stronger than a school bar magnet. It uses more electricity than three million electric lamps."],[17,"comparing-electromagnets","Comparing electromagnets","The strength of an electromagnet can be measured by the force it exerts on a magnetic material. The easiest way to do this is to count how many magnetic objects the electromagnet can lift and hold. Each of these four circuits has the same current and the same number of turns in the coil. Only the core material is different. Rubber, wood and aluminium are not magnetic, so only the nickel core will make a working electromagnet."],[17,"summary-checklist","Summary checklist","- I can describe what is meant by the term magnetic field. - I can explain how to detect a magnetic field. - I can draw magnetic field lines around a bar magnet and around a horseshoe magnet. - I can use field lines to say where a magnetic field is strongest. - I can draw the field lines between two magnets whose opposite poles are together, and whose like poles are together. - I know that the Earth has a magnetic field. - I can draw a diagram to show the Earth's magnetic field lines. - I can explain why the north pole of a freely rotating magnet points north. - I know that the core of the Earth is the origin of the Earth's magnetic field. - I can explain the difference between an electromagnet and a permanent magnet. - I know how to make an electromagnet from a coil, a core, a cell and a switch. - I can find and reverse the poles of an electromagnet. - I know some applications of electromagnets and why a permanent magnet would not suit them. - I can state the three factors that affect the strength of an electromagnet. - I can describe what soft iron is and why it is used as a core."],[18,"1-1-photosynthesis","1.1 Photosynthesis","Key words: chlorophyll · light intensity · photosynthesis"],[18,"how-plants-make-food","How plants make food","- Photosynthesis means \"making with light\" ( photo = light, synthesis = making). Plants use it to make their own food. - Plants take in three things: - water — from the soil, through the roots - carbon dioxide — from the air - energy — from sunlight - The energy in sunlight is captured by a green pigment called chlorophyll , which is inside some of the cells in the leaves. - The energy makes water and carbon dioxide react together. Two new substances are made: glucose and oxygen . $$\\text{water} + \\text{carbon dioxide} \\rightarrow \\text{glucose} + \\text{oxygen}$$ - Reactants: water and carbon dioxide. Products: glucose and oxygen. - Photosynthesis happens in the leaves of a plant."],[18,"experiment-collecting-the-gas-produced-in-photosynthesis","Experiment: collecting the gas produced in photosynthesis","Apparatus — a water plant under an upside-down funnel in a beaker of pond water, with a test tube full of water over the funnel neck. Method 1. Set up the apparatus and put it where the plant will get sunlight. Leave it for at least one day. 2. When plenty of gas has collected, put your hand into the beaker and remove the tube from the funnel, keeping the opening under the water so the gas cannot escape. 3. Put your thumb over the end, lift the tube out, and keep it upside down. 4. Let the water run out, then test the gas with a glowing splint . What to observe — the splint relights . That shows the gas contains a lot of oxygen. Why it is done this way - A water plant is used because the gas it releases forms bubbles that are easy to collect under water. - The apparatus is left in sunlight because light is needed for photosynthesis; without it, no gas would be produced."],[18,"experiment-how-light-intensity-affects-the-rate-of-photosynthesis","Experiment: how light intensity affects the rate of photosynthesis","- Independent variable: distance of the lamp from the test tube (this changes the light intensity). - Dependent variable: number of bubbles produced in one minute. - Control variables: the same plant, the same temperature, the same volume of water, the same length of stem. - Repeat the count three times at each distance and calculate the mean . - Conclusion: as the distance of the lamp increases, the light intensity decreases ; as the light intensity increases, the rate of photosynthesis increases . - Safety: keep electrical wires, plugs and the lamp away from the water."],[18,"why-is-photosynthesis-important","Why is photosynthesis important?","1. It provides energy for all other organisms. Plants store some of the Sun's energy in carbohydrates. Animals get that energy when they eat plants, or eat animals that ate plants. A food chain shows the energy being passed along. 2. It provides oxygen for the atmosphere. Oxygen is a waste product of photosynthesis. It is released from leaves and mixes with the other gases in the air. About 20% of the air around us is oxygen, and all organisms need it for respiration. - When the Earth formed about 4.6 billion years ago there was almost no oxygen in the atmosphere. Oxygen began to collect once bacteria started to photosynthesise. Without photosynthesis, no animals could ever have lived here. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.1</b </summary 1 What are the reactants in photosynthesis? Water and carbon dioxide. 2 What are the products in photosynthesis? Glucose and oxygen. 3 The picture shows a lizard eating a grasshopper. Grasshoppers eat grass. Explain how the lizard relies on photosynthesis to provide it with energy. Grass photosynthesises and stores energy from sunlight in carbohydrates. The grasshopper eats the grass and takes in some of that energy. The lizard eats the grasshopper, so the energy in the lizard originally came from photosynthesis in the grass. 4 Explain one other reason (apart from food) why the lizard would not be able to survive if there were no plants on Earth. Plants release oxygen as a waste product of photosynthesis. The lizard needs oxygen for respiration, so without plants there would not be enough oxygen in the air. </details"],[18,"1-2-more-about-photosynthesis","1.2 More about photosynthesis","Key words: fertiliser · stomata (singular: stoma) · yield"],[18,"chloroplasts-and-chlorophyll","Chloroplasts and chlorophyll","- Chlorophyll is kept inside chloroplasts , which are inside plant cells. So photosynthesis happens inside chloroplasts . - Not all plant cells have chloroplasts, so not all plant cells can photosynthesise. In most plants the leaf cells have the most chloroplasts. - On warm, sunny days a plant can make more carbohydrate than it needs straight away. It stores the extra as starch , inside the chloroplasts, for use at night or in a season with less sunlight. - One way to check whether a leaf has been photosynthesising is to test it for starch ."],[18,"experiment-testing-a-leaf-for-starch","Experiment: testing a leaf for starch","Method 1. Boil water in a beaker on a tripod and gauze. Drop in a leaf for about two minutes — this breaks the cell membranes so iodine can get in. 2. Turn off the flame before using ethanol, because ethanol is very flammable. 3. Stand a test tube of ethanol with the leaf in it in the hot water. The green colour dissolves out of the leaf, so the colour change is easy to see. 4. Dip the brittle leaf in warm water to soften it, spread it on a tile and add iodine solution . Result — starch turns iodine solution blue-black . Parts with no starch stay orange-brown. Variegated leaves — a leaf with green parts and white parts only makes starch in the green parts, because only those parts contain chlorophyll."],[18,"inside-a-leaf","Inside a leaf","- Chloroplasts are mostly in the cells in the middle layers of the leaf. Leaves are very thin, so light reaches those cells easily. - The palisade layer does most of the photosynthesis. The spongy layer has lots of air spaces and does a small amount. - The upper and lower epidermis protect the cells inside; a waxy layer on the surface stops the leaf drying out. - A vein carries water to the cells in the leaf. - Stomata (singular: stoma) are tiny holes, mostly in the lower epidermis. Carbon dioxide diffuses in through them, down a concentration gradient, because photosynthesising cells keep using it up. Oxygen diffuses out the same way."],[18,"minerals-and-plant-growth","Minerals and plant growth","- Plants get minerals from the soil through their roots. They need only small quantities, but if the soil is short of one, the plant cannot grow to its full potential. - Farmers add fertiliser (which contains minerals) to increase the yield — the quantity of crop harvested. Mineral What it is needed for What happens if it is short Magnesium making chlorophyll leaves look yellow instead of green; less chlorophyll, so less photosynthesis and poor growth Nitrate (supplies nitrogen) converting carbohydrates into proteins , and also making chlorophyll leaves die and the plant stays small - Both shortages look similar, which is why they are hard to tell apart: both make the plant small and pale, because nitrogen is needed for chlorophyll as well as for protein. - Farmers can test the soil to find which minerals are lacking in each part of a field, and use GPS with their machinery to add fertiliser only where it is needed. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.2</b </summary 1 Use what you have learnt about diffusion to explain how carbon dioxide diffuses into the leaf. The cells inside the leaf use up carbon dioxide, so its concentration inside the leaf is lower than in the air outside. Carbon dioxide therefore diffuses from the higher concentration outside, through the stomata, into the air spaces inside the leaf. 2 The cells inside a leaf produce oxygen. Suggest what happens to this oxygen. Some is used by the cells for respiration. The rest diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata into the air, because its concentration inside the leaf is higher than outside. 3 Compare the way plants obtain protein with the way animals obtain protein. Plants make their own protein, using carbohydrates made in photosynthesis plus nitrate taken from the soil. Animals cannot make protein from minerals; they must eat plants or other animals to get it. 4 It is difficult to tell the difference between a plant short of magnesium and one short of nitrate. Suggest why. Nitrogen is needed to make chlorophyll as well as protein, and magnesium is needed to make chlorophyll too. So a shortage of either mineral gives a plant with little chlorophyll — pale or yellow leaves and poor growth. Starch test questions — a In which part of a cell is starch stored? In the chloroplasts. b Explain why it was important to boil the leaf before the starch test could work. Iodine solution cannot get through cell membranes. Boiling breaks the membranes apart so the iodine can reach the starch inside. </details"],[18,"1-3-the-carbon-cycle","1.3 The carbon cycle",""],[18,"carbon-and-living-organisms","Carbon and living organisms","- Carbon is an element ; its symbol is C . It is a non-metal and occurs naturally in different forms — diamond and graphite (the \"lead\" in a pencil) are both carbon. - Organisms cannot use carbon as an element. They can only use it as part of a compound . Carbohydrates, proteins and fats all contain carbon. - Plants take carbon dioxide from the air and use it in photosynthesis to make carbohydrates, then use those to make proteins and fats. - Animals get carbon compounds by feeding on plants or on other animals. Decomposers get theirs by breaking down waste products and dead remains."],[18,"returning-carbon-dioxide-to-the-air","Returning carbon dioxide to the air","- Respiration puts carbon dioxide back into the air: $$\\text{glucose} + \\text{oxygen} \\rightarrow \\text{carbon dioxide} + \\text{water}$$ - All organisms respire all the time. Plants respire at night too, when they cannot photosynthesise, so they give out carbon dioxide just as we do. Decomposers respire as they break down dead material."],[18,"investigation-hydrogencarbonate-indicator","Investigation: hydrogencarbonate indicator","Hydrogencarbonate indicator changes colour with the concentration of carbon dioxide: Colour Carbon dioxide concentration purple none red low yellow higher Tube Contents Light? What happens A water plant dark (black paper) plant only respires → carbon dioxide released → yellow B water plant light photosynthesis is faster than respiration → carbon dioxide used up → purple C maggots dark animals respire → carbon dioxide released → yellow D maggots light animals respire → carbon dioxide released → yellow E water plant + maggots dark both respire → carbon dioxide released → yellow F water plant + maggots light the plant photosynthesises faster than both respire → carbon dioxide used up → purple - The platform is put in every tube, even those with no animals, so that the tubes are otherwise identical — it is a control variable."],[18,"fossil-fuels-and-combustion","Fossil fuels and combustion","- When organisms die in places with no oxygen (a peat bog, deep ocean), decomposers cannot respire, so the bodies are not broken down. They are buried by sediment, and high pressure and heat slowly change them into fossil fuels : coal, oil and natural gas. - Oil and natural gas formed from tiny marine organisms that fell to the sea bed. Coal formed from plants that grew in huge swamps. - This takes a very long time — most fossil fuels used today formed hundreds of millions of years ago. That is why they are non-renewable : we use them far faster than they can be replaced. - Burning a fossil fuel is combustion : the carbon in it combines with oxygen from the air and forms carbon dioxide. - Fossil fuels are not fossils. A fossil is the remains or traces of an organism turned to rock, and you can still see the organism's shape. Fossil fuels look nothing like organisms."],[18,"the-carbon-cycle","The carbon cycle","Process Moves carbon Effect on carbon dioxide in the air photosynthesis air → plants decreases respiration organisms → air increases feeding plants → animals → decomposers no direct change decomposition dead remains → decomposers → air increases formation of fossil fuels dead organisms → fossil fuels (very slow) decreases combustion fossil fuels → air increases <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.3</b </summary 1 b The arrows in a food chain represent energy. Do they also show how carbon atoms pass along? Explain. Yes. The energy is passed on inside carbohydrates, fats and proteins, and all of those compounds contain carbon atoms. So when one organism eats another, carbon atoms move along the arrow as well as energy. 2 Name three different compounds in your body that contain carbon atoms. Carbohydrates (such as glucose), proteins and fats. 3 How would a carbon cycle drawn before humans were present differ from the one above? There would be no combustion of fossil fuels, so no arrow returning carbon from fossil fuels to the air (apart from natural fires). Much less carbon dioxide would be added to the atmosphere, so the amount in the air would stay roughly steady. 4 Explain why fossil fuels are non-renewable resources. They take hundreds of millions of years to form from dead organisms, but we burn them within a few hundred years. They are being used up far faster than they can be replaced. </details"],[18,"1-4-climate-change","1.4 Climate change","Key words: slush · mass extinction · meteorites · meteoroids · meteors"],[18,"greenhouse-gases","Greenhouse gases","- Carbon dioxide and methane are greenhouse gases. They trap heat energy in the atmosphere and keep the Earth warm. - Without any carbon dioxide the Earth would be frozen and could not support life. The problem is that there is now too much of it. - Carbon that was locked in fossil fuels for millions of years is released as carbon dioxide when we extract and burn them. Carbon dioxide levels are rising, and this is changing the climate."],[18,"climate-change-in-the-past","Climate change in the past","- Climate is the long-term pattern of temperature, wind and rainfall. - Ice ages: the first one we know about was around 2 billion years ago. Since then the Earth has cycled between warm periods (no ice, even at the poles) and cold periods called ice ages (ice at the poles). - Snowball Earth: perhaps about 650 million years ago the whole Earth was covered in ice and snow. It is also called \"slushball Earth\" because scientists are not sure everything froze completely ( slush is melting ice). The cause is still not fully understood. - Asteroid impact: dust thrown into the air meant less light reached the surface, so plants could not photosynthesise, food chains collapsed and animals had less food. Less heat from the Sun reached the surface, so the Earth became much colder. This caused a mass extinction — up to 75% of all species alive at the time died out, including the dinosaurs. - There are about 175 known asteroid impact craters on Earth. The crater in the USA is more than 1 km wide and 170 m deep, formed about 50 000 years ago."],[18,"objects-from-space","Objects from space","Term Meaning asteroid a large rocky object orbiting the Sun meteoroid an object in space smaller than an asteroid meteor a meteoroid that has entered Earth's atmosphere (a \"shooting star\") meteorite the part of a meteoroid that reaches Earth's surface — about 500 a year - Most meteors burn up because of the heat from friction with the atmosphere. Large ones can create shockwaves — in 2013 a meteor exploded over Russia and shattered windows. - Since 1998 scientists have made detailed observations of objects that could damage Earth. They assess the risk from the mass or diameter of the object and its closest possible approach to Earth. A more massive asteroid passing further away can be a greater risk than a small one passing close."],[18,"climate-change-today-and-in-the-future","Climate change today and in the future","- For the last 2000 years or so the climate has been fairly stable, which let people choose crops and know when to sow and harvest. - Now the mean temperature of the Earth is increasing, caused by rising carbon dioxide concentration. More carbon dioxide traps more heat close to the surface. - Between 1880 and 2016 the mean temperature rose by about 1.0 °C . The trend was roughly flat from 1880 to about 1910, then rose steadily from 1910 to 2016. Impacts - More extreme weather. Higher temperatures mean more energy in the atmosphere, so more and stronger hurricanes and typhoons. Tropical cyclone Idai hit south-east Africa in 2019 and killed more than 1300 people. No single storm can be blamed on climate change, because storms happen anyway — but there seem to be more of them and they are more violent. - Less predictable rainfall. Rains may come late, not at all, or far too heavily. The 2019 monsoon in Pakistan, India, Nepal and Bangladesh came late and was heavier than usual, flooding millions of people. Long droughts also increase the risk of wildfires . - Rising sea levels. Water expands when it is heated, so a warmer ocean takes up more space. Melting ice caps and glaciers on land add extra water. Sea level is rising about 3 mm per year , so roughly 300 mm (30 cm) more by the end of this century. More than 600 million people are at risk of flooding, and coastal megacities such as Shanghai, Mumbai and Los Angeles are especially vulnerable."],[18,"experiment-how-rising-temperature-affects-sea-level","Experiment: how rising temperature affects sea level","- Experiment 1 — melting ice. Ice floating in the water (like sea ice) does not change the level when it melts. Ice held above the water in a funnel (like ice on land) raises the level when it melts. - Experiment 2 — a conical flask completely full of cold coloured water with a thermometer and a thin glass tube. As a lamp warms the water, the water level in the tube rises , because water expands when heated. - Conclusion: sea level rises both because land ice melts and because sea water expands as it warms. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 1.4</b </summary 1 Describe what an asteroid is, and where asteroids are found. An asteroid is a large rocky object that orbits the Sun. Most are found in the asteroid belt, between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter. 2 Outline three ways in which the asteroid collision would have made survival difficult. Dust blocked sunlight, so plants could not photosynthesise and food chains collapsed. Less heat from the Sun reached the surface, so the Earth became much colder. The impact itself destroyed habitats over a huge area. 3 How much higher was the mean temperature in 2016 than in 1880? About 1.0 °C higher. 4 Describe the trend between 1880–1910 and between 1910–2016. Between 1880 and 1910 the temperature stayed roughly the same, with small ups and downs. Between 1910 and 2016 it rose steadily, and the rise became faster towards the end. 5 Calculate by how much sea level is predicted to rise by the end of this century. About 3 mm per year for about 100 years, so roughly 300 mm (30 cm). 6 Explain why sea level is predicted to rise. Sea water expands as it gets warmer, and ice caps and glaciers on land are melting and adding extra water to the oceans. </details"],[18,"check-your-progress-worked-answers","Check your progress — worked answers","1.1 Choose the correct word. a carbon dioxide · b oxygen · c soil · d chlorophyll · e stomata 1.2 Marcus compares two types of seaweed. a The type of seaweed. b Any three of: light intensity, temperature, mass of seaweed, volume of seawater, time left. c The volume of gas collected, and the time taken to collect it. 1.3 Fertiliser and grain yield. a About 2 tonnes per hectare. b Above about 60 kg per hectare the graph levels off, so extra fertiliser gives almost no extra grain; since fertiliser is expensive, the extra cost is not repaid. c Nitrate supplies nitrogen, which the plant needs to turn carbohydrates into protein for making new cells, so the plants grow larger and produce more grain. d The soil elsewhere may already contain a different amount of nitrate, and the temperature, rainfall and light would differ. e Magnesium — it is needed to make chlorophyll, without which the plant cannot photosynthesise properly. 1.4 The carbon cycle diagram. a i carbon in fossil fuels · ii carbon compounds in plants · iii carbon dioxide in the air · iv carbon compounds in animals. b i feeding · ii combustion · iii photosynthesis. c Any of: carbohydrate (starch, glucose, cellulose), protein, fat. 1.5 Climate change and mass extinction. a Tick: \"Sea level will rise\" and \"There will be more extreme weather events, such as typhoons and hurricanes.\" b i A mass extinction is when a very large number of species — a large proportion of all species alive at the time — die out over a short period. ii Dust thrown up by the impact blocks sunlight, so plants cannot photosynthesise and die. Animals that eat plants then starve, and so do the animals that eat them. The Earth also becomes much colder because less heat reaches the surface, so many species cannot survive."],[19,"2-1-atomic-structure-and-the-periodic-table","2.1 Atomic structure and the Periodic Table","Key words: atomic number · concentric · density · electron shells · electronic structure · electrostatic forces · energy levels · highest energy level · mass number · outermost electron shell · Periodic Table"],[19,"the-periodic-table","The Periodic Table","- The Periodic Table lists the elements in order. The columns are called groups and the rows are called periods . Metals are on the left, non-metals on the right. - Atoms get heavier as you go left to right and down the table. An atom of hydrogen has less mass than an atom of sodium. - Every element has an atomic number — the number of protons in one of its atoms. It goes up by one for every element. - Every element has a mass number — the number of protons plus neutrons in one of its atoms. - Protons carry a charge of +1 ; electrons carry a charge of −1 ; neutrons carry no charge. - An atom has no overall charge , because it has the same number of protons as electrons. Worked example — lithium (Li) atomic number 3 mass number 7 protons 3 electrons 3 neutrons 7 − 3 = 4 The rule: neutrons = mass number − atomic number."],[19,"mass-and-density","Mass and density","- The higher the mass number, the more mass one atom has. Hydrogen has an atomic mass of 1 and carbon has an atomic mass of 12, so one carbon atom has 12 times the mass of one hydrogen atom. - Compare equal volumes: 1 cm³ of sodium has a mass of 0.97 g , while 1 cm³ of iron has a mass of 7.87 g . - The mass of a fixed volume of a substance is its density , measured in g/cm³ . Iron (7.87 g/cm³) is more dense than sodium (0.97 g/cm³)."],[19,"arranging-electrons","Arranging electrons","- Thomson and Rutherford built the first models of the atom. In Rutherford's model the electrons were drawn as one cloud around the nucleus. - In 1913 the Danish scientist Niels Bohr showed that electrons move in separate electron shells (also called energy levels ) around the nucleus. He won a Nobel prize for it, and his idea is still the model used today. - The arrangement of the electrons in the shells is the electronic structure . How many electrons fit in a shell Shell Room for first 2 electrons second 8 electrons third 8 electrons - The first shell always fills up before electrons go into the second shell. - Electrons are held in place by electrostatic forces — the pull between the positive protons and the negative electrons. - The electronic structure is written as numbers. Boron is 2,3 : two electrons in the first shell and three in the second."],[19,"activity-2-1-1-a-card-model-of-an-atom","Activity 2.1.1 — a card model of an atom","- A white circle of card is the base for the nucleus ; small red circles are protons and blue circles are neutrons, stuck on both sides of it. - Concentric rings of card about 2 cm wide are the electron shells ; small green circles stuck on both sides of a ring are the electrons. - The finished model hangs from a string , with a label giving the element's name, symbol and its numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.1</b </summary 1 How many electrons are there in an atom of carbon? 6. 2 How many protons are there in an atom of beryllium? 4. 3 How many neutrons are there in an atom of boron? 6. (Mass number 11 − atomic number 5 = 6.) 4 Draw a diagram to show the structure of an atom of magnesium. Three shells with the electronic structure 2,8,2, around a nucleus containing 12 protons and 12 neutrons. 5 What is the name of the element that has the electronic structure 2,8,3? Aluminium. 6 Draw a labelled atomic diagram of the element fluorine. Two shells with the electronic structure 2,7, around a nucleus containing 9 protons and 10 neutrons. 7 How is the model of the atom we use today different from the Rutherford model? In both models the nucleus is made of protons and neutrons. In the Rutherford model the electrons are all drawn in one cloud around the nucleus. In today's model the electrons are arranged in separate shells, or energy levels, and each shell can hold only up to a certain number of electrons. </details"],[19,"2-2-trends-in-groups-within-the-periodic-table","2.2 Trends in Groups within the Periodic Table","Key words: alkali metals · halogens · noble gases"],[19,"group-1-the-alkali-metals","Group 1: the alkali metals","The first column of the Periodic Table is Group 1 , also called the alkali metals . It contains lithium, sodium and potassium. Element Atomic number Mass number Melting point / °C Boiling point / °C lithium, Li 3 7 180 1360 sodium, Na 11 23 98 900 potassium, K 19 39 63 777 - Going down the group, the atomic number and the mass number increase — so the atoms are getting bigger . - Going down the group, the melting points and boiling points decrease . The next element down is rubidium , so we can predict its melting point is below 63 °C."],[19,"think-like-a-scientist-group-1-metals-reacting-with-water","Think like a scientist — Group 1 metals reacting with water","Safety — the teacher demonstrates behind a safety screen , everyone wears safety glasses , only a small piece of metal is cut with a scalpel and moved with long forceps , and the metal goes into a large trough of water . Word equations $$\\text{lithium} + \\text{water} \\rightarrow \\text{lithium hydroxide} + \\text{hydrogen}$$ $$\\text{sodium} + \\text{water} \\rightarrow \\text{sodium hydroxide} + \\text{hydrogen}$$ $$\\text{potassium} + \\text{water} \\rightarrow \\text{potassium hydroxide} + \\text{hydrogen}$$ - Similarities: every metal floats, moves about on the surface, fizzes, gives off heat and produces hydrogen gas and an alkaline hydroxide . They are all soft enough to cut, and all light enough to float. - Differences: the reaction gets more violent as you go down the group — lithium fizzes gently, potassium gets hot enough to catch fire. - The group is called the alkali metals because the hydroxides they make dissolve to give alkaline solutions. - Rubidium is not used in schools because the trend says its reaction would be extremely violent — it explodes hard enough to crack the container."],[19,"the-structure-of-the-group-1-metals","The structure of the Group 1 metals","Element Atomic number Mass number Protons Electrons Neutrons Electronic structure lithium 3 7 3 3 4 2,1 sodium 11 23 11 11 12 2,8,1 potassium 19 39 19 19 20 2,8,8,1 The pattern that explains the group: every Group 1 atom has exactly one electron in its outermost shell , with all the shells below it full. That is why they behave alike — and why the group is numbered 1."],[19,"group-7-the-halogens","Group 7: the halogens","Element Atomic number Electronic structure Mass number Colour Melting point / °C Boiling point / °C fluorine, F 9 2,7 19 pale yellow −220 −188 chlorine, Cl 17 2,8,7 35 yellowish green −101 −34 bromine, Br 35 (2,8,18,7) 80 brown −7 59 - Fluorine and chlorine are gases at room temperature; bromine is a liquid . - Reactivity falls down the group: fluorine is the most reactive, then chlorine, then bromine. - Melting points and boiling points rise down the group, and the colour gets darker . - Fluorine has 9 protons, 9 electrons and 10 neutrons; its structure is 2,7 . Chlorine has 17 protons, 17 electrons and 18 neutrons; its structure is 2,8,7 . The pattern: every halogen atom has seven electrons in its outermost shell ."],[19,"group-8-the-noble-gases","Group 8: the noble gases","Element Atomic number Electronic structure Mass number Melting point / °C Boiling point / °C helium, He 2 2 4 −270 −269 neon, Ne 10 2,8 20 −249 −246 argon, Ar 18 2,8,8 40 −189 −186 - Helium: 2 protons, 2 electrons, 2 neutrons. Neon: 10 protons, 10 electrons, 10 neutrons. Argon: 18 protons, 18 electrons, 22 neutrons. - All of them are gases, and all of them are inert — unreactive — so they form no compounds. Why they are inert: every shell in a noble gas atom is full . A full outermost shell is the most stable arrangement there is, so the atom has no reason to react. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.2</b </summary 1 Where in the Periodic Table do you find the metals? On the left-hand side of the table. 2 What is the trend in the boiling points of Group 1 elements? They decrease as you go down the group. 3 What prediction can you make about the boiling point of rubidium? It is lower than 777 °C (the boiling point of potassium, the element above it). 4 How many more electrons than lithium does sodium have? Eight more (3 → 11). 5 What happens to the size of the atoms as you go down this group? The atoms get bigger. 6 What similarity is there in the structure of these atoms? They all have one electron in the outermost shell, and a full inner shell of two electrons. 7 Suggest why this group of metals is called Group 1? Because each atom has one electron in its outermost shell. 8 What are the trends in the structure and behaviour of these elements in Group 1? The atoms get larger going down the group, and their reaction with water gets more violent as the atoms get larger. 9 Are the halogens metals or non-metals? Non-metals. 10 What is the trend in melting points in Group 7? The melting points increase as you go down the group. 11 What is the trend in colour in Group 7? The colour gets darker as you go down the group. 12 What is the trend in boiling points in Group 7? The boiling points increase as you go down the group. 13 What would you predict about the boiling and melting points of iodine, the next element in Group 7? Its melting point would be above −7 °C and its boiling point above 59 °C. 14 Would you expect iodine to be more or less reactive than bromine? Less reactive — reactivity falls as you go down Group 7. 15 The electronic structure for bromine is missing from the table. How many electrons do you predict will be in the outer shell? Seven. 16 What happens to the size of the atoms as you go down this group? The atoms get bigger. 17 What similarity is there in the structure of these atoms? They all have seven electrons in their outer shell. 18 Suggest why this group is called Group 17. Because every atom in it has seven electrons in its outermost shell. 19 What trend in melting points can be seen in Group 8? The melting points increase as you go down the group. 20 What happens to the size of the atoms as you go down Group 8? The atoms get bigger. 21 What similarity is there in the structure of these atoms? Their outer shells are all full, with eight electrons — except helium, whose single shell is full with two. 22 Suggest why this group is called Group 8. Because the atoms have eight electrons in their outer shell. 23 What predictions can you make about the melting and boiling points of krypton, which is the next gas in this group? They will both be higher than those of argon. </details"],[19,"2-3-why-elements-react-to-form-compounds","2.3 Why elements react to form compounds","Key words: chemical bonds · covalent bond · dot and cross diagram · ion · ionic bond · ionic compounds · molecule · stable"],[19,"why-elements-react-at-all","Why elements react at all","- The shell furthest from the nucleus is the outermost electron shell , the one with the highest energy level . - An atom is most stable when its outermost shell is completely full . - The noble gases already have full outer shells, so they do not react. - Every other element has an incomplete outer shell, so it reacts in order to fill it. The atoms are then held together by chemical bonds , made in one of two ways: - atoms lose or gain electrons → an ionic bond - atoms share electrons → a covalent bond"],[19,"losing-electrons","Losing electrons","- A sodium atom (2,8,1) loses the single electron in its outer shell. The shell underneath, which is already full, becomes the new outermost shell — so the particle left behind is more stable than the atom was. - Losing one negative electron leaves one extra positive proton, so the particle carries a charge of +1 . It is now an ion , written Na⁺ , with the electronic structure [2,8]⁺ ."],[19,"gaining-electrons","Gaining electrons","- A chlorine atom (2,8,7) needs just one more electron to fill its outer shell, so it gains one. - Gaining one negative electron gives it one more electron than it has protons, so it carries a charge of −1 : Cl⁻ , structure [2,8,8]⁻ . It is now a chloride ion , and it is more stable than the atom. The one sentence to remember: metals lose electrons and become positive ions; non-metals gain electrons and become negative ions."],[19,"ionic-bonding","Ionic bonding","- Sodium hands its outer electron to chlorine. Now there is a positive Na⁺ and a negative Cl⁻ . - Opposite charges attract, and that attraction is the ionic bond . The compound formed is sodium chloride, NaCl . - Counting the charge on Na⁺: 11 protons, 10 electrons → 11 positive against 10 negative → +1 . - Counting the charge on Cl⁻: 17 protons, 18 electrons → 17 positive against 18 negative → −1 . - Other Group 1 metals react with Group 7 elements in exactly the same way: $$\\text{lithium} + \\text{chlorine} \\rightarrow \\text{lithium chloride}$$ $$\\text{lithium} + \\text{fluorine} \\rightarrow \\text{lithium fluoride}$$ $$\\text{potassium} + \\text{chlorine} \\rightarrow \\text{potassium chloride}$$ $$\\text{potassium} + \\text{fluorine} \\rightarrow \\text{potassium fluoride}$$"],[19,"why-reactivity-changes-down-a-group","Why reactivity changes down a group","- Group 1: potassium is the most reactive and lithium the least. The outer electron of potassium sits furthest from the nucleus , so the electrostatic pull holding it is weakest and it escapes most easily. Lithium's outer electron is closest in, so it is hardest to remove. - Group 7: fluorine is more reactive than chlorine. Fluorine's outer shell is closest to the nucleus , so an incoming electron is pulled in most strongly. Both trends come from the same idea: the further an electron is from the nucleus, the weaker the electrostatic force on it."],[19,"ionic-compounds-where-two-electrons-move","Ionic compounds where two electrons move","Magnesium oxide, MgO - A magnesium atom is 2,8,2 — two electrons in the outer shell. An oxygen atom is 2,6 — six in its outer shell. - Magnesium loses both outer electrons and becomes Mg²⁺ , structure [2,8]²⁺. Oxygen gains both and becomes the oxide ion O²⁻ , structure [2,8]²⁻. - The two ions attract each other and form magnesium oxide, MgO . Magnesium chloride, MgCl₂ - Magnesium still has two electrons to give away, but each chlorine atom can only take one . - So two chlorine atoms are needed. Magnesium becomes Mg²⁺ and the two chlorine atoms become two Cl⁻ ions. - The formula is MgCl₂ — the little 2 records that two chloride ions are needed to balance one magnesium ion. Ionic compounds form when a metal reacts with a non-metal."],[19,"sharing-electrons-covalent-bonds","Sharing electrons — covalent bonds","Non-metals cannot both lose electrons, so instead they share them. A shared pair counts as full membership of both atoms' outer shells. Hydrogen chloride, HCl - A hydrogen atom has 1 electron in a shell with room for 2. A chlorine atom has 7 in a shell with room for 8. - They share one pair of electrons, and now both outer shells are full. The particle formed is a molecule of hydrogen chloride, HCl . - Drawing one atom's electrons as dots and the other's as crosses gives a dot and cross diagram . This kind of shared-electron bond is a covalent bond . Hydrogen, H₂ - Two hydrogen atoms share one pair of electrons, so each has two electrons in its only shell. The molecule is H₂ . - This shows that covalent molecules form between atoms of the same non-metal as well as between different ones. Ammonia, NH₃ - A nitrogen atom has atomic number 7: two electrons in the first shell and five in its outer shell, which has room for eight. - Three hydrogen atoms each share a pair with the nitrogen — three shared pairs in all — so every atom ends up with a full outer shell. The molecule is NH₃ . Molecule Formula Element or compound? hydrogen H₂ element oxygen O₂ element nitrogen N₂ element chlorine Cl₂ element hydrogen chloride HCl compound water H₂O compound ammonia NH₃ compound methane CH₄ compound carbon dioxide CO₂ compound <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.3</b </summary 1 What is the electronic structure of a sodium atom? 2,8,1. 2 What is the electronic structure of a sodium ion? 2,8 (written [2,8]⁺). 3 What is the symbol for a chlorine atom? Cl. 4 What is the symbol for a chlorine ion? Cl⁻. 5 Draw the electronic structure of a potassium ion. Three shells with the electron structure 2,8,8, around a nucleus. 6 Draw the electronic structure for a fluorine ion. Two shells with the electron structure 2,8, around a nucleus. 7 Potassium is more reactive than lithium because it can lose an electron more easily. Why is this? The outer electron of potassium is a long way from the nucleus, so the electrostatic pull of the protons on it is weak and it escapes easily. In lithium the outer electron is much closer to the nucleus, so that pull is far stronger and the electron is harder to remove. 8 Fluorine is more reactive than chlorine because it can gain an electron more easily. Why is this? Fluorine's outermost shell is closer to the nucleus than chlorine's, so an extra electron is attracted to it more strongly. 9 Draw diagrams to explain how the structure of an atom of calcium is different from an ion of calcium. The calcium atom has four shells with the structure 2,8,8,2. The calcium ion has lost both outer electrons, so it has three shells with the structure 2,8,8 and a charge of 2+. 10 How many ions of chlorine react with one ion of calcium to form calcium chloride? Two. 11 Write the chemical formula for calcium chloride. CaCl₂. 12 Write the chemical formula for calcium oxide. CaO. 13 Which of the molecules shown in the diagram above are compounds? Carbon dioxide, methane, water, hydrogen chloride and ammonia. (The others — H₂, O₂, N₂ and Cl₂ — contain only one kind of atom, so they are elements.) 14 Draw a dot and cross diagram to show how a molecule of chlorine is formed. Two chlorine atoms, each with seven outer electrons, share one pair between them — draw one atom's electrons as dots and the other's as crosses. Each atom then has eight electrons in its outer shell. 15 Is the compound calcium chloride an ionic or a covalent compound? Give a reason for your answer. Ionic, because it is formed from a metal (calcium) and a non-metal (chlorine). 16 Write the formula for a molecule of methane, carbon dioxide and nitrogen. Methane CH₄; carbon dioxide CO₂; nitrogen N₂. </details"],[19,"2-4-simple-and-giant-structures","2.4 Simple and giant structures","Key words: graphite · intermolecular forces · lattice · layers"],[19,"giant-structures-in-ionic-compounds","Giant structures in ionic compounds","- In sodium chloride the Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions have equal and opposite charges, so the electrostatic forces between them are strong. Those forces act in all directions . - The result is not a molecule but a giant structure called a lattice , repeating in every direction. - In the lattice each Na⁺ ion is surrounded by six Cl⁻ ions . - Because the ions sit in a regular repeating pattern, salt grows crystals with a regular shape ."],[19,"simple-molecules-and-the-forces-between-them","Simple molecules and the forces between them","- Many covalent substances — oxygen, carbon dioxide, methane — are made of simple molecules . - The covalent bonds inside each molecule are very strong . The forces between separate molecules are weak ; these are the intermolecular forces . - That difference is the key to their properties: melting or boiling only has to overcome the weak forces between the molecules, never the strong bonds inside them."],[19,"giant-covalent-structures-diamond-and-graphite","Giant covalent structures: diamond and graphite","Not every covalent substance is a simple molecule. Silicon dioxide , diamond and graphite are giant covalent structures , also called macromolecules . Diamond - Every carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds, giving a rigid three-dimensional lattice. - This makes diamond the hardest material on Earth — used for jewellery, and for cutting and drilling tools. Graphite - Every carbon atom forms only three covalent bonds, which builds flat layers . - The bonds inside a layer are strong, but the bonds between the layers are weak , so the layers slide over one another very easily. - That makes graphite soft and slippery: it is used as the \"lead\" in pencils and as a lubricant for moving parts in machines. A pencil mark is layers of graphite sliding off onto the paper. Same element, opposite properties. Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon. The difference is the structure : four bonds per atom in a rigid 3-D network versus three bonds per atom in sliding layers."],[19,"melting-points-and-boiling-points","Melting points and boiling points","Substance Melting point / °C Boiling point / °C sodium chloride 801 1413 methane −182 −161 magnesium chloride 714 1412 ammonia −77 −34 calcium oxide 2613 2850 chlorine −101 −34.6 water 0 100 - Ionic substances have very high melting and boiling points , because the electrostatic forces holding the whole lattice together are extremely strong and a great deal of energy is needed to break them. - Simple covalent molecules have low melting and boiling points , because only the weak intermolecular forces have to be overcome. - Giant covalent structures also have very high melting points , because melting them means breaking strong covalent bonds. How to read the table: a melting point in the hundreds or thousands points to a giant structure — ionic or giant covalent. A melting point below room temperature points to simple molecules."],[19,"conducting-electricity","Conducting electricity","- Ionic compounds conduct electricity when dissolved in water or when melted , because the ions carry an electrical charge and, once the lattice has broken up, they are free to move and carry that charge through the circuit. - A solid ionic crystal does not conduct: the ions are locked in the lattice and cannot move. - Simple covalent molecules never conduct , because they have no charged particles at all. The test — carbon rods dipped into the substance, wired to a battery and a bulb. If the bulb lights, the charge is moving. Safety — some crystals and solutions, such as copper sulfate, are irritants : wear surgical gloves and safety glasses and avoid touching them."],[19,"summary-of-the-properties","Summary of the properties","Ionic substances Covalent substances Structure giant lattices of ions simple molecules, or giant macromolecules Melting / boiling point very high — the forces between ions are very strong low for simple molecules (weak forces between them); very high for macromolecules (strong covalent bonds) Conducting electricity conducts when dissolved or melted, because the ions are then free to move simple molecules do not conduct <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 2.4</b </summary 1 How is an ionic bond different from a covalent one? An ionic bond forms when atoms lose or gain electrons; a covalent bond forms when atoms share electrons. 2 When a metal joins with a non-metal, is a covalent or an ionic compound made? An ionic compound. 3 Oxygen atoms join together to form an oxygen molecule. Explain what the word molecule means. A molecule is a particle made of more than one atom, where the atoms share at least one pair of electrons in a covalent bond. 4 What is a macromolecule? Give an example. A giant molecule. Silicon dioxide, diamond and graphite are examples. 5 Is magnesium chloride an ionic compound or a simple molecule with covalent bonds? Give reasons for your answer. Ionic: it has very high melting and boiling points (714 °C and 1412 °C), and it is made from a metal and a non-metal. 6 Is ammonia an ionic compound or a simple molecule with covalent bonds? Give reasons for your answer. A simple molecule with covalent bonds: it has very low melting and boiling points (−77 °C and −34 °C), and it is made from two non-metals. 7 Is ammonia a solid, liquid or gas at room temperature? A gas — its boiling point of −34 °C is far below room temperature. 8 Why do magnesium chloride and calcium oxide have high melting points? They are ionic compounds. The electrostatic forces between the ions are very strong, so a great deal of energy is needed to break them and melt the solid. 9 Why do methane and chlorine have low melting points? They are made of simple molecules. The forces inside each molecule are strong, but the forces between the molecules are weak, so little energy is needed to separate them. 10 Look at this photograph of copper sulfate crystals. What sort of chemical bonds does this substance have? Ionic bonds — it forms a giant structure of crystals and it is made from a metal and a non-metal. 11 A substance has a melting point of 3078 °C and a boiling point of 4300 °C. What sort of chemical bonds does it have? Ionic bonds, because the melting and boiling points are extremely high. 12 Silicon dioxide is a compound formed from two non-metals. It is a hard substance with a melting point of 1610 °C. Which properties suggest that it has a giant covalent structure? It is hard and its melting point is very high — properties you would not expect from simple covalent molecules. Since it is made from two non-metals its bonds must be covalent, so the only explanation is a giant covalent structure. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — ionic compounds conducting electricity</b </summary 1 Did all your ionic solutions conduct electricity? Yes. 2 Explain your answer. When an ionic compound dissolves, the lattice breaks up and the ions become free to move about in the solution. Moving ions carry the electric charge, so the solution conducts. 3 Did all the ionic crystals conduct electricity? No. 4 Explain your answer. In a solid crystal the ions are locked in a strong lattice, so they cannot move to carry the charge. (If a crystal did seem to conduct, check whether the electrodes were touching each other or the crystal was wet.) 5 Predict, with reasons, what would happen if you used a covalent substance instead. It would not conduct. The forces inside the molecules are strong and there are no charged particles free to carry the current. </details"],[19,"check-your-progress-worked-answers","Check your progress — worked answers","2.1 Four elements from the same group (lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium). a Between 63 °C and 180 °C — the melting points fall steadily down the group. b Between 688 °C and 883 °C, for the same reason. c Hydrogen. d Sodium would give off more bubbles of gas and more heat than lithium, but less than potassium. e lithium 7 · sodium 23 · potassium 39 · rubidium 85. f The mass number tells you the number of protons plus the number of neutrons in the atom. g A nucleus with three shells around it, holding the electrons 2,8,1. 2.2 The fluorine atom, electronic structure 2,7. a The same diagram with one extra electron in the outer shell, giving 2,8. b Because its outermost shell — the highest energy level — is now full, and a full outer shell is the stable arrangement. c F⁻. 2.3 Covalent or ionic? a metal + non-metal → ionic b two atoms share electrons → covalent c two non-metals join → covalent d dissolves in water and conducts → ionic e atoms gain or lose electrons → ionic 2.4 The molecule of methane. CH₄. 2.5 The lattice structure of sodium chloride. a The strong electrostatic forces between the positive sodium ions and the negative chloride ions. b They are high, because those electrostatic forces are strong and a lot of energy is needed to break them."],[19,"project-building-the-periodic-table","Project: building the Periodic Table","Year Scientist Contribution 1817 Johann Dobereiner The 'Law of Triads' — grouped elements in threes by their properties and appearance. 1864 John Newlands Arranged the 63 known elements in order of atomic mass and noticed that every eighth element had similar properties. The pattern did not hold all the way through. 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev Took Newlands' idea and left gaps so that the pattern of eights continued and similar elements stayed grouped. He predicted the properties of the missing elements , and he was right. Mendeleev's table is the basis of the Periodic Table used today. His table contained no helium, argon or neon because the noble gases had not been discovered yet — they are inert, so they form no compounds and are very hard to detect. The point of the story: science advances by scientists building on each other's work and changing their ideas when new information arrives."],[20,"3-1-density","3.1 Density","Key words: density · hollow · irregular · regular · solid"],[20,"mass-volume-and-the-idea-of-density","Mass, volume and the idea of density","- People often say metal is \"heavier\" than polystyrene. That is sloppy. A very large block of polystyrene can have a larger mass than a small piece of metal. - What is always true is this: the same volume of iron has a larger mass than the same volume of polystyrene. The word for that difference is density . - Hollow means the object has a space filled with air inside it — a polystyrene food container. Solid here is the opposite of hollow: an iron nail has no air space inside."],[20,"calculating-volume","Calculating volume","- A regular block has faces that meet at right angles . Measure the sides and multiply them: - a block 10 mm × 6 mm × 4 mm has a volume of 10 × 6 × 4 = 240 mm³ . - The order you multiply in makes no difference to the answer. - The unit follows the unit of the sides: sides in mm give mm³, sides in cm give cm³. - An irregular object has sides or angles that are not equal, so there is nothing to multiply. Use the displacement method : - put the object into a measuring cylinder of water and read the water level before and after; - the rise in the water level is the volume of the object . - Worked example: the water starts at 40 cm³ and rises to 56 cm³, so the rock's volume is 56 − 40 = 16 cm³ ."],[20,"the-density-equation","The density equation","density = mass ÷ volume - Rearranged: mass = density × volume , and volume = mass ÷ density . - The unit of density is the unit of mass divided by the unit of volume: - mass in g and volume in cm³ → density in g/cm³ - mass in kg and volume in m³ → density in kg/m³ Iron Polystyrene volume 1 cm × 1 cm × 1 cm = 1 cm³ 1 cm × 1 cm × 1 cm = 1 cm³ mass 7.9 g 0.05 g density 7.9 ÷ 1 = 7.9 g/cm³ 0.05 ÷ 1 = 0.05 g/cm³"],[20,"comparing-densities","Comparing densities","Material Density / g/cm³ helium 0.00018 air 0.0012 soft wood 0.35 – 0.52 hard wood 0.60 – 0.95 cooking oil 0.91 – 0.93 water 1.0 concrete 2.4 aluminium 2.7 iron 7.9 osmium 22 - Gases have the lowest densities because their particles are far apart. As particles are packed closer together the density rises, so the highest densities in the table are all solids . - For the elements, density usually increases as the atomic number increases . Osmium (atomic number 76) is denser than iron (atomic number 26). Did you know? The densest substance in the universe is found in collapsed stars. The forces there are so large that atoms are crushed and the electrons, neutrons and protons are pushed together. This material is called neutronium , with a density of about 1 000 000 000 000 000 g/cm³."],[20,"floating-and-sinking","Floating and sinking","- Compare an object's density with the density of water (1.0 g/cm³) : - more dense than water → it sinks - less dense than water → it floats - Swimming pool floats are made from polystyrene, or are hollow with only air inside , to keep their density very low. - How a steel ship floats. Steel has a density of 8–9 g/cm³, far more than water. But a ship is not a solid block of steel: it contains large spaces full of air, so its volume is huge and its average density — the mass of everything on board, air included, divided by the total volume — is less than 1.0 g/cm³ . - Cargo, passengers and fuel all add mass, which raises the ship's density . Load a ship too heavily and its density passes that of water, and it sinks."],[20,"liquids-of-different-density","Liquids of different density","- Pour liquids together carefully and they form separate layers , with the less dense liquids floating on the more dense ones . - In the picture, honey has the highest density and vegetable oil the lowest . - Crude oil is less dense than water, which is why oil spilled from a ship floats on the sea and is washed up on beaches. Oil spills are a major source of water pollution."],[20,"gases-and-liquids","Gases and liquids","- Gases are less dense than liquids because their particles are much further apart . - Raindrops fall because the water in them is more dense than air . - The gas in a fizzy drink is carbon dioxide, density 0.002 g/cm³ ; the drink itself is mostly water, density about 1.0 g/cm³ . The bubbles rise because the gas is far less dense than the liquid."],[20,"gases-of-different-density","Gases of different density","- Helium is one of the least dense gases, so a helium balloon floats up through the air. - Hydrogen is even less dense than helium, but it is not used in balloons because it forms an explosive mixture with air . - Solids and liquids are hard to compress — their particles are already touching. Gases compress easily because their particles are far apart. - Compress a gas: same number of particles in a smaller volume → density increases . - Let a gas expand: same number of particles in a larger volume → density decreases . - Heating makes a gas expand, which is why a hot air balloon floats: the hot air inside is less dense than the colder air outside. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.1</b </summary 1 Densities: pine wood 0.41, polycarbonate 1.2, polyethene 0.95, water 1.0 g/cm³. a Pine wood has the lowest density. b i Polycarbonate will sink. ii Its density (1.2 g/cm³) is greater than the density of water (1.0 g/cm³). 2 a The equation for density. density = mass ÷ volume. b 13.5 ÷ 5.0 = 2.7 g/cm³ . c It will sink: 2.7 g/cm³ is greater than 1.0 g/cm³. 3 A block of wood 4 mm × 3 mm × 6 mm... (sides given in the diagram). a Multiply the three sides, then convert to cm³ (1 cm³ = 1000 mm³). b density = 54 g ÷ the volume you calculated. c Compare that density with 1.0 g/cm³: less → floats, more → sinks. 4 Sea water: volume 10 m³, mass 10 300 kg. a 10 300 ÷ 10 = 1030 kg/m³ . b i In sea water (1030 kg/m³) a block of density 1020 kg/m³ floats . ii In pure water (1000 kg/m³) the same block sinks . 5 Copper, density 8.96 g/cm³. a mass = density × volume = 8.96 × 20 = 179.2 g . b volume = mass ÷ density = 4.5 ÷ 8.96 = 0.50 cm³ . 6 A toy car in a measuring cylinder. a Volume = final reading − starting reading, read from the diagram. b density = 84 g ÷ that volume. c The car is not solid steel — it is partly plastic, which is less dense, and it may contain air spaces. Both lower the average density below 7.8 g/cm³. 7 Three liquids: kerosene 0.81, mercury 14, water 1.0 g/cm³. The least dense floats on top: layer A = kerosene , layer B = water , layer C = mercury . 8 A balloon that floats in air. The gas inside must have a density lower than that of air . 9 Why hot air balloons fly early and late in the day. They rise because the air inside is much hotter, and so much less dense, than the air outside. In the middle of the day the outside air is already hot, so the density difference is small and the lift is poor. (Early and late the air is also calmer.) </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.1.1 — densities of regular objects</b </summary Method in one line: measure length, width and height to the nearest 0.1 cm, multiply for the volume, weigh the object, then divide. 2 Why check that the balance reads 0.0 g first? So that there is no zero error . If the balance starts at anything other than zero, every mass you record is wrong by that amount. 3 Blocks that are old and slightly damaged. a The volume is wrong : you calculate it from the outside dimensions as though the block were a perfect box, but material is missing from the damaged corners, so the true volume is smaller than the value you calculated. b The masses are still correct — a balance measures whatever is actually there. c You divide a correct (smaller) mass by a volume that is too large, so the calculated density comes out too low . </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — densities of irregular objects</b </summary Safety: do not drop heavy objects into a glass measuring cylinder. Use a plastic one, or tilt the glass cylinder so the object slides down slowly. 1 Why read the volume with your eye level with the liquid surface? To avoid a parallax error — reading from above or below makes the scale line up with the wrong part of the surface. Read the bottom of the meniscus. 2 The volume of an object that floats. The displacement method only works for an object that is completely under the water , so push it under with a thin needle or pin, or tie it to a sinker of known volume and subtract that volume afterwards. 3 An object too big for the measuring cylinder. Use a displacement (eureka) can : fill it to the spout, lower the object in, and collect the water that overflows in a measuring cylinder. 4 Zara gets 52.7 g/cm³ for a wooden toy. Anomalous. Wood floats, so its density must be below 1.0 g/cm³; 52.7 g/cm³ is denser than iron. She has probably mixed up mass and volume, or misread the cylinder. </details"],[20,"3-2-heat-and-temperature","3.2 Heat and temperature",""],[20,"heat","Heat","- Thermal energy can be stored in an object and transferred between objects, and it eventually dissipates into the surroundings. It is measured in joules (J) . - When the thermal energy of an object increases, its particles vibrate faster — the energy of the particles increases. - Heat is the total thermal energy of all the vibrating particles in an object. - Two glasses holding the same volume of water: the one at the higher temperature has the greater heat, because its particles move faster while the number of particles is the same. - Two glasses at the same temperature but holding different volumes : the larger volume has the greater heat, because it has more particles , so the total energy of all of them is larger."],[20,"temperature","Temperature","- Temperature is not the same as heat. Temperature tells you two things: - the direction thermal energy will be transferred; - the average energy of the particles. - Thermal energy is always transferred from higher temperature to lower temperature . Ice cream at −20 °C in a room at 24 °C is a temperature difference of 44 °C, so thermal energy moves from the air into the ice cream . - The bigger the temperature difference, the faster the transfer. - Because temperature is an average , it lets you compare particle energy in objects of different size or material. Saying the soup is hotter than the water means 100 particles of soup carry more energy than 100 particles of water. Hold both ideas at once: heat = total energy of the particles · temperature = average energy of the particles."],[20,"heat-and-temperature-in-a-sparkler","Heat and temperature in a sparkler","- A sparkler burns at about 1000 °C , yet a spark landing on your hand does not burn you badly. Why not? - A spark has a very small mass , so very few particles , so its total thermal energy (heat) is tiny. - The temperature difference between the spark and the air is huge , so it loses that energy to the air very quickly . - By the time the spark reaches your skin, both its temperature and its heat have dropped a long way."],[20,"how-low-can-you-go","How low can you go?","- As an object cools, its particles move more slowly . In the 1800s Kelvin predicted that at a low enough temperature the particles would stop moving altogether, and that this would be the lowest possible temperature . - He called it absolute cold; we call it absolute zero , and it is −273 °C . - Particles cannot actually be stopped completely, but laboratories have reached within billionths of a degree of absolute zero. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.2</b </summary 1 a The unit of heat. J (joules). b The unit of temperature. °C. 2 Two copper blocks at 25 °C: block A 10 g, block B 80 g. a The temperatures are the same — the average particle energy is the same in both. b Block B has more heat. It has eight times the mass, so eight times as many particles, so a greater total thermal energy. 3 a Heat is the total thermal energy of all the particles in an object. b Temperature is the average energy of the particles in an object. 4 Three objects in contact: A 10 °C, B 20 °C, C 30 °C. Thermal energy flows from hotter to colder, so C → B, C → A and B → A . Nothing flows out of A, because A is the coldest. 5 \"If X has a higher temperature than Y, X must always have more heat than Y.\" False. A spark at 1000 °C has a far higher temperature than a bath of warm water, but the bath contains vastly more particles and so holds far more heat. </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.2.1 — the Mpemba effect</b </summary Safety: with plastic cups, do not use water hotter than 60 °C. 2 Temperature differences from a freezer at −18 °C. Subtracting a negative number adds: 40 °C − (−18 °C) = 58 °C ; water at 10 °C gives 28 °C; at 30 °C gives 48 °C. 3 The link. The larger the temperature difference, the faster the thermal energy transfer — so the hotter samples lose energy fastest at the start. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — measuring heat and temperature</b </summary Safety: never touch the immersion heater while it is switched on, and clamp it so it does not touch the bottom of the cup. Method in one line: known mass of water in the polystyrene cup → record the starting temperature and the Joulemeter reading → switch on, stir just before each minute, record temperature and energy every minute until the water has risen by 10 °C. 3 The trend. Temperature rises in proportion to the energy supplied — the graph is a straight line . 4 Three variables to keep the same on a repeat. The mass (volume) of water · the same immersion heater and power supply · the same cup and starting temperature. 5 Three other ways energy is transferred here. By conduction into the cup, the thermometer and the stirring rod · by convection and evaporation from the water surface into the air · by radiation from the surfaces of the apparatus. 6 Two ways to get more of the energy into the water. Put a lid on the cup and insulate it, and keep the heater fully submerged so no energy is wasted heating the air. </details"],[20,"3-3-conservation-of-energy","3.3 Conservation of energy","Key words: conserved · created · destroyed · system"],[20,"energy-is-conserved","Energy is conserved","- In everyday speech \"conserving energy\" means using less of it. In physics it means something different: when energy is conserved, the total quantity of energy stays the same — whether it is stored, changed, transferred or dissipated. - For every 100 J of electrical energy supplied to the lamp, 10 J becomes light energy and 90 J becomes thermal energy . The light is useful ; the thermal energy is wasted . - The electrical energy is the energy input ; the light and thermal energy together are the energy output . - Input 100 J = output 10 J + 90 J = 100 J. Energy is conserved. - A car engine has more outputs. Written as percentages: 100% chemical energy in fuel in; out come 25% kinetic energy , 35% thermal and sound energy and 40% chemical energy in exhaust gas . Again the outputs add to 100% . - A system is whatever has been chosen to study, especially in terms of energy changes. The lamp and the car engine are both systems. - The total output can never be greater than the total input , because energy cannot be created (made, brought into existence). - Wasted energy is dissipated — it spreads out into the surroundings and becomes less useful . It is not destroyed ; destroyed is the opposite of created, and energy is never destroyed. The law of conservation of energy: energy cannot be created or destroyed , only changed from one form to another . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.3</b </summary 1 a The law. Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another. b i False — input and output are equal. ii False — output can never exceed input. iii True . 2 Missing values. a Electric motor: 1000 J electrical in, 500 J kinetic out → 500 J thermal. b Television: 100 J electrical in, 80 J light and sound → 20 J thermal. c Bus engine: 700 J kinetic + 1300 J thermal and sound → 2000 J chemical in. 3 a Wood fire: 100% − 70% = 30% to light and waste chemicals. b Power station: 100% − 55% − 10% = 35% to electrical energy. 4 An 18 J flashlight wasting 7 J as thermal energy. 18 − 7 = 11 J, so Anna's figure of 12 J is wrong ; only 11 J is changed to light. </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.3.1 — conservation of energy with two bottles</b </summary Method in one line: fill both bottles completely so their masses are equal, hang them 40–50 cm apart from a supporting string stretched between two strong supports, then pull one aside and release it. 1 What you see. The first bottle swings and gradually stops, while the second bottle — which started still — begins to swing. Then the movement passes back again. 2 Why. Energy is transferred through the supporting string from one bottle to the other. It is not created or destroyed, only moved, so as one gains kinetic energy the other loses it. 3 Why they eventually stop. Energy is dissipated to the surroundings as thermal energy through air resistance and friction at the knots and supports, until no movement energy is left. </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.3.2 — bottle racers</b </summary 1 The part that stores the energy. The elastic band . 2 The type of energy stored before it moves. Elastic (strain) potential energy . 3 The energy that makes it move. Kinetic energy . 4 More turns of the pencil. More turns means more elastic energy stored, so the bottle travels further and faster — until the band is wound so tightly it cannot unwind freely. </details"],[20,"3-4-moving-from-hot-to-cold","3.4 Moving from hot to cold","Key words: colder · hotter"],[20,"feeling-heat","Feeling heat","- Put your hands around a hot drink and you feel heat — but only because the drink is hotter (at a higher temperature ) than your hands. Thermal energy moves from the drink, through the container, into your hands. - Thermal energy always moves from hotter places to colder places. Colder means at a lower temperature. - Everyday uses of that rule: - animals go into water to cool down — thermal energy moves from the body into the colder water; - food put in a refrigerator loses thermal energy to the colder surroundings; - water circulates in a car engine to carry thermal energy away from the metal parts and stop it overheating. - When thermal energy is removed from a hot object, we say it has dissipated ."],[20,"feeling-cold","Feeling cold","- Hold a piece of ice and it feels cold. It is tempting to say cold moves into your hand — it does not . Cold is not a type of energy ; cold simply means less thermal energy . - What actually happens is that thermal energy leaves your hand and goes into the ice . You feel cold because energy has been taken away from you. Hold ice too long and you damage your skin, because skin needs a certain amount of thermal energy to work. - Open a window when it is colder outside and the room cools, because thermal energy moves from the hotter inside to the colder outside ."],[20,"dissipation","Dissipation","- Dissipation describes energy that spreads out and becomes less useful . - The rate of transfer increases when the temperature difference increases . - Energy is still conserved — dissipated thermal energy has not disappeared or been destroyed, it has just spread out into a colder place. - Some scientists hypothesise that billions of years from now all the thermal energy in the universe will have dissipated and everything will sit at the same low temperature. It is only a hypothesis. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.4</b </summary 1 Complete the sentence. Thermal energy moves from hotter places to colder places. 2 Hot food carried home. a Its temperature falls . b The food is hotter than the surroundings, so thermal energy is transferred from the food to the colder air until it cools. 3 Ice cream at −18 °C in a room at 22 °C. Thermal energy is transferred from the warmer air into the ice cream , so the ice cream's temperature rises and it melts. The transfer is fast at first because the temperature difference is large. 4 Sofia: \"The gloves keep the cold away.\" Zara: \"The gloves keep the heat in.\" Zara is correct. Cold cannot move into anything, because cold is not energy. The gloves are insulators that slow the transfer of thermal energy out of the hands . 5 The car engine diagram. a The cold water entering the bottom hose is colder than the engine block, so thermal energy is transferred from the metal into the water , which carries it away and keeps the engine from overheating. b In the radiator the hot water is much hotter than the air rushing past, so thermal energy is transferred from the water to the air and dissipates into the surroundings. The cooled water then returns to the engine. </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.4.1 — hot coffee (a thought experiment)</b </summary 1 Which is hottest after five minutes? Method B — add the cold milk straight away , then wait. 2 Why. The rate of transfer depends on the temperature difference with the room. Adding the milk first drops the coffee's temperature immediately, so for the whole five minutes it is less hot than the room-facing alternative and therefore loses energy more slowly . Method A keeps the coffee at its hottest for five minutes, so it dissipates energy fastest, and it still has the milk to cool it afterwards. 3 Variables to keep constant. Same volume of coffee and of milk · same starting temperatures · identical cups · same room temperature · same waiting time · stirred (or not stirred) the same way. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — temperature change from heat dissipation</b </summary Safety: this must be done in the school laboratory, with only the glass part of the lamp in the water. Never put a lamp in water at home. 3 Why the temperature rises at first. The lamp wastes most of its electrical energy as thermal energy , which is transferred to the water, so the water's temperature climbs. 4 Why it eventually stops rising. As the water warms, the temperature difference between the water and the room grows, so the water loses energy to the surroundings faster . The temperature stops changing when energy is being lost as fast as the lamp supplies it. 5 Predicting the result with an LED. An LED changes a bigger percentage of the electrical energy into light, so less is wasted as thermal energy . The water would warm more slowly and level off at a lower temperature . </details"],[20,"3-5-ways-of-transferring-thermal-energy","3.5 Ways of transferring thermal energy","Key words: conduction · convection · convection current · expand · radiation · vigorously"],[20,"heat-and-particle-movement","Heat and particle movement","- Heat any substance and its particles move faster . - Solids — particles vibrate about fixed positions; the vibration gets faster and more vigorous (with more energy, speed and force), so they take up more space and the solid expands . - Liquids — particles vibrate and move around; heating makes them move faster and take up more space, so liquids expand too. - Gases — particles travel in straight lines until they collide; heating makes them collide harder and more often , so gases expand as well."],[20,"conduction","Conduction","- Vibrating particles push against their neighbours . The more vigorous the vibration, the harder the push, so the neighbouring particles begin to vibrate more vigorously as well. That chain of pushes is conduction . - Conduction works best in solids , where particles are close together and can only vibrate. - Metals are the best thermal conductors , because the electrons that are free to move in a metal help pass the vibrations along. - Fabrics such as wool and cotton, and plastics, are poor conductors. A poor conductor is a good thermal insulator . - Design follows from this: a pan is metal so food heats quickly, while the handle is plastic and the spoon wooden , because those are insulators and stay comfortable to touch. Cold-weather clothes are made from insulators to slow conduction away from the body. - Conduction is poor in liquids (particles move around instead of just vibrating) and poor in gases (particles are far apart, so collisions are infrequent). Conduction cannot happen in a vacuum — there are no particles to vibrate."],[20,"convection","Convection","- Heat a liquid or gas and the particles take up more space, so the volume increases while the mass stays the same . Since density = mass ÷ volume, the density decreases . - Less dense material floats in more dense material. So the warmed part floats upwards through the colder, denser material — that upward movement is convection . - The colder, denser material sinks to take its place, gets heated in turn, and rises. This circulation is a convection current , and it eventually heats all of the liquid or gas. - Convection happens in liquids and gases , because their particles are free to move. - Convection cannot happen in a solid (particles only vibrate about fixed positions) and cannot happen in a vacuum (no particles)."],[20,"radiation","Radiation","- Between the Sun and the Earth there is a vacuum , so neither conduction nor convection can carry the Sun's energy to us. The third way is radiation . - Radiation is a type of wave that we cannot see . It transfers thermal energy very quickly — energy from the Sun reaches Earth in about nine minutes . - All objects emit thermal radiation , and the hotter the object, the more it emits. Cooler objects absorb radiation from hotter ones. - Radiation needs no particles , so it passes through a vacuum. It also passes through transparent solids, liquids and gases. - Colour and texture matter: Best emitters and absorbers Worst emitters and absorbers Texture dull shiny Colour black white or silver Surface large surface area small surface area - Shiny, white and silver surfaces reflect radiation away . Penguins have black backs to absorb as much radiation as possible; a car radiator is black with thin fins to emit as much as possible; buildings in hot countries are painted white to absorb less."],[20,"conduction-convection-and-radiation-together","Conduction, convection and radiation together","- Most objects gain or lose thermal energy by a combination of all three. In a room heater with hot water inside: conduction carries energy from the water to the metal and from the metal to the air, the warm air rises and heats the room by convection , and the metal surface also emits radiation . - A vacuum flask is designed to slow every route at once: - the vacuum between the double walls prevents conduction (and convection — no particles); - the silvered wall reflects radiation back into the drink; - the bottle touches the outer case only at the top, through small cork supports , so there is almost no path for conduction. - Double glazing puts a thin layer of argon — a poor conductor — between two sheets of glass. Cavity walls are filled with foam , which is a poor conductor and also fills the space so no convection current can form . - Silver film on car windows reflects the Sun's radiation away and keeps the inside cooler. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.5</b </summary 1 a The three ways. Conduction, convection and radiation. b Conduction and convection cannot occur in a vacuum , because both need particles — one to pass on vibrations, the other to move around. c Conduction works best in solids , because the particles are closest together and can pass vibrations straight on. 2 A white T-shirt and a black T-shirt in the Sun. The black shirt is the better absorber of radiation, so Sofia feels hotter. The white shirt reflects more radiation away, so Marcus feels cooler. 3 a Through a metal cooking pan — conduction. b From the Sun to Earth — radiation. 4 The water tank. a The electric heater at the bottom warms the water around it; that water expands, becomes less dense and rises , while colder, denser water sinks to take its place. The resulting convection current heats all the water in the tank. b Hot water is taken from the top because the hottest, least dense water collects there. c A second heater used for a small amount of hot water should be placed near the top of the tank, so only the water above it is heated. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — conduction in different materials</b </summary Method in one line: stick paper clips along each metal rod at 5 cm intervals with melted candle wax, heat one end of each rod, and watch the order in which the clips drop off. 1 Variables to keep constant. Same length and thickness of rod · same size of paper clip and same amount of wax · same spacing · same heat source at the same distance · same starting temperature. 2 Variables you could not control. The exact amount of wax on each clip, small differences in the flame, draughts in the room, and the starting temperature of each rod. 3 and 4 Observations and explanation. The clips fall first on the best conductor . Vibrating particles at the heated end pass their energy along, and in a metal the free electrons carry it faster still; the wax reaches its melting point sooner along the better conductor. 5 Why wood and plastic cannot be tested this way. They are such poor conductors that the heated end would burn or melt long before the far end warmed enough to melt the wax. </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.5.1 — observing convection</b </summary Method in one line: fill a beaker three-quarters with cold water, place a small drop of coloured ink at one side of the bottom with a pipette, heat directly under the ink, and do not stir. What you see and why. The coloured water above the flame is heated, expands and becomes less dense , so it rises in a stream up that side of the beaker. It spreads across the top, cools, becomes more dense and sinks down the far side, then travels back along the bottom — a complete convection current made visible by the ink. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — emitting thermal energy by radiation</b </summary Method in one line: paint identical cans dull black, white and silver , fill each with the same volume of water at about 60 °C, and record the temperature every two minutes. 1 Prediction. The water in the black can cools fastest , because a dull black surface is the best emitter of radiation. The silver can cools slowest , because a shiny surface is the worst emitter. 6 Recognising anomalous results. A point that lies well off the smooth curve of its own line — usually a misread thermometer or a mistimed reading. 7 Variables to keep the same. Same size and shape of can · same volume of water · same starting temperature · same room and same position (no draughts, no direct sunlight) · same thermometers · same timing. 8 Comparing absorption instead of emission. Fill the cans with the same volume of cold water, place them the same distance from the same radiant heat source (a lamp or the Sun), and record how fast each warms up. The dull black can should warm fastest. </details"],[20,"3-6-cooling-by-evaporation","3.6 Cooling by evaporation","Key words: porous · random"],[20,"evaporation","Evaporation","- Evaporation is the change of state from liquid to gas , and it happens at temperatures far below the boiling point — water evaporates at 15 °C even though it boils at 100 °C. - In a liquid at room temperature the particles move in random directions at random speeds. Random means unpredictable. - The faster particles carry more energy . Some at the surface have enough energy to escape and become gas particles . Some of those are pulled back; the ones with the most energy leave and stay gone."],[20,"evaporation-and-cooling","Evaporation and cooling","- Temperature is the average energy of the particles. When the highest-energy particles leave , the average energy of those left behind falls — so evaporation causes cooling . - Getting out of a swimming pool feels cold for exactly this reason: the water on your skin evaporates, and the energy needed to change its state comes from your skin , so your skin loses thermal energy. - Sweat. A common mistake is to think the liquid alone does the cooling. Sweat is made in the skin, so it starts at skin temperature . When it evaporates its temperature drops, thermal energy then flows from the skin into the cooler sweat , and the skin cools. In very humid conditions sweat cannot evaporate, so this cooling fails — which is dangerous, because body temperature can then climb. - Clay water coolers are made from porous clay, meaning water can slowly soak through it. Water reaching the outside evaporates , cooling the water still inside. - Evaporative air coolers blow warm room air through a wet sponge. The warm air supplies the energy that evaporates the water, so the air loses thermal energy and comes out cooler ."],[20,"different-liquids-evaporate-at-different-rates","Different liquids evaporate at different rates","- The forces holding the particles together differ from liquid to liquid. - Perfume has weaker forces between its particles, so it evaporates quickly — that is how you smell it, and why it feels colder on the skin than water does. - Liquid soap has stronger forces, so it evaporates much more slowly than water; put both on your skin and the water feels colder. Safety note: put nothing on your skin except water, soap or perfume, and wash soap off with plenty of water. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 3.6</b </summary 1 Complete the sentences. The energies of the particles in water at 25 °C are different . Particles with the least energy stay in the liquid. Particles with the most energy can leave the liquid. When liquid water turns to gas, the water is said to evaporate . 2 Hot water at 80 °C left in a room at 20 °C. C — evaporation. 3 a Evaporation removes the highest-energy particles, so the average energy of the particles left behind decreases . b The temperature falls , because temperature is a measure of that average particle energy. 4 a How sweat cools the skin. Sweat evaporates; the highest-energy particles leave, so the sweat cools; thermal energy then transfers from the warmer skin into the cooler sweat, and the skin loses thermal energy. b Why skin gets too warm when sweat cannot evaporate. With no evaporation there is no cooling route, so the thermal energy the body produces stays in the body and its temperature rises. 5 Isopropanol feels cold on the skin. It evaporates quickly , so it removes thermal energy from the skin rapidly ; the energy for the change of state comes from the skin, which cools. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — making an air cooler</b </summary Safety: keep water away from cables, sockets and the fan itself, and keep the fan as far from the socket as possible. 1 Why not hold the thermometer in your hand? Thermal energy from your hand would transfer into the thermometer and you would record your temperature, not the air's. 2 Explaining the three readings. The room air and the air straight from the fan are at the same temperature — a fan does not cool air, it only moves it. The air measured after passing the wet towel is cooler , because water evaporating from the towel takes thermal energy from that air. 3 With a dry towel. No evaporation, so no temperature drop — the air behind the towel stays at room temperature. </details <details <summary <b Activity 3.6.1 — feeling the effects of evaporation</b </summary 1 Why spread each liquid thinly? A thin film has a large surface area for its volume, so it evaporates faster and the effect is easy to feel. 3 What you feel. The ethanol or isopropanol feels coldest — it evaporates fastest . Liquid soap feels least cold — it evaporates slowest . Water sits between the two. 4 Why a bar chart is hard here. The results are descriptions of a feeling , not measured numbers — there is nothing to plot. 5 How to fix that. Measure instead of feel: put equal volumes of each liquid on a surface with a thermometer or temperature sensor and record the temperature drop , or record the time taken for each to dry . Those are numbers a bar chart can show. </details"],[20,"check-your-progress-worked-answers","Check your progress — worked answers","3.1 The correct equation for density. B — density = mass ÷ volume. 3.2 True statements about the density of air. B (less dense than solids because the particles are further apart) and D (more dense than a vacuum, which has no particles). A is wrong because air does have mass; C is wrong because gas particles are further apart than those in a liquid, not closer. 3.3 An object of mass 60 g and volume 50 cm³. a density = 60 ÷ 50 = 1.2 g/cm³ . b It sinks — 1.2 g/cm³ is greater than the 1.0 g/cm³ of water. 3.4 Beaker X 50 cm³ and beaker Y 100 cm³ of water at the same temperature. a The average energy of the particles is the same in both, because temperature is the average particle energy and the temperatures are equal. b Y has more heat. It contains twice as many particles, so the total thermal energy of all its particles is greater. 3.5 a The unit of temperature. °C. b The unit of heat. J. 3.6 The law of conservation of energy. Energy is always conserved , meaning that energy cannot be created or destroyed . 3.7 A lamp using 50 J, of which 5 J becomes light. 50 − 5 = 45 J wasted as thermal energy. 3.8 A metal ball at 200 °C dropped into water at 4 °C. a Thermal energy transfers from the hotter ball to the colder water, so the ball's temperature falls and the water's temperature rises . The transfer is fastest at the start, when the temperature difference is largest. b C — the temperatures of the ball and the water become the same , and the net transfer then stops. 3.9 Why the handle of a metal spoon in hot tea gets warmer. Thermal energy is transferred from the tea to the spoon by conduction . The particles at the hot end vibrate faster and more vigorously, push against their neighbours and pass the vibration along the metal — helped by the free electrons in the metal — until the particles in the handle are vibrating faster too, so its temperature rises. 3.10 The convection current around a heater. a Position 1 (above the heater). The air here has been heated, so its particles move faster and take up more space. Its volume increases while its mass stays the same, so it becomes less dense and rises . b Position 2 (away from the heater). This air has cooled by transferring thermal energy to the room, so it takes up less space, becomes more dense and sinks , moving back to take the place of the rising warm air. 3.11 A black car and a white car parked in the sun for three hours. Yes — the black car is hotter inside . A dull black surface is the better absorber of thermal radiation, while the white surface reflects more of it away. 3.12 How sweat cools the skin even at the same temperature as the skin. The highest-energy particles in the sweat evaporate and escape , lowering the average energy of the particles left, so the sweat is now cooler than the skin . Thermal energy then transfers from the skin into the cooler sweat , and the skin loses thermal energy and cools."],[20,"project-load-lines","Project: load lines","- Load lines (also called Plimsoll lines ) are painted on the side of a ship, half way between the front and the back. They show how far down in the water a ship can safely sit , and the letters stand for different types of water and different conditions — TF tropical fresh, F fresh, T tropical sea, S summer sea, W winter sea, WNA winter North Atlantic. - Why the lines differ. A ship floats when it has displaced its own weight of water. Denser water supports the ship higher, so the safe line is lower on the hull ; less dense water lets it sit deeper. - Fresh water is less dense than sea water (no dissolved salt), so a ship floats lower in it — the fresh-water lines sit highest . - Warm water is less dense than cold water, so tropical lines allow the ship deeper than winter ones. - Modelling it. Float a block of wood in pure water, then in water with a measured mass of salt dissolved in it, then in warm and in cold water, and mark where the surface reaches each time. Winter sea water can be as cold as −2 °C , summer sea water as warm as 27 °C . - Why they matter. They give the crew a direct, visible check that the ship is not overloaded for the water it is sailing in — an overloaded ship has an average density too close to that of water and can be swamped."],[21,"4-1-plants-and-water","4.1 Plants and water","Key words: root hairs · xylem vessels"],[21,"why-plants-need-to-absorb-water-all-the-time","Why plants need to absorb water all the time","- Plants, like all living things, are made mostly of water , and they are absorbing it almost all of the time. - All the absorbing is done by the roots , which take water out of the soil . - Water matters because chemicals have to be dissolved before the chemical reactions inside a cell — respiration, for example — can happen at all."],[21,"what-soil-is-actually-like","What soil is actually like","- Soil is made of tiny particles of rock . The particles are irregularly shaped and come in all sorts of sizes . - Because they are irregular they do not pack together tightly , so there are spaces between them . - Those spaces are filled with air and water . The water in those spaces is the water the plant takes up, and mineral ions are already dissolved in it ."],[21,"how-root-hairs-absorb-water","How root hairs absorb water","- Root hairs are special cells that grow out of the surface of a root . Each root hair is part of a single cell — not a cell of its own. - Their job is surface area : the long, thin extension gives a really big surface through which water and mineral ions can be absorbed, so absorption happens faster . - Root hairs are very tiny, but they are just visible without a lens . - The route water takes into the cell is: soil water → cell wall → cell membrane → cytoplasm . - Mineral ions travel with the water. Nitrate ions and magnesium ions are dissolved in the soil water and move into the root hair cell along with it . Mineral ion What the plant makes with it nitrate proteins magnesium chlorophyll"],[21,"how-water-moves-up-the-plant","How water moves up the plant","- Cut a root across and you see the root hairs on the outside and, in the centre , a cross-shaped area where the xylem vessels are. Xylem is the plant's water transport system . - After water has been absorbed into a root hair cell it moves from the outside of the root towards the inside , and enters the xylem vessels in the centre . - The root cap protects the tip of the root as it pushes through the soil. What a xylem vessel is: - long, tube-like cells - dead cells — all of the contents (cytoplasm, nucleus) have disappeared - all that is left is the cell wall , with an empty space inside - the end walls have gone too , so many vessels stack on top of one another into long empty tubes running from the roots to the highest parts of the plant - diameter only about 0.05 mm , though they come in a range of sizes - The wood in a tree trunk is made of xylem vessels — that is what carries water from the roots to the top of the tree. - In a cross-section you look down on the cut surface. Xylem sits in the centre of a root (the cross shape) and in separate bundles in a stem . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.1</b </summary 1 How the structure of a root hair cell helps it carry out its function. The cell has a long extension sticking out into the soil, and this increases its surface area . A bigger surface means a bigger area across which water and mineral salts can be taken up, so uptake happens faster . 2 Why plants need nitrate ions and magnesium ions. Nitrate ions are needed to make proteins . Magnesium ions are needed to make chlorophyll . </details <details <summary <b Activity 4.1.1 — annotating a diagram</b </summary Make a large copy of the plain circular diagram showing where the xylem vessels sit in a root, leaving space around it for writing. Then use the three-dimensional diagram and the text to annotate it — an annotation says what each part does , not just what it is called, so that a reader can follow how water is absorbed by the root and how it travels upwards. Useful annotations: root hair — long extension, large surface area for absorbing water and mineral salts ; cell wall and cell membrane — water passes through these into the cytoplasm ; xylem vessels — dead, empty tubes with no end walls, carry water up the plant ; root cap — protects the growing tip . Self assessment: put your diagram on the wall next to everyone else's. What makes one diagram better than another? Usually: clear labels with lines that touch the right part, annotations that explain a function , and a drawing large enough to read. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — transport in a celery stalk</b </summary Method. Put 1–2 cm of coloured water (blue ink or methylene blue) in a small container. Cut across the lower end of a thick stalk so the cut end is clean and not blocked. Stand the cut end in the coloured water and look at it every five minutes. When the dye reaches the top, take the stalk out, rinse the dye off the end, and cut across it about half way up. Safety. Put the stalk on a non-slip surface and cut away from yourself, so a slipping blade does not hit your hand. 1 Draw the cut end of the stalk with the coloured spots in the positions you actually see them. 2 Which cells are found where the blue spots are? Xylem cells — label the drawing with that name. 3 a Why use no more than 2 cm of water at the start? So you can be sure the coloured areas appeared because the dye was carried up through the stalk , and did not simply leak in from the side of a deeply submerged stalk. b Why wash the stalk before cutting it? To remove dye from the outside of the stalk, so the coloured spots you see can only have come from dye carried up inside it. 4 How water would reach the stalk in a complete plant. Water moves from the soil into the root hairs , then across the root to its centre. There it enters the xylem vessels , which carry it up through the stalk . </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — planning a temperature experiment</b </summary 1 What effect would temperature have? The water would move up faster at a higher temperature, because higher temperatures give the particles more kinetic energy . 2 The hypothesis. As temperature increases, the speed at which water moves up the stem increases. 3–4 The plan. Modify the celery experiment so that temperature is the variable you change , and measure how fast the dye moves up the stem at each temperature. Part of the plan What it should say apparatus a container, coloured water, several stalks, a timer and a ruler independent variable temperature of the coloured water dependent variable the rate the dye moves up the stalk — measure a distance and a time control variables depth of coloured water, size of the stalk, light intensity risk assessment cutting the stalk risks a cut — cut on a firm, non-slip surface , blade moving away from the body </details"],[21,"4-2-transpiration","4.2 Transpiration","Key words: transpiration · wilted"],[21,"how-water-moves-through-a-leaf","How water moves through a leaf","The whole path, in order: 1. Water arrives at the leaf in the xylem vessels and moves out into the leaf cells . 2. Cells that have chloroplasts use some of it for photosynthesis — but not very much, and most of the water does not stay in the cell. 3. The liquid water soaks into the cellulose cell wall . 4. There it evaporates — this is where liquid water becomes water vapour . 5. The water vapour diffuses into the air spaces between the cells. 6. The air spaces connect to the outside through the stomata , tiny holes in the underside of the leaf. 7. The water vapour diffuses out through the stomata into the air. Transpiration is the loss of water vapour from leaves. - Why the underside loses most water. The upper surface is covered with a layer of wax that stops water passing through, while the underside has the stomata — openings that lead to the air spaces inside the leaf, so water vapour can diffuse out."],[21,"why-plants-need-water","Why plants need water","Reason How it works Support Plant cells hold a lot of water, especially in their vacuoles . A cell with plenty of water is strong and firm ; the cells press on one another and hold the whole plant up. A cell without enough water goes soft and floppy , and when all the cells are like that the plant collapses — it has wilted . Transport As water moves through the xylem vessels it carries dissolved mineral salts from the roots to every other part of the plant. Cooling When water evaporates it takes heat away with it , so evaporation from the cell wall of a leaf cell cools the cell . This matters for plants in very hot places. Photosynthesis Water is one of the reactants : water + carbon dioxide → glucose + oxygen. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.2</b </summary 1 Identifying the upper epidermis, palisade layer and spongy layer on this leaf section, using the leaf diagram from Topic 1.2. The upper epidermis is the single layer of cells across the top ; the palisade layer is the row of tall, closely packed cells just beneath it, where most photosynthesis happens; the spongy layer is the loosely packed layer below, full of the air spaces the water vapour diffuses through. 2 Why most transpiration happens from the underside of a leaf. The upper surface is covered with a waxy layer that water cannot pass through. The underside has stomata , which open into the air spaces inside the leaf, so water vapour can diffuse out through them . </details <details <summary <b Getting started — 4.2</b </summary Draw the particles in liquid water : randomly arranged, but each particle touching at least two others . Then the particles in water as a gas : not touching at all , and far apart . When liquid water turns to a gas, the particles move faster and spread further apart . </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — investigating transpiration</b </summary Method. Take two very similar plants in the same size pots and check the soil in both is moist. Cover plant 1 completely — plant and pot — with a plastic bag tied firmly at the top. On plant 2 , cover the pot and soil only , tying the bag tightly around the stem so the leaves stay exposed . Measure the mass of both plants in their pots, then again each day for at least a week, and draw both sets of results as two lines on one graph. 1 The independent variable. Whether the leaves were exposed to the air or not. 2 Variables kept the same. The soil was covered in both . Also: same temperature , same size of plant , the same quantity of water in each pot, and the same light intensity . 3 Comparing the two. The plant whose leaves were not covered loses mass much faster and loses more mass in total. 4 Explaining it. Water vapour was lost from the uncovered leaves by transpiration and escaped into the room. On the fully bagged plant the water vapour lost from the leaves was trapped inside the bag and could not escape, so that pot-and-plant kept its mass. </details <details <summary <b Think like a scientist — which side of a leaf loses most water?</b </summary Method. Choose eight similar leaves from the same plant and letter them A–H. Leaves Treatment Surfaces left free to lose water A and B no petroleum jelly both C and D jelly on the top surface only underside only E and F jelly on the bottom surface only upper surface only G and H jelly on both surfaces neither Measure each leaf's mass (on filter paper, so no jelly reaches the balance), hang all eight on a washing line in the same order, leave them at least a day, then measure the masses again. Expected rank order, best at conserving water last: no jelly loses most, then jelly on the upper surface only, then jelly on the lower surface only, then jelly on both — which loses least. Why. The upper surface has few or no stomata , so blocking it changes little. The underside has the stomata , so blocking the underside blocks the main escape route and the leaf keeps far more of its water. Why two leaves that had the same treatment did not lose exactly the same mass. The leaves probably had different starting masses and contained different quantities of water , and one may have hung in a slightly draughtier place than the other. Why use two leaves per treatment, and would more be better? Two gives more reliable results — one leaf on its own might be unusual. More would be better still , because then you could spot anomalous results and the data would be more reliable. </details <details <summary <b Workbook exercise 4.2 — how temperature affects water loss</b </summary Sofia tested the hypothesis plants lose more water from their leaves when the temperature increases . She set up three similar potted plants and kept each at a different temperature. Why she covered the pot and soil of each plant with a plastic bag. So that any water lost could only have come from the plants , not by evaporation from the soil . Without the bag she could not tell which of the two had caused the mass to fall. Her readings of mass of plant + pot, in g, over eight days: Day 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 plant A, 4 °C 863 854 845 837 829 822 814 807 plant B, 20 °C 893 873 856 837 861 792 779 761 plant C, 30 °C 842 810 780 748 714 682 650 618 The anomalous result is 861 g for plant B on day 5 — the mass had been falling steadily and cannot rise again, because the plant only loses water. When drawing the graph, plot it but ignore it when drawing the line . Plotting the graph. Time in days on the x-axis , mass in grams on the y-axis ; the y-axis does not have to start at 0. Use scales that fill most of the grid and rise in even, sensible intervals, plot all three sets of points, and draw and label a separate line for each plant . Mean daily loss of mass for plant B. Day 1 = 893 g and day 8 = 761 g, so the loss over the seven intervals is 893 − 761 = 132 g , and the mean is 132 ÷ 7 = 19 g per day . What the results show. The warmer the plant was kept, the more mass it lost — plant C at 30 °C lost 224 g while plant A at 4 °C lost only 56 g. Higher temperature gives the water particles more energy , so water evaporates faster inside the leaf and transpiration is faster. Sofia's hypothesis is supported . </details <details <summary <b Activity 4.2.1 — conserving water in the desert</b </summary Desert plants have to solve two problems: get as much water as possible from the soil, and lose as little as possible by transpiration. - Getting water: very deep roots to reach water far down, or very wide-spreading shallow roots to catch rain over a large area. - Losing less: small leaves (less surface area), a thick waxy layer on the leaf surface, and fewer stomata , all of which reduce transpiration. </details"],[21,"4-3-excretion-in-humans","4.3 Excretion in humans","Key words: bladder · excretion · kidneys · renal · urea · ureter · urethra · urine"],[21,"what-excretion-is","What excretion is","- Excretion means getting rid of waste materials — but not all waste materials. - Excretion only applies to waste that has truly been inside the body , even if only for a short while. - Food travelling down the long tube from mouth to anus has never really been part of the body . Only substances that move out of the digestive system into the blood or the body cells are part of it. - So getting rid of faeces is not excretion — that waste was never part of the body. Excretion = all the waste substances that organisms make in their cells , plus any substance they have too much of that has been part of their body . Humans excrete Where it comes from carbon dioxide made by body cells in respiration urea made in liver cells excess water water the body does not need"],[21,"excreting-urea","Excreting urea","1. Proteins in food are broken down to smaller molecules in the digestive system. 2. Those small molecules go into the blood , which transports them to the liver . 3. If there is more protein than the body needs , the liver changes the small molecules into urea . 4. Urea is poisonous — if it builds up in the body it makes a person ill — so as soon as it is made it is carried away from the liver in the blood . 5. The kidneys remove the urea from the blood."],[21,"the-excretory-system","The excretory system","- The excretory system is also called the renal system . Renal means 'to do with kidneys' . - As blood flows through the kidneys , they filter it: they remove all of the urea and also excess water . - The urea dissolves in that excess water , and the solution of urea dissolved in water is urine . Part What it does artery brings blood to the kidneys vein takes blood away from the kidneys kidney filters the blood , removing all the urea and the excess water ureter tube carrying urine from a kidney down to the bladder bladder stores urine for a while urethra tube carrying urine from the bladder to the outside Watch the spelling. Urea is the waste substance; urine is the liquid it is dissolved in. The ureter runs kidney → bladder; the urethra runs bladder → outside. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.3</b </summary 1 Another name for the excretory system. The renal system . 2 How carbon dioxide is excreted. Carbon dioxide diffuses into the blood from the body cells that made it in respiration and is carried to the lungs . There it diffuses from the blood capillaries into the alveoli , and it leaves the body in expired air . 3 Why plants do not produce urea. Plants make their own proteins , using carbohydrates made in photosynthesis together with nitrates absorbed from the soil . They make only what they need, so they are unlikely to have excess protein to get rid of — and urea is made from excess protein. </details <details <summary <b Getting started — 4.3</b </summary Reasons that are the same for plants and humans: both need water for transport and for cooling . A reason for humans that is not the same: humans need water for getting rid of waste materials in urine . Reasons for plants that are not the same: plants use water for support (firm cells hold the plant up) and as a reactant in photosynthesis . </details <details <summary <b Activity 4.3.1 — excretory system words and meanings</b </summary A group of four or five makes sixteen identical cards : eight carry the key words from this topic, and eight carry their meanings — agree on each meaning as a group first. Shuffle them and lay them all face down . Turn over two: a matching word and meaning is kept and earns another turn; a non-match goes back face down. The winner has the most cards at the end. </details"],[21,"4-4-keeping-a-fetus-healthy","4.4 Keeping a fetus healthy","Key words: fetal · fetus"],[21,"a-healthy-pregnancy","A healthy pregnancy","- For the first nine months of its life a new human being grows inside its mother. During that time it is called a fetus — a baby before it is born . - The fetus needs to do everything a body does: take in nutrients , respire to release energy, and excrete waste. It relies entirely on its mother for all of it. - The substances the fetus needs are brought to it in the mother's blood — but the two blood systems never mix . They come very close together but do not touch . - Substances the fetus needs diffuse from the mother's blood into the fetus's blood; substances the fetus must excrete diffuse the other way . Direction Substances mother → fetus oxygen and nutrients such as water, glucose, vitamins, minerals fetus → mother carbon dioxide and urea"],[21,"diet-during-pregnancy","Diet during pregnancy","A pregnant woman needs a balanced diet — some of every nutrient — and a little more than usual , because some of what she eats is passed to the growing fetus. Nutrient Why more is needed Good sources protein to build the fetus's new cells so it can grow; for her own muscles; and for extra haemoglobin , because her blood must carry oxygen for the fetus as well as herself — the fetus also makes its own haemoglobin meat, fish, pulses, milk and other dairy products carbohydrate supplies energy — cells combine glucose with oxygen in respiration — for both mother and fetus. Not too much , or the extra is changed to fat and she puts on unhealthy weight bread, rice, potatoes, pasta, cereal grains iron needed to make haemoglobin ; iron pills can help if her diet cannot supply enough meat, dark green vegetables, fish and shellfish, nuts and seeds calcium for the baby to grow strong bones , and to keep the mother's bones and teeth healthy dairy products, nuts and seeds vitamin A good vision for mother and fetus, and for their white blood cells to fight pathogens green vegetables, carrots, squash, fruit, dairy products, fish vitamin C strong skin and blood vessels for both citrus fruits, potatoes, colourful berries vitamin D strong bones and teeth for both oily fish"],[21,"smoking-cigarettes","Smoking cigarettes","- Tobacco smoke contains carbon monoxide, nicotine and tar . - When a pregnant woman smokes, carbon monoxide and nicotine diffuse from her blood into the fetus's blood — it is as though the fetus is smoking too . - Carbon monoxide reduces the amount of oxygen that haemoglobin can transport . The fetus's cells therefore get less oxygen , so they cannot respire as much and cannot release as much energy as normal. This can affect the fetus's development, and babies born to mothers who smoked tend to be smaller . - Nicotine is an addictive drug that can damage blood vessels — not something a developing fetus should have in its blood."],[21,"drugs","Drugs","A drug is something taken into the body that affects the way the body works . - Some drugs are good for health . Antibiotics kill bacteria in the body and cure the infections they cause; some painkillers such as aspirin also help. These are medicinal drugs , and without them many more people would die or suffer pain from illnesses we can now cure. - Any drug we do not need can cause harm , and these drugs can be even more harmful to a fetus than to an adult . - Most drugs a pregnant woman takes will go into her fetus's blood , so the fetus is taking the drug too. Recommendations for women during pregnancy: - If she regularly takes a prescribed drug , check with a doctor or pharmacist whether it is safe to continue — and do not stop taking it without checking first . - Check with a doctor before taking drugs that do not need a prescription , such as aspirin. - She must stop smoking . - She should avoid alcohol . - She should never take illegal drugs at all , and it is especially important to avoid them during pregnancy. - There is not much evidence that caffeine (in coffee and cola) harms a fetus, but most doctors still recommend not drinking a lot of coffee or cola. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 4.4</b </summary 1 Three substances that move from the mother's blood into the fetus's blood. Oxygen , and nutrients such as glucose , water , vitamins and minerals . 2 Two substances the fetus excretes that the mother's blood removes. Carbon dioxide and urea . 3 Why a pregnant mother needs plenty of vitamins A, C and D. Vitamin A for good vision for herself and the fetus, and for their white blood cells to fight pathogens. Vitamin C for strong skin and blood vessels for both. Vitamin D for strong bones and teeth for both. 4 Foods supplying each nutrient. Protein — meat, fish, pulses, milk and other dairy products. Carbohydrate — bread, rice, potatoes, pasta, cereal grains. Iron — meat, dark green vegetables, fish and shellfish, nuts and seeds. Calcium — dairy products, nuts and seeds. Vitamin A — green vegetables, carrots, squash, fruit, dairy products, fish. Vitamin C — citrus fruits, potatoes, colourful berries. Vitamin D — oily fish. </details <details <summary <b Activity 4.4.1 — display about diet during pregnancy</b </summary In a group of three or four, make a display about why eating well during pregnancy matters . Collect information first: look for advice for pregnant women, talk to mothers you know about whether they ate differently and whether a healthy diet was easy to keep up, find pictures, and ask at a doctor's surgery or health centre for leaflets. Then build a bright display that catches attention and explains why a good diet is important . Afterwards, look at other groups' work — what did they do well, and what would you change about your own? </details"],[21,"check-your-progress-worked-answers","Check your progress — worked answers","4.1 Matching the word to the description (bladder, kidney, urea, ureter, urethra, urine). Description Answer a This organ filters the blood kidney b A tube that carries urine from the kidneys to the bladder ureter c A waste substance made in the liver from excess proteins urea d Where urine is stored before leaving the body bladder e A tube that carries urine to the outside of the body urethra f A liquid containing urea dissolved in water urine 4.2 Completing the sentences about plants and water. Plants take up water from the soil , into their root hairs . The water flows through xylem vessels , which carry it to the plant's leaves . In the leaves, a lot of the water changes from liquid to gas . It diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata . 4.3 Arun's two potted plants. Plant A had nothing covered ; plant B had the plant itself covered by a bag . Their masses (pot included, in g) were measured for seven days: Day 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 plant A + pot 945 925 901 877 855 832 808 plant B + pot 960 952 946 940 936 932 929 a Apparatus for measuring the mass. A balance . b Mass plant B lost from day 6 to day 7. 932 − 929 = 3 g . c Mass plant A lost during the whole experiment. The fastest route is to use the first and last readings rather than adding up the six daily losses: 945 − 808 = 137 g . d Mean loss per day for plant A. There are six day-to-day intervals between seven readings, so 137 ÷ 6 = 22.8 g per day . e Was more mass lost from the soil or from the plant? From the plant . Plant A, with neither the plant nor the soil covered, lost 137 g in total, while plant B, which had the plant covered so only its soil was exposed, lost only 960 − 929 = 31 g . The difference — over 100 g — must have come from the leaves , by transpiration. f Why little drops of water appeared inside the bag around plant B. Water travelled up the plant in the xylem , evaporated from the cell walls inside the leaves and diffused out through the stomata as water vapour . Trapped inside the bag, the vapour condensed back to liquid on the cool inside surface, forming drops. 4.4 Recommended daily nutrient amounts. Nutrient Not pregnant Pregnant protein / g 45 60 calcium / g 0.8 1.2 iron / mg 15 30 a Why more of each is needed. Protein — to build the new cells of the growing fetus and to make the extra haemoglobin her larger blood supply needs. Calcium — to build the fetus's bones and teeth without weakening the mother's own. Iron — to make the extra haemoglobin for both her blood and the fetus's, so enough oxygen can be carried. b A food for each. Protein — fish . Calcium — milk . Iron — dark green vegetables . c Why more fat is not recommended. The fetus does not need extra fat to grow, and the mother already gets enough energy from her carbohydrate; extra fat is stored and she would put on too much weight , which is unhealthy during pregnancy. d Why a woman should not smoke while pregnant. Carbon monoxide from the smoke diffuses into the fetus's blood and reduces the oxygen its haemoglobin can carry , so its cells respire less and release less energy , which affects development — babies of mothers who smoked tend to be smaller . Nicotine also crosses over; it is addictive and damages blood vessels . Cell revision questions that come with this section. - Moves mucus up through the airways — ciliated cell . Absorbs water from the soil — root hair cell . Makes food by photosynthesis — palisade cell . - On the animal cell : A cell membrane , B cytoplasm , C nucleus , D mitochondrion . Two ways to tell it is an animal cell and not a plant cell: it has no cell wall (only a membrane, so no fixed rectangular shape), and it has no chloroplasts and no large permanent vacuole . - On the plant cell , matching each part to its function: cell wall — holds the cell in shape; cell membrane — controls what goes in and out of the cell; chloroplast — where photosynthesis takes place; mitochondrion — where energy is released from nutrients; nucleus — controls the activities of the cell."],[21,"project-how-scientists-linked-smoking-to-health","Project: how scientists linked smoking to health","- The problem. You cannot experiment on human health here. Nobody can ask one group of pregnant women to smoke and another not to, and then compare their babies — it would harm people on purpose. - What scientists do instead. They collect information from as many people as possible and look for correlations between smoking and health. They publish how they did the study and what they found , so other scientists can examine the method and results and decide for themselves what the evidence shows. - Why publishing the method matters as much as the result. A correlation on its own does not prove one thing caused the other. Other scientists need to see how the people were chosen , how many there were and what else differed between the groups before they can judge whether smoking is really the cause. - Questions to research for the class display: When did scientists first begin to think smoking was bad for health, what made them think so, and how did they share their ideas? What evidence is there that smoking harms health? What evidence is there that it harms the development of a fetus? How have people responded — has the number of smokers fallen in most countries, and has the number of women smoking during pregnancy fallen?"],[22,"5-1-reactivity-and-displacement-reactions","5.1 Reactivity and displacement reactions","In this topic you will: put metals in order of how reactive they are · use that order to work out what a metal will react with · find out what a displacement reaction is Key words: reactivity series · displacement · displacement reaction"],[22,"the-reactivity-series","The reactivity series","- In Stage 8 you tested metals against oxygen , water (or steam) and dilute acid . Put all those results together and the metals fall into a single order. - That order is called the reactivity series . It has the most reactive metals at the top and the least reactive at the bottom . - The order you have to know: Metal Symbol most reactive potassium K sodium Na calcium Ca magnesium Mg aluminium Al zinc Zn iron Fe lead Pb copper Cu silver Ag least reactive gold Au"],[22,"what-each-part-of-the-series-actually-does","What each part of the series actually does","The table below is the evidence the order was built from. Read it downwards and you can see the reactions fading out. Metal With oxygen With water With dilute acid potassium, sodium burns brightly when heated to form an oxide very vigorous reaction in cold water; the hydroxide is formed violent reaction and very dangerous calcium, magnesium burns brightly in air when heated to form an oxide slow reaction in cold water to form the hydroxide reaction, which becomes less vigorous as you go down the list aluminium, zinc, iron slow reaction when heated to form an oxide reacts with steam but not water to form an oxide reaction, which becomes less vigorous as you go down the list lead slow reaction when heated to form an oxide no reaction with steam or water reaction, which becomes less vigorous as you go down the list copper slow reaction when heated to form an oxide no reaction with steam or water no reaction silver, gold no reaction no reaction with steam or water no reaction - Notice the pattern: the higher a metal is, the more violently it reacts, and the more different things it will react with. - Gold and silver are so unreactive that they are found in the ground as the metal itself , not as a compound."],[22,"displacement-reactions","Displacement reactions","- Put a clean iron nail in a beaker of copper sulfate solution and two things happen: - the blue copper sulfate solution goes a slightly paler colour - the nail turns copper coloured — it has become coated with copper - The equations: - Why it happens: iron is more reactive than copper , so the iron has 'pushed out' the copper from the copper sulfate and taken its place, forming iron sulfate. - That 'pushing out' is called displacement , so this type of reaction is a displacement reaction . - The rule: a more reactive metal can replace a less reactive one in a salt. - Run it the other way and nothing happens. A copper nail in iron sulfate gives no reaction , because copper is less reactive than iron — it cannot displace it. How to answer any displacement question: find both metals in the reactivity series. If the metal you are adding is higher than the metal in the salt, there is a reaction. If it is lower , there is none. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.1</b </summary 1a Where does lithium go? Below sodium and above calcium. 1b Why? Because lithium reacts with water more vigorously than calcium but less vigorously than sodium . 2a Where does platinum go? Below gold (any plausible position near gold is acceptable). 2b Why? Platinum is very unreactive — it does not react with oxygen at all, so it does not tarnish. 3 Three metals A–C in dilute hydrochloric acid. Which is most reactive, and how can you tell? Metal B , because it gives off the most bubbles . 4 Zinc and sulfuric acid. zinc + sulfuric acid - zinc sulfate + hydrogen 6 Zinc or copper — which is more reactive? Zinc. 7 Can zinc displace the copper in copper sulfate? Yes — zinc is above copper. 8 Silver or magnesium — which is more reactive? Magnesium. 9 Can silver displace the magnesium in magnesium sulfate? No — silver is below magnesium. </details"],[22,"5-2-using-the-reactivity-series-and-displacement-reactions","5.2 Using the reactivity series and displacement reactions","In this topic you will: use displacement reactions to identify an unknown metal · learn about some useful displacement reactions Key words: blast furnace · molten · ores · thermite reaction"],[22,"identifying-a-mystery-metal","Identifying a mystery metal","- If you know the reactivity series you can work backwards from how a metal behaves to which metal it is . - The evidence you can collect: its appearance , and its reactions with water , oxygen and dilute acid — plus displacement reactions with different salt solutions. - Safety: you do not know what the metal is, so treat it with care and pick it up using forceps . - Each test narrows the field. A metal that fizzes in cold water is near the top; a metal that does nothing in acid is near the bottom; a metal that turns a copper sulfate solution paler is above copper."],[22,"the-thermite-reaction","The thermite reaction","- Aluminium is more reactive than iron , so aluminium will displace iron from solid iron oxide if the mixture is heated: - This reaction releases a lot of energy — it is an exothermic reaction. - It gets so hot that the iron produced is molten (liquid). Iron's melting point is 1535 °C , so this tells you how extreme the temperature is. - What it is used for: welding railway rails together . The rails often have to be welded in situ — out on the line, not in a workshop — and the thermite reaction can be carried out anywhere. The iron oxide and aluminium powder react in a container placed on the rails, and the molten iron is shaped to join the two rails. - Starting it off: the mixture has to be ignited , using another exothermic reaction — this time between magnesium powder and barium nitrate . That reaction supplies the energy that starts the aluminium/iron oxide reaction."],[22,"displacement-using-carbon","Displacement using carbon","- Carbon is not a metal , but it can still displace some metals from their compounds. - Carbon will displace zinc, iron, tin and lead from their ores. (An ore is a rock that contains a metal compound.) - So carbon sits below magnesium and above zinc in the reactivity series — it beats zinc, iron, tin and lead, but not the metals above them. - People discovered this about 3500 years ago : iron ore heated with charcoal at very high temperatures produced molten iron . - Today the same displacement reaction is done on a huge scale in a blast furnace : - Iron ore and coke (a form of carbon) are added at the top . - Air is blown into the furnace to burn the coke and create the high temperatures the reaction needs. - The iron is displaced from its ore by the carbon . - Molten iron collects at the bottom , where it can be run off. - The iron ore is mainly iron oxide , and it reacts with the carbon:"],[22,"practical-extracting-a-metal-using-carbon","Practical: extracting a metal using carbon","- Method: a spatula of copper oxide in a test tube, a spatula of charcoal powder on top — do not mix the powders . Heat strongly in a Bunsen flame for five minutes, cool, then look where the two layers meet . - What you see: pink-brown copper at the boundary between the layers, identified by its distinctive colour. - What it proves: carbon is more reactive than copper. - Keeping the powders in separate layers matters: it means any new substance can only have appeared at the interface , so you know it came from the reaction and was not there already. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.2</b </summary 1 Could you use iron to displace aluminium from aluminium oxide? No — iron is less reactive than aluminium. 2 Why is the thermite reaction so useful for welding rails? It can be carried out a long way from a workshop or laboratory , and it produces molten iron which can be used to join the two rails together. 3 Why were early ironworks built close to the mines? So that the raw materials — iron ore and coal — did not have to be transported very far . That reduced the cost of production. Extracting metals using carbon — 1 Was there a reaction? Yes. There should be copper at the interface of the two powders , recognisable by its distinctive colour . Extracting metals using carbon — 2 Word equation. copper oxide + carbon - copper + carbon dioxide Extracting metals using carbon — 3 What does this tell you about carbon? That carbon is more reactive than copper . Extracting metals using carbon — 4 Where would you place carbon in the reactivity series? Below magnesium and above zinc — carbon can displace zinc, iron, tin and lead, and zinc is the most reactive of those. </details"],[22,"5-3-salts","5.3 Salts","In this topic you will: explain what a salt is · give examples of salts and their uses · prepare a salt by reacting a metal with an acid · prepare a salt by heating a metal oxide with an acid Key words: carbonate · chloride · citrates · crystallisation · formula (plural: formulae) · nitrate · salt · sulfate"],[22,"what-is-a-salt","What is a salt?","- In everyday life \"salt\" means the stuff on the table — that one is sodium chloride . In chemistry a salt is a much bigger family, and you have already met several: copper sulfate , silver nitrate , calcium carbonate . - Salts are made in enormous quantity: the chemical industry makes hundreds of thousands of tonnes of different salts every day. Salts you should be able to name a use for: Salt What it is used for sodium chloride preserving food, and as table salt to flavour food magnesium carbonate gymnasts use it to keep their hands dry so they do not slip on the apparatus calcium sulfate making blackboard chalk aluminium sulfate added to dyes to help them stick to fibres ammonium nitrate a fertiliser , to help crops grow well copper sulfate coating seeds — it stops fungi growing on them when they are planted"],[22,"acids-and-the-salts-they-make","Acids and the salts they make","- All acids contain hydrogen. Many methods of making salts start with an acid , and the acid decides which family of salt you get . Name of acid Formula Salts formed from the acid Example of salt Formula of salt hydrochloric acid HCl chlorides sodium chloride NaCl sulfuric acid H2SO4 sulfates copper sulfate CuSO4 nitric acid HNO3 nitrates potassium nitrate KNO3 - Two more acids worth knowing: - Carbonic acid — a weak acid formed when carbon dioxide reacts with water . Its salts are carbonates . - Citric acid — found in fruits such as oranges and lemons . Its salts are citrates . Exam habit: if a question tells you the acid, you already know half the answer. Hydrochloric - chloride, sulfuric - sulfate, nitric - nitrate. Then take the metal from whatever else went in."],[22,"route-1-metal-acid","Route 1 — metal + acid","- You have reacted metals with dilute acids before; it is often a good way to make a salt. - The worked example: - The catch: the metal has to be reactive enough to displace hydrogen from the acid, but not so reactive that the reaction is dangerous . You would never make sodium chloride by putting sodium into hydrochloric acid — sodium is far too reactive and the reaction would be explosive . Practical: making the salt zinc sulfate 1. Pour about 50 cm3 of dilute sulfuric acid into a 250 cm3 beaker. 2. Add 1–5 g of zinc to the acid. 3. Once the mixture stops fizzing , pour it into an evaporating dish. 4. Heat very gently until you see crystals forming at the edge of the solution. 5. Remove from the heat and leave for a few days for crystals to form. This is crystallisation . - Safety: the solution may spit and burn you when heated, so heat gently and wear safety glasses. Use tongs to move the evaporating basin onto a heatproof mat. - Getting big crystals: leave the solution to evaporate slowly . Boiling it dry gives small crystals; slow evaporation gives large ones."],[22,"route-2-metal-oxide-acid","Route 2 — metal oxide + acid","- Some metals are too unreactive to displace hydrogen from an acid — silver and copper , for example. Putting copper in sulfuric acid gets you nothing. - The way round it is to start from the metal oxide instead: - The worked example: - Notice what is missing compared with the metal + acid route: no hydrogen gas . The products are just the salt and water. Practical: making the salt copper sulfate 1. Pour about 100 cm3 of dilute sulfuric acid into a 250 cm3 beaker and add black copper oxide powder . 2. Heat very gently , stirring all the time. Do not boil the acid. 3. When the mixture turns blue , turn off the heat and let it cool. 4. Filter the mixture. The filtrate is a solution of copper sulfate. Pour it into an evaporating basin. 5. Heat very gently on a pipe-clay triangle until crystals form at the edge , then leave for a few days to crystallise. - Why filter? To remove the unreacted copper oxide powder . You add copper oxide until no more will react, so some black powder is always left over, and it would spoil the crystals. - Colour change as a clue: the mixture going blue tells you copper sulfate is being formed. - Swap the acid and you swap the salt: use hydrochloric acid instead and the same method makes copper chloride ; use nitric acid and it makes copper nitrate . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.3</b </summary 1a Properties of acids. Turn litmus red ; turn universal indicator yellow or red ; pH less than 7 ; taste sour ; strong acids are corrosive . 1b Everyday products containing acids. Vinegar, fruit juices, lemonade, fizzy drinks, citrus fruits, tomato and other savoury sauces, pickled vegetables. 2 Which elements are in nitric acid? Hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen. 3 Which elements are in sulfuric acid, and how many atoms of each? Hydrogen, sulfur and oxygen — two hydrogen atoms, one sulfur atom and four oxygen atoms. 4a What is similar about HCl and NaCl? Both formulae contain chlorine, Cl . 4b What is different? Hydrochloric acid contains hydrogen, H ; sodium chloride contains sodium, Na . 5a Which ingredient on the jam label is a salt? Sodium citrate. 5b Why is it added? It helps to keep the tangy taste of the preserve and reduces the risk of the product decaying . 6 Which compound in the zinc equation is a salt? Zinc chloride. 7 Which acid would you add to magnesium to make magnesium sulfate? Sulfuric acid. 8 Iron and hydrochloric acid. iron + hydrochloric acid - iron chloride + hydrogen 9 Why would preparing sodium chloride from sodium and hydrochloric acid be dangerous? Sodium is very reactive and the reaction would be explosive . Making zinc sulfate — 1 Word equation. zinc + sulfuric acid - zinc sulfate + hydrogen Making zinc sulfate — 2 Practical point when evaporating. The solution may spit when heated , which could cause burns. Making zinc sulfate — 3 Which way gives larger crystals? Leaving it to evaporate slowly. Making copper sulfate — 3 Why was the mixture filtered? To remove any unreacted copper oxide powder . Making copper sulfate — 4 How would you make copper chloride instead? Use hydrochloric acid instead of sulfuric acid. Making copper sulfate — 5 and 6 Word equations. copper oxide + hydrochloric acid - copper chloride + water; copper oxide + nitric acid - copper nitrate + water </details"],[22,"5-4-other-ways-of-making-salts","5.4 Other ways of making salts","In this topic you will: prepare a salt using an acid and a carbonate · prepare a salt using neutralisation · do risk assessments for practical work · use word and symbol equations Key words: bases · crystallise · erodes · limestone · neutralisation"],[22,"route-3-acid-carbonate","Route 3 — acid + carbonate","- Carbonates are themselves salts — calcium carbonate is a salt, formed from a metal and carbonic acid . But you can use a carbonate to make a different salt by reacting it with another acid. - Three worked examples, all with the same carbonate: - The give-away for this route is the carbon dioxide. If a reaction fizzes and the gas turns limewater cloudy, a carbonate was one of the reactants. - Where it happens outside the lab: - Limestone is made of calcium carbonate , so it reacts with acid rain and erodes . - Coral skeletons are also calcium carbonate. As the oceans take up more carbon dioxide they become slightly more acidic , and the coral reacts with the acid. Practical: preparing copper chloride from a carbonate 1. Place 25 cm3 of hydrochloric acid in a small beaker. 2. Add a spatula of copper carbonate . 3. Keep adding copper carbonate until it stops reacting , so a small amount is left unreacted . This is called adding excess copper carbonate, and it makes sure all the acid has reacted . 4. Filter the mixture. The unreacted copper carbonate is trapped in the filter paper . 5. Pour the filtrate into an evaporating basin and heat gently to drive off the water. 6. Stop heating when crystals appear around the edge . 7. Leave for a few days to cool and crystallise slowly. - What you observe: bubbling , as carbon dioxide is given off. - What the filtering tells you about solubility: the copper carbonate is insoluble — it is left behind in the filter paper. The copper chloride is soluble — it passes through as a solution. - Why excess carbonate is deliberate: leftover solid carbonate can be filtered out, but leftover acid cannot. Adding excess is how you guarantee the acid is used up."],[22,"route-4-acid-alkali-neutralisation","Route 4 — acid + alkali (neutralisation)","- Alkalis react with acids to neutralise them , and a salt is produced. The other product is water . - The general equation: - Bases and alkalis — the difference that gets asked every time: - When a metal oxide dissolves in water it forms an alkaline solution . - Metal oxides are called bases. - Soluble bases form alkalis when they dissolve in water. For example: sodium oxide + water - sodium hydroxide . Sodium oxide is a base ; the sodium hydroxide is an alkali . - So: every alkali is a base, but only the soluble bases are alkalis. Iron oxide and copper oxide are bases that are not soluble, so they never form alkalis — but they will still react with acids to make salts (that is Route 2). Practical: preparing a salt by neutralisation 1. Place hydrochloric acid in a burette . 2. Measure 20 cm3 of sodium hydroxide into a small flask. 3. Add a few drops of universal indicator solution . In the alkali it is blue . 4. Add the acid from the burette slowly , swirling the flask as you go — so it mixes and reacts before more acid is added. 5. When the indicator turns green you have a neutral solution. 6. Add a spatula of charcoal powder and stir. The charcoal takes the green colour out of the solution. 7. Filter the mixture to remove the charcoal. 8. Put the filtrate into an evaporating basin and heat gently . 9. Stop heating when crystals appear around the edge . 10. Leave for a few days; the water evaporates slowly, leaving crystals of the salt — here, sodium chloride , which forms white cubic crystals. - Why the burette: it lets you add the acid a drop at a time near the end point, so you can stop exactly at neutral. - Why the charcoal: the indicator has to come out or your salt crystals would be stained green. - If you overshoot and add too much acid, add a little more alkali until the indicator goes green again. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.4</b </summary 1 Magnesium carbonate and nitric acid. magnesium carbonate + nitric acid - magnesium nitrate + water + carbon dioxide 2 Magnesium carbonate and sulfuric acid, in symbols. MgCO3 + H2SO4 - MgSO4 + H2O + CO2 3 How would you check the gas is carbon dioxide? Bubble it through limewater , which goes cloudy if the gas is carbon dioxide. 4 How can you test whether a liquid is an acid or an alkali? Add universal indicator solution . Yellow or red means acid; blue or purple means alkali. 5 What word describes a solution that is neither acid nor alkali? Neutral. 6 Properties of alkalis. Soapy feel ; turn litmus blue ; turn universal indicator blue or purple ; pH greater than 7 . 7 What is the difference between a base and an alkali? A base is a metal oxide. If a metal oxide dissolves in water it makes an alkaline solution — an alkali . 8 Magnesium oxide and sulfuric acid. magnesium oxide + sulfuric acid - magnesium sulfate + water 9 The same in symbols. MgO + H2SO4 - MgSO4 + H2O 10 How could you use iron oxide to make iron chloride? React the iron oxide with hydrochloric acid . Copper chloride practical — 3 What did you observe? Bubbling , as a gas is given off. Copper chloride practical — 4 Which gas? Carbon dioxide. Copper chloride practical — 6 Appearance of the copper chloride. Pale green crystals or powder. Copper chloride practical — 8 What about solubility? Copper carbonate is not soluble — it stays in the filter paper. Copper chloride is soluble — it passes through as a solution. Copper chloride practical — 9 How would you make copper sulfate instead? Repeat the experiment using sulfuric acid in place of hydrochloric acid. Neutralisation practical — 3 and 4 Indicator colours. Blue in the sodium hydroxide; green when neutral. Neutralisation practical — 5 Why swirl the flask? So the acid and alkali mix and react before you add more acid. Neutralisation practical — 6 What if you add too much acid? Add a little more alkali until the indicator turns green again. Neutralisation practical — 7 and 8 The salt and the equation. Sodium chloride. sodium hydroxide + hydrochloric acid - sodium chloride + water Neutralisation practical — 9 The crystals. White and cubic . </details"],[22,"5-5-rearranging-atoms","5.5 Rearranging atoms","In this topic you will: look at how atoms are rearranged in chemical reactions · learn what happens to the mass of reactants and products · learn what happens to the energy in a reaction · carry out practical work safely Key words: crucible · endothermic reactions · exothermic reaction · the law of conservation of energy · the law of conservation of mass"],[22,"what-a-chemical-reaction-actually-does-to-atoms","What a chemical reaction actually does to atoms","- In a chemical reaction atoms form new combinations . Atoms on their own may join with others; atoms that were bonded may separate and bond to something else. - Take iron and sulfur. The iron and sulfur atoms that were there at the start are still there at the end . They have just rearranged . - In a chemical reaction no atoms are lost and no new atoms are produced. The atoms are simply rearranged into new combinations. - The consequence you can use in any question: every element in the reactants is still there in the products. Nothing disappears; nothing new appears. - Follow the elements through: magnesium goes in as the metal and comes out inside the salt. Hydrogen goes in as part of the acid and comes out as hydrogen gas. Chlorine goes in as part of the acid and comes out inside the salt."],[22,"conservation-of-mass","Conservation of mass","- Atoms have mass. If no atoms are gained or lost, then no mass is gained or lost either. - The classroom test: put calcium carbonate in a flask, add hydrochloric acid , stopper the flask and stand it on a top pan balance. - One prediction was that the mass would decrease , because one product is a gas and gases are light. - Another was that it would increase , because there are two reactants and three products. - The correct prediction was that it would stay the same , because the stopper means no atoms can enter or leave the flask . - The mass does not change . This is the law of conservation of mass : the mass you begin with is the mass you end with."],[22,"not-the-results-you-expect-the-mass-that-seems-to-fall","Not the results you expect — the mass that seems to fall","- Arun does the same reaction in an open flask : Time in minutes Mass of flask and contents in g 0 250 10 207 - The mass appears to decrease by 43 g . The law has not been broken: - The carbon dioxide escapes into the air because the flask is open, so you cannot measure it. All the elements are still present in the products — remember that water is made from hydrogen and oxygen atoms . - The fix: put a stopper in the flask so the gas cannot escape, and the balance reading holds steady."],[22,"another-surprising-result-the-mass-that-seems-to-rise","Another surprising result — the mass that seems to rise","- Some magnesium is put in a crucible and the crucible plus magnesium is weighed. The crucible is heated carefully, and the lid is lifted from time to time to let the air in . Afterwards it is weighed again. - The crucible now contains white ashes , and their mass is more than the mass of the magnesium at the start . - People expect the opposite, because the ashes look smaller and they see flames escaping , so it seems as if something has been lost. - The word equation explains it: - The mass increases because oxygen from the air has combined with the magnesium. The oxygen was always there — it just was not on the balance to begin with. - The history: the French scientist Antoine Lavoisier did this experiment in 1772 . He repeated it many times and always got an increase, and eventually concluded that when something burns it combines with a gas from the air . He found the same gas is involved in respiration , and he named it oxygen . The pattern behind both surprises: mass only seems to change when a gas enters or leaves without being weighed. Close the system and the law holds every time."],[22,"energy-and-chemical-reactions","Energy and chemical reactions","- All chemical reactions involve energy. Energy is used to break bonds in the reactants and released when new bonds form in the products. - Exothermic reaction — less energy is needed to break the bonds than is released when the new bonds form, so energy is given out . When metals react with water or acids, energy is released as thermal energy , and sometimes as light, sound or kinetic energy . - Example: potassium and water . The potassium catches fire , gets hot enough to melt , burns with a pinkish purple flame , hisses or explodes , and moves across the surface of the water. - Endothermic reactions — more energy is needed to break the bonds than is released when the new bonds form, so these reactions take energy in . - Example: sherbet sweets . The reaction happens in your mouth and gives a cool refreshing feeling , because it is taking thermal energy from your mouth. - Either way, there is no overall change in the amount of energy during the reaction, because energy cannot be created or destroyed . This is the law of conservation of energy : energy may be transferred from one form to another , but the same amount remains after the reaction as before. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 5.5</b </summary 1 Copper carbonate + hydrochloric acid (from 5.4). a Which product contains copper? Copper chloride. b Which reactant contains hydrogen? Hydrochloric acid. c Which product contains hydrogen? Water. d Which reactant contains carbon? Copper carbonate. e Which product contains carbon? Carbon dioxide. 2 Copper oxide + sulfuric acid (from 5.3). a and c Which kinds of atom are present at the start, and at the end? Copper, oxygen, hydrogen and sulfur — the same list both times. b and d How many of each? 1 copper, 5 oxygen, 2 hydrogen, 1 sulfur — the same count both times. 3 If 37 g of magnesium reacts with 150 g of sulfuric acid, what is the total mass of the products? 187 g — because no mass is lost. 4 If you start with 10 g of magnesium in that reaction, how much magnesium is in the magnesium sulfate? 10 g — every magnesium atom ends up in the salt. 5 Calcium carbonate + hydrochloric acid. a Which reactant contains oxygen? Calcium carbonate. b Which two products contain oxygen? Carbon dioxide and water. c Where does the hydrogen in the water come from? From the hydrochloric acid. 6 What should Arun have done to show mass is conserved? Used a stopper in the flask to stop the gas escaping. Burning magnesium — 1 and 2 What happened to the mass and why? It increased , because the magnesium reacted with the oxygen in the air and that oxygen added to the mass. Burning magnesium — 3 Name of the product. Magnesium oxide. Burning magnesium — 4 Safety risks. All to do with heating the crucible . It gets very hot, and because the lid has to be raised during the experiment the tongs or spatula must be used with care . The crucible must be left to stand a long time before it is cool enough to touch. </details"],[23,"6-1-loudness-and-pitch-of-sound","6.1 Loudness and pitch of sound","In this topic you will: find out about the amplitude and the frequency of a sound wave · learn how amplitude is linked to loudness · learn how frequency is linked to pitch · learn to read amplitude and frequency off a diagram of a sound wave Key words: amplitude · frequency · loudness · oscilloscope · peak · pitch · trough"],[23,"how-a-sound-wave-travels","How a sound wave travels","- Sound is made when objects vibrate . You met this in Stage 7. - When a sound wave travels forward through air, the particles do not travel with it . They move backwards and forwards about their normal position, and it is the wave that moves forward. - Loudness describes how quiet or loud a sound appears to be. It depends on two things: - how much the object is vibrating — the greater the range of each vibration, the louder the sound - how far away the vibrating object is — the further away, the quieter the sound. - Sounds get quieter with distance because of energy dissipation (Stage 7). - The greater the distance of each vibration in the object, the greater the distance the air particles get pushed and pulled."],[23,"amplitude","Amplitude","The movement of particles is hard to draw in a picture of a wave, so it is drawn as a graph instead. The graph shows how far the particles move forward and backward with time . - The amplitude is the maximum distance that the particles move , either forward or backward. - On the graph you can measure it in two ways, and both give the same answer for the same wave: - from zero to the peak (the highest point of the graph) - from zero to the trough (the lowest point of the graph). - Peak = highest point of the graph. Trough = lowest point of the graph. The rule to remember: as the amplitude of a sound wave increases, the loudness of the sound increases."],[23,"seeing-sound-on-an-oscilloscope","Seeing sound on an oscilloscope","- An oscilloscope is a piece of equipment that displays a graph of a sound wave on a screen. - It uses a microphone to detect the sound, then draws the graph. - The louder sound has the larger amplitude . Everything else about the two traces is the same."],[23,"pitch-and-frequency","Pitch and frequency","- Pitch is how high or low a sound appears on a musical scale. - Hold a ruler over the edge of a desk and twang it: it vibrates and makes a sound. Move the ruler so the vibrating part is shorter and the pitch changes , because the speed of the vibrations changes. - The faster the vibrations, the higher the pitch. - The speed of vibrations is measured as the number of vibrations per second . That number is called the frequency . - Frequency is measured in hertz (Hz) . A frequency of 500 Hz means 500 vibrations every second . The rule to remember: as the frequency of a sound wave increases, the pitch of the sound increases. - The horizontal axis of a wave graph is time , so a wave with a higher frequency fits more waves into the same time . - Look carefully at those two traces: the amplitudes are the same , so the loudness of the two sounds is the same . Only the pitch is different. - Usually, shorter vibrating objects make higher pitch sounds than longer ones. On a gayageum , the bridges are moved to change the length of string that vibrates — the shorter the string, the higher the pitch."],[23,"reading-a-wave-the-two-measurements-do-not-mix","Reading a wave: the two measurements do not mix","What you want Where you look on the graph What it tells you amplitude vertical: zero to peak, or zero to trough how loud the sound is frequency horizontal: how many waves fit into the time shown how high the pitch is - Change the amplitude only → the loudness changes, the pitch does not. - Change the frequency only → the pitch changes, the loudness does not."],[23,"investigating-a-vibrating-rule","Investigating a vibrating rule","- A rule vibrating at the edge of a desk is too fast to count . Clamping a metre rule between blocks of wood with a mass taped to the free end slows the vibration down enough to measure. - Method for frequency: count the number of complete vibrations in 10 seconds , then divide by 10 . That gives the frequency in Hz. - Counting over 10 seconds rather than 1 second makes the measurement more accurate — a small timing error is a much smaller fraction of 10 s than of 1 s. - Independent variable: the mass. Dependent variable: the frequency. Control variables: the same metre rule, the same free length, the mass in the same position, the end pulled down the same distance each time. - Result: as the mass increases , the frequency decreases , so the pitch gets lower . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.1</b </summary 1a Link between amplitude and loudness? As amplitude increases, loudness increases (and as amplitude decreases, loudness decreases). 1b Link between frequency and pitch? As frequency increases, pitch increases (and as frequency decreases, pitch decreases). 2a Which describes the amplitude of a sound wave? A — the maximum distance that particles move from their position when there is no wave. 2b Which describes the frequency of a sound wave? C — the number of times that particles vibrate backwards and forwards every second. 3a Which letter represents the amplitude? B — the height from the middle line up to the peak. 3b Which letter shows a measurement that depends on the frequency? C or D — a horizontal distance along the time axis. 4 A → B, then C → D. a amplitude increases · b loudness increases · c pitch decreases · d loudness stays the same · e frequency decreases . Activity 1 Trend in the A notes? The frequency doubles for each octave. Activity 2 How does pitch change left to right on the keyboard? Pitch increases from left to right. Activity 3 Predict A5, A6, A7. 880 Hz, 1760 Hz, 3520 Hz. Activity 4 The soft pedal makes the note quieter without changing the pitch. What does it do to… a the amplitude of the string vibration — decreases it · b the frequency of the string vibration — does not affect it. </details"],[23,"6-2-interference-of-sound","6.2 Interference of sound","In this topic you will: find out how sound waves can reinforce each other to make louder sounds · find out how sound waves can cancel each other to make no sound Key words: interference · reinforce · cancel"],[23,"interference","Interference","- Water waves are a useful analogy for sound waves. The particles do not move in the same way, but the analogy shows how waves behave. - Dip two fingers in and out of water at the same frequency and the same amplitude and the two sets of waves meet and make a pattern. - The effect produced when waves meet each other is called interference . - Interference can only happen between waves of the same type : sound can interfere with sound, but sound cannot interfere with water waves . - It is easiest to detect when the waves have the same frequency and the same amplitude . - Interference produces two effects: the waves reinforce , or the waves cancel ."],[23,"waves-that-reinforce","Waves that reinforce","Reinforce means to make stronger. Waves reinforce when they meet peak with peak and trough with trough . - The amplitudes of the two waves are added together . - The frequency does not change . - For sound, a bigger amplitude means a louder sound, so when two sound waves reinforce, the sound gets louder . Where this happens in real life: at a concert there is a loudspeaker on either side of the stage. Both play the same sound, so at certain places in the audience the two sets of waves arrive peak on peak and a person there hears a louder sound of that pitch. On a diagram of the wave pattern, the curved lines are peaks ; wherever two lines cross , the waves reinforce."],[23,"waves-that-cancel","Waves that cancel","Waves cancel when they meet peak against trough . Think of a peak as the maximum positive amplitude and a trough as the maximum negative amplitude: adding a positive number to a negative number of equal size gives zero , for example 2 + (−2) = 0. - The amplitudes are added together and the result is zero amplitude — no sound . - For two sound waves to cancel completely , their frequencies must be the same and their amplitudes must be the same . Why a room never goes completely silent: sound waves reflect off walls, furniture and the people in the audience. Those reflections add many more waves to the pattern, so it is very unlikely that only two identical waves are left to cancel each other."],[23,"noise-cancelling-headphones","Noise-cancelling headphones","1. A small microphone picks up the sound coming from the surroundings. 2. The headphones analyse that sound wave. 3. They create another sound wave with the same amplitude and the same frequency , but out of phase with it — peaks where the original has troughs. 4. The two waves cancel , so the wearer does not hear the outside sound. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.2</b </summary 1a Which always results in a louder sound? B — two sound waves reinforce. 1b Which always results in no sound being heard? A — two sound waves cancel. 2a How must two sound waves meet in order to reinforce? Peak to peak (and trough to trough). 2b How must two sound waves meet in order to cancel? Peak to trough , with equal amplitudes. 3 Two waves of 450 Hz and amplitude 0.5 mm reinforce. The wave that is formed? frequency 450 Hz , amplitude 1.0 mm . The frequency is unchanged; the amplitudes add. 4a Two waves of 600 Hz; one has amplitude 0.25 mm. What amplitude must the other have to cancel it? 0.25 mm — the amplitudes must be equal. 4b What is the amplitude after they cancel? Zero (0 mm). Activity 1 Why are both nails fixed to the same piece of wood? So the two sets of waves have the same frequency and the same amplitude — one piece of wood forces both nails to move together. Activity 2 a larger amplitude → the waves reinforce · b zero amplitude → the waves cancel . Activity 3a Two sources that give equal frequency and amplitude? Two loudspeakers driven by the same signal, two identical tuning forks , or two identical musical instruments. Activity 3b Why is it hard to make sound cancel completely in a quiet room? Because sound waves reflect off objects, walls and furniture , adding extra waves to the pattern. Think like a scientist 3–4 Frequency and the length of pipe above the water? As the frequency increases, the length decreases . This is why longer wind instruments produce lower pitch notes . </details"],[23,"6-3-formation-of-the-moon","6.3 Formation of the Moon","In this topic you will: describe the evidence for the collision theory for the formation of the Moon Key words: collision theory · Theia"],[23,"where-did-the-moon-come-from","Where did the Moon come from?","- In the early twentieth century scientists thought the Moon had formed by splitting away from the Earth soon after the Earth formed. - Calculations showed that, if that theory were right, the Moon would still be moving away from the Earth. Accurate modern measurements show the Moon is moving away, at about 4 cm each year . - But that measurement could not confirm the splitting theory — it fits other explanations too. - In 1974 a different idea was suggested: the collision theory (also called the giant impact hypothesis )."],[23,"the-collision-theory","The collision theory","The collision happened relatively soon after the Solar System formed , and the stages are: 1. A newly formed planet about the size of Mars , which scientists call Theia , was on a path towards the newly formed Earth . 2. The two planets collided . The impact broke rocks and dust away from both planets. 3. The Earth we have now was formed from the two planets joined together . 4. The broken rocks and dust settled into a ring around the Earth. 5. Gravity pulled the ring of rocks and dust together to form the Moon ."],[23,"weighing-up-the-evidence","Weighing up the evidence","There is more evidence for the collision theory than for any other current theory — but not all the evidence agrees with it, and a good scientist looks at both columns. Evidence that supports the collision theory Evidence that contradicts the collision theory The Moon is less dense than the Earth. The surface of the Earth does not appear ever to have been molten , yet such a collision would have melted it (and it would later have solidified). Rock samples from the Moon show its surface was once molten . Venus has no moon , although collisions were common in the early Solar System and scientists would expect Venus to have gained one the same way. The Moon has a small iron core , similar to the Earth. Moon rock would be expected to be more like Theia's rock than the Earth's; in fact its composition is more similar to Earth's . There is evidence outside the Solar System of similar collisions causing rings of rock and dust. It fits with the theory of how the Solar System formed . The composition of rocks on the Earth and the Moon is the same . Other theories that have been suggested: the Earth and Moon formed at the same time in the same way as the rest of the Solar System; the Moon formed from asteroids pulled together soon after the Solar System formed; a passing object was captured by the Earth's gravity; the Moon split away from the Earth. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.3</b </summary 1 Which planet is closest in size to Theia? A — Mars. 2 Why is the composition of Earth rock and Moon rock so similar? C — rocks from Theia and Earth were completely mixed during the collision. 3 Phobos and Deimos, the two moons of Mars. a If they were originally asteroids , their rock would be different from Mars's rock , and possibly different from each other. b If they were formed by a collision with Mars, their rock would be very similar to Mars's rock and very similar to each other. 4 Why are collisions between planet-sized objects no longer common? Most of the objects travelling on paths close to the planets have already collided with them. Soon after the Solar System formed there were still many left-over objects; the Solar System has become more stable over time . </details"],[23,"6-4-nebulae","6.4 Nebulae","In this topic you will: discover that nebulae are clouds of dust and gas in space · learn how stars can form in nebulae Key words: nebula (plural: nebulae) · northern hemisphere · southern hemisphere · stellar nursery"],[23,"what-a-nebula-is","What a nebula is","- A nebula is a cloud of dust and gas in space . The plural is nebulae . - The gases are mostly hydrogen , with a smaller quantity of helium . - The particles of gas and dust are very far apart . A nebula the same size as the Earth would have a mass of only a few kilograms . - Most nebulae are very large — many are more than 10 000 times bigger than the Solar System . - Some nebulae form when giant stars reach the end of their life and explode , sending dust and gas over a wide area of space. Easiest nebula to see What that hemisphere is Northern hemisphere the Orion nebula the part of the Earth north of the equator Southern hemisphere the Tarantula nebula the part of the Earth south of the equator - Both can be seen without a telescope ; a powerful telescope shows the detail. Nebula is not the same as galaxy. A galaxy is much larger ; a galaxy may contain nebulae, but a nebula never contains a galaxy. A galaxy also contains older stars, planet systems, comets and asteroids."],[23,"stellar-nurseries","Stellar nurseries","A stellar nursery is an area in space where stars are formed . Stellar means anything to do with stars; a nursery is a place where young things are cared for — here the young things are stars . Only some types of nebula act as stellar nurseries. Where one does, a new star forms like this: 1. Dust and gas start to collapse together under the force of gravity . 2. As the mass collects together and grows larger , the inward force of gravity increases , which attracts still more material. 3. The growing force makes the pressure inside the new star very large. 4. High pressure causes heat. 5. The heat lets atoms react with each other , and the new star gives out heat and light . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.4</b </summary 1a Features common to all nebulae? They are clouds of dust and gas in space . 1b Difference between a nebula and a galaxy? Galaxies are much larger ; a galaxy may contain nebulae but not the other way round; galaxies contain older stars and planet systems formed millions of years ago, and other objects such as comets and asteroids. 2 The two gases most commonly found in nebulae? Hydrogen and helium . 3a What is a stellar nursery? A place in a nebula where stars are formed . 3b Which statement is correct? B — only some nebulae act as stellar nurseries. 4 Outline how a new star forms. Dust and gas are pulled together by gravity ; as the object grows, the force of gravity increases and attracts more material; the pressure inside increases ; the high pressure starts reactions that give out heat and light . 5 How many times bigger is the Orion nebula (24 ly) than the Solar System (0.001 ly)? 24 ÷ 0.001 = 24 000 times . </details"],[23,"6-5-tectonics","6.5 Tectonics","In this topic you will: discover how convection currents cause the movement of tectonic plates · learn about the evidence we have for tectonic plates Key words: tectonic plate · convection current · jigsaw · continental coasts · fossil record · alignment"],[23,"what-drives-the-plates","What drives the plates","- The outer layers of the Earth are the solid, rocky crust , which rests on the more fluid mantle (Stage 7). - The mantle is heated from the inner core , estimated to be over 5000 °C . That heat comes from thermal energy left over from the formation of the Earth , friction inside the Earth, and reactions in the rocks . - Thermal energy is transferred through fluids by convection (Unit 3), and the mantle behaves as a fluid. So: - The mantle is a very thick fluid and does not flow easily like water, so the convection currents move very slowly . - As the currents move across underneath the crust, the tectonic plates that make up the crust are pulled along — between 0.6 and 10 cm per year . - A tectonic plate is a part of the Earth's crust that can move ; it is supported on the mantle and moves on the mantle."],[23,"evidence-1-the-continents-fit-like-a-jigsaw","Evidence 1: the continents fit like a jigsaw","- A jigsaw puzzle is a picture cut into interlocking pieces that can be put back together to make the original picture. Scientists have done exactly this with the continental coasts . - The hypothesis: there was once one large continent which separated ; the separate parts became some of the tectonic plates, and convection currents in the mantle drove the movement . - That single continent is called Pangaea , thought to have existed between 335 and 175 million years ago and to have started breaking apart about 175 million years ago ."],[23,"evidence-2-the-fossil-record","Evidence 2: the fossil record","- The fossil record is the collection of thousands of fossils that tell us about the time before humans. Fossils are the remains of dead animals and plants that have turned to stone over millions of years . - Mesosaurus , an extinct crocodile-like reptile that lived about 275 million years ago , is found in the parts of Africa and South America that fit together in the jigsaw. It lived at the coast and in shallow water , so it is very unlikely to have crossed the 5000 km wide Atlantic Ocean that separates those continents today. - Glossopteris , a plant, is found as 300 million-year-old fossils in Antarctica, India, Australia, Africa and South America — more evidence that those continents were once joined."],[23,"evidence-3-the-alignment-of-magnetic-materials","Evidence 3: the alignment of magnetic materials","- The Earth has a magnetic field . Today a compass needle points north, but about 780 000 years ago the same needle would have pointed south . The magnetic poles have swapped positions almost 200 times in the last 100 million years . - Magnetic crystals in molten rock line up with the Earth's magnetic field, like a compass needle. When the rock solidifies , that direction is locked in. The direction the crystals point is called their alignment . - At mid-oceanic ridges , magma comes up from the mantle and solidifies into new rock, pushing the continents apart . The new rock there is always aligned to north , because that is the field direction now. - Rocks further from the ridge have the opposite alignment , which shows that they are much older — they solidified when the field pointed the other way. The pattern is symmetrical on both sides of the ridge ."],[23,"evidence-4-where-earthquakes-and-volcanoes-happen","Evidence 4: where earthquakes and volcanoes happen","- If the plates really are moving, we would expect more earthquakes and volcanoes at plate boundaries . A world map of recorded earthquakes and volcanoes shows exactly that. - Be careful with the wording though: most earthquakes and volcanoes occur at or close to plate boundaries, but a few have been recorded far from any boundary — so 'always' is too strong. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 6.5</b </summary 1 How do convection currents happen in the mantle? The mantle is heated by the core ; molten rock in contact with the core is heated by conduction; it expands, becomes less dense and rises through the mantle; it cools next to the cooler crust and sinks again. 2 What is a tectonic plate? Part of the Earth's crust that can move ; it is supported on the mantle and moves on the mantle. 3 How is the jigsaw fit evidence for tectonic plates? The continents were originally one large mass of land which split apart , so today's coastlines are the lines made by the splitting. The movement was caused by the movement of tectonic plates — in theory the continents could be pushed back together to fit again. 4a How does the fossil record give evidence? Fossils of the same species are found on continents that look as if they could fit together, such as South America and Africa , suggesting those continents were once joined. 4b How does magnetic alignment give evidence? Magnetic materials in molten rock line up with the Earth's magnetic field and stay in that alignment when the rock solidifies. The field reverses over millions of years, and rocks either side of a mid-ocean ridge show symmetrical patterns of opposite alignment — so new rock forms at the ridge and pushes the plates apart. 5 The field has reversed 183 times in 83 million years. Average time between reversals? 83 000 000 ÷ 183 = about 454 000 years . 6 Discuss: 'Earthquakes and volcanoes always occur at tectonic plate boundaries.' The map supports the statement in that most earthquakes and volcanoes occur at or close to boundaries — but a few have been recorded far from boundaries , so the word always makes the statement wrong. Activity: Pangaea 1 Which continents fit together most easily? Africa and South America. Activity: Pangaea 4 Africa and South America are 5000 km apart and separated 140 million years ago. Average speed? 5000 km = 500 000 000 cm; 500 000 000 ÷ 140 000 000 = about 3.6 cm per year . </details <details <summary <b Check your progress — Chapter 6</b </summary 6.1 B 6.2 a the loudness increases · b the pitch increases · c the pitch increases 6.3 a B · b C 6.4 a Two waves drawn with peaks aligned and troughs aligned , of the same amplitude ; the result has the same frequency and double the amplitude . b Two waves drawn with the peaks of one aligned to the troughs of the other , of the same amplitude ; the result is a horizontal line . </details"],[24,"7-1-chromosomes-genes-and-dna","7.1 Chromosomes, genes and DNA","In this topic you will: learn that chromosomes contain genes · find out what genes and chromosomes are made of Key words: chromosomes · DNA · genes"],[24,"chromosomes","Chromosomes","- In Stage 7 you learnt that the nucleus of a cell controls its activities . This unit explains how it does that. - The nucleus of every cell contains threads called chromosomes . - Chromosomes were discovered in the nineteenth century , as soon as microscopes were powerful enough to show the structure of cells. Why you almost certainly never saw one down a school microscope. Two things have to be true before a chromosome shows up: - the cell must be dividing — with a light microscope, chromosomes only become visible just before a cell divides - the cell must be stained — chromosomes have to be coloured with a special stain before you can see them. - In the photograph above, the stain has coloured the chromosomes dark red , and you cannot really see any of the other structures in the cell. - Fruit flies have unusually thick chromosomes, called giant chromosomes . A giant chromosome looks like a long, striped thread — much easier to see than most chromosomes."],[24,"how-many-chromosomes","How many chromosomes?","- Different species have different numbers of chromosomes in the nuclei of their cells. Species Chromosomes in each cell human 46 fruit fly 8 mango tree 40 - Scientists number the chromosomes by length . In a human cell, the longest chromosome is chromosome 1 , the next longest is chromosome 2, and so on. - We have two of each kind of chromosome. So the 46 human chromosomes are really two sets of 23 . - In a photograph of a dividing cell, each chromosome looks like a cross shape . That is because, just before a cell divides , each chromosome makes a copy of itself , and the two copies stay joined together part way along their length . Careful: a photograph that shows the chromosomes neatly lined up in size order has been made by cutting and pasting the pictures. Chromosomes do not line themselves up like that inside a real cell."],[24,"genes","Genes","- Each chromosome is made up of hundreds of different genes . - The genes are arranged in a particular sequence along the chromosome — each gene has its own place. - Each gene helps to control a particular characteristic of the organism. - Longer chromosomes carry more genes: chromosome 1 has about 2000 genes, while the much shorter chromosome 15 has about 600 . Different versions of the same gene. Two genes on chromosome 15 help to determine eye colour . Everyone has eye-colour genes in the same place on their chromosome 15s — but there are different versions of those genes. One person's version gives blue eyes, another's gives brown eyes. That is why two sisters can have different eye colours."],[24,"dna","DNA","- Chromosomes are made of a chemical substance called DNA . Each chromosome is one enormously long molecule of DNA . - Because genes are parts of chromosomes, genes are also made of DNA . - A DNA molecule has the shape of a twisted ladder . This shape is called a double helix . - One gene could be a length of DNA with about 2500 of these twists . We cannot see the twists with a microscope, because DNA molecules are much too small to see . - DNA was first discovered in the 1950s . - The DNA in a cell determines what the cell does . It contains a complete set of instructions to make a working cell — and a whole organism."],[24,"getting-the-three-words-in-the-right-order","Getting the three words in the right order","- Say it as one sentence: a chromosome is a long thread of DNA, and a gene is a part of that thread that controls one characteristic. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.1</b </summary 1 Explain why we cannot usually see the chromosomes in a cell, even using a microscope. Chromosomes do not usually become visible until just before a cell divides , so unless the cell you are looking at is about to divide you will not see them. They also need to be coloured with a stain first. 2 Red blood cells do not contain a nucleus. Do you think they contain any chromosomes? Explain your answer. No. Chromosomes are only found in a nucleus , and a mature red blood cell has no nucleus. (Red blood cells do have a nucleus when they are first made, but they lose it as they fill with haemoglobin.) 3 Explain the difference between a chromosome and a gene. A chromosome is a long thread of DNA . A gene is a part of a chromosome that helps to control one particular characteristic . Each chromosome contains many genes. 4 All fruit flies have a gene for wing shape in the same place on chromosome 2, but some have normal wings and some have tiny wings. What can explain this? The flies have different versions of the same gene . The DNA in the different versions is slightly different , so the characteristic it produces is different. </details"],[24,"7-2-gametes-and-inheritance","7.2 Gametes and inheritance","In this topic you will: learn what a gamete is · compare male and female gametes · find out what happens at fertilisation · explain what determines whether a baby is a boy or a girl Key words: egg cell · fertilisation · gametes · inheritance · sex inheritance · sperm cell · zygote"],[24,"gametes","Gametes","- Every cell in a human body contains 46 chromosomes . Where did they come from? Think about how a new human life begins. - Every human being began life as a single cell , formed when a sperm cell joined with an egg cell . - Sperm cells and egg cells are specialised cells known as gametes . - a sperm cell is the male gamete - an egg cell is the female gamete . - The sperm cell has a tail, which produces swimming movements , a nucleus containing 23 chromosomes , a cell surface membrane , and only a small amount of cytoplasm . - Sperm cells are very small and very active , using their tails to swim vigorously . - The egg cell has a cell surface membrane , a nucleus containing 23 chromosomes , and cytoplasm containing food reserves . - An egg cell is much bigger than a sperm cell — about the same size as a full stop — but still very small. It is larger because it carries food reserves . - An egg cell cannot move by itself . The photographs in the book were taken with a scanning electron microscope — a special kind of very powerful microscope. The labelled drawings next to them are diagrams , not photographs."],[24,"sperm-cell-and-egg-cell-compared","Sperm cell and egg cell compared","Sperm cell Egg cell male or female male gamete female gamete size smaller larger movement has a tail and can swim no tail , cannot move by itself food reserves very little cytoplasm, few food reserves cytoplasm containing food reserves chromosomes 23 23 sex chromosome carried either an X or a Y always an X - Both are gametes, both have a nucleus , a cell surface membrane and cytoplasm , and both carry 23 chromosomes ."],[24,"fertilisation","Fertilisation","- A body cell has 46 chromosomes — two sets of 23 . A gamete has only one set , so it has 23 . - Fertilisation is the joining of a sperm cell with an egg cell . - First the head of the sperm cell enters the egg cell . Then the nucleus of the sperm cell and the nucleus of the egg cell fuse together . - 23 chromosomes from the sperm + 23 chromosomes from the egg = 46 chromosomes , two complete sets again. - The new cell formed when the two nuclei join is called a zygote . - That single cell then divides over and over again , over days, weeks and years, eventually producing all of the millions of cells in a human body . This is why gametes have half the number. If gametes had 46 each, the zygote would have 92, and the number would double every generation. Halving it in the gametes keeps the number of chromosomes the same in every generation ."],[24,"boy-or-girl","Boy or girl?","- All the chromosomes in a person's body came from their mother and father — one set from each . - One pair of chromosomes are the sex chromosomes . They determine whether a person is male or female . - XX = female - XY = male - A Y chromosome is much smaller than an X chromosome . - All egg cells contain one X chromosome. A gamete only has one of each kind of chromosome, and a woman's cells only contain X. - Sperm cells can have either one X chromosome or one Y chromosome — half carry X and half carry Y. - So at each fertilisation: - X sperm + X egg → XX zygote → a girl - Y sperm + X egg → XY zygote → a boy - The chance of each of these is about equal , which is why approximately equal numbers of boys and girls are born . The question people get wrong: the father's sperm decides the sex of the baby , because only the sperm can carry a Y. The mother's egg always supplies an X, whatever the baby turns out to be."],[24,"inheritance","Inheritance","- Inheritance means passing on DNA — as chromosomes containing genes — from parents to offspring . - Sex inheritance is the name for the part of this that decides a baby's sex, because a baby inherits X or Y chromosomes from its parents . - All organisms also inherit many other features from their parents. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.2</b </summary 1 What is a gamete? A sex cell — one of the two cells that fuse together at fertilisation to begin a new life. 2 Compare a sperm cell and an egg cell. Sperm: male gamete · smaller · has a tail and can swim · few food reserves. Egg: female gamete · larger · no tail and cannot move · contains food reserves. Both have 23 chromosomes. 3 Is it correct to say that the sex of a baby is determined by its father, not its mother? Yes. The sex is determined by the sperm cell , which can carry either an X or a Y chromosome. Every egg carries an X. 4 Explain why the numbers of boy and girl babies born each year is approximately equal. There are equal numbers of X-carrying and Y-carrying sperm cells . An X sperm gives an XX zygote (a girl); a Y sperm gives an XY zygote (a boy). The chances of the two events are equal , so the numbers born are about equal. Activity 7.2.1 Modelling sex inheritance — Question 1 The results should show that there is an equal chance of a baby being a boy or a girl. </details"],[24,"7-3-variation","7.3 Variation","In this topic you will: learn what is meant by variation · think about how genes help to produce variation · investigate and record some examples of variation Key words: genetic differences · variation"],[24,"variation-within-a-species","Variation within a species","- Organisms of different species usually look very different: horses look different from donkeys, lions look different from tigers. - But individuals of the same species are also different from one another. Everyone in your class belongs to the same species, and you are all different. - Variation = the differences between individuals belonging to the same species . Note the wording carefully. Variation is about differences within one species , not differences between species."],[24,"recording-variation-tally-table-bar-chart","Recording variation: tally, table, bar chart","Zara studied kidney vetch , a plant whose flowers can be any one of five colours, and counted the plants of each colour in a small area of a field. Colour Red Pink Orange Yellow Cream Tally \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ Number 5 2 3 9 3 - For each plant she found , Zara put one stroke in the Tally row. At the end she added up the strokes and wrote the total in the Number row. - Then she drew a bar chart of the results. - The colour of flowers goes on the horizontal axis — it is the thing she chose to sort by, and the categories are separate names , not numbers. - The number of plants goes on the vertical axis — it is what she counted. - The bars in a bar chart for separate categories have spaces between them . (When the categories are number ranges , such as wrist circumference, the bars touch , and the graph is called a frequency diagram or histogram.) - Total number of plants: 5 + 2 + 3 + 9 + 3 = 22 . Most common colour: yellow ."],[24,"dna-and-variation","DNA and variation","Why are the individuals of one species different? Follow the DNA: - Take the goats in the book. All goat gametes contain genes that affect the development of horns . There are several versions of these genes: one version might give long horns, another short horns, another no horns at all ; other genes decide curved or straight horns. - The same is true of genes for height , coat colour and tail shape . - Each gene is different because its DNA is different. - Genetic differences = differences in the DNA of organisms within a species . 'Genetic' means 'to do with genes'. - In a family photograph of three generations, the similarities you can see exist because some of their DNA is the same ; everyone still has a slightly different combination , so there are genetic differences between them."],[24,"environment-and-variation","Environment and variation","- DNA is not the only cause of variation. An organism's environment also affects it. - Coat colour in the goats is determined by DNA alone . - Body mass is different: a goat's size is affected by its DNA and by how much it eats. A goat that eats more grows fatter — and that difference is not caused by its genes . - So: not all variation within a species is caused by differences in DNA. Feature What causes the variation eye colour genes only natural hair colour mostly genes — but hair dye and other products can change it shoe size mostly genes , partly environment (food while growing) height partly genes , partly environment (diet during the growing years) body mass of a goat genes and environment"],[24,"median-mode-and-mean","Median, mode and mean","When you collect a set of numbers — leaflets on a leaf, beans in a pod — three different averages are useful: Word What it means How to find it median the middle value put the values in order, take the middle one mode the most common value the value that appears most often mean the average add up all the values and divide by how many values there are <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.3</b </summary 1 These goats all belong to the same species. What similarities are there between them? For example, they all have four legs and two ears . 2 The goats show variation. What differences can you see? Size , age , coat colour , whether they have horns , and the size of the horns . 3 How many plants did Zara find? 5 + 2 + 3 + 9 + 3 = 22 . 4 Which was the most common flower colour? Yellow. 5 Table or bar chart — which shows the results more clearly? Either answer is acceptable as long as you give a reason . A bar chart makes the comparison of sizes obvious at a glance; a table gives you the exact numbers . 6 For each feature you investigated, are the differences caused by genes only, environment only, or genes and environment? For the sample features: eye colour — genes alone ; natural hair colour — mostly genes, but hair products can change it ; shoe size — mostly genes, partly environment ; height — partly genes and partly environment such as diet during the growing years . Think like a scientist: leaves — Question 4. Did you find differences between leaves on the same tree? Could genes have caused them? All the leaves on the same tree have the same genes , so any differences between them cannot be caused by genes . They must be caused by the environment. </details"],[24,"7-4-natural-selection","7.4 Natural selection","In this topic you will: learn about the scientific theory of natural selection · find out about some examples of natural selection in action Key words: advantageous feature · natural selection · resistant"],[24,"an-imaginary-story","An imaginary story","A long time ago, a species of herbivore lived on the grassy plains of Africa and ate leaves from trees. - As in all species , there was genetic variation among them: some had versions of genes that gave them long necks , some had versions that gave them shorter necks . - In some years the rains did not come and the animals ran short of food . Once the lower leaves had been eaten, only the long-necked animals could reach food . - Most of the short-necked animals died of starvation. - When the survivors reproduced they passed on their genes . Because only the long-necked animals had survived, only their genes passed to the next generation , so most of the offspring grew up with long necks . This story is imaginary . It describes how giraffes might have come to have long necks — we do not know that this is what happened, and we can never really know. What the theory does do is explain how almost every species comes to be so well adapted to its environment ."],[24,"the-theory-of-natural-selection-the-five-steps","The theory of natural selection — the five steps","- This process is called natural selection . - An advantageous feature is one that makes an individual more likely to survive — and therefore more likely to reproduce. - Natural selection causes genetic changes over time : some versions of genes become more common , others become less common . Say it the right way round. Natural selection does not give an individual the feature it needs. The variation is already there , caused by genes; natural selection only decides which individuals survive to pass their genes on ."],[24,"bacteria-and-antibiotics","Bacteria and antibiotics","- Antibiotics are medicinal drugs that cure diseases caused by bacteria . - Doctors are finding that some antibiotics do not work any more : the bacteria have become resistant to them. - Resistant means not killed by the antibiotic . - Stage 1 — In a population of bacteria, not every one is alike . By chance , one may have a gene that makes it resistant to an antibiotic. - Stage 2 — The antibiotic is added , and it kills the bacteria that are not resistant . - Stage 3 — The resistant one can now multiply and form a population of resistant bacteria just like itself . Why doctors say to save antibiotics for serious illness. Every time an antibiotic is used, it gives an advantage to the resistant bacteria , so the gene for resistance becomes more common. If the antibiotic is not used, resistant bacteria have no advantage , and natural selection does not build a resistant population. Using antibiotics less often keeps them working for when they are really needed ."],[24,"peppered-moths","Peppered moths","- Peppered moths live in England . Most have pale wings , but some have a gene that gives them dark wings . - They fly at night and rest on tree trunks during the day , where they are hunted and eaten by birds . - Pale moths are camouflaged perfectly against lichen-covered bark . ( Lichen is a plant-like organism that grows on rocks and trees.) What happened, and when: Time Tree trunks Most common moth up to 1849 pale, covered in lichen pale by 1900 , near English cities dark — the Industrial Revolution burnt coal, the smoke killed the lichens and blackened the bark dark today pollution is much lower, lichens grow again pale - Experiments show that birds see pale moths more easily on dark trunks , and dark moths more easily on lichen-covered trunks . - So when the trunks went dark, the pale moths lost their camouflage and were more likely to be eaten . The dark moths were more likely to survive and reproduce , and passed on their genes for dark colour . Over several generations the dark form became more and more common . - The moths are a real example of the five steps: variation existed before the pollution; the environment changed; the environment decided which variety survived . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 7.4</b </summary 1 Explain what we mean when we say that bacteria have become resistant to an antibiotic. The bacteria are not killed by the antibiotic . 2 Explain how the development of resistance in bacteria can be explained in terms of genetic changes over time. When an antibiotic is used, bacteria with versions of genes that make them resistant are more likely to survive and reproduce . Over many generations that version of the gene becomes more common , until whole populations of bacteria are not killed by the antibiotic . 3 Suggest why doctors advise that we should not use antibiotics unless we really need them. Whenever an antibiotic is used we give an advantage to the bacteria that are resistant to it , so the gene for resistance becomes more common. If the antibiotic is not used there is no advantage for the resistant bacteria , so natural selection does not produce a resistant population. Using antibiotics sparingly helps to stop resistance developing , so the antibiotic still works when we really need it for a serious infection. 4 Use the five-step list to explain how dark peppered moths became more common in the late nineteenth century. - Step 1 Most peppered moths were pale, but some were dark — there was variation . - Step 2 The difference in colour was caused by having different versions of the gene that determines wing colour . - Step 3 Before the Industrial Revolution, pale moths were most likely to survive because they were better camouflaged from birds. After it, the lichens were killed or covered in soot , so the dark moths were most likely to survive. - Step 4 The dark moths were therefore more likely to reproduce and passed on the gene for dark colour . - Step 5 Over several generations the gene for dark colour became more common and the gene for pale colour became less common, until most moths near the cities were dark. Getting started: how is the waxy monkey leaf frog adapted to survive? Any reasonable feature with a reason — for example, the colour of its skin camouflages it , so it can hide from predators. </details <details <summary <b Check your progress — Chapter 7</b </summary 1 Copy and complete. The nucleus of every cell contains several long threads, called chromosomes . In most cells, there are two copies of each thread. Each thread contains many genes . The threads are made of a chemical called DNA . 2 Egg cells and sperm cells — tick the correct boxes. Description Egg cells Sperm cells contain a nucleus ✔ ✔ are gametes ✔ ✔ can swim ✔ in humans, contain 23 chromosomes ✔ ✔ contain either an X chromosome or a Y chromosome ✔ 3 Arun counted the beans in 20 pods: 7, 3, 8, 6, 3, 4, 7, 5, 5, 8, 6, 4, 6, 7, 5, 5, 6, 5, 4, 8. a Mean number of beans in a pod. Total = 112. 112 ÷ 20 = 5.6 beans per pod . b Tally table. Number of beans in a pod 3 4 5 6 7 8 Tally \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ Number of pods 2 3 5 4 3 3 c Drawing the frequency diagram. Put number of beans in a pod on the horizontal axis and number of pods on the vertical axis, choose a suitable scale on each axis , and draw touching bars , because the categories are numbers. 4 Penicillin-resistant pneumonia. a What is an antibiotic? A medicinal drug that cures a disease caused by bacteria . b Describe the trend shown in the graph. The percentage of cases that are resistant to penicillin increases over the years shown. c Suggest how natural selection has caused this trend. There is variation among the bacteria, and by chance some carry a gene for resistance . When penicillin is used, the non-resistant bacteria are killed but the resistant ones survive and reproduce , passing the gene on. Over many generations the gene for resistance becomes more common , so a larger and larger percentage of cases is resistant. </details"],[25,"8-1-measuring-the-rate-of-reaction","8.1 Measuring the rate of reaction","In this topic you will: learn how the rate of reaction can change · measure the rate of reaction · use graphs to discuss and measure the rate of reaction · explain why the rate of reaction changes Key words: anomalous result · collecting a gas over water · collisions · gradient · rate of reaction"],[25,"what-rate-of-reaction-means","What \"rate of reaction\" means","- When magnesium ribbon is added to dilute sulfuric acid , you can tell a reaction is happening because bubbles of hydrogen gas are given off. - Watch the bubbles carefully and you see a pattern: a lot of bubbles quickly at the start , fewer as the reaction goes on, and eventually none at all . - So the reaction started quickly, slowed down, and then stopped . How quickly a reaction goes is called the rate of reaction . The definition to learn. The rate of reaction can be measured by working out how much of one of the products has been made in a given time , or how much of a reactant has been used up in a given time . The equations for this reaction: word equation magnesium + sulfuric acid → magnesium sulfate + hydrogen symbol equation Mg + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂"],[25,"choosing-what-to-measure","Choosing what to measure","- In the magnesium experiment it is difficult to measure how quickly the reactants are used up, and difficult to measure how quickly the magnesium sulfate is formed. - The easiest thing to measure is how quickly the hydrogen gas is produced — that is, the volume of gas produced in a particular length of time . - To catch the gas, attach a syringe to the top of the flask so that no hydrogen can escape , then read the scale on the syringe at different times. Three methods you can choose between Method What you actually measure Notes gas syringe volume of gas, from the scale on the syringe the flask must be sealed so no gas escapes top pan balance the falling mass of the flask as gas escapes only works if the gas is allowed to leave collecting a gas over water volume of gas pushed into a measuring cylinder full of water uses a trough, a bee-hive shelf and a delivery tube - The top pan balance method works with the reaction between calcium carbonate and dilute hydrochloric acid . As carbon dioxide is lost from the flask, the mass of the flask decreases — quickly at first, then more and more slowly ."],[25,"anomalous-results","Anomalous results","- A result that does not fit the pattern made by the others is called an anomalous result . - A graph makes an anomalous result easy to spot, because the point sits off the line that the other points make. - What to do about one: repeat the experiment . If you keep getting the same odd value, the pattern really does change there; if the repeat fits the line, the original reading was an error ."],[25,"reading-a-rate-off-a-graph","Reading a rate off a graph","- The slope , or gradient , of the line tells you how quickly the reaction is taking place . The steeper the slope, the faster the reaction. - The line is steepest at the start — that is when the reaction is fastest . - As the line becomes less steep , the reaction is slowing down . - When the line levels out , no more product is being formed: the reaction has finished . Working out an average rate between two times. On a graph of volume of carbon dioxide against time: - Do the same between 60 s and 80 s on that graph and you get 6 cm³ in 20 s = 0.3 cm³ per second — less than a third of the earlier rate. The reaction has slowed right down."],[25,"why-the-rate-changes","Why the rate changes","- For a chemical reaction to happen, the particles of the reactants have to collide with one another with enough energy to react . - At the start there are a lot of unreacted particles , so collisions happen frequently and a lot of product is made in the first 30 seconds. - As the reaction goes on , the number of unreacted particles gets lower and lower . The chance of two unreacted particles colliding decreases , so less product forms in each later 30-second period — the rate is slower . - Eventually all the particles have reacted, there are no more useful collisions, and the reaction has finished . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.1</b </summary 1 Write the word and symbol equations for magnesium with sulfuric acid. magnesium + sulfuric acid → magnesium sulfate + hydrogen; Mg + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂ 2 How can you tell from the table that the reaction has finished at 270 seconds? Because there is no further increase in the volume of gas after 270 s — the readings at 270, 300 and 330 s are all 66 cm³. 3 What might happen if more than 100 cm³ of hydrogen were made, and how could you reduce the risk? The syringe plunger might shoot out of the barrel and break or hurt someone. Watch the experiment carefully and open the flask if the volume gets close to 100 cm³, or tie the plunger to the barrel with string. 5 Which result is anomalous? The reading at 90 seconds . It does not fit the pattern made by the rest of the points. 6 What would you do about an anomalous result? Repeat the experiment. If the odd value comes back, the pattern really does change; if the repeat fits the line, the first reading was an error. 7 Word equation for copper carbonate with hydrochloric acid. copper carbonate + hydrochloric acid → copper chloride + water + carbon dioxide 8 Average rate between 60 s and 80 s. 6 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 0.3 cm³ per second — less than a third of the rate between 10 s and 30 s. </details"],[25,"8-2-surface-area-and-the-rate-of-reaction","8.2 Surface area and the rate of reaction","In this topic you will: investigate the rate of reaction when the surface area of one of the reactants is changed · carry out an investigation using calcium carbonate and dilute hydrochloric acid · consider how changing the shape of a material affects its surface area Key words: surface area"],[25,"the-magnesium-clue","The magnesium clue","- Put magnesium ribbon in a Bunsen flame and it reacts very quickly, burning with a bright white flame to form magnesium oxide . - Put a large piece of magnesium in the same flame and it does not burn . - Put magnesium powder in the flame and it burns much faster than the ribbon . Same metal, same flame, three different rates. The difference is surface area . - Only the magnesium atoms on the surface can make contact with the oxygen in the air and react with it. - In a block , most of the atoms are inside , away from the oxygen, so they cannot react. - In ribbon , more of the atoms are on the surface , so more of them can react. Ribbon has a larger total surface area than a block of the same mass . - Powder of the same mass has a larger total surface area still , so it has the most atoms available to react and the reaction is quickest of all . The rule: small pieces of a solid always react faster than larger pieces of the same mass. Every time you cut a solid into smaller pieces you increase the total surface area — slices of bread have a greater total surface area than the whole loaf."],[25,"the-cube-activity-where-the-numbers-come-from","The cube activity — where the numbers come from","Take 27 identical small cubes . Call the area of one face of one small cube a . Arrangement Total surface area Why stacked into one big 3 × 3 × 3 cube 54 × a each face of the big cube is 9 small faces, and there are 6 faces: 6 × 9a spread into a 3 × 9 slab 78 × a more of the small faces are now on the outside all 27 cubes separated 162 × a every cube shows all 6 of its faces: 27 × 6a - The mass has not changed at all — only the shape . Yet the surface area more than triples . - If those cubes were zinc reacting with acid , the rate of reaction would increase at each step , because more atoms are in contact with the acid."],[25,"burning-iron-the-same-idea-with-a-different-metal","Burning iron — the same idea with a different metal","Form of iron What you see iron nail just glows as it gets hot iron wool burns , and pieces fly off iron filings burn brightly Increasing the total surface area increases the rate of reaction , because more iron atoms are in contact with the oxygen in the air."],[25,"the-marble-chip-investigation","The marble chip investigation","You compare large and small marble chips (calcium carbonate) reacting with hydrochloric acid, using any of the three methods from topic 8.1. independent variable the size of the marble chips — and so their total surface area dependent variable the time taken for the reaction (or the volume of gas at each time) kept the same the mass of calcium carbonate, and the volume, type and concentration of the acid - Those control variables matter because each of them would change the rate on its own . The whole point is to let only surface area vary. - Result: the smaller chips give the steeper line and react more quickly . As total surface area increases, the rate of reaction increases. - Powdered calcium carbonate would be faster still . Careful — the trap. A faster reaction does not make more product. With the same mass of calcium carbonate and the same acid, both experiments end up producing the same volume of carbon dioxide ; the small chips just get there sooner . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.2</b </summary Burning iron 2 What effect does increasing the total surface area have on the rate of reaction? It increases the rate of reaction. Burning iron 3 Explain why. For a reaction, iron atoms must collide with oxygen atoms. The larger the surface area, the more iron atoms are in contact with the oxygen , so the faster the reaction. Cubes 6 and 8 How would each change of shape affect the rate? Each rearrangement gives a larger total surface area , so the rate of reaction would be greater . Cubes 9 Explain why a change in surface area changes the rate. The greater the surface area, the more atoms are in contact with the other reactant , so the reaction is faster. Marble chips 3 Why keep the volume, type and concentration of acid the same? Because each of those factors could affect the rate on its own. Surface area is the only variable that should change. Marble chips 4 Dependent and independent variables? Dependent: the time taken for the reaction. Independent: the size (surface area) of the marble chips. Marble chips 9 What happens to the rate as total surface area increases? The rate of reaction increases . </details"],[25,"8-3-temperature-and-the-rate-of-reaction","8.3 Temperature and the rate of reaction","In this topic you will: investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of reaction · do a trial run and preliminary work and use them to improve the method · select equipment · plot a graph of results"],[25,"a-reaction-that-is-easy-to-time","A reaction that is easy to time","- Mix hydrochloric acid with a solution of sodium thiosulfate (a salt) and the mixture becomes cloudy . - The cloudiness is sulfur . Sulfur is insoluble in water , so it forms a precipitate . - That precipitate is opaque — so when you can no longer see through the solution , you know the sulfur has formed. - This makes the reaction perfect for rate work: you simply time how long it takes for a black cross under the tube to disappear . Safety. Sulfur dioxide gas is produced, so the room must be well ventilated . Used solutions go into a container of solid sodium hydrogen carbonate , which reacts with the sulfur dioxide, for the teacher to remove later."],[25,"trial-run-preliminary-work-investigation","Trial run, preliminary work, investigation","Stage What it is for trial run a practice experiment to check you can do it safely and effectively , and to find the problems before they cost you data preliminary work deciding which temperatures to use — how big an interval (5 °C? 10 °C? 20 °C?), how wide a range, whether to start at room temperature the investigation the real thing, planned using what the first two stages taught you - To change the temperature, warm the sodium thiosulfate in a water bath before adding the acid , keeping a stopper in the tube while it warms."],[25,"what-the-results-look-like","What the results look like","- The 40 °C line is steeper , so that reaction is faster . The steeper line always shows the faster reaction. - Both lines level off at the same height : you do not get any more carbon dioxide at the higher temperature. You get the same volume of gas, in less time. - At 50 °C the line would be steeper still , levelling off at the same volume."],[25,"explaining-it-with-particles","Explaining it with particles","- Particles are moving all the time . - When the temperature is increased , the particles move faster . So they collide more often , and with more energy . - More collisions, and more of them energetic enough to react, means the higher the temperature, the faster the rate of reaction . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.3</b </summary 1 How can you tell which line shows the faster reaction? The steeper line shows the faster reaction. 2 What would the line look like at 50 °C? Steeper than the 40 °C line — but it would level off at the same volume of gas . 10 How did you make sure the investigation was a fair test? By using the same volume of sodium thiosulfate and the same volume and type of acid every time. (Repeating readings makes results reliable , not fair — do not confuse the two.) 12 Describe the relationship between temperature and rate. The higher the temperature, the greater the rate of reaction . </details"],[25,"8-4-concentration-and-the-rate-of-reaction","8.4 Concentration and the rate of reaction","In this topic you will: investigate the effect of concentration on the rate of reaction · plan an investigation · explain the effect using particle theory · learn about another way to speed up a reaction, using a catalyst Key words: biological catalysts · catalase · catalyst · decompose · enzymes"],[25,"the-investigation","The investigation","Here the measurement is turned around: instead of reading the volume every 30 seconds, you time how long it takes to collect 25 cm³ of carbon dioxide in the measuring cylinder. Making up the five concentrations. Dilute the acid you are given, making 50 cm³ each time : Solution Acid / cm³ Water / cm³ A 10 40 least concentrated B 20 30 C 30 20 D 40 10 E 50 0 most concentrated - Fair test: same mass of marble chips , same volume of acid , same temperature each time. - Reliable results: repeat the readings. Repeating is about reliability, not fairness."],[25,"what-the-results-look-like","What the results look like","- The high concentration line is steeper , so that reaction is faster . - Again both lines level off at the same volume : you do not get any more carbon dioxide from the more concentrated acid. You get the same volume of gas, but more quickly. - With an even less concentrated acid the line would be less steep , but it would still level off at the same volume ."],[25,"explaining-it-with-particles","Explaining it with particles","- The higher the concentration of hydrochloric acid, the more acid particles there are in a given amount of space . - So there will be more frequent collisions between hydrochloric acid particles and calcium carbonate particles — and a faster reaction."],[25,"catalysts","Catalysts","Manufacturers want products made quickly and cheaply . They can raise the temperature , the concentration , or the surface area — but each costs money: Way to speed it up The cost increase the temperature energy is expensive increase the concentration more concentrated reactants are expensive increase the surface area crushing solids adds to the cost A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a reaction , is unchanged by the reaction , and can be used over and over again . Catalysts are used in industry because a catalyst is usually much cheaper than heating the reaction . - Remember: speeding a reaction up with any of these does not make any more product . It just makes it more quickly . - Many cars have catalytic converters in the exhaust. The catalysts inside speed up reactions that get rid of harmful gases produced in the engine. The hydrogen peroxide demonstration - Hydrogen peroxide is a colourless liquid that decomposes to water and oxygen. You can tell it is happening because you see bubbles of oxygen . - Left on its own, it breaks down slowly . - Add manganese oxide powder and the breakdown happens much faster . At the end the manganese oxide is still there , can be recovered (it is insoluble in water ), and works again on a fresh tube — which is exactly what makes it a catalyst. - A tube of hydrogen peroxide with nothing added is kept as the control , for comparison."],[25,"biological-catalysts","Biological catalysts","- Reactions inside the cells of living things are sped up by biological catalysts called enzymes . - Enzymes speed up most of the reactions in your body — for example the breakdown of food in your digestive system , so that the small molecules can be absorbed into your blood . - Hydrogen peroxide is made as a waste product in many reactions in plant and animal cells. It is poisonous : if it is not broken down quickly into harmless water and oxygen , it will kill living cells . - So all living cells contain an enzyme called catalase , which speeds up that breakdown. That is why the fruit, vegetables and meat in the experiment made the hydrogen peroxide fizz so quickly. - Biological washing powders, liquids and gels contain enzymes that help break down stains such as food and blood . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 8.4</b </summary 1 How can you tell which line shows the faster reaction? The steeper line. 2 What would the line be like with an even less concentrated acid? Less steep — but it would level off at the same volume . 2 (investigation) How did you ensure this was a fair test? Same mass of marble chips , same volume of acid , same temperature . Repeating the experiment is not part of fairness — it improves reliability . Catalyst task 2 Did the manganese oxide work a second time after being recovered? Yes. A catalyst is unchanged by the reaction, so the recovered manganese oxide speeds up a fresh tube of hydrogen peroxide just as well. 3 What is another name for a biological catalyst? An enzyme . 4 What sort of washing substance has a catalyst in it? A biological washing powder, liquid or gel. 5 Why do cars have catalytic converters fitted? The catalyst inside speeds up reactions that remove harmful gases made in the engine. </details <details <summary <b Check your progress — Chapter 8</b </summary 1 Equal masses of marble lumps, small marble chips and powdered marble in equal volumes of dilute hydrochloric acid. - a The reaction is fastest with the powder . - b The powder has the largest total surface area , so more calcium carbonate particles are in contact with the acid and collisions between acid particles and calcium carbonate particles are more frequent . - c Bubble the gas through limewater . If it is carbon dioxide, the limewater turns milky (cloudy white) . - d The salt formed is calcium chloride . 2 Magnesium ribbon in dilute sulfuric acid took 45 seconds to disappear. - a magnesium + sulfuric acid → magnesium sulfate + hydrogen - b Hydrogen is tested with a lighted splint : a positive result is a squeaky pop . - c Faster than 45 s: warming the acid and stirring the mixture . Using 2 g of magnesium does not speed the reaction up, and adding water to the acid makes it more dilute , so slower. 3 Reading a rate graph. - c The reaction is fastest at the start (steepest part of the line), then slows down as the line becomes less steep, and has finished where the line becomes horizontal. - d Increasing the temperature would increase the rate of reaction . - e The particles would move faster , so they would collide more often and with more energy , so more collisions lead to a reaction. </details"],[26,"9-1-parallel-circuits","9.1 Parallel circuits","In this topic you will: find out the difference between series and parallel circuits · find out how current flows in a parallel circuit Key words: branches · connected in parallel · connected in series · parallel circuit"],[26,"series-circuits","Series circuits","- Series means all the components are connected end-to-end , one after the other. We say the components are connected in series . - In a series circuit there is only one path for the current to flow. That is why the current is the same all the way around a series circuit . - If the switch in a series circuit is opened , every component stops working at the same time , because the current in the whole circuit stops. - So if you want to operate a lamp and a buzzer separately from the same cell, a series circuit is no good — you need a parallel circuit ."],[26,"parallel-circuits","Parallel circuits","- In a parallel circuit there is more than one path for the current to flow. The separate paths are called branches . - The name parallel comes from the circuit diagram, because the branches are drawn parallel to each other . Components in the branches are said to be connected in parallel . - Current from the cell flows to the branch, and at the branch the current is divided . If the two lamps are the same, the current is divided equally between them. - Where the branches join again, the current combines (adds together) again . The rule for parallel circuits. The current through the cell is equal to the total of the current in all the branches . - In that circuit the readings on A1 and A4 are equal — both are outside the branches, so both measure the current through the cell. - The readings on A2 and A3 add up to the reading on A1 (and A4) . - The rule still works when the branches carry different currents. - Here 1.0 A + 2.0 A + 0.5 A = 3.5 A , which is the current through the cell."],[26,"finding-a-missing-current","Finding a missing current","If you know the current through the cell but not the current in one branch, you can calculate the missing one. The total current is 5.5 A, so the branch currents must add up to 5.5 A. Call the missing current X : So the missing current through the buzzer is 1.0 A . As with all calculations, do not forget the unit ."],[26,"why-parallel-circuits-are-useful","Why parallel circuits are useful","In a parallel circuit the current in one branch can keep flowing even if the current stops in the other branches . This means: - components in the same circuit can be switched on and off independently - if a component in one branch stops working , the other branches are not affected . - Closing S1 lights L1 . This does not affect L2 , because L2 is on a different branch. - Closing S2 lights L2 . This does not affect L1 , for the same reason. - If both lamps are on and L1 stops working , L2 is not affected . Putting a switch between the cell and the branches lets you control everything at once. - For any lamp to light, S4 must be closed . S1, S2 and S3 then control each lamp separately. - If all the lamps are on, opening S4 turns them all off . - If the lamps are off but S1, S2 and S3 are closed, closing S4 lights them all together . Everyday examples. Car headlamps are connected in parallel so they can be switched on and off together. A string of lamps is connected in parallel so that, if one lamp fails, the others still light. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 9.1</b </summary 1. Which of these describes the current through the cell in a parallel circuit? C — the current through the cell is the total of the current in each branch. 2. Four identical lamps in parallel, current through the cell 2.0 A → current through each lamp = 2.0 ÷ 4 = 0.5 A . 3. Two advantages of parallel over series for four lamps: the lamps can be switched on and off independently , and if one lamp fails the others still light . </details Summary checklist — 9.1. I know how current divides in a parallel circuit · how to calculate missing current values · how to measure current in a parallel circuit · the advantages of parallel circuits over series circuits."],[26,"9-2-current-and-voltage-in-parallel-circuits","9.2 Current and voltage in parallel circuits","In this topic you will: compare current and voltage in series and parallel circuits · describe the effects of adding cells and lamps on current and voltage in circuits Key words: rating · voltage · voltmeter · volts"],[26,"what-is-voltage","What is voltage?","- Voltage is a measure of the electrical energy in a circuit. It is measured in volts , symbol V . - Voltage can measure the electrical energy supplied to a circuit by the cell, battery or power supply . - Most cells supply 1.5 V . A battery is two or more cells connected in series ; batteries commonly supply 6 V, 9 V or 12 V . - The sockets on the walls of buildings supply a mains voltage . Mains here means a supply that comes from a power station or generator . In most countries the mains voltage is between 220 and 240 V ; in some countries it is 110 V . - We often call the source of energy in a circuit the supply — a cell, a battery, a power supply or the mains. - Voltage also measures the energy changed by components . A lamp changes electrical energy into light and thermal energy , and that energy can be measured in volts. - Most components have a voltage rating . School lamps are often rated at 3 V or 6 V . The rating tells you the maximum voltage that can be used."],[26,"measuring-voltage","Measuring voltage","Meter What it measures How it is connected ammeter the current flowing through a component in series with the component voltmeter the energy difference either side of a component in parallel with the component - A voltmeter is connected differently from an ammeter, and that difference follows from what each one measures."],[26,"voltage-in-a-series-circuit","Voltage in a series circuit","- Energy is always conserved , so the energy changed by the components must equal the energy supplied by the cell, battery or power supply. - Therefore the voltages across each component in a series circuit add up to the voltage of the supply . - All three lamps are identical , so they change the same quantity of energy and have the same voltage : 4 V + 4 V + 4 V = 12 V. - When the components are not the same — say a lamp and a buzzer on a 9 V battery — the lamp changes more energy , so the voltage across the lamp (6 V) is higher than across the buzzer (3 V). They still add up to 9 V . - Current is the same all the way around a series circuit, because there are no branches. Voltage in a series circuit can be different across different components ."],[26,"adding-more-components-in-a-series-circuit","Adding more components in a series circuit","- The supply voltage is shared between the components, so adding components gives each one a smaller share of the voltage . Circuit Supply Number of identical lamps Voltage across each lamp first 12 V 2 6 V second 12 V 6 2 V - Adding more components in series also decreases the current , because as components are added it becomes more difficult for the power supply to push the electrons around the circuit . - Adding more cells in series increases the supply voltage . One 1.5 V cell gives 1.5 V; two give 2 × 1.5 V = 3 V; a 12 V battery contains eight 1.5 V cells (8 × 1.5 V = 12 V). - Increasing the number of cells in a series circuit will increase the current in the circuit and increase the voltage across each component ."],[26,"voltage-in-a-parallel-circuit","Voltage in a parallel circuit","- Lamp 1 is connected directly across the terminals of the 1.5 V cell, so the voltage across lamp 1 is 1.5 V . - Following the wires from lamp 2 , you reach the terminals of the cell without going through lamp 1 . So lamp 2 is also connected directly across the cell, and the voltage across it is also 1.5 V . The rule. The voltages across each of the branches of a parallel circuit are equal to the voltage of the supply . - This is true whether or not the components are the same . With a 9 V battery, a lamp branch and a buzzer branch both have 9 V across them, even though the components are different. - Compare the two rules: current can be different in different branches, because it can flow along different paths; voltage in all branches is the same ."],[26,"adding-more-components-in-a-parallel-circuit","Adding more components in a parallel circuit","- Adding more branches gives more paths for the current, and the more paths there are the easier it becomes, so the current through the cell increases . - Adding more components to one branch decreases the current in that branch . The voltage across the branch stays the same, so extra components just make it harder for current to flow in that branch . - Adding cells to a parallel circuit increases the supply voltage, so it also increases the voltage across each branch , increases the current through the cell , and increases the current through each branch . <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 9.2</b </summary 1. An ammeter measures the current through a component and is connected in series with it. A voltmeter measures the voltage across a component and is connected in parallel with it. 2. Six identical lamps in parallel across a 220 V supply: the voltage across one lamp is 220 V . If the total current is 2.4 A, the current through one lamp is 2.4 ÷ 6 = 0.4 A . Adding two more lamps in new branches increases the total current from the supply. 3. Ten lamps rated 2.0 V with a 12 V battery: they cannot be connected individually in parallel, because each would get the full 12 V , far above its 2.0 V rating. In series , 12 ÷ 2.0 = 6 lamps will operate properly. 4. A 9 V battery with a lamp (2 V) and a buzzer in series: the buzzer has 9 − 2 = 7 V across it. Adding another lamp in series decreases the voltage across L1 and decreases the current in the circuit. </details Summary checklist — 9.2. I know how to measure current and voltage in series and parallel circuits · how adding more cells or lamps affects current and voltage in series circuits · and in parallel circuits."],[26,"9-3-resistance","9.3 Resistance","In this topic you will: discover how resistance affects current in a circuit · calculate resistance from voltage and current Key words: filament · ohms · Ohm's law · resistor · resistance"],[26,"what-resistance-is","What resistance is","- Resistance is a measure of how easy or difficult it is for electrons to move through a material . - Conductors , such as copper , have very low resistance. Insulators , such as most plastics , have very high resistance. - Resistance is measured in ohms , symbol Ω , which is the Greek letter omega . Example Resistance 100 m of copper wire about 0.5–1.0 Ω 1 cm of some plastics over 1 000 000 000 000 Ω - Resistance slows the flow of electrons, so it lowers the current. Copper wire is designed to have very low resistance so that the current is affected as little as possible between the components. - The plastic around a copper wire has such high resistance that it does not allow current to flow at all under most circumstances . - Some conductors are designed to have a high resistance. Inside some lamps there is a small wire called a filament . Current flowing through the high-resistance filament transfers so much thermal energy that the filament glows and emits light . The rule to learn. The greater the resistance in a circuit, the smaller the current in the circuit."],[26,"ohm-s-law","Ohm's law","- Georg Simon Ohm was a scientist who studied resistance. He discovered a link between voltage, current and resistance in wires. The link applies to many electrical components , not only to wires. Ohm's law: voltage = current × resistance Symbol Quantity Unit V voltage volts I current amps R resistance ohms - The letter I is used for current because in French current was originally called intensité du courant . - So Ohm's law is written V = I × R . Worked example. A current of 2 A flows through a buzzer and the voltage across it is 10 V . Calculate the resistance."],[26,"resistors","Resistors","- A resistor is an electrical component designed to have a known resistance . - Many resistors have coloured bands ; the colours form a code that shows the resistance value in ohms. - The value of the resistor is usually written beside its circuit symbol — for example 150 Ω . When the value does not matter, or you have to calculate it, the letter R is written beside the symbol instead. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 9.3</b </summary 1. Copper wires have a very low resistance. That makes it very easy for current to flow in copper wires. A resistor of 10 000 Ω has a high resistance. 2. The unit of resistance is the ohm (Ω) . Ohm's law is voltage = current × resistance . The letter I represents current . 3. R1: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 Ω . R2: 9 ÷ 2 = 4.5 Ω . R3: 1.5 ÷ 0.5 = 3 Ω . 4. (a) V = I × R = 2 × 6 = 12 V . (b) I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 100 = 0.12 A . </details Summary checklist — 9.3. I know how resistance affects current in a circuit · the equation linking voltage, current and resistance · how to use Ohm's law to calculate resistance from voltage and current."],[26,"9-4-practical-circuits","9.4 Practical circuits","In this topic you will: draw circuit diagrams that include symbols for cells, switches, resistors, variable resistors, ammeters, voltmeters, lamps and buzzers · make circuits that include some or all of these components Key words: dimmer · fixed resistor · variable resistor · volume"],[26,"variable-resistors","Variable resistors","- In many circuits it is useful to be able to change the resistance — in a dimmer switch for a lamp, or in the volume control of a music player. - The component used to change resistance is a variable resistor . - Dimmer switches contain variable resistors that can be turned to control the brightness of lamps in a room. - In audio equipment, volume means the loudness from loudspeakers or headphones. The sliding controls on sound recording equipment are variable resistors for adjusting volume. - The circuit symbol for a variable resistor is the same as the one for a fixed resistor, but with an arrow through it . - The term fixed resistor is used for the ordinary resistors of Topic 9.3, to avoid confusion with variable resistors. Controlling the brightness of a lamp Change to the variable resistor Current in the circuit The lamp resistance increased decreases becomes dimmer resistance decreased increases becomes brighter"],[26,"everyday-circuits","Everyday circuits","- Fridge light. The switch is opened and closed by the movement of the fridge door . When the door is open the switch is closed ; when the door is closed the switch is open — which is why the light comes on when you open the door. - Adjustable buzzer. A buzzer can warn you that something has been done incorrectly, and a variable resistor lets you adjust the loudness of that sound. - Car door buzzer. Some cars buzz when the driver opens the door while the headlamps are on. The headlamps are connected in parallel so they can be switched on and off together, and the buzzer is part of the same branch as the door switch. - S1 controls the headlamps; S2 is controlled by the door (door open → S2 closed). - Current cannot flow to S2 unless S1 is closed , so the buzzer only sounds when the headlamps are on. - Battery voltage indicator. Cars, trucks and some boats use 12 V batteries that need recharging. A voltmeter is connected in parallel across the battery , with a switch in that branch , so the voltage can be displayed when you want it."],[26,"running-a-6-v-lamp-from-a-12-v-battery","Running a 6 V lamp from a 12 V battery","- Sometimes a lamp with a low voltage rating is needed in a circuit with a battery of higher voltage. Resistors are used to produce different voltages. - If two resistors of equal resistance are connected in series with the battery, the battery voltage is shared equally across each resistor — 6 V across each of them from a 12 V battery. - A 6 V lamp can then be safely connected in parallel with one of the resistors . The lamp receives a 6 V supply and works properly. <details <summary <b Questions from the book — 9.4</b </summary 1. The circuit symbol for a fixed resistor is a plain rectangle; the symbol for a variable resistor is the same rectangle with an arrow through it . 2. A circuit with a variable resistor in the lamp's own branch can change the brightness of the lamp while keeping the sound from the buzzer constant . 3. For one switch to control both lamps together, put that switch between the cell and the branches ; to control each lamp's brightness separately, put a variable resistor in each branch . </details Summary checklist — 9.4. I know how to draw the circuit symbol for a variable resistor · how variable resistors are used in circuits · how to compare circuit diagrams containing cells, switches, fixed and variable resistors, ammeters, voltmeters, lamps and buzzers · how to build such circuits. <details <summary <b Check your progress — Chapter 9</b </summary - Ammeters in a parallel circuit. The reading outside the branches equals the total of the branch readings; two ammeters both outside the branches read the same . - Connecting meters. An ammeter to measure the current through both components goes in series anywhere in the loop. A voltmeter to measure the voltage across the resistor goes in parallel with the resistor . - Adding a third identical lamp in series: the lamps get dimmer , the current decreases , and the voltage across one lamp decreases . - Changing a circuit with one resistor: adding another cell in series increases the current through the cell; adding another resistor in parallel with the first also increases the current through the cell, because it adds another path. - Ohm's law calculations. R = V ÷ I = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 Ω . V = I × R = 0.5 × 18 = 9 V . I = V ÷ R = 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 A . - A variable resistor labelled X. Increasing the value of X increases the resistance , so the reading on the ammeter decreases . </details"]],"cards":[[0,"y7c01-f01a","Diagram labelling","This is a light microscope of the kind you use in the laboratory. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.1",""],[0,"y7c01-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part you look down through with your eye.","1.1",""],[0,"y7c01-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the flat platform that the slide is placed on.","1.1",""],[0,"y7c01-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that reflects light up through the slide.","1.1",""],[0,"y7c01-f02a","Diagram labelling","This is a typical animal cell as it appears under a microscope. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.2",""],[0,"y7c01-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the thin, flexible layer that controls what goes in and out of the cell.","1.2",""],[0,"y7c01-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that controls the activities of the cell.","1.2",""],[0,"y7c01-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part where energy is released from food.","1.2",""],[0,"y7c01-f03a","Diagram labelling","This is a red blood cell, cut in half so that you can see inside it. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that holds the haemoglobin.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the layer that forms the outer surface of this cell.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f04a","Diagram labelling","This is a neurone, a cell that carries electrical signals around the body. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the very long strand of cytoplasm that the electrical signal travels along.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the short strands that collect signals from other nearby nerve cells.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that controls the activities of this cell.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f05a","Diagram labelling","This is a ciliated cell from the lining of the tubes leading to the lungs. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tiny moving threads that sweep mucus towards the mouth.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that controls the activities of this cell.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f06a","Diagram labelling","This is a root hair cell from the outside of a plant root. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the strong, stiff layer made of cellulose that holds this cell in shape.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the space filled with cell sap.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f07a","Diagram labelling","This is a palisade cell from the leaf of a plant. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that contains chlorophyll and makes food for the plant.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the outer layer that a palisade cell has but an animal cell does not.","1.3",""],[0,"y7c01-f08a","Diagram labelling","This is a leaf that has been cut open so that you can see the tissues inside it. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tissue that covers the top surface of the leaf.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tissue made of tall cells packed with chloroplasts.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f08h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tissue with large air spaces between its cells.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f09a","Diagram labelling","This is Hydra, a small animal that lives in ponds. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that Hydra uses to catch smaller animals.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the space inside the body where digestion takes place.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f09h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tissue on the outside of the body, the one that carries the sting cells.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f10a","Diagram labelling","This is a sting cell from the tentacle of a Hydra. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that shoots out and wraps around the prey.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that the prey animal touches to fire the cell.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-f10h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that controls the activities of this cell.","1.4",""],[0,"y7c01-r001","Short answer","Cells were only described after the microscope was invented in the 1600s, even though people had been studying plants and animals for thousands of years. Explain why.","1.1","Cells are far too small for the naked eye to resolve, so no amount of careful looking without a lens could ever reveal them. A microscope magnifies the view, making the cells look much bigger than they really are, and only then do the tiny 'boxes' become visible. So the limit was never the observers' effort or care, it was the resolving power of the eye, and it took a new instrument to remove that limit. too small cannot see with eyes alone microscope magnifies new instrument"],[0,"y7c01-r002","Short answer","A plant cell and an animal cell are both placed in the same watery solution. The plant cell keeps its shape but the animal cell does not. Explain this difference.","1.1","Every plant cell has a cell wall, and the wall is strong and stiff, so it holds the plant cell in shape no matter what the cell contents do. An animal cell has only a cell membrane, which is very thin and flexible, like the skin of a soap bubble, so it cannot resist being pushed out of shape. The difference in behaviour therefore comes from the presence of a rigid outer layer, not from the membrane. cell wall strong and stiff holds the cell in shape animal cell has no wall membrane is flexible"],[0,"y7c01-r003","Short answer","Cellulose is a substance humans cannot digest. Suggest why a plant still builds its cell walls out of it.","1.1","The job of a plant cell wall is to be strong and stiff so that it holds the cell in shape, and cellulose is a substance that does exactly that. Being hard to break down is an advantage for the plant, not a problem, because a wall that dissolved or was digested easily would stop supporting the cell. Whether humans can digest it is irrelevant to the plant; the property that matters is mechanical strength. cellulose strong and stiff supports the cell hard to break down is an advantage"],[0,"y7c01-r004","Short answer","The sap vacuole is described as a space, yet it is one of the most obvious parts of a plant cell under a microscope. Explain why it is so easy to see.","1.1","The sap vacuole is a large, fluid-filled space inside a plant cell, and it is large enough to take up much of the cell's volume, pushing the other contents to the edges. Because it is filled with cell sap, a solution of sugars and other substances dissolved in water, rather than with jelly-like cytoplasm, it looks clear and different from everything around it. Size plus contrast is what makes it stand out. large fluid-filled space cell sap takes up much of the cell clear"],[0,"y7c01-r005","Short answer","A student says the cell wall is what decides which substances enter a plant cell. Explain why this is wrong.","1.1","The structure that controls what goes in and out of the cell is the cell membrane, not the cell wall. The wall's job is mechanical: it is strong and stiff and holds the cell in shape. The membrane lies along the inner edge of the cell wall, so it sits between the wall and the cytoplasm and is the last barrier anything must cross. A wall that also selected substances would leave the membrane with nothing to do. cell membrane controls what goes in and out cell wall gives shape membrane inside the wall"],[0,"y7c01-r006","Short answer","Cells from a potato growing underground contain no chloroplasts, while cells from a potato plant's leaves contain many. Explain why.","1.1","Plant cells that are in the sunlight often contain chloroplasts, because the chloroplast is where plants make their food and that process needs light. Leaf cells are in the sunlight, so chloroplasts are useful there. Cells in a potato growing underground never receive light, so chloroplasts in them would be of no use, and the plant does not build structures where they cannot work. chloroplasts in the sunlight where plants make their food underground has no light"],[0,"y7c01-r007","Short answer","Explain why chloroplasts look green, and why this colour is not just decoration.","1.1","Chloroplasts look green because they contain a green substance called chlorophyll. The chlorophyll is not there for its colour; it is the substance that lets the chloroplast do its job, which is where the plant makes its food. So the green colour is a visible clue that the working substance is present, which is why a cell packed with chloroplasts is also a cell that can make food. chlorophyll green substance inside chloroplasts makes food"],[0,"y7c01-r008","Short answer","The cytoplasm is described as being like clear jelly. Explain why a cell would die if its cytoplasm were removed, even with the nucleus left intact.","1.1","Chemical reactions happen inside the cytoplasm, and these reactions keep the cell alive. The nucleus controls the activities of the cell, but controlling an activity is not the same as carrying it out. Remove the cytoplasm and there is nowhere for the life-sustaining reactions to take place, so the instructions from the nucleus would have nothing to act on and the cell could not stay alive. chemical reactions happen in the cytoplasm keep the cell alive nucleus only controls"],[0,"y7c01-r009","Short answer","A muscle cell that is working hard contains far more mitochondria than a cell that is not. Suggest why.","1.1","Inside mitochondria, energy is released from food. A cell that is working hard needs more energy released every second than a cell that is not, so it needs more of the structures that do that job. Having many mitochondria therefore means the cell can release energy faster, which is exactly what a hard-working cell requires; the number of mitochondria matches the cell's energy demand. mitochondria energy released from food more energy needed more mitochondria"],[0,"y7c01-r010","Short answer","Some cells lose their nucleus as they mature and can no longer divide or repair themselves. Explain why losing the nucleus has this effect.","1.1","The nucleus controls the activities of the cell, so it is the part that issues the instructions for what the cell does. Once the nucleus is gone, those instructions are no longer available, so activities that need new instructions, such as dividing or rebuilding damaged parts, cannot be started. The cell may keep working for a while on what is already inside it, but it cannot direct any new activity. nucleus controls the activities no instructions cannot divide or repair"],[0,"y7c01-r011","Short answer","Both the cell wall and the cell membrane surround a plant cell. Explain why a plant cell needs both rather than just one of them.","1.1","They do different jobs, so one cannot replace the other. The cell wall is strong and stiff and holds the plant cell in shape, but a stiff wall cannot select what passes through it. The cell membrane is very thin and flexible and controls what goes in and out of the cell, but something that thin cannot hold a shape. The plant needs support and control at the same time, so it needs both layers. wall is strong and stiff membrane controls what goes in and out different jobs both needed"],[0,"y7c01-r012","Short answer","In the onion slide method a drop of water is put on the slide before the onion is added. Suggest why the water is needed.","1.1","The drop of water holds the thin layer of onion flat and spread out so that light can pass through it evenly, and it stops the layer drying out and curling while it is being handled. It also lets the cover slip be lowered gently without trapping the specimen against dry glass. Without the water the layer would fold, and folded tissue is several cells thick, so nothing would be in focus at once. water keeps the layer flat stops it drying light passes through avoids folds"],[0,"y7c01-r013","Short answer","Explain why the onion layer used on a slide must be peeled as thinly as possible.","1.1","A microscope image is formed by light passing through the specimen, so the specimen has to be thin enough for light to get through. If several layers of cells are stacked on top of each other, the light is blocked and the cells at different depths cannot all be in focus at the same time. Peeling off just the thin layer from the inside of the onion gives a specimen about one cell thick, which is why the view is clear. thin light passes through one cell thick otherwise cells overlap cannot focus"],[0,"y7c01-r014","Short answer","Explain why air bubbles under a cover slip are a problem when looking at onion cells.","1.1","An air bubble is round and bends light very strongly, so it appears as a thick black-edged circle that hides whatever is underneath it. It can also be mistaken for a cell, because it has a dark outline and a clear middle. Either way it costs you part of the view, so the cover slip is lowered gently to push the air out ahead of it rather than trapping it. air bubble hides the cells mistaken for a cell lower the cover slip gently"],[0,"y7c01-r015","Short answer","Explain why the smallest objective lens is turned into place before the slide is even looked at.","1.1","The smallest, lowest-power lens gives the widest view, so it is the easiest one for finding where the specimen actually is on the slide. It also sits furthest from the slide, so there is no risk of driving the lens into the glass while focusing. Starting at low power therefore solves two problems at once: locating the specimen, and protecting the lens and slide. low power first widest view easier to find the specimen lens further from slide avoids damage"],[0,"y7c01-r016","Short answer","A student looks at onion cells and says the cells in the picture are about 2 cm across. Explain what is wrong with this statement.","1.1","The microscope magnifies the view of the cells, so that they look much bigger than they really are. What the student has measured is the size of the image, not the size of the cell. Cells are so small that you cannot see them with your eyes alone, so a real width of 2 cm is impossible. To get the true size you would have to allow for how many times the microscope has magnified the view. microscope magnifies image is not the real size cells are too small to see must allow for magnification"],[0,"y7c01-r017","Short answer","Animal cells and plant cells share three structures. Explain why it makes sense that these particular three are the shared ones.","1.2","The shared structures are the cell membrane, cytoplasm and a nucleus, and each does a job that every living cell needs. The membrane controls what goes in and out of the cell, the cytoplasm is where the chemical reactions that keep the cell alive happen, and the nucleus controls the activities of the cell. Structures like the cell wall and chloroplasts relate to jobs only plants do, so only plants have them. cell membrane cytoplasm nucleus every cell needs these jobs wall and chloroplasts are plant jobs"],[0,"y7c01-r018","Short answer","One estimate of the number of cells in a human body is about 100 trillion, and no one knows the exact figure. Suggest why an exact count is impossible.","1.2","Cells are so small that you cannot see them with your eyes alone, so they can only be counted through a microscope, a tiny sample at a time. A body contains so many that counting them one by one would never finish, and cells are also being made and lost all the time, so the true number changes while you count. Any figure can therefore only be an estimate scaled up from a sample. too small to see far too many to count cells made and lost all the time estimate from a sample"],[0,"y7c01-r019","Short answer","Cheek cells are stained with methylene blue before being viewed. Explain why staining is necessary.","1.2","Cheek cells are almost colourless and very thin, so light passes straight through them and they show almost no contrast against the glass slide. Methylene blue is a dye that will stain the cells blue, making them easier to see, because the stained parts now absorb light while the background does not. The microscope has not changed; what has changed is how much of the light the specimen takes out. cells are colourless little contrast dye stains them blue easier to see"],[0,"y7c01-r020","Short answer","After rubbing the cotton bud on the slide, you cannot see anything. Explain why this does not mean the transfer failed.","1.2","You will not be able to see the cells yet, because they are so small. The cells scraped loose from the inside of the cheek are individually invisible to the naked eye, so an apparently empty slide is exactly what a successful transfer looks like. Only after staining and viewing under a microscope can you tell whether cells are there, so judging the step by eye at this point tells you nothing. cells are too small to see empty-looking slide is expected check under the microscope"],[0,"y7c01-r021","Short answer","When focusing, you turn the knob while looking from the side rather than through the eyepiece. Explain why.","1.2","Looking from the side lets you see how much space is left between the lens and the slide. If you watched through the eyepiece instead you could not judge that gap, and you might drive the lens down into the slide, cracking the cover slip or the lens. So the side view is a safety step: it lets you bring the lens close without touching, and you then focus upwards, away from the glass. look from the side see the gap avoid hitting the slide protects lens and cover slip"],[0,"y7c01-r022","Short answer","When deciding whether a photograph shows a plant cell or an animal cell, which single feature is most useful, and why?","1.2","The most useful feature is the cell wall, because every plant cell has a cell wall and animal cells have none. A wall shows up as a straight, thick, clearly outlined edge, so cells packed in a regular box-like pattern are plant cells. Chloroplasts are also a giveaway, but they are only present in plant cells that are in the sunlight, so their absence proves nothing, while the wall is always there. cell wall all plant cells have one animal cells have none chloroplasts can be absent"],[0,"y7c01-r023","Short answer","Explain why it is not always possible to tell from a picture whether a cell is a plant cell or an animal cell.","1.2","A picture only shows the features that happen to be visible in that view. A plant cell from underground has no chloroplasts, so the green clue is missing, and if the cell has been cut or squashed the wall may not show a clear straight edge. The three structures both kinds share, cell membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus, look much the same in either, so a view that shows only those is genuinely ambiguous. only shows what is visible chloroplasts can be absent wall may not be clear shared structures look alike"],[0,"y7c01-r024","Short answer","A model of a plant cell is to be turned into a model of an animal cell. Explain what must be removed and why.","1.2","The cell wall, the chloroplasts and the large sap vacuole must go, because those are the parts an animal cell does not have. The wall is removed because animal cells are held in shape only by a thin, flexible membrane; the chloroplasts because animals do not make their own food; and the large sap vacuole because it belongs to the plant cell's water-filled interior. What stays is the membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus. remove cell wall remove chloroplasts remove large sap vacuole keep membrane cytoplasm nucleus"],[0,"y7c01-r025","Short answer","Explain what is meant by the statement that your body is made of cells, given that you can see none of them.","1.2","All living organisms are made of cells, and that includes you: every part of your body is built from cells even though cells are so small that you cannot see them with your eyes alone. What you see is the effect of enormous numbers of them grouped together, in the same way that you see a wall rather than the individual bricks. The cells are there; they are simply below the resolving power of your eye. all organisms are made of cells too small to see huge numbers grouped together"],[0,"y7c01-r026","Short answer","Both a mouse and an oak tree are made of cells. Explain why being made of cells does not make them the same kind of organism.","1.2","Being made of cells is something all living organisms share, so it cannot be what separates one kind from another. The differences lie in which structures the cells contain: plant cells have a cell wall, a large sap vacuole and often chloroplasts, while animal cells have none of these. So the shared fact places both in the living world, and the contents of the cells place them in different parts of it. all organisms are made of cells shared feature cannot distinguish plant cells have wall and chloroplasts"],[0,"y7c01-r027","Short answer","Explain why the cotton bud is rubbed only very gently along the inside of the cheek.","1.2","The aim is to collect some loose cells that are already coming away from the surface, not to injure the lining. Rubbing hard would damage the tissue and could make it bleed, and it would also collect clumps of cells stuck together rather than the separate single cells that are easiest to see. A gentle rub gives a thin scatter of individual cells, which is what the microscope needs. collect loose cells avoid damaging the lining separate cells not clumps"],[0,"y7c01-r028","Short answer","Explain what scientists mean when they say a cell is specialised.","1.3","A specialised cell has a structure that helps it to do its function really well, where its function is the job that it does or the role that it plays. The word does not just mean 'different'; it means the differences are matched to the job. The cell is adapted to carry out its function, so if you know the structure you can often work out the job, and if you know the job you can predict the structure. structure suits the function function is the job it does adapted structure predicts function"],[0,"y7c01-r029","Short answer","Explain how the small size of a red blood cell helps it do its job.","1.3","Red blood cells are smaller than most other cells in the body, and this allows them to get through tiny blood capillaries. Because they can pass through the narrowest vessels, they can deliver oxygen to every part of the body, including tissues that only the smallest capillaries reach. A larger cell would jam in those vessels, so the parts of the body served by them would receive no oxygen. smaller than most cells fit through tiny capillaries deliver oxygen everywhere"],[0,"y7c01-r030","Short answer","A red blood cell has no nucleus. Explain how this is an advantage rather than a defect.","1.3","Losing the nucleus leaves more space for haemoglobin, the red pigment in the cytoplasm that carries oxygen around the body. Because the cell's only job is to carry oxygen, the space is better spent on the substance that does the carrying than on the structure that controls the cell's activities. The cell gives up the ability to direct new activity, and in exchange it carries more oxygen per cell. no nucleus more space for haemoglobin carries more oxygen trade-off"],[0,"y7c01-r031","Short answer","Explain why red blood cells are red, and why that colour is useful evidence.","1.3","The cytoplasm contains a red pigment called haemoglobin, and it is the haemoglobin that carries oxygen around the body. So the colour comes from the very substance that does the cell's job. That makes the colour useful evidence: a cell that is packed with red pigment is a cell equipped to carry oxygen, which is why blood as a whole looks red. haemoglobin red pigment in the cytoplasm carries oxygen colour shows the working substance"],[0,"y7c01-r032","Short answer","Explain why a neurone needs a very long axon.","1.3","Neurones carry electrical signals from one part of the body to another, for example from the brain to a muscle, so the cell has to physically reach between those two places. The axon is a very long strand of cytoplasm, and electrical signals can travel along this very quickly, so one cell can span the distance without the signal being handed on repeatedly. Length is therefore the adaptation that makes long-distance messaging possible. axon is a long strand of cytoplasm carries signals between distant parts signals travel quickly"],[0,"y7c01-r033","Short answer","Explain why dendrites are short and branching while the axon is long and single.","1.3","The two ends of the cell do different jobs. Dendrites are short strands of cytoplasm that collect electrical signals from other nearby nerve cells, so they need to reach many neighbours a short distance away, which favours many short branches. The axon has to carry the signal to a distant part of the body, so it favours one long strand. Each shape fits the distance and the number of connections its job involves. dendrites collect from nearby cells many short branches axon carries far one long strand"],[0,"y7c01-r034","Short answer","Explain why an organism made only of neurones could not survive.","1.3","Neurones carry electrical signals and help the different parts of the body communicate with each other, but communication is useless if there is nothing to communicate with. Signals from the brain to muscles only matter if muscle cells exist to move, and the whole body still needs cells to carry oxygen, absorb water or make food. Each kind of cell has a particular function, so a body needs many kinds working together. neurones only carry signals need other cells to act on each kind of cell has its own function"],[0,"y7c01-r035","Short answer","Explain how mucus and cilia work as a pair in the tubes leading to the lungs.","1.3","Neither part would work alone. Cells in the lining make a sticky substance called mucus, and when you breathe in the mucus traps dust and bacteria in the air, to stop them going into your lungs. But trapped dirt still has to leave, so the ciliated cells sweep the mucus up to the back of your mouth and you swallow it. Mucus does the catching and cilia do the removing; together they clean the air. mucus traps dust and bacteria cilia sweep the mucus up swallowed two jobs together"],[0,"y7c01-r036","Short answer","Explain why the cilia are on one edge of the cell only, rather than all over it.","1.3","Ciliated cells have tiny threads along one edge, and that edge is the one facing the inside of the tube, where the mucus lies. Cilia on the other sides would be pressed against neighbouring cells and against the tissue underneath, where there is nothing to sweep, so they would do no work. Putting them only on the exposed surface means every cilium is in contact with the mucus it has to move. threads along one edge faces the inside of the tube only that surface touches the mucus"],[0,"y7c01-r037","Short answer","Smoking damages the cilia in the airways. Predict the consequence and explain your reasoning.","1.3","The cilia sweep the mucus up to the back of your mouth so that it can be swallowed. If they are damaged, the mucus is still made but it can no longer be moved, so it collects in the tubes leading from the mouth to the lungs along with the dust and bacteria it has trapped. That means more coughing to shift it, and more of the trapped bacteria staying in the airways instead of being removed. cilia cannot sweep mucus builds up trapped dust and bacteria stay coughing"],[0,"y7c01-r038","Short answer","Explain how the shape of a root hair cell helps it absorb water.","1.3","Each root hair cell has a long, thin extension that allows water to move easily from the soil into the cell. The extension pushes out between the soil particles, so it reaches water the rest of the root could not touch, and it gives the cell a much larger surface in contact with the soil than a plain round cell would have. More contact with the soil water means faster absorption. long thin extension reaches between soil particles large surface in contact with soil absorbs water faster"],[0,"y7c01-r039","Short answer","Suggest why root hair cells contain no chloroplasts.","1.3","Chloroplasts are where plants make their food, and that needs light, which is why plant cells that are in the sunlight often contain chloroplasts. Root hair cells are on the outside of plant roots, buried in the soil, where no light reaches them. Chloroplasts there could never work, so the space and materials are better used for the cell's real job, which is to absorb water from the soil. chloroplasts need light roots are in the soil no light job is to absorb water"],[0,"y7c01-r040","Short answer","Explain why palisade cells contain a lot of chloroplasts while other leaf cells contain fewer.","1.3","Palisade cells are found in the leaves of plants and their function is to make food by photosynthesis. The chlorophyll inside the chloroplasts absorbs energy from sunlight, which is used to help the plant make food, so the more chloroplasts a cell has the more light energy it can capture. Palisade cells sit near the top of the leaf where the light is strongest, so packing them with chloroplasts uses that light rather than wasting it. function is photosynthesis chlorophyll absorbs light energy more chloroplasts capture more light near the top of the leaf"],[0,"y7c01-r041","Short answer","Red blood cells, neurones and ciliated cells look nothing alike. Explain how you can still tell that all three are animal cells.","1.3","None of them has a cell wall, a large sap vacuole or chloroplasts, and those are the structures that mark a cell as a plant cell. What they do share is the cell membrane and cytoplasm that all cells have. So the identification does not depend on their shapes at all; it depends on which plant-only structures are absent, and all three are missing the same ones. no cell wall no chloroplasts no large sap vacuole absence of plant structures"],[0,"y7c01-r042","Short answer","Red blood cells, neurones and ciliated cells all do different jobs. Explain why they nonetheless have some parts in common.","1.3","They are all still cells, and some jobs have to be done inside every living cell no matter what its speciality is. All cells have a cell membrane to control what goes in and out, and all cells have cytoplasm, where the chemical reactions that keep the cell alive happen. Specialisation adds features on top of these basics; it does not replace them, because the basic needs of staying alive do not go away. all cells have a membrane all cells have cytoplasm basic needs remain specialisation adds features"],[0,"y7c01-r043","Short answer","Water travelling from the soil into a root hair cell's sap vacuole must pass several parts in order. List them and explain why that order is fixed.","1.3","The water crosses the cell wall, then the cell membrane, then the cytoplasm, and finally enters the sap vacuole. The order is fixed by where the parts lie: the wall is the outermost layer, the cell membrane lies along the inner edge of the cell wall, the cytoplasm fills the space inside the membrane, and the sap vacuole is the space inside that. Nothing can reach an inner layer without crossing the ones outside it. cell wall cell membrane cytoplasm sap vacuole order set by position"],[0,"y7c01-r044","Short answer","A student writes that a palisade cell 'is adapted for its function'. Explain why this sentence earns no marks on its own.","1.3","Saying a cell is adapted repeats the question instead of answering it. An explanation has to name the structure, say what the structure does, and then link that to the job: a palisade cell has a lot of chloroplasts, the chlorophyll in them absorbs energy from sunlight, and that energy is used to help the plant make food. Only when those three steps are present has the adaptation actually been explained. must name the structure say what it does link to the function naming is not explaining"],[0,"y7c01-r045","Short answer","Explain why comparing two specialised cells in a table makes their adaptations easier to see than describing them one after the other.","1.3","A table puts the same feature for both cells side by side, so a difference in that one feature stands out immediately instead of having to be held in memory across two paragraphs. Because each row is one feature, it also forces you to ask the same question of both cells, which stops you describing one in detail and the other briefly. The comparison then points straight at which differences relate to the different functions. same feature side by side differences stand out same question asked of both links differences to function"],[0,"y7c01-r046","Short answer","Explain why a group of cells has to be both similar and working together before it counts as a tissue.","1.4","A tissue is a group of similar cells, which all work together to carry out a particular function. Both parts matter. Cells that are similar but doing unrelated jobs are not achieving anything as a group, and cells that work together but are of many different kinds form an organ, not a tissue. It is the combination of one cell type and one shared function that defines the level. group of similar cells work together one particular function many kinds would be an organ"],[0,"y7c01-r047","Short answer","Explain the difference between a tissue and an organ, using the leaf as an example.","1.4","A tissue is a group of similar cells all doing one function, while each organ is made up of several different kinds of tissue, working together. A leaf is a plant organ, and contains several different kinds of tissue, such as the palisade cells that make food and the surface layers that cover it. So the leaf is one level up: it is a combination of tissues, each of which is itself a combination of similar cells. tissue is similar cells organ contains several tissues leaf is an organ palisade tissue inside it"],[0,"y7c01-r048","Short answer","Explain why the heart on its own is not called an organ system.","1.4","An organ system is a set of organs that all work together to carry out the same function, so it needs more than one organ. The heart is a single organ, made of several kinds of tissue working together, but it cannot move blood around the body by itself; it needs blood vessels as well. Heart and blood vessels together form the organ system, and the heart alone is one component of it. organ system is a set of organs heart is one organ needs blood vessels too together they form the system"],[0,"y7c01-r049","Short answer","Explain why ciliated epithelium is called a tissue rather than an organ.","1.4","Ciliated epithelium is the tissue that lines the tubes leading down to our lungs, and it is made of one kind of cell, the ciliated cell, all doing the same job. An organ is made up of several different kinds of tissue, working together, and this lining is not; it is a single layer of similar cells. So it sits at the tissue level, while the lung it helps protect sits at the organ level. one kind of cell same job organ needs several tissues lining is a single layer"],[0,"y7c01-r050","Short answer","Explain why it matters that the cilia all wave together, like grass in the wind, rather than independently.","1.4","The job is to sweep the mucus up to the back of the mouth, and moving something in one direction requires the pushes to act the same way at the same time. If neighbouring cilia beat in random directions their pushes would cancel and the mucus would stay where it is. Waving together turns millions of tiny movements into one steady flow, which is why the tissue as a whole can shift the mucus. all wave together pushes act in one direction random beating would cancel steady flow of mucus"],[0,"y7c01-r051","Short answer","The onion epidermis is a tissue. Explain what its cells must have in common for it to be called one.","1.4","The onion epidermis covers the surface of the layers inside the onion, and that covering job is done by cells of one kind, arranged in a single layer. Because a tissue is a group of similar cells which all work together to carry out a particular function, the cells have to be alike and have to share the same job. Here both are true: similar box-shaped cells, all forming the covering. similar cells same function covers the surface single layer"],[0,"y7c01-r052","Short answer","The word 'tissue' has an everyday meaning and a scientific meaning. Explain why using the everyday meaning in a science answer causes problems.","1.4","In everyday speech a tissue is a piece of soft paper, which has nothing to do with living things. In science a tissue is a group of similar cells which all work together to carry out a particular function. If the two are confused, a sentence like 'the lungs contain tissue' can be read as if something has been put inside the lungs rather than describing what they are built from, and the whole hierarchy of cell, tissue, organ stops making sense. everyday meaning is paper scientific meaning is a group of similar cells confusion breaks the hierarchy"],[0,"y7c01-r053","Short answer","The brain is called an organ even though neurones are its best-known cells. Explain why.","1.4","Each organ is made up of several different kinds of tissue, working together, and your brain contains neurones and also several other kinds of cell. If the brain were nothing but neurones it would be a tissue. Because it combines several tissues into one structure with one overall job, it qualifies as an organ; the famous cell type is only part of what is in there. brain contains neurones and other cells several tissues organ not tissue"],[0,"y7c01-r054","Short answer","A plant root contains root hair cells and also several other kinds of cell. Explain why this makes the root an organ.","1.4","An organ is made up of several different kinds of tissue, working together, and that is exactly what the root is: root hair cells absorbing water from the soil, plus other kinds of cell doing other jobs such as carrying that water upwards and anchoring the plant. Because more than one kind of tissue is present and they contribute to one overall job, the root sits at the organ level rather than the tissue level. several different kinds of cell several tissues one overall job organ"],[0,"y7c01-r055","Short answer","Explain why organs working together is a useful arrangement, using a named example.","1.4","A set of organs that all work together to carry out the same function is called an organ system, and the arrangement lets a job be split into parts that different structures are built for. In the circulatory system the heart pushes the blood and the blood vessels carry it to every part of the body; neither could do the whole job alone. Splitting the work means each organ can be specialised for its part of it. organ system job split between organs heart pushes, vessels carry each organ specialised"],[0,"y7c01-r056","Short answer","Explain why cells of the same kind are usually grouped together rather than scattered through the body.","1.4","Most cells are specialised to carry out a particular activity, and cells of the same kind do the same job, so grouping them means all that work happens in one place where it is needed. Grouping also lets them act together, as the cilia do when they all wave in the same direction, producing an effect no single cell could produce. Scattering them would waste both the specialisation and the teamwork. specialised cells same job in one place act together effect one cell cannot produce"],[0,"y7c01-r057","Short answer","Hydra's body is made of only two layers of cells. Suggest why it has no organ systems even though it digests food.","1.4","An organ system is a set of organs working together, and an organ is made up of several different kinds of tissue. The body of Hydra is made up of two layers of cells, which is not enough to build several distinct tissues into separate organs and then group those organs together. Digestion still happens, in a cavity the mouth opens into, but it is carried out by the cell layers directly rather than by a chain of organs. two layers of cells not enough tissues for organs digestion in a cavity no organ system"],[0,"y7c01-r058","Short answer","Explain how a sting cell is adapted to catch prey.","1.4","The sting cell contains tiny coiled threads, and the coil stores the thread in a small space ready to be released. When a prey animal touches the trigger on the sting cell, the thread shoots out and wraps around the prey, so the cell responds only when prey is actually in contact rather than firing at random. Some of these threads may have poisonous chemicals on them, which kill the prey, so the animal is held and stopped. coiled thread stored ready trigger fires only on contact thread wraps around prey poison kills it"],[0,"y7c01-r059","Short answer","Explain how you can tell from a diagram that a sting cell is an animal cell and not a plant cell.","1.4","The test is which plant-only structures are missing. A sting cell has no cell wall, so its outline is irregular rather than a straight stiff box; it has no chloroplasts, and it has no large sap vacuole filling most of its middle. It still has the cell membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus that all cells have, so those tell you nothing, and it is the absent structures that settle the question. no cell wall no chloroplasts no large sap vacuole shared structures prove nothing"],[0,"y7c01-r060","Short answer","Explain why an organism is placed above organ systems in the sequence cell, tissue, organ, organ system, organism.","1.4","Each level is built from several units of the level below it, and an organism may contain many different organ systems, organs and tissues. So the organism is the whole living thing that all the systems belong to, and it sits at the top because nothing in the body contains it. Reading the sequence downwards takes you from the whole animal or plant to the single cells it is built from. each level built from the one below organism contains many systems top of the hierarchy"],[0,"y7c01-r061","Short answer","Explain why both a daisy and a dog are called organisms, despite being so different.","1.4","Living things, including animals and plants, are called organisms, so the word describes the whole living individual rather than any particular kind of body. Both a daisy and a dog are built from cells, grouped into tissues, then organs, then organ systems, so both reach the top of the same hierarchy. The differences lie in which cells, tissues and organs they contain, not in whether they are organisms. organism means a whole living thing both built from cells same hierarchy differences are in the parts"],[0,"y7c01-o002","Short answer","Why do you need a microscope to study cells?","1.1","Cells are so small that you cannot see them with your eyes alone. A microscope magnifies the view so that the cells look much bigger than they really are. too small eyes alone magnifies"],[0,"y7c01-o009","Short answer","Give three differences between a cell wall and a cell membrane.","1.1","Any three of: the wall is on the outside and the membrane is further in; the wall is made of cellulose and the membrane is not; the wall is strong and stiff while the membrane is thin and flexible; the wall holds the cell in shape while the membrane controls what goes in and out. cellulose flexible holds the cell in shape controls what goes in and out"],[0,"y7c01-o019","Short answer","When you look at onion cells under a microscope, why is the onion layer peeled from the inside of the onion instead of cut thickly?","1.1","The layer has to be thin enough for light to pass through it. Light from the mirror shines up through the specimen, so a thick piece would just look dark. thin light through"],[0,"y7c01-o021","Short answer","In which three ways is an animal cell similar to a plant cell?","1.2","Both have a cell membrane, cytoplasm and a nucleus. cell membrane cytoplasm nucleus"],[0,"y7c01-o022","Short answer","Name three parts that a plant cell has but an animal cell does not.","1.2","A cell wall, a large sap vacuole and chloroplasts. cell wall sap vacuole chloroplast"],[0,"y7c01-o023","Short answer","You are shown a photograph of some cells taken through a microscope. What is the most useful feature to look for to decide whether they are plant cells or animal cells?","1.2","Look for a cell wall. Cells with a cell wall have a regular, straight-sided shape and are plant cells; animal cells have no wall and look irregular. cell wall regular shape irregular"],[0,"y7c01-o024","Short answer","What is a stain, and why is methylene blue used when looking at cheek cells?","1.2","A stain is a dye that colours the cells. Methylene blue stains the cells blue and makes them much easier to see, because cheek cells are almost colourless. dye stain easier to see"],[0,"y7c01-o025","Short answer","Where do you get the cells from when you make a slide of your own animal cells?","1.2","You gently rub a cotton bud along the inside of your cheek, which collects some loose cells. cotton bud inside of your cheek"],[0,"y7c01-o026","Short answer","Before you look down the eyepiece, you turn the focussing knob while watching the microscope from the side. Why is it done in that order?","1.2","So that you can see how close the lens is to the slide and stop before it touches it. Looking from the side is the only way to judge that distance safely. see how close not touch slide"],[0,"y7c01-o027","Short answer","After you have found the cells with the smallest lens, how do you get a more magnified view?","1.2","Turn the lenses until a larger objective lens is over the stage, then look down the eyepiece again. larger lens"],[0,"y7c01-o028","Short answer","Roughly how many cells is a human body thought to contain?","1.2","About 100 trillion. 100 trillion"],[0,"y7c01-o037","Short answer","What is the axon of a neurone, and how does its shape help the cell?","1.3","The axon is a very long strand of cytoplasm. Electrical signals can travel along it very quickly, so they can travel a long way in a short time. long strand of cytoplasm quickly"],[0,"y7c01-o060","Short answer","What is ciliated epithelium, and what is its function?","1.4","It is the tissue that lines the tubes leading down to our lungs. The cilia all wave together and sweep mucus, with dust and bacteria trapped in it, away from the lungs. tissue lines the tubes lungs mucus"],[0,"y7c01-o061","Short answer","Explain why a leaf is called an organ and not a tissue.","1.4","A leaf contains several different kinds of tissue working together, such as the upper epidermis, the palisade layer, the spongy layer and the lower epidermis. A tissue is only one kind of cell. several different kinds of tissue organ"],[0,"y7c01-o069","Short answer","How does a Hydra catch its food?","1.4","It has tentacles that it uses to catch even smaller animals, which it pushes into its mouth. Sting cells on the tentacles fire threads that wrap around the prey. tentacles mouth sting cells"],[0,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the cell wall made of?","1.1","cellulose chlorophyll cytoplasm sap"],[0,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The liquid inside the sap vacuole is called ____.","1.1","cell sap"],[0,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which part of the plant cell controls what goes in and out?","1.1","cell membrane nucleus chloroplast mitochondrion"],[0,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Chloroplasts look green because they contain a substance called ____.","1.1","chlorophyll"],[0,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","Where do chemical reactions happen to keep the cell alive?","1.1","cytoplasm nucleus cell wall vacuole"],[0,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","Energy is released from food inside the ____.","1.1","mitochondrion"],[0,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","Which part of a plant cell holds the cell in shape?","1.1","cell wall cell membrane cytoplasm sap vacuole"],[0,"qwen-04-01","Multiple choice","What is the estimated number of cells in a person?","1.2","100 trillion 100 million 100 billion 100 thousand"],[0,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","Animal cells have a cell membrane, cytoplasm and a ____.","1.2","nucleus"],[0,"qwen-04-04","Gap fill","You are an animal, and your body is made of ____.","1.2","cells"],[0,"qwen-04-12","Gap fill","The liquid inside the cell where organelles are suspended is called ____.","1.2","cytoplasm"],[0,"qwen-05-01","Multiple choice","What is the purpose of using methylene blue in this experiment?","1.2","To kill the cells To stain the cells so they are easier to see To clean the microscope slide To make the cells grow larger"],[0,"qwen-05-03","Multiple choice","Why can you not see the cells immediately after rubbing them onto the slide?","1.2","Because they are dead Because they are too small to see with the naked eye Because the slide is dirty Because you need a cover slip first"],[0,"qwen-05-05","Multiple choice","Which part of the microscope do you turn to bring the lens close to the slide?","1.2","The stage The focussing knob The objective lens The dropper pipette"],[0,"qwen-07-01","Multiple choice","What is the function of a cell?","1.3","The job that it does The shape of its nucleus The colour of its cytoplasm The size of its membrane"],[0,"qwen-07-02","Gap fill","Red blood cells contain a red pigment called ____ that carries oxygen.","1.3","haemoglobin"],[0,"qwen-07-03","Multiple choice","Why do red blood cells have no nucleus?","1.3","To make them smaller To leave more space for haemoglobin To help them move faster To protect the DNA"],[0,"qwen-07-04","Gap fill","The very long strand of cytoplasm in a neurone is called the ____.","1.3","axon"],[0,"qwen-07-05","Multiple choice","What do cilia sweep up to the back of your mouth?","1.3","Oxygen Mucus Blood cells Nutrients"],[0,"qwen-07-06","Gap fill","Root hair cells are found on the outside of plant ____.","1.3","roots"],[0,"qwen-07-07","Multiple choice","What is the function of a root hair cell?","1.3","To make food by photosynthesis To absorb water from the soil To carry electrical signals To trap dust and bacteria"],[0,"qwen-07-09","Multiple choice","What is a group of similar cells that work together called?","1.4","An organ A tissue An organism A system"],[0,"qwen-07-10","Gap fill","A set of organs that work together to carry out the same function is called an ____ system.","1.4","organ"],[0,"qwen-07-12","Gap fill","Living things, including animals and plants, are called ____.","1.4","organisms"],[0,"qwen-08-02","Gap fill","The stomach is an example of an ____.","1.4","organ"],[0,"qwen-08-04","Gap fill","In a complex organism, similar cells are grouped together to form a ____.","1.4","tissue"],[0,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The part of the plant cell that contains sap is called the ____.","1.1","sap vacuole"],[0,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","What should you do to improve a drawing of a plant cell?","1.1","Shade the structures Colour the diagram Ensure label lines touch the structure Make the shapes large"],[0,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","When drawing a biological diagram, you should not ____ or colour your drawing.","1.1","shade"],[0,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which part of the microscope is used to look through?","1.2","stage eyepiece mirror coarse focusing knob"],[0,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","The part of the microscope that holds the slide is called the ____.","1.2","stage"],[0,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Red blood cells contain a substance called ____ which helps them carry oxygen.","1.3","cytoplasm haemoglobin food capillaries"],[0,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","Red blood cells are small so they can squeeze through tiny blood vessels called ____.","1.3","capillaries"],[0,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","The system in the human body that includes the heart and blood vessels is the ____ system.","1.4","circulatory"],[0,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","Which of the following is an organ system in the human body?","1.4","nervous system chloroplast sap vacuole mitochondrion"],[0,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","The part of the microscope with a low-power objective lens is used for initial viewing, while the ____ focusing knob makes small adjustments.","1.2","fine"],[0,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","When a prey animal touches the trigger, the ____ shoots out and wraps around the prey.","1.4","thread"],[0,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","Hydra lives in ____ ponds.","1.4","freshwater"],[1,"y7c02-f01a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows how water moves between its three states. Name the state, or the change of state, labelled {n}.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the state that water turns into when it is heated to 100 °C.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the state that water turns into when it is cooled below 0 °C.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the change of state that turns a gas back into a liquid.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f02a","Diagram labelling","This is the apparatus used to heat water and measure its temperature. Name the piece of apparatus labelled {n}.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the piece of apparatus that supplies the heat.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the piece of apparatus used to measure out a known volume of water.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the piece of apparatus that protects the bench from the flame.","2.2",""],[1,"y7c02-f03a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows the water cycle. Name the stage labelled {n}.","2.4",""],[1,"y7c02-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the stage where water falls from the clouds as rain, snow or hail.","2.4",""],[1,"y7c02-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the stage where liquid water from the oceans changes into water vapour.","2.4",""],[1,"y7c02-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the stage where water vapour cools and forms the tiny droplets that make clouds.","2.4",""],[1,"y7c02-f04a","Diagram labelling","This is part of the Periodic Table. Name the element, or the group of elements, labelled {n}.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that names the elements which conduct heat and electricity well.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label that names the elements which do not conduct electricity.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the element whose atoms have the second smallest mass of all the elements.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f05a","Diagram labelling","These are samples of six different elements. Name the element labelled {n} and give its symbol.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the reddish-brown metal that is used to make electrical wiring.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the magnetic metal whose symbol does not start with the same letter as its English name.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the only element here that is a liquid at room temperature.","2.5",""],[1,"y7c02-f06a","Diagram labelling","This pie chart shows what air is made of. Give the name of the gas labelled {n} and the percentage of the air it makes up.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the gas that makes up roughly four-fifths of the air.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the gas that living things take out of the air when they respire.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-f07a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows some of the particles found in air. Name the substance labelled {n}.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the substance that is an element and is also the most common gas in air.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the compound that plants take in from the air when they make food.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-f07h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the compound whose particles contain hydrogen atoms.","2.7",""],[1,"y7c02-r001","Short answer","Explain what scientists mean by the properties of a state of matter, and why properties are more useful than examples.","2.1","The ways the states of matter behave are called their properties, so a property is a description of behaviour rather than a list of substances. Properties are more useful because they let you classify a material you have never met: if a sample keeps its own shape and cannot be poured you can call it a solid without knowing its name. A list of examples only helps with the substances already on the list. properties describe how matter behaves classify an unknown material examples only cover known substances"],[1,"y7c02-r002","Short answer","Explain why a solid cannot be poured, using the arrangement of its particles.","2.1","Matter can only flow, or be poured, if the particles can move past one another. In a solid the particles are arranged in a fixed pattern and are held together strongly by attractive forces, and those forces stop the particles from moving around, so they can only vibrate in the same place. Because no particle can slide past its neighbour, the whole material cannot flow, which is why a solid cannot be poured. flow needs particles to move past one another fixed pattern strong attractive forces particles only vibrate"],[1,"y7c02-r003","Short answer","A liquid takes the shape of its container but its volume stays the same. Explain how both can be true at once.","2.1","Shape and volume depend on two different things. Shape can change because the particles touch each other but can move past one another, so they rearrange to fit whatever container they are in. Volume cannot change because the particles are already very close together and cannot be squashed, so however they rearrange they still take up the same amount of space. One property comes from movement, the other from spacing. particles move past one another so shape changes particles already close together cannot be squashed volume fixed"],[1,"y7c02-r004","Short answer","Explain why a gas can be compressed easily but a liquid cannot.","2.1","Matter can only change volume if the particles in it can spread out or move closer together. In a gas the particles do not touch each other and are a long way apart, so there is empty space between them and squashing simply moves them closer, which is why the gas takes up less space. In a liquid the particles are already very close together and touching, so there is no space left to close up and the volume cannot be reduced. gas particles far apart empty space to close up liquid particles already touching no space left"],[1,"y7c02-r005","Short answer","A gas released in one corner of a room is soon detected everywhere. Explain this using particle theory.","2.1","In a gas the particles are far apart and can move about freely, because there are no attractive forces between them holding them back. The particles spread out by themselves until they fill up the space they are in, so they travel from the corner throughout the room without anything pushing them. This is why you can smell food cooking in another room, and why a gas has no fixed shape or volume. particles far apart no attractive forces move freely spread out to fill the space"],[1,"y7c02-r006","Short answer","A vacuum is described as a space where there are no particles at all. Explain why sound cannot be carried through a vacuum.","2.1","A vacuum contains nothing, so there are no particles in it. Every effect that passes through matter does so because particles are there to be pushed and to pass the push on to their neighbours. With no particles present there is nothing to carry the disturbance from one place to the next, so the effect simply does not travel. The emptiness is not a weak medium; it is the absence of a medium. vacuum contains no particles nothing to pass the push on no medium to carry it"],[1,"y7c02-r007","Short answer","Explain the difference between an hypothesis and a theory in science.","2.1","An hypothesis is a suggestion for an explanation, put forward before it has been checked. It can then be tested by carrying out more investigations. When an hypothesis has been tested and widely accepted as valid by other scientists, it is called a theory. So the difference is not how confident one person feels; it is how much testing the idea has survived and whether the wider community accepts it. hypothesis is a suggested explanation tested by investigations theory once tested and widely accepted"],[1,"y7c02-r008","Short answer","Particle theory is called a theory rather than a fact. Explain why scientists are still confident in it.","2.1","The theory says that all matter is made up of tiny particles arranged in different ways, and it is the best theory to explain how matter behaves. Its strength is that one idea accounts for many separate observations at once: why solids keep their shape, why gases can be squashed, why liquids flow and why you can smell cooking from another room. It has been tested and widely accepted as valid by other scientists, which is exactly what makes an idea a theory. one idea explains many observations tested repeatedly widely accepted best available explanation"],[1,"y7c02-r009","Short answer","Particles are said to be much too small to see. Explain how scientists can nonetheless claim that they exist.","2.1","All matter is made up of tiny particles that are much too small to see, so they are not observed directly. The evidence is indirect: the theory predicts what should happen when matter is heated, squashed or left to spread, and those predictions match what is actually seen. When one idea keeps predicting correctly across many different investigations, the idea is accepted even though the particles themselves are never seen. too small to see indirect evidence predictions match observations accepted because it keeps working"],[1,"y7c02-r010","Short answer","Explain why the melting point is a useful property for identifying a substance.","2.2","The temperature at which a solid melts is called the melting point, and for a pure substance that temperature is always the same however much of the substance you have. Because it does not depend on the size of the sample, a measured melting point can be compared with known values to identify what the substance is. A property that changed with the amount present would be useless for identification. melting point same for a pure substance does not depend on amount compare with known values"],[1,"y7c02-r011","Short answer","Explain why wet clothes dry faster on a warm day than on a cool one.","2.2","Water on the ground, or in cloth, gradually disappears as it changes to water vapour, an invisible gas, and this is called evaporation. The warmer the water, the more quickly it evaporates, because heat energy is transferred to the particles and more of them gain enough energy to break the weak attractive forces holding them in the liquid. More particles escaping each second means the clothes dry sooner. evaporation warmer evaporates more quickly particles gain energy escape the attractive forces"],[1,"y7c02-r012","Short answer","Explain the difference between evaporation and boiling.","2.2","Evaporation happens at any temperature: water on the ground gradually disappears as it changes to water vapour, and the warmer it is the faster this goes. Boiling happens only when the temperature reaches the boiling point, which for water is 100 degrees Celsius, and then all of the water rapidly changes to steam rather than just the surface slowly disappearing. So the two differ in the temperature needed and in how fast and how completely the change happens. evaporation at any temperature boiling at the boiling point 100 degrees for water boiling is rapid and throughout"],[1,"y7c02-r013","Short answer","Explain why a bathroom mirror fogs up when someone takes a hot shower.","2.2","The hot water evaporates, so the air holds a lot of water vapour. If the water vapour or steam touches something cold, it condenses and changes back to liquid water, and this is called condensation. The mirror is colder than the vapour, so when gas particles reach that cold surface some of their heat energy transfers to the surface, they move less and get closer together, and they form the tiny drops of liquid you see. water vapour touches a cold surface loses energy condenses to liquid"],[1,"y7c02-r014","Short answer","Melting, freezing, boiling and condensation are all called changes of state. Explain what they have in common.","2.2","In each one the substance stays the same substance and only its state changes, so ice, liquid water and steam are all water. What changes is how much energy the particles have and therefore how they are arranged and how freely they can move. Because no new substance is made, every change of state can be reversed by putting the energy back in or taking it out again. same substance throughout only the state changes energy of the particles changes reversible"],[1,"y7c02-r015","Short answer","Explain why the volume of a liquid is read from the bottom of the meniscus rather than the top.","2.2","The liquid forms a curve at the top called the meniscus, so the liquid surface is not at one single level. A rule is needed so that everyone reading the same cylinder gets the same number, and the bottom of the curve is the agreed point. If some people read the top and some the bottom their results would differ even with identical apparatus, which would make comparing measurements impossible. meniscus is a curve surface is not at one level agreed rule so readings can be compared"],[1,"y7c02-r016","Short answer","Explain why your eye must be level with the meniscus when reading a measuring cylinder.","2.2","If your eye is above or below the liquid level you look at the scale at an angle, so the mark that appears to line up with the surface is not the mark that really does. This makes the reading too high or too low depending on which side you are on, and the error changes every time you move. Keeping your eye level with the meniscus removes the angle, so the reading depends only on the liquid, not on where you are standing. eye level viewing at an angle shifts the reading error changes with position removes the angle"],[1,"y7c02-r017","Short answer","Explain how a liquid-in-glass thermometer works, using what you know about heating matter.","2.2","The liquid inside the thermometer expands as it gets hotter, so it rises up inside the thermometer and you read the temperature from the scale. The expansion happens because heat energy transferred to the particles makes them vibrate and move more, so they take up more space. The thermometer therefore converts a change in particle energy, which you cannot see, into a change in length, which you can. liquid expands when heated particles move more and take up more space rises up the tube read from the scale"],[1,"y7c02-r018","Short answer","Water on the ground disappears without being heated to 100 degrees Celsius. Explain why this does not contradict the boiling point of water.","2.2","Boiling at 100 degrees Celsius is the temperature at which all of the water rapidly changes to steam throughout the liquid. Evaporation is a different process: at any temperature some particles at the surface happen to have enough energy to escape as water vapour, an invisible gas. So water can disappear slowly far below its boiling point, and the boiling point only tells you when the fast, whole-liquid change begins. evaporation happens at any temperature only some surface particles escape boiling is the rapid whole-liquid change"],[1,"y7c02-r019","Short answer","Explain why the steam you can see coming from a kettle is not really the gas called steam.","2.2","Steam is water heated to the point that it turns into a gas, and gases generally cannot be seen. What is visible above a kettle is the cloud formed after the steam has touched cooler air and condensed back into tiny droplets of liquid water. So the visible cloud is evidence that condensation has already happened, and the truly gaseous steam is the clear region closer to the spout. steam is a gas gases cannot be seen visible cloud is condensed droplets condensation has happened"],[1,"y7c02-r020","Short answer","Explain why a solid expands when it is heated, even though the particles stay in place.","2.3","When the solid is heated, heat energy is transferred to the particles in the solid, and the more energy the particles have, the more they vibrate. As the particles vibrate more, they take up more space, so the whole solid gets bigger. The particles are still held in position by the attractive forces between them, so they do not change their pattern; the expansion comes from each particle needing more room to vibrate in. heat energy transferred particles vibrate more take up more space still held in a fixed pattern"],[1,"y7c02-r021","Short answer","Explain, using particle theory, what happens when a solid melts.","2.3","Heating transfers heat energy to the particles, so they vibrate more and more. Eventually the particles vibrate so much that the attractive forces between them are not strong enough to hold them in a fixed pattern, and they can slide past one another, so they can now move, not just vibrate. The forces are still strong enough for the particles to stay in touch with one another, which is why the result is a liquid and not a gas. energy makes particles vibrate more forces can no longer hold the fixed pattern particles slide past one another still touching"],[1,"y7c02-r022","Short answer","Explain why a liquid evaporates from its surface rather than from the middle.","2.3","The particles in a liquid move more as heat energy is transferred to them, and some particles have enough energy to break the weak attractive forces holding them together. A particle can only leave the liquid if it is at the surface, because anywhere else it is surrounded by other particles that pull it back. So escape happens at the boundary, and that is where the liquid is lost. particles gain energy break the weak attractive forces only surface particles can escape others are surrounded"],[1,"y7c02-r023","Short answer","Explain why a gas spreads out to fill its container while a liquid does not.","2.3","The particles in a gas are free to move anywhere and spread out, because there are no forces holding them. In a liquid the attractive forces are weak enough to allow the particles to move but strong enough to hold them together, so the particles can rearrange but cannot leave one another. The difference is therefore whether any attraction remains at all, not how fast the particles are moving. no forces between gas particles weak forces still hold liquid particles gas spreads, liquid stays together"],[1,"y7c02-r024","Short answer","Explain why condensation warms the cold surface it happens on.","2.3","When gas particles reach a cold surface, some of the heat energy from the particles transfers to the surface. The particles then move less and get closer together, which is what turns the gas into a liquid. Energy is not destroyed in the change; it moves out of the particles and into the surface, so the surface gains heat energy at the same time as the gas condenses. energy transfers from particles to the surface particles move less and get closer surface gains heat energy"],[1,"y7c02-r025","Short answer","Explain, using particle theory, what happens when a liquid freezes.","2.3","As heat energy is transferred from the particles to the surroundings, the particles move more slowly, because the cooler the liquid the less energy the particles have. The less energy they have, the less able they are to move or slide past one another. Eventually the particles have so little energy they cannot move and flow any more, so they can only vibrate, and they become arranged in a fixed pattern to form a solid. energy transferred to the surroundings particles move more slowly can only vibrate fixed pattern forms a solid"],[1,"y7c02-r026","Short answer","A gas is squashed into a smaller container. Explain what happens to its particles and what does not.","2.3","When you squash a gas, the particles move closer together and the gas takes up less space. The number of particles does not change, and neither do the particles themselves; only the empty space between them is reduced. This is why squashing is possible for a gas at all, and why squashing further and further eventually becomes hard, as the space available to remove runs out. particles move closer together number of particles unchanged empty space is reduced"],[1,"y7c02-r027","Short answer","A solid iron bar is heated and no longer fits its holder. Use particle theory to explain why.","2.3","Heating transfers heat energy to the particles in the iron, so they vibrate more, and as the particles vibrate more they take up more space. The bar therefore expands in every direction, becoming slightly longer and wider, which is enough to stop it fitting the holder. The iron is still a solid, because the particles are still held in their fixed pattern by attractive forces; only the spacing needed for vibration has grown. particles vibrate more take up more space bar expands still a solid"],[1,"y7c02-r028","Short answer","'Explaining' something means saying not only what happens but how or why. Apply this to ice melting to form water.","2.3","Saying that the ice turns to water only states what happens. The explanation is that heat energy is transferred to the particles, so they vibrate more and more; eventually they vibrate so much that the attractive forces between them cannot hold them in a fixed pattern; the particles can then slide past one another while still touching, which is the arrangement of a liquid. Each step says why the next one follows. what happens is not an explanation energy makes particles vibrate more forces can no longer hold the pattern particles slide past each other"],[1,"y7c02-r029","Short answer","Explain why the water cycle is described as a continuous system rather than a one-way journey.","2.4","Water on Earth is constantly moving between rivers, lakes, oceans, the atmosphere and the land, and it is recycled over and over again. Every stage feeds another: water that evaporates condenses, water that condenses falls as precipitation, and precipitation returns to the rivers and oceans it came from. Because the last stage leads back to the first, there is no beginning or end, which is what makes it a cycle. water moves between land, oceans and atmosphere recycled over and over last stage returns to the first"],[1,"y7c02-r030","Short answer","Explain how it can be true that you are still using the same water the ancient Romans used.","2.4","The Earth has been recycling water for more than four billion years, and the water cycle does not destroy water; it only moves it between states and places. Water that was drunk long ago evaporated, condensed, fell as rain and collected again, over and over. So the same particles keep circulating, and the water reaching you now has passed through the cycle countless times before. water is recycled not destroyed moves between states and places same particles circulate again and again"],[1,"y7c02-r031","Short answer","Explain the role of the Sun in driving the water cycle.","2.4","Energy from the Sun heats the Earth and the temperature of the water in the rivers, lakes and oceans increases. Some of the particles in the liquid water then gain enough energy to break free from the forces holding them together, so liquid water forms water vapour. Without that energy input no water would leave the surface, and the rest of the cycle, condensation and precipitation, would have nothing to work on. energy from the Sun water temperature increases particles gain enough energy to break free evaporation starts the cycle"],[1,"y7c02-r032","Short answer","Explain what transpiration is and why it means plants are part of the water cycle.","2.4","Water can also evaporate into the atmosphere from plants, and this is called transpiration. It matters because plant roots take up water from the ground and that water then leaves through the leaves into the air, so plants move water from the soil to the atmosphere. A landscape covered in plants therefore returns water to the air by a route that bare ground does not have. transpiration water evaporates from plants roots take water from the ground returns water to the atmosphere"],[1,"y7c02-r033","Short answer","Explain why water vapour rising into the atmosphere turns back into droplets.","2.4","As the water vapour goes up into the atmosphere it cools, and this process is called condensation. It happens because the particles in the water vapour lose energy and cannot move so quickly, so the attractive forces between them can hold them together again and they form little droplets of water in the air. Height matters because the atmosphere is colder higher up, so rising vapour always loses energy. vapour cools as it rises particles lose energy move more slowly forces pull them together into droplets"],[1,"y7c02-r034","Short answer","Explain why clouds eventually produce rain instead of holding their water forever.","2.4","When a lot of water has condensed, the water droplets in the clouds become too heavy for the air to hold them, so the droplets fall back to Earth as rain. Condensation keeps adding to the droplets, so their weight keeps increasing while the air's ability to support them does not. At some point weight wins, which is why rain comes after a period of cloud building rather than immediately. condensation makes droplets grow droplets become too heavy air can no longer hold them fall as rain"],[1,"y7c02-r035","Short answer","Explain why the same cloud can produce rain, snow, hail or sleet.","2.4","All of these are precipitation, which is water falling from clouds, and they all begin as condensed droplets. If the drops become colder they may form snow, hail or sleet instead of rain, so the difference is the temperature the falling water passes through, not what the cloud is made of. This is why the form of precipitation can change with the season or with height above the ground. all are precipitation start as condensed droplets colder drops form snow, hail or sleet temperature decides the form"],[1,"y7c02-r036","Short answer","Explain the difference between water that stays in the shallow soil layer and water that becomes groundwater.","2.4","Some of the water from precipitation soaks into the soil and rocks. Some of this water will stay in the shallow soil layer and will move towards streams and rivers, so it returns to the surface quickly. When groundwater soaks deeper into the soil it refills underground stores instead, where it may remain for a very long time. Depth therefore decides how soon that water rejoins the visible part of the cycle. shallow water moves towards streams and rivers deeper water refills underground stores depth decides how quickly it returns"],[1,"y7c02-r037","Short answer","Explain why precipitation in cold climates can leave the water cycle for long periods.","2.4","In cold climates the precipitation may build up on land as snow, ice or glaciers, and while it stays frozen the particles cannot move or flow, so the water is locked in place. It only rejoins the cycle if the temperatures rise, when the solid snow and ice will melt into liquid water which soaks into the ground or flows away. So cold storage pauses the cycle rather than ending it. builds up as snow, ice or glaciers frozen water cannot flow melts when temperatures rise rejoins the cycle"],[1,"y7c02-r038","Short answer","Explain why heavy surface run-off is a problem for rivers.","2.4","Water that reaches the surface of the land may flow directly across the ground into the rivers, lakes and oceans. When there is a lot of this surface run-off, soil can be carried off the land and into the rivers, which can cause them to become silted up and blocked. So the damage is done twice over: the land loses soil it needs, and the river gains material it cannot carry away. run-off flows across the ground carries soil off the land rivers become silted up and blocked"],[1,"y7c02-r039","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain how a pool of water on the road disappears on a warm day.","2.4","Energy from the Sun is transferred to the water, so the particles in the liquid move more. Some particles at the surface gain enough energy to break free from the attractive forces holding them together, and they escape into the air as water vapour, an invisible gas. This is evaporation, and the warmer the water the more quickly it happens, so on a warm day the pool shrinks until no liquid is left. energy from the Sun particles move more break free from the attractive forces escape as water vapour"],[1,"y7c02-r040","Short answer","The word atom means 'cannot be divided'. Explain why Democritus chose that name for his idea.","2.5","Democritus suggested that everything was made up of tiny pieces, and his argument was that if you kept cutting matter into smaller and smaller parts you would eventually reach a piece that could not be cut any further. The name records that idea: an atom was, by definition, the end point of dividing. Naming the idea this way made the claim clear, that matter is not endlessly divisible. tiny pieces of matter cannot be divided further end point of cutting matter is not endlessly divisible"],[1,"y7c02-r041","Short answer","Democritus had no microscopes, yet his idea survived for over 2000 years. Explain why scientists now say atoms really do exist.","2.5","Today we can even see some of the large kinds of atom, using special microscopes called scanning tunnelling microscopes, and photographs show the atoms in materials such as carbon nanotubes. Before that, the idea was supported indirectly because it explained how matter behaves. Direct images turned a long-standing explanation into something observed, which is why the claim changed from a suggestion to an accepted fact. scanning tunnelling microscopes atoms can now be imaged previously supported indirectly explanation became observation"],[1,"y7c02-r042","Short answer","Explain why there are the same number of naturally occurring elements as there are naturally occurring kinds of atom.","2.5","A substance made of just one kind of atom is called an element, so each kind of atom defines exactly one element and no more. Scientists have discovered 94 different types of atom that occur naturally in the universe, so there must be 94 naturally occurring elements. The two numbers match because the definition ties them together one to one. element is made of one kind of atom 94 naturally occurring types of atom one kind of atom gives one element"],[1,"y7c02-r043","Short answer","Explain why different elements have different properties.","2.5","Each type of atom has different properties, and an element is made of just one kind of atom. So whatever the atom is like, the element built from it will behave that way in bulk. Gold is made only of gold atoms and silver only of silver atoms, which is why the two metals look and behave differently even though both are elements made of a single kind of atom. each type of atom has different properties element is made of one kind of atom bulk properties follow from the atom"],[1,"y7c02-r044","Short answer","In oxygen and sulfur the atoms join together into small particles, yet both are still elements. Explain why.","2.5","An element is a substance made of just one kind of atom, and that is still true when the atoms are joined. An oxygen particle is made from two oxygen atoms and a sulfur particle from eight sulfur atoms, so every atom in the particle is the same kind. Joining only becomes bonding into a compound when two or more different kinds of atom are involved, which is not the case here. element means one kind of atom oxygen particle is two oxygen atoms sulfur particle is eight sulfur atoms all the same kind"],[1,"y7c02-r045","Short answer","Explain why the Periodic Table is arranged in rows and columns rather than as a simple list.","2.5","The rows are called periods and the columns are called groups, and the table is organised so that elements with similar properties are close together. A simple list could only put the elements in one order at a time, but a grid lets two patterns show at once: mass increasing across each period, and shared properties running down each group. That is what makes the arrangement useful rather than merely tidy. rows are periods columns are groups similar properties close together a grid shows two patterns at once"],[1,"y7c02-r046","Short answer","Explain how the order of the elements in the Periodic Table is decided.","2.5","The atoms are organised so that, as you read across each row from left to right, the atoms increase in mass. Hydrogen atoms have the smallest mass, then helium atoms, then lithium atoms, and so on. The order is therefore a measured property of the atoms themselves, not alphabetical or based on when each element was discovered, which is why the same table is used everywhere. atoms increase in mass across a period hydrogen smallest, then helium, then lithium order is a measured property"],[1,"y7c02-r047","Short answer","Explain why the rule about upper and lower case letters in element symbols matters.","2.5","The first letter of the symbol is always upper case and the second letter, if there is one, is always lower case. Without this rule CO and Co could not be told apart, yet one means carbon joined to oxygen and the other means the element cobalt. Because formulae are built by writing symbols next to each other, the capital letter is what marks where one element's symbol ends and the next begins. first letter upper case second letter lower case CO and Co mean different things capitals mark where a symbol starts"],[1,"y7c02-r048","Short answer","Explain why the symbol for sodium is Na rather than S or So.","2.5","Sometimes the symbol is taken from another language, and the symbol for sodium is Na, from the old Latin name natrium. The English first letter is not available because S is already the symbol for sulfur, and symbols must be unique or a formula could mean two things. Using the Latin name solves the clash while keeping the symbol short. Na from the Latin natrium S already taken by sulfur symbols must be unique"],[1,"y7c02-r049","Short answer","Explain the difference between a substance made of more than one type of atom and a compound.","2.6","Many substances are made up of more than one type of atom, but that alone does not make a compound. If the different types of atom are joined tightly together, then the substance is a compound; the chemical term for two atoms joining tightly together is bonding. If the different atoms are merely present together without being bonded, the substance is a mixture. So it is the bonding, not the variety of atoms, that decides. more than one type of atom is not enough atoms must be bonded bonding otherwise it is a mixture"],[1,"y7c02-r050","Short answer","Explain why sodium chloride is safe to eat although sodium and chlorine are both dangerous.","2.6","When two different elements are bonded, they completely lose the properties of the individual elements, and the compound has totally new properties. Sodium chloride is not at all like either sodium or chlorine, so its behaviour cannot be predicted by adding up theirs. The atoms are still the same atoms, but bonded together they form a substance that has to be judged on its own properties. bonded elements lose their own properties compound has totally new properties cannot predict from the elements"],[1,"y7c02-r051","Short answer","A student writes 'sulfur calcium' as the name of a compound. Explain what is wrong and give the correct name.","2.6","If the compound contains a metal, then the name of the metal comes first in the name of the compound, and calcium is the metal here, so it must come first. If the compound contains a non-metal, the name of the non-metal is usually changed, and when two elements form a compound the name often ends in 'ide'. The correct name is therefore calcium sulfide. metal comes first calcium is the metal non-metal name changes calcium sulfide"],[1,"y7c02-r052","Short answer","Explain why calcium carbonate ends in 'ate' while calcium sulfide ends in 'ide'.","2.6","When two elements form a compound the name often ends in 'ide', so calcium sulfide contains just calcium and sulfur. Some compounds contain two different elements plus a third element, oxygen, and these compounds often have names ending with 'ate'. Calcium carbonate is a compound of calcium, carbon and oxygen, so the ending tells you that oxygen is present as well. ide means two elements ate means two elements plus oxygen calcium carbonate contains oxygen"],[1,"y7c02-r053","Short answer","Explain why carbon monoxide is not simply called carbon oxide.","2.6","Sometimes the name of a compound tells you how many of each kind of atom are bonded together, and 'mon' or 'mono' means one. Carbon monoxide particles are made up of one carbon atom joined to one oxygen atom, while carbon dioxide particles are made up of one carbon atom joined to two oxygen atoms. Both contain only carbon and oxygen, so without the number words the two names would be identical for two very different substances. mono means one carbon monoxide has one oxygen carbon dioxide has two names must distinguish them"],[1,"y7c02-r054","Short answer","Explain how you can tell from a particle diagram whether a substance is an element or a compound.","2.6","Particle diagrams show which atoms of which elements make up the particle, usually by using a different colour or size for each kind of atom. If there are different kinds of atom bonded together, then it is a compound. If every atom in the particle is the same kind it is an element, which is why oxygen is an element even though its particle contains two atoms joined together. look at the kinds of atom different kinds bonded means compound all the same kind means element number of atoms is irrelevant"],[1,"y7c02-r055","Short answer","Explain what the small number in a formula such as H2O tells you, and what it does not.","2.6","The little number written below and to the right of a symbol tells you how many atoms of that element are found in the particle, so H2O has two hydrogen atoms. If there is no number, it means there is just one atom of that element, so there is one oxygen atom. The number counts atoms in one particle; it says nothing about how much of the substance you have. small number counts atoms of that element no number means one atom counts atoms in one particle only"],[1,"y7c02-r056","Short answer","Explain why confusing the symbols C and Ca changes the meaning of a formula.","2.6","C is the symbol for carbon and Ca is the symbol for calcium, so they stand for different elements with different properties. A formula is built from the symbols of the elements that are bonded together in the compound, so swapping one symbol for another describes a different substance entirely. CO would become CaO, which is a compound of a metal and oxygen rather than of two non-metals. C is carbon Ca is calcium different elements the formula would describe a different substance"],[1,"y7c02-r057","Short answer","Sodium hydroxide is NaOH and potassium hydroxide is KOH. Explain what this pattern tells you about all hydroxides.","2.6","Both formulae contain O and H alongside the metal, so all hydroxides must contain oxygen and hydrogen. The part of the name that stays the same, 'hydroxide', corresponds to the part of the formula that stays the same, OH, while the part that changes, sodium or potassium, corresponds to Na or K. Reading names and formulae side by side like this lets you predict that LiOH is lithium hydroxide. OH appears in both hydroxides contain oxygen and hydrogen the changing part is the metal predict LiOH is lithium hydroxide"],[1,"y7c02-r058","Short answer","Iron is magnetic but iron sulfide is not. Explain what this shows about compounds.","2.7","Iron is hard, grey, strong, conducts heat and electricity and is magnetic. When iron and sulfur are heated they combine together to form the compound iron sulfide, and iron sulfide is not magnetic and does not conduct heat or electricity. This shows that when two different elements are bonded they completely lose the properties of the individual elements, and the compound has totally new properties. iron is magnetic iron sulfide is not magnetic bonded elements lose their properties compound has new properties"],[1,"y7c02-r059","Short answer","Marcus mixes iron and sulfur; Sofia heats them together. Explain how a magnet could tell you who has a compound.","2.7","When you mix iron and sulfur together you make a mixture of two elements, and in a mixture the substances keep their own properties, so the iron in Marcus's beaker is still magnetic and would be pulled out by a magnet. Heating makes iron atoms and sulfur atoms bond together to form the compound iron sulfide, which is not magnetic, so nothing in Sofia's tube would be attracted. The magnet therefore distinguishes mixture from compound. mixture keeps the original properties iron in the mixture is still magnetic iron sulfide is not magnetic magnet distinguishes them"],[1,"y7c02-r060","Short answer","Explain what scientists mean by a pure substance, and why bottled mineral water is not pure in that sense.","2.7","In science, the word pure is used to describe something that only contains a single substance, so pure water contains only water with no other substances mixed with it. A bottle of mineral water contains a mixture of water and other substances, because the minerals are dissolved in the water. In everyday language 'pure' suggests clean or healthy, but in science it is a statement about composition only. pure means a single substance only mineral water contains dissolved minerals it is a mixture everyday meaning differs"],[1,"y7c02-r061","Short answer","Explain how the particles in a mixture differ from those in a compound.","2.7","A mixture is made up of different kinds of particle that are mixed together, and the mixture may be of elements, compounds or both, but nothing is bonded. In a compound two or more different kinds of atom are bonded, so a single particle contains both kinds joined tightly. A particle diagram therefore shows a mixture as separate particles side by side, and a compound as different atoms joined within one particle. mixture has different particles side by side nothing is bonded compound has different atoms joined in one particle"],[1,"y7c02-r062","Short answer","Explain why air is called a mixture rather than a compound.","2.7","Air contains nitrogen, oxygen, carbon dioxide, water vapour and small quantities of some other gases, and these are simply present together rather than bonded into one substance. Because they are not bonded, each keeps its own properties, and the proportions can change without anything new being made. A compound would have a fixed composition and totally new properties, and air has neither. air contains several gases not bonded together each keeps its properties proportions can change"],[1,"y7c02-r063","Short answer","Explain why the composition of air is not exactly the same everywhere and at all times.","2.7","The composition of air varies because the amount of water vapour changes all the time, depending on the weather, and the amounts of carbon dioxide and other gases also change. This can be a result of natural emissions, such as when animals and plants produce carbon dioxide when they respire, while plants also use carbon dioxide when they make food, which removes it from the air. Pollution changes the composition too. water vapour changes with the weather respiration adds carbon dioxide plants remove carbon dioxide pollution changes it"],[1,"y7c02-r064","Short answer","The composition of air has changed over millions of years. Explain why this supports calling air a mixture.","2.7","At one time there was much less oxygen in the atmosphere, so the proportions of the gases in air have altered over time. A compound has a fixed composition, because its atoms are bonded in set numbers, so its make-up cannot drift. Something whose composition can change while it remains the same material must be a mixture, which is exactly what the history of the atmosphere shows. oxygen was once much less proportions have changed compounds have fixed composition so air is a mixture"],[1,"y7c02-r065","Short answer","Explain why mineral water is described as a solution.","2.7","The label on a bottle of mineral water lists many minerals, so there is more than just water in the bottle; it contains a mixture of water and other substances. The minerals are dissolved in the water, which means their particles are spread evenly among the water particles rather than settling out or forming a separate layer. A mixture in which one substance is dissolved in another like this is called a solution. minerals are dissolved in the water spread evenly do not settle out a dissolved mixture is a solution"],[1,"y7c02-r066","Short answer","Explain the difference between a compound made of two elements and a mixture of the same two elements.","2.7","In a compound, two or more elements are bonded together to make a new product, so the atoms are joined and the substance has totally new properties. In a mixture, two or more elements are mixed together but they do not form a new product, so each element keeps its own properties and could in principle be separated again. Iron and sulfur show both cases: mixed they stay iron and sulfur, heated they become iron sulfide. compound has elements bonded into a new product mixture has no new product mixture keeps original properties iron and sulfur example"],[1,"y7c02-o002","Short answer","Name the three states of matter.","2.1","Solids, liquids and gases. solid liquid gas"],[1,"y7c02-o003","Short answer","What do scientists call the different ways in which the states of matter behave?","2.1","Their properties. properties"],[1,"y7c02-o004","Short answer","Give three properties of solids.","2.1","Solids keep the same shape, take up the same amount of space and keep the same volume. shape volume space"],[1,"y7c02-o019","Short answer","What is the melting point of a substance?","2.2","The temperature at which the solid melts and becomes a liquid. temperature melts solid"],[1,"y7c02-o020","Short answer","What is evaporation?","2.2","It is when liquid water gradually changes into water vapour, an invisible gas. liquid water vapour gas"],[1,"y7c02-o030","Short answer","What happens to the size of a solid when it is heated?","2.3","It expands, which means it gets bigger. expands bigger"],[1,"y7c02-o031","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why a solid expands when it is heated.","2.3","The particles vibrate more when heat energy is transferred to them, and vibrating more makes them take up more space. vibrate more energy more space"],[1,"y7c02-o032","Short answer","What happens to a solid if it is heated more strongly still?","2.3","It melts and becomes a liquid, because even more heat energy is transferred to the particles. melts liquid energy"],[1,"y7c02-o041","Short answer","About how much of your body is made up of water?","2.4","At least 60% of it. 60 per cent percent"],[1,"y7c02-o053","Short answer","What did the Greek philosopher Democritus suggest over 2000 years ago?","2.5","That everything was made up of tiny pieces. tiny pieces everything"],[1,"y7c02-o054","Short answer","What does the word atom mean?","2.5","It means cannot be divided. cannot be divided divided"],[1,"y7c02-o055","Short answer","What kind of microscope lets us see some of the larger kinds of atom?","2.5","A scanning tunnelling microscope. scanning tunnelling microscope"],[1,"y7c02-o066","Short answer","What is a compound?","2.6","A substance in which different types of atom are joined tightly together. different types of atom joined bonded"],[1,"y7c02-o067","Short answer","What is the chemical term for atoms joining tightly together?","2.6","Bonding. bonding bonded"],[1,"y7c02-o080","Short answer","Describe the properties of iron.","2.7","Iron is a metal. It is hard, grey, strong, conducts heat and electricity and is magnetic. metal grey conducts magnetic"],[1,"y7c02-o081","Short answer","Describe the properties of sulfur.","2.7","Sulfur is a non-metal. It is yellow, brittle, does not conduct heat or electricity and is not magnetic. non-metal yellow brittle not magnetic"],[1,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which state of matter can be compressed easily?","2.1","Gas Solid Liquid Plasma"],[1,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","In solids, the particles are held together strongly and are tightly ____.","2.1","packed"],[1,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","Why do solids have a fixed shape?","2.1","Particles are held together strongly Particles move past one another Particles are far apart Particles spread out by themselves"],[1,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","The temperature at which a solid melts is called the ____ point.","2.2","melting"],[1,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What is the process called when water on the ground changes to an invisible gas?","2.2","Condensation Evaporation Freezing Boiling"],[1,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the curve formed at the top of a liquid in a measuring cylinder?","2.2","Meniscus Thermometer Gauze Beaker"],[1,"qwen-06-02","Gap fill","When a solid melts, the particles can slide past one another because the ____ between them are not strong enough to hold a fixed pattern.","2.3","attractive forces"],[1,"qwen-06-04","Gap fill","When gas particles reach a cold surface, they lose energy and form a liquid. This process is called ____.","2.3","condensation"],[1,"qwen-06-06","Gap fill","The process where water evaporates from plants into the atmosphere is called ____.","2.4","transpiration"],[1,"qwen-06-08","Gap fill","Water that flows directly across the ground into rivers and oceans is called surface ____.","2.4","run-off"],[1,"qwen-06-10","Gap fill","The word 'atom' means 'cannot be ____'.","2.5","divided"],[1,"qwen-06-12","Gap fill","Special microscopes used to see some large kinds of atoms are called scanning tunnelling ____.","2.5","microscopes"],[1,"qwen-07-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of a substance made up of just one kind of atom?","2.5","Compound Element Mixture Particle"],[1,"qwen-07-02","Gap fill","In most elements, such as gold, atoms are packed ____ together.","2.5","closely"],[1,"qwen-07-03","Multiple choice","How many oxygen atoms make up one particle of oxygen?","2.5","One Two Eight Four"],[1,"qwen-07-05","Multiple choice","What is the chemical symbol for sodium?","2.5","S So Na N"],[1,"qwen-07-06","Gap fill","The chemical term for two atoms joining tightly together is ____.","2.6","bonding"],[1,"qwen-07-07","Multiple choice","Why is sodium chloride different from the elements sodium and chlorine?","2.6","It has totally new properties It is made of only one atom type It is a mixture, not a compound It has the same properties as sodium"],[1,"qwen-07-09","Multiple choice","What does the prefix 'di' mean in a chemical name like carbon dioxide?","2.6","One Two Three Four"],[1,"qwen-07-11","Multiple choice","Which statement about iron sulfide is true?","2.7","It is magnetic It conducts electricity It does not conduct heat or electricity It is yellow and brittle"],[1,"qwen-08-01","Multiple choice","What happens to the iron and sulfur when they are mixed together in a beaker?","2.7","They combine to form iron sulfide They keep their original properties and do not change chemically The sulfur turns into a gas The iron dissolves in the sulfur"],[1,"qwen-08-02","Gap fill","In a mixture of iron and sulfur, you can use a ____ to remove the iron filings.","2.7","magnet"],[1,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a gas?","2.1","Flour Water Air Sugar"],[1,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Flour is a solid, but it is in ____ form.","2.1","powder"],[1,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a solid?","2.1","Water Air Butane gas Flour"],[1,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","Marcus heats water and records its temperature. Which property of the water is he investigating?","2.2","How its temperature changes as it is heated How much it weighs before heating What colour it turns when heated How much salt it can dissolve"],[1,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","Marcus carries out an experiment to investigate what happens to the temperature of ____ when it is heated.","2.2","water"],[1,"qwenw-02-03","Multiple choice","How often does Marcus measure the temperature of the water?","2.2","Every two minutes Every minute Every five minutes Only at the start"],[1,"qwenw-03-02","Gap fill","If the particles in a liquid lose enough energy, the liquid will change state and become a ____.","2.3","solid"],[1,"qwenw-03-03","Multiple choice","For a solid to melt, what must the particles do?","2.3","gain energy lose energy stop moving completely vibrate less"],[1,"qwenw-03-04","Gap fill","The process where a gas is cooled to form a liquid is called ____.","2.3","condensation"],[1,"qwenw-03-05","Multiple choice","Which state of matter can be compressed?","2.3","gas solid liquid plasma"],[1,"qwenw-03-06","Gap fill","In the water cycle, the process that takes place at stage d needs energy from the ____.","2.4","sun"],[1,"qwenw-03-07","Multiple choice","What is the symbol for the element sodium?","2.4","Na S So N"],[1,"qwenw-03-08","Gap fill","The symbol for the element magnesium is ____.","2.4","Mg"],[1,"qwenw-03-09","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a compound?","2.6","Water Oxygen Calcium Sulfur"],[1,"qwenw-03-10","Gap fill","A compound is made up of more than one type of ____.","2.6","atom"],[1,"qwenw-03-11","Multiple choice","Which elements are found in potassium chloride?","2.6","Potassium and chlorine Sodium and chloride Magnesium and oxygen Iron and sulfur"],[1,"qwenw-04-01","Multiple choice","What is the main difference between a compound and a mixture?","2.7","In a compound the elements are bonded together to make a new product; in a mixture they are not In a compound the elements are only mixed; in a mixture they are bonded together Compounds contain only one element, while mixtures contain two or more elements Mixtures are always solids, while compounds are always liquids or gases"],[1,"qwenw-04-02","Gap fill","In a mixture, two or more elements are mixed together, but they do not form a new ____.","2.7","product"],[1,"qwenw-04-03","Multiple choice","Marcus mixes iron filings and sulfur in a beaker. What is the result?","2.7","A new compound is formed. A mixture of iron and sulfur is formed. The iron turns into sulfur. The sulfur disappears."],[2,"y7c03-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the place in this diagram where the strength of gravity is greatest.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the planet you live on.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the ringed planet that has the same strength of gravity as Earth.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the planet closest to the Sun.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h6","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the second planet from the Sun.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h7","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the fourth planet from the Sun.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h8","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the seventh planet from the Sun.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f01h9","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the planet furthest from the Sun.","3.1",""],[2,"y7c03-f02a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows the Earth from above the North Pole, with the Moon to one side. Name the tide labelled {n}.","3.4",""],[2,"y7c03-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the words that name the tide on the side of the Earth closest to the Moon.","3.4",""],[2,"y7c03-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the words that name the tide on the side of the Earth furthest from the Moon.","3.4",""],[2,"y7c03-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the words that name the tide at the top of this diagram.","3.4",""],[2,"y7c03-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the energy the bus has because it is moving.","3.5",""],[2,"y7c03-f03h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the energy stored in the hot engine.","3.5",""],[2,"y7c03-f03h5","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the energy transferred from the noisy engine.","3.5",""],[2,"y7c03-r001","Short answer","A pen and the Earth both cause forces of gravity, yet you never see objects fall towards a pen. Explain why.","3.1","All objects, even pens and pencils, cause forces of gravity, but objects with small mass cause very weak forces of gravity. The force from a pen is so weak that we do not notice other objects being attracted to it, while objects with large mass, such as the Earth, cause strong forces. So the difference is one of size of force, not of whether the force exists at all. all objects cause gravity small mass gives a very weak force large mass gives a strong force too weak to notice"],[2,"y7c03-r002","Short answer","Explain why people at the South Pole do not fall off the Earth.","3.1","The force of gravity caused by an object acts towards the centre of the object, so at every position around the Earth gravity pulls towards the centre. For someone at the South Pole, that direction is the one they call down, just as it is for someone at the North Pole, even though the two directions are opposite in space. There is no single direction called down for the whole Earth, so no one is upside down. gravity acts towards the centre of the Earth down is different at each place no single down direction"],[2,"y7c03-r003","Short answer","Explain what happens to the force of gravity on a spacecraft as it travels away from Earth.","3.1","The strength of gravity decreases as you go further from a large object such as Earth, so the force of gravity from the Earth acting on the spacecraft gets steadily weaker with distance. It does not switch off at some boundary; it simply keeps getting smaller. This is why a spacecraft needs its largest push at the start of the journey, when the Earth's pull on it is strongest. gravity decreases with distance gets weaker gradually does not switch off strongest near the Earth"],[2,"y7c03-r004","Short answer","Explain why a book resting on a desk does not move, even though gravity pulls it down.","3.1","The weight of the book pulls it down, but the desk pushes up on it with a contact force. The contact force acts up from any surface to support an object, and it is always equal to the weight of the object when the surface is not moving. Because the two forces are equal in size and opposite in direction, they balance, so there is no overall force and the book stays still. weight pulls down contact force pushes up equal when the surface is not moving forces balance"],[2,"y7c03-r005","Short answer","Explain why a car parked on soft sand sinks but the same car on a road does not.","3.1","A surface supports an object by providing a contact force equal to the object's weight. Sometimes the weight of the object is larger than the contact force the surface can provide, and if this happens the surface will break, or the object will sink into the surface. Soft sand can only push up with a small force, so the car sinks; a road can provide a contact force as large as the car's weight, so it does not. contact force must equal the weight sand provides too small a force object sinks road can provide enough"],[2,"y7c03-r006","Short answer","A car's wheels need a contact force of 24000 N each but the mud first provides only 2000 N. Explain why the wheels sink and then stop.","3.1","At first the contact force from the mud is far smaller than the force needed to support each wheel, so the weight is larger than the contact force and the wheel sinks into the surface. The contact force from the mud increases with depth, so as the wheel goes deeper the upward force grows. Sinking stops at the depth where the contact force has risen to match the force needed, because the forces then balance. weight larger than contact force wheel sinks contact force increases with depth stops when forces balance"],[2,"y7c03-r007","Short answer","'The weight of my bag is 10 kg' is a sentence scientists would not accept. Explain what is wrong with it.","3.1","Weight is the force of gravity on an object and it is measured in newtons, while mass is the quantity of matter in an object and is measured in kilograms. The sentence attaches a mass unit to the word weight, so it mixes up two different quantities. The correct sentence is that the mass of the bag is 10 kg; its weight would be 100 N on Earth. weight is a force in newtons mass is matter in kilograms wrong unit for the quantity mass of the bag is 10 kg"],[2,"y7c03-r008","Short answer","An astronaut takes a 2 kg tool to the Moon. Explain what changes and what does not.","3.1","The mass of an object does not change, because mass is the quantity of matter in the object and no matter is added or removed by the journey. The weight of an object changes when the strength of gravity changes, and the Moon's gravity is weaker than the Earth's, so the tool weighs less there. The astronaut would find the tool just as hard to shake from side to side but easier to lift. mass stays the same quantity of matter unchanged weight changes with gravity strength weighs less on the Moon"],[2,"y7c03-r009","Short answer","Explain what the figure 10 N/kg actually tells you.","3.1","On Earth, the force of gravity is 10 N on every 1 kg of mass, and the force of gravity that pulls on 1 kg tells you the strength of gravity. So 10 N/kg is read as ten newtons per kilogram and it is a rate: it says how much weight each kilogram of matter is given. That is why weight is calculated as mass multiplied by 10, and why the same mass has a different weight where the strength of gravity is different. 10 newtons per kilogram force on each kilogram of mass a rate not a total used to calculate weight"],[2,"y7c03-r010","Short answer","When you stand on bathroom scales you see a value in kilograms. Explain whether the scales measure your mass or your weight.","3.1","The scales actually respond to the force pressing down on them, which is your weight in newtons. They then divide by 10 N/kg and display the answer in kilograms, because on Earth the strength of gravity is 10 N/kg. So the quantity measured is weight and the quantity displayed is mass, and the conversion only works because the scales assume they are on Earth. scales respond to the force, the weight display mass in kilograms assumes 10 N/kg would be wrong on another planet"],[2,"y7c03-r011","Short answer","Explain why a larger force is needed for a spacecraft to travel from Earth to the Moon than to come back.","3.1","The strength of gravity at the Earth's surface is much greater than at the Moon's surface, because the Earth has far more mass. Leaving Earth therefore means pushing against a much stronger gravitational pull than leaving the Moon does, so a much larger force is needed for the outward journey. The mass of the spacecraft is the same on both journeys, so the difference comes entirely from the strength of gravity. Earth's gravity is much stronger Earth has more mass must push against a stronger pull same spacecraft mass"],[2,"y7c03-r012","Short answer","The strength of gravity is 25 N/kg on Jupiter and 8.7 N/kg on Uranus. Explain what this means for a 50 kg astronaut.","3.1","Weight is mass multiplied by the strength of gravity, so the astronaut would weigh 50 times 25, which is 1250 N on Jupiter, and 50 times 8.7, which is 435 N on Uranus. The astronaut's mass stays 50 kg in both places because the quantity of matter has not changed. Jupiter's greater strength of gravity comes from its much greater mass, which is why the same body is pulled almost three times as hard there. weight = mass x strength of gravity 1250 N on Jupiter 435 N on Uranus mass stays 50 kg"],[2,"y7c03-r013","Short answer","No one saw the Solar System form. Explain how scientists can still make claims about how it happened.","3.2","Observe means to watch something happening, and when the Solar System formed there were no people to observe it. Scientists can still work on the problem in two ways: they can look for evidence, in the form of facts from observations or experiments, to support a theory, and they can think of a testable theory, called an hypothesis, and then look for evidence to support it. Both routes replace direct watching with evidence that must fit. no one could observe it look for evidence to support a theory propose a testable hypothesis evidence must fit"],[2,"y7c03-r014","Short answer","All the planets orbit the Sun in the same direction and in the same plane. Explain why these facts count as evidence for the flat-disc hypothesis.","3.2","If each planet had formed separately and been captured later, there would be no reason for them all to share a direction or a plane, so those features would be coincidences. A hypothesis that the planets formed together from one spinning flat disc of dust and gas predicts exactly these shared features. Scientists use these facts as evidence because the hypothesis explains them while the alternative leaves them unexplained. shared direction and plane would be coincidences one spinning disc predicts them hypothesis explains the facts"],[2,"y7c03-r015","Short answer","Explain what it means to say that all the planets orbit the Sun in the same plane.","3.2","Objects that are in the same plane could all be placed on the same flat surface, just like objects on a tabletop. Applied to the Solar System this means the orbits are not tilted at random angles to one another but lie close to a single flat sheet, which is why the Solar System looks flat. If the orbits had random tilts the system would look like a ball of paths rather than a disc. same plane means on one flat surface orbits not tilted at random Solar System looks flat"],[2,"y7c03-r016","Short answer","Explain how watching other parts of space helps scientists study something that happened 4.6 billion years ago.","3.2","Scientists can see distant stars forming in other parts of space, from clouds of dust and gas called nebulae, and some young stars can also be seen with a flat disc of dust around them. Because these systems are at different stages, looking at many of them shows the sequence even though no single one can be watched from start to finish. Scientists think our Solar System was formed this way. stars forming in nebulae can be seen now young stars with discs of dust many systems at different stages sequence pieced together"],[2,"y7c03-r017","Short answer","Explain what a scientific model is and why computers are used to model the birth of the Solar System.","3.2","A model is a way of representing something that is difficult to observe directly, and scientists cannot observe a star or Solar System forming in an experiment. So they put many of the known laws of physics into a computer program, and the computer uses this information to predict what will happen, starting with a cloud of dust and gas. The prediction, a star surrounded by planets, can then be compared with what is actually seen. model represents what cannot be observed directly laws of physics put into a program computer predicts the outcome compare with observations"],[2,"y7c03-r018","Short answer","Explain why a clump of dust and gas in space grows faster and faster once it starts to form.","3.2","All the particles of dust and gas have their own weak gravity and pull on each other. As they stick together, their total mass increases, and as their mass increases, so does the strength of their gravity. That means they attract more dust and gas with a stronger force, which adds still more mass. Each round of growth makes the next round quicker, so the process speeds up as the ball gets bigger. particles pull on each other mass increases as they stick together greater mass means stronger gravity attracts more, so it speeds up"],[2,"y7c03-r019","Short answer","Explain what decides whether a growing ball of dust and gas becomes a star or a planet.","3.2","If the ball gets beyond a certain size, it will get hot enough to become a star; otherwise it will become a planet. So the deciding factor is how much mass it manages to gather, because more mass means stronger gravity squeezing the material and a higher temperature at the centre. Scientists think the planet Jupiter almost reached the size to be a star, which shows the boundary is a matter of degree. size and mass gathered beyond a certain size it gets hot enough otherwise it becomes a planet Jupiter nearly reached it"],[2,"y7c03-r020","Short answer","Venus spins in the opposite direction to the other planets. Explain why this does not simply prove the hypothesis wrong.","3.2","Most of the facts about the Solar System support or agree with the hypothesis, and only this one seems to contradict it, where contradict means to go against something. A hypothesis is judged on the whole body of evidence, not on a single fact, and an unusual case may have its own later explanation such as a collision. Scientists therefore keep the hypothesis while treating Venus as a problem still to be solved. most facts support the hypothesis contradict means to go against judged on all the evidence the exception needs its own explanation"],[2,"y7c03-r021","Short answer","It takes millions of years to form a star or a planet. Explain why this makes the process hard to study.","3.2","Scientists can see stars forming in clouds of dust and gas that are visible from Earth, but the complete process takes millions of years, far longer than any person or any instrument will last. No one can watch a single star from cloud to finished system, so the process cannot be followed directly. Evidence has to come from many objects caught at different stages, and from computer models. process takes millions of years longer than any observer can watch cannot follow one star through use many objects and models"],[2,"y7c03-r022","Short answer","Explain why the Sun, rather than any planet, holds the Solar System together.","3.3","Objects with more mass have more gravity, and the Sun is the object with the largest mass in the Solar System; in fact the mass of the Sun is more than the mass of all the other planets added together. Its gravity is therefore far stronger than any planet's, which is why it holds all the planets in their orbits rather than being pulled around by them. more mass means more gravity Sun has the largest mass more than all the planets together holds the planets in orbit"],[2,"y7c03-r023","Short answer","Neptune is 30 times further from the Sun than Earth. Explain how the Sun can still hold it in orbit.","3.3","The Sun's gravity gets weaker as the distance from the Sun increases, so the pull on Neptune is much weaker than the pull on Earth. But weaker is not the same as absent: the force is still large enough to keep Neptune turning, partly because Neptune's mass is about 17 times the mass of Earth. So although the Sun's gravity gets weaker, it is strong enough to hold Neptune in orbit. gravity weakens with distance weaker is not zero Neptune has large mass still strong enough to hold it"],[2,"y7c03-r024","Short answer","Explain what would happen to a planet if the Sun's gravity suddenly stopped acting on it.","3.3","To keep any object moving in a circle, there needs to be a force causing it to turn, and the force of gravity from the Sun that acts on a planet always acts towards the Sun. If this force did not act in this way, the planet would travel off in a straight line into space. So gravity is not what makes the planet move; it is what keeps bending its path into an orbit. a force is needed to keep turning gravity acts towards the Sun without it the planet goes straight gravity bends the path"],[2,"y7c03-r025","Short answer","Explain why Mercury travels faster in its orbit than Earth does.","3.3","Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, so it has the strongest pull from the Sun's gravity. A stronger pull turns the planet more sharply, and this causes Mercury to orbit with the highest speed of all the planets: its average speed around the Sun is 170000 km/h against about 100000 km/h for Earth. Distance from the Sun therefore sets both the strength of the pull and the orbital speed. closest to the Sun strongest pull from the Sun's gravity highest orbital speed 170000 km/h against 100000 km/h"],[2,"y7c03-r026","Short answer","Explain why air resistance always slows a moving object rather than speeding it up.","3.3","Air resistance is caused by a moving object having to push against the particles in the air, and it acts in the opposite direction to movement. Because the force opposes the direction the object is going, it can only reduce the speed. The faster an object moves, the greater the air resistance on the object, so the effect grows exactly when the object is going fastest. object pushes against air particles acts opposite to the movement opposing force reduces speed greater at higher speed"],[2,"y7c03-r027","Short answer","Explain how changing the shape of an aeroplane wing helps the aeroplane slow down after landing.","3.3","The shape of the wing can be changed to produce extra air resistance, and air resistance acts in the opposite direction to movement. Making the wing present a larger, blunter surface means more air particles have to be pushed aside each second, so the backward force is bigger. A bigger backward force slows the aeroplane more quickly, which is why the wing is reshaped at exactly that moment. wing shape changed to increase air resistance acts opposite to movement more air pushed aside slows the aeroplane faster"],[2,"y7c03-r028","Short answer","Explain why the planets do not gradually slow down and stop.","3.3","On Earth all objects that move have forces acting on them to slow them down, and air resistance is one of those forces. Earth and the other planets are moving in a vacuum, which is a space where there are very few particles, so there is no air resistance to slow them down. The only force acting on the planets is from gravity, and that acts sideways to the motion, turning the planet rather than slowing it. planets move in a vacuum no air resistance only gravity acts gravity turns rather than slows"],[2,"y7c03-r029","Short answer","The Juno probe reached a record speed near Jupiter. Explain why the same probe could not reach that speed on Earth.","3.3","The Juno probe would have a lot of air resistance if it were moving on Earth, because air resistance is caused by pushing against the particles in the air and the faster an object moves the greater that force becomes. In space there is a vacuum and no air resistance, so nothing opposes the motion and the probe keeps gaining speed. On Earth the growing resistance would stop the speed rising long before that value. air resistance on Earth grows as speed increases vacuum in space nothing opposes the motion"],[2,"y7c03-r030","Short answer","Some spacecraft launched in the 1970s have no engines yet keep a constant speed. Explain how this is possible.","3.3","These spacecraft are now far outside the Solar System, moving through a vacuum where there is no air resistance. An object only changes speed when a force acts along its direction of travel, and out there essentially nothing does, so the speed stays the same without any engine. On Earth the same spacecraft would slow down immediately, because air resistance would act in the opposite direction to movement. no air resistance in space no force along the direction of travel speed stays constant no engine needed"],[2,"y7c03-r031","Short answer","Explain why a bar graph is the right way to show the orbital speeds of the planets.","3.3","A bar graph is used when the horizontal axis carries separate named categories rather than a continuous measurement, and the planets are separate objects, not points along a scale. The bars are spaced evenly and not touching, which shows that there are no values in between. A line graph would suggest that a planet could exist at any point between Mercury and Venus, which is not true. planets are separate categories not a continuous scale bars evenly spaced and not touching a line would imply values in between"],[2,"y7c03-r032","Short answer","Explain what a tide is and how it differs from waves on the surface of the sea.","3.4","The depth is the distance from the surface of the water to the bottom of the ocean, and in some parts of the world that depth changes by several metres during the day. This change in depth of the water is called a tide. Unlike waves, which come and go in seconds at the surface, a tide is a slow change of the whole water level, with high and low tides about six hours apart. tide is a change in the depth of the water several metres during the day slow change of the whole level six hours between high and low"],[2,"y7c03-r033","Short answer","Explain what the tidal range is and why it differs between the Bay of Fundy and the Mediterranean.","3.4","The difference in depth of the water between high and low tides is the tidal range. The largest tidal range in the world is 16.3 m in the Bay of Fundy in Canada, while some of the smallest are in the Caribbean and Mediterranean seas. So the same tidal forces act on the whole Earth but the range they produce depends on the local shape of the coast and how the water can pile up there. tidal range is the difference between high and low tide 16.3 m in the Bay of Fundy very small in the Mediterranean depends on the local coast"],[2,"y7c03-r034","Short answer","The land itself rises and falls by about 30 cm each day. Explain why this earth tide is much smaller than the ocean tide.","3.4","Tides are caused by the pull of the Moon's gravity acting on the Earth. As the oceans are made from water, the gravity from the Moon can pull the water more easily than the land, because water can flow and rearrange while rock cannot. The same tidal force therefore produces a movement of several metres in the ocean but only about 30 cm in the solid land. same tidal force acts on both water can flow and rearrange rock cannot move as easily 30 cm earth tide"],[2,"y7c03-r035","Short answer","Explain why there are two high tides each day rather than one.","3.4","The side of the Earth closer to the Moon has a high tide because of the pull of gravity from the Moon, but the side furthest away also has a high tide. The Earth takes 24 hours to spin on its axis, so 12 hours later the side that was closest to the Moon is now furthest away, and it is in a high-tide position again. A place therefore passes through two high tides in each 24-hour spin. high tide on the near side also on the far side Earth spins once in 24 hours two high tides per day"],[2,"y7c03-r036","Short answer","Explain why the time between a high tide and the next low tide is about six hours.","3.4","High tides are about 12 hours apart, because a place passes through two high-tide positions during each 24-hour spin of the Earth. Low tide occurs halfway between them, at the positions a quarter turn away from the Moon. Half of 12 hours is six, so the gap between a high tide and the next low tide is about six hours, which is why the two photographs of the same beach were taken six hours apart. 12 hours between high tides low tide halfway between half of 12 is six six hours apart"],[2,"y7c03-r037","Short answer","Explain why the Sun causes a weaker tidal force than the Moon, even though it is far more massive.","3.4","The Sun also produces a tidal force on Earth, but this is weaker as the Sun is further away than the Moon. The strength of gravity decreases as distance increases, and the Sun is so much further away that this outweighs its far greater mass. Tidal effects depend on the difference in pull across the Earth, and that difference falls away with distance faster than the pull itself. Sun is much further away gravity weakens with distance distance outweighs the greater mass weaker tidal force"],[2,"y7c03-r038","Short answer","Explain why the largest tidal ranges happen when there is a full moon or a new moon.","3.4","When the Sun and the Moon are in line with Earth, this produces a larger tidal force, because their two pulls act along the same line and add together instead of partly cancelling. A full moon and a new moon are exactly the times when the Sun, Earth and Moon are lined up. The combined tidal force then raises the high tides higher and lowers the low tides further, giving the greatest tidal range. Sun and Moon in line with Earth pulls add together full moon and new moon are line-ups greatest tidal range"],[2,"y7c03-r039","Short answer","Explain why some harbours can only be used at certain times of the day.","3.4","The tide changes the depth of the water in the harbour by several metres between high and low tide. If the water in the harbour is not deep enough, boats cannot move safely, because the hull would touch the bottom. Since high tides are about 12 hours apart and low tide comes six hours after each one, a boat's safe window repeats on that same cycle rather than being available all day. tide changes the harbour depth too shallow at low tide boats cannot move safely window repeats on the tidal cycle"],[2,"y7c03-r040","Short answer","Explain why studying earth tides might help scientists warn people about volcanoes.","3.4","Tides also cause the land to change in height through the day, and volcano eruptions have been linked with earth tides, as have earthquakes. If the flexing of the land by tidal forces makes an eruption more likely at particular times, then tracking earth tides gives a pattern to compare eruptions against. By studying earth tides, scientists may be able to predict when a volcano will become dangerous. earth tides flex the land eruptions linked with earth tides tides follow a known pattern may allow prediction"],[2,"y7c03-r041","Short answer","Explain how tides affect food chains in coastal areas.","3.4","The tide repeatedly uncovers and covers the shore, and at low tide birds can eat some types of shellfish when they are not covered with water. Some types of fish also move to find food according to tides in coastal areas. So the tide sets when each animal can reach its food, which means the feeding relationships in the food chain follow the tidal cycle rather than being available all the time. low tide uncovers shellfish for birds fish move with the tides tide controls when food is reachable food chain follows the cycle"],[2,"y7c03-r042","Short answer","Explain why energy is described as something that must be changed or transferred in order to do something.","3.5","Energy that stays where it is and stays in the same form makes nothing happen; when energy is stored, the energy is not doing anything. It is the change or the transfer that produces an effect, such as heating a pot or moving a bus. So energy on its own is not an action; it is the capacity for one, and the action appears only when the energy moves or changes form. stored energy is not doing anything change or transfer produces the effect energy is the capacity for action"],[2,"y7c03-r043","Short answer","Explain why energy needs its own unit, the joule, rather than being measured in newtons.","3.5","The unit for measuring the amount of energy is called the joule, while the newton measures force. They describe different things: a force is a push or pull acting on an object, while energy is what must be changed or transferred to make something happen. Using one unit for both would suggest they are the same quantity, and you could not then say that walking upstairs and boiling water need comparable amounts of energy. joule measures energy newton measures force different quantities comparisons need a common energy unit"],[2,"y7c03-r044","Short answer","A bus driving uphill is said to have chemical, kinetic, thermal, sound, gravitational potential and elastic energy at once. Explain how this is possible.","3.5","Each name describes a different store or transfer, and they refer to different parts of the situation: the bus carries fuel, which is chemical; the bus is moving, which is kinetic; the engine is hot, which is thermal; the engine makes noise, which is sound; the bus is going uphill, which is gravitational potential; and the tyres get squeezed, which is elastic. They are not competing labels for one thing, so all can be true together. different stores describe different parts fuel is chemical movement is kinetic height is gravitational potential all true at once"],[2,"y7c03-r045","Short answer","Explain why uncooked rice and a battery are both described as stores of chemical energy.","3.5","Both hold energy in a form that stays put until something releases it, and in both cases the energy is held in the substances themselves. You can keep uncooked rice for a long time, and a battery is another example of how chemical energy can be stored, so it is quite easy to store chemical energy. The energy only does something when the rice is eaten or the battery is connected in a circuit. energy held in the substances stays put until released rice and batteries both store it easy to store chemical energy"],[2,"y7c03-r046","Short answer","Coal and crude oil formed millions of years ago. Explain what this tells you about chemical energy stores.","3.5","Coal and crude oil are stores of chemical energy that formed millions of years ago, which shows that some energy stores can last for a very long time. The energy has not leaked away over that time, because chemical energy stays in the substance until a process such as burning releases it. This is what makes fuels useful: they can be moved and kept for years and still release their energy when needed. chemical energy can last millions of years does not leak away released only by a process such as burning fuels can be stored and moved"],[2,"y7c03-r047","Short answer","Explain why kinetic energy is harder to store than chemical or gravitational potential energy.","3.5","A tennis ball has a store of kinetic energy while it is moving, but the ball will eventually stop, because forces such as air resistance and friction keep transferring that energy away. Chemical energy sits in a substance and gravitational potential energy sits in a raised object, and neither needs the object to keep doing anything. Kinetic energy exists only while the movement lasts, so keeping it means keeping the movement going. kinetic energy exists only while moving movement is opposed by resistance chemical and gravitational stores need no motion harder to keep"],[2,"y7c03-r048","Short answer","Water is pumped up into a tank. Explain what has been stored and why the pump was needed.","3.5","A pump has been used to lift up the water, and the water then stores gravitational potential energy because of its height. Lifting requires pulling against gravity, so the pump had to transfer energy to the water to raise it, and that energy is now held in the raised position. It can be released later by letting the water fall, which is why storing energy this way is useful. pump lifts the water against gravity raised water stores gravitational potential energy energy can be released by letting it fall"],[2,"y7c03-r049","Short answer","Explain why thermal energy is described as a store that only lasts a short time.","3.5","Hot objects will eventually cool down, so they lose their store of thermal energy to whatever is around them. Thermal energy moves from hotter places to cooler ones whenever they are in contact, and nothing in an ordinary room stops that happening. A store that leaks away by itself cannot be kept, which is why thermal energy is unlike chemical energy that can sit in coal for millions of years. hot objects cool down thermal energy spreads to the surroundings leaks away by itself unlike chemical energy"],[2,"y7c03-r050","Short answer","Describe the sequence of energy changes when wood is burned to heat a cooking pot.","3.6","Wood is a store of chemical energy. Burning the wood changes the chemical energy to thermal energy, and the thermal energy is then transferred to the pot and the food inside. The important distinction is that burning changes the form of the energy, while heating the pot transfers energy from one place to another without changing its form, so the sequence contains both a change and a transfer. wood stores chemical energy burning changes it to thermal thermal energy transferred to the pot change and transfer are different"],[2,"y7c03-r051","Short answer","Explain the energy changes when a person walks up a flight of stairs.","3.6","They are changing chemical energy from their food into kinetic energy for movement. The movement is taking the people higher, so kinetic energy is being changed to gravitational potential energy as they rise. The chain runs from a store in the food, through movement, to a store in the raised position of the body, and each step happens because a process, in this case the person walking, is making it happen. chemical energy from food changed to kinetic for movement kinetic changed to gravitational potential a process drives each step"],[2,"y7c03-r052","Short answer","Explain the sequence of energy changes in a gas-fired power station.","3.6","The power station uses the chemical energy stored in natural gas. The gas is burned, which changes chemical energy to thermal energy; the thermal energy is then changed to kinetic energy in large generators that spin around; the kinetic energy is then changed to electrical energy; and the electrical energy is transferred through wires into homes and buildings. Each step needs a process, and the last step is a transfer rather than a change. chemical in the gas burning gives thermal thermal to kinetic in the generators kinetic to electrical transferred through wires"],[2,"y7c03-r053","Short answer","Explain why every energy change needs a process or an event.","3.6","In all these examples, there is a process or event that changes or transfers the energy; for example, burning is a process, and a book falling from a shelf is an event. Energy that is stored is not doing anything, so something has to happen for the change to take place. Naming the process is what turns a list of energy types into an explanation of why the change occurred. energy stored does nothing by itself a process or event is needed burning is a process a falling book is an event"],[2,"y7c03-r054","Short answer","A book falls from a shelf. Explain the energy change, and say why it is called an event rather than a process.","3.6","When the book is on the shelf it has stored gravitational potential energy because of its height, and this energy is changed to kinetic energy as the book falls. It is called an event because it happens once, at a particular moment, rather than being something kept going like burning. Either way, something had to happen before the stored energy could change into movement. gravitational potential energy on the shelf changed to kinetic as it falls an event happens once a process is kept going"],[2,"y7c03-r055","Short answer","Explain what makes an energy change useful, and give an example of one that is not.","3.6","An energy change is useful when the energy is changed in a way that we want, such as electrical energy changed to light in a lamp. Energy changes are not always helpful: typhoons, hurricanes, earthquakes and tsunamis are examples of how energy changes can be very dangerous. So useful is a judgement about our purposes, not a property of the energy itself, which behaves the same way in both cases. useful means changed the way we want storms and earthquakes are damaging energy changes useful is a judgement about purpose"],[2,"y7c03-r056","Short answer","A television converts electrical energy to sound and light. Explain why one input can give two useful outputs.","3.6","A single store can be changed into more than one form at the same time, because different parts of the device do different jobs: the screen produces light and the speaker produces sound, both drawing on the same electrical supply. Nothing requires an energy change to have only one output. The total energy out, including the thermal energy the set also produces, matches the electrical energy put in. one input can give several outputs different parts do different jobs screen gives light, speaker gives sound total out matches total in"],[2,"y7c03-r057","Short answer","Water freezing is an energy change. Explain what happens to the energy.","3.6","Freezing happens when energy is transferred out of the water to its surroundings, which is why a freezer has to remove heat rather than add anything. As the particles lose energy they move more slowly until they can only vibrate, and they settle into the fixed pattern of a solid. The energy has not vanished; it has been transferred to the surroundings, which become slightly warmer. energy transferred out of the water particles move more slowly settle into a fixed pattern surroundings gain the energy"],[2,"y7c03-r058","Short answer","Explain what is meant by wasted energy.","3.7","Every time you use energy to make something happen, energy is transferred or changed. Some of the energy transferred or changed is useful, but some of it is wasted, meaning it ends up in forms or places we did not want. The wasted energy has not disappeared and it is not destroyed; it has simply gone somewhere that is of no use to us, which is why it is called wasted rather than lost. some energy is useful, some wasted wasted energy goes where we did not want not destroyed no longer useful"],[2,"y7c03-r059","Short answer","Explain what dissipated energy means and why it cannot be recovered.","3.7","Dissipated energy is energy that spreads out where there is no use for it. You cannot gather thermal energy or sound and bring them back into one place to be stored, changed or transferred, because once the energy has spread through the air, the ground and the surrounding objects it is shared among an enormous number of particles. The energy still exists, but it is too thinly spread to be collected and used again. dissipated means spread out no use for it where it goes cannot be gathered back still exists but too thinly spread"],[2,"y7c03-r060","Short answer","Only about 25% of the chemical energy in a motorcycle's fuel is used for movement. Explain where the rest goes.","3.7","The motorcycle engine changes chemical energy stored in the fuel into useful kinetic energy to move the motorcycle and rider, but chemical energy from the fuel is also changed into thermal energy and sound energy. The other 75% of the energy is therefore wasted energy that heats the engine and the air and makes noise. This wasted energy is dissipated and cannot be recovered. 25% becomes useful kinetic energy rest becomes thermal and sound 75% is wasted dissipated and cannot be recovered"],[2,"y7c03-r061","Short answer","Lamp A wastes 85% of its energy and Lamp B wastes 50%, yet both give the same brightness. Explain what this means for the electricity each uses.","3.7","Both lamps change electrical energy to light energy, but Lamp A only changes about 15% of the electrical energy into light while Lamp B changes about 50%. To produce the same brightness, Lamp A must therefore be supplied with much more electrical energy than Lamp B, and the extra is dissipated as thermal energy. Lamp B does the same job for less electricity, which is why the wasted fraction matters. A converts 15% to light B converts 50% same brightness needs more electricity for A the extra is dissipated as thermal energy"],[2,"y7c03-r062","Short answer","Explain why some thermal energy is wasted in every energy change, even in a device designed to produce heat.","3.7","Every time energy is changed or transferred, some thermal energy is wasted and this wasted thermal energy is dissipated. Even when you want to produce thermal energy some of it is wasted: when water is heated over a fire, thermal energy also heats the rocks, the metal container and the air around it, and some escapes in the steam. Only the part that reaches the water counts as useful, so waste happens even here. some thermal energy is always wasted heats the container, rocks and air some escapes in the steam only the part reaching the water is useful"],[2,"y7c03-r063","Short answer","Explain why no machine can be built that wastes no energy at all.","3.7","For everything that uses energy change or transfer, some of that energy will always be dissipated. Every moving part rubs, every hot part warms its surroundings and every working device makes some sound, and each of these spreads energy out where there is no use for it. So waste is not a fault in the design that better engineering could remove entirely; it is a feature of every energy change. some energy is always dissipated rubbing, heating and sound in every device waste is not merely a design fault"],[2,"y7c03-r064","Short answer","As you move away from a hot object you feel less heat. Explain why, using the idea of dissipation.","3.7","Thermal energy from the object spreads out in all directions, so the further away you are the larger the area that same energy has spread across and the smaller the share that reaches you. The temperature will go down as you move further away, and the fall is steep close to the object and gentler further out. This is dissipation seen directly: the energy is not gone, it is simply thinner where you are standing. energy spreads out in all directions spread over a larger area further away smaller share reaches you temperature falls with distance"],[2,"y7c03-r065","Short answer","Hot water in a beaker cools down. Name the energy that leaves and list where it goes.","3.7","The energy that leaves is thermal energy. It goes into the glass of the beaker and then into the air around it, into the bench the beaker stands on, and into the surroundings by evaporation from the water surface. In every case it spreads out among far more particles than it started in, so it is dissipated: the total energy is unchanged but none of it can be gathered back into the water. thermal energy leaves the water goes to the beaker, air and bench some carried away by evaporation dissipated and not recoverable"],[2,"y7c03-r066","Short answer","Diesel engines are more expensive than petrol engines of the same size, yet large trucks use them. Suggest why.","3.7","A diesel engine transfers a larger share of the energy it uses to useful energy, so less is wasted as thermal and sound energy. Large trucks travel for long periods and carry heavy loads, so the fuel they use over their lifetime is very large, and a small improvement in the useful share saves a great deal of fuel. The extra cost of the engine is paid back by the fuel it does not waste. diesel wastes a smaller share trucks travel long distances for long periods large total fuel use fuel saving repays the extra cost"],[2,"y7c03-o001","Short answer","Name the force that pulls a dropped object towards the ground.","3.1","The force of gravity. gravity"],[2,"y7c03-o021","Short answer","Explain why scientists cannot simply watch how the Solar System was formed.","3.2","There were no people alive at the time to observe it happening. no people observe"],[2,"y7c03-o022","Short answer","What does the word 'observe' mean?","3.2","To watch something happening. watch"],[2,"y7c03-o023","Short answer","What is a nebula?","3.2","A cloud of dust and gas in space. cloud dust gas"],[2,"y7c03-o026","Short answer","Give one reason why scientists use a computer model to study how the Solar System formed.","3.2","It is impossible to do the real experiment: forming a star takes millions of years, needs far too much space, and cannot be set up in a laboratory. millions of years cannot do an experiment"],[2,"y7c03-o034","Short answer","Which object has the largest mass in the Solar System?","3.3","The Sun. Sun"],[2,"y7c03-o035","Short answer","How many times stronger is the Sun's gravity than the Earth's gravity?","3.3","27 times stronger. 27"],[2,"y7c03-o036","Short answer","What does the Sun's gravity do to the planets?","3.3","It holds all the planets in their orbits. holds orbit"],[2,"y7c03-o050","Short answer","What is a tide?","3.4","The regular change in the depth of the ocean during the day. change depth"],[2,"y7c03-o051","Short answer","What is meant by the depth of the ocean?","3.4","The distance from the surface of the water to the bottom of the ocean. surface bottom"],[2,"y7c03-o062","Short answer","Explain why the time between high tides is about 12 hours.","3.4","The Earth spins once in 24 hours, so 12 hours after a place is closest to the Moon it is furthest away, and both positions give a high tide. 24 hours 12 hours furthest"],[2,"y7c03-o069","Short answer","What is energy?","3.5","Energy is something that must be changed or transferred in order to make something happen. changed transferred"],[2,"y7c03-o070","Short answer","What kind of energy does a moving object have?","3.5","Kinetic energy. kinetic"],[2,"y7c03-o071","Short answer","Give three examples of things that are stores of chemical energy.","3.5","Food, batteries and chemical fuels such as wood, oil and coal. food batteries fuel"],[2,"y7c03-o072","Short answer","What kind of energy is stored when an object is stretched or squeezed out of shape?","3.5","Elastic potential energy. elastic"],[2,"y7c03-o073","Short answer","What kind of energy is stored when an object is lifted away from a source of gravity?","3.5","Gravitational potential energy. gravitational potential"],[2,"y7c03-o084","Short answer","Complete the sentence: when something happens, energy is ... or ... .","3.6","Changed or transferred. changed transferred"],[2,"y7c03-o085","Short answer","Which energy store does the wood in a cooking fire hold?","3.6","Chemical energy. chemical"],[2,"y7c03-o086","Short answer","Describe the energy change that happens when wood is burned.","3.6","Chemical energy is changed to thermal energy. chemical thermal"],[2,"y7c03-o087","Short answer","Where does the thermal energy from a cooking fire go?","3.6","It is transferred to the pot and to the food inside it. pot food"],[2,"y7c03-o088","Short answer","Describe the energy changes that happen when a person walks up a flight of stairs.","3.6","Chemical energy from their food is changed to kinetic energy for movement, and the kinetic energy is then changed to gravitational potential energy as they go higher. chemical kinetic gravitational potential"],[2,"y7c03-o096","Short answer","Every time energy is transferred or changed, what happens to it?","3.7","Some of the energy is useful, but some of it is wasted. useful wasted"],[2,"y7c03-o097","Short answer","Describe the useful and wasted energy in a motorcycle engine.","3.7","The chemical energy in the petrol is changed to useful kinetic energy to move the motorcycle. The rest is wasted, mostly as thermal energy and sound. chemical kinetic wasted"],[2,"y7c03-o098","Short answer","Two lamps give out light of the same brightness. Must they use the same amount of energy?","3.7","No. Two lamps of the same brightness can waste very different quantities of energy. no different waste"],[2,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the unit used to measure weight?","3.1","kilograms (kg) newtons (N) grams (g) metres (m)"],[2,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Mass is the quantity of matter in an object and it is measured in ____.","3.1","kilograms"],[2,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","In which direction does the force of gravity act on an object near Earth?","3.1","Away from the centre of the Earth Towards the centre of the Earth Sideways along the surface Upwards into space"],[2,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","The force from a surface that pushes up to support an object is called the ____ force.","3.1","contact"],[2,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the name given to a cloud of dust and gas in space?","3.2","nebula orbit axis model"],[2,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","Scientists use computers to create a ____ to represent something that is difficult to observe directly.","3.2","model"],[2,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Why do scientists use computer models to study the formation of the Solar System?","3.2","It takes millions of years for stars and planets to form The Solar System is too far away to see Computers are cheaper than telescopes Dust and gas cannot be put in a computer"],[2,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","As particles of dust and gas stick together, their total mass increases, so the strength of their ____ also increases.","3.2","gravity"],[2,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which planet spins on its axis in the opposite direction to most other planets?","3.2","Venus Mars Jupiter Saturn"],[2,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","A space where there are no particles is called a ____.","3.3","vacuum"],[2,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","What force keeps the planets in their orbits around the Sun?","3.3","The Sun's gravity Air resistance Friction from space dust Magnetic force"],[2,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","The force of gravity from the Sun always acts towards the ____.","3.3","Sun"],[2,"qwen-02-11","Multiple choice","Why can spacecraft reach very high speeds in space but not on Earth?","3.3","There is no air resistance in a vacuum Spacecraft are lighter in space Gravity is stronger in space The Sun pushes them faster"],[2,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name given to the pull of gravity that causes tides?","3.4","tidal range tidal force high tide earth tide"],[2,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","The difference in depth of the water between high and low tides is called the ____.","3.4","tidal range"],[2,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","Why does the Moon cause a stronger tidal force than the Sun?","3.4","The Moon is larger than the Sun. The Moon is closer to Earth than the Sun. The Sun does not have gravity. The Moon spins faster than the Sun."],[2,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","High tides are about ____ hours apart.","3.4","12"],[2,"qwen-03-08","Gap fill","The unit for measuring the amount of energy is called the ____.","3.5","joule"],[2,"qwen-03-09","Multiple choice","Which type of energy is stored in food and batteries?","3.5","kinetic chemical thermal elastic potential"],[2,"qwen-03-11","Multiple choice","Which of these is an example of a luminous object?","3.5","The Sun The Moon A mirror A white wall in sunlight"],[2,"qwen-04-07","Multiple choice","When wood is burned, chemical energy changes into which type of energy?","3.6","electrical kinetic thermal gravitational potential"],[2,"qwen-04-08","Gap fill","People walking up stairs change chemical energy from food into ____ energy for movement.","3.6","kinetic"],[2,"qwen-04-09","Multiple choice","In a power station using natural gas, what happens after the thermal energy is produced?","3.6","It changes to chemical energy. It changes to kinetic energy in generators. It is stored in the gas tank. It turns into sound energy."],[2,"qwen-06-01","Multiple choice","What is dissipated energy?","3.7","Energy that spreads out where there is no use for it Energy that can be gathered and stored again Energy that becomes more useful over time Energy that is kept in one place for later use"],[2,"qwen-06-02","Gap fill","In a motorcycle engine, chemical energy from fuel is changed into useful kinetic energy and wasted ____ energy.","3.7","thermal"],[2,"qwen-06-03","Multiple choice","About what fraction of the chemical energy in a motorcycle's fuel is used for movement?","3.7","About one quarter, or 25% About three quarters, or 75% About one half, or 50% Almost all of it, about 85%"],[2,"qwen-06-04","Gap fill","Lamp A changes about 15% of electrical energy into light, while the rest is wasted as ____ energy.","3.7","thermal"],[2,"qwen-06-05","Multiple choice","Why can dissipated thermal energy not be recovered?","3.7","It spreads out and cannot be gathered back into one place It turns into light energy immediately It is destroyed by the air It becomes chemical energy"],[2,"qwen-06-06","Gap fill","When wood burns to heat water, thermal energy is also used to heat the rocks, the metal container and the ____ around it.","3.7","air"],[2,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Mass can be measured in grams or ____.","3.1","kilograms"],[2,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","Which property of an object stays the same even when gravity changes?","3.1","weight mass force volume"],[2,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which piece of equipment is used to find the weight of an object?","3.1","top pan balance measuring cylinder force meter ruler"],[2,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","Scientists use computers to ____ the formation of the Solar System.","3.2","model"],[2,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","A space that contains no particles is called a ____.","3.3","vacuum"],[2,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","When scientists see stars forming in clouds of dust and gas, this is called an ____.","3.2","observation"],[2,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","Why are spacecraft that have no engines able to keep a constant speed in space?","3.3","There is no air resistance They have no mass They have no energy They have no weight"],[2,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","The rings of the planet Saturn are made from millions of separate pieces of ____.","3.3","rock"],[2,"qwenw-02-03","Multiple choice","What causes high and low tides to happen on Earth?","3.4","Tidal forces from the Sun and Moon Air resistance Gravity from the planets only Tidal forces from the Sun only"],[2,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","The force caused by gravity from the Moon that makes tides is called a ____ force.","3.4","tidal"],[2,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","A book lifted up onto a shelf has a store of ____ energy.","3.5","gravitational potential"],[2,"qwenw-02-07","Multiple choice","What type of energy is transferred by current in wires?","3.5","Electrical Light Sound Thermal"],[2,"qwenw-02-09","Multiple choice","Which description matches the form of energy called 'kinetic'?","3.5","Energy of moving objects Energy stored in an object that has changed shape Energy transferred from vibrations Energy we can see"],[2,"qwenw-03-07","Multiple choice","In the process of burning gas for heating, what type of energy is transferred to thermal energy?","3.6","electrical chemical kinetic sound"],[2,"qwenw-03-08","Gap fill","When using an electric lamp, electrical energy is transferred to light and ____ energy.","3.6","thermal"],[2,"qwenw-03-10","Gap fill","In Zara's investigation, which variable must remain constant to make a fair test: the temperature of the water or the ____ of water used?","3.6","mass"],[3,"y7c04-f01","Diagram labelling","These two animals belong to two different species of elephant. Name the elephant labelled {n}.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f02","Diagram labelling","This dichotomous key identifies five small animals. Name the animal labelled {n}.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f03","Diagram labelling","These photographs show four different species of cat. Name the cat labelled {n}.","4.5",""],[3,"y7c04-f04","Diagram labelling","Name the animal labelled {n}.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the elephant that has the larger ears and the longer tusks.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the elephant that will not reproduce with the other one to produce fertile offspring.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f05h1","Click the diagram","The brown objects in this photograph are influenza viruses. Click on the group of three viruses near the top of the photograph, to the right of the centre.","4.2",""],[3,"y7c04-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the large green cell that fills the bottom of this photograph.","4.2",""],[3,"y7c04-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the red spike proteins at the top of this virus.","4.2",""],[3,"y7c04-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the animal that is a liger.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the animal that is the mother of the liger.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f07h3","Click the diagram","Click on the animal that is the father of the liger.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f02h1","Click the diagram","Use the key. Click on the name of the animal that has more than six legs.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f02h2","Click the diagram","Use the key. Click on the name of the animal that has no legs and a body made up of rings.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f02h3","Click the diagram","Use the key. Click on the name of the animal that has six legs and four wings.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the cat that has stripes.","4.5",""],[3,"y7c04-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the cat that has spots.","4.5",""],[3,"y7c04-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the cat that has long black tufts on the tips of its ears.","4.5",""],[3,"y7c04-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the letter of the flower that has exactly four petals.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the letter of the flower that is Limnanthes.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f08h3","Click the diagram","Click on the letter of the flower that is Silene.","4.4",""],[3,"y7c04-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the animal that is infertile.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the animal that is the mother of the mule.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the animal that is the father of the mule.","4.3",""],[3,"y7c04-r001","Short answer","Checking for breathing and a heartbeat works for a person but not for a plant. Explain why scientists need the seven characteristics instead.","4.1","If it is a person, you can check to see if they are breathing, or if they have a heartbeat, but plants do not breathe or have hearts, yet they are alive. Breathing and a heartbeat are features of one group of animals, not of life itself, so a test built on them would wrongly classify every plant as non-living. The seven characteristics are chosen because they apply to every living organism, which is what a general test requires. breathing and heartbeat are animal features plants have neither yet are alive the test must apply to all organisms seven characteristics are general"],[3,"y7c04-r002","Short answer","Explain what the seven characteristics of living organisms are actually used for.","4.1","Living organisms have a set of seven characteristics that make them different from non-living things, so the list is a test for deciding which side of that boundary something falls on. Anything showing all seven is classed as living, and anything showing none of them is non-living. The list matters most in the difficult cases, such as a seed or a virus, where a single feature would not settle the question. a test for living versus non-living all seven means living useful in difficult cases seeds and viruses"],[3,"y7c04-r003","Short answer","Explain the difference between respiration and breathing.","4.1","Respiration is the process in which food is broken down inside cells to provide energy, and it happens in every living cell of every organism. Breathing is the movement of air in and out of the body, which only some animals do and which only supplies the oxygen that respiration uses. A plant respires without breathing, which is why respiration is one of the seven characteristics and breathing is not. respiration breaks down food inside cells for energy happens in all living cells breathing is only movement of air plants respire without breathing"],[3,"y7c04-r004","Short answer","Explain why excretion is a characteristic of all living organisms and not just of animals.","4.1","Living organisms get rid of waste materials, such as carbon dioxide, because the chemical reactions inside their cells produce substances the organism cannot use. Those reactions happen in plant cells as well as animal cells, so plants also produce waste that must be removed. Excretion is therefore about the removal of waste made by the body's own reactions, which is something every living cell needs. waste made by reactions inside cells such as carbon dioxide plant cells do this too removal of the body's own waste"],[3,"y7c04-r005","Short answer","A river moves and a car moves, yet neither is alive. Explain why movement alone does not prove something is living.","4.1","Living organisms can move, but movement on its own is not enough, because non-living things can be moved by forces such as wind, gravity or an engine. The seven characteristics are used together, and an organism must show all of them, not just one. A car does not grow, reproduce, excrete or respire, so it fails the other six tests even though it moves. non-living things can be moved by forces movement alone is not enough all seven characteristics are needed a car fails the other six"],[3,"y7c04-r006","Short answer","Explain why growth in a living organism is different from a pile of sand getting bigger.","4.1","All living organisms grow, and growth means a permanent increase in size produced by the organism itself, using materials it has taken in as nutrition and built into new cells. A pile of sand only gets bigger because more sand has been added from outside, and nothing inside the pile has made it happen. The difference is whether the increase is produced by processes going on inside the thing. growth is a permanent increase in size produced by the organism itself uses materials taken in as nutrition sand is added from outside"],[3,"y7c04-r007","Short answer","Plants do not eat, yet nutrition is one of the seven characteristics. Explain how plants meet it.","4.1","Nutrition means taking in the nutrients that are needed to keep the organism alive, and plants feed by photosynthesis. They do not eat ready-made food, but they make their own food using light, so they still obtain the substances they need to grow and respire. The characteristic is about obtaining nutrients, not about the particular method, which is why it covers both plants and animals. nutrition is obtaining nutrients plants feed by photosynthesis they make their own food method differs but the characteristic is met"],[3,"y7c04-r008","Short answer","Explain what sensitivity means and give a reason why an organism needs it.","4.1","Living organisms are sensitive to changes going on around them, which means they can detect a change and respond to it. This matters because the surroundings change constantly, and an organism that could not detect light, temperature, danger or the presence of food would be unable to move towards what it needs or away from what harms it. Sensitivity is what turns information about the surroundings into useful action. sensitive to changes around them detect and respond surroundings change constantly allows movement towards food or away from danger"],[3,"y7c04-r009","Short answer","Explain why reproduction is listed as a characteristic of living organisms even though an individual can survive without reproducing.","4.1","Living organisms can produce young, and the characteristic describes what the kind of organism is able to do rather than what each individual happens to do in its lifetime. A particular animal may never reproduce, but its species continues only because reproduction is possible. Without it, every species would end within one generation, so the ability is a defining feature of life even when it is not being used. organisms can produce young describes the ability, not every individual the species continues through reproduction without it life would end in one generation"],[3,"y7c04-r010","Short answer","Do all living organisms show all seven characteristics all of the time? Use an elephant, an apple and a seed to explain.","4.1","No. An elephant shows all seven while it is active, but an apple and a seed are living and yet show very few of them at a given moment: a seed does not move or grow while it is dormant, and it will not reproduce until it has become a plant. The characteristics describe what a living organism is capable of over its whole life, not a checklist that must be ticked at every instant. not all the time a dormant seed does not grow or move capability over the whole life not a checklist for every moment"],[3,"y7c04-r011","Short answer","A list of features of a horse is given and you must pick out the characteristics of all living things. Explain how to decide.","4.1","You keep a feature only if it belongs to the set of seven characteristics that every living organism shows, such as the ability to sense changes in its environment, and you reject anything that is only true of horses, such as having four legs or a mane. The test is whether the feature would also be true of a plant, a fish or a bacterium. Features that fail that test describe this species, not life. keep only the seven general characteristics reject features special to horses ask whether a plant would also show it species features are not life features"],[3,"y7c04-r012","Short answer","Explain why you cannot see a virus with a school microscope.","4.2","A virus is much smaller than one of your cells, and you cannot see a virus with the kind of microscope that you use in school. A light microscope can only separate objects down to a certain size, and viruses are far below that limit, so they would not appear at all rather than appearing blurred. To see a virus, you need to use a special kind of microscope called an electron microscope. much smaller than a cell below the limit of a light microscope would not appear at all an electron microscope is needed"],[3,"y7c04-r013","Short answer","Explain why viruses are not classed as cells.","4.2","Viruses are not made of cells, and they do not have a cell membrane or cytoplasm, which are the structures every real cell has. What a virus does have is an outer coat made of protein and, inside it, RNA carrying instructions. Missing the basic cell structures means a virus cannot carry out the chemical reactions a cell carries out, which is why it has to use a host cell instead. no cell membrane or cytoplasm only a protein coat and RNA cannot carry out cell reactions must use a host cell"],[3,"y7c04-r014","Short answer","Explain what the RNA inside a virus does.","4.2","The RNA is made of little threads that contain a set of coded instructions for making more viruses. It carries no energy and does no work by itself; it is information. When the virus gets inside a cell, the virus forces the cell to copy those instructions and use them to build many new viruses, so the RNA is the only part of the virus that has to reach the inside of the cell for replication to happen. RNA carries coded instructions instructions for making more viruses it is information, not machinery the cell does the building"],[3,"y7c04-r015","Short answer","Explain the job of the little pegs on the outside of a virus's coat.","4.2","The little pegs on the virus's coat help it to stick onto one of your cells and then get inside the cell. Without them the virus could touch a cell and simply drift away again, so the pegs are what makes infection possible in the first place. The same idea appears in SARS-CoV-2, where the red spike proteins on the outside help the virus attach to cells and get inside. pegs let the virus stick to a cell then get inside without them it would drift away spike proteins do the same job"],[3,"y7c04-r016","Short answer","Describe how a virus replicates once it is inside a cell.","4.2","When the viruses are inside the cell, each virus bursts open, and the virus forces the cell to copy the instructions on its RNA and make many new viruses. This is called replication. Then the new viruses burst out of the dying cell, ready to infect more cells, which is why one infected cell leads to many. The virus never builds anything itself; every new virus is made by the cell's own machinery. virus bursts open inside the cell cell copies the RNA instructions many new viruses made they burst out and infect more cells"],[3,"y7c04-r017","Short answer","Explain why a viral infection makes an animal feel ill.","4.2","New viruses burst out of the dying cell, so each round of replication destroys the cells that were used to make them. This makes the animal whose cells are infected feel ill, because the body is losing working cells faster than it can replace them, and the damaged tissue cannot do its normal job. The illness is therefore a result of cell destruction, not of the virus itself being poisonous. replication destroys the host cells cells lost faster than replaced damaged tissue cannot work normally illness from cell destruction"],[3,"y7c04-r018","Short answer","Give the evidence that supports classifying viruses as non-living.","4.2","Viruses cannot do anything at all on their own: they do not respire, feed, excrete or grow, they are not sensitive and cannot move. That is six of the seven characteristics of living organisms failed, and they are also not made of cells. On the seven-characteristic test, a virus is much closer to a non-living particle than to an organism. cannot do anything on their own do not respire, feed, excrete or grow not sensitive and cannot move not made of cells"],[3,"y7c04-r019","Short answer","Give the evidence that supports classifying viruses as living.","4.2","Viruses have to get inside a living cell before they can make copies of themselves, but they do make copies of themselves, which is reproduction, one of the seven characteristics. They also carry coded instructions in RNA, as living organisms carry instructions, and each kind of virus has a particular kind of cell that it infects, which looks like a response to its surroundings. So they show at least one characteristic of life. they make copies of themselves reproduction is a characteristic of life they carry coded instructions they target particular cells"],[3,"y7c04-r020","Short answer","Two scientists disagree about whether viruses are living. Explain why this disagreement can continue.","4.2","One scientist says viruses are living organisms and the other does not agree that viruses should be classed as living organisms, and both can point to real evidence: viruses replicate, but only inside a host cell, and they show none of the other characteristics. The seven-characteristic test was designed for organisms made of cells, so a virus sits at the boundary the test was never built to handle. The disagreement is about where to draw a line, not about the facts. both sides use real evidence replication supports living failing the other characteristics supports non-living the test was not designed for this case"],[3,"y7c04-r021","Short answer","Explain why a virus that infects plant cells cannot make you ill.","4.2","Each kind of virus has a particular kind of cell that it infects, and some viruses infect plant cells. The virus attaches using pegs or spikes that fit the surface of its own kind of host cell, so it cannot stick to and enter a cell it does not match. Without getting inside a cell it cannot replicate at all, so a plant virus does nothing in a human body. each virus infects a particular kind of cell attachment depends on matching the cell surface cannot get inside a cell it does not match cannot replicate, so no illness"],[3,"y7c04-r022","Short answer","A photograph shows viruses 100000 times bigger than they really are. Explain why magnification must always be stated.","4.2","The viruses in the photograph look 100000 times bigger than they really are, so without that figure a reader has no way of knowing the true size of what is shown. The same image could otherwise be mistaken for a picture of cells or of dust. Stating the magnification lets anyone work back from the size in the picture to the real size, which is what makes the photograph scientific evidence rather than just a picture. image is 100000 times larger than real life true size cannot be known without it allows the real size to be calculated makes the image usable as evidence"],[3,"y7c04-r023","Short answer","Scientists think SARS-CoV-2 developed in a wild animal and then spread to humans. Explain why they can only say they think this.","4.2","We do not know exactly where it came from, but scientists think it developed in a wild animal and then spread to humans. No one observed the first infection, so the claim rests on indirect evidence such as how similar the virus is to viruses found in animals. A conclusion built on indirect evidence is stated as the best current explanation and can be revised if better evidence appears. no one observed the first infection based on indirect evidence similarity to animal viruses best current explanation, open to revision"],[3,"y7c04-r024","Short answer","Explain why work on Covid-19 involves both vaccination and drugs rather than only one of them.","4.2","Scientists will work hard for many years to find the best ways of preventing this, including vaccination, and drugs to treat Covid-19. The two approaches solve different problems: vaccination prepares the body before it meets the virus, so infection is prevented, while drugs help someone who is already infected. Since not everyone can be vaccinated in time, both are needed. vaccination prevents infection beforehand drugs treat people already infected two different problems both are needed"],[3,"y7c04-r025","Short answer","Many people get SARS-CoV-2 without being ill at all. Explain why this makes the virus harder to control.","4.2","Many people get the virus without being ill at all, or just have mild symptoms, but in some people it causes dangerous illness and even death. Someone with no symptoms has no reason to stay away from others, yet the virus is still replicating in their cells and can be passed on. Control measures that rely on identifying ill people therefore miss a large share of those who are spreading it. no symptoms means no reason to isolate the virus still replicates and spreads control based on spotting ill people fails hidden transmission"],[3,"y7c04-r026","Short answer","Members of the same species are not identical. Explain why this does not break the idea of a species.","4.3","All the organisms in a species share the same characteristics but they are not all identical to each other; for example, some Indian elephants have straighter tusks than others. This difference between individuals is called variation, and it is expected within every species. A species is defined by the characteristics its members share and by their ability to reproduce together, not by their being copies of one another. members share the same characteristics variation between individuals is normal not copies of one another defined by shared features and reproduction"],[3,"y7c04-r027","Short answer","Explain why appearance alone is not a reliable way to decide whether two organisms are the same species.","4.3","Variation between individuals can sometimes make it difficult to decide whether two organisms belong to the same species, because two members of one species may look quite different while two members of different species may look very similar. To be sure, scientists try to find out if they can reproduce with one another, which tests the biological relationship directly rather than relying on how the organisms look. variation makes appearance unreliable same species can look different different species can look similar test whether they can reproduce"],[3,"y7c04-r028","Short answer","Give the full definition of a species and explain why the word 'fertile' must be in it.","4.3","We can describe a species as a group of organisms that can reproduce together to produce fertile offspring. The word fertile is essential because organisms of two different species can occasionally reproduce and produce healthy young, such as ligers, but ligers cannot reproduce and cannot have offspring. Without the word fertile, the definition would wrongly place lions and tigers in the same species. reproduce together to produce fertile offspring ligers are healthy but cannot reproduce fertile excludes cross-species offspring definition would fail without it"],[3,"y7c04-r029","Short answer","Explain why a liger is almost never seen in the wild.","4.3","A male lion and female tiger in a zoo sometimes reproduce together, but they will only do this if they do not have a member of their own species to reproduce with. In the wild each animal has members of its own species available, and lions and tigers rarely share the same area anyway, so the situation that produces a liger does not normally arise. It happens in a zoo because two animals from different species are put into the same enclosure. only happens when no member of their own species is available zoo enclosure forces the situation in the wild own-species mates are available lions and tigers rarely share an area"],[3,"y7c04-r030","Short answer","Ligers are healthy animals but cannot reproduce. Explain what this shows about lions and tigers.","4.3","Ligers are healthy animals, but ligers cannot reproduce and cannot have offspring, so the offspring are not fertile. Since a species is a group of organisms that can reproduce together to produce fertile offspring, the infertility of the liger shows that lions and tigers fail the test and therefore belong to two different species. Producing young is not enough; the young must themselves be able to reproduce. liger is healthy but infertile offspring must be fertile lions and tigers fail the test different species"],[3,"y7c04-r031","Short answer","Explain why the offspring of two members of a species always belong to that same species.","4.3","Each species reproduces only with other members of its own species, and when they have offspring, the offspring belong to the same species as their parents, and the offspring are fertile. The young inherit their characteristics from parents that share the same characteristics, so they carry those same features and can in turn reproduce with other members of the species. This is what keeps a species continuing over generations. parents share the same characteristics offspring inherit those characteristics offspring are fertile the species continues"],[3,"y7c04-r032","Short answer","A scientist never finds a yellow-crowned parakeet paired with a red-crowned parakeet. Explain what conclusion this supports and how strong the evidence is.","4.3","She never finds a yellow-crowned parakeet that has paired up with a red-crowned parakeet, which supports the conclusion that they belong to two different species, since each species reproduces only with members of its own species. But absence of a mixed pair is weaker evidence than a failed reproduction: the two kinds might simply prefer different habitats or nest at different times. The conclusion is reasonable but not yet certain. no mixed pairs supports different species each species reproduces with its own kind absence is weaker than a direct test could be habitat or timing instead"],[3,"y7c04-r033","Short answer","Explain which of the three suggested actions would best strengthen the parakeet conclusion, and why.","4.3","Checking more pairs of parakeets in the wild is the best of the three, because the conclusion rests on not having seen a mixed pair and a larger number of observations makes that absence far less likely to be chance. Looking at stuffed specimens in a museum only shows appearance, and looking at other species of parakeets does not address whether these two interbreed. More observations of the same kind test the same claim. check more pairs in the wild larger sample makes chance less likely museum specimens only show appearance other species do not test this claim"],[3,"y7c04-r034","Short answer","Give two pieces of evidence that horses and donkeys belong to different species.","4.3","First, donkeys and horses belong to different species is supported by the fact that people breed a female horse with a male donkey and the offspring is called a mule, so the two only produce young when people arrange it. Second, and decisively, mules cannot reproduce, so the offspring are not fertile, and a species is defined as a group that can reproduce together to produce fertile offspring. The infertility is the stronger evidence. crossing needs human intervention the offspring is a mule mules are not fertile fails the fertile-offspring test"],[3,"y7c04-r035","Short answer","Suggest how researchers should decide whether their tiny frog is a new species.","4.3","They should collect enough specimens to show what the normal variation of the kind looks like, since a single specimen could be an unusual individual of a known species, while remembering the species might be rare and should not be over-collected. They should gather evidence on appearance, on the call the males make, and on whether the frogs breed with the Microhyla species already known. Only consistent differences across many specimens, together with no interbreeding, would justify naming a new species. collect enough specimens to show variation one specimen may be an unusual individual compare appearance, call and breeding consistent differences plus no interbreeding"],[3,"y7c04-r036","Short answer","The males of the new frog make a call like a cricket chirping. Explain why a call can be evidence about species.","4.3","A call is how males of many frog species attract females of their own kind, so a call that differs from every known species suggests that these frogs would not attract, and would not be attracted by, members of those species. Since each species reproduces only with other members of its own species, a distinct call is a sign that reproduction is separate. It is supporting evidence, not proof, and would need testing alongside other features. calls attract mates of the same kind a distinct call suggests separate reproduction species reproduce only within themselves supporting evidence, not proof"],[3,"y7c04-r037","Short answer","The new frog is only about 16 mm long. Explain why size alone would not make it a new species.","4.3","The frog is tiny, only about 16 mm long, but there is variation between individuals within every species, and size is one of the features that varies most. A small individual could simply be a young or unusually small member of one of the eight species of Microhyla known to live in India. Size becomes useful evidence only if many specimens are consistently outside the range of the known species. size varies within a species could be a young or small individual one measurement is not enough needs many specimens outside the known range"],[3,"y7c04-r038","Short answer","Explain why biologists use keys rather than only looking at pictures in a reference book.","4.4","A good way to start is to look at pictures in a reference book, or on the internet, and the biologist may be able to find a picture of the organism with its name, but this does not always work. Many species look very similar, and a book cannot show every one, so matching by eye can easily give the wrong name. A key asks about specific features in a fixed order, so the identification is based on checked characteristics rather than on a general impression. matching pictures does not always work many species look similar a key asks about specific features identification based on checked characteristics"],[3,"y7c04-r039","Short answer","Explain how a key works.","4.4","A key is a set of questions about the organism you want to identify, and the answer to each question takes you to another question. You work through all of the questions until you arrive at the name of the organism. Each answer removes a group of possibilities, so the number of organisms still in the running gets smaller at every step, which is why a short list of questions can separate many species. a set of questions about the organism each answer leads to the next question each step removes possibilities ends at the organism's name"],[3,"y7c04-r040","Short answer","Explain why this kind of key is called a dichotomous key.","4.4","Dichotomous means branching into two, and at every step the key offers exactly two choices, such as whether the organism has more than six legs or not. Because each answer splits the remaining organisms into two groups, the path through the key always branches in two. A key that offered three or four options at a step would not be dichotomous even if it worked. dichotomous means branching into two exactly two choices at every step each answer splits the group in two"],[3,"y7c04-r041","Short answer","A key can be drawn as branching questions or written as numbered pairs of statements. Explain what is the same and what is different.","4.4","Instead of a question, the key starts with a pair of statements to choose from, and instead of arrows pointing to where you go next, there is a number telling you which pair of statements to go to next. The features tested, the order of the tests and the answers reached are identical, so the two styles are the same key. Only the way the route is shown differs: arrows in one, numbers in the other. same features tested in the same order same answers reached arrows replaced by numbers only the presentation differs"],[3,"y7c04-r042","Short answer","Working through the key, the dragonfly is reached through steps 1a, 2a, 4a. Explain what each step has ruled out.","4.4","Choosing 1a confirms the animal has legs, which rules out every legless organism such as the earthworm. Choosing 2a confirms it has exactly six legs, which rules out organisms with more than six legs. Choosing 4a confirms it has four wings rather than two, which separates the dragonfly from the two-winged fly. Each step throws away a group, and the name at the end is what is left. 1a rules out legless organisms 2a rules out more than six legs 4a rules out two-winged insects the name is what remains"],[3,"y7c04-r043","Short answer","Explain how the key distinguishes the earthworm from the other organisms.","4.4","The earthworm is reached by first choosing that it does not have legs, which separates it from every insect in the key, and then by the statement that its body is made up of rings. Rings are a feature an earthworm has and the other legless organisms do not, so that single statement completes the identification. Each question is chosen because it splits the remaining group, not because it is interesting on its own. first choose that it has no legs separates it from the insects then body made up of rings questions are chosen to split the group"],[3,"y7c04-r044","Short answer","Explain why a key that identifies four species of fish still needs several questions.","4.4","Each question in a dichotomous key splits the remaining organisms into two groups, so one question can only separate the four fish into two pairs. A second question is then needed within each pair to reach a single name. Four organisms therefore need at least two questions on every route through the key, and more if the features do not split the group evenly. each question splits the group in two one question gives two pairs a second question separates each pair uneven splits need more questions"],[3,"y7c04-r045","Short answer","A key asks 'Are the stripes vertical?'. Explain why this is a good question and how it could be made better.","4.4","It is a good question because vertical and not vertical is a clear pair of alternatives that anyone looking at the fish can decide, and it splits the striped fish into two groups. It could be made better by being made harder to misread, for example by asking whether the stripes run from back to belly, since a reader holding the picture at an angle might disagree about vertical. A key only works if every user gives the same answer. clear pair of alternatives splits the striped group could be misread depending on orientation every user must give the same answer"],[3,"y7c04-r046","Short answer","Some people find the branching style of key easier and some prefer the numbered statements. Explain a reason for each preference.","4.4","The branching style shows the whole structure at once, so a user can see how many steps remain and where the route is going, which helps when learning how keys work. The numbered-statement style puts the two choices side by side in words, which makes the comparison sharper and takes far less space, so it can hold a much longer key. The better style depends on whether seeing the structure or comparing the statements matters more. branching shows the whole structure numbered pairs put choices side by side numbered style saves space for long keys depends on what the user needs"],[3,"y7c04-r047","Short answer","The key says to imagine you have the whole animal to look at, not just the pictures. Explain why this matters.","4.4","You will have to imagine that you have the whole animal to look at, not just these pictures, because a key asks about features such as the number of legs or wings that a single photograph may not show. If a feature is hidden, the user cannot answer the question and the key stops working. This is why a key should only ask about features that a person examining the actual organism can reliably see. a picture may hide a feature the user cannot answer the question the key stops working ask only about features that can be seen"],[3,"y7c04-r048","Short answer","Describe how to construct a key, and explain why you start by splitting the whole group in two.","4.5","You think of a way you can split the learners into two groups, for example male and female, then look at just one of these groups and think of a way to split those into two, repeating until every individual has been separated. You start with the whole group because a first question that only picks out one individual leaves all the others still together, so the work is not shared out; splitting the whole group evenly makes every later step smaller. split the whole group into two then split each group again repeat until each one is separated even splits keep later steps small"],[3,"y7c04-r049","Short answer","Explain why hair colour might be a good feature for a key but height would be a poor one.","4.5","You could use the colour of their hair, which is a good feature because each person's hair is one colour or another and any user would give the same answer. Height is poor because it is continuous: without a stated measurement, one user may call someone tall and another may not, so two users would take different routes and reach different names. A key needs features that give the same answer every time. hair colour gives a clear answer height is continuous users would disagree about tall a key needs features with the same answer every time"],[3,"y7c04-r050","Short answer","One of the peer-assessment criteria is that it is easy to choose between the statements each time. Explain why this is being marked.","4.5","A key only works if every user follows the same route, and that depends on each pair of statements being clear enough that no one hesitates or guesses. If a statement uses a vague word, two users can choose differently and arrive at two different names, which makes the key worthless even though it contains the right organisms. Clarity of each choice is therefore not a matter of style but of whether the key functions. every user must follow the same route vague statements let users choose differently different routes give different names clarity decides whether the key works"],[3,"y7c04-r051","Short answer","One criterion is that there are no more than three pairs of statements. Explain why a shorter key is better when it still works.","4.5","Each pair of statements splits the remaining group in two, so three pairs are enough to separate up to eight individuals and four learners need only two or three. A key with more steps than necessary asks the user to make extra judgements, and every extra judgement is another chance to choose wrongly. So the shortest key that still separates everyone is the most reliable as well as the quickest. each pair splits the group in two three pairs can separate eight individuals extra steps add chances to go wrong shortest working key is most reliable"],[3,"y7c04-r052","Short answer","The last criterion is simply that the key works. Explain how you would test this.","4.5","You test it by giving the key to someone who has not seen it before, together with the four photographs, and asking them to identify each learner without help. If every learner is reached correctly, the key works; if the user hesitates, takes a wrong turn or reaches a name that does not match, the step where that happened is the one to rewrite. Testing with a new user matters because the writer already knows the answers. give it to someone who has not seen it they identify each one unaided find the step where it fails the writer already knows the answers"],[3,"y7c04-r053","Short answer","Explain why a key must work when the person has only one leaf, with no others to compare it with.","4.5","Remember that your key must work when the person has only one leaf, with no others to compare it with, because someone using a key in the field usually finds a single specimen. That means every statement must describe the leaf on its own, such as whether the edge is toothed, and must never say that it is larger or darker than another leaf. A comparison the user cannot make stops the key at that step. users usually have one specimen statements must describe the leaf by itself no comparisons with other leaves an impossible comparison stops the key"],[3,"y7c04-r054","Short answer","Explain why exchanging your key with a partner is a useful part of making it.","4.5","You exchange your key with a partner and each of you tries to use the other's key, which tests it on someone who does not already know the answers. The writer can always follow their own key because they remember what each statement was meant to mean, so only another user can reveal a statement that is ambiguous or a step that leads nowhere. The feedback then shows exactly which part to rewrite. tests the key on someone who does not know the answers the writer can always follow their own key another user reveals ambiguity feedback shows what to rewrite"],[3,"y7c04-r055","Short answer","Before writing a key for five leaves you must list three features that tell them apart. Explain why this comes first.","4.5","You make a list of three features of the leaves that you can use to tell them apart before writing anything, because a key can only be built from features that actually differ between the specimens. If you start writing questions first, you may reach a step where the remaining leaves share every feature you have thought of and the key cannot continue. Choosing the features first shows whether the group can be separated at all. a key can only use features that differ otherwise a step cannot continue choosing features first shows it is possible avoids rewriting the key"],[3,"y7c04-r056","Short answer","Explain what happens to a key if one of your questions splits the group unevenly, for example picking out one individual from six.","4.5","The key still works, but the remaining five individuals are all still together and must be separated by later steps, so the key becomes long and lopsided: identifying the last individual takes five steps while the first takes one. Splitting the group as evenly as possible at each step keeps every route short. Repeating Step 2 until you have thought of ways to identify each learner is quicker when each split halves the group. the key still works but becomes long remaining individuals stay together routes become lopsided even splits keep every route short"],[3,"y7c04-r057","Short answer","After using each other's keys, students are asked what problems they had writing them. Explain why this reflection is worth doing.","4.5","The problems that come up while writing a key are nearly always the same few: choosing a feature that is vague, choosing one that needs a comparison, or splitting the group unevenly. Naming the problem you actually hit turns a single mistake into a rule you can apply to the next key, whereas simply correcting the key teaches nothing general. That is why the question asks what could each of you do better next time. the same few problems recur vague features, comparisons, uneven splits naming the problem gives a general rule correcting silently teaches nothing"],[3,"y7c04-o001","Short answer","What word do scientists use for a living thing?","4.1","An organism. organism"],[3,"y7c04-o002","Short answer","How many characteristics does a living organism have to show?","4.1","Seven. seven"],[3,"y7c04-o003","Short answer","List the seven characteristics of living organisms.","4.1","Nutrition, growth, movement, excretion, reproduction, respiration and sensitivity. nutrition growth movement excretion reproduction respiration sensitivity"],[3,"y7c04-o004","Short answer","How can you check that a person is alive?","4.1","Check whether they are breathing, or whether they have a heartbeat. breathing heartbeat"],[3,"y7c04-o005","Short answer","Explain why breathing and a heartbeat are not good tests for deciding whether something is alive.","4.1","Plants do not breathe and have no heart, but they are still alive. plants alive"],[3,"y7c04-o006","Short answer","What does nutrition mean?","4.1","Feeding - taking in materials from the environment for energy and growth. feeding taking in"],[3,"y7c04-o007","Short answer","How does a plant carry out nutrition?","4.1","It feeds by photosynthesis, making its own food. photosynthesis"],[3,"y7c04-o008","Short answer","What does growth mean?","4.1","A permanent increase in size. permanent increase in size"],[3,"y7c04-o020","Short answer","Give two ways in which a car is like a living organism.","4.1","It uses fuel and oxygen to release energy, which is like respiration, and it gives off waste gases including carbon dioxide, which is like excretion. fuel oxygen energy carbon dioxide"],[3,"y7c04-o025","Short answer","How does the size of a virus compare with the size of one of your cells?","4.2","A virus is much smaller than one of your cells. much smaller"],[3,"y7c04-o026","Short answer","What kind of microscope do you need in order to see a virus?","4.2","An electron microscope. electron microscope"],[3,"y7c04-o028","Short answer","In a typical electron-microscope photograph, roughly how many times bigger do the viruses look than they really are?","4.2","About 100 000 times bigger. 100000 times"],[3,"y7c04-o029","Short answer","Are viruses made of cells?","4.2","No. Viruses are not made of cells. not cells"],[3,"y7c04-o030","Short answer","Name two parts of a cell that a virus does not have.","4.2","A cell membrane and cytoplasm. cell membrane cytoplasm"],[3,"y7c04-o031","Short answer","What is the outer layer of a virus made of?","4.2","Protein. protein"],[3,"y7c04-o058","Short answer","What is a species?","4.3","A group of organisms that can reproduce together to produce fertile offspring. reproduce fertile offspring"],[3,"y7c04-o059","Short answer","What name do scientists give to each different kind of organism?","4.3","A species. species"],[3,"y7c04-o060","Short answer","How do organisms of the same species usually look, compared with organisms of other species?","4.3","They usually look similar to one another, and different from organisms of other species. similar different"],[3,"y7c04-o061","Short answer","Are all the organisms in a species identical to each other?","4.3","No. They share the same characteristics but they are not all identical. not identical"],[3,"y7c04-o062","Short answer","What is the name for the differences between individual organisms of the same species?","4.3","Variation. variation"],[3,"y7c04-o063","Short answer","Give one example of variation between individual Indian elephants.","4.3","Some have straighter tusks than others, and the pink markings on their skin are in different places. tusks markings"],[3,"y7c04-o064","Short answer","Explain why variation can make it difficult to decide whether two organisms belong to the same species.","4.3","Two individuals of the same species can look different from each other, so appearance alone does not settle it. look different appearance"],[3,"y7c04-o065","Short answer","What do scientists try to find out when they want to be sure whether two organisms belong to the same species?","4.3","Whether they can reproduce with one another. reproduce"],[3,"y7c04-o084","Short answer","What is a key, in biology?","4.4","A set of questions or statements about an organism that leads you to its name. questions name"],[3,"y7c04-o085","Short answer","What does the word dichotomous mean?","4.4","Branching into two. branching into two"],[3,"y7c04-o086","Short answer","Explain why a key with two choices at every step is called a dichotomous key.","4.4","Dichotomous means branching into two, and at every step there are exactly two statements or answers to choose between. two branching"],[3,"y7c04-o087","Short answer","Where should you start when you use a dichotomous key?","4.4","At the top of the key, with the first question. top first question"],[3,"y7c04-o088","Short answer","Describe how you use a dichotomous key to identify an organism.","4.4","Choose one organism, start at the top of the key and answer the first question, then follow the line to the next question. Keep going until you arrive at the name of the organism. one organism top keep going name"],[3,"y7c04-o089","Short answer","In the second style of key, what appears instead of a question?","4.4","A pair of statements to choose from. pair of statements"],[3,"y7c04-o090","Short answer","In the second style of key, what appears instead of arrows?","4.4","A number telling you which pair of statements to go to next. number next"],[3,"y7c04-o091","Short answer","Which steps of the key lead you to the dragonfly?","4.4","Steps 1a, 2a and 4a. 1a 2a 4a"],[3,"y7c04-o092","Short answer","Which steps of the key lead you to the earthworm?","4.4","Steps 1b and 3a. 1b 3a"],[3,"y7c04-o093","Short answer","In the key for five small animals, which animal has more than six legs?","4.4","The crab. crab"],[3,"y7c04-o105","Short answer","Describe the first step in constructing a key for a group of organisms.","4.5","Think of a way to split the organisms into two groups, and use that as your first question. split two groups"],[3,"y7c04-o106","Short answer","After you have split a group in two, what do you do next when constructing a key?","4.5","Take one of the two groups and think of a way to split that group into two as well. one of these groups split"],[3,"y7c04-o107","Short answer","When do you stop adding steps to a key?","4.5","When you have found a way to identify each organism in turn. each identify"],[3,"y7c04-o108","Short answer","Explain why 'Is the plant tall?' is a poor question to use in a key.","4.5","Tall compared with what? Different people would give different answers, so it does not give a clear yes or no. different people clear"],[3,"y7c04-o109","Short answer","Explain why 'Does the plant have dark green leaves?' is a poor question to use in a key.","4.5","Dark green is a matter of opinion, so two people could answer it differently. opinion differently"],[3,"y7c04-o110","Short answer","Explain why 'Do the flowers on the plant have five or more petals?' is a good question to use in a key.","4.5","You can count the petals, so everyone gets the same answer. count same answer"],[3,"y7c04-o111","Short answer","Explain why 'Are the leaves darker on the upper surface than on the lower surface?' is a good question to use in a key.","4.5","Both surfaces are on the same leaf, so you can always make the comparison even with only one specimen. same leaf one specimen"],[3,"y7c04-o112","Short answer","Give two features of a well-written key.","4.5","It is made up of pairs of statements that are easy to choose between, and there are no more than about three pairs. pairs of statements easy to choose"],[3,"y7c04-o113","Short answer","How can you find out whether the key you have written actually works?","4.5","Exchange it with a partner and ask them to try it out. partner try it out"],[3,"y7c04-o114","Short answer","Your partner cannot use your key to identify one of the organisms. What should you do?","4.5","Ask them for suggestions and use their ideas to change the key so that it works better. suggestions change"],[3,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the special microscope needed to see a virus?","4.2","Light microscope Electron microscope Digital camera Magnifying glass"],[3,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The outer coat of a virus is made of ____.","4.2","protein"],[3,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","What substance is found inside the virus that contains coded instructions?","4.2","DNA RNA Cytoplasm Cell membrane"],[3,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Viruses must get inside a living cell before they can ____ themselves.","4.2","replicate"],[3,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","Why are ligers considered infertile?","4.3","They die immediately after birth They cannot reproduce to have offspring They are too small to mate They only live in zoos"],[3,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","A species is a group of organisms that can reproduce together to produce ____ offspring.","4.3","fertile"],[3,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","What does the term 'dichotomous' mean in the context of a key?","4.4","Branching into two Having four parts Using only pictures Listing all names"],[3,"qwen-01-08","Gap fill","The process where food is broken down inside cells to provide energy is called ____.","4.1","respiration"],[3,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","Which gas do animals excrete when they breathe out?","4.1","Oxygen Nitrogen Carbon dioxide Hydrogen"],[3,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","The little pegs on the outside of a virus coat help it to ____ onto cells.","4.2","stick"],[3,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","Variation between individuals can make it difficult to decide whether two organisms belong to the same ____.","4.3","species"],[3,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the first step when using a dichotomous key to identify an organism?","4.4","Start at the bottom of the key Choose one organism you want to identify Look for the name of the organism first Count how many legs it has"],[3,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","A dichotomous key is called 'dichotomous' because it involves choosing between ____ statements or answers at each step.","4.4","two"],[3,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","In the numbered key style, what do you follow after answering a question?","4.4","A line to the next question A number telling you which pair of statements to go to next The name of the organism immediately A picture of the animal"],[3,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","An organism has no legs and its body is not made up of rings. The key identifies it as a ____.","4.4","slug"],[3,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","In the fish key, if a fish has stripes and they are vertical, it is identified as a ____.","4.4","zebra fish"],[3,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","What is the first step in constructing a key for four learners?","4.5","Write down their names Think of a way to split the learners into two groups Ask them about their hobbies Take a photograph of each learner"],[3,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","When constructing a key, after splitting the group into two, you look at just one of these groups and think of a way to split it into ____.","4.5","two"],[3,"qwen-02-10","Gap fill","In the peer assessment for keys, you give 2 marks if the key is made up of pairs of ____ to choose from.","4.5","statements"],[3,"qwen-02-11","Multiple choice","What can happen when biologists disagree about whether two organisms belong to the same species?","4.5","It makes the rainforest grow faster It can cause arguments about whether to protect a habitat It helps farmers plant more coffee trees It makes the birds extinct immediately"],[3,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the definition of respiration in living organisms?","4.1","Getting rid of waste materials Breaking down nutrients to release energy Taking in materials from the environment for growth Changing the position of part or all of the body"],[3,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The characteristic of living organisms that involves getting rid of waste materials is called ____.","4.1","excretion"],[3,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","Mules are the offspring of a horse and a donkey, but they are ____.","4.3","infertile"],[3,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","According to the key for identifying fish, if a fish has no gill slits and long spines on its top fin, what is it?","4.4","Basking shark Greenland shark John Dory Sea bream"],[3,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","In the key for identifying tree leaves, if a leaf is made up of many small leaflets, it is identified as ____.","4.4","rowan"],[3,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","The process of taking in materials from the environment for energy and growth is called ____.","4.1","nutrition"],[4,"y7c05-f01","Diagram labelling","This apparatus separates water from a mixture of food dye and water. What is labelled {n}?","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f02","Diagram labelling","Name the piece of apparatus labelled {n}.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f03","Diagram labelling","Universal indicator turns a different colour at each pH. What type of substance is labelled {n}?","5.6",""],[4,"y7c05-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that shows where cold water enters the condenser.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label that shows where the separated water is collected.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label that shows where heat is applied to the mixture.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of apparatus that catches the clear liquid that passes through the filter paper.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of apparatus that the clear liquid is heated in, so the salt is left behind.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of safety equipment that protects your eyes from spitting liquid.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of apparatus that the evaporating dish stands on while it is heated.","5.4",""],[4,"y7c05-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the pH of a substance that is neither an acid nor an alkali.","5.6",""],[4,"y7c05-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the type of substance that turns universal indicator purple.","5.6",""],[4,"y7c05-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the type of substance that has a pH of about 4.","5.6",""],[4,"y7c05-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the arrow that shows the force being applied to the metal.","5.3",""],[4,"y7c05-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the block of atoms that shows what the metal looks like after the layers have slid.","5.3",""],[4,"y7c05-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the atom that disrupts the regular pattern in this alloy.","5.3",""],[4,"y7c05-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the artificial hip joint in this X-ray.","5.3",""],[4,"y7c05-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the iron nails that the magnet has attracted.","5.1",""],[4,"y7c05-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the fruit that contains citric acid and tastes sour.","5.5",""],[4,"y7c05-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the bottle of cola, a drink that is acidic.","5.5",""],[4,"y7c05-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the bottle of thick bleach, a cleaning product that contains an alkali.","5.5",""],[4,"y7c05-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the box of washing soda.","5.5",""],[4,"y7c05-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the strip that was dipped into the most acidic liquid.","5.6",""],[4,"y7c05-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the strip that was dipped into the most alkaline liquid.","5.6",""],[4,"y7c05-r001","Short answer","Explain why iron is chosen for bridges rather than a non-metal.","5.1","Iron is used for bridges because it is strong. Metals are strong and tough, they do not shatter when dropped and they do not crack easily, and they can hold large weights without breaking, which is exactly what a bridge must do. Non-metals that are solids are brittle and may shatter if they are dropped, so they could fail suddenly under a heavy load. metals are strong and tough hold large weights without breaking do not crack easily solid non-metals are brittle"],[4,"y7c05-r002","Short answer","Explain the difference between malleable and ductile.","5.1","Metals are malleable, which means they can be hammered into shape, and metals are ductile, which means that they can be drawn out into wires. Both describe a metal changing shape without breaking, but they describe different ways of doing it: hammering squashes the metal flatter, while drawing pulls it longer and thinner. A material could in principle be good at one and poor at the other, which is why two separate words are used. malleable means hammered into shape ductile means drawn out into wires both change shape without breaking squashing versus pulling"],[4,"y7c05-r003","Short answer","Explain why copper, rather than any other material, is chosen for electrical wiring.","5.1","Copper is used for electrical wiring because it conducts electricity well and is flexible. Conducting electricity well means an electric current can flow through the wire without much being wasted, and being flexible, which follows from copper being ductile and malleable, means the wire can be bent around corners and pulled through walls without snapping. A material with only one of the two properties would not do the job. conducts electricity well current can flow through it flexible so it can be bent both properties are needed"],[4,"y7c05-r004","Short answer","Explain why a metal spoon feels cold when you pick it up, even in a warm room.","5.1","Metals are good conductors of heat, so when you touch them they conduct heat energy away from the hand, which is why they feel cold. The spoon is not actually colder than the room; it simply removes heat from your skin faster than a non-metal object would. A plastic spoon at the same temperature feels warm because most non-metals do not conduct heat energy well and take heat from your hand only slowly. metals conduct heat away from the hand not actually colder than the room your skin loses heat quickly non-metals conduct heat poorly"],[4,"y7c05-r005","Short answer","Explain why a cooking pan is made of steel but its handle is made of a non-metal.","5.1","Steel conducts heat well, which is useful for cooking pans, because heat must pass quickly from the flame through the base into the food. Most non-metals do not conduct heat energy well, and this is very useful because some of them can be used to make handles for cooking pans. The same object uses two materials because the base and the handle need opposite thermal properties. steel conducts heat into the food non-metals conduct heat poorly the handle stays cool enough to hold opposite properties needed in one object"],[4,"y7c05-r006","Short answer","Explain why the covering of an electric cable is made from a non-metal.","5.1","Most non-metals do not conduct electricity, and this is very useful because some can be used to make coverings for electric plugs and cables. They are known as insulators, which means they do not conduct heat or electricity, so the current stays in the metal wire and cannot pass through the covering into a person touching it. A metal covering would conduct the current straight to whoever held the cable. non-metals do not conduct electricity insulators do not conduct heat or electricity current stays in the wire a metal covering would be dangerous"],[4,"y7c05-r007","Short answer","Explain why the ringing sound test is not by itself enough to identify a metal.","5.1","Metals make a ringing sound like a bell when they are hit and the word for this is sonorous, but bottles and cups also make a ringing sound when they are hit, and they are not made of metal. So a ringing sound is produced by more than one kind of material, and the test cannot separate them. A reliable identification needs several tests together, such as conducting electricity, being malleable and being shiny. sonorous means it rings when hit glass bottles also ring one test does not separate them use several tests together"],[4,"y7c05-r008","Short answer","Explain why the shininess test can be misleading when you test an old piece of metal.","5.1","Metals are shiny when they are freshly cut or polished, but the surface of most metals will become dull after a while, because the surface reacts with the air. An old piece of metal can therefore look dull even though it is a metal, and a student using shininess alone would classify it wrongly. Cutting or polishing the surface first exposes the unreacted metal underneath and makes the test fair. metals are shiny when freshly cut or polished surfaces become dull over time an old metal looks dull polish the surface before testing"],[4,"y7c05-r009","Short answer","Mercury is a metal that is liquid at room temperature. Explain why this does not break the rule about metals.","5.1","Most metals do not melt easily, and they have high melting points and high boiling points, so a lot of heat is needed to melt metal. Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature, so it is a single exception to a general pattern rather than a counter-example that destroys it. Properties of metals are typical properties, not laws that every metal must obey. most metals have high melting points mercury is the only liquid metal a single exception to a pattern properties are typical, not absolute"],[4,"y7c05-r010","Short answer","Explain why 'metals are magnetic' is a statement that needs correcting.","5.1","Some metals are magnetic, and iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are magnetic, but most metals are not. Copper, aluminium, gold and silver are all metals and none of them is attracted to a magnet, so being magnetic cannot be used as a test for metals in general. It is a property of a small group of metals, which is why the textbook says some rather than all. only some metals are magnetic iron, steel, nickel and cobalt copper and aluminium are not cannot be used as a general test"],[4,"y7c05-r011","Short answer","Explain why a solid non-metal shatters when it is dropped but a metal does not.","5.1","Non-metals that are solids are brittle, and if you drop them they may shatter, while metals are strong and tough, they do not shatter when dropped and they do not crack easily. The difference is what the material does with the force of the impact: a metal can change shape slightly and absorb it, because metals are malleable, whereas a brittle solid cannot change shape and so breaks apart instead. solid non-metals are brittle metals do not shatter when dropped metals change shape and absorb the force brittle solids cannot, so they break"],[4,"y7c05-r012","Short answer","Explain what an insulator is and why one material can insulate against both heat and electricity.","5.1","They are known as insulators, which means they do not conduct heat or electricity. In most non-metals the same feature of the material blocks both, because both conduction of heat and conduction of electricity depend on particles being free to carry energy or charge through the material, and non-metals do not have them. That is why a plastic handle keeps heat out of your hand and a plastic cable covering keeps current in the wire. insulator does not conduct heat or electricity both need free particles to carry energy or charge non-metals lack them same material does both jobs"],[4,"y7c05-r013","Short answer","Explain why chlorine is added to swimming pool water even though it is dangerous.","5.1","Chlorine is used to kill bacteria, and it can be dissolved in water and then added to swimming pools. Non-metals are often very useful because of the chemical reactions they have with other substances, and in this case the reaction that makes chlorine dangerous is exactly the reaction that kills bacteria in the water. It is safe to use because it is diluted to a very low concentration, so it harms bacteria but not swimmers. chlorine kills bacteria useful because of its chemical reactions the same reactivity makes it dangerous safe because it is very dilute"],[4,"y7c05-r014","Short answer","Explain what is meant by saying there is a lot of variation between non-metals.","5.1","There is a lot of variation between non-metals: many non-metals are gases, while others such as sulfur, carbon and silicon are solids, and their uses are completely different, from filling balloons with helium to making computer chips from silicon. Metals share a fairly consistent set of properties, but non-metals are really defined by not being metals, which is why they have far less in common with each other. non-metals range from gases to solids very different uses metals are more consistent non-metals are defined by not being metals"],[4,"y7c05-r015","Short answer","A material is dull, brittle and does not conduct electricity. Explain what it is and how confident you can be.","5.2","It is a non-metal. Each of the three observations fits a non-metal: they do not reflect light very well, non-metals that are solids are brittle, and most non-metals do not conduct electricity. Three independent tests all pointing the same way is much stronger evidence than any one of them alone, because a metal would have had to fail all three, which is very unlikely. all three properties fit a non-metal dull, brittle, non-conducting three independent tests agree much stronger than one test"],[4,"y7c05-r016","Short answer","Explain which test is the most useful for distinguishing metals from non-metals, and why.","5.2","Testing whether the material conducts electricity is the most useful, because metals are good conductors of electricity while most non-metals do not conduct electricity, so the two groups give clearly opposite results. It also gives a definite yes or no rather than a judgement, unlike shininess, which fades as the surface becomes dull, or the ringing sound, which glass also produces. A good test is one that separates the groups and that every user reads the same way. conducting electricity separates the groups gives a definite yes or no shininess fades and glass also rings a good test is unambiguous"],[4,"y7c05-r017","Short answer","Explain why some materials are difficult to place in the metals or non-metals group.","5.2","Some materials give mixed results, because the properties are typical rather than absolute: mercury is a metal but is liquid at room temperature, carbon is a non-metal but conducts electricity, and an old metal surface looks dull. A material also may be an alloy or a compound rather than a pure element. When tests disagree, the right response is to do more tests and weigh them together rather than to trust the first result. properties are typical, not absolute carbon conducts but is a non-metal alloys and compounds complicate it do more tests and weigh them together"],[4,"y7c05-r018","Short answer","Describe how you would test whether a material conducts electricity, and say what makes the test fair.","5.2","You build a simple circuit with a cell and a lamp and leave a gap, then put the material into the gap: if the lamp lights, a current is flowing and the material conducts. The test is fair only if the surface of the sample is clean, since a dull layer may stop contact, if the contacts press firmly on the sample each time, and if the same circuit and the same length of sample are used for every material tested. circuit with a cell, a lamp and a gap lamp lights if the material conducts clean surface and firm contact same circuit and sample size each time"],[4,"y7c05-r019","Short answer","Write down two things a metal can do better than a non-metal, and explain why each matters in a real object.","5.2","A metal conducts electricity, which is why copper is used for electrical wiring, and a metal can be bent and hammered into shape without breaking, which is why metal sheets can be pressed into car bodies and coins can be stamped with patterns. A solid non-metal would shatter under the hammer and would not carry the current, so neither object could be made from one. metals conduct electricity metals can be bent and hammered wiring and stamped coins non-metals would shatter and not conduct"],[4,"y7c05-r020","Short answer","Explain what an alloy is and why it is a mixture rather than a compound.","5.3","In an alloy the atoms of the different metals mix but do not bond together, so the elements are simply mixed among each other rather than joined chemically. Because there is no chemical bonding, the proportions can be varied continuously, which is why there are many different types of brass. A compound would have a fixed formula and a single melting point, which an alloy does not. atoms mix but do not bond together no chemical bonding proportions can be varied compounds have a fixed formula"],[4,"y7c05-r021","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why a pure metal is soft enough to hammer into shape.","5.3","In a pure metal, the atoms are all the same size and arranged in regular rows, so the layers can slide over one another easily. This is what happens when the metal is hit with a hammer, and it also happens when the metal is stretched out into a wire. Sliding layers mean the metal changes shape instead of breaking, which is what malleable and ductile describe. atoms all the same size in regular rows layers slide over one another easily happens when hammered or stretched shape changes instead of breaking"],[4,"y7c05-r022","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why an alloy is harder than the pure metal it is made from.","5.3","Adding atoms of another element puts in atoms of a different size, which disrupts the regular pattern, so the atoms cannot slip past one another easily now and the layers of atoms cannot slide over each other as easily. Since changing shape depends on layers sliding, blocking that sliding makes the alloy a lot harder and stronger than the original metal. Nothing has been bonded; the arrangement has simply been spoiled. different sized atoms disrupt the regular pattern layers cannot slide as easily shape change depends on sliding so the alloy is harder and stronger"],[4,"y7c05-r023","Short answer","Explain why steel is more useful than pure iron.","5.3","Pure iron is not hard enough to be very useful, but when it is mixed with other elements to form steel it is much harder, because the carbon atoms are a different size from the iron atoms and disrupt the regular rows so the layers cannot slide past one another. Steel is an unusual alloy because one of the elements in the mixture, carbon, is not a metal, but the same particle explanation applies. pure iron is not hard enough carbon atoms are a different size disrupt the rows so layers cannot slide steel is harder"],[4,"y7c05-r024","Short answer","Explain why cutlery is made from steel containing chromium and nickel rather than ordinary steel.","5.3","Sometimes chromium and nickel are also added to steel, and this type of steel does not rust and is used for cutlery. Cutlery is repeatedly washed and left wet, so ordinary steel would rust and become both unpleasant and weaker, while the extra elements give a surface that resists reacting with water and air. The alloy is chosen for the conditions the object will meet, not only for hardness. chromium and nickel added this steel does not rust cutlery is repeatedly wet alloy chosen for the conditions"],[4,"y7c05-r025","Short answer","Explain why coins must be made from an alloy rather than a pure metal.","5.3","Coins must be hardwearing but also malleable enough to be stamped with complex patterns, and pure metals are too soft to withstand all the wear they get. An alloy blocks the sliding of layers enough to resist everyday wear, while still allowing the metal to change shape under the heavy force of a stamping press. A pure metal would take the pattern easily but would wear smooth within a few years. coins must be hardwearing and stampable pure metals are too soft alloy resists wear still changes shape under a press"],[4,"y7c05-r026","Short answer","Explain why silver-coloured coins are not made of silver.","5.3","The coins that look silver are not made of silver, because it is too soft and far too expensive; the silver coins are made of alloys containing copper and nickel. The colour a coin needs is only a matter of appearance, which an alloy can provide, while the properties a coin really needs are hardness and low cost. Choosing the material by appearance alone would give a coin that wore away and cost more than its value. silver is too soft and too expensive coins made of copper and nickel alloys colour is only appearance hardness and cost decide the material"],[4,"y7c05-r027","Short answer","Explain why a wedding ring is made from 18 carat gold rather than 24 carat gold.","5.3","Pure gold is 24 carat, meaning 24 parts out of 24 are gold, while 18 carat gold has 18 parts out of 24 of pure gold and six parts of other metals such as copper, silver or zinc. If you used pure gold for something like a wedding ring, which gets a lot of wear and tear, it would wear away, because pure gold is soft and its layers slide easily. The added metals disrupt the arrangement and make the ring hard enough to last. 24 carat is pure gold, 18 carat is 18 parts in 24 pure gold is soft and wears away other metals disrupt the arrangement the alloy lasts"],[4,"y7c05-r028","Short answer","Explain why aircraft are built from duralumin rather than pure aluminium.","5.3","The metal used to build planes needs to be light but very strong, and by adding magnesium and copper an alloy called duralumin is formed that is about five times stronger than pure aluminium. The added atoms are a different size, so they disrupt the regular rows and stop the layers sliding, which gives strength without adding much mass. Pure aluminium is light enough but would deform under the forces on a wing. planes need light but strong metal duralumin is five times stronger added atoms block layer sliding pure aluminium would deform"],[4,"y7c05-r029","Short answer","Copper and zinc each have one melting point but brass has a range. Explain why.","5.3","There are many different types of brass, which are made by using different amounts of copper and zinc, so there is no specific melting point for brass; it depends on the proportions of copper and zinc that have been used. An alloy is a mixture, not a compound, so its composition is not fixed and each composition melts at its own temperature. Copper and zinc are pure elements, so each has a single fixed melting point. brass compositions vary no fixed proportions in a mixture each composition melts at its own temperature pure elements have one melting point"],[4,"y7c05-r030","Short answer","Explain why a range of temperatures is also given for the melting point of bronze.","5.3","Mixing different amounts of copper and tin makes the different forms of bronze, and each different mixture has its own different melting point. The name bronze therefore refers to a family of mixtures rather than to one substance, so a single value would be wrong for most of them. This is a general feature of alloys and is one way to tell a mixture from a compound. different amounts of copper and tin each mixture has its own melting point bronze is a family of mixtures a general feature of alloys"],[4,"y7c05-r031","Short answer","Bronze is harder than either copper or tin. Explain how a mixture can be harder than both of its ingredients.","5.3","Bronze is an alloy made by mixing copper and tin, and the atoms of the two metals are different sizes. In either pure metal the atoms are all the same size and arranged in regular rows so the layers slide easily, but in the mixture the different sized atoms disrupt the regular pattern, so the layers cannot slide over each other. The hardness comes from the disorder created by mixing, which is why it exceeds both pure metals. copper and tin atoms are different sizes pure metals have regular rows that slide mixing disrupts the pattern hardness comes from the disorder"],[4,"y7c05-r032","Short answer","In the bubble raft model the bubbles represent the atoms in a metal. Explain what the model shows well and what it cannot show.","5.3","It shows well that equal-sized particles pack into regular rows and that whole rows can slide past each other when a force is applied, which is exactly the particle explanation of malleability. It cannot show the forces that actually hold metal atoms together, it is two-dimensional while a metal is three-dimensional, and bubbles burst while atoms do not. A model is judged by which features it represents correctly, not by looking like the real thing. shows regular packing and sliding rows cannot show the forces between atoms two-dimensional and bubbles burst judged by the features it represents"],[4,"y7c05-r033","Short answer","Explain what properties an alloy used inside the body must have.","5.3","It must be hard and strong enough to take the forces a joint carries for many years without wearing away, since pure metals are too soft to withstand all the wear they get. It must also not react with the fluids in the body, so it does not corrode or release substances into the tissues, and it must not be attacked in a way that would weaken it. An X-ray of the pelvis showing a hip replacement shows a part expected to last decades. hard and strong for many years of force must not react with body fluids must not corrode or weaken expected to last decades"],[4,"y7c05-r036","Short answer","Explain why the substances in a mixture can be separated but those in a compound cannot be separated so easily.","5.4","Mixtures contain different substances that are not combined together chemically, so each substance keeps its own properties and can be removed using a property that the others do not share. That is why iron can be pulled out of a mixture with sulfur using a magnet. In a compound the elements are chemically joined and the compound has its own properties, so no physical method will pick one element out of it. mixtures are not combined chemically each substance keeps its own properties separate using a property others lack compounds are chemically joined"],[4,"y7c05-r037","Short answer","Explain the general rule for choosing a way to separate a mixture, using iron and sulfur as the example.","5.4","You used the difference in the properties of iron and sulfur to separate them: iron is magnetic and sulfur is not magnetic, so a magnet removes one and leaves the other. The general rule is to find a property that one substance has and the other does not, then use a method that acts on that property. If the two substances share every property you can test, you cannot separate them by that route. find a property one has and the other lacks iron is magnetic, sulfur is not use a method acting on that property no difference means no separation"],[4,"y7c05-r038","Short answer","Explain how leaving a mixture of water and copper sulfate in a warm room separates it.","5.4","If it is left in a warm room, the water evaporates and leaves the copper sulfate behind in the dish. The two substances differ in how easily they change to a gas: water evaporates at room temperature while copper sulfate is a solid that does not, so only the water leaves. The copper sulfate is recovered as crystals, but the water is lost to the air, so this method is only suitable when the liquid is not wanted. water evaporates, copper sulfate does not difference in changing to a gas solid left behind as crystals the water is lost to the air"],[4,"y7c05-r039","Short answer","Explain why a condenser is used instead of simple evaporation when separating food dye from water.","5.4","The food dye and water have different properties that allow you to separate them: they have different boiling points. Simple evaporation would leave the dye behind but the water would escape into the air, whereas the condenser cools the gas so it condenses back into liquid water and collects in the beaker. The condenser is used when both substances are wanted, not just the solid. the two have different boiling points evaporation loses the water to the air condenser recovers the liquid water used when both substances are wanted"],[4,"y7c05-r040","Short answer","Describe what happens to the water in the flask when the mixture is heated, using the idea of state change.","5.4","The water and food dye mixture is heated and boils, and the liquid water reaches the temperature where it changes state and becomes a gas. Water that is in the gas state is called steam when it has been formed by boiling the water. The dye has a much higher boiling point, so it is not turned into a gas at this temperature and the food dye remains in the heated container. heated until it reaches boiling changes state to a gas gas from boiling is called steam the dye stays behind"],[4,"y7c05-r041","Short answer","Explain how the condenser turns the steam back into liquid water.","5.4","The gas travels along the tube into the condenser, and the cold water that is circulating around the outside of the condenser cools the gas down. Removing energy from the gas slows the particles until they can no longer stay apart, so this makes the gas condense back into liquid water, and the liquid water collects in the beaker. The circulating water must keep flowing, or the condenser would warm up and stop working. cold water circulates around the outside energy removed from the gas gas condenses back to liquid water must keep flowing"],[4,"y7c05-r042","Short answer","Marcus has swept broken glass and copper sulfate crystals into a container. Explain how to separate them.","5.4","Add water to the container and stir: the copper sulfate dissolves in water but the glass does not, which is the property difference that makes separation possible. Filter the mixture so the glass pieces stay on the filter paper while the copper sulfate solution passes through, then leave the solution in an evaporating dish in a warm place so the water evaporates and leaves clean copper sulfate crystals. copper sulfate dissolves, glass does not stir with water filter to remove the glass evaporate to recover the crystals"],[4,"y7c05-r043","Short answer","Explain the safety precautions Marcus must take while separating the glass from the copper sulfate.","5.4","He must not pick up the broken glass with bare hands, so he should use a brush and dustpan or tongs and wear gloves, because sharp glass cuts skin. He should wear eye protection throughout, since copper sulfate is harmful and stirring or heating can splash it. When evaporating he should heat gently and stop before the dish is completely dry, so that hot solid does not spit out of the dish. use a brush or tongs, not bare hands wear gloves and eye protection copper sulfate is harmful heat gently and stop before fully dry"],[4,"y7c05-r044","Short answer","Zara has a mixture of sand, salt and water. Explain the order of the steps needed to separate all three.","5.4","Filter first: the sand does not dissolve, so it stays on the filter paper while the salt solution passes through. Then separate the salt from the water, either by evaporating the water to leave the salt, or by using a condenser if the water is also wanted. The order matters because filtering removes the solid that would otherwise be left mixed with the salt at the end. filter first to remove the sand sand does not dissolve then evaporate or distil to get the salt order matters for a clean separation"],[4,"y7c05-r045","Short answer","Explain how Marcus can be sure the copper sulfate crystals he returns are clean and dry.","5.4","Clean means no glass is left with them, which he checks by filtering the solution so that every piece of glass is caught on the filter paper before the solution is evaporated. Dry means no water remains, which he achieves by letting the crystals stand in a warm place until no further change in appearance occurs. Heating strongly instead would risk spitting and could change the crystals themselves. filter so all glass is removed evaporate until no water remains leave in a warm place strong heating risks spitting"],[4,"y7c05-r046","Short answer","Explain why acids in the laboratory carry a hazard warning label.","5.5","If strong acid gets onto your skin, it will dissolve the skin and you will get a chemical burn, so the bottles have a hazard warning label to tell you before you open them what the substance can do. The label works because it is understood without reading a long description, which matters when several bottles are in use. It is a warning about what to prevent, not a description of what has happened. strong acid dissolves skin causes a chemical burn label warns before the bottle is opened understood at a glance"],[4,"y7c05-r047","Short answer","Explain why a dilute acid is safer than a concentrated one but still needs eye protection.","5.5","Acids can be diluted with water, and this makes them less dangerous, because there is less acid in the same volume of liquid. But dilute acids are still harmful; they can irritate your skin and eyes, so the hazard is reduced rather than removed. Eyes are far more easily damaged than skin and cannot be washed as easily, so eye protection is required even at concentrations that only irritate skin. dilution reduces the amount of acid present less dangerous but still harmful dilute acid irritates skin and eyes eyes are more easily damaged"],[4,"y7c05-r048","Short answer","Explain why washing a spill with lots of water is the right response to spilt acid.","5.5","If you spill acid, wash the area with lots of water, because the water dilutes the acid, and a more dilute acid does less damage. Using a lot of water matters because a small amount would only spread the acid without reducing its concentration much, and the water also carries the acid away from the skin. Trying to neutralise it with another chemical instead would risk a reaction that releases heat. water dilutes the acid dilute acid does less damage a lot of water is needed neutralising instead could release heat"],[4,"y7c05-r049","Short answer","Explain what is meant by saying acids and alkalis are chemical opposites.","5.5","Acids and alkalis are chemical opposites, and they cancel each other out when they are mixed together, so adding an alkali to an acid reduces the acidity and adding enough gives a substance that is neither. The opposite behaviour is not about how dangerous they are, since strong alkalis are dangerous too and also dissolve skin, but about the effect each has on the other when mixed. they cancel each other out when mixed adding alkali reduces acidity enough gives neither not about how dangerous they are"],[4,"y7c05-r050","Short answer","Explain why strong alkalis need the same precautions as strong acids.","5.5","If strong alkali gets on your skin, it dissolves your skin, just as a strong acid does, and alkalis are harmful if you get them in your eyes, so you should always wear eye protection when using alkalis. Students often treat alkalis as the safe ones because cleaning products contain them, but sodium hydroxide is a strong alkali and is corrosive. Being the chemical opposite of an acid does not make a substance gentle. strong alkali dissolves skin harmful in the eyes always wear eye protection opposite of acid does not mean safe"],[4,"y7c05-r051","Short answer","Explain the reason for standing up when handling chemicals.","5.5","You should stand up to work, so that if you spill something it does not spill on to you. Someone sitting down has their legs directly under the bench edge and cannot move away quickly, so a spill runs off the bench straight onto them. Standing means the spill lands on the bench or floor and the person can step back, which turns a possible injury into a mess to clean up. a spill runs off the bench onto a seated person standing allows stepping back spill lands on bench or floor injury becomes a cleaning job"],[4,"y7c05-r052","Short answer","Explain why a bottle top is placed upside down on the bench.","5.5","You take the top of the bottle and place it upside down on the work surface, so that it does not get acid onto the surface or dirt into the acid. Placing it the other way up would put the wet inner face on the bench, spreading acid where someone will put their hand, and would pick up dust that would then be carried back into the bottle and contaminate the chemical. inner face must not touch the bench otherwise acid spreads onto the surface and dirt is carried into the bottle prevents contamination"],[4,"y7c05-r053","Short answer","Explain why bottle tops are replaced as soon as you have finished with the bottle.","5.5","You replace the bottle top as soon as you have finished using the bottle, because this prevents spills and reduces the risk of replacing the wrong top on the wrong bottle. An open bottle can be knocked over, and if several tops are left off at once they become mixed, which contaminates both chemicals and can make a bottle's label no longer true. The habit removes both risks at once. open bottles can be knocked over several loose tops get mixed up wrong top contaminates the chemical the label becomes untrue"],[4,"y7c05-r054","Short answer","A truck loading acid has an orange warning notice placed nearby. Explain why this is important.","5.5","The notice tells anyone approaching, including emergency services who arrive after an accident, what substance is present before they get close enough to be harmed. Strong acid will dissolve skin and cause a chemical burn, so someone who treats a spill as if it were water could be injured. The notice also tells responders how to deal with a spill, which they could not know from looking at the liquid. warns people before they get close emergency services know what is present acid causes chemical burns tells responders how to deal with it"],[4,"y7c05-r055","Short answer","Suggest what could be done if acid is spilt on the ground at the factory.","5.5","Keep people away from the area first, since the spill is corrosive, and use eye protection and protective clothing when approaching. The acid should then be diluted with large amounts of water, as water dilutes the acid and makes it less dangerous, or covered with a substance that neutralises it, since acids and alkalis cancel each other out when they are mixed together. The diluted liquid must then be contained rather than allowed to run into drains. keep people away wear protection dilute with large amounts of water or neutralise contain it rather than letting it reach drains"],[4,"y7c05-r056","Short answer","Three identical-looking containers hold water, acid and alkali. Explain how an indicator solves the problem.","5.6","These three containers all look the same, so appearance gives no information, but you can tell them apart when you add a few drops of an indicator, because an indicator turns one colour in an acid and a different colour in an alkali. The indicator converts a chemical property that cannot be seen into a colour that can, which is why a property of the substance becomes visible without tasting or touching it. appearance gives no information indicator gives different colours in acid and alkali turns an invisible property into a visible one no need to taste or touch"],[4,"y7c05-r057","Short answer","Explain how red cabbage juice would let you identify lemon juice.","5.6","Red cabbage indicator turns red in acids, blue in water and yellow in alkalis, so adding a few drops to lemon juice gives red. Lemons and limes taste sour and they contain citric acid, so the red colour confirms an acid, and the test does this without anyone having to taste the liquid. A colour on a three-way scale tells you which of the three groups the liquid belongs to. red cabbage turns red in acid blue in water and yellow in alkali lemon juice contains citric acid identified without tasting"],[4,"y7c05-r058","Short answer","Explain what litmus shows when it turns purple, and what this tells you about water.","5.6","Litmus turns purple when it is in a neutral substance, and a neutral substance is one that is neither acid nor alkali. Litmus turns purple in water, and this means water is neither an acid nor an alkali. Purple is therefore not a failure of the test but a third result, telling you that the liquid belongs to neither group. purple means neutral neutral is neither acid nor alkali litmus turns purple in water a third result, not a failed test"],[4,"y7c05-r059","Short answer","Explain why universal indicator is more useful than litmus.","5.6","Litmus and other simple indicators just show if a substance is an acid or an alkali, while universal indicator shows how acidic or alkaline a substance is. Universal indicator is made up of a mixture of different indicators, so it can change to many different colours and each colour corresponds to a pH value. Litmus therefore sorts liquids into two or three groups, while universal indicator places each one on a scale. litmus only shows acid or alkali universal indicator shows how acidic or alkaline made of a mixture of indicators gives a pH value on a scale"],[4,"y7c05-r060","Short answer","Explain why universal indicator has to be made from a mixture of different indicators.","5.6","Universal indicator is made up of a mixture of different indicators, because a single indicator changes colour only over a narrow part of the range and gives just one colour on each side of that point. By combining several indicators, each changing at a different pH, the mixture produces a different overall colour at each pH, so universal indicator can change to many different colours across the whole scale. one indicator changes over a narrow range gives only two colours several indicators change at different pH values the mixture gives a different colour at each pH"],[4,"y7c05-r061","Short answer","Explain what the pH scale measures and why a number is more useful than a colour name.","5.6","The strength of acids and alkalis is measured on the pH scale, and the pH of a substance is one of the chemical properties of that substance. A number can be compared, ordered and recorded, so two liquids can be ranked and a change can be tracked over time, whereas a colour name depends on the person describing it and cannot be put in order reliably. The colour chart exists only to turn the observation into the number. pH measures the strength of acids and alkalis a chemical property of the substance numbers can be compared and ordered colour names depend on the describer"],[4,"y7c05-r062","Short answer","Explain why a home-made indicator cannot tell you whether coffee or cola is an acid or an alkali.","5.6","An indicator works by showing a colour change, but coffee and cola are themselves strongly coloured, so the dark brown of the liquid hides whatever colour the indicator turns. The observation the test depends on cannot be made, so no conclusion can be drawn either way. A pH meter or a test on a diluted, paler sample would be needed instead. the liquid is already strongly coloured the indicator colour is hidden the observation cannot be made use a pH meter or dilute the sample"],[4,"y7c05-r063","Short answer","Describe how you could make your own indicator and how you would check that it works.","5.6","Indicators can be made from the brightly coloured berries, flowers and other parts of plants, so you crush the plant material with a small amount of water, warm it gently and filter the liquid to get a coloured solution. To check it works, you add a few drops to a known acid such as dilute hydrochloric acid, to water, and to a known alkali such as sodium hydroxide, and see whether it gives three distinguishable colours. crush coloured plant material with water warm and filter to get the solution test on a known acid, water and a known alkali must give distinguishable colours"],[4,"y7c05-r064","Short answer","Zara tests a liquid with several indicators and must suggest its pH. Explain how several indicators can pin down a value.","5.6","Each simple indicator changes colour at its own point on the scale, so its colour tells you only whether the liquid is above or below that point. Using several indicators gives several such statements, and the pH must lie in the range that satisfies all of them at once, which is much narrower than any one indicator gives. This is the same principle that makes universal indicator work, since it is a mixture of different indicators. each indicator changes at its own point gives an above-or-below statement combining them narrows the range the principle behind universal indicator"],[4,"y7c05-r065","Short answer","Explain why acidity is described as a chemical property rather than a physical one.","5.6","The acidity or alkalinity of a substance is a chemical property of that substance, because it describes how the substance behaves when it meets other substances, not what it looks like or how it feels. An acid and an alkali cancel each other out when they are mixed together, which is a change in the substances themselves, whereas melting or dissolving would leave the substance chemically the same. This is why acidity is found by reaction with an indicator rather than by inspection. describes how it behaves with other substances acids and alkalis cancel each other out the substances themselves change found by reaction, not inspection"],[4,"y7c05-o030","Short answer","Give two differences between the appearance of metals and non-metals.","5.2","Metals are shiny but non-metals are dull; metals do not shatter but solid non-metals are brittle. shiny dull brittle"],[4,"y7c05-o031","Short answer","A material is dull, brittle and does not conduct electricity. Is it a metal or a non-metal?","5.2","A non-metal. non-metal"],[4,"y7c05-o032","Short answer","Write down two things a metal can do better than a non-metal.","5.2","Conduct electrical energy and conduct heat energy. conduct electricity heat"],[4,"y7c05-o033","Short answer","What unusual property does mercury have for a metal?","5.2","It is a liquid at room temperature. liquid room temperature"],[4,"y7c05-o034","Short answer","Describe how you would test whether a material conducts electricity.","5.2","Connect a cell, a lamp and wires in a circuit with a gap, and put the material in the gap using crocodile clips. If the lamp lights, the material conducts. circuit lamp lights conducts"],[4,"y7c05-o035","Short answer","Why must you make good contact between the crocodile clips and the test material?","5.2","Because a poor contact stops the current flowing, so a conductor could look like an insulator. contact current wrong result"],[4,"y7c05-o036","Short answer","Why should you check the lamp works before you start testing materials?","5.2","So that you know a lamp that does not light is telling you about the material, not about a broken lamp. check lamp working"],[4,"y7c05-o037","Short answer","Name three questions you could ask to decide whether a material is a metal.","5.2","Is it shiny or dull? Does it ring when hit? Does it conduct electricity? (Also: is it brittle, can you bend it, does it feel cold?) shiny ring conduct"],[4,"y7c05-o038","Short answer","What does it mean to distinguish between metals and non-metals?","5.2","It means to identify which is which. identify"],[4,"y7c05-o083","Short answer","What is a mixture?","5.4","Different substances that are together but not combined together chemically. not combined chemically"],[4,"y7c05-o084","Short answer","How can a mixture of iron filings and sulfur be separated?","5.4","With a magnet, because iron is magnetic and sulfur is not. magnet iron magnetic"],[4,"y7c05-o085","Short answer","What is the general rule for separating any mixture?","5.4","Use a difference in the properties of the substances in it. difference properties"],[4,"y7c05-o093","Short answer","Explain in terms of particles how water in the flask changes into a gas.","5.4","Heating transfers energy to the water particles, so they move more. Eventually some overcome the weak forces holding them in place, and the liquid changes state to a gas. energy move more overcome forces change state"],[4,"y7c05-o104","Short answer","What do foods that contain acid taste like?","5.5","They have a sour, sharp, tangy taste. sour sharp tangy"],[4,"y7c05-o105","Short answer","Which acid do lemons and limes contain?","5.5","Citric acid. citric acid"],[4,"y7c05-o106","Short answer","Name the three common laboratory acids.","5.5","Hydrochloric acid, sulfuric acid and nitric acid. hydrochloric sulfuric nitric"],[4,"y7c05-o107","Short answer","What does corrosive mean?","5.5","It means the substance can dissolve your skin and clothing and cause chemical burns. dissolve skin chemical burns"],[4,"y7c05-o125","Short answer","What does an indicator do?","5.6","It turns one colour in an acid and a different colour in an alkali. one colour acid different colour alkali"],[4,"y7c05-o126","Short answer","What colour is red cabbage indicator in an acid?","5.6","Red. red"],[4,"y7c05-o127","Short answer","What colour is red cabbage indicator in an alkali?","5.6","Yellow. yellow"],[4,"y7c05-o129","Short answer","How do you make an indicator from plant material?","5.6","Cut up the plant material, crush it in a pestle and mortar, add a little ethanol, crush again, then transfer the coloured liquid to a test tube with a pipette. crush pestle ethanol pipette"],[4,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What does it mean for a metal to be malleable?","5.1","It can be drawn out into wires It can be hammered into shape It makes a ringing sound when hit It is shiny and smooth"],[4,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Metals that can be drawn out into wires are described as ____.","5.1","ductile"],[4,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which metal is the only one that is liquid at room temperature?","5.1","Iron Copper Mercury Gold"],[4,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","When metals are hit, they make a ringing sound. This property is called ____.","5.1","sonorous"],[4,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Which two metals are mixed to make bronze?","5.3","Copper and tin Iron and carbon Chromium and nickel Gold and silver"],[4,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Bronze is ____ than either copper or tin.","5.3","harder"],[4,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which non-metal is mixed with iron to make steel?","5.3","Carbon Oxygen Sulfur Nitrogen"],[4,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","Pure iron is not ____ enough to be very useful on its own.","5.3","hard"],[4,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","What happens when the atoms of different metals are mixed to form an alloy?","5.3","They bond together They mix but do not bond They separate completely They turn into a gas"],[4,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","In a pure metal, the atoms are arranged in regular ____.","5.3","rows"],[4,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","Why are alloys generally harder than pure metals?","5.3","The layers of atoms cannot slide over each other easily The atoms are all the same size The layers can slide over one another easily The metal is lighter"],[4,"qwen-02-10","Gap fill","A type of steel that does not rust is used for making ____.","5.3","cutlery"],[4,"qwen-03-10","Gap fill","Mixtures contain different substances that are not combined together ____.","5.4","chemically"],[4,"qwen-03-11","Multiple choice","How is a mixture of iron filings and sulfur separated?","5.4","By heating it until it melts Using a magnet to attract the iron Filtering it through paper Boiling off the sulfur"],[4,"qwen-03-12","Gap fill","In the separation of food dye and water, the gas travels into a ____ to cool down.","5.4","condenser"],[4,"qwen-04-01","Multiple choice","What piece of equipment is used to hold the filter paper during the separation process?","5.4","Conical flask Filter funnel Evaporating basin Beaker"],[4,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","The clear liquid that passes through the filter paper is collected in a ____.","5.4","conical flask"],[4,"qwen-04-03","Multiple choice","Why should you add the mixture to the funnel slowly?","5.4","To make it filter faster So the mixture does not go down the outside of the paper To keep the sand from dissolving To prevent the water from evaporating"],[4,"qwen-04-05","Multiple choice","Where should the filter paper be left after filtering is complete?","5.4","In a cold place In the conical flask In a warm place to dry Under running water"],[4,"qwen-06-01","Multiple choice","What is the taste of lemons and limes?","5.5","Sweet Sour, sharp tangy Bitter Salty"],[4,"qwen-06-02","Gap fill","Citric acid is found in lemons and limes. It is described as a ____ acid.","5.5","weak"],[4,"qwen-06-03","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a common acid found in the laboratory?","5.5","Sodium hydroxide Hydrochloric acid Calcium hydroxide Potassium hydroxide"],[4,"qwen-06-04","Gap fill","If strong acid gets onto your skin, it will ____ the skin.","5.5","dissolve"],[4,"qwen-06-06","Gap fill","Sodium hydroxide is a compound made of sodium, hydrogen and ____.","5.5","oxygen"],[4,"qwen-06-07","Multiple choice","How do acids and alkalis behave when mixed together?","5.5","They explode They cancel each other out They form a solid precipitate only They become more acidic"],[4,"qwen-06-08","Gap fill","When handling chemicals, you should place the bottle top ____ on the work surface.","5.5","upside down"],[4,"qwen-06-09","Multiple choice","What does a 'Corrosive' hazard symbol mean?","5.5","A substance that can explode A substance that can destroy living tissue A substance that is flammable A substance that is toxic"],[4,"qwen-07-01","Multiple choice","What colour does red cabbage indicator turn in an alkali?","5.6","red blue yellow purple"],[4,"qwen-07-02","Gap fill","Litmus turns ____ when it is in a neutral substance.","5.6","purple"],[4,"qwen-07-03","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a neutral substance?","5.6","hydrochloric acid sodium hydroxide water lemon juice"],[4,"qwen-07-04","Gap fill","Universal indicator is made up of a mixture of different ____.","5.6","indicators"],[4,"qwen-07-05","Multiple choice","What colour does universal indicator turn in a strongly alkaline substance?","5.6","red green blue purple"],[4,"qwen-07-06","Gap fill","The strength of acids and alkalis is measured on the ____ scale.","5.6","pH"],[4,"qwen-07-07","Multiple choice","What is the pH of a neutral solution?","5.6","1 4 7 13"],[4,"qwen-07-08","Gap fill","Litmus is a very common ____.","5.6","indicator"],[4,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Gold is used for jewellery because metals are usually ____.","5.1","shiny"],[4,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","Which metal is used for electrical wiring because it conducts electricity?","5.1","Iron Gold Copper Silver"],[4,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","A material that can be pulled out into wires is described as ____.","5.1","ductile"],[4,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a property of metals?","5.1","Brittle Dull surface Conducts heat Does not conduct electricity"],[4,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Which statement about metals is true?","5.2","All metals are magnetic Solid non-metals are brittle All metals are solids Non-metals conduct electricity well"],[4,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","Substance A is a shiny liquid that conducts electricity, so it is a ____.","5.2","metal"],[4,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","In the distillation setup, cold water enters the condenser to cool the steam so it ____ back into liquid.","5.4","condenses"],[5,"y7c06-f01","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows how a loudspeaker makes a sound wave. What is labelled {n}?","6.1",""],[5,"y7c06-f02","Diagram labelling","A bat is using sound to find an insect. What is labelled {n}?","6.2",""],[5,"y7c06-f03","Diagram labelling","Name the layer of the Earth labelled {n}.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-f04","Diagram labelling","This pie chart shows the most common elements in the Earth's crust. Name the element labelled {n}.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-f05","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows the parts of a volcano. What is labelled {n}?","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-f06","Diagram labelling","An aeroplane is making a shadow on the ground. What is labelled {n}?","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-f07","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows a solar eclipse. What is labelled {n}?","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-f08","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows a lunar eclipse. What is labelled {n}?","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-h01","Click the diagram","Click on the label that names the part of the loudspeaker that makes the sound.","6.1",""],[5,"y7c06-h02","Click the diagram","Click on the label that shows how the air particles move.","6.1",""],[5,"y7c06-h03","Click the diagram","Click on the label that shows the echo coming back to the bat.","6.2",""],[5,"y7c06-h04","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the thin solid layer of rock on the outside of the Earth.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h05","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the solid metal layer at the very centre of the Earth.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h06","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the most common non-metal in the Earth's crust.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h07","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the most common metal in the Earth's crust.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h08","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the opening that carries magma from the mantle to the crater.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h09","Click the diagram","Click on the label for liquid rock that is running down the outside of the volcano.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h11","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the dark area where light from the Sun cannot reach.","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-h12","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the place where all the light rays from the Sun are blocked.","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-h13","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the place where only some of the light rays from the Sun are blocked.","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-h14","Click the diagram","Click on the object that is casting the shadow in this diagram.","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-h15","Click the diagram","Click on the object that is casting the shadow in this diagram.","6.5",""],[5,"y7c06-h16","Click the diagram","Click on the sea bed, where the sound from the ship is reflected.","6.2",""],[5,"y7c06-h17","Click the diagram","Click on the boat that is sending out the sound.","6.2",""],[5,"y7c06-h18","Click the diagram","Click on the map that shows all the land joined as one large continent.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h19","Click the diagram","Click on the map that shows the Earth today.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h20","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the African plate.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h21","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the Antarctic plate.","6.3",""],[5,"y7c06-h22","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the Nazca plate.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h23","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the Pacific plate.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h24","Click the diagram","Click on the part of the plate that is sinking down into the mantle.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h25","Click the diagram","Click on the mantle, the molten rock beneath the plates.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h26","Click the diagram","Click on the liquid rock rising up where the two plates move apart.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h27","Click the diagram","Click on the arrow showing the left-hand plate moving away from the boundary.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h28","Click the diagram","Click on the mantle, the molten rock the plates are sliding on.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h29","Click the diagram","Click on the arrow showing the left-hand plate moving.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h30","Click the diagram","Click on the rock layers that have been pushed into folds.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-h31","Click the diagram","Click on the arrow showing the compression from tectonic plate movement.","6.4",""],[5,"y7c06-r001","Short answer","Explain why every sound you hear must start with something vibrating.","6.1","Things that vibrate make sounds, and to vibrate means to move backwards and forwards very quickly. A sound wave is made when a moving surface pushes the particles next to it, so without something moving backwards and forwards there is nothing to set the particles going. That is why hitting a drum causes the drum to vibrate, which makes the sound, and why a still object makes no sound at all. vibration means moving backwards and forwards quickly the moving surface pushes nearby particles no vibration means nothing to start the wave a still object makes no sound"],[5,"y7c06-r002","Short answer","Explain why hitting a drum harder makes a louder sound.","6.1","If you hit a drum with more force, it vibrates more, and this makes a louder sound. A bigger vibration means the drum skin moves further backwards and forwards each time, so it pushes the air particles harder and they vibrate through a larger distance. Loudness therefore depends on how big the vibration is, not on how fast the object vibrates. more force gives a bigger vibration skin moves further each time air particles vibrate through a larger distance loudness depends on the size of the vibration"],[5,"y7c06-r003","Short answer","Explain how you can show that your own voice comes from a vibration.","6.1","If you touch the front of your neck while you are speaking, you can feel a vibration, and the vibration comes from your vocal cords, which make the sound when you speak. Feeling the movement through your fingers is direct evidence that something is moving backwards and forwards while the sound is being made, and the vibration stops the moment you stop speaking. The same test works on a loudspeaker cone. feel the vibration at the front of the neck vocal cords make the sound vibration stops when you stop speaking direct evidence of movement"],[5,"y7c06-r004","Short answer","Explain how small objects placed on a loudspeaker cone show that the cone is vibrating.","6.1","If you put small objects into the paper cone of a loudspeaker, the objects will bounce around, and this shows that the paper cone in the loudspeaker is vibrating. The objects can only be thrown upwards if the surface underneath them is moving up and down, so their bouncing is evidence of a movement too fast and too small to see clearly. The vibration is made visible by its effect on something else. objects bounce on the cone they can only bounce if the surface moves the vibration is too fast to see made visible by its effect"],[5,"y7c06-r005","Short answer","Explain how a sound wave travels from a vibrating object across a room.","6.1","Every time the object vibrates forward, the air in front of the object gets pushed forward, and the particles in the air are made to vibrate backwards and forwards in time with the vibrating object. When the particles in front of the object vibrate, those particles make other particles in front of them vibrate, so the vibration is passed on from particle to particle across the room. Each particle only moves a little; it is the vibration that travels. the object pushes the air in front of it particles vibrate in time with the object each particle makes the next one vibrate the vibration travels, not the particles"],[5,"y7c06-r006","Short answer","A person feels a breeze from a fan but not from a loudspeaker. Explain the difference.","6.1","A sound wave travels to your ear by the vibration of air particles, and each particle vibrates backwards and forwards about its own position rather than moving across the room. A fan pushes the air itself from one place to another, which is why you feel moving air. The loudspeaker passes on a vibration through the air without transporting the air, so nothing arrives at your face except the wave. sound particles vibrate about their own position the air itself does not move across the room a fan moves the air sound transports the vibration, not the air"],[5,"y7c06-r007","Short answer","Explain why sound can travel through a solid table as well as through air.","6.1","Sound waves travel by making particles vibrate, and sound will travel through anything that has particles: gas, liquid or solid. A table is made of particles too, so a tap on the table makes those particles vibrate and each one passes the vibration to the next, exactly as happens in air. This is why putting an ear on the table lets you hear the tap through the solid. sound travels by making particles vibrate solids, liquids and gases all have particles each particle passes the vibration on the tap is heard through the table"],[5,"y7c06-r008","Short answer","Explain what is meant by a medium for sound, and state what is needed to hear any sound.","6.1","The substance that the sound wave moves through is called the medium, and therefore solids, liquids and gases can all be a medium for sound. To hear a sound there must be a vibration to make the sound and a medium containing particles through which the sound wave can travel. Both parts are essential: a vibration with no medium produces no wave, and a medium with nothing vibrating carries nothing. the medium is the substance the wave travels through solids, liquids and gases can all be a medium need a vibration and a medium both are essential"],[5,"y7c06-r009","Short answer","Explain why sound cannot travel in a vacuum.","6.1","A vacuum is a space where there are no particles, and as there are no particles in a vacuum, there is nothing to vibrate to make a sound wave. A sound wave exists only as a vibration passed from particle to particle, so with nothing to carry it the wave cannot begin or continue. Therefore sound will not travel in a vacuum, no matter how strong the vibration that made it. a vacuum has no particles nothing to vibrate sound exists only as a particle-to-particle vibration no wave can travel"],[5,"y7c06-r010","Short answer","Describe the bell jar demonstration and explain what it proves.","6.1","If you put an electric bell in a glass jar, you can see it vibrate as it makes the sound, and if the air is pumped out of the jar to make a vacuum, you can see the bell vibrating but you cannot hear the sound of the bell. Seeing it still vibrating rules out the explanation that the bell has stopped working, so the only thing that has changed is the removal of the particles. That isolates the medium as the cause. bell is seen vibrating in air and in the vacuum sound disappears when the air is removed rules out the bell stopping isolates the medium as the cause"],[5,"y7c06-r011","Short answer","People working on the Moon could not hear their own hammering. Explain why.","6.1","The Moon has no atmosphere, so the space around the workers is a vacuum with no particles, and a sound wave cannot travel without particles to vibrate. The hammer still made vibrations in the tools and the ground, but those vibrations could not cross the gap to the astronauts' ears. People who went to the Moon wore suits that contained air, so they could hear sounds made inside their own suit but not outside it. no atmosphere means a vacuum no particles to carry the wave vibrations cannot cross the gap sound inside the suit could still be heard"],[5,"y7c06-r012","Short answer","Explain whether you would really hear a loud bang from an explosion in space.","6.1","No. Science fiction films are made in studios on Earth, and these films often show explosions in space with a loud bang, but space is a vacuum where there are no particles to vibrate. The explosion would produce violent movement of material, but with no medium the movement cannot be passed on as a sound wave, so nothing would reach an observer's ears. The bang is added for effect, not because it is physically possible. space is a vacuum no particles to carry the wave the explosion still moves material the bang is added for effect"],[5,"y7c06-r013","Short answer","Explain why we cannot hear the Sun.","6.1","If sound waves could travel through space, we would be able to hear the Sun, and scientists think it would make a high-pitched humming sound. The Sun is certainly vibrating and is enormous, so the vibration is not the problem; the problem is that the space between the Sun and the Earth is a vacuum with no particles to pass the vibration on. The sound exists at the Sun but has no route to us. the Sun does vibrate space between us is a vacuum no particles to pass the vibration on the sound has no route to us"],[5,"y7c06-r014","Short answer","A fly walking on a window makes vibrations, yet no one can hear it. Explain why.","6.1","The fly's feet make vibrations, so a sound wave is produced, but the vibrations are extremely small because the fly's feet are tiny and push with very little force. Vibrations from very quiet sounds can be too small for the ears to detect, so although the wave reaches the ear it does not move the ear enough to be sensed. The sound exists; the ear simply cannot detect it. the fly does make vibrations the vibrations are very small too small for the ear to detect the sound exists but is not sensed"],[5,"y7c06-r015","Short answer","Explain why thunder can make objects inside a room vibrate.","6.1","Sound waves transfer sound energy, and the vibration of particles in the air is transferred to other objects. When the vibration is transferred, the other objects will start to vibrate, so the air particles set in motion by the thunder push against windows and ornaments and make them move. Thunder does this and quieter sounds do not because it is a very loud sound, meaning the particle vibrations are large. sound waves transfer energy air particle vibration is transferred to objects objects start to vibrate thunder is loud, so the vibrations are large"],[5,"y7c06-r021","Short answer","Explain why sound waves can be reflected.","6.2","One property of all waves is that they can be reflected from surfaces, therefore sound waves can be reflected. Reflection is like bouncing a ball off a wall, and when a wave is reflected the wave behaves like the ball, with the only difference being that a wave is not affected by gravity. So reflection is not something special about sound; it follows from sound being a wave. reflection is a property of all waves sound is a wave, so it reflects like bouncing a ball off a wall a wave is not affected by gravity"],[5,"y7c06-r022","Short answer","Explain what kind of surface gives the best echo and why.","6.2","Sound waves reflect best from large, smooth, flat surfaces, and surfaces such as glass, tiles, flat metal and smooth concrete give good reflections of sound. A large flat surface sends the whole wave back in one direction, so the reflected sound stays strong, while a rough or uneven surface scatters the wave in many directions so that only a small part returns. A soft surface absorbs the vibration instead of returning it. large, smooth, flat surfaces reflect best the whole wave returns in one direction rough surfaces scatter the wave soft surfaces absorb the vibration"],[5,"y7c06-r023","Short answer","Explain what an echo is and why clapping in an empty room sounds strange.","6.2","The reflection of a sound wave is called an echo. Clapping your hands makes a sound wave that will travel away from your hands in all directions, and when the sound wave hits a wall it is reflected back. Because the reflected waves arrive after the original sound and after each other, the sound of the clap seems to last longer than usual, then fade away, which is the strange effect you hear. an echo is a reflected sound wave the clap spreads out in all directions reflections arrive after the original the sound seems to last longer then fade"],[5,"y7c06-r024","Short answer","Explain how a bat uses echoes to find an insect.","6.2","The bat makes a sound wave, the sound wave reflects off the insect, and there is an echo. The bat can work out where the insect is from the time taken for the echo to reach the bat, and the direction the echo comes from. Time gives distance, because a longer time means the sound had further to travel, and direction tells the bat which way to fly, so the two together give a position. bat makes a sound that reflects off the insect time taken gives the distance direction the echo comes from gives the bearing together they give a position"],[5,"y7c06-r025","Short answer","Explain how a boat measures the depth of water beneath it.","6.2","A sound is sent from the bottom of the boat, the sound travels through the water and reflects off the solid ground, and the echo comes back to the boat. The time taken for the echo to come back can be used to work out the depth, because sound travels through water at a known speed, so time multiplied by speed gives a distance. The depth is half that distance, since the sound made the journey twice. sound sent down reflects off the sea bed time taken is measured speed times time gives the distance travelled depth is half of it"],[5,"y7c06-r026","Short answer","Explain why the distance travelled by the sound is double the distance to the reflecting surface.","6.2","The sound has to travel from the object to the reflecting surface and back again, so the journey covers the gap twice. Anyone calculating from the echo time gets the total path length, and must halve it to find the distance to the object. Forgetting this gives an answer twice as large as the truth, which is why the textbook points it out for both the bat and the boat. the sound goes there and back the journey covers the gap twice halve the total path length forgetting it doubles the answer"],[5,"y7c06-r027","Short answer","A sound takes 0.2 s to return from fish, and sound travels at 1500 m/s in water. Explain how to find the distance to the fish.","6.2","Use the equation distance = speed times time, which gives 1500 multiplied by 0.2, that is 300 metres. But remember that the distance travelled by the sound wave in 0.2 seconds is from the ship to the fish and back again, so 300 m is the whole journey. The distance from the boat to the fish is half of it, 150 metres. distance = speed x time 1500 x 0.2 = 300 m 300 m is there and back distance to the fish is 150 m"],[5,"y7c06-r028","Short answer","Sofia stands 85 m from a cliff and the speed of sound is 340 m/s. Explain how to find the time until she hears the echo.","6.2","The sound must travel to the cliff and back, so the distance is 85 multiplied by 2, which is 170 metres. Rearranging distance = speed times time gives time = distance divided by speed, which is 170 divided by 340, that is 0.5 seconds. Using 85 m instead of 170 m would give 0.25 s, which is the mistake of forgetting the return journey. distance is 85 x 2 = 170 m time = distance / speed 170 / 340 = 0.5 s using 85 m would halve the answer wrongly"],[5,"y7c06-r029","Short answer","Explain how echoes are used to make an image of an unborn baby.","6.2","Sounds sent into the mother's body echo back out of her body, and this method is used to make the image of the unborn baby. Different tissues reflect the sound differently, so the pattern of echoes returning from each direction, together with the time each takes, tells the machine how far away each reflecting surface is. Building up those distances over the whole area produces a picture without anything entering the body. sound sent in echoes back out different tissues reflect differently time gives the distance to each surface distances build up into an image"],[5,"y7c06-r030","Short answer","Explain why echoes spoil a music recording.","6.2","When recording music, echoes change the sound, because a musical note that is played once will repeat with an echo. The microphone records both the direct sound and the reflections that arrive slightly later, so a single note is heard more than once and notes blur into each other. A room with large flat walls would give many echoes, which is why recording rooms are treated. microphone records direct sound and reflections reflections arrive later a single note repeats notes blur together"],[5,"y7c06-r031","Short answer","Explain how the walls of a recording room are changed to stop echoes.","6.2","The picture shows how the walls of a room are changed to stop echoes, and the shapes on the walls of this room are made to stop echoes. Since sound reflects best from large, smooth, flat surfaces, covering the walls with soft, shaped material removes exactly the surfaces that cause reflections: the shapes scatter the wave in many directions and the soft material absorbs the vibration rather than returning it. sound reflects best from large flat surfaces shaped surfaces scatter the wave soft material absorbs the vibration removes the surfaces that cause echoes"],[5,"y7c06-r032","Short answer","Explain why theatres are designed with no large flat surfaces.","6.2","In a theatre, the audience needs to hear the voices of people on the stage, and if there were echoes in a theatre the voices would not be clear, because each word would arrive again a moment later and overlap with the next word. Theatres usually have no large flat surfaces that could cause echoes, since large, smooth, flat surfaces are exactly what reflects sound best. Removing them removes the cause of the problem. echoes make speech unclear each word overlaps the next large flat surfaces cause echoes removing them removes the cause"],[5,"y7c06-r033","Short answer","Arun says music sounded better in theatre B than theatre A. Explain what in the pictures could cause this.","6.2","Theatre A is likely to have large, smooth, flat surfaces, which reflect sound best and so produce echoes that make each note repeat and the music blur. Theatre B is likely to have shaped or soft surfaces that scatter and absorb the sound instead of reflecting it back, so the audience hears mainly the direct sound from the stage. The difference is in the surfaces, not in the musicians. flat surfaces in A cause strong reflections echoes blur the notes B has shaped or soft surfaces B lets the audience hear the direct sound"],[5,"y7c06-r034","Short answer","Zara wants to stop echoes in the empty room where she practises drums. Explain what material she should put on the walls and why.","6.2","She should put a soft, thick material such as heavy fabric or foam on the walls. An empty room has bare, large, flat surfaces, which reflect sound best, so the drum sound bounces repeatedly and the beats run into each other. A soft material absorbs the vibration instead of returning it, and a shaped surface scatters what it does reflect, so far less sound comes back into the room. bare flat walls reflect strongly beats run into each other soft thick material absorbs the vibration shaped surfaces scatter the rest"],[5,"y7c06-r035","Short answer","Arun hears more echoes in a tunnel than in a field. Explain why.","6.2","A tunnel is surrounded by large, smooth, hard surfaces close on every side, and sound waves reflect best from large, smooth, flat surfaces, so almost all the sound that leaves him comes back. In a field the sound spreads out in all directions with nothing near enough to reflect it, and grass and soil absorb and scatter what does reach them. The difference is whether there are reflecting surfaces close by. a tunnel has hard surfaces on every side almost all the sound returns in a field the sound spreads away grass and soil absorb and scatter"],[5,"y7c06-r038","Short answer","Describe the structure of the Earth from the outside in.","6.3","The Earth has a crust of solid rock, and under the crust is the mantle, which is molten hot liquid rock that can flow. In the centre of the Earth is the core, made of the metals nickel and iron, and the core has two parts: the outer part of the core is molten and the inner part of the core is solid. So the Earth is not uniformly solid or uniformly liquid; the state changes with depth. crust of solid rock mantle of molten rock that can flow core of nickel and iron outer core molten, inner core solid"],[5,"y7c06-r039","Short answer","The outer core is molten but the inner core is solid, even though the inner core is hotter. Explain why this is surprising and what it suggests.","6.3","The outer part of the core is molten and the inner part of the core is solid, yet the inner core is deeper and hotter, and heating usually melts a solid rather than the reverse. This tells us that temperature is not the only thing that decides the state of a material: at the centre of the Earth the enormous weight of everything above presses the particles together so firmly that they cannot move past each other. State depends on pressure as well as temperature. inner core is hotter yet solid heating usually melts a material the weight above creates enormous pressure state depends on pressure as well as temperature"],[5,"y7c06-r040","Short answer","Explain why the mantle being able to flow matters for the rest of the topic.","6.3","Under the crust is the mantle, which is molten hot liquid rock that can flow, and these tectonic plates move slowly on the liquid rock called magma beneath them, which is how continental drift occurs. If the mantle were solid, the plates would be locked in place and the continents could not move, so there would be no continental drift, no fold mountains and no earthquakes at plate boundaries. Everything in topics 6.3 and 6.4 depends on this one property. the mantle is molten and can flow plates move on the magma beneath a solid mantle would lock the plates drift, mountains and earthquakes depend on it"],[5,"y7c06-r041","Short answer","Scientists say the Earth is about 4500 million years old and know what is inside it, yet no one has been there. Explain how this is possible.","6.3","Scientists have worked out that the Earth is about 4500 million years old, and they have also worked out what is inside the Earth. The deepest hole ever drilled reaches only a tiny way into the crust, so the knowledge comes from indirect evidence: the way waves from earthquakes travel through the Earth changes with the material they pass through, and the Earth's mass and magnetism also constrain what the core can be made of. Conclusions built this way can be revised if new evidence appears. no one has been below the crust knowledge comes from indirect evidence waves from earthquakes change with the material conclusions can be revised"],[5,"y7c06-r042","Short answer","Explain how ideas about the Earth changed after 1912.","6.3","People used to think that the Earth was only a few thousand years old and that the Earth had never changed. In 1912 a German scientist called Alfred Wegener suggested that, millions of years ago, all the land was one large continent, and that over millions of years the land broke up and drifted apart. This idea is called continental drift, and it replaced a picture of a fixed Earth with one in which the surface is slowly but constantly moving. people thought the Earth never changed Wegener proposed one large continent the land broke up and drifted apart a fixed Earth replaced by a moving one"],[5,"y7c06-r043","Short answer","State Wegener's three pieces of evidence and explain why they are stronger together than separately.","6.3","His evidence was that the shapes of the continents fit together, that the types of rock on the different continents match up where they fit together, and that the fossils on the different continents match up where they fit together. Matching shapes alone could be coincidence, but the rocks and fossils matching at exactly the places where the shapes fit is far too much agreement to be chance. Three independent kinds of evidence pointing to the same join make the conclusion much harder to explain any other way. shapes of the continents fit rock types match at the join fossils match at the join three independent lines agreeing is not chance"],[5,"y7c06-r044","Short answer","Explain why many scientists rejected Wegener's idea at the time.","6.3","Wegener could not explain how continental drift happened, so not everyone believed his ideas. He had strong evidence that the continents had once been joined, but no mechanism that could move something as large as a continent, and a claim with no possible mechanism is easy to dismiss however good the evidence. It was accepted once tectonic plates moving on the magma beneath them supplied the missing mechanism. he could not explain how it happened strong evidence but no mechanism a claim with no mechanism is easy to dismiss accepted once plate movement supplied it"],[5,"y7c06-r045","Short answer","Explain what tectonic plates are and how they differ from continents.","6.3","The Earth's crust is made up of large tectonic plates; some of the plates are under the oceans and are called oceanic plates, and some of the plates form the continents and are called continental plates. So a plate is a piece of the crust, and a continent is the land on top of part of a plate. A single plate can carry both land and ocean floor, which is why plate boundaries do not follow coastlines. plates are pieces of the Earth's crust oceanic plates and continental plates a continent is land on top of a plate plate boundaries do not follow coastlines"],[5,"y7c06-r046","Short answer","The plates move about 4 cm each year. Explain how such a slow movement can separate continents.","6.3","The plates only move about 4 cm each year, which is about the same speed as your fingernails grow, so nothing visible happens in a human lifetime. But continental drift took place over millions of years, and 4 cm a year is 40 km in a million years, so over a hundred million years the same rate carries a continent thousands of kilometres. Very slow processes produce very large effects when the time available is long enough. 4 cm a year, like fingernails growing invisible in a human lifetime 40 km in a million years large effects over very long times"],[5,"y7c06-r047","Short answer","Explain why geological change happens at plate boundaries rather than in the middle of plates.","6.4","The places where tectonic plates meet are called plate boundaries, and geological change happens at plate boundaries because the tectonic plates are always moving. In the middle of a plate the whole slab moves together, so nothing is being pushed, torn or rubbed, while at a boundary two plates move relative to one another and the rock there must break, fold or melt. That is why earthquakes and volcanoes trace out the edges of the plates. change happens where plates meet plates are always moving the middle of a plate moves as one piece boundaries are where rock breaks, folds or melts"],[5,"y7c06-r048","Short answer","Explain how fold mountains are formed.","6.4","Sometimes, when tectonic plates move together, the rocks crumple and fold upwards, and the mountains that this produces are called fold mountains. The plates are enormous and the force pushing them together is immense, so the rock layers between them have nowhere to go but up, exactly as a cloth pushed from both ends rises into folds. This can happen under the ocean or on land. plates move together rock layers are compressed they crumple and fold upwards nowhere to go but up"],[5,"y7c06-r049","Short answer","The Himalayas are 10 to 25 million years old and the Urals more than 200 million. Explain what this difference tells you.","6.4","The newest fold mountains are between 10 and 25 million years old, including the Himalayas in Asia and the Rocky Mountains in North America, while the oldest fold mountains are more than 200 million years old, including the Ural Mountains in Russia. This shows that mountain building is not a single past event but has happened repeatedly through the Earth's history, and it continues now. The older ranges are lower because weathering has worn them down for much longer. mountain building happened repeatedly not a single past event it continues today older ranges have been worn down for longer"],[5,"y7c06-r050","Short answer","Explain how a volcano is formed at a plate boundary.","6.4","Volcanoes are usually formed at the plate boundaries when magma from the mantle rises up through cracks in the Earth's crust. At the Earth's surface, magma erupts to form lava flows and ash deposits, and the lava and ash harden as they cool to form new rocks, so each time the volcano erupts it gets bigger. The boundary matters because that is where the crust is cracked enough to let the magma through. magma rises through cracks in the crust erupts as lava flows and ash lava and ash harden into new rock the volcano grows with each eruption"],[5,"y7c06-r051","Short answer","Explain the difference between magma and lava.","6.4","Magma is the name for liquid rock when it is underground, and lava is the name for liquid rock when it is on the surface. It is the same material; only its position has changed, which is why the name changes at the moment it erupts. The distinction is useful because the two behave differently: underground the magma is under great pressure and holds dissolved gas, while at the surface that gas escapes. magma is underground, lava is on the surface the same material, different position name changes at eruption underground it is under pressure and holds gas"],[5,"y7c06-r052","Short answer","Explain why some eruptions are violent and others are not.","6.4","Sometimes, if the magma is really thick and contains dissolved gas, pressure builds up and the eruption is violent, and gases and rock shoot up through the opening. Thick magma cannot flow easily, so the gas trapped inside it cannot escape gradually and instead builds pressure until the rock above gives way all at once. Thin, runny magma lets the gas escape steadily, so the lava flows out rather than exploding. thick magma containing dissolved gas gas cannot escape gradually pressure builds until the rock gives way thin magma lets gas escape steadily"],[5,"y7c06-r053","Short answer","Explain how a volcanic eruption can cause a tsunami.","6.4","Violent eruptions can even cause avalanches and earthquakes, and tsunamis if the volcano is close to the sea. At Anak Krakatau in Indonesia, part of the volcano was blown off into the sea, causing a tsunami, because a huge mass of rock entering the water suddenly displaces an enormous volume of it. That displaced water spreads outwards as a wave that can reach coasts far away. violent eruption can collapse part of the volcano rock falls into the sea displaces a huge volume of water the wave spreads to distant coasts"],[5,"y7c06-r054","Short answer","Explain the difference between an active, a dormant and an extinct volcano.","6.4","Some volcanoes are active and may erupt, some are inactive or dormant, which means they have not erupted for a very long time, and other volcanoes are extinct, which means they will not erupt again. The important difference is between dormant and extinct: dormant is a statement about the past, that nothing has happened recently, while extinct is a claim about the future. That makes extinct much harder to be sure of, and dormant volcanoes do sometimes erupt. active may erupt dormant has not erupted for a long time extinct will not erupt again dormant is about the past, extinct about the future"],[5,"y7c06-r055","Short answer","Explain what causes an earthquake.","6.4","Because the plates are very large and heavy, there is a lot of friction between the plates, and over the years this makes the plates stick together. There is always force on the tectonic plates, so the pressure builds up and eventually the pressure causes violent movement. The earthquake is the sudden release of movement that friction had been preventing, which is why it comes all at once rather than gradually. friction makes the plates stick together force keeps acting so pressure builds eventually violent movement occurs sudden release of held-back movement"],[5,"y7c06-r056","Short answer","Explain what decides how large an earthquake is.","6.4","The size or magnitude of the earthquake depends on the size of the faults at the plate boundaries, and how far the rocks move when the earthquake happens. A larger fault means more rock moves at once, and a greater movement means more energy released, so in the largest earthquakes the rocks can move tens of metres in seconds. Some are so slight that they only register on scientific instruments, because very little rock moved very little distance. depends on the size of the fault and how far the rocks move more rock and more movement means more energy small ones only register on instruments"],[5,"y7c06-r057","Short answer","Earthquake magnitude uses a times 10 scale. Explain how much stronger magnitude 7 is than magnitude 4.","6.4","Every time the magnitude number increases by 1, the earthquake becomes 10 times stronger, so each step multiplies rather than adds. Going from 4 to 7 is three steps, so the strength is multiplied by 10 three times, that is 10 times 10 times 10, which is 1000. A magnitude 7 earthquake is therefore 1000 times stronger than a magnitude 4, even though the numbers look close together. each step multiplies by 10 4 to 7 is three steps 10 x 10 x 10 = 1000 numbers look close but differ hugely"],[5,"y7c06-r058","Short answer","Explain how an earthquake hundreds of kilometres out at sea can cause damage on land.","6.4","Earthquakes under the oceans can occur hundreds of kilometres from land, and the violent movement of the sea floor pushes the whole column of water above it, which spreads outwards as a tsunami. The wave crosses deep water quickly and only grows tall as it reaches shallow water near the coast, so damage happens far from where the rocks moved. The energy, not the water at the earthquake site, is what travels. sea floor movement pushes the water above spreads outwards as a tsunami grows tall in shallow water at the coast the energy travels, not the original water"],[5,"y7c06-r059","Short answer","Two sheets of paper are pushed so one slides over the other. Explain what this models and what it fails to show.","6.4","This is similar to what happens when one tectonic plate slides over another, so the paper models the plates and the table models the mantle beneath. It shows well that two slabs can move relative to each other and that one can ride over the other. It fails to show the friction that makes real plates stick and then slip suddenly, the enormous thickness of real plates, and the molten rock the plates actually float on. paper models plates sliding over one another shows relative movement does not show friction, sticking and sudden slip does not show thickness or molten mantle"],[5,"y7c06-r060","Short answer","Explain what an opaque object is and why opaque objects make shadows.","6.5","An opaque object is an object that will not allow light to pass through, and when an opaque object passes in front of a source of light a shadow will form. Light travels in straight lines called rays, so light rays that reach the object cannot bend around it and cannot reach the region behind it. That region receives no light and therefore looks dark, which is the shadow. opaque means light cannot pass through light travels in straight lines rays cannot bend around the object the region behind receives no light"],[5,"y7c06-r061","Short answer","Explain why the grass around an aeroplane's shadow looks brighter than the shadow itself.","6.5","The shadow looks dark because the light from the Sun has been blocked from reaching the ground, while the grass around the shadow looks brighter because light from the Sun is reaching those areas. The grass has not changed; the difference is only in how much light arrives there. Brightness is about the light reaching a place, which is why the same grass looks dark for the moment the shadow crosses it and bright again afterwards. shadow is where sunlight is blocked surrounding grass still receives sunlight the grass itself has not changed brightness depends on the light arriving"],[5,"y7c06-r063","Short answer","Explain the difference between a total and a partial solar eclipse.","6.5","In the middle of the shadow, all the light rays from the Sun are blocked, and people in the middle of the shadow observe a total solar eclipse. Away from the middle of the shadow of the Moon, some of the light rays from the Sun can reach the Earth, so away from the middle of the shadow there is a partial solar eclipse. Which one you see therefore depends on where you are standing, not on the eclipse itself. total in the middle where all rays are blocked partial away from the middle where some rays reach depends on where you stand the same eclipse gives both"],[5,"y7c06-r064","Short answer","Explain how a lunar eclipse happens and how it differs from a solar eclipse.","6.5","A lunar eclipse happens when the Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon; the Earth is also an opaque object, so the Earth blocks the light from the Sun and the shadow of the Earth is formed on the Moon. In a solar eclipse the Moon is in the middle and the shadow lands on the Earth, so the two events differ in which body is in the middle and which surface the shadow falls on. Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon the Earth blocks the sunlight the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon solar eclipse has the Moon in the middle instead"],[5,"y7c06-r065","Short answer","Explain why a solar eclipse can only ever be seen in the daytime.","6.5","A solar eclipse happens when the Moon comes between the Sun and the Earth and the shadow of the Moon forms on the Earth, so you must be on the side of the Earth facing the Sun to be in that shadow. The side of the Earth facing the Sun is the side experiencing daytime, so anyone in night-time is on the far side and the Moon's shadow cannot reach them. Seeing the Sun at all is a requirement for seeing it covered. shadow falls on the side facing the Sun that side is in daytime night is the far side of the Earth you must be able to see the Sun"],[5,"y7c06-r066","Short answer","Is it true that a total lunar eclipse can only ever be seen in the daytime? Explain your answer.","6.5","False. A lunar eclipse happens when the Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon, so the Moon is on the opposite side of the Earth from the Sun and can only be seen from the night side of the Earth. Anyone in daytime is on the side facing the Sun and has the Moon below their horizon, so they cannot see it at all. A lunar eclipse is therefore always seen at night, which is the opposite of a solar eclipse. false the Moon is opposite the Sun during a lunar eclipse only visible from the night side always seen at night"],[5,"y7c06-r067","Short answer","Eclipses do not happen every month even though the Moon orbits every 28 days. Explain why.","6.5","The Moon takes 28 days to orbit the Earth, but the orbit of the Moon is tilted slightly, so the orbit of the Moon is not exactly in the same plane as the orbit of the Earth around the Sun. Most months the Moon passes above or below the line joining the Sun and the Earth, so its shadow misses the Earth entirely. It is only when the Sun, Earth and Moon are in the same straight line that eclipses can happen. the Moon's orbit is tilted not in the same plane as the Earth's orbit the Moon usually passes above or below the line all three must be in a straight line"],[5,"y7c06-r068","Short answer","Explain why you must never look directly at the Sun, even during an eclipse.","6.5","You must never look directly at the Sun, even when there is an eclipse, because the light from the Sun is very bright and can cause permanent damage to your eyes. During a partial eclipse the uncovered part is just as bright as usual, and the sky being darker makes the eye open wider so that even more light enters. There is also no pain to warn you while the damage is happening. sunlight is bright enough to cause permanent damage the uncovered part is as bright as usual the darker sky makes the eye open wider no pain warns you"],[5,"y7c06-r069","Short answer","In the classroom model, the tennis ball is moved between the lamp and the soccer ball. Explain which eclipse this shows and how you can tell.","6.5","The lamp is the Sun, the white soccer ball is the Earth and the tennis ball is the Moon, so moving the tennis ball between the lamp and the soccer ball puts the Moon between the Sun and the Earth. The shadow of the tennis ball should be seen on the soccer ball, that is, the Moon's shadow falls on the Earth, which is a solar eclipse. You tell them apart by which ball the shadow lands on. Moon between the Sun and the Earth shadow of the tennis ball on the soccer ball the Moon's shadow on the Earth a solar eclipse"],[5,"y7c06-r070","Short answer","In the model the tennis ball is then moved into the shade of the soccer ball. Explain which eclipse this shows.","6.5","Putting the tennis ball in the shade of the soccer ball means the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon and the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, which is a lunar eclipse. The tennis ball goes dark because no light from the lamp reaches it, exactly as the real Moon darkens when it enters the Earth's shadow. Again the test is which body the shadow falls on. Earth between the Sun and the Moon the tennis ball is in the soccer ball's shadow the Moon darkens a lunar eclipse"],[5,"y7c06-r071","Short answer","Explain why the model of the Earth is bigger than the model of the Moon.","6.5","The Earth really is much larger than the Moon, so using a soccer ball for the Earth and a tennis ball for the Moon keeps the relative sizes roughly right. This matters because the difference in size is what produces the observed difference between the two eclipses: the Earth's shadow is large enough to cover the whole Moon at once, while the Moon's much smaller shadow covers only a small patch of the Earth. A model with equal balls would predict the wrong thing. the Earth is much larger than the Moon relative sizes are kept roughly right the Earth's shadow covers the whole Moon the Moon's shadow covers only a patch of the Earth"],[5,"y7c06-r072","Short answer","Someone says they saw a lunar eclipse in the middle of the day. Explain why this cannot be true.","6.5","A lunar eclipse happens when the Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon, so the Sun and the Moon must be on opposite sides of the Earth. Anyone in the middle of the day is on the side of the Earth facing the Sun, which puts the Moon on the far side of the Earth from them, below their horizon. They cannot see the Moon at all at that time, so they cannot have seen it eclipsed. Sun and Moon on opposite sides of the Earth midday means facing the Sun the Moon is then below the horizon the Moon cannot be seen at all"],[5,"y7c06-o046","Short answer","How old do scientists think the Earth is?","6.3","About 4500 million years old. 4500 million"],[5,"y7c06-o047","Short answer","Name the four layers of the Earth, starting from the outside.","6.3","Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core. crust mantle outer core inner core"],[5,"y7c06-o048","Short answer","Describe the mantle.","6.3","It is molten (hot liquid) rock that can flow, and it lies just under the crust. molten liquid rock flow"],[5,"y7c06-o049","Short answer","Which two metals are the Earth's core mostly made from?","6.3","Iron and nickel. iron nickel"],[5,"y7c06-o050","Short answer","What is the difference between the outer core and the inner core?","6.3","The outer core is molten metal; the inner core is solid metal. outer molten inner solid"],[5,"y7c06-o051","Short answer","Name the most common non-metal in the Earth's crust.","6.3","Oxygen. oxygen"],[5,"y7c06-o065","Short answer","What is the name for the place where two tectonic plates meet?","6.4","A plate boundary. plate boundary"],[5,"y7c06-o066","Short answer","Why does geological change happen at plate boundaries?","6.4","Because the tectonic plates are always moving. always moving"],[5,"y7c06-o097","Short answer","What is an opaque object?","6.5","An object that will not allow light to pass through it. not allow light pass through"],[5,"y7c06-o098","Short answer","What forms when an opaque object passes in front of a source of light?","6.5","A shadow. shadow"],[5,"y7c06-o113","Short answer","True or false: a total lunar eclipse can only be seen in the daytime. Explain your answer.","6.5","False. To see the Earth's shadow pass across the Moon you must be on the side of the Earth facing away from the Sun, and it is night time there. false night facing away"],[5,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The vibration that makes the sound when you speak comes from your ____.","6.1","vocal cords"],[5,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Sound travels from a vibrating object to our ears as a sound ____.","6.1","wave"],[5,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","The substance that a sound wave moves through is called the ____.","6.1","medium"],[5,"qwen-01-08","Gap fill","A vacuum is a space where there are no ____.","6.1","particles"],[5,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","Sound vibrations travel easily through ____.","6.1","solids"],[5,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","Sound waves transfer ____ energy.","6.1","sound"],[5,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","Sound waves reflect best from large, smooth, flat surfaces such as glass and ____.","6.2","tiles"],[5,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","What is the reflection of a sound wave called?","6.2","A vibration An echo A frequency A pitch"],[5,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Bats use ____ to find insects for food.","6.2","echoes"],[5,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","How do boats use echoes to find the depth of water?","6.2","By sending sound from the bottom of the boat By listening to waves hitting the hull By measuring the speed of light in water By using a magnet to pull up rocks"],[5,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","When a sound wave hits a wall, it is reflected back. This reflection is called an ____.","6.2","echo"],[5,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","Which of these surfaces gives the best reflection of sound?","6.2","Rough carpet Smooth concrete Soft fabric Grass"],[5,"qwen-02-10","Gap fill","Sound waves travel as a ____.","6.1","wave"],[5,"qwen-07-01","Multiple choice","What is the outer layer of the Earth called?","6.3","mantle crust core magma"],[5,"qwen-07-02","Gap fill","Under the crust is the ____, which is molten rock that can flow.","6.3","mantle"],[5,"qwen-07-03","Multiple choice","Which metals make up the core of the Earth?","6.3","copper and gold nickel and iron silver and tin lead and zinc"],[5,"qwen-07-04","Gap fill","The outer part of the core is ____, while the inner part is solid.","6.3","molten"],[5,"qwen-07-08","Gap fill","The rocks found in the crust contain metals and ____.","6.3","non-metals"],[5,"qwen-07-09","Multiple choice","How old is the Earth, according to scientists?","6.3","4500 million years 450 million years 45 million years 10 000 years"],[5,"qwen-08-09","Multiple choice","What is the name of the area around the Pacific Ocean with many volcanic eruptions?","6.4","Pacific Ring of Fire Atlantic Ring of Ice Indian Ocean Zone Caribbean Plate Boundary"],[5,"qwen-08-10","Gap fill","When one plate slides underneath another, the process is called ____.","6.4","subduction"],[5,"qwen-08-11","Multiple choice","What causes an earthquake when plates slide past each other?","6.4","friction magma cooling rock melting plate expansion"],[5,"qwen-08-12","Gap fill","Mountains formed when rocks crumple and fold upwards are called ____ mountains.","6.4","fold"],[5,"qwen-09-02","Gap fill","The name for liquid rock when it is on the surface is ____.","6.4","lava"],[5,"qwen-09-08","Gap fill","Light travels in straight lines called ____.","6.5","rays"],[5,"qwen-09-10","Gap fill","A solar eclipse happens when the ____ comes between the Sun and the Earth.","6.5","Moon"],[5,"qwen-10-01","Multiple choice","What is needed for a sound to be made?","6.5","heat light liquid vibration"],[5,"qwen-10-02","Gap fill","A ____ travels from the pen to Sofia’s ears.","6.5","sound wave"],[5,"qwen-10-03","Multiple choice","What does a slinky spring show when used to demonstrate sound waves?","6.5","how particles move the speed of light the colour of sound the weight of air"],[5,"qwen-10-09","Multiple choice","What is the solid outer layer of the Earth called?","6.5","Mantle Crust Outer core Inner core"],[5,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name for a reflected sound wave?","6.2","echo vibration note sound"],[5,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","Which surface is best at reflecting sound?","6.2","a flat wall tall grass a large tree a sandy beach"],[5,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","The line where tectonic plates meet is called a plate ____.","6.4","boundary"],[5,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","What is the name for violent movement between two tectonic plates?","6.4","earthquake volcano flood storm"],[5,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","The part of the Earth made of solid iron and nickel is the inner ____.","6.3","core"],[5,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","What is formed when an opaque object blocks rays of light?","6.5","shadow reflection sound light"],[5,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","The part of the Earth made of molten rock and metal is the outer ____.","6.3","core"],[6,"y7c07-f01","Diagram labelling","This food web shows part of the feeding network on the African plains. What is labelled {n}?","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-f02","Diagram labelling","This food web has decomposers added to it. What is labelled {n}?","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h01","Click the diagram","Click on one of the two producers in this food web.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h02","Click the diagram","Click on the other producer in this food web, the one that is not a tree.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h03","Click the diagram","Click on the herbivore that is eaten by the aardvark.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h04","Click the diagram","Click on the carnivore at the top right of this food web.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h05","Click the diagram","Click on the predator that hunts the springbok and is drawn at the top of the web on the right.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h06","Click the diagram","Click on the herbivore that eats grass and is the prey of both the cheetah and the leopard.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h07","Click the diagram","Click on the organism that the springbok feeds on.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h08","Click the diagram","Click on the consumer that is eaten by the leopard and is drawn on the left of the web.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h09","Click the diagram","Click on the decomposers in this food web.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h10","Click the diagram","Click on the producer in this food web.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h11","Click the diagram","Click on the consumer at the top of this food web.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h12","Click the diagram","Click on the animal on the left of the web that feeds on bananas.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h14","Click the diagram","Click on the place where the energy in this food chain begins.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h15","Click the diagram","Click on the producer in this food chain.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h16","Click the diagram","Click on the consumer in this food chain.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h17","Click the diagram","Click on one of the arrows that shows energy being transferred.","7.2",""],[6,"y7c07-h18","Click the diagram","Click on the largest pale colony growing on the agar jelly.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h19","Click the diagram","Click on one of the small round white colonies near the top of the dish.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h20","Click the diagram","Click on the agar jelly that the microorganisms are growing on.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h21","Click the diagram","Click on the rim of the Petri dish.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h22","Click the diagram","Click on the round green colony of algae near the bottom of the photograph.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h23","Click the diagram","Click on one of the pale, animal-like protozoa in this drop of pond water.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h24","Click the diagram","Click on the protozoan in the top left of the photograph.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h25","Click the diagram","Click on one of the rod-shaped bacteria in this photograph.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h26","Click the diagram","Click on the powdery white yeast on the surface of the grapes.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h27","Click the diagram","Click on one of the yeast cells in this photograph.","7.1",""],[6,"y7c07-h28","Click the diagram","Click on the cap of one of the mushrooms.","7.3",""],[6,"y7c07-h29","Click the diagram","Click on the fruit that the microorganisms have made decay.","7.3",""],[6,"y7c07-h30","Click the diagram","Click on one of the leaves of the apple tree.","7.3",""],[6,"y7c07-h31","Click the diagram","Click on the mould growing on the slice of bread.","7.3",""],[6,"y7c07-h32","Click the diagram","Click on the mould in this close-up photograph.","7.3",""],[6,"y7c07-h33","Click the diagram","Click on one of the fungi growing on the dung.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h34","Click the diagram","Click on the animal dung that the fungi are growing on.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h35","Click the diagram","Click on the mould growing on the left-hand orange.","7.3",""],[6,"y7c07-h36","Click the diagram","Click on the cluster of fungi growing on the log.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h37","Click the diagram","Click on the dead log that the fungus is breaking down.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h38","Click the diagram","Click on the bird in which DDT has been found.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h39","Click the diagram","Click on the point on the graph where the fewest young eagles were raised.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h40","Click the diagram","Click on the label that marks the year DDT was banned.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h41","Click the diagram","Click on the first point on the graph, plotted in 1966.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h42","Click the diagram","Click on the cow dung in the field.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h43","Click the diagram","Click on the cattle that left the dung in the field.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-h44","Click the diagram","Click on the grass growing in the field.","7.4",""],[6,"y7c07-r001","Short answer","Explain why the definition of a microorganism depends on an instrument rather than on what the organism is made of.","7.1","A microorganism is a living organism that is so small that you can only see it clearly by using a microscope, so the group is defined by size rather than by any shared body plan. That is why it contains such different things as bacteria, microscopic fungi, protozoa and algae, which are not close relatives. Grouping by size is useful for studying them because they all need the same equipment and methods, but it does not tell you that they are similar organisms. defined by being too small to see without a microscope defined by size, not by body plan includes bacteria, fungi, protozoa and algae size grouping is practical, not a claim about relatedness"],[6,"y7c07-r002","Short answer","Explain why microorganisms count as living organisms.","7.1","Like all living organisms, microorganisms are made of cells, and being made of cells is one of the things that separates living things from non-living material. Many microorganisms are made of only one cell, so that single cell must do everything the organism needs: feeding, respiring, excreting, growing and reproducing. Being small does not make something non-living; it only means each organism has fewer cells to do the work. made of cells like all living organisms many are single-celled the one cell carries out all life processes small does not mean non-living"],[6,"y7c07-r003","Short answer","Explain what it means to say bacteria are much smaller than animal or plant cells.","7.1","Cells of bacteria are much smaller than animal cells or plant cells, so the comparison is not between a bacterium and a whole animal but between a bacterium and a single cell of that animal. You could fit 1000 of the bacteria in the photograph, lined up end to end, between two of the millimetre marks on a ruler, which means each one is about a thousandth of a millimetre long. This is why a much higher magnification is needed to see bacteria than to see cheek or onion cells. the comparison is cell against cell 1000 bacteria fit in one millimetre about a thousandth of a millimetre each needs much higher magnification"],[6,"y7c07-r004","Short answer","Explain why it is wrong to say that bacteria are germs that make you ill.","7.1","Most bacteria are harmless but there are a few kinds that can make you ill, so illness is caused by a small minority rather than by bacteria in general. Some bacteria live in our digestive systems and are completely harmless, and others are used to change milk into yoghurt. Treating all bacteria as dangerous would mean destroying organisms that we depend on, so the correct statement names the few harmful kinds rather than the whole group. most bacteria are harmless only a few kinds cause illness some live harmlessly in our digestive systems the whole group must not be judged by the minority"],[6,"y7c07-r005","Short answer","Explain why fungi cannot simply be listed as microorganisms.","7.1","Fungi are not always microorganisms, because many fungi, including mushrooms and toadstools, are large enough to see easily without a microscope. Only some fungi are small enough to meet the definition, such as yeast, which is made of single cells. So the fungi are split by the size definition: the microscopic ones belong with the microorganisms and the large ones do not, even though they are the same kind of organism. many fungi are large, such as mushrooms only microscopic fungi meet the definition yeast is single-celled and microscopic the size definition splits the fungi"],[6,"y7c07-r006","Short answer","Explain why you cannot judge how much fungus is present by looking for mushrooms.","7.1","Mushrooms and toadstools are only part of the fungus's body, and they only grow at certain times of year. Most of the time, the fungus is just a tangle of very thin threads, and the threads often grow under the ground or inside a dead log where nothing shows on the surface. So a log with no mushrooms on it may still be full of fungus, and the mushroom is only the part that appears when the fungus reproduces. mushrooms are only part of the fungus they appear only at certain times most of the body is threads underground or in wood no mushroom does not mean no fungus"],[6,"y7c07-r007","Short answer","Explain why a fungus made of threads is hard to study without a microscope.","7.1","The threads are so thin that they are difficult to see without a microscope, and they often grow under the ground or inside a dead log, so they are hidden as well as thin. That means you cannot easily count them, measure them or tell one species from another by eye. A microscope is needed both to see that the threads exist and to see the detail that distinguishes different fungi. threads are too thin to see clearly they are hidden underground or inside wood cannot be counted or identified by eye a microscope is needed for existence and detail"],[6,"y7c07-r008","Short answer","Explain why the white powder on grapes is a living thing rather than dust.","7.1","The powdery substance that you sometimes see on the surface of grapes is made up of millions of cells of yeast, which is a microscopic fungus. The yeast cells feed on sugar in the grapes, which is something dust cannot do, and they reproduce, since little buds can be seen growing out of some of the cells under a microscope. Feeding and reproducing are characteristics of living organisms, so the powder is a huge population of tiny organisms. made of millions of yeast cells the cells feed on sugar in the grapes buds show they reproduce feeding and reproducing mean living"],[6,"y7c07-r009","Short answer","Explain why yeast is classed as a microorganism.","7.1","Yeast is a microscopic fungus whose cells are single cells, not threads, and a single yeast cell is far too small to see clearly without a microscope. Since a microorganism is a living organism that is so small that you can only see it clearly by using a microscope, yeast meets the definition exactly. What you can see on a grape is not one yeast but millions of cells together. yeast is a microscopic fungus made of single cells too small to see clearly without a microscope the visible powder is millions of cells"],[6,"y7c07-r010","Short answer","Suggest why viruses are not usually said to be microorganisms.","7.1","Viruses are even smaller than bacteria, so they easily pass the size test, but the definition also requires a microorganism to be a living organism, and like all living organisms microorganisms are made of cells. A virus is not made of cells and cannot feed, grow or reproduce by itself, so it fails the living part of the definition. That is why viruses are usually left out of the group even though they are small enough. viruses are smaller than bacteria the definition also requires a living organism microorganisms are made of cells; viruses are not fails the living part, not the size part"],[6,"y7c07-r011","Short answer","Explain why a colony is visible when a single cell is not.","7.1","A collection of cells is called a colony, and the colonies are big enough for you to see without a microscope even though each cell in them is not. One cell divides again and again until millions of cells are piled in one place, and millions of tiny cells together occupy a patch large enough for the eye to detect. Seeing a colony is therefore indirect evidence that microorganisms are present. a colony is a collection of cells one cell divides to make millions millions together cover a visible patch a visible colony is evidence of microorganisms"],[6,"y7c07-r012","Short answer","Explain why the Petri dish and the agar jelly must be sterile before the experiment starts.","7.1","The dish and the jelly have to be sterile, which means that any living organisms on them have been killed. If microorganisms were already present on the dish, colonies would grow no matter what you put in, so you could not tell whether they came from the air you were testing or from the dish itself. Starting sterile is what makes any colony that appears afterwards evidence about the sample. sterile means all living organisms have been killed otherwise colonies would grow regardless cannot tell where the microorganisms came from sterility makes the result evidence"],[6,"y7c07-r013","Short answer","Explain how you could find out whether there are microorganisms in material from a compost heap.","7.1","Take a sterile Petri dish containing sterile agar jelly, add a small sample of the compost to the jelly, replace the lid and leave the dish in a warm place for several days. Because the dish and jelly were sterile, any colonies that appear must have grown from microorganisms in the compost sample, and the colonies are big enough to see without a microscope. Setting up a second sterile dish with no compost added shows that the jelly itself was not contaminated. use sterile agar in a Petri dish add a sample of the compost leave warm for several days and look for colonies an untouched dish acts as the control"],[6,"y7c07-r014","Short answer","Marcus compares the microorganisms in the air of two classrooms. Explain which variables he must keep the same and why.","7.1","He must keep the same the type of jelly in the dish and the length of time he leaves the lid off, because both of these would change how many colonies grow regardless of the air in the room. The room where he puts the dish is the one variable he is deliberately changing, so it must not be kept the same. The type of pen he uses to label the dish cannot affect how microorganisms grow, so it does not matter. keep the type of jelly the same keep the time the lid is off the same the room is the variable being changed the pen cannot affect the result"],[6,"y7c07-r015","Short answer","Of the three statements Marcus is offered, explain which one is a testable hypothesis.","7.1","The testable one is that bacteria grow faster at a temperature of 30 degrees Celsius than at 10 degrees Celsius, because it predicts a difference that an experiment can produce and measure: set up identical dishes at the two temperatures and compare the colonies. The other two, that all bacteria are single-celled and that bacterial cells are smaller than yeast cells, are statements checked by looking down a microscope rather than by doing an experiment with a changed variable. A hypothesis worth testing must predict what will happen when something is changed. the temperature statement is testable by experiment set up identical dishes at two temperatures the other two are checked by observation a hypothesis predicts the effect of a change"],[6,"y7c07-r016","Short answer","One dish grows more colonies than the other. Explain what conclusion Marcus can and cannot draw.","7.1","He can conclude that there are more microorganisms in the air in one classroom than in the other, because the dishes were set up identically and only the room was changed. He cannot conclude which microorganisms they are, whether any of them are harmful, or why one room has more, since counting colonies tells you about number only. Going further than the evidence allows would be a conclusion the experiment does not support. can conclude one room's air had more microorganisms the dishes were identical apart from the room cannot say which kinds or whether harmful cannot say why without further evidence"],[6,"y7c07-r017","Short answer","Explain what ecology studies and why it needs more than a list of species.","7.2","The study of organisms in their environment is called ecology, and all the different organisms that live together affect one another in some way. One species of animal may eat another animal, and a plant may provide shelter for an animal, so knowing only which species live in a place tells you nothing about how the place works. Ecology is about the relationships, which is why food chains and food webs are drawn rather than just lists. ecology is the study of organisms in their environment organisms affect one another feeding and shelter are relationships, not lists webs show relationships that a list cannot"],[6,"y7c07-r018","Short answer","Trace where the energy in a plate of rice originally came from.","7.2","The energy in food begins in the Sun, and energy from the Sun reaches the Earth in sunlight. Plants use energy from sunlight to make their own food, and some of the energy from the sunlight goes into the food that the plant stores in its roots, stems, fruits and leaves. When a person eats part of the plant, they eat the food the plant made, and that is how the animal gets energy. energy begins in the Sun sunlight reaches the Earth plants use it to make food they store eating the plant transfers that energy"],[6,"y7c07-r019","Short answer","Explain what the arrows in a food chain mean and why their direction matters.","7.2","The arrows in the food chain show how energy is passed from the Sun to the plant, and then is transferred to the rice, and then to the person, so an arrow means 'energy is transferred to'. It therefore points from the organism that is eaten to the organism that eats it, which is the opposite of the direction students often expect. An arrow drawn the wrong way says that the food is eating the animal, so the diagram would be stating something false. an arrow means energy is transferred to points from the eaten to the eater opposite of what students expect a reversed arrow states something false"],[6,"y7c07-r020","Short answer","Explain why a food chain must begin with a producer.","7.2","The first organism in a food chain is a producer, because plants use energy from the Sun to produce food. All the other organisms in a food chain are consumers, and animals have to eat ready-made food to get their energy, so they cannot start a chain: there would be nothing for the first animal to eat. Every chain therefore has to begin with an organism that can make food rather than take it. the first organism is a producer plants make food using energy from the Sun consumers must eat ready-made food nothing would feed the first animal otherwise"],[6,"y7c07-r021","Short answer","Explain the difference between a herbivore, a carnivore and a predator.","7.2","Consumers that consume only plants are herbivores, and consumers that consume other animals are carnivores, so those two terms describe what an organism eats. Animals that catch, kill and eat other animals are predators, and the animals they eat are their prey, so predator describes how an animal gets its food. That is why all predators are carnivores but a carnivore that eats animals it did not kill is not a predator. herbivores eat only plants carnivores eat other animals predators catch and kill their prey predator describes how the food is obtained"],[6,"y7c07-r022","Short answer","Explain why a food web describes a habitat better than a single food chain.","7.2","A food web is a diagram showing how the organisms in several food chains, and some other organisms, are connected by their feeding habits. A single chain shows only one path, which suggests each animal eats one thing and is eaten by one thing, while in reality most animals eat several kinds of food and are eaten by several predators. The web keeps all those links, so it shows what would be affected if one species disappeared. a web connects several chains a chain shows only one path most animals have several foods and predators the web shows the effect of losing a species"],[6,"y7c07-r023","Short answer","Explain how to read a food chain out of a food web.","7.2","Put your finger on a producer in the web and follow an arrow to the animal that eats it, then follow another arrow from that animal to its predator; you are tracing the path of the energy as it transfers along a food chain. Each chain you pull out must start at a producer and follow the arrows forwards, never backwards. Several different chains can be traced from the same web because most organisms have more than one link. start at a producer follow arrows forwards to each eater never trace backwards several chains exist in one web"],[6,"y7c07-r024","Short answer","In a food web, explain how you can tell which organisms are the producers without being told.","7.2","Producers are the organisms with arrows leaving them but no arrows arriving from another organism, because nothing in the web feeds them: they make their own food using energy from the Sun. Every consumer has at least one arrow coming into it from the food it eats. Looking at which arrows arrive is therefore a reliable test, and it works even when the organisms are unfamiliar. producers have arrows leaving but none arriving nothing in the web feeds them they make their own food from sunlight the test works for unfamiliar organisms"],[6,"y7c07-r025","Short answer","Explain why it helps to identify the producers first and put them at the bottom when constructing a food web.","7.2","It is a good idea to identify the producers first and put them at the bottom of the web, then build up carefully from there. Every chain must begin with a producer, so fixing them first gives every arrow a place to start and stops you from stranding an organism with nothing to feed it. Building upwards also keeps all the arrows pointing the same general way, which makes it much easier to check that none has been drawn backwards. every chain must start with a producer fixing producers first gives arrows a start prevents stranded organisms keeps arrow directions consistent and checkable"],[6,"y7c07-r026","Short answer","In the African plains web, wild dogs, cheetahs and lions all eat the same herbivores. Suggest what this means for them.","7.2","Wild dogs, cheetahs and lions are predators of all of the vertebrate herbivores, so they depend on the same food supply. If the number of herbivores fell, all three predators would be short of food at the same time, and they would also be in competition with one another for what remained. Sharing prey links the three species together even though none of them eats the others. three predators share the same prey a fall in herbivores affects all three they compete for the same food linked without eating each other"],[6,"y7c07-r027","Short answer","Many kinds of birds eat locusts, and locusts eat grass. Explain what would happen to the birds if the grass died in a drought.","7.2","Locusts and grass rats eat grass, so if the grass died the locusts would have almost nothing to eat and their numbers would fall. Many kinds of birds eat locusts, so those birds would then lose their food supply and their numbers would fall as well, even though the birds never ate grass. This shows that an effect on a producer passes along the arrows to organisms further up the chain. locusts lose their food when grass dies locust numbers fall birds that eat locusts lose their food effects pass along the arrows"],[6,"y7c07-r028","Short answer","Explain what actually travels along the arrows of a food chain.","7.2","Food chains and food webs describe how energy, in the form of food, is transferred between organisms, so what passes along the arrow is energy held in the substances of the food. This happens when one organism eats another, meaning that the material of the eaten organism becomes food inside the eater. The arrow therefore records a transfer of energy, not a movement of an organism from one place to another. energy in the form of food is transferred transfer happens when one organism eats another the eaten organism's material becomes food the arrow records a transfer, not a journey"],[6,"y7c07-r029","Short answer","Another group must build your food web from your written descriptions. Explain what your descriptions must include to make that possible.","7.2","Each description must say which organism eats which, because that is what fixes the direction of every arrow, and it must name the producers so that the other group knows where to start the web. It must also cover every organism you want included, since an organism that is never mentioned as eating or being eaten cannot be placed. If any 'eats' statement is missing or ambiguous, the other group will draw a different web from yours. state which organism eats which name the producers so they know where to start mention every organism at least once ambiguity produces a different web"],[6,"y7c07-r030","Short answer","Sea urchins eat sea grass. Explain where the arrow goes and why.","7.2","The arrow goes from the sea grass to the sea urchins, because the arrows in a food chain show how energy flows from one organism to the next and this happens when one organism eats another. The energy was stored in the sea grass, which made it using sunlight, and it moves into the sea urchin when the urchin eats. Drawing the arrow from the urchin to the grass would claim the grass eats the urchin. arrow from sea grass to sea urchins arrows show energy flowing to the eater energy was stored in the grass the reverse arrow would be false"],[6,"y7c07-r031","Short answer","Phytoplankton are tiny microscopic protoctists eaten by several animals. Explain what role they play in the sea grass food web.","7.2","They are producers, because nothing in the web feeds them and several animals feed on them: they are eaten by sea urchins, queen conch and pen shells. Like plants, they make their own food using energy from sunlight, which is why arrows only leave them. They show that a producer does not have to be a plant, and that microscopic organisms can support large parts of a food web. phytoplankton are producers nothing feeds them but several animals eat them they make food using sunlight producers need not be plants"],[6,"y7c07-r032","Short answer","Explain why microorganisms are involved in almost everything that happens in an ecosystem.","7.3","Microorganisms are everywhere: they live in the air, in the soil, in water, on our skin and inside our bodies. Because they are present wherever there is organic matter, any dead body or waste anywhere will have decomposers on it almost immediately. That is why decay is not an occasional event but something happening continuously throughout the habitat. microorganisms live in air, soil, water and on organisms present wherever there is organic matter decomposers reach dead material immediately decay happens continuously"],[6,"y7c07-r033","Short answer","Explain what a decomposer is and give the range of organisms that are decomposers.","7.3","Organisms that make things decay are called decomposers, and many different kinds of microorganisms, including some kinds of bacteria and microscopic fungi, are decomposers. So decomposer is a description of what an organism does rather than a group it belongs to, which is why it covers both bacteria and fungi. Almost all decomposers are microorganisms, with fungi and bacteria being the most important. decomposers make things decay includes bacteria and microscopic fungi describes a role, not a relatedness group fungi and bacteria are the most important"],[6,"y7c07-r034","Short answer","Explain what organic matter is and why an apple counts as organic matter.","7.3","Any substance that has been made by a living organism, by a plant or animal, is called organic matter. Apples come from plants, which are living organisms, so apples are organic matter. This matters because some microorganisms can break down organic matter when they feed on it, so being organic matter is exactly what makes the apple something decomposers can use as food. organic matter is made by a living organism apples come from plants microorganisms break down organic matter as food being organic matter makes it decomposable"],[6,"y7c07-r035","Short answer","Explain how microorganisms cause an apple to rot.","7.3","Some microorganisms can break down organic matter when they feed on it, and this is what has caused the apple to decay. The microscopic fungi have broken down the crisp, fresh apple and made it become brown and soft, because breaking down the substances that gave the apple its structure destroys that structure. The rotting is a side effect of the fungi feeding, not something done to the apple on purpose. microorganisms feed by breaking down organic matter the structure of the apple is broken down it becomes brown and soft rotting is a side effect of feeding"],[6,"y7c07-r036","Short answer","Each spot on a rotting apple is one colony. Explain why the spots start small and spread.","7.3","Each spot on the apple is made up of millions of cells of microscopic fungi, and it began where a single cell or spore landed on the surface. That cell fed on the apple and divided repeatedly, so the colony grew outwards from the point where it started, which is why the spots are roughly round and get larger over time. Several separate spots mean several separate starting points. each spot is a colony of millions of cells started from one cell or spore landing the colony grows outwards as cells divide separate spots mean separate starting points"],[6,"y7c07-r037","Short answer","Describe one way in which decay by microorganisms is not useful, and one way in which it is.","7.3","It is not useful when it spoils food, because the microorganisms that make an apple decay have spoiled the food and no-one would want to eat a rotten apple, so the food is wasted. It is useful because microorganisms break down dead bodies and animal waste, and when they decay organic matter they return the nutrients to the soil where plants can use them to grow. The same process is harmful or helpful depending on whether we wanted the material kept or removed. harmful when it spoils food we wanted to eat useful when it breaks down dead bodies and waste returns nutrients to the soil for plants same process judged by whether we wanted the material"],[6,"y7c07-r038","Short answer","In the bread experiment, one plate is kept warm and the other in a refrigerator. Explain what this is designed to find out and why the pieces must be similar.","7.3","Two similar pieces of bread are used, the same amount of water is added to both, and both are covered, so the only thing different between them is the temperature. That means any difference in how much mould grows must be caused by temperature, which is exactly what the experiment is investigating. If the pieces or the water differed as well, you could not tell which difference caused the result. only temperature is different between the plates bread, water and covering are kept the same any difference in mould must be due to temperature otherwise the cause would be unknown"],[6,"y7c07-r039","Short answer","Explain why the bread is left open to the air for about 30 minutes before being covered.","7.3","Leaving the bread open to the air lets microorganisms from the air settle onto it, since microorganisms live in the air and are everywhere. Without that step there might be too few microorganisms on the bread for mould to appear in a few days, and the two pieces would not have a similar starting population. Covering the plates afterwards keeps the moisture in and stops more microorganisms arriving later. lets microorganisms from the air land on the bread ensures enough are present to grow gives both pieces a similar starting population covering keeps moisture in and later arrivals out"],[6,"y7c07-r040","Short answer","Sofia and Zara test five temperatures from -4 to 60 degrees Celsius. Explain why mould growth is low at both ends of that range.","7.3","Decomposers are living organisms, and like all living organisms they only grow well within a range of temperatures. At about minus 4 degrees it is too cold: the microorganisms are alive but their processes are far too slow, so hardly any growth happens in three days. At 60 degrees it is so hot that the microorganisms are damaged or killed, so again almost no mould grows, and the most growth is at a temperature in between. decomposers only grow well in a range of temperatures too cold means processes are far too slow too hot damages or kills the microorganisms most growth at a temperature in between"],[6,"y7c07-r041","Short answer","Explain what an anomalous result is and why it is left out of the mean.","7.3","One of the results for the dish kept at 40 degrees does not fit the pattern of all the other results, and a result like this is an anomalous result. It is very likely to have come from a mistake in setting up or measuring rather than from the effect being investigated, so including it would pull the mean away from the true value. Ignoring the circled result and averaging the other two gives a mean that better represents what really happened. an anomalous result does not fit the pattern probably caused by a mistake, not the effect including it would distort the mean the mean of the others is more representative"],[6,"y7c07-r042","Short answer","Explain why the girls take three pieces of bread at each temperature instead of one.","7.3","They place three pieces in each of five identical dishes, so each temperature gives three readings rather than one. Repeats let them spot a result that does not fit the pattern, which is how the anomalous result at 40 degrees was identified, and calculating a mean from the remaining results reduces the effect of small random differences. With only one piece at each temperature, a single accident would be invisible and would be treated as the true value. three pieces give repeat readings repeats reveal anomalous results a mean reduces random differences with one reading an accident is invisible"],[6,"y7c07-r043","Short answer","Suggest how the girls could estimate the area of mould growing on the bread.","7.3","Place a clear grid of equal squares over the bread and count how many squares are covered by mould, counting a square when it is more than half covered, then multiply the number of squares by the area of one square. The same grid and the same rule must be used for every piece so that the estimates can be fairly compared. It is an estimate rather than a measurement because the edges of the mould are irregular. place a squared grid over the bread count squares more than half covered multiply by the area of one square use the same grid and rule for every piece"],[6,"y7c07-r044","Short answer","Arun predicts that earthworms and other small animals also help leaves to decay. Explain how his two mesh bags test this.","7.3","The two bags are identical except for the size of the mesh holes: through one, only microorganisms can get through the holes, while through the other, earthworms and other small animals can get through the holes as well. Both bags hold 50 leaf rectangles cut to the same size and are buried close to each other in the same soil, so the only difference is which organisms can reach the leaves. Any extra loss of leaf in the wide-mesh bag must therefore be caused by the larger animals. bags identical apart from mesh size one admits only microorganisms the other also admits small animals extra loss must be due to the larger animals"],[6,"y7c07-r045","Short answer","Explain why Arun digs the bags up every two months for nearly a year instead of once.","7.3","Every two months he digs up the bags and measures how much of the leaves has disappeared from each bag, so he can see how the difference between the bags develops over time rather than just its final size. A single measurement would not show whether decay was steady or whether it slowed in the cold months, and a difference that had not yet appeared would be missed. Repeated readings also let him spot a result that does not fit, which is how he decided one of his results was wrong. shows how the difference develops over time reveals whether decay is steady or seasonal a single reading could miss the difference repeats reveal a result that does not fit"],[6,"y7c07-r046","Short answer","Explain why a gardener should not put glass onto a compost heap.","7.3","Decomposers break down organic matter, which is any substance that has been made by a living organism, and glass has not been made by a living organism. The microorganisms in the heap cannot feed on it, so it will not break down and will simply remain in the compost that is spread onto the soil, where it is dangerous to handle and useless to plants. Items like apple peel and grass cuttings are organic matter and do break down. decomposers only break down organic matter glass is not made by a living organism it will never break down it stays in the compost and is dangerous"],[6,"y7c07-r047","Short answer","Explain why a compost heap turns grass cuttings into something useful for plants.","7.3","Gardeners put waste material, such as grass cuttings, onto the heap and decomposers break these things down until the waste material becomes a dark, crumbly compost. When microorganisms decay organic matter, they return the nutrients to the soil, so the compost carries the nutrients that were locked inside the plant material. The gardener puts the compost onto the soil to help plants to grow well, which is the same recycling that happens naturally in a habitat. decomposers break down the waste material it becomes dark crumbly compost nutrients are returned rather than lost the same recycling that happens naturally"],[6,"y7c07-r048","Short answer","Explain what would happen if there were no decomposers.","7.4","If the dead bodies and waste are not broken down by decomposers, they would just build up, and there would be heaps of dead plants, dead animals and animal dung everywhere. Worse than the mess, the nutrients inside that material would stay locked up, because it is decomposers that return the nutrients to the soil where plants can use them. Plants would run short of nutrients, and since plants are the producers, every consumer in the food web would eventually be affected. dead material would build up nutrients would stay locked inside it plants could not get the nutrients they need all consumers depend on the producers"],[6,"y7c07-r049","Short answer","Explain why returning nutrients is a more important job than clearing away waste.","7.4","Clearing away dead bodies and waste prevents them building up, but there is an even more important reason why decomposers are useful: the dead bodies and waste contain substances called nutrients that living organisms can use to supply them with energy or to help them to grow. When microorganisms decay organic matter they return the nutrients to the soil, so plants can then use the nutrients to help them to grow. Without that return, the supply of nutrients in the soil would run out no matter how tidy the habitat looked. clearing waste only prevents a build-up nutrients are substances organisms need to grow decomposers return them to the soil without the return the soil supply runs out"],[6,"y7c07-r050","Short answer","Explain how decomposers indirectly help animals, even though most animals do not eat them.","7.4","When microorganisms decay organic matter they return the nutrients to the soil, and plants can then use the nutrients to help them grow. This also helps animals, because there are more plants to eat, so more herbivores can be supported and in turn more carnivores. The benefit reaches animals through the producers rather than directly, which is why the link is easy to miss. nutrients are returned to the soil plants grow better more plants means more food for herbivores the benefit reaches animals through the producers"],[6,"y7c07-r051","Short answer","Explain how decomposers get their energy and why that makes them consumers.","7.4","Decomposers feed on almost every organism after it dies and they also feed on waste from animals, and this is how decay microorganisms get their energy: energy from the dead organisms and their waste is transferred to the decomposers. They do not make their own food from sunlight, so they cannot be producers; they take ready-made food, which is what a consumer does. The difference from other consumers is only that their food is already dead. they feed on dead organisms and waste energy is transferred from that material to them they do not make their own food consumers, whose food happens to be dead"],[6,"y7c07-r052","Short answer","Explain why decomposers are usually left out of food web diagrams even though they are important.","7.4","You can show decomposers by adding them to food chains or food webs, but you do not usually do this because you have to draw an arrow from every organism in the food chain or food web to the decomposers, since decomposers feed on almost every organism after it dies. That would mean an arrow from every single box, which makes the diagram look very complicated and hides the feeding relationships it was drawn to show. Leaving them out is a choice for clarity, not a claim that they do not matter. an arrow would be needed from every organism decomposers feed on almost everything after death the diagram becomes too complicated to read omitted for clarity, not because unimportant"],[6,"y7c07-r053","Short answer","Explain why almost all decomposers are microorganisms rather than large animals.","7.4","Almost all decomposers are microorganisms, with fungi and bacteria being the most important, because breaking down organic matter is done by feeding on it wherever it lies. Microorganisms are everywhere, they are small enough to grow right through dead material rather than only on its surface, and a single cell can multiply into millions in days. Larger animals may break dead material into pieces, but it is the microorganisms that break down the substances themselves. microorganisms are everywhere already they grow right through the material, not just on it they multiply into millions quickly larger animals break material up, not down"],[6,"y7c07-r054","Short answer","The grass is greener and taller next to cow dung. Explain how the fungi and the dung help the grass to grow better.","7.4","The cattle leave dung in the field, and dung is organic matter containing nutrients that were in the grass the cattle ate. Fungi and other decomposers feed on the dung and break it down, and when microorganisms decay organic matter they return the nutrients to the soil. The grass roots close to the dung can then take up those nutrients, and plants use nutrients to help them grow, so that grass grows taller and looks greener. dung is organic matter containing nutrients decomposers break it down nutrients are returned to the soil nearby grass takes them up and grows better"],[6,"y7c07-r055","Short answer","The farmer measured five leaves in each place. Explain whether she has enough evidence to conclude that grass grows longer next to cow dung.","7.4","The means do support her conclusion, since the leaves next to dung averaged longer than those away from it, but five leaves from one spot in one field is a small sample and the two sets of readings overlap, with a 9 cm leaf appearing in both groups. Other differences between the two places, such as shade, water or trampling, were not controlled, so the dung may not be the cause. To be confident she should measure many more leaves in several matched pairs of places. the means do point that way only five leaves and the readings overlap other differences were not controlled needs a larger sample and matched places"],[6,"y7c07-r056","Short answer","Explain how bacteria change milk into yoghurt.","7.4","One kind of bacterium changes milk to yoghurt, and when the bacteria do this they change sugar in the milk to acid. The bacteria are decomposers feeding on the milk: they break down a substance in it and release a different substance as a result. Many people like the sharp taste that the acid provides, which is why this particular decay is wanted rather than treated as spoiling. bacteria feed on the milk they change sugar in the milk to acid the acid gives the sharp taste a wanted decay rather than spoiling"],[6,"y7c07-r057","Short answer","Explain why Sofia washes the container with boiling water before making yoghurt.","7.4","Boiling water kills the microorganisms already on the container, in the same way that a Petri dish and agar jelly have to be sterile before an experiment. If other bacteria and fungi were left in the pot they would feed on the milk too and could spoil it, giving an unpleasant taste instead of yoghurt. Starting with a container that is as clean as possible means the bacteria she adds deliberately are the ones that grow. boiling water kills microorganisms on the container same idea as sterilising a Petri dish other microorganisms would spoil the milk the added bacteria are then the ones that grow"],[6,"y7c07-r058","Short answer","Suggest what is in the bought yoghurt that turns Sofia's fresh milk into yoghurt.","7.4","The bought yoghurt contains living bacteria of the kind that changes milk to yoghurt, because that yoghurt was itself made by those bacteria feeding on milk. Adding a spoonful puts a starting population of them into the fresh milk, where they feed and multiply and change sugar in the milk to acid. Without them the milk has no supply of the right bacteria, so it would not turn into yoghurt. the bought yoghurt contains living bacteria they were what made that yoghurt a spoonful seeds the fresh milk they multiply and change sugar to acid"],[6,"y7c07-r059","Short answer","Sofia's milk takes a long time to turn into yoghurt. Suggest what she should change and explain why.","7.4","She put the container in the refrigerator, which is the problem: bacteria grow much faster at warm temperatures than at cold ones, which is why refrigerating food slows decay. If she keeps the container in a warm place instead, the bacteria will feed and multiply much more quickly and the milk will turn to yoghurt in far less time. The same temperature effect makes bread go mouldy quickly in a warm place and slowly in a refrigerator. the refrigerator is slowing the bacteria bacteria grow faster when warm keep the container in a warm place instead same effect as bread going mouldy faster when warm"],[6,"y7c07-r060","Short answer","Suggest how the pH of Sofia's milk changes over four days, and explain why.","7.4","The pH falls, so the milk becomes more acidic. The bacteria change sugar in the milk to acid as they feed, so acid builds up in the container over the four days, and a higher concentration of acid means a lower pH number. Measuring the pH before and after is therefore a way of showing that the bacteria have been active even though you cannot see them. the pH falls bacteria change sugar into acid acid builds up over the days the pH change is evidence the bacteria were active"],[6,"y7c07-r061","Short answer","A gardener is told to compost apple peel but not glass. Explain the general rule this follows.","7.4","The rule is that decomposers can only break down organic matter, which is any substance that has been made by a living organism. Apple peel and grass cuttings come from plants, so microorganisms can feed on them and break them down into compost. Glass was not made by a living organism, so no decomposer can feed on it and it will stay in the heap unchanged however long it is left. decomposers only break down organic matter organic matter is made by a living organism apple peel and grass cuttings qualify glass is not made by a living organism"],[6,"y7c07-r062","Short answer","Describe the ecological role that some microorganisms have as decomposers.","7.4","They feed on dead bodies and animal waste and break this organic matter down, which stops it building up in the habitat. As they do so they return the nutrients in that material to the soil, where plants can take them up and use them to grow. Since plants are the producers that support every consumer in the food web, decomposers keep the whole web supplied by recycling material that would otherwise be lost. feed on dead bodies and waste, breaking them down prevents dead material building up returns nutrients to the soil for plants recycling keeps the whole food web supplied"],[6,"y7c07-r063","Short answer","Explain why decay should be described as recycling rather than destruction.","7.4","Microorganisms break down dead bodies and animal waste, but the material is not destroyed: the nutrients it contained are returned to the soil and plants then use those nutrients to help them grow. The same substances therefore pass out of a dead organism and into a living one, so decay is the step that sends material back round the cycle. Calling it destruction would suggest the material is lost, which would leave no explanation for where plants get their nutrients. the material is not destroyed nutrients return to the soil and then into plants substances pass from dead organisms into living ones destruction would leave no source of nutrients"],[6,"y7c07-o014","Short answer","A mushroom is easy to see. Explain why some fungi that produce mushrooms are still called microorganisms at other times of the year.","7.1","The mushroom is only part of the fungus's body and only grows at certain times of year. Most of the time the fungus is a tangle of very thin threads that you need a microscope to see. part threads certain times"],[6,"y7c07-o021","Short answer","Why is yeast classed as a microorganism even though you can see it on a grape?","7.1","What you see is a colony of millions of individual cells. Each individual cell is a complete organism, and a single cell can only be seen with a microscope. colony single cell microscope"],[6,"y7c07-o046","Short answer","What is ecology?","7.2","The study of organisms in their environment. study environment"],[6,"y7c07-o047","Short answer","Do organisms that live together affect one another?","7.2","Yes. All the different organisms that live together affect one another in some way. yes affect"],[6,"y7c07-o090","Short answer","Give one strength of a food web as a model.","7.2","It shows the feeding relationships between organisms in a simple, visual way, so you can see how energy passes from one organism to another. simple visual energy"],[6,"y7c07-o111","Short answer","Describe an experiment to test whether bacteria grow faster at higher temperatures.","7.3","Set up three identical Petri dishes of sterile agar, expose them for the same time in the same place, then keep one in a fridge, one at room temperature and one in an incubator, and compare the sizes of the colonies after a set time. identical same time different temperatures compare"],[6,"y7c07-o158","Short answer","A fungus on a dead log produces toadstools. Is it a microorganism? Explain.","7.4","It is not a microorganism while it is producing toadstools. At other times the fungus is a microorganism, existing as microscopic threads growing through the wood. toadstools threads microscopic"],[6,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Many microorganisms are made of only one cell, so they are called ____.","7.1","single-celled"],[6,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","One of these single cells is a bacterium; the word for more than one of them is ____.","7.1","bacteria"],[6,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What are mushrooms and toadstools actually part of?","7.1","The fungus's body A type of plant root Animal skin Rock formations"],[6,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","What is the white powdery substance on grapes made of?","7.1","Millions of cells of yeast Dust particles Salt crystals Mould spores only"],[6,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","What special jelly do scientists use to grow microorganisms in a Petri dish?","7.1","Agar jelly Jelly from fruit Gelatin dessert Mud"],[6,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","The study of organisms in their environment is called ____.","7.2","ecology"],[6,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Animals that catch, kill and eat other animals are called ____.","7.2","predators"],[6,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What is the first organism in a food chain?","7.2","A producer A carnivore A predator A herbivore"],[6,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","Where does the energy in food originally come from?","7.2","The Sun The soil Other animals Water"],[6,"qwen-06-11","Multiple choice","Which file number is in the middle of the list?","7.2","1246 1247 1248 1249"],[6,"qwen-12-01","Multiple choice","What are organisms called that make things decay?","7.3","Producers Decomposers Predators Herbivores"],[6,"qwen-12-02","Gap fill","Any substance that has been made by a living organism is called ____ matter.","7.3","organic"],[6,"qwen-12-04","Gap fill","The spots on the mouldy apple are made up of millions of cells of microscopic ____.","7.3","fungi"],[6,"qwen-12-05","Multiple choice","Where do microorganisms live?","7.3","Only in water In the air, soil, and on skin Only inside bodies Only in the sun"],[6,"qwen-12-11","Multiple choice","What is the process called when microorganisms change an apple?","7.3","Photosynthesis Decay Evaporation Combustion"],[6,"qwen-13-06","Gap fill","Substances in dead bodies and waste that living organisms use for energy or growth are called ____.","7.4","nutrients"],[6,"qwen-13-08","Gap fill","In a food web, arrows show the direction in which ____ flows from one organism to another.","7.4","energy"],[6,"qwen-13-10","Gap fill","Decomposers feed on almost every organism after it dies and also feed on ____ from animals.","7.4","waste"],[6,"qwen-13-12","Gap fill","The part of the fungus that is inside a log, breaking it down, is not visible without a ____.","7.4","microscope"],[6,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the jelly used in the dish for growing microorganisms?","7.1","Agar Gelatin Jelly Starch"],[6,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The dish used in the experiment contains sterile ____ jelly.","7.1","agar"],[6,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","What should Marcus write on the base of each dish?","7.1","The date The classroom number His name The time"],[6,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","Marcus must label the base of each dish with the ____ number.","7.1","classroom"],[6,"qwenw-04-01","Multiple choice","In the food chain involving sea grass, what do sea urchins eat?","7.2","Sea grass Phytoplankton Helmet snails Pen shells"],[6,"qwenw-04-02","Gap fill","Phytoplankton are tiny, microscopic ____.","7.2","protoctists"],[6,"qwenw-04-04","Gap fill","Decomposers break down waste material into a dark, crumbly ____.","7.3","compost"],[6,"qwenw-04-05","Multiple choice","Which of the following items should NOT be put on a compost heap?","7.3","Apple peel Grass cuttings Newspaper Glass"],[6,"qwenw-04-07","Multiple choice","Which organisms could get through the holes in Bag B?","7.3","Earthworms Small animals Only microorganisms All soil creatures"],[6,"qwenw-04-08","Gap fill","Giraffes and impala eat the leaves from ____ trees.","7.2","acacia"],[6,"qwenw-04-09","Multiple choice","Which animals are predators of all the vertebrate herbivores in the African grassy plains?","7.2","Locusts and grass rats Wild dogs, cheetahs and lions Eagles and birds Giraffes and impala"],[6,"qwenw-04-10","Gap fill","Sofia and Zara investigated how ____ affects the growth of mould on moist bread.","7.3","temperature"],[6,"qwenw-06-06","Gap fill","A diagram showing how energy passes from one organism to another is called a ____.","7.4","food web"],[6,"qwenw-06-08","Gap fill","An organism that is so small it cannot be seen without a microscope is called a ____.","7.4","microorganism"],[6,"qwenw-06-11","Multiple choice","Which word from the list describes an organism that is so small it cannot be seen without a microscope?","7.4","Bacterium Decomposer Microorganism Producer"],[6,"qwenw-07-01","Multiple choice","In the meadow food web described, what do lizards eat?","7.4","Grass Caterpillars Grasshoppers Hawks"],[7,"y7c08-f01","Diagram labelling","Six everyday changes are shown. Which change is shown in photograph {n}?","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-f02","Diagram labelling","These diagrams show the test for hydrogen. What is labelled {n}?","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-f03","Diagram labelling","This apparatus collects hydrogen gas over water. What is labelled {n}?","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-f04","Diagram labelling","Acid is being run into a flask of alkali from a burette. What is labelled {n}?","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-f05","Diagram labelling","Indigestion powder is being added to acid. What is labelled {n}?","8.3",""],[7,"y7c08-f06","Diagram labelling","This is the test for oxygen. What is labelled {n}?","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-f07","Diagram labelling","This apparatus tests the gas given off when calcium carbonate reacts with acid. What is labelled {n}?","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-f08","Diagram labelling","Acid is being added to an alkali from a burette. What is labelled {n}?","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-f09","Diagram labelling","Zinc metal has been added to an acid. What is labelled {n}?","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h01","Click the diagram","Click on the flames, where carbon is combining with oxygen from the air.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h02","Click the diagram","Click on a piece of charcoal that is still glowing red hot.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h04","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph that shows magnesium ribbon burning with a bright flame.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h05","Click the diagram","Click on the white powder that is left after the magnesium has burned.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h07","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph that shows chocolate melting, a physical change.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h08","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph that shows coal burning.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h11","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the glass trough.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h12","Click the diagram","Click on the smoke and flame above the water, where the hydrogen is burning.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h13","Click the diagram","Click on the bubbles of gas filling the test tube.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h14","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of magnesium at the bottom of the test tube.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h15","Click the diagram","Click on the burning splint being put into the mouth of the test tube.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h16","Click the diagram","Click on the hand keeping a finger over the end of the test tube.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h18","Click the diagram","Click on the thistle funnel that the acid is poured in through.","8.1",""],[7,"y7c08-h22","Click the diagram","Click on the burette that the acid is added from.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h23","Click the diagram","Click on the flask in which the indicator has turned green, showing the solution is neutral.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h24","Click the diagram","Click on the flask in which the indicator is still blue, showing the solution is alkaline.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h25","Click the diagram","Click on the flask in which too much acid has been added, so the solution is weakly acidic.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h26","Click the diagram","Click on the red part of the tube, where the solution is most strongly acidic.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h27","Click the diagram","Click on the yellow band, where the solution is only weakly acidic.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h28","Click the diagram","Click on the dark purple part of the tube, where the solution is most strongly alkaline.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h29","Click the diagram","Click on the clamp that holds the test tube still.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h30","Click the diagram","Click on one of the antacid medicines used to treat indigestion.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h31","Click the diagram","Click on the container of antacid tablets on the right of the photograph.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h32","Click the diagram","Click on the toothpaste on the brush.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h33","Click the diagram","Click on the bristles of the toothbrush.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h34","Click the diagram","Click on the machine that is spreading lime onto the field.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h35","Click the diagram","Click on the bare brown soil that the lime is being spread onto.","8.2",""],[7,"y7c08-h36","Click the diagram","Click on the spatula that is being used to add the powder.","8.3",""],[7,"y7c08-h37","Click the diagram","Click on the indigestion powder in the dish.","8.3",""],[7,"y7c08-h38","Click the diagram","Click on the acid and universal indicator in the beaker.","8.3",""],[7,"y7c08-h39","Click the diagram","Click on the froth of bubbles at the top of the test tube.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h40","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of magnesium that is reacting in the acid.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h41","Click the diagram","Click on the test tube in which the limewater has turned most cloudy.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h42","Click the diagram","Click on the test tube of limewater that is still clear.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h44","Click the diagram","Click on the glowing splint being placed in the neck of the test tube.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h47","Click the diagram","Click on the rubber bung that seals the test tube.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h51","Click the diagram","Click on the blue liquid in the beaker.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h52","Click the diagram","Click on the Bunsen burner that is heating the beaker.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h54","Click the diagram","Click on the burette that the hydrochloric acid is added from.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h57","Click the diagram","Click on the white solid that has formed in the test tube.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h58","Click the diagram","Click on the test tube in which the liquid has gone completely milky.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-h59","Click the diagram","Click on the pieces of zinc at the bottom of the test tube.","8.4",""],[7,"y7c08-r001","Short answer","Explain the difference between a physical property and a chemical property, using iron and hydrochloric acid as examples.","8.1","Physical properties are features such as whether a substance is a solid, liquid or gas and what its boiling or melting temperature is, so some physical properties of iron are that it is a grey, heavy solid with a melting point of 1538 degrees Celsius. Chemical properties are features such as how acidic or alkaline a substance is and how it reacts with water, acids or metals, so a chemical property of hydrochloric acid is that it has a pH of 2. The difference is that a physical property can be observed without changing the substance, while a chemical property only shows itself when the substance reacts and becomes something else. physical properties include state and melting or boiling point chemical properties include pH and how it reacts physical properties are seen without changing the substance chemical properties show only during a reaction"],[7,"y7c08-r002","Short answer","Explain why freezing water is a physical change but heating iron with sulfur is a chemical change.","8.1","In a physical change, no new substances are formed: when liquid water freezes, the water has changed state but it is still the same substance after the change, so it can be melted back to exactly what it was. In a chemical change, new substances are formed, and when iron and sulfur are heated together they form a new substance, a compound called iron sulfide, whose properties are different from both starting materials. The test is not whether the appearance changed but whether a different substance now exists. physical change forms no new substance frozen water is still water and can be melted back chemical change forms a new substance the test is whether a different substance now exists"],[7,"y7c08-r003","Short answer","Explain what is meant by reactants and products, using the iron and sulfur reaction.","8.1","The reactants are the substances that react together, which here are the iron and the sulfur, and the products are the new substances made in the reaction. In this reaction there is only one product, iron sulfide, formed because the iron atoms have combined and bonded with the sulfur atoms. Reactants and products are simply the before and after of the same set of atoms, which is why the words are used for every reaction rather than only this one. reactants are the substances that react together products are the new substances made iron and sulfur are reactants, iron sulfide is the product before and after of the same atoms"],[7,"y7c08-r004","Short answer","Explain why splitting water into oxygen and hydrogen still counts as a chemical reaction.","8.1","In some chemical reactions a substance breaks apart to make new substances, and water can be split apart to form oxygen and hydrogen. The test for a chemical reaction is whether new substances are formed, and hydrogen and oxygen are different substances from water, with completely different properties. A reaction does not have to be two things joining; one substance breaking into two also produces new substances. a substance can break apart in a reaction water splits into hydrogen and oxygen new substances with different properties are formed reactions need not be things joining together"],[7,"y7c08-r005","Short answer","Explain why burning is a chemical reaction rather than a physical change.","8.1","When a substance burns, the substance reacts with the oxygen in the air, so a second substance is involved and new substances are formed. Sometimes ashes are formed, and the new substances in the ashes are oxides, which have different properties from the material that burned. Since a chemical change is one in which new substances are formed, burning meets the test, and nothing you do can turn the ash back into the original material. burning is a reaction with oxygen in the air new substances such as oxides are formed ash has different properties from the original the change cannot be reversed"],[7,"y7c08-r006","Short answer","Charcoal burns and leaves ash, yet most of it seems to vanish. Explain where it goes.","8.1","Charcoal is made up of the element carbon, and when carbon burns it combines with oxygen in the air to make the gas carbon dioxide. The carbon has not disappeared; it has become part of a gas that spreads into the air where it cannot be seen, so only the ash that is left behind is visible. The mass appears to be lost only because the product is a gas rather than a solid. charcoal is carbon carbon combines with oxygen to make carbon dioxide the product is a gas that spreads into the air mass seems lost because it left as a gas"],[7,"y7c08-r007","Short answer","A student says burning magnesium destroys it. Explain why this is wrong.","8.1","When magnesium metal is burnt, a white powder is formed, and this powder is magnesium oxide: a new substance has been formed from magnesium and oxygen. The magnesium atoms are still there, now bonded to oxygen atoms, so nothing has been destroyed; the atoms have been rearranged into a different substance. A chemical property of magnesium is that it burns in air to form magnesium oxide by combining with oxygen. a white powder, magnesium oxide, is formed the product contains both magnesium and oxygen atoms are rearranged, not destroyed burning in air is a chemical property of magnesium"],[7,"y7c08-r008","Short answer","Explain why the properties of magnesium oxide are not halfway between those of magnesium and oxygen.","8.1","When you compare the properties of the reactants and products in burning magnesium, you can see that the properties of the product are different from those of the reactants. Magnesium oxide is a new substance in which the atoms are bonded together in a new arrangement, so it behaves as itself rather than as a mixture of a metal and a gas. This is exactly what separates a compound formed in a reaction from a mixture, whose parts keep their own properties. the product's properties differ from the reactants' atoms are bonded in a new arrangement the compound behaves as itself unlike a mixture, whose parts keep their properties"],[7,"y7c08-r009","Short answer","Potassium is soft enough to cut with a knife and must be stored under oil. Explain which of these is a physical property and which is chemical.","8.1","Potassium being very soft and able to be cut with a knife is a physical property, because it can be observed without the potassium becoming a different substance. Potassium being so reactive that it has to be stored under oil to prevent it reacting with the water vapour in the air is a chemical property, because it describes what it turns into when it meets another substance. The oil works by keeping air and water vapour away from the metal surface. softness is a physical property, observed without change reactivity with water vapour is a chemical property chemical properties describe what it turns into oil keeps air and water vapour off the metal"],[7,"y7c08-r010","Short answer","Explain what happens when a small piece of potassium is placed in a large trough of water.","8.1","When a very small piece of potassium is placed in a large trough of water, hydrogen gas is given off, and the reaction produces so much heat that the gas burns. So two things show a reaction has happened: a gas is produced and heat is released, and that heat is enough to set the hydrogen alight. A new substance, potassium hydroxide, is also formed in the water. hydrogen gas is given off so much heat is produced that the gas burns gas production and heat both signal a reaction potassium hydroxide is also formed"],[7,"y7c08-r011","Short answer","Explain why the potassium and water reaction is done as a teacher demonstration behind a safety screen.","8.1","You cannot carry out the potassium and water reaction yourself; if your teacher shows it as a demonstration you must wear safety glasses and there must be a safety screen in front of the beaker. The reaction releases so much heat that the hydrogen produced catches fire, and the hot alkaline solution can be thrown out of the beaker. The screen stops anything reaching the class, and only a very small piece is used so the reaction stays small enough to control. the reaction is violent and releases much heat the hydrogen produced catches fire hot alkaline liquid can be thrown out the screen protects observers and only a tiny piece is used"],[7,"y7c08-r012","Short answer","Explain how you know that a reaction has happened when magnesium is placed in hydrochloric acid.","8.1","When magnesium is placed in hydrochloric acid, bubbles of gas are given off, and the magnesium has reacted with the hydrochloric acid and formed new substances: the gas is hydrogen and magnesium chloride has been formed. Bubbles show a gas is being produced, which is one of the most useful clues that a reaction has taken place, and the magnesium is used up as it is converted into the new compound. Since new substances exist afterwards, this is a chemical reaction. bubbles show a gas is produced the gas is hydrogen and magnesium chloride forms the magnesium is used up new substances mean a chemical reaction"],[7,"y7c08-r013","Short answer","Explain why seeing bubbles is not enough to identify the gas produced.","8.1","When you see bubbles forming in a reaction, you know that a gas is being produced, but you cannot tell what type of gas it is, because all gases look the same when they form bubbles in a liquid. Different gases behave differently in a chemical test, so a test must be done to identify which one it is: hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop, oxygen relights a glowing splint and carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy. The bubble tells you the kind of change; the test tells you the substance. bubbles show a gas but all gases look alike a chemical test is needed to identify it squeaky pop, relit splint, cloudy limewater bubbles show the change, the test names the substance"],[7,"y7c08-r014","Short answer","Describe the test for hydrogen and explain why the finger must stay over the test tube until the last moment.","8.1","To carry out the test you light a splint and place it in the mouth of the test tube, and hydrogen gas burns with a squeaky pop. You need to keep your finger over the end of the test tube until the last moment or you will have no hydrogen left to test, because hydrogen gas is lighter than air and will float straight out of an open tube. When the hydrogen pops it is reacting with oxygen in the air to form water. light a splint at the mouth of the tube hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop hydrogen is lighter than air and escapes the pop is hydrogen reacting with oxygen to form water"],[7,"y7c08-r015","Short answer","Zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid in the same way as magnesium. Predict the products and explain your reasoning.","8.1","The products are zinc chloride and hydrogen. Magnesium and hydrochloric acid give magnesium chloride and hydrogen, and zinc is a metal reacting with the same acid, so the pattern is that a metal plus hydrochloric acid gives the metal chloride plus hydrogen. The chlorine comes from the acid and joins the metal, while the hydrogen from the acid is released as a gas. zinc chloride and hydrogen follows the pattern metal plus acid chlorine from the acid joins the metal hydrogen from the acid is released as gas"],[7,"y7c08-r016","Short answer","Give two chemical properties and two physical properties of magnesium, and explain why they belong in those groups.","8.1","Two chemical properties are that magnesium burns in air to form magnesium oxide by combining with oxygen, and that it reacts with hydrochloric acid to give magnesium chloride and hydrogen; both describe magnesium turning into a different substance. Two physical properties are that it is a solid at room temperature and that it is a grey metal that can be drawn into a ribbon; both can be observed while it is still magnesium. The grouping depends on whether the substance has to change identity for the property to show. chemical: burns in air to form magnesium oxide chemical: reacts with acid to give a chloride and hydrogen physical: solid, grey metal, drawn into ribbon grouping depends on whether it changes identity"],[7,"y7c08-r017","Short answer","Explain how you would decide from a photograph whether a change is physical or chemical.","8.1","Look for evidence that new substances have been formed, since in a chemical change new substances are formed while in a physical change no new substances are formed. Clues for a chemical reaction include a gas being given off, a permanent colour change, a solid appearing from two liquids, or heat being released, and a good check is whether the change could be reversed simply by cooling or warming. If the material has only changed state or shape, and could be changed back, it is a physical change. look for evidence of new substances gas, colour change, solid forming, heat released check whether it can simply be reversed state or shape change only means physical"],[7,"y7c08-r018","Short answer","Explain what neutralisation is.","8.2","Acids and alkalis can cancel each other out: when you mix them together they react and make a neutral solution, and this is called neutralisation. It is a chemical change because new substances are formed, not merely a dilution, and neutrality is also a chemical property of a substance. The neutral solution has a pH of 7, which is neither acidic nor alkaline. acid and alkali cancel each other out they react to make a neutral solution a chemical change forming new substances neutral means pH 7"],[7,"y7c08-r019","Short answer","Explain why the amount of acid added to an alkali matters so much.","8.2","If you add too much acid to an alkali it makes an acidic liquid, and if you add too little acid to an alkali it stays as an alkaline liquid, so only one particular amount gives a neutral solution. That amount is where the acid exactly cancels the alkali, and going even slightly past it takes the solution out the other side. This is why you add the acid very slowly, a few drops at a time, which makes it easier to judge exactly when it becomes neutral. too much acid makes the mixture acidic too little leaves it alkaline only one amount gives neutral add slowly, a few drops at a time, to catch that point"],[7,"y7c08-r020","Short answer","Explain why a burette is used rather than pouring acid from a beaker.","8.2","You can use a special piece of equipment called a burette to neutralise an alkali very accurately, because a burette lets you add the acid a few drops at a time and read exactly what volume has been added. Since only one particular volume of acid makes the solution neutral, and a little too much makes it acidic, control over the amount is what decides whether you can find that point. Pouring from a beaker gives neither fine control nor a measurement. a burette adds acid a few drops at a time it measures the exact volume added only one volume gives neutrality a beaker gives no control and no measurement"],[7,"y7c08-r021","Short answer","Describe how the pH in the flask changes as acid is added from the burette, and explain each stage.","8.2","At the start the pH in the flask is about 13 because it contains alkali, and as the acid is added the pH becomes lower because each addition cancels some of the alkali. After 25 cubic centimetres of acid the pH is 7 and the liquid is neutral, since the acid has reacted with the alkali and neutralised it exactly. When a little more acid is added the pH falls to about 6 and the liquid is weakly acidic, because there is now acid left over with no alkali to react with. starts at about pH 13 with alkali present pH falls as acid cancels alkali pH 7 when exactly neutralised below 7 because acid is now left over"],[7,"y7c08-r022","Short answer","Explain why the flask is shaken each time some acid is added.","8.2","The flask is shaken each time some acid is added so that the acid mixes right through the alkali rather than sitting in one part of the flask. If it is not mixed, the indicator near where the acid landed may change colour while most of the solution is still alkaline, so you would think the solution was neutral before it really was. Shaking makes the colour you see represent the whole flask. shaking mixes the acid through the alkali unmixed acid sits in one region the indicator would change locally and mislead you the colour then represents the whole flask"],[7,"y7c08-r023","Short answer","Name the products when hydrochloric acid neutralises sodium hydroxide, and explain why a neutral solution can still contain substances.","8.2","If you use hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide, these are the reactants, and when they react together the products that form are sodium chloride and water. The solution is neutral because neither product is acidic or alkaline, not because the flask is empty; the atoms from both reactants are still present in the new substances. This shows neutralisation is a chemical reaction that makes new substances, not a cancelling out that destroys them. products are sodium chloride and water neither product is acidic or alkaline the atoms are still present in the new substances neutralisation makes new substances, not nothing"],[7,"y7c08-r024","Short answer","Explain why the stomach produces acid and what happens when it produces too much.","8.2","Your stomach produces hydrochloric acid, and this acid gives the stomach the right conditions to digest your food, so the acid is needed rather than being a fault. When your stomach produces too much acid you have indigestion and it can be very uncomfortable. The problem is therefore an excess rather than the presence of acid, which is why the treatment reduces the acid instead of removing it altogether. stomach acid gives the right conditions for digestion the acid is needed, not a fault too much acid causes indigestion treatment reduces the excess, not all of it"],[7,"y7c08-r025","Short answer","Explain how antacid medicines relieve indigestion.","8.2","Antacid medicines are all alkalis and they neutralise the acid, so when one is swallowed it reacts with the excess hydrochloric acid in the stomach. Acids and alkalis cancel each other out to make a neutral solution, so the amount of acid falls and the discomfort is eased. They do not remove all the acid, because the stomach still needs acid to digest food. antacids are alkalis they react with the excess stomach acid acid and alkali cancel to give a neutral solution not all acid is removed, since digestion needs it"],[7,"y7c08-r026","Short answer","Explain how bacteria in your mouth cause tooth decay and how toothpaste helps.","8.2","There are millions of bacteria in your mouth and these bacteria feed on the pieces of food left on your teeth, producing acid when they feed. This acid damages your teeth and makes them decay, so the damage comes from the acid rather than from the bacteria touching the tooth. Toothpaste contains alkali, which helps to neutralise the acid, and brushing also removes the food pieces the bacteria were feeding on. bacteria feed on food left on teeth they produce acid as they feed the acid damages the teeth toothpaste is alkaline and neutralises it; brushing removes the food"],[7,"y7c08-r027","Short answer","Explain why alkalis are dropped into some lakes.","8.2","In some parts of the world there are harmful chemicals in the air that make the rain acidic, and this acid rain damages trees and changes the pH of the lakes, rivers and ponds. The plants and animals that live in the lakes cannot live in acid conditions, so the species there die out as the pH falls. Some countries drop alkalis into the lakes to neutralise the acid, raising the pH back towards 7 so that the organisms can survive. acid rain lowers the pH of lakes lake plants and animals cannot live in acid conditions alkali neutralises the acid raises the pH back towards 7"],[7,"y7c08-r028","Short answer","Explain why farmers spread lime on acidic soil.","8.2","In some areas the soil is very acidic and plants do not grow well, so farmers spread lime on the soil to neutralise the acid so that the plants can grow better. Lime is an alkali, and acids and alkalis cancel each other out to make a neutral solution, so adding lime raises the pH of the soil towards 7. The crops grow better because most plants take up nutrients properly only when the soil is not strongly acidic. acidic soil stops plants growing well lime is an alkali it neutralises the acid and raises the pH plants then take up nutrients properly"],[7,"y7c08-r029","Short answer","Adding alkali to a lake and adding lime to a field are treatments for the same problem. Explain what is the same and what the limitation is.","8.2","Both add an alkali to neutralise an acid, raising the pH back towards 7 so that organisms can live and grow. In both cases the treatment deals with the acid that is already there but does nothing about what is producing it, so acid rain will keep falling and acid will keep being formed in the soil. That means the treatment has to be repeated, and solving the problem properly means stopping the harmful chemicals getting into the air. both neutralise acid with an alkali both raise the pH towards 7 neither removes the cause the treatment must be repeated"],[7,"y7c08-r030","Short answer","Explain why 'Which is the best indigestion remedy?' is not a good scientific question.","8.3","It is not a very precise question, because 'best' could mean the most pleasant tasting, the cheapest, the most effective or the most cost-effective, and different answers would win under each meaning. Scientists need to write their question in a way that they can test, so it must say exactly what is to be measured. A better version is 'Which indigestion powder neutralises the acid, using the least amount of powder?', which names one measurable quantity. 'best' has several different meanings different remedies win under different meanings a testable question must say what is measured rewrite it to name one measurable quantity"],[7,"y7c08-r031","Short answer","Explain why a beaker of acid is used instead of real stomach acid, and what the drawback is.","8.3","If you are investigating the effect of indigestion powders on stomach acid you cannot use your own stomach acid, so you have to use a model instead, such as a beaker of acid. The model lets you control exactly how much acid there is and watch the colour change, which you could never do inside a person. Its drawback is that a beaker has no food, no stomach lining and no continuing production of acid, so a powder that works well in the beaker may behave differently in a real stomach. real stomach acid cannot be used a beaker of acid is a model it allows control and observation no food, lining or continuing acid supply"],[7,"y7c08-r032","Short answer","In Marcus and Arun's test of three powders, explain which variables are kept the same and which is changed.","8.3","They put 20 cubic centimetres of hydrochloric acid into each of three beakers and a few drops of universal indicator in each, so the volume of acid, its concentration and the amount of indicator are kept the same. The variable being changed is which indigestion powder is used, A, B or C. What they measure is the number of spatulas needed until the acid is neutralised and the universal indicator is green, and that measurement is only fair because everything except the powder was identical. same volume and strength of acid in each beaker same amount of indicator the powder is the variable changed the count of spatulas is only fair if the rest is identical"],[7,"y7c08-r033","Short answer","Explain how the students know when the acid has been neutralised.","8.3","They add the indigestion powder, spatula by spatula, until the acid is neutralised and the universal indicator is green, since green is the colour universal indicator shows at pH 7. Adding the powder in small equal amounts means they can stop at the first sign of green rather than overshooting into blue. Counting the spatulas turns a colour change into a number they can compare between powders. universal indicator turns green at pH 7 powder added spatula by spatula small additions avoid overshooting counting spatulas turns colour into a comparable number"],[7,"y7c08-r034","Short answer","After one set of results, explain why there is not enough evidence to be certain which powder is most effective.","8.3","One run of each powder gives a single reading, and a single reading could be wrong because of a spilled spatula, an uneven scoop or the colour being judged too early. With no repeats there is nothing to compare it against, so a mistake looks exactly like a real result. Marcus and Arun repeat their investigation twice more precisely so that a result that does not fit can be spotted and a mean can be worked out. one reading per powder gives nothing to compare an uneven scoop or misjudged colour could distort it a mistake is indistinguishable from a real result repeats allow an odd result to be spotted and a mean taken"],[7,"y7c08-r035","Short answer","One of the three repeats looks wrong. Explain what they should do about it and why.","8.3","They should identify it as an anomalous result, check whether they can explain it, for example an unevenly filled spatula or a colour judged too early, and leave it out when working out the mean. Including it would drag the mean away from the value the other two repeats agree on, giving a misleading comparison between the powders. If possible they should repeat that particular measurement to replace it rather than simply discard it. identify it as anomalous and look for a cause leave it out of the mean it would drag the mean away from the agreed value better to repeat that measurement than just discard it"],[7,"y7c08-r036","Short answer","Explain what you should ask when you are shown evidence from someone else's investigation.","8.3","Ask how many times the measurement was repeated and whether the repeats agreed, because a single reading may be a mistake, and ask what was kept the same so that you can judge whether the comparison was fair. Ask exactly what was measured and how the endpoint was judged, since a vague endpoint makes the numbers unreliable. Only then can you decide whether the conclusion really follows from the results. how many repeats and did they agree what was kept the same exactly what was measured and how only then judge whether the conclusion follows"],[7,"y7c08-r037","Short answer","An environmental scientist tests lake water with sodium hydroxide. Explain what she must do to make it a fair test.","8.3","She must use the same volume of lake water from each lake, the same concentration of sodium hydroxide solution, the same amount of universal indicator and the same colour as the endpoint, so that the only difference between the tests is which lake the sample came from. She must also add the sodium hydroxide in the same small steps each time and stir each sample the same way. Then the volume of alkali needed depends only on how acidic the sample was. same volume of lake water from each lake same concentration of alkali and same indicator same endpoint colour and same adding and stirring volume of alkali then depends only on acidity"],[7,"y7c08-r038","Short answer","Explain why the scientist tests each lake sample three times.","8.3","Testing three times gives repeat readings, and if the three agree closely she can be confident the value is reliable rather than the result of a single mistake in measuring or judging the colour. If one reading does not fit, she can identify it as anomalous and leave it out. Taking a mean of the readings also reduces the effect of small random differences between tests. three readings can be compared close agreement gives confidence an odd reading can be identified and excluded the mean reduces random differences"],[7,"y7c08-r039","Short answer","Explain how the volume of sodium hydroxide needed tells the scientist which lake is most acidic.","8.3","Acids and alkalis cancel each other out, so the more acid a sample contains, the more sodium hydroxide solution is needed before the sample becomes neutral. If the same volume of water is taken from each lake and the same alkali is used, the sample that needs the most alkali is the most acidic. The scientist needs to know which lake is the most acidic so that work can start on neutralising the acid there first. more acid needs more alkali to neutralise it same water volume and same alkali for each sample the sample needing most alkali is most acidic tells her which lake to treat first"],[7,"y7c08-r040","Short answer","Arun and Zara add 4 g of antacid, 1 g at a time, to 25 cm3 of acid at pH 2. Explain which variables they kept the same and why it matters.","8.3","They kept the same the volume of acid at 25 cubic centimetres, the acidity of that acid at pH 2, the size of each addition at 1 gram and the total mass added at 4 grams, and they checked the pH the same way with a pH meter each time. Only the identity of the antacid changed. This matters because the comparison is between the powders: if one powder had been tested against weaker acid it would appear more effective when it was not. same volume and pH of acid same 1 g additions to the same total same pH meter used each time only the powder differs, or the comparison is meaningless"],[7,"y7c08-r041","Short answer","The pH after 2 g and after 3 g of powder B are known. Explain how you would predict the pH at 2.5 g.","8.3","Plot the results as a line graph with mass of powder on the horizontal axis and pH on the vertical axis, then read the pH from the line at 2.5 grams, halfway between the two known points. This works because the readings either side were measured under identical conditions, so the value in between can reasonably be read off the curve joining them. Reading between measured points like this is only sensible inside the range that was actually tested. plot pH against mass of powder read the value at 2.5 g from the line valid because the surrounding points were measured only reliable within the range tested"],[7,"y7c08-r042","Short answer","Antacids that work very quickly produce a lot of gas quickly. Explain which antacid you would choose as ideal and why.","8.3","The ideal antacid should raise the pH of the acid to about 7 using a small mass of powder, so that a small dose is effective, and it should act quickly enough to relieve the discomfort. However, the antacids that work very quickly produce a lot of gas quickly, which would be uncomfortable for the person taking it, so the fastest is not automatically the best. The best choice is one that is effective per gram and raises the pH steadily rather than violently. should raise pH to about 7 with a small mass should act fast enough to relieve discomfort very fast ones produce a lot of gas choose effective per gram with a steady rise"],[7,"y7c08-r043","Short answer","Write the correct method for neutralising an acid safely with a burette.","8.3","Put on safety glasses before collecting any chemicals, and use only the acid the teacher has provided rather than a different bottle from the shelf. Measure a known volume of alkali into a conical flask, add a few drops of universal indicator, then fill a burette with the acid and add it slowly, swirling the flask after each addition. Stop as soon as the indicator turns green, which shows the solution is neutral, and record the volume of acid used. wear safety glasses and use the acid provided measured volume of alkali and a few drops of indicator add acid slowly from the burette, swirling each time stop at green and record the volume"],[7,"y7c08-r044","Short answer","A student did not wear goggles because they hurt. Explain why this is unacceptable in this practical and what should be done instead.","8.3","The practical uses hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide, both of which are harmful and can cause serious damage if splashed into the eyes, and splashes are likely when liquids are poured, stirred and added from a burette. Eyes cannot be washed clean the way skin can, so the protection has to be in place before any chemical is handled rather than after a splash. If the goggles are uncomfortable the student should ask for a better-fitting pair, not work without them. acid and alkali can seriously damage eyes splashes are likely when pouring and adding eyes cannot simply be washed clean afterwards ask for a better fitting pair, never work without"],[7,"y7c08-r045","Short answer","Explain how to read the volume in a measuring cylinder correctly.","8.3","The surface of the liquid is slightly curved, and this curved surface is called the meniscus; the volume of the liquid is measured from the bottom of the curve. Your eye should be level with that point so that you do not read the scale from above or below, which would give a value that is too high or too low. The units must always be included with the answer, since a number alone does not say what volume was measured. the curved surface is the meniscus read from the bottom of the curve keep your eye level with that point always include the units"],[7,"y7c08-r049","Short answer","Explain why clues are needed to tell that a chemical reaction has taken place.","8.4","In a chemical reaction new products are formed from the reactants, but you cannot see the atoms rearranging, so you have to judge from what you can observe. There are some clues you can look out for that might mean a reaction has taken place, such as a gas being given off, a colour change, a solid appearing or a temperature change. The clues are evidence rather than proof, which is why more than one is worth looking for. atoms rearranging cannot be seen judgement rests on observable clues gas, colour change, solid forming, temperature change clues are evidence, so look for more than one"],[7,"y7c08-r050","Short answer","Explain why a gas being given off is one of the most useful clues.","8.4","One of the most useful clues to help decide if a chemical reaction has taken place is whether a gas is given off, because a gas appearing from solids or liquids means a substance now exists that was not there before. It is also easy to see as bubbles or fizzing, and the gas can then be tested to find out which one it is. That combination of being obvious and being identifiable is what makes it so useful. a gas appearing means a new substance exists easy to see as bubbles or fizzing the gas can be identified by a test obvious and identifiable"],[7,"y7c08-r051","Short answer","Describe the test for carbon dioxide and explain what is happening in it.","8.4","You can test for carbon dioxide by using limewater: when limewater mixes with the carbon dioxide, the limewater turns cloudy. Limewater is a solution of calcium hydroxide, and the cloudiness appears because a precipitate of calcium carbonate is formed when the gas reacts with it. So the test is itself a chemical reaction, in which a gas added to a liquid produces a solid. bubble the gas through limewater limewater turns cloudy for carbon dioxide limewater is calcium hydroxide solution a precipitate of calcium carbonate causes the cloudiness"],[7,"y7c08-r052","Short answer","Describe the test for oxygen and explain why a glowing splint is used rather than a lit one.","8.4","To test for oxygen you use a glowing splint, and when the glowing splint is placed in the mouth of the test tube it will relight if the gas is oxygen. A splint that is already burning would burn in any tube where there is enough oxygen, including ordinary air, so it would not distinguish the gases. A glowing splint only relights where the oxygen is much more concentrated than in air, which is what makes the result meaningful. use a glowing splint at the mouth of the tube it relights if the gas is oxygen a lit splint would burn in ordinary air too glowing only relights in concentrated oxygen"],[7,"y7c08-r053","Short answer","You have four test tubes containing air, oxygen, hydrogen and carbon dioxide. Describe how you would find out which is which.","8.4","Test each tube in turn. Hold a lighted splint at the mouth: the tube that burns with a squeaky pop is hydrogen. Then use a glowing splint on the remaining tubes: the one that relights the splint is oxygen. Bubble the contents of the remaining two through limewater: the one that turns the limewater cloudy is carbon dioxide, and the tube left over, which does none of these things, is air. lighted splint gives a squeaky pop for hydrogen glowing splint relights in oxygen limewater turns cloudy with carbon dioxide the tube that does nothing is air"],[7,"y7c08-r054","Short answer","Explain why the magnesium ribbon seems to disappear when it reacts with acid.","8.4","When magnesium ribbon reacts with acid, hydrogen is produced and the magnesium ribbon appears to disappear, but the magnesium is used up in the reaction: it combines with the chlorine from the hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride. Magnesium chloride dissolves in the liquid, so the magnesium atoms are still in the beaker but no longer as a visible solid. A reactant being used up is itself one of the clues that a reaction has occurred. magnesium is used up, not destroyed it combines with chlorine to form magnesium chloride the chloride dissolves so nothing solid is seen a reactant being used up is a clue to a reaction"],[7,"y7c08-r055","Short answer","Copper oxide is black and copper sulfate solution is blue. Explain why this colour change is evidence of a reaction.","8.4","Gently heating black copper oxide with sulfuric acid produces a blue solution of copper sulfate, and the change from a black solid to a blue solution means the substance present at the end is not the one that was there at the start. A colour change of this kind cannot be undone by cooling, unlike a physical change, so a new substance must have been formed. Colour change is therefore a clue to a chemical reaction. black solid becomes a blue solution the substance at the end differs from the start cannot be undone by cooling colour change is a clue to a reaction"],[7,"y7c08-r056","Short answer","Explain why you must not boil the blue copper sulfate liquid.","8.4","If you try this reaction in the laboratory you must make sure you do not boil the blue liquid, as harmful fumes will be given off. Boiling makes the liquid evaporate rapidly and carries substances from the hot acidic mixture into the air where they can be breathed in. Gentle heating is enough to make the reaction happen without producing those fumes. boiling gives off harmful fumes rapid evaporation carries substances into the air they could be breathed in gentle heating is enough for the reaction"],[7,"y7c08-r057","Short answer","Adding zinc to hydrochloric acid makes the test tube feel hot. Explain what this tells you.","8.4","When you add zinc to hydrochloric acid, hydrogen gas is given off and the test tube feels hot, so two clues appear at once: a gas is produced and heat is released. A temperature change means energy has been transferred during the change, which happens when bonds are broken and new ones are made, so it is evidence that a chemical reaction rather than mere mixing has taken place. Two independent clues together make the conclusion much stronger than one. gas given off and heat released a temperature change means energy was transferred evidence of a reaction rather than mixing two clues together strengthen the conclusion"],[7,"y7c08-r058","Short answer","Explain what a precipitate is, using silver nitrate and calcium chloride.","8.4","If you mix solutions of silver nitrate and calcium chloride, a chemical reaction takes place, and when the two solutions are mixed a solid is formed; this solid is called a precipitate. In this example the solid is silver chloride, formed because that combination of atoms does not dissolve in water while the starting substances did. A solid appearing when two clear liquids are mixed is strong evidence of a reaction, because nothing solid was there to begin with. a precipitate is a solid formed when solutions are mixed here the precipitate is silver chloride it forms because that substance does not dissolve a solid from two liquids is strong evidence of a reaction"],[7,"y7c08-r059","Short answer","Explain why limewater turning cloudy is really the same kind of change as a precipitate forming.","8.4","As carbon dioxide is bubbled into limewater, a precipitate is formed: limewater is a solution of calcium hydroxide and the carbon dioxide reacts with it to form calcium carbonate, which does not dissolve. The cloudiness is that solid suspended through the liquid, so the test for carbon dioxide is a precipitation reaction. The only difference from mixing two solutions is that here you added a gas to a liquid and a solid was formed. limewater is calcium hydroxide solution carbon dioxide forms calcium carbonate, which does not dissolve the cloudiness is suspended solid a gas added to a liquid gives a solid"],[7,"y7c08-r060","Short answer","Explain why a change in pH counts as evidence that a reaction has happened.","8.4","When you neutralise an alkali there is a change in pH, and it is called a neutralisation reaction. pH is a chemical property of a substance, so a change in pH means the substances present are not the same ones as before: sodium hydroxide and hydrochloric acid have been replaced by sodium chloride and water. If nothing had reacted, the pH would only be changed by dilution rather than moving to 7 and beyond. pH is a chemical property a pH change means different substances are present acid and alkali replaced by salt and water dilution alone would not take it past 7"],[7,"y7c08-r061","Short answer","A piece of chocolate is melted in a test tube in hot water. Explain whether a chemical reaction has taken place and what evidence you have.","8.4","No chemical reaction has taken place: the chocolate has only changed from solid to liquid, which is a change of state, and in a physical change no new substances are formed. The evidence is that no gas was given off, no precipitate formed and there was no permanent colour change, and above all the melted chocolate sets back into chocolate when it cools. Being reversible by simply cooling is the clearest sign that this is a physical change. only a change of state, solid to liquid no gas, no precipitate, no permanent colour change it sets back into chocolate on cooling reversible by cooling means physical"],[7,"y7c08-r062","Short answer","Apart from a gas being given off, give three other things to look for in deciding whether a reaction has happened, with an example of each.","8.4","Look for a colour change that cannot be undone, such as black copper oxide with sulfuric acid giving a blue copper sulfate solution. Look for a solid appearing when solutions are mixed, such as the precipitate of silver chloride from silver nitrate and calcium chloride. Look for a temperature change, such as the test tube feeling hot when zinc is added to hydrochloric acid, and also for a change in pH, as happens in a neutralisation reaction. a permanent colour change, as with copper oxide and sulfuric acid a precipitate forming, as with silver nitrate and calcium chloride a temperature change, as with zinc and acid a change in pH, as in neutralisation"],[7,"y7c08-r063","Short answer","Explain why a spill of concentrated acid on a road is dangerous and what the emergency services must do.","8.4","Concentrated acid attacks skin, eyes and clothing on contact and damages metal and other materials, so anyone driving through it would spread it and could have it thrown onto people and vehicles. The emergency services must first identify which chemical it is, then keep people away and wear full protective clothing and eye protection. They neutralise the spilt acid by adding an alkali such as lime, and they know the task is complete when the pH of the area is tested and found to be about 7. concentrated acid attacks skin, eyes and materials driving through it would spread it identify the chemical, keep people away, wear protection neutralise with an alkali and test until pH is about 7"],[7,"y7c08-r064","Short answer","Explain what effect spilt acid reaching the soil beside a road would have.","8.4","The soil would become very acidic, and in areas where the soil is very acidic plants do not grow well, so the plants beside the road would be damaged or would die. The acid could also drain into a stream or pond, where plants and animals that live in the water cannot live in acid conditions. Treating the soil with lime would neutralise the acid, in the same way that farmers spread lime on acidic soils. the soil becomes very acidic plants do not grow well in acidic soil acid can drain into water and harm organisms there lime would neutralise it, as farmers do"],[7,"y7c08-o043","Short answer","What happens when you mix an acid and an alkali together?","8.2","They react and make a neutral solution. react neutral solution"],[7,"y7c08-o044","Short answer","What is the name of the reaction between an acid and an alkali?","8.2","Neutralisation. neutralisation"],[7,"y7c08-o045","Short answer","Is neutralisation a physical change or a chemical change?","8.2","A chemical change. chemical change"],[7,"y7c08-o046","Short answer","What happens if you add too much acid to an alkali?","8.2","It makes an acidic liquid. too much acid acidic"],[7,"y7c08-o047","Short answer","What happens if you add too little acid to an alkali?","8.2","It stays as an alkaline liquid. too little alkaline"],[7,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the new substance formed when iron and sulfur are heated together?","8.1","iron oxide sulfur dioxide iron sulfide magnesium oxide"],[7,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","In a chemical reaction, the substances that react together are called ____.","8.1","reactants"],[7,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which of these is a chemical property of iron?","8.1","It is grey and heavy It has a melting point of 1538 °C It combines with oxygen to form rust It is a solid at room temperature"],[7,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","When carbon burns, it combines with oxygen to make the gas ____.","8.1","carbon dioxide"],[7,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","What gas is given off when magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid?","8.1","oxygen carbon dioxide hydrogen nitrogen"],[7,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","When an acid and an alkali are mixed, they react to make a ____ solution.","8.2","neutral"],[7,"qwen-01-11","Multiple choice","What is the name of the process where an acid and an alkali cancel each other out?","8.2","combustion neutralisation evaporation condensation"],[7,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","You can use a special piece of equipment called a ____ to neutralise an alkali very accurately.","8.2","burette"],[7,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the pH of the liquid in the flask when 25 cm3 of acid has been added?","8.2","13 7 6 0"],[7,"qwen-05-10","Gap fill","When planning an investigation, you must decide what to change and what to keep the same. This is called a ____ test.","8.3","fair"],[7,"qwen-05-11","Multiple choice","Why is the question 'Which is the best indigestion remedy?' not precise enough for a scientist?","8.3","It does not define what 'best' means It is too short to write down Indigestion remedies are not chemicals Scientists do not like indigestion"],[7,"qwen-05-12","Gap fill","If you are investigating indigestion powders, you cannot use your own stomach acid. You must use a ____ instead.","8.3","model"],[7,"qwen-06-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the gas given off when baking powder reacts with vinegar?","8.4","Hydrogen Carbon dioxide Oxygen Nitrogen"],[7,"qwen-06-02","Gap fill","When limewater mixes with carbon dioxide, the limewater turns ____.","8.4","cloudy"],[7,"qwen-06-03","Multiple choice","Which tool is used to test for the presence of oxygen gas?","8.4","A glowing splint Limewater Universal indicator A lit match"],[7,"qwen-06-07","Multiple choice","In the investigation with powders A, B, and C, what was kept the same for each beaker?","8.3","The type of powder The amount of acid The number of spatulas The pH of the final solution"],[7,"qwen-06-08","Gap fill","When a glowing splint is placed in oxygen, it will ____.","8.4","relight"],[7,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What happens to substances during a physical change?","8.1","New substances are formed No new substances are formed The atoms change into different elements Energy is always released"],[7,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","When iron atoms bond with sulfur atoms, it is a ____ change.","8.1","chemical"],[7,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","In the reaction where oxygen reacts with hydrogen, the compound made is ____.","8.1","water"],[7,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","What are the reactants in the reaction that forms water from oxygen and hydrogen?","8.1","Water and oxygen Hydrogen and oxygen Water and hydrogen Oxygen only"],[7,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","The curved surface of the liquid in a measuring cylinder is called the ____.","8.2","meniscus"],[7,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","From which part of the curve is the volume of liquid measured?","8.2","The top of the curve The bottom of the curve The middle of the cylinder The rim of the cylinder"],[7,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","Where is the zero mark located on a burette scale?","8.2","At the bottom In the middle At the top On the side"],[7,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","When measuring liquid volume, you must read from the bottom of the ____.","8.2","curve"],[7,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","Indigestion medicine acts as an ____ to neutralise stomach acid.","8.3","alkali"],[7,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","In Marcus's investigation, what colour does the acid and universal indicator turn when neutralised?","8.3","Red Blue Green Yellow"],[7,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","Marcus uses a ____ to add the powdered medicine to the acid.","8.3","spatula"],[7,"qwenw-04-03","Multiple choice","Which substance is added to the lake water to neutralise the acid?","8.3","Sodium chloride Sodium hydroxide Hydrochloric acid Calcium carbonate"],[7,"qwenw-05-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the reaction where an acid is cancelled out by an alkali?","8.4","neutralisation precipitation corrosion dissolution"],[7,"qwenw-05-02","Gap fill","A solution with a pH of less than 7 is called an ____.","8.4","acid"],[7,"qwenw-05-03","Multiple choice","What are the substances you start with in a chemical reaction called?","8.4","products reactants precipitates alkalis"],[7,"qwenw-05-04","Gap fill","The solid formed when two liquids react is called a ____.","8.4","precipitate"],[8,"y7c09-f01","Diagram labelling","The drawing shows a simple circuit. Name the component labelled {n}.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-f02","Diagram labelling","The diagram shows what happens in a circuit when the lamp is lit. What is label {n}?","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-f03","Diagram labelling","The diagram shows the inside of a piece of metal. What is label {n}?","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-f04","Diagram labelling","A drawing of a circuit and its circuit diagram are shown. Name the component labelled {n}.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-f05","Diagram labelling","The photograph shows the wires used in houses and schools. What is label {n}?","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-f06","Diagram labelling","The photograph shows part of a power line. What is label {n}?","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-f07","Diagram labelling","The drawing shows some types of plug. Name the part labelled {n}.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-f08","Diagram labelling","The drawing shows the circuit used to test materials. Name the component labelled {n}.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h01","Click the diagram","Click on the component that stores the chemical energy for this circuit.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h02","Click the diagram","Click on the component that gives out light in this circuit.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h04","Click the diagram","Click on the + symbol printed on the upright cell.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h05","Click the diagram","Click on the top end of the upright cell, where its positive terminal is.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h06","Click the diagram","Click on one of the separate cells inside this battery pack.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h07","Click the diagram","Click on one of the coloured wires that connect the cells to the rest of the car.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h08","Click the diagram","Click on the cell, the component that pushes the electrons around this circuit.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h12","Click the diagram","Click on the negative terminal marked at the side of this piece of metal.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h15","Click the diagram","Click on the positive terminal that these electrons are moving towards.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h16","Click the diagram","Click on the negative terminal that these electrons are being pushed away from.","9.1",""],[8,"y7c09-h18","Click the diagram","In the circuit diagram on the right, click on the symbol for the cell.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h19","Click the diagram","In the circuit diagram on the right, click on the symbol for the lamp.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h22","Click the diagram","Click on the largest lamp in this photograph.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h23","Click the diagram","Click on the small round lamp, third from the left.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h24","Click the diagram","Click on the lamp on the far right of the photograph.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h25","Click the diagram","Click on the circuit symbol for an ammeter.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h26","Click the diagram","Click on the circuit symbol for a cell.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h27","Click the diagram","Click on the circuit symbol for a lamp.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h31","Click the diagram","Click on the cell in this circuit diagram.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h32","Click the diagram","Click on the switch in this circuit diagram.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h34","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in this circuit diagram.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h35","Click the diagram","Click on the lamp in this circuit diagram.","9.2",""],[8,"y7c09-h38","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter with a pointer and a printed scale.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h39","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter that shows the current as a digital display.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h40","Click the diagram","On the left-hand ammeter, click on the pointer that shows the reading.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h41","Click the diagram","On the left-hand ammeter, click on the red terminal.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h43","Click the diagram","Click on ammeter A.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h44","Click the diagram","Click on ammeter B.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h45","Click the diagram","Click on ammeter C.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h46","Click the diagram","Click on ammeter D.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h47","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in circuit X.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h48","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in circuit Y.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h49","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in circuit Z.","9.3",""],[8,"y7c09-h50","Click the diagram","Click on the metal flask at the top right of the photograph.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h53","Click the diagram","Click on the bare copper strands at the end of the left-hand wire.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h56","Click the diagram","Click on the tall pylon that is nearest to the camera.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h58","Click the diagram","Click on one of the copper tracks on this circuit board.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h60","Click the diagram","Click on one of the blue wires.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h62","Click the diagram","Click on the metal pylon that holds up the power lines.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h64","Click the diagram","Click on the metal pins of the plug on the right.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h67","Click the diagram","Click on the glass, which is an electrical insulator.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h73","Click the diagram","Click on the cell in this test circuit.","9.4",""],[8,"y7c09-h76","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in the left-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h77","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in the right-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h78","Click the diagram","Click on the lamp in the left-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h79","Click the diagram","Click on the single cell in the left-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h80","Click the diagram","Click on one of the two cells in the right-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h81","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter that reads the smaller current.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h82","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter that reads the larger current.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h83","Click the diagram","Click on the single lamp in the left-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h84","Click the diagram","Click on one of the two lamps in the right-hand circuit.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-h85","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter in the circuit with two lamps.","9.5",""],[8,"y7c09-r001","Short answer","Explain the difference between a cell and a battery.","9.1","A cell stores chemical energy that can be changed to electrical energy in a circuit, and a battery contains two or more cells joined together. So a battery is not a different kind of object but a group of cells, which is why the battery from an electric car is made from hundreds of separate cells connected together. Using the words carefully matters because a battery of several cells supplies more energy than a single cell. a cell stores chemical energy changed to electrical energy a battery is two or more cells joined an electric car battery is hundreds of cells more cells supply more energy"],[8,"y7c09-r002","Short answer","Explain why cells, batteries and power supplies all have two terminals.","9.1","The cell in a circuit has two connections, called terminals, and all cells, batteries, power supplies and many other components have two terminals. Two are needed because electrons can only flow in a complete circuit: they must leave the supply at one terminal and return to it at the other. A supply with only one connection would leave the circuit broken, so nothing would flow at all. a supply has two terminals electrons leave at one and return at the other electrons only flow in a complete circuit one connection would leave the circuit broken"],[8,"y7c09-r003","Short answer","Explain what makes the electrons move when a circuit is switched on.","9.1","When connected into a circuit, the negative terminal of a cell, battery or power supply pushes electrons around in the wires. Electrons have a negative charge, and like charges repel, so the negative terminal pushes them away, while the positive terminal attracts them because opposite charges attract. The cell therefore does not create the electrons; it pushes the ones already in the metal. the negative terminal pushes electrons round like charges repel, so the negative terminal pushes them the positive terminal attracts them the cell pushes existing electrons rather than making them"],[8,"y7c09-r004","Short answer","Explain what an electron is and what current is.","9.1","All materials contain atoms, atoms contain smaller particles, and an electron is one type of smaller particle in an atom. The flow of electrons in the circuit is called current, so current is not a substance in its own right but a movement of particles that were already in the wire. That is why current can start everywhere in the circuit at once, and why a wire is no lighter after current has flowed through it. electrons are small particles inside atoms current is the flow of electrons current is a movement, not a substance the electrons were already in the wire"],[8,"y7c09-r005","Short answer","Explain the difference between how electrons move in a metal before and after it is placed in a circuit.","9.1","Some of the electrons in a metal are free to move, which means they can move through the metal, and in a metal these electrons move randomly. When the metal is placed into a circuit, the electrons move in the same direction, because the terminals of the supply push and pull them all one way. The electrons were always moving; what the circuit adds is a direction shared by all of them. free electrons move randomly in a metal in a circuit they all move in the same direction the terminals push and pull them one way the circuit adds direction, not movement"],[8,"y7c09-r006","Short answer","Explain, using charges, which way electrons travel around a circuit.","9.1","Electrons are negatively charged so they move toward the positive terminal in a circuit. Opposite charges attract and like charges repel, so electrons will be attracted towards the positive terminal of the power supply and be repelled from the negative terminal. Both the push at one end and the pull at the other act in the same direction, which is why the flow is steady rather than random. electrons are negatively charged opposite charges attract, like charges repel attracted to the positive, repelled from the negative push and pull act the same way"],[8,"y7c09-r007","Short answer","Explain what attract and repel mean and give the three combinations of charge.","9.1","Opposite charges attract and like charges repel: to attract means to pull together and repel means to push apart. Positive and negative attract, positive and positive repel, and negative and negative repel. This single rule is what decides which way electrons move in every circuit, since the electrons are negative and the terminals are positive and negative. attract means pull together, repel means push apart positive and negative attract positive and positive repel negative and negative repel"],[8,"y7c09-r008","Short answer","Explain why a lamp goes out the instant a wire is disconnected anywhere in the circuit.","9.1","If there is a break in the circuit, all the electrons stop flowing, because electrons can only flow in a complete circuit. The break does not only stop the electrons at that point: with nowhere for them to go, the flow stops everywhere at once, including in the lamp on the far side. That is why it does not matter where in the loop the switch or the break is. a break stops all the electrons flowing electrons only flow in a complete circuit the flow stops everywhere at once the position of the break does not matter"],[8,"y7c09-r009","Short answer","Explain what a switch does in terms of electrons.","9.1","When you close the switch, the lamp lights, because closing the switch completes the circuit so that there is an unbroken path of conducting material all the way round. Electrons can only flow in a complete circuit, so while the switch is open there is a break and all the electrons stop flowing. Closing it is therefore not turning the electricity on but removing the gap that was stopping the flow. closing the switch completes the circuit an unbroken conducting path is needed an open switch is a break, so nothing flows the switch removes a gap rather than creating electricity"],[8,"y7c09-r010","Short answer","The diagram of electrons in a metal is not to scale. Explain why that matters when using it.","9.1","Electrons are very small and the diagram is not to scale, so the picture shows only a handful of large dots where a real piece of metal contains an enormous number of extremely small electrons. The diagram is useful for showing the difference between random movement and movement in one direction, which is what it was drawn for. It should not be used to judge how many electrons there are or how far apart they are. electrons are far smaller and far more numerous the diagram shows only a few large dots useful for showing random versus directed movement not useful for judging number or spacing"],[8,"y7c09-r011","Short answer","A student says electricity is made in the cell and used up in the lamp. Explain what is wrong with this.","9.1","The flow of current in a circuit is the movement of electrons around the circuit, and those electrons are already in the metal, since some of the electrons in a metal are free to move. The cell pushes them round rather than making them, and they continue round the loop back to the cell rather than being used up in the lamp. What the lamp takes is energy from the electrons, not the electrons themselves. the electrons are already in the metal the cell pushes them, it does not make them they return to the cell rather than being used up the lamp takes energy, not electrons"],[8,"y7c09-r012","Short answer","Explain why circuit diagrams are used instead of drawings of the real apparatus.","9.2","Circuits can be shown in a drawing or a circuit diagram, but circuit diagrams are easier to draw because the components have standard symbols and wires are drawn with straight lines, which is easy to interpret. Components in circuits can look very different, so a realistic drawing of a lamp tells you nothing that another person can rely on. The symbol carries the information that matters, which is what the component does and how it is connected. symbols are quicker and easier to draw straight lines for wires are easy to interpret real components look very different from one another the symbol carries what the component does"],[8,"y7c09-r013","Short answer","Explain why standard symbols are used in all countries.","9.2","Standard circuit symbols are used in circuit diagrams so there is no confusion, and the same symbols are used in all countries. Since components in circuits can look very different, for example all the different types of lamp that share the same circuit symbol, a symbol that everyone agrees on is the only way a diagram drawn by one person can be built by another. It also means an engineer can read a circuit drawn anywhere in the world without translation. standard symbols prevent confusion the same symbols are used worldwide components that look different share one symbol a diagram can be read and built by anyone"],[8,"y7c09-r014","Short answer","Explain how you can tell which way round a cell is connected in a circuit diagram.","9.2","In the circuit symbol for a cell, the positive is the longer of the two lines and the negative is the shorter line. This matters because the negative terminal pushes electrons around the wires and the positive terminal attracts them, so the symbol tells you which way the electrons will flow. Drawing the two lines the same length would leave the direction of flow undefined. the longer line is positive the shorter line is negative this fixes the direction electrons flow equal lines would leave the direction undefined"],[8,"y7c09-r015","Short answer","Explain why gaps and wires drawn through components are mistakes in a circuit diagram.","9.2","Take care when drawing circuit diagrams that there are no gaps in the lines, especially at the corners and where wires meet components. A gap in a diagram means a break in the circuit, and electrons can only flow in a complete circuit, so the diagram would be showing a circuit in which nothing works. A wire drawn through a component says the current bypasses it, which is a different circuit from the one intended. a gap means a break in the circuit electrons only flow in a complete circuit the diagram would show a circuit that does not work a wire through a component means the current bypasses it"],[8,"y7c09-r016","Short answer","Arun's diagram of a cell, a switch and a lamp has three mistakes. Explain what to look for.","9.2","Look first for gaps in the lines, especially at the corners and where wires meet components, since a gap means the circuit is broken. Look next for a wire drawn straight through a component, which would mean the current bypasses it. Then check that each symbol is the correct one and drawn properly, in particular that the cell has one long line for positive and one short line for negative. gaps at corners or where wires meet components wires drawn through components wrong or badly drawn symbols cell drawn with unequal lines, long for positive"],[8,"y7c09-r017","Short answer","Explain how a doorbell circuit works.","9.2","The circuit contains a cell, a switch and a buzzer, and it could be used in a doorbell: if the switch is pushed outside a door, it makes the buzzer sound and attracts attention inside. Pressing the switch completes the circuit, so electrons flow round and the buzzer changes electrical energy into sound. Releasing the switch breaks the circuit again, and since electrons can only flow in a complete circuit the buzzer immediately stops. cell, switch and buzzer in one loop pressing the switch completes the circuit the buzzer changes electrical energy to sound releasing it breaks the circuit so the sound stops"],[8,"y7c09-r018","Short answer","Explain why you might put an ammeter into a lighting circuit.","9.2","The ammeter measures the electric current, so the circuit could be used to light a room and measure the current flowing through the circuit. By measuring the current you could tell if the cell needs to be replaced, since a smaller current means the cell is low on stored energy. The lamp would also become less bright, but you might not notice that gradual change until you find yourself sitting in the dark. the ammeter measures the current a smaller current means the cell is low on energy gives warning before the cell fails the dimming lamp is too gradual to notice"],[8,"y7c09-r019","Short answer","Explain how you would list the differences between two circuit diagrams.","9.2","Compare them component by component: check which components each circuit contains and how many of each, since an extra cell or lamp changes the current. Check the order and arrangement of the components and whether there are any branches, because that determines whether they are connected in series. Check the direction the cell is connected, using the rule that the longer line is positive. compare which components and how many compare the order and whether there are branches check for series connection check which way the cell is connected"],[8,"y7c09-r020","Short answer","Explain how the speed of the electrons is related to the size of the current.","9.3","When electrons move more quickly, the current increases, and when electrons move more slowly, the current decreases. Current is the flow of electrons, so it measures how much flow there is past a point, and faster-moving electrons mean more of them pass each second. This is why anything that slows the electrons down, such as adding a lamp, reduces the current. faster electrons mean a larger current slower electrons mean a smaller current current measures the flow past a point slowing the electrons reduces the current"],[8,"y7c09-r021","Short answer","State the unit of current and explain why a reading must always include its unit.","9.3","Current is measured in units called amps, so a reading of 2 means nothing until it is written as 2 A. A number on its own does not say what quantity was measured or on what scale, so it could be read as amps, volts or anything else, and the answer would be ambiguous. Including the unit is what turns a number into a measurement. current is measured in amps a bare number does not say what was measured it could be mistaken for another quantity the unit turns a number into a measurement"],[8,"y7c09-r022","Short answer","Explain why the red terminal of an ammeter must be connected towards the positive terminal of the supply.","9.3","In a circuit, the red terminal of the ammeter must be connected to the positive terminal of the power supply, either directly or through other components. The ammeter is built to measure current flowing through it in one particular direction, so connecting it the other way round makes the pointer try to move the wrong way and no useful reading is obtained. Getting the terminals right is therefore part of setting the instrument up, not a matter of tidiness. the red terminal goes towards the positive terminal may be connected directly or through other components the ammeter measures current in one direction reversed, the pointer moves the wrong way"],[8,"y7c09-r023","Short answer","Explain why the circuit symbol for an ammeter does not show red and black terminals.","9.3","Both ends of the ammeter are the same in a circuit diagram, so you do not need to show the red and black terminals on the circuit symbol. A circuit diagram records which components are connected and in what order, not how to wire up the instrument in the laboratory. The terminals still matter when you build the circuit, which is why the rule about the red terminal is given in the text rather than in the symbol. both ends look the same on the symbol the diagram records connections, not wiring detail the terminals still matter when building it the rule is given in words instead"],[8,"y7c09-r024","Short answer","Explain what it means to connect an ammeter in series and why it must be connected that way.","9.3","Ammeters are always connected in series with other components in a circuit, which means the components are all connected one after another with no branches, so every electron that passes through the lamp must also pass through the ammeter. That is the only arrangement in which the ammeter measures the same current that flows through the component you are interested in. If it were connected across a component instead, the current through it would not be the current you wanted to measure. in series means one after another with no branches every electron passing the lamp passes the ammeter it then measures the current you want connected across a component it would measure something else"],[8,"y7c09-r025","Short answer","Marcus wants to measure the current in a circuit with a cell, a lamp and a buzzer. Explain where the ammeter should go.","9.3","It can go at any point in the loop, as long as it is in series with the other components, so that the same current passes through it as through the lamp and buzzer. Ammeters are always connected in series, and current is the same all the way around a series circuit, so every position in the loop gives the same reading. The red terminal must face towards the positive terminal of the cell. anywhere in the loop, in series the same current passes through everything in series all positions give the same reading red terminal towards the positive terminal"],[8,"y7c09-r026","Short answer","Explain why you can put an ammeter at any position in a series circuit.","9.3","Current is the same all the way around a series circuit, because electrons flow at the same speed in the wires of a series circuit and when electrons start to flow in one part of the circuit they all start to flow. That means you can put an ammeter at any position in a series circuit and it will give the same result. If the current were smaller after the lamp, electrons would have to be piling up inside it, which does not happen. current is the same all round a series circuit electrons flow at the same speed everywhere in it any position gives the same reading otherwise electrons would pile up in the lamp"],[8,"y7c09-r027","Short answer","Explain the train model of a series circuit and what it shows.","9.3","You can think of the electrons in a series circuit like a train: all parts of the train move at the same speed on the track, and the back of the train cannot go faster than the front. As soon as one part of the train moves, all of the train will move, which models the fact that when electrons start to flow in one part of the circuit, they all start to flow. It shows why the current is the same everywhere and why it starts everywhere at once. all parts of the train move at the same speed the back cannot outrun the front when one part moves, all of it moves models equal current everywhere, starting at once"],[8,"y7c09-r028","Short answer","In the ammeter-scale activity, explain why your partner can only place the pointer correctly if the scale is drawn properly.","9.3","The pointer's position has meaning only because the divisions on the scale are evenly spaced and there is the correct number of divisions between whole numbers. If the divisions are uneven or the wrong number has been drawn, the same pointer position stands for a different current, so your partner cannot place it to match the value you wrote. Reading any instrument depends on the scale being drawn to a consistent rule. the pointer's meaning comes from the scale divisions must be evenly spaced the correct number between whole numbers an uneven scale makes the same position mean something else"],[8,"y7c09-r029","Short answer","Explain why reading a current such as 1.7 A is harder than reading 2 A.","9.3","A whole number of amps falls on a labelled mark, so you only have to read the label, but 1.7 A falls between labelled marks and you must count the small divisions to see how far past 1 the pointer has gone. That means you need to know how many divisions there are between whole numbers and what each one is worth. An error of one division changes the answer by a tenth of an amp. whole numbers fall on labelled marks decimals fall between marks you must know what each division is worth one division out changes the reading"],[8,"y7c09-r030","Short answer","Explain what a conductor is, in terms of electrons.","9.4","Conductors are materials that conduct electricity, which means conductors allow current to flow, and since electrons move when current flows, a conductor is a material in which electrons are free to move. Metals conduct electricity because electrons in metals are free to move, so the property belongs to the structure of the material rather than to the circuit. All metals are conductors, which is why wires that carry electric current are made from metal. conductors allow current to flow current is the movement of electrons in a conductor electrons are free to move all metals are conductors"],[8,"y7c09-r031","Short answer","Explain what an insulator is, in terms of electrons.","9.4","Electrical insulators are materials that do not allow current to flow through, and in an insulator the electrons are not free to move. Because the electrons are not free to move, current cannot flow, since current is the flow of electrons. Most non-metals, such as plastic, wood, air and cotton, are insulators, and they inhibit electron flow rather than simply blocking something that was already moving. insulators do not allow current through their electrons are not free to move no free electrons means no current most non-metals are insulators"],[8,"y7c09-r032","Short answer","Gold is one of the best conductors and glass one of the best insulators. Explain the difference in terms of electrons.","9.4","In gold, as in all metals, some of the electrons are free to move through the material, so when a cell pushes them they travel through the gold and a current flows. In glass, the electrons are not free to move, so no matter how hard the terminals push and pull, the electrons stay where they are and no current can flow. The difference lies in whether the material's own electrons can move, not in how hard the supply pushes. gold has electrons free to move they travel through it when pushed, giving a current in glass the electrons are not free to move the difference is in the material, not the supply"],[8,"y7c09-r033","Short answer","Explain why most wires in houses are copper with a plastic coating.","9.4","Copper is a good conductor of electricity because its electrons are free to move, so current flows through it easily and the wire carries electricity where it is wanted. Plastic is an insulator in which the electrons are not free to move, so the coating stops current leaving the wire and protects people from electric shocks. The two materials are chosen for opposite reasons, and the wire needs both. copper conducts because its electrons are free to move plastic is an insulator, so current cannot leave the coating protects people from shocks the two materials are chosen for opposite reasons"],[8,"y7c09-r034","Short answer","Explain why the plastic insulation on wires is made in different colours.","9.4","Plastic insulation on wires is useful because the plastic can be coloured differently to identify each wire, and the colour does not affect the insulation itself. When several wires run together inside one cable, an electrician must be able to tell which is which without testing each one, since connecting the wrong wire is dangerous. So the colour is a safety feature added to a material chosen for a different safety reason. colour identifies each wire colour does not affect the insulation wires must be told apart inside a cable wrong connections are dangerous"],[8,"y7c09-r035","Short answer","Explain why long-distance wires are made from aluminium or steel rather than copper.","9.4","Wires that cover long distances are usually made from aluminium or steel because these metals are cheaper than copper, and some wires are hundreds of kilometres long, so the cost of the metal is enormous. All metals are conductors, since their electrons are free to move, so aluminium and steel still do the job even though copper conducts better. The choice is a trade-off between how well a metal conducts and how much it costs. aluminium and steel are cheaper than copper wires can be hundreds of kilometres long all metals conduct, so they still work a trade-off between conducting and cost"],[8,"y7c09-r036","Short answer","Silver conducts better than copper. Suggest why wires for home use are not made from silver.","9.4","Silver is far more expensive than copper, and a house needs a large total length of wire, so the cost would be very high for a small improvement in conduction. Copper is a good conductor of electricity already, so wires made from it carry the current perfectly well for the distances involved in a house. A material is chosen by weighing how well it works against what it costs, not by taking the best conductor. silver is much more expensive a house needs a large length of wire copper already conducts well enough choose by weighing performance against cost"],[8,"y7c09-r037","Short answer","Explain why the body of a plug is plastic and the pins are metal.","9.4","The pins must carry current from the supply into the appliance, and metals are conductors because their electrons are free to move, so the pins are made of metal. The body is the part people hold, so it is made from plastic, an insulator in which the electrons are not free to move, meaning current cannot pass into the person. The same object needs both a conductor and an insulator, each placed where its property is wanted. pins must carry current, so metal metals conduct because electrons are free to move the body is held, so plastic insulates each material placed where its property is needed"],[8,"y7c09-r038","Short answer","Explain why insulators are fitted between power lines and the metal pylon.","9.4","The insulators on power lines stop the current from flowing from the wires into the metal pylon, because in an insulator the electrons are not free to move so current cannot pass through. If the current flowed to the pylon, electrical energy would be dissipated into the ground, so energy would be wasted instead of reaching the places that need it. People and animals touching the pylon would also get electric shocks. insulators stop current passing into the pylon electrons in an insulator are not free to move otherwise energy would be dissipated into the ground people and animals touching the pylon would be shocked"],[8,"y7c09-r039","Short answer","Explain how a printed circuit board carries current without wires.","9.4","Some circuits, such as those in phones and laptops, do not use wires: the printed circuit board has copper tracks instead of wires. Copper is a metal, so its electrons are free to move and the tracks conduct current exactly as a wire does, just in a flat fixed path. The tracks appear green because the copper is coated with another material, which insulates the tracks from one another so that current cannot cross between them. copper tracks replace wires copper conducts because its electrons are free to move a flat fixed path instead of a wire the coating insulates tracks from each other"],[8,"y7c09-r040","Short answer","Explain how a simple circuit with a cell and a lamp can test whether an object is a conductor.","9.4","Connect the object into the circuit so that it forms part of the loop. If the object is a conductor, its electrons are free to move, so current can flow all the way round the complete circuit and the lamp lights. If it is an insulator, its electrons are not free to move, so there is effectively a break in the circuit, no current flows and the lamp stays off. the object forms part of the loop a conductor lets current flow and the lamp lights an insulator acts like a break in the circuit no current means the lamp stays off"],[8,"y7c09-r041","Short answer","Arun puts an object into his testing circuit and the lamp does not light. Suggest two possible reasons.","9.4","The first possibility is that the object is an insulator, so its electrons are not free to move and current cannot flow round the circuit. The second is that the object is a conductor but something else has gone wrong, for example a loose connection, a gap somewhere else in the circuit, a broken lamp or a cell with too little stored energy. Since electrons can only flow in a complete circuit, any break anywhere gives the same result, so one test on its own does not prove the object is an insulator. the object may be an insulator or a fault elsewhere: loose connection, break, broken lamp, flat cell any break anywhere gives the same result one test does not prove it is an insulator"],[8,"y7c09-r044","Short answer","Explain why the current is the same all the way around a series circuit.","9.5","Current is the same all the way around a series circuit, because electrons flow at the same speed in the wires of a series circuit, and when electrons start to flow in one part of the circuit, they all start to flow. The train model shows why: all parts of the train move at the same speed and the back cannot go faster than the front. If the current were different in different places, electrons would have to be building up somewhere in the loop. current is the same all round a series circuit electrons flow at the same speed everywhere they all start to flow together otherwise electrons would build up somewhere"],[8,"y7c09-r045","Short answer","Explain why rearranging the components of a series circuit does not change the current.","9.5","You can put the same components of a series circuit in a different order and the current will be the same, because in series the electrons must pass through every component one after another whichever order they are in. Since current is the same all the way around a series circuit, the reading does not depend on where a component sits in the loop. What matters is which components are present, not their order. in series every electron passes through every component order does not change what they pass through current is the same all round the loop what matters is which components, not their order"],[8,"y7c09-r046","Short answer","Explain why adding more cells to a series circuit increases the current.","9.5","Cells and batteries are stores of chemical energy, and in a complete circuit the chemical energy gets changed to electrical energy, so if you add more cells to a circuit you have more chemical energy to change into electrical energy. The circuit with two cells has double the electrical energy of the circuit with one cell, so the electrons are pushed harder and move more quickly, and when electrons move more quickly the current increases. The ammeter in the circuit with two cells shows that the current is doubled. cells store chemical energy changed to electrical energy two cells give double the electrical energy electrons are pushed harder and move faster faster electrons mean a larger current"],[8,"y7c09-r047","Short answer","Explain why you must be careful when adding more cells to a circuit.","9.5","Adding more cells increases the current when the other components are kept the same, and a larger current means more energy is transferred every second in the components. The lamps can be damaged and the wires can get hot, because the components were designed to work with a particular current. So the danger is not the extra cell itself but the larger current it causes. more cells increase the current more energy is transferred each second lamps can be damaged and wires get hot components are designed for a particular current"],[8,"y7c09-r048","Short answer","Explain what happens to the electrons and the energy when current passes through a lamp.","9.5","Lamps change electrical energy to light energy and thermal energy. Electrons carry electrical energy around a circuit, and when the electrons pass through a lamp, the electrons transfer some of their electrical energy to the lamp, which changes that energy into light and thermal energy. The electrons themselves continue round the circuit; it is their energy, not the electrons, that is given up. electrons carry electrical energy round the circuit they transfer some of it to the lamp the lamp changes it to light and thermal energy the electrons continue round; only energy is given up"],[8,"y7c09-r049","Short answer","Explain why putting a lamp in a circuit slows the electrons everywhere, not only in the lamp.","9.5","Putting a lamp in a circuit will make the electrons move more slowly, but they move more slowly the whole way around the circuit, because the current is the same at all positions in a series circuit. The train model explains it: as soon as one part of the train slows, all of it slows, since the back cannot go faster than the front. That is why the ammeter reads the same lower value wherever it is placed. the lamp slows the electrons they slow the whole way round the circuit current is the same at all positions in series like a train, all parts slow together"],[8,"y7c09-r050","Short answer","Explain why two lamps in a series circuit are dimmer than one.","9.5","The circuit with two lamps transfers twice as much energy as the circuit with one lamp, so the electrons are slowed more. This makes the electrons move at half the speed, so the reading on the ammeter is halved, and adding more lamps decreases the current when the other components are kept the same. With a smaller current, each lamp receives less energy each second, so the lamps will be dimmer and do not shine as brightly. two lamps transfer twice as much energy the electrons move at half the speed the ammeter reading is halved less energy per second per lamp, so both are dimmer"],[8,"y7c09-r051","Short answer","Explain what happens to the current when a lamp is removed from a series circuit.","9.5","Removing lamps increases the current when the other components are kept the same, because there is now less to slow the electrons down. The remaining electrons move more quickly, and when electrons move more quickly the current increases, so the ammeter reading rises. The lamp that is left will also be brighter, since it now receives more energy each second. removing a lamp increases the current less to slow the electrons down faster electrons mean a larger reading the remaining lamp is brighter"],[8,"y7c09-r052","Short answer","Describe two ways to increase the current in a series circuit and two ways to decrease it.","9.5","To increase the current you can add another identical cell, since more cells give more electrical energy and push the electrons faster, or remove a lamp, since there is then less to slow the electrons down. To decrease the current you can remove a cell, which reduces the energy available to push the electrons, or add another identical lamp, which slows the electrons the whole way round. In every case the other components must be kept the same, or the comparison means nothing. add a cell or remove a lamp to increase current remove a cell or add a lamp to decrease it cells push harder, lamps slow the electrons other components must be kept the same"],[8,"y7c09-r053","Short answer","An ammeter reads 2 A with one lamp. Predict the reading with two identical lamps and with three, and explain.","9.5","With two identical lamps the reading is 1 A, because two lamps make the electrons move at half the speed, so the reading on the ammeter is halved. With three identical lamps the reading is about 0.67 A, since three lamps slow the electrons to a third of their original speed. The pattern is that the current is divided by the number of identical lamps, because each extra lamp takes energy from the same electrons moving round the one loop. two lamps halve the current to 1 A three lamps give about one third, 0.67 A current divided by the number of identical lamps each lamp takes energy from the same moving electrons"],[8,"y7c09-r054","Short answer","Explain which component, added in series, would decrease the current in a circuit.","9.5","Adding another identical lamp decreases the current, because adding more lamps decreases the current when the other components are kept the same: the extra lamp slows the electrons the whole way round the circuit. Adding another cell would increase the current instead, and adding another ammeter or switch makes almost no difference, since their job is to measure and to break the circuit rather than to transfer energy. So the answer follows from what each component does to the electrons. another identical lamp decreases the current it slows the electrons all the way round another cell would increase it an ammeter or switch does not transfer energy"],[8,"y7c09-r055","Short answer","Marcus wants a larger current and has one more of each component. Describe two ways and explain why each works.","9.5","He can add the extra cell in series, because more cells mean more chemical energy changed into electrical energy, so the electrons are pushed harder and move faster, and faster electrons mean a larger current. Alternatively he can remove one of the lamps, because removing lamps increases the current when the other components are kept the same, since there is less to slow the electrons. Adding the extra ammeter or switch would not help, as neither changes how fast the electrons move. add the extra cell for more electrical energy faster electrons mean a larger current or remove a lamp, leaving less to slow them an extra ammeter or switch would not help"],[8,"y7c09-o001","Short answer","What does a cell store?","9.1","Chemical energy. chemical energy"],[8,"y7c09-o036","Short answer","Name the two ways a circuit can be shown on paper.","9.2","As a drawing or as a circuit diagram. drawing circuit diagram"],[8,"y7c09-o037","Short answer","Give one advantage of a circuit diagram over a picture of a circuit.","9.2","Circuit diagrams are easier to draw. easier draw"],[8,"y7c09-o038","Short answer","Why is it helpful that components have standard symbols?","9.2","Because everyone uses the same symbols, so there is no confusion. standard symbols confusion"],[8,"y7c09-o039","Short answer","How are wires drawn in a circuit diagram?","9.2","With straight lines. straight lines"],[8,"y7c09-o069","Short answer","What happens to the current when electrons move more quickly?","9.3","The current increases. increases"],[8,"y7c09-o070","Short answer","What happens to the current when electrons move more slowly?","9.3","The current decreases. decreases"],[8,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the flow of electrons in a circuit called?","9.1","voltage current resistance charge"],[8,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","A battery contains two or more ____ joined together.","9.1","cells"],[8,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which particles move around a circuit to make electric current?","9.1","protons neutrons electrons atoms"],[8,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","The two connections on a cell are called ____.","9.1","terminals"],[8,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What type of charge do electrons have?","9.1","positive negative neutral zero"],[8,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Why are standard circuit symbols used in diagrams?","9.2","To make the drawing look artistic So there is no confusion about components Because they are easier to colour in To hide the names of the parts"],[8,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Circuit diagrams are easier to draw because wires are drawn with straight ____.","9.2","lines"],[8,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","The same circuit symbols are used in all ____.","9.2","countries"],[8,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","A circuit diagram is a drawing that shows the ____ of a circuit.","9.2","components"],[8,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","What is one advantage of using a circuit diagram over a picture?","9.2","It shows the colour of the wires The components have standard symbols It is more realistic to look at It shows the size of the battery"],[8,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the function of an ammeter in a circuit?","9.2","It provides energy to make current flow It measures the electric current It makes a buzzing sound It gives out light"],[8,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","Ammeters are always connected in ____ with other components.","9.3","series"],[8,"qwen-03-06","Gap fill","The symbol for amps is ____.","9.3","A"],[8,"qwen-03-10","Gap fill","If components are connected in series, they are all connected end-to-end and there are no ____ in the circuit.","9.3","branches"],[8,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","In a metal conductor, the particles that move to create current are called ____.","9.4","electrons"],[8,"qwen-04-03","Multiple choice","Which of the following materials is an electrical insulator?","9.4","copper aluminium plastic steel"],[8,"qwen-04-05","Multiple choice","Why is plastic used to coat the wires in a plug?","9.4","to make the wire stronger to stop current flowing into the metal pylon to keep people safe from electric shocks to increase the speed of electrons"],[8,"qwen-04-09","Multiple choice","What happens to electric current as it goes around a series circuit?","9.3","It increases It decreases It stays the same It stops"],[8,"qwen-05-03","Gap fill","In a series circuit, the current is the same at every point because electrons flow at the same ____.","9.5","speed"],[8,"qwen-05-04","Multiple choice","What happens to the current if you add more cells to a circuit?","9.5","The current decreases The current stays the same The current doubles if you double the cells The current stops flowing"],[8,"qwen-05-05","Gap fill","Cells and batteries are stores of ____ energy.","9.5","chemical"],[8,"qwen-05-06","Multiple choice","In a complete circuit, what happens to the chemical energy in cells?","9.5","It is lost as heat It changes into electrical energy It turns into light energy directly It remains as chemical energy"],[8,"qwen-05-08","Multiple choice","Why does a lamp get brighter when you add more cells to the circuit?","9.5","The lamp uses less energy There is more electrical energy in the circuit The wires become thinner The current decreases"],[8,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What happens to electrons in a wire when current flows?","9.1","They vibrate more slowly They flow around the circuit They do not move in the circuit They move randomly in the circuit"],[8,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","When electrons flow faster in a circuit, the current is ____.","9.3","larger"],[8,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","How should an ammeter be connected to measure current in a circuit?","9.3","In series with the cell and the lamp In parallel with the lamp By replacing the lamp Across the two terminals of the cell"],[8,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","Metal is a good ____ of electricity.","9.4","conductor"],[8,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Which material could NOT be used to make electric wires?","9.4","Steel Copper Cotton Aluminium"],[8,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","Gold is one of the best electrical ____.","9.4","conductors"],[8,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","Which component will increase the current when added correctly into a series circuit?","9.5","A cell A lamp A buzzer A switch"],[9,"y8c01-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.1",""],[9,"y8c01-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the entrance to nose in the diagram.","1.1",""],[9,"y8c01-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the entrance to mouth in the diagram.","1.1",""],[9,"y8c01-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the voicebox (larynx) in the diagram.","1.1",""],[9,"y8c01-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the air moving in in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the air moving out in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the air inside sac in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the rubber tubing in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the glass tubing in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the rubber bung in the diagram.","1.2",""],[9,"y8c01-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.3",""],[9,"y8c01-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the small hole in the diagram.","1.3",""],[9,"y8c01-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the rubber bung in the diagram.","1.3",""],[9,"y8c01-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the syringe with end cut off in the diagram.","1.3",""],[9,"y8c01-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the glucose in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the oxygen in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the thermometer in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the cotton wool in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the dead peas in the diagram.","1.4",""],[9,"y8c01-f07a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the cell membrane in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the cytoplasm in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-f08a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the alveolus in lungs in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-f09a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the bacterium in the diagram.","1.5",""],[9,"y8c01-m001","Multiple choice","Where in the body does respiration take place?","1.1","Inside every living cell Only inside the lungs Only inside the blood Only inside the heart"],[9,"y8c01-m002","Multiple choice","Which gas does aerobic respiration use?","1.1","Oxygen Carbon dioxide Nitrogen Hydrogen"],[9,"y8c01-m011","Multiple choice","What is another name for the air sacs in the lungs?","1.2","Alveoli Capillaries Bronchioles Villi"],[9,"y8c01-m021","Multiple choice","What happens to the pressure of a gas when the volume of its container decreases?","1.3","The pressure increases The pressure decreases The pressure stays the same The gas turns into a liquid"],[9,"y8c01-m028","Multiple choice","What do our digestive systems break carbohydrates down into?","1.4","A kind of sugar called glucose A gas called oxygen A pigment called haemoglobin A liquid called plasma"],[9,"y8c01-m037","Multiple choice","What is the liquid part of the blood called?","1.5","Blood plasma Haemoglobin Oxyhaemoglobin Cytoplasm"],[9,"y8c01-m052","Multiple choice","How do you calculate the mean of three values?","1.3","Add them up and divide by 3 Multiply them together and divide by 3 Take the largest and subtract the smallest Add them up and divide by 2"],[9,"y8c01-m054","Multiple choice","What is the correct way to measure the temperature of the mixture?","1.4","Hold the thermometer in the liquid and stir gently Take the thermometer out of the liquid before reading it Rest the thermometer on the bottom of the cup Hold the thermometer just above the liquid"],[9,"y8c01-m055","Multiple choice","Where should your eyes be when you read a thermometer?","1.4","Level with the meniscus Well above the meniscus Well below the meniscus It does not matter where they are"],[9,"y8c01-m057","Multiple choice","What happens to the amount of oxygen in the air at high altitudes?","1.5","There is less oxygen in the air There is more oxygen in the air The oxygen is exactly the same as at sea level There is no oxygen at all"],[9,"y8c01-m060","Gap fill","Respiration is a series of ____ reactions that happens inside every living cell.","1.1","chemical"],[9,"y8c01-m064","Gap fill","Another name for the air sacs in the lungs is ____.","1.2","alveoli"],[9,"y8c01-m069","Gap fill","Breathing works by changing pressure, which is possible because air is a ____.","1.3","gas"],[9,"y8c01-m073","Gap fill","Glucose is an energy ____.","1.4","store"],[9,"y8c01-m077","Gap fill","The ____ pumps constantly, to keep the blood moving.","1.5","heart"],[9,"y8c01-r001","Short answer","Explain why the rings of cartilage around the trachea are important.","1.1","They keep the trachea open and stop it collapsing, so air can keep moving in and out of the body. open collapsing air"],[9,"y8c01-r002","Short answer","Explain why each bronchus divides into many smaller bronchioles.","1.1","The branching carries air deeper into the lungs and spreads it out so it reaches all of the air sacs. branch deeper air sacs"],[9,"y8c01-r003","Short answer","Explain why the air sacs, rather than the trachea, are where gas exchange happens.","1.1","The air sacs are where the oxygen goes into the blood and the carbon dioxide comes out, because they are tiny, thin-walled and surrounded by blood capillaries. oxygen blood thin"],[9,"y8c01-r004","Short answer","Explain why the wall of an air sac is made of only one layer of very thin cells.","1.2","A thin wall means the gases have only a very short distance to diffuse between the air and the blood, so gas exchange is fast. thin short distance diffuse"],[9,"y8c01-r005","Short answer","Explain why oxygen diffuses from the air in the air sac into the blood.","1.2","The oxygen molecules move from where there are a lot of them, in the air, to where there are fewer of them, in the blood. a lot fewer diffus"],[9,"y8c01-r006","Short answer","Explain why carbon dioxide diffuses in the opposite direction to oxygen.","1.2","There is a lot of carbon dioxide in the blood in the capillary and only a small quantity in the air in the air sac, so it diffuses out into the air sac. a lot small diffus"],[9,"y8c01-r007","Short answer","Explain why the blood arriving at the lungs contains only a small amount of oxygen and a lot of carbon dioxide.","1.2","It has just come from the organs of the body, where respiring cells used up the oxygen and made carbon dioxide. respir used up made"],[9,"y8c01-r008","Short answer","Use the jelly and dye experiment to suggest why it is better to have a lot of very small air sacs in the lungs rather than a few large ones.","1.2","The dye spreads through all of the small holes much faster, because many small holes have a much larger total surface area than a few large ones of the same volume, so more oxygen is absorbed in the same time. surface area faster small"],[9,"y8c01-r009","Short answer","Suggest one way in which agar jelly with holes in it is not a good model for the lungs.","1.2","The jelly does not move like a breathing lung, the holes are not surrounded by blood capillaries, and dye is not a gas. not capillar gas"],[9,"y8c01-r010","Short answer","Explain what it shows if the limewater in only one of the two tubes goes cloudy.","1.2","Limewater goes cloudy with carbon dioxide, so the tube that goes cloudy is the one that the expired air passed through, showing that expired air contains more carbon dioxide than inspired air. carbon dioxide expired more"],[9,"y8c01-r011","Short answer","Explain how contracting the intercostal muscles and the diaphragm makes air move into the lungs.","1.3","The ribs move up and out and the diaphragm moves down, so the volume inside the chest increases, the pressure inside falls, and air moves down the trachea into the lungs to fill the extra space. volume pressure air moves"],[9,"y8c01-r012","Short answer","Explain why the pressure inside the chest falls when the volume of the chest increases.","1.3","The pressure of a gas increases when the volume of its container decreases, so increasing the volume has the opposite effect and the pressure decreases. volume pressure decreas"],[9,"y8c01-r013","Short answer","Explain why air is squeezed out of the lungs when the intercostal and diaphragm muscles relax.","1.3","The ribs drop and the diaphragm returns to its domed shape, so the volume of the chest decreases, the pressure increases, and air is pushed out. volume pressure out"],[9,"y8c01-r014","Short answer","Explain why you should use three results to calculate a mean value for the volume of air you can breathe out.","1.3","A single reading may be unusually large or small; taking three and finding a mean reduces the effect of that and gives a more reliable value. mean reliab three"],[9,"y8c01-r015","Short answer","Describe one way in which the bottle-and-syringe model does not completely represent what happens when you breathe in.","1.3","The model has a rigid container and only one 'lung', the plunger is pulled by a hand rather than by muscles, and the model has no ribs or intercostal muscles. rigid muscle ribs"],[9,"y8c01-r016","Short answer","Explain why energy has to be released from glucose before a cell can use it.","1.4","The energy in glucose is locked up inside it as an energy store, so the mitochondria must release it by respiration before the cell can use it. locked store release"],[9,"y8c01-r017","Short answer","Explain why it matters that respiration releases energy in a controlled way, a little at a time.","1.4","The cell only needs a small amount of energy at a time; releasing it all at once would waste it and would damage the cell with heat. little needs controlled"],[9,"y8c01-r018","Short answer","Use the word equation for aerobic respiration to explain why the air you breathe out contains more carbon dioxide than the air you breathe in.","1.4","Respiration uses glucose and oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water, so the cells add carbon dioxide to the blood, which carries it to the lungs where it is breathed out. carbon dioxide product cells"],[9,"y8c01-r019","Short answer","Suggest why neurones contain more mitochondria than cheek cells do.","1.4","Neurones need more energy to do their job, and mitochondria are the structures that release energy from glucose, so more mitochondria means more energy released. more energy mitochondria release"],[9,"y8c01-r020","Short answer","Explain why respiring cells are slightly warmer than their surroundings.","1.4","Every time energy is transferred some of it is changed to heat energy, so respiration leaves the cells a little warmer. heat transfer warmer"],[9,"y8c01-r021","Short answer","Explain the difference between breathing and respiration.","1.4","Breathing is the movement of air into and out of the lungs; respiration is a chemical reaction inside every cell that releases energy from glucose. air chemical cell"],[9,"y8c01-r022","Short answer","Explain why blood looks red even though blood plasma is a very pale yellow.","1.5","Blood contains a very large number of red blood cells floating in the plasma, and the haemoglobin inside them is red. red blood cells haemoglobin plasma"],[9,"y8c01-r023","Short answer","Explain why it is useful that a red blood cell does not have a nucleus.","1.5","Not having a nucleus makes more space inside the cell for haemoglobin, so the cell can carry more oxygen. space haemoglobin oxygen"],[9,"y8c01-r024","Short answer","Explain why it is useful that a red blood cell does not have mitochondria.","1.5","Without mitochondria the red blood cell cannot respire aerobically, so it does not use up the oxygen it is carrying and can deliver it to other cells instead. use up oxygen deliver"],[9,"y8c01-r025","Short answer","Explain why being very small helps red blood cells to do their job.","1.5","Being small lets them pass through the very tiny blood capillaries, so they can get close to the alveoli in the lungs and to the respiring cells elsewhere. capillar close small"],[9,"y8c01-r026","Short answer","Explain how a white blood cell destroys a bacterium by phagocytosis.","1.5","It changes shape and pushes its cytoplasm out into 'fingers' that capture the bacterium, then produces chemicals that kill and digest it. capture chemicals digest"],[9,"y8c01-r027","Short answer","Explain why a different kind of antibody is needed for each different kind of pathogen.","1.5","Antibodies work by sticking onto the pathogen, so each antibody must match the particular pathogen it sticks to. stick match different"],[9,"y8c01-r028","Short answer","Suggest why gluing lots of pathogens together helps to protect the body.","1.5","Stuck together the pathogens cannot move, and it is easier for other white blood cells to capture and kill them all at once. cannot move easier capture"],[9,"y8c01-r2001","Short answer","Explain why the respiratory system is described as a system rather than a single organ.","1.1","It is a group of organs that work together for one purpose: taking oxygen out of the air and getting rid of carbon dioxide. The nose, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles and lungs each do part of the job, and none of them could do it alone. group of organs work together oxygen in carbon dioxide out"],[9,"y8c01-r2002","Short answer","Explain why every cell in the body needs the blood as well as the lungs.","1.1","The lungs only put oxygen into the blood. The blood then delivers that oxygen to every cell in the body so the cells can use it for respiration, and collects the waste carbon dioxide to take back to the lungs. Without the transport step the oxygen would never reach the cells that need it. lungs take in oxygen blood delivers every cell collects carbon dioxide"],[9,"y8c01-r2003","Short answer","Explain why it is better to breathe through your nose than through your mouth.","1.1","Air gets into your body through your mouth or nose, and both connect to your trachea. The nose warms and moistens the air and traps dust and bacteria on its lining, so the air arriving at the lungs is cleaner and closer to body temperature than air taken straight in through the mouth. nose warms moistens traps dust cleaner"],[9,"y8c01-r2004","Short answer","Explain why it is not enough to memorise one particular diagram of the respiratory system.","1.1","In a test the diagram might not be exactly the same as the one you learned — it may be drawn from a different angle, or labelled in a different order. What you need to know is what each structure does and where it lies in the pathway, so you can identify it on any diagram. not exactly the same different diagram know the function pathway"],[9,"y8c01-r2005","Short answer","Explain why the air sacs are wrapped in a very large number of tiny blood vessels.","1.2","The capillaries carry the blood that has to collect the oxygen. Wrapping many of them tightly around each air sac means a large area of blood is pressed close to the air, so a lot of oxygen can diffuse across at once and the blood is fully loaded before it leaves the lungs. capillaries pressed tightly large area close to the air diffuse"],[9,"y8c01-r2006","Short answer","Explain why there are only two thin cells between the air and the blood.","1.2","Diffusion is faster over a shorter distance. The wall of the air sac and the wall of the capillary are each a single layer of very thin cells, so oxygen and carbon dioxide have only a tiny distance to travel and can move between the air and the blood quickly. two thin cells short distance diffusion faster quickly"],[9,"y8c01-r2007","Short answer","Explain, in terms of particle numbers, why oxygen moves into the blood and not out of it.","1.2","The oxygen molecules move from where there are a lot of them, in the air in the air sac, to where there are fewer of them, in the blood arriving from the body. Diffusion always runs down this difference, so the net movement is into the blood until the numbers even out. a lot of them fewer of them diffusion net movement"],[9,"y8c01-r2008","Short answer","Explain what happens to oxygen immediately after it enters the blood.","1.2","It dissolves in the blood and goes into the red blood cells, where it combines with haemoglobin. Combining with haemoglobin takes the oxygen out of solution, which keeps the amount of dissolved oxygen low so more can keep diffusing in from the air. dissolves red blood cells haemoglobin keeps diffusion going"],[9,"y8c01-r2009","Short answer","The 32 small holes have the same total volume as the 8 large holes. Explain why this matters.","1.2","It makes the comparison fair. Because the total volume of jelly and dye is the same in both dishes, any difference in how fast the dye spreads must be caused by the number and size of the holes — that is, by the surface area — and not by there being more dye in one dish. same total volume fair comparison only surface area differs not more dye"],[9,"y8c01-r2010","Short answer","Explain why it is important not to get dye on the surface of the jelly.","1.2","Dye on the surface would spread into the jelly from the top as well as from inside the holes, so you could not tell how much of the spreading came from the holes. The results would then overstate the diffusion and the comparison between the two dishes would be spoiled. spreads from the surface cannot tell overstate spoils the comparison"],[9,"y8c01-r2011","Short answer","In the limewater experiment, explain why the glass tubing into tube A and tube B is different.","1.2","One tube has a long tube reaching down into the limewater and a short tube above it, and the other is the reverse. This arrangement means that when you blow, air is forced through the limewater in one tube, and when you suck, air from the room is drawn through the limewater in the other, so each tube tests a different sample of air. long tube short tube blow suck different air"],[9,"y8c01-r2012","Short answer","State the pressure rule used in this topic and explain why it is needed to understand breathing.","1.3","The pressure of a gas increases when the volume of its container is decreased. Breathing works by changing the volume of the chest cavity, so this rule is what links the movement of the ribs and diaphragm to the change in pressure that makes air flow. pressure increases volume decreased chest volume air flows"],[9,"y8c01-r2013","Short answer","Explain why air moves down the trachea on its own when you breathe in.","1.3","When the volume of the chest cavity increases, the pressure inside the chest and lungs decreases and becomes lower than the pressure of the air outside. Gases move from higher to lower pressure, so air moves down through the trachea into the lungs to fill the extra space. volume increases pressure decreases lower than outside air moves in"],[9,"y8c01-r2014","Short answer","Explain how a measuring cylinder can be used to mark a volume scale on a plastic bottle.","1.3","You add a known volume of water from the measuring cylinder to the bottle and mark the level, then repeat, adding the same volume each time and marking again, until the scale goes all the way from the bottom to the top. Each mark then stands for a known extra volume. known volume mark the level repeat same volume scale"],[9,"y8c01-r2015","Short answer","Explain why the water stays in the upside-down bottle instead of running out into the bowl.","1.3","The bottle is full, so no air can get in above the water, and the pressure of the atmosphere pushing down on the water in the bowl holds the water up inside the bottle. Water can only leave if something takes its place, which is exactly what the expired air does. full no air can get in atmospheric pressure holds the water up"],[9,"y8c01-r2016","Short answer","Describe one way in which the bell-jar or syringe model does not represent real breathing.","1.3","In the model the plunger is pulled by your hand, but in the body there is no outside pull — the diaphragm and intercostal muscles do the work themselves. The model also uses a rigid container and a simple balloon, whereas the real chest wall moves and the lungs are made of millions of air sacs. pulled by hand muscles do the work rigid container millions of air sacs"],[9,"y8c01-r2017","Short answer","Explain what 'contract' means for a muscle and what the intercostal muscles do when they contract.","1.3","To contract means to get shorter. When the intercostal muscles between the ribs contract they pull the ribs upwards and outwards, which is one of the two movements that make more space inside the chest cavity. get shorter pull the ribs upwards and outwards more space"],[9,"y8c01-r2018","Short answer","Explain the chain from eating a carbohydrate meal to a cell having usable energy.","1.4","The digestive system breaks the carbohydrates down to glucose, the blood delivers glucose to every cell, the mitochondria inside the cells carry out aerobic respiration using oxygen, and that reaction releases the energy from the glucose in a form the cell can use. digested glucose blood delivers mitochondria respiration energy released"],[9,"y8c01-r2019","Short answer","Explain what the word 'aerobic' tells you about this kind of respiration.","1.4","Aerobic means that the reaction uses oxygen from the air. That is why the body needs a respiratory system to take oxygen in and a blood system to carry it to the cells — without oxygen arriving, aerobic respiration cannot happen. uses oxygen from the air needs delivering cannot happen without"],[9,"y8c01-r2020","Short answer","Explain why mitochondria are sometimes described as where a cell gets its energy.","1.4","Mitochondria are the tiny structures inside cells that carry out aerobic respiration, and it is that reaction which releases the energy from glucose so the cell can use it. A cell with more mitochondria can release energy faster, which is why very active cells contain many of them. mitochondria aerobic respiration release energy more mitochondria faster"],[9,"y8c01-r2021","Short answer","Explain why some energy always ends up as heat when respiration takes place.","1.4","Every time energy is transferred or transformed, some of it is changed to heat energy. In respiration the chemical energy stored in glucose is transferred to other substances for the cell to use, so some of it becomes heat and the respiring cells get a little warmer than their surroundings. every transfer some becomes heat chemical energy warmer"],[9,"y8c01-r2022","Short answer","Explain why a person who eats no food at all would eventually have no energy.","1.4","All of our energy comes from the food that we eat. Food supplies the glucose that mitochondria use in respiration, so with no food arriving the body must use its stores, and once those run out there is no glucose left for the cells to respire and release energy from. all energy from food glucose respiration stores run out"],[9,"y8c01-r2023","Short answer","Explain why every cell in the body needs to be close to a blood vessel.","1.5","Every cell needs a constant supply of glucose and oxygen for respiration, and the carbon dioxide and water it makes must be taken away. The blood does both the delivery and the removal, and substances can only diffuse a short distance, so a cell far from a blood vessel would be starved and poisoned by its own waste. glucose and oxygen waste removed blood does both short distance diffuse"],[9,"y8c01-r2024","Short answer","Explain why the heart must pump constantly rather than occasionally.","1.5","The blood only delivers glucose and oxygen and removes carbon dioxide while it is moving. Cells respire all the time, so the supply and removal must also be continuous, and that means the heart has to keep the blood moving without stopping. blood must keep moving cells respire all the time continuous supply"],[9,"y8c01-r2025","Short answer","Explain the function of blood plasma, giving two examples of what it carries.","1.5","Plasma is the pale liquid part of the blood. It transports the red and white blood cells around the body, and it also carries many substances in solution — for example glucose dissolved in plasma is carried from the digestive system to every cell, and carbon dioxide dissolves in plasma and is carried from the cells to the lungs. liquid carries the cells dissolved substances glucose carbon dioxide"],[9,"y8c01-r2026","Short answer","Explain how haemoglobin lets a red blood cell pick up oxygen in the lungs and release it in the body.","1.5","In the capillaries next to the alveoli, oxygen diffuses into the red blood cell and combines with haemoglobin to form oxyhaemoglobin. As the blood passes cells that are respiring, and so have very little oxygen, the oxyhaemoglobin lets go of its oxygen and gives it to those cells. The blood then returns to the lungs to collect more. combines oxyhaemoglobin lets go respiring cells returns"],[9,"y8c01-r2027","Short answer","Explain how you would tell a white blood cell from a red blood cell under a microscope.","1.5","White blood cells always have nuclei, which red blood cells do not have, and in a stained sample the nucleus shows up as a dark purple area. Some kinds of white blood cell are also larger than red blood cells, and there are far fewer of them. nucleus red cells have none stained dark larger fewer"],[9,"y8c01-r2028","Short answer","Explain why a stain is added to a blood sample before it is looked at under a microscope.","1.5","White blood cells are almost colourless, so they are very hard to see against the plasma. A stain is added to make the cells show up more clearly, and it colours the nuclei strongly, which is what lets you tell the white cells from the red ones. colourless hard to see show up clearly nuclei"],[9,"y8c01-r2029","Short answer","Explain why having had one disease does not protect you from a different one.","1.5","Different kinds of antibody are needed for each different kind of pathogen, because an antibody only attaches to the pathogen it matches. The antibodies made against one pathogen will not fit another, so the body has to make a new kind for each new pathogen it meets. different antibody each pathogen only fits its match new kind needed"],[9,"y8c01-r2030","Short answer","At high altitude there is less oxygen in the air. Predict and explain what happens to a person's red blood cell count.","1.5","The number of red blood cells increases. With less oxygen in each breath, less oxygen diffuses into the blood, so the body makes more red blood cells, and therefore more haemoglobin, to carry as much oxygen as possible from the smaller amount available. increases less oxygen in the air more haemoglobin carry more oxygen"],[9,"y8c01-r2031","Short answer","Suggest two variables the scientists should have kept the same in the high-altitude rat experiment.","1.5","The rats should have been of the same species, age, sex and size, and both groups should have had the same food, water and temperature, with blood samples taken on the same days and measured the same way. Only the altitude should have differed, otherwise a change in red blood cell count could have had another cause. same age same species same food same days only altitude differs"],[9,"y8c01-o001","Short answer","Which characteristic shared by all living things is the subject of this chapter?","1.1","Respiration. respiration"],[9,"y8c01-o002","Short answer","What is respiration?","1.1","A series of chemical reactions that happens inside every living cell. chemical reactions every living cell"],[9,"y8c01-o003","Short answer","What is the name of the kind of respiration that usually happens inside our cells?","1.1","Aerobic respiration. aerobic"],[9,"y8c01-o006","Short answer","Where does the oxygen your cells use come from?","1.1","The air around you. air"],[9,"y8c01-o007","Short answer","What happens when you breathe in?","1.1","You take air into your lungs. air lungs"],[9,"y8c01-o008","Short answer","What happens to some of the oxygen in the air you breathe in?","1.1","It goes into your blood. into blood"],[9,"y8c01-o009","Short answer","What does the blood do with the oxygen it collects in the lungs?","1.1","It delivers the oxygen to every cell in the body, so that the cells can use it for respiration. delivers every cell respiration"],[9,"y8c01-o010","Short answer","What does the blood do with the carbon dioxide made by the cells?","1.1","It collects the waste carbon dioxide from the cells and takes it back to the lungs. collects lungs"],[9,"y8c01-o011","Short answer","What is the respiratory system?","1.1","The organs that help you to take oxygen out of the air and get rid of carbon dioxide. organs oxygen carbon dioxide"],[9,"y8c01-o032","Short answer","What does a tiny part of the lungs look like under a powerful microscope?","1.2","It is mostly holes. mostly holes"],[9,"y8c01-o033","Short answer","What are the holes seen in lung tissue under a microscope called?","1.2","Air sacs. air sacs"],[9,"y8c01-o066","Short answer","What safety point must you check about the bottle and tubing in the lung volume experiment?","1.3","They must be clean, and the water used must be safe to drink. clean safe to drink"],[9,"y8c01-o067","Short answer","What extra hazard is created by the lung volume experiment?","1.3","Water may get on the floor, so you could slip. water floor slip"],[9,"y8c01-o068","Short answer","What apparatus do you use to mark a volume scale on the plastic bottle?","1.3","A measuring cylinder. measuring cylinder"],[9,"y8c01-o069","Short answer","How far should the scale go along the plastic bottle?","1.3","All the way from the bottom to the top of the bottle. bottom top"],[9,"y8c01-o070","Short answer","What should you find when you take the lid off the full, upside-down bottle under water?","1.3","All the water stays in the bottle. water stays"],[9,"y8c01-o096","Short answer","Where does all of our energy come from?","1.4","From the food that we eat. food"],[9,"y8c01-o097","Short answer","Which type of nutrient is especially good for giving us energy?","1.4","Carbohydrates. carbohydrates"],[9,"y8c01-o098","Short answer","What does the digestive system break carbohydrates down into?","1.4","A kind of sugar called glucose. glucose"],[9,"y8c01-o099","Short answer","Where does the glucose go after digestion?","1.4","Into our blood. blood"],[9,"y8c01-o100","Short answer","Which cells does the blood deliver glucose to?","1.4","Every cell in the body. every cell"],[9,"y8c01-w001","Multiple choice","Which of these is the correct order that air passes through on its way into the lungs?","1.1","trachea, bronchus, bronchiole, air sacs air sacs, bronchiole, bronchus, trachea bronchus, trachea, air sacs, bronchiole trachea, air sacs, bronchus, bronchiole"],[9,"y8c01-w003","Multiple choice","A table lists several mammals with their body mass and their breathing rate, in no particular order. How should you reorganise it so that a pattern is easier to see?","1.2","Reorganise the entries so that the body masses are in order Delete any mammal that does not seem to fit Round every number to the nearest whole number Put the mammals into alphabetical order"],[9,"y8c01-w023","Gap fill","To calculate the mean of three values, add them up and ____ by 3.","1.3","divide"],[9,"y8c01-w024","Gap fill","The pale yellow liquid part of blood is called ____.","1.5","plasma"],[9,"y8c01-w029","Gap fill","Looking for a relationship between two sets of numbers is called looking for a ____ in the data.","1.2","correlation pattern"],[9,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the tube that carries air from your mouth or nose to your lungs?","1.1","bronchus trachea bronchiole larynx"],[9,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The trachea has strong rings of ____ around it to keep it open.","1.1","cartilage"],[9,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","The very tiny blood vessels wrapped around the air sacs are called ____.","1.2","capillaries"],[9,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","What process allows oxygen to move from the air into the blood?","1.2","respiration diffusion circulation digestion"],[9,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","Which gas moves from the blood into the air sacs?","1.2","oxygen nitrogen carbon dioxide hydrogen"],[9,"qwen-03-07","Multiple choice","What do you use to measure the volume of air pushed out in the lung capacity experiment?","1.3","a ruler a plastic bottle with a scale a thermometer a balance"],[9,"qwen-03-08","Gap fill","In the lung capacity experiment, you fill the bottle right to the top with ____.","1.3","water"],[9,"qwen-04-04","Gap fill","The tiny structures inside cells that release energy from glucose are called ____.","1.4","mitochondria"],[9,"qwen-04-06","Gap fill","Carbohydrates from food are broken down into a sugar called ____ which goes into the blood.","1.4","glucose"],[9,"qwen-05-02","Gap fill","Red blood cells contain a red pigment called ____ that carries oxygen.","1.5","haemoglobin"],[9,"qwen-05-03","Multiple choice","Why do red blood cells not have mitochondria?","1.5","To make them smaller So they do not use up the oxygen for themselves To allow more room for nuclei To help them change shape"],[9,"qwen-05-05","Multiple choice","What is the word equation for aerobic respiration?","1.4","glucose + water → carbon dioxide + oxygen glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen oxygen + haemoglobin → carbon dioxide + water"],[9,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Which part of the respiratory system is also known as the voice box?","1.1","larynx trachea bronchus diaphragm"],[9,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The small tubes that branch off from the bronchi are called ____.","1.1","bronchioles bronchiole"],[9,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","What is the relationship between body mass and the total surface area of air sacs?","1.2","As body mass increases, surface area increases As body mass increases, surface area decreases Body mass has no effect on surface area Smaller mammals have larger surface areas"],[9,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","Sofia and Zara used a large, empty ____ to measure lung volume.","1.3","bottle"],[9,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","How did the musicians push air into the bottle in the experiment?","1.3","They blew into it as hard as they could They used a pump to force air in They poured water into it They shook the bottle vigorously"],[9,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","In the experiment with rats at high altitude, what was the independent variable?","1.5","The number of red blood cells The altitude (sea level vs high) The time in days The volume of blood"],[9,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","Sofia put the yeast mixture in an insulated cup to prevent ____ from escaping.","1.4","heat"],[10,"y8c02-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","2.1",""],[10,"y8c02-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the top pan balance in the diagram.","2.1",""],[10,"y8c02-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the filter paper in the diagram.","2.1",""],[10,"y8c02-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the salt in the diagram.","2.1",""],[10,"y8c02-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the stirring rod in the diagram.","2.1",""],[10,"y8c02-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the beaker.","2.1",""],[10,"y8c02-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on a sugar particle in the concentrated solution.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on a water particle in the concentrated solution.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the curve for sodium nitrate.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the curve for potassium nitrate.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the curve for copper sulfate.","2.2",""],[10,"y8c02-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","2.3",""],[10,"y8c02-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the spatula in the diagram.","2.3",""],[10,"y8c02-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the salt on the spatula.","2.3",""],[10,"y8c02-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on a blue spot on the chromatogram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the brown spot on the chromatogram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on a yellow spot on the chromatogram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the pestle in the diagram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the mortar in the diagram.","2.4",""],[10,"y8c02-m001","Multiple choice","What is the name for the substance that dissolves?","2.1","The solute The solvent The solution The filtrate"],[10,"y8c02-m002","Multiple choice","What is the name for the substance that a solute dissolves into?","2.1","The solvent The solute The solution The residue"],[10,"y8c02-m012","Multiple choice","How does a concentrated solution differ from a dilute one?","2.2","More particles of the solute are dissolved in it It is always a darker colour It always has a larger volume It always has a lower mass"],[10,"y8c02-m013","Multiple choice","What word describes a solid that dissolves in water?","2.2","Soluble Insoluble Saturated Transparent"],[10,"y8c02-m023","Multiple choice","What is the variable you change called?","2.3","The independent variable The dependent variable The control variable The fair variable"],[10,"y8c02-m024","Multiple choice","What is the variable you measure called?","2.3","The dependent variable The independent variable The control variable The measured constant"],[10,"y8c02-m030","Multiple choice","What technique separates the different coloured inks in black ink?","2.4","Paper chromatography Filtration Evaporation Distillation"],[10,"y8c02-m031","Multiple choice","What is the finished image on the chromatography paper called?","2.4","A chromatogram A chromatograph A filtrate A solvent front"],[10,"y8c02-m042","Multiple choice","Sofia's drop of liquid did not move up the paper at all, although the water did. Why?","2.4","The liquid did not dissolve in the water, so the water could not carry it She used too much liquid The paper was too long She waited too long before looking"],[10,"y8c02-m044","Multiple choice","Marcus recorded the same number of spatulas for all three solutes. What does this suggest?","2.3","He stopped adding solute before the solution was really saturated All three solutes have exactly the same solubility He used too much water His thermometer was broken"],[10,"y8c02-m046","Gap fill","The substance that dissolves is called the ____.","2.1","solute"],[10,"y8c02-m052","Gap fill","A solution is made when a solute is dissolved in a ____.","2.2","solvent"],[10,"y8c02-m059","Gap fill","The variable you change is called the ____ variable.","2.3","independent"],[10,"y8c02-m065","Gap fill","You can separate out the coloured inks by using a technique called paper ____.","2.4","chromatography"],[10,"y8c02-r001","Short answer","Explain why the sugar has not disappeared, even though you can no longer see it.","2.1","The sugar is still there; its particles have simply been separated and spread out among the water particles, so they are too small to see. still there spread out too small"],[10,"y8c02-r002","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain how a sugar crystal dissolves in water.","2.1","The vibrating, sliding water particles bump into the tightly packed sugar particles and separate them, until the sugar particles are all mixed in among the water particles. water particles separate mixed"],[10,"y8c02-r003","Short answer","Explain how you can tell, just by looking, that milk is not a solution.","2.1","All solutions are transparent, and you cannot see through milk, so milk cannot be a solution. transparent see through not"],[10,"y8c02-r004","Short answer","Explain why a blue copper sulfate solution is still called transparent.","2.1","Transparent means you can see through it, and you can see through the blue solution even though it is coloured. see through coloured transparent"],[10,"y8c02-r005","Short answer","Explain why the mass of a salt solution equals the mass of the salt plus the mass of the water.","2.1","The salt particles are still in the water and nothing has been lost, so the mass is conserved. still conserved not lost"],[10,"y8c02-r006","Short answer","9 g of salt is dissolved in 50 g of water. Explain how you work out the mass of the solution.","2.1","Mass of solute plus mass of solvent equals mass of solution, so 9 g + 50 g = 59 g. 9 50 59"],[10,"y8c02-r007","Short answer","A green powder was stirred into water; the water looked cloudy and lumps could still be seen. Explain whether a solution has been formed.","2.1","No solution has formed, because a solution is transparent and this mixture is cloudy with visible lumps, so the powder has not dissolved. no transparent cloudy"],[10,"y8c02-r008","Short answer","Explain the difference between dissolving and melting.","2.1","Melting is one substance changing from solid to liquid when it is heated; dissolving is a solute spreading out among the particles of a solvent to make a solution, with two substances involved. melting solvent two"],[10,"y8c02-r009","Short answer","Explain how a concentrated solution differs from a dilute solution in terms of particles.","2.2","A concentrated solution has more particles of the solute dissolved in the same amount of solvent than a dilute solution has. more particles same"],[10,"y8c02-r010","Short answer","Explain why it is important to measure the food dye solution and the water accurately when making solutions of different concentrations.","2.2","The concentration depends on the ratio of dye to water, so an inaccurate measurement would give a concentration different from the one intended and the comparison would not be valid. accurate concentration compar"],[10,"y8c02-r011","Short answer","Explain why using a measuring cylinder that reads up to 100 cm3 would make these small volumes less accurate.","2.2","A large cylinder has widely spaced divisions, so measuring only a few cubic centimetres in it gives a much larger percentage error than using a small cylinder. large divisions error"],[10,"y8c02-r012","Short answer","Explain what has happened when no more of a solid will dissolve in a beaker of water.","2.2","The solution has become saturated: at that temperature the water can hold no more of that solute. saturated no more temperature"],[10,"y8c02-r013","Short answer","Explain what it means to say that sodium chloride has a greater solubility than lead chloride.","2.2","Much more sodium chloride than lead chloride will dissolve in the same volume of water at the same temperature. more same dissolve"],[10,"y8c02-r014","Short answer","Counting spatulas is a rough measure of how much solute was added. Suggest a better way, and explain why it is better.","2.2","Weigh the solute on a balance and record the mass added, because spatulas hold different amounts each time while a mass is a measured quantity that can be repeated. mass balance repeat"],[10,"y8c02-r015","Short answer","Explain why you must use a known amount of solvent when comparing the solubility of different solutes.","2.2","Solubility is how much solute dissolves in a fixed amount of solvent, so unless the amount of solvent is the same for each solute the results cannot be compared. same fixed compare"],[10,"y8c02-r016","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why most solutes dissolve faster in hot water than in cold water.","2.2","The more energy the particles have, the more they vibrate and move, so the water particles collide with the solute more often and separate its particles more quickly. energy move faster"],[10,"y8c02-r017","Short answer","204 g of sugar dissolves in 100 g of water at 20 °C. Explain how to work out how much dissolves in 50 g of water at the same temperature.","2.2","Halving the mass of water halves the mass of sugar that will dissolve, so 204 divided by 2 gives 102 g. half 102 proportion"],[10,"y8c02-r018","Short answer","Look at the solubility graph for three salts. Describe the general trend and explain what it tells you.","2.2","For all three salts the solubility increases as the temperature increases, which shows that hotter water can hold more dissolved solute. increases temperature all three"],[10,"y8c02-r019","Short answer","Nail polish will not dissolve in water but does dissolve in propanone. Explain what this shows about solvents.","2.2","A substance that is insoluble in one solvent may be soluble in another, so the right solvent has to be chosen for the job. insoluble another choose"],[10,"y8c02-r020","Short answer","Explain how the results of the salt-dissolving investigation would be affected if the volume of water were not kept the same.","2.3","More water would dissolve more salt whatever the temperature, so you could not tell whether a change in the result was caused by the temperature or by the volume of water. more water temperature cannot tell"],[10,"y8c02-r021","Short answer","Explain the difference between the independent variable and the dependent variable.","2.3","The independent variable is the one you deliberately change; the dependent variable is the one you measure, because its value depends on the change you made. change measure depends"],[10,"y8c02-r022","Short answer","Explain which variable goes on each axis when you plot a graph of your results.","2.3","The independent variable goes along the horizontal axis and the dependent variable goes up the vertical axis. independent horizontal vertical"],[10,"y8c02-r023","Short answer","As well as the volume of water, suggest another variable in this investigation that must be controlled, and explain why.","2.3","The size of the spatula and how full it is loaded each time, because otherwise each 'spatula' would be a different mass of salt and the counts could not be compared. spatula same mass"],[10,"y8c02-r024","Short answer","Explain how paper chromatography separates the different coloured inks in black ink.","2.4","As the water soaks up the paper it dissolves the inks and carries their particles with it, and because the inks have different solubilities their particles are carried different distances before being left behind. carries solubil different distances"],[10,"y8c02-r025","Short answer","On a chromatogram, explain how you can tell which coloured substance is the most soluble.","2.4","The more soluble substance is carried further by the solvent, so the spot that has travelled furthest up the paper is the most soluble. furthest more soluble carried"],[10,"y8c02-r026","Short answer","Explain why the line on the chromatography paper is drawn in pencil rather than in ink.","2.4","Pencil is not soluble in the solvent, so it will not dissolve and run up the paper and spoil the chromatogram, as an ink line would. pencil not soluble ink"],[10,"y8c02-r027","Short answer","Explain why the ink spot must stay above the level of the water in the beaker.","2.4","If the spot were under the water the ink would simply dissolve away into the beaker instead of being carried up the paper, so no chromatogram would form. dissolve into the water not carried"],[10,"y8c02-r028","Short answer","Explain why the paper must be taken out before the water reaches the top of the strip.","2.4","Once the solvent reaches the end it can go no further, so the spots stop separating and you cannot tell how far each substance was really carried. no further separat how far"],[10,"y8c02-r029","Short answer","Explain how a chromatogram shows you which food dyes are pure substances.","2.4","A pure substance gives only one spot on the chromatogram, while a mixture separates into two or more spots. one spot pure mixture"],[10,"y8c02-r030","Short answer","Explain why ethanol, and not water, was used as the solvent for the green pigment from spinach.","2.4","The green pigment does not dissolve in water but does dissolve in ethanol, so only ethanol will carry it up the paper. dissolve ethanol not water"],[10,"y8c02-r031","Short answer","Explain how public health scientists use chromatography to check that a hair dye is safe.","2.4","They run a chromatogram of the dye alongside a chromatogram of the permitted colourings, and compare the spots: any spot that does not match a permitted one shows an unapproved substance is present. compare permitted match"],[10,"y8c02-r2001","Short answer","Explain why a sugar crystal can be seen but dissolved sugar cannot.","2.1","The crystal is visible because it is made of lots of groups of particles tightly packed together, which is big enough for your eye to see. Once it dissolves the sugar particles are no longer in groups — they are spread out singly among the water particles — and single particles are far too small to be seen. groups of particles tightly packed spread out too small to be seen"],[10,"y8c02-r2002","Short answer","Explain the part played by the moving water particles in dissolving.","2.1","The water particles vibrate and slide past one another and bump into the vibrating sugar particles. Those collisions knock sugar particles off the crystal and separate them, and the particles then get mixed up among the water particles until the whole crystal has been broken apart. water particles move bump into separate mixed up"],[10,"y8c02-r2003","Short answer","Explain why nail polish remover is called a solvent even though it is not water.","2.1","The solvent is whichever substance the solute dissolves into — it does not have to be water. Nail polish dissolves in propanone, so in that mixture propanone is the solvent and the nail polish is the solute. solvent dissolves into not only water propanone"],[10,"y8c02-r2004","Short answer","Explain why a blue copper sulfate solution is still a solution even though it is not colourless.","2.1","A solution is a mixture in which the solute particles are spread evenly among the solvent particles and are too small to see. Copper sulfate is a coloured salt, so the solution formed is blue, but it is still clear and even throughout, which is what makes it a solution. spread evenly too small to see coloured salt still clear"],[10,"y8c02-r2005","Short answer","Explain, in terms of particles, why no mass is lost when salt dissolves in water.","2.1","Dissolving only spreads the salt particles out among the water particles; it does not destroy any of them. All the particles that were in the salt and in the water are still in the beaker, so the mass of the solution equals the mass of the solute plus the mass of the solvent. particles spread out none destroyed still there mass of solute plus solvent"],[10,"y8c02-r2006","Short answer","Explain how to measure the volume of a liquid as accurately as possible.","2.1","Use the smallest measuring cylinder that will hold the volume, stand it on a level surface, and read the bottom of the meniscus with your eye level with it. A cylinder that is too large has big divisions, and reading from above or below gives a value that is too high or too low. smallest cylinder level surface meniscus eye level"],[10,"y8c02-r2007","Short answer","Explain how the number of dye particles differs between the most concentrated and the most dilute solution you made.","2.2","Both contain the same kind of dye particles, but the concentrated solution has more dye particles in the same volume. In the dilute solution some of the dye has been replaced with water, so the dye particles are more spread out and there are fewer of them in each cubic centimetre. more particles same volume more spread out fewer"],[10,"y8c02-r2008","Short answer","Explain why you could not judge the concentrations by eye if you used salt solution instead of food dye.","2.2","Food dye solutions can be ranked by eye because the deeper the colour, the more dye particles there are in the same volume. Salt and sugar solutions are all colourless, so there is no visible difference at all and you would have to measure something else, such as mass or density, instead. colour depth more particles colourless no visible difference measure instead"],[10,"y8c02-r2009","Short answer","Explain what has happened at the particle level when no more solid will dissolve in a beaker of water.","2.2","The water particles can only hold so many solute particles apart among themselves. Once that limit is reached, any extra solid that is added has no room to spread out into, so it stays as a solid at the bottom and the solution is described as saturated. limit reached no room stays solid saturated"],[10,"y8c02-r2010","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why more of a solid dissolves in hot water than in cold water.","2.2","The more energy the particles have, the more they vibrate and move. In hot water the particles move faster and collide with the solid more often and more forcefully, so they knock particles off the solid more effectively and can keep more of them separated among themselves. more energy move faster collide more often knock particles off"],[10,"y8c02-r2011","Short answer","Explain what the statement 'as temperature increases, solubility increases' allows you to predict.","2.2","It lets you predict that the same mass of water will dissolve more of a solute if it is heated, and that a saturated solution made when hot will deposit crystals as it cools, because the cooler water can no longer hold all the dissolved solute. more dissolves when hot crystals form on cooling cannot hold as much"],[10,"y8c02-r2012","Short answer","Explain why heating the water lets you dissolve more sugar, using the figures given.","2.2","At 20 °C, 100 g of water dissolves 204 g of sugar, but at 80 °C the same 100 g dissolves 362 g. The hot water particles have more energy and move more, so they can separate and hold apart a far greater number of sugar particles — an extra 158 g for every 100 g of water. 204 g 362 g more energy hold more apart"],[10,"y8c02-r2013","Short answer","Explain why alcohol rather than water must be used to separate the ink in a permanent marker.","2.2","Chromatography only works if the solvent dissolves the substances being separated and carries them up the paper. Permanent marker ink is not soluble in water, so water would leave the spot where it is. Alcohol does dissolve it, so it can carry the different inks different distances. not soluble in water solvent must dissolve it alcohol carries the ink"],[10,"y8c02-r2014","Short answer","Explain why the students spend time listing everything that could affect their results before starting.","2.3","Any factor they overlook could change the amount of salt that dissolves without them realising, so a difference in the results might be caused by that factor instead of by temperature. Identifying the variables first lets them decide which one to change and which ones to keep the same. could affect results another cause decide which to change keep the same"],[10,"y8c02-r2015","Short answer","Explain why the number of spatulas of salt is called the dependent variable.","2.3","It is the variable the students measure, and its value depends on the variable they chose to change — the temperature of the water. Its name says exactly that: what happens to it depends on what they did to the independent variable. measured depends on temperature independent variable"],[10,"y8c02-r2016","Short answer","Explain the rule for which variable goes on which axis of a graph, and why it matters.","2.3","The independent variable always goes along the horizontal axis and the dependent variable up the vertical axis. Following the rule means anyone reading the graph knows immediately which quantity was changed and which one responded, so the graph can be interpreted without extra explanation. independent horizontal dependent vertical same for everyone interpreted"],[10,"y8c02-r2017","Short answer","Besides the volume of water, suggest another variable in this investigation that must be controlled, and explain why.","2.3","The size of the spatula and how full it is loaded must be the same each time, otherwise 'one spatula' means a different mass at different temperatures and the counts cannot be compared. The stirring, the type of salt and the length of time allowed for each spatula to dissolve must also be kept the same. size of spatula same mass stirring same time comparable"],[10,"y8c02-r2018","Short answer","Zara and Arun get different numbers of spatulas for the same solute. Explain how that can happen without either being wrong.","2.3","They may have used different sizes of spatula, or loaded them differently, or used different volumes of water. Each is counting in a unit of their own, so the two numbers are not comparable — but the order in which they rank the solutes can still agree, and that is the part of the result that means something. different spatula different volume not comparable order can still agree"],[10,"y8c02-r2019","Short answer","Marcus gets the same number of spatulas for all three solutes. Explain why his results are so different from the others'.","2.3","He has probably stopped adding at a fixed number rather than at the point where no more would dissolve, or has not stirred long enough for each spatula to dissolve before adding the next. Either way he has not reached saturation, so he is measuring his own procedure rather than the solubility of the solutes. stopped too early not stirred not saturated measuring the procedure"],[10,"y8c02-r2020","Short answer","Explain what a student should do about a point on their graph that does not fit the pattern.","2.3","They should mark it as anomalous and repeat that measurement under the same conditions rather than simply ignoring it. If the repeat falls on the line the first value was an error and can be replaced; if it does not, there is something real to investigate. anomalous repeat same conditions replace or investigate"],[10,"y8c02-r2021","Short answer","Explain why different coloured inks travel different distances up the chromatography paper.","2.4","The water carries the ink particles with it as it moves up the paper, but not all the ink particles have the same solubility. The more soluble an ink is, the longer it stays dissolved and the further its particles are carried before they are left behind on the paper. carried by the water different solubility more soluble travels further"],[10,"y8c02-r2022","Short answer","Explain why a scientist comparing many samples runs them side by side on one large sheet rather than separately.","2.4","The solvent moves up through all the samples at the same time, under exactly the same conditions of paper, solvent, temperature and time. The distances travelled can then be compared directly, whereas separate runs might differ for reasons that have nothing to do with the samples. same time same conditions compared directly separate runs differ"],[10,"y8c02-r2023","Short answer","Explain how a public health scientist decides whether a dye in a product is permitted.","2.4","They run a chromatogram of the product alongside a chromatogram of all the permitted colourings. Each permitted dye travels a known distance under those conditions, so any spot in the product that does not line up with one of the permitted spots must be a different substance, and further tests are then needed. compare chromatograms permitted colourings does not match further tests"],[10,"y8c02-r2024","Short answer","Explain how a chromatogram shows whether a food dye is a pure substance or a mixture.","2.4","A pure substance contains only one kind of particle, so it travels one distance and leaves a single spot. A mixture contains substances of different solubilities, which are carried different distances, so it separates into two or more spots. one spot pure several spots mixture different solubilities"],[10,"y8c02-r2025","Short answer","Explain why the crushed spinach is left to stand for about ten minutes in the ethanol.","2.4","Dissolving is not instant — the ethanol particles have to collide with the pigment and separate its particles out of the leaf. Standing for ten minutes gives time for enough of the green pigment to dissolve in the ethanol so the spot loaded onto the paper is concentrated enough to see. time to dissolve collide concentrated enough visible spot"],[10,"y8c02-r2026","Short answer","Explain why each drop of liquid must be dried before the next one is added.","2.4","Adding a wet drop on top of a wet spot lets the liquid spread sideways, giving a large smeared start instead of a small one. Drying between drops builds up more colour in the same small spot, so the separated substances appear as sharp bands rather than overlapping smudges. dry between drops small spot does not spread sharp bands"],[10,"y8c02-r2027","Short answer","Sofia's drop of flower liquid did not move at all while the water rose past it. Explain what this shows and what she should change.","2.4","It shows the pigment is not soluble in water, so the water could not dissolve it and carry it up the paper. She should repeat the chromatogram using a different solvent, such as ethanol, in which the pigment does dissolve. not soluble in water cannot be carried different solvent ethanol"],[10,"y8c02-r2028","Short answer","Explain what the solvent front is and why it must not reach the top of the paper.","2.4","The solvent front is the point that the solvent has reached. Once it runs off the top edge you no longer know how far the solvent travelled, so the distances moved by the spots cannot be compared with it, and spots near the top may be carried off the paper altogether. point the solvent reaches runs off cannot compare distances spots lost"],[10,"y8c02-o001","Short answer","What is the name given to a substance that dissolves?","2.1","The solute. solute"],[10,"y8c02-o002","Short answer","What is the name given to the substance that a solute dissolves into?","2.1","The solvent. solvent"],[10,"y8c02-o003","Short answer","What is the mixture called that forms when a solute dissolves in a solvent?","2.1","A solution. solution"],[10,"y8c02-o004","Short answer","In a mixture of sugar and water, which substance is the solute?","2.1","The sugar. sugar"],[10,"y8c02-o005","Short answer","In a mixture of sugar and water, which substance is the solvent?","2.1","The water. water"],[10,"y8c02-o006","Short answer","Is a solution a mixture or a compound?","2.1","A mixture. mixture"],[10,"y8c02-o007","Short answer","When sugar dissolves in water it seems to disappear. Has it really gone?","2.1","No, the sugar is still there. no still there"],[10,"y8c02-o008","Short answer","What has happened to the sugar particles once the sugar has dissolved?","2.1","They have spread out among the water particles. spread out water particles"],[10,"y8c02-o046","Short answer","When is a solution made?","2.2","When a solute is dissolved in a solvent. solute solvent"],[10,"y8c02-o047","Short answer","Which has more dissolved solute particles, a concentrated solution or a dilute solution?","2.2","A concentrated solution. concentrated"],[10,"y8c02-o048","Short answer","What word describes a solution that contains a lot of solute particles?","2.2","Concentrated. concentrated"],[10,"y8c02-o106","Short answer","What name is given to the different things that could affect the results of an investigation?","2.3","Variables. variables"],[10,"y8c02-o107","Short answer","What are the students investigating in Topic 2.3?","2.3","How temperature affects the amount of salt that will dissolve in water. temperature salt dissolve"],[10,"y8c02-o108","Short answer","What volume of water do the students use in each test?","2.3","50 cm3. 50"],[10,"y8c02-o109","Short answer","What range of temperatures do the students use?","2.3","From 20 degrees Celsius to 80 degrees Celsius. 20 80"],[10,"y8c02-o110","Short answer","Which variable do the students change?","2.3","The temperature of the water. temperature"],[10,"y8c02-o111","Short answer","Which variable do the students measure?","2.3","The number of spatulas of salt that dissolve. spatulas salt"],[10,"y8c02-o112","Short answer","Which variable do the students keep the same?","2.3","The volume of water. volume water"],[10,"y8c02-o116","Short answer","Which axis of a graph does the independent variable go on?","2.3","The horizontal axis. horizontal x"],[10,"y8c02-o117","Short answer","Which axis of a graph does the dependent variable go on?","2.3","The vertical axis. vertical y"],[10,"y8c02-o118","Short answer","In the salt investigation, what is the independent variable?","2.3","The temperature of the water. temperature"],[10,"y8c02-o142","Short answer","Black ink looks like one colour. What is it really?","2.4","A mixture of different coloured inks. mixture"],[10,"y8c02-o143","Short answer","Which technique is used to separate the coloured inks in black ink?","2.4","Paper chromatography. chromatography"],[10,"y8c02-o144","Short answer","What kind of paper is used for chromatography?","2.4","Special paper, a bit like filter paper. filter paper special"],[10,"y8c02-o145","Short answer","What happens to the water in the beaker during chromatography?","2.4","It soaks up into the paper. soaks up"],[10,"y8c02-o146","Short answer","What happens to the coloured inks as the water moves up the paper?","2.4","They separate out. separate"],[10,"y8c02-w001","Multiple choice","20 g of salt is added to 100 g of water. The salt dissolves and seems to disappear. What has happened to it?","2.1","The salt particles have spread out between the water particles to form a solution The salt particles have been destroyed by the water The salt particles have turned into water particles The salt particles have floated out of the beaker as a gas"],[10,"y8c02-w002","Multiple choice","What is the difference between dissolving and melting?","2.1","Dissolving needs a solvent; melting only needs the solid to be heated Dissolving happens to metals and melting happens to salts Melting needs a solvent; dissolving only needs heating They are two words for exactly the same change"],[10,"y8c02-w004","Multiple choice","Solution A has more solute particles in it than solution B. What does that make solution A?","2.2","More concentrated than solution B More dilute than solution B Less soluble than solution B More transparent than solution B"],[10,"y8c02-w005","Multiple choice","Sofia adds more and more copper sulfate to a beaker of water until no more will dissolve. What has she made?","2.2","A saturated solution of copper sulfate A pure sample of copper sulfate A suspension of copper sulfate A dilute solution of copper sulfate"],[10,"y8c02-w023","Gap fill","A substance that will not dissolve in water is said to be ____.","2.1","insoluble"],[10,"y8c02-w025","Gap fill","Copper sulfate dissolves in water, so copper sulfate is said to be ____.","2.2","soluble"],[10,"y8c02-w027","Gap fill","The variable you measure in order to see the effect is called the ____ variable.","2.3","dependent"],[10,"y8c02-w029","Gap fill","Separating the substances in a mixture by letting a solvent carry them up a piece of paper is called ____.","2.4","chromatography"],[10,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","In a mixture of sugar and water, what is the name for the substance that dissolves?","2.1","Solvent Solute Solution Mixture"],[10,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","What property do all solutions have?","2.1","They are opaque They are transparent They are always colourless They are solid"],[10,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Milk is not a solution because it is ____ and you cannot see through it.","2.1","opaque"],[10,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","A solution with a lot of solute particles is called a ____ solution.","2.2","concentrated"],[10,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the name for a solution where no more solid will dissolve?","2.2","Dilute Saturated Concentrated Insoluble"],[10,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Which of these substances is insoluble in water?","2.2","Iron filings Sodium chloride Sugar Copper sulfate"],[10,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the image produced on paper after separating ink colours using a solvent?","2.4","Chromatogram Solvent front Filter paper Spatula"],[10,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","Why do different coloured inks separate from each other on the paper?","2.4","They have different solubilities The water is too hot The paper is wet They are different weights"],[10,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","In an investigation, the variable that you change is called the ____ variable.","2.3","independent"],[10,"qwen-03-05","Multiple choice","In a graph of investigation results, which variable is plotted on the vertical axis?","2.3","Dependent variable Independent variable Control variable Range"],[10,"qwen-03-06","Gap fill","Variables that are kept the same to ensure a fair test are called ____ variables.","2.3","control"],[10,"qwen-03-11","Gap fill","Scientists use chromatography to check if food dyes contain substances that could be ____ or cause allergic reactions.","2.4","toxic"],[10,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the term for a substance that will not dissolve in water?","2.2","soluble insoluble saturated diluted"],[10,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","If a solution has more solute particles than another, it is described as being more ____.","2.2","concentrated"],[10,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","Marcus and Sofia investigate how much salt dissolves in different volumes of water. The volume of water is the ____ variable.","2.3","independent"],[10,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","When salt is added to water and dissolves, the resulting liquid is called a ____.","2.1","solution"],[10,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","The liquid in which a solute dissolves is called the ____.","2.1","solvent"],[10,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","In the investigation comparing salts X and Y, what is the independent variable?","2.3","The type of salt used The mass of salt dissolved The temperature of the water The volume of water used"],[10,"qwenw-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the technique used to separate mixtures by moving a solvent up paper?","2.4","Paper chromatography Filtration Evaporation Distillation"],[10,"qwenw-03-02","Gap fill","In paper chromatography, a solvent such as ____ is used to carry the colourings up the paper.","2.4","water"],[11,"y8c03-f37h1","Click the diagram","Click on the container of one gas at the stage where the tap is still closed.","3.7",""],[11,"y8c03-f37h2","Click the diagram","Click on the container of one gas at the stage where the two gases have completely diffused.","3.7",""],[11,"y8c03-f37h3","Click the diagram","Click on the glass in which the ink is at high concentration in only a small part of the water.","3.7",""],[11,"y8c03-f37h4","Click the diagram","Click on the glass in which the ink has completely diffused throughout the water.","3.7",""],[11,"y8c03-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the contact force in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f07a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f08a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.1",""],[11,"y8c03-f09a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.3",""],[11,"y8c03-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the Distance in the diagram.","3.3",""],[11,"y8c03-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the Time in the diagram.","3.3",""],[11,"y8c03-f10a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the lever in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f11a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f11h1","Click the diagram","Click on the pivot in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f12a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f12h1","Click the diagram","Click on the pivot in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f13a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f13h1","Click the diagram","Click on the wrench in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f14a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.4",""],[11,"y8c03-f15a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","3.6",""],[11,"y8c03-f15h1","Click the diagram","Click on the holes in the diagram.","3.6",""],[11,"y8c03-m001","Multiple choice","When two forces acting on an object are balanced, what is true about them?","3.1","They are equal in size and opposite in direction They are equal in size and act in the same direction They are different in size and opposite in direction They are different in size and act in the same direction"],[11,"y8c03-m012","Multiple choice","Sofia is riding her bicycle along a level road at a steady speed. Which two horizontal forces on the bicycle are balanced?","3.1","The driving force and the force of friction The driving force and the weight of the bicycle The force of friction and the contact force The weight of the bicycle and the force of friction"],[11,"y8c03-m013","Multiple choice","What is the standard unit for speed used by scientists?","3.2","Metres per second Kilometres per hour Miles per hour Knots"],[11,"y8c03-m021","Multiple choice","Which of these is the standard unit of distance in science?","3.2","Metre Foot Mile Kilometre"],[11,"y8c03-m023","Multiple choice","On a distance-time graph, which quantities go on the axes?","3.3","Distance travelled on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis Time on the vertical axis and distance travelled on the horizontal axis Speed on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis Distance travelled on both axes"],[11,"y8c03-m033","Multiple choice","Which of these can be worked out from a distance-time graph?","3.3","The speed of an object The mass of an object The weight of an object The force on an object"],[11,"y8c03-m034","Multiple choice","What is the name given to an object that turns when a force is applied?","3.4","A lever A pivot A moment A newton"],[11,"y8c03-m043","Multiple choice","Why does using a longer spanner make a nut easier to turn?","3.4","The force acts further from the pivot, so the moment is greater The force acts closer to the pivot, so the moment is greater A longer spanner is heavier, so the moment is greater A longer spanner reduces the moment needed to turn the nut"],[11,"y8c03-m044","Multiple choice","How can you think of pressure?","3.5","As the pushing effect of a force As the turning effect of a force As the stretching effect of a force As the total size of a force"],[11,"y8c03-m052","Multiple choice","Why is touching the thorns on a rose stem more dangerous than touching other parts of the stem?","3.5","A thorn has a very small area, so it exerts a very large pressure on your skin A thorn has a very large area, so it exerts a very large pressure on your skin A thorn exerts a much larger force than the rest of the stem A thorn has a greater weight than the rest of the stem"],[11,"y8c03-m054","Multiple choice","Water comes out of a low hole in a dam with more force than from a higher hole. Why?","3.6","Pressure in the water increases with depth Pressure in the water decreases with depth Pressure in the water is the same at all depths Water at the bottom is heavier than water at the top"],[11,"y8c03-m067","Multiple choice","What does diffusion mean?","3.7","The overall random movement of particles from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration The movement of particles from an area of lower concentration to an area of higher concentration The movement of all the particles in one fixed direction The settling of particles at the bottom of a container"],[11,"y8c03-m077","Gap fill","Balanced forces are equal in size and ____ in direction.","3.1","opposite"],[11,"y8c03-m083","Gap fill","The standard unit for speed is metres per ____.","3.2","second"],[11,"y8c03-m088","Gap fill","A graph with distance on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis is called a ____-time graph.","3.3","distance"],[11,"y8c03-m093","Gap fill","An object that turns when a force is applied to it is called a ____.","3.4","lever"],[11,"y8c03-m098","Gap fill","You can think of pressure as the ____ effect of a force.","3.5","pushing"],[11,"y8c03-m102","Gap fill","Pressure in a liquid increases with ____.","3.6","depth"],[11,"y8c03-m107","Gap fill","The movement of the particles of each gas as they spread out is called ____.","3.7","diffusion"],[11,"y8c03-r001","Short answer","A rock rests on the ground. Explain why it does not move towards the centre of the Earth even though gravity pulls it that way.","3.1","The ground pushes up on the rock with a contact force equal in size and opposite in direction to its weight, so the two forces are balanced and the rock stays still. contact force balanced equal"],[11,"y8c03-r002","Short answer","The wind pushes sideways on the rock but the rock does not move sideways. Explain why.","3.1","The pushing force from the wind is balanced by friction between the rock and the ground, so the sideways forces are equal and opposite. friction balanced wind"],[11,"y8c03-r003","Short answer","Explain how you would draw the arrows on a force diagram to show that two forces are balanced.","3.1","The arrows must be the same length, because arrow length shows the size of the force, and they must point in opposite directions. same length opposite size"],[11,"y8c03-r004","Short answer","Explain what happens to the rock when a vehicle pushes it with a force larger than friction.","3.1","The forces are no longer balanced, so the rock starts to move in the direction of the larger sideways force. unbalanced move larger"],[11,"y8c03-r005","Short answer","Explain how a parachute slows a falling object down.","3.1","The parachute causes a force of air resistance that is larger than the weight of the object, so the unbalanced force acts upwards and the object slows down. air resistance larger slows"],[11,"y8c03-r006","Short answer","Explain why the hitting force from the racket must be larger than the force from the ball if the ball is to go back the other way.","3.1","To change the ball's direction the forces must be unbalanced in the direction you want it to go, so the hitting force has to be the larger of the two. unbalanced larger direction"],[11,"y8c03-r007","Short answer","Explain why a constant unbalanced force is needed to keep a planet moving in a circle.","3.1","When an object moves in a circle its direction is always changing, and a change of direction always needs an unbalanced force, so gravity must keep acting on it. direction changing unbalanced"],[11,"y8c03-r008","Short answer","In a tug of war, suggest two changes that could make the rope move towards team B.","3.1","Team B could pull with a larger force, for example by adding more people or by pulling harder, or team A could pull with a smaller force; either way the forces become unbalanced towards B. larger force more people unbalanced"],[11,"y8c03-r009","Short answer","Explain why scientists use standard units of measurement in all countries.","3.2","Different countries and different jobs use different units, such as knots, Mach, kilometres per hour and miles per hour, so a standard unit avoids confusion when results are compared. different confusion compare"],[11,"y8c03-r010","Short answer","Explain what it means to say that a horse runs at a speed of 15 metres per second.","3.2","It means the horse travels a distance of 15 metres in each second. 15 metres each second distance"],[11,"y8c03-r011","Short answer","Explain why the term 'average speed' is often used rather than just 'speed'.","3.2","The speed of an object during a journey is not always constant, so the calculated value is the average over the whole journey rather than the speed at any one moment. not constant average whole journey"],[11,"y8c03-r012","Short answer","Explain how you would find the time taken for a journey if you know the speed and the distance.","3.2","Rearrange the speed equation: time taken equals the distance travelled divided by the speed. distance divided speed"],[11,"y8c03-r013","Short answer","Explain why a distance–time graph is more useful than a description in words for showing a journey.","3.3","From the graph you can read any value of distance or time during the journey, calculate other values such as speed, and see information about the whole journey at once. read calculate whole journey"],[11,"y8c03-r014","Short answer","Explain why constant speed appears on a distance–time graph as a straight, upward sloping line.","3.3","At constant speed the object travels the same distance in each second, so the distance increases by equal amounts in equal times, which plots as a straight slope. same distance each second straight"],[11,"y8c03-r015","Short answer","Explain why a stationary object gives a horizontal line on a distance–time graph.","3.3","The distance from the start does not change while the object is stationary, but time still passes, so the line goes across without rising. distance does not change time"],[11,"y8c03-r016","Short answer","Two straight sloping lines on the same distance–time graph have different steepness. Explain what that tells you.","3.3","The steeper line shows the faster journey, because a steeper slope means a greater distance is travelled in each second. steeper faster greater distance"],[11,"y8c03-r017","Short answer","Explain why the return journey appears as a downward sloping line.","3.3","On the way back, the distance of the object from the start decreases with time, so the line slopes downwards. decrease back downward"],[11,"y8c03-r018","Short answer","Explain why the moment of a force depends on the distance from the pivot as well as the size of the force.","3.4","The moment is force multiplied by the distance from the pivot, so the same force applied further from the pivot gives a greater turning effect. force distance greater"],[11,"y8c03-r019","Short answer","Arun cannot turn a nut with his maximum force. Explain why a longer spanner lets him turn it.","3.4","The longer spanner puts his force further from the pivot, so the same force produces a bigger moment and the nut turns. longer further bigger moment"],[11,"y8c03-r020","Short answer","Explain the condition for a seesaw to be balanced.","3.4","The moments on the two sides of the pivot must be equal and opposite, so weight multiplied by distance must be the same on each side. moments equal each side"],[11,"y8c03-r021","Short answer","Marcus weighs 600 N and sits 2 m from the pivot. Explain how to find where Arun, who weighs 800 N, must sit to balance the seesaw.","3.4","Marcus exerts a moment of 600 x 2 = 1200 N m, so Arun must produce the same moment; distance = moment divided by force = 1200 / 800 = 1.5 m from the pivot. 1200 divide 1.5"],[11,"y8c03-r022","Short answer","Explain why a sharp knife cuts more easily than a blunt one, using the idea of pressure.","3.5","A sharp knife has less surface area in contact with the material, and as the area decreases the pressure caused by the same force increases. area decreases pressure increases"],[11,"y8c03-r023","Short answer","Explain why some objects are designed with large areas and others with very small areas.","3.5","Large areas spread a force out and so decrease the pressure, while small areas concentrate a force and so increase the pressure, depending on what the object has to do. large decrease increase"],[11,"y8c03-r024","Short answer","Explain what shape of sole gives shoes that will not sink into snow.","3.5","A wide sole gives a large area of contact, so the same weight produces a smaller pressure on the snow and the wearer does not sink in. wide large area smaller pressure"],[11,"y8c03-r025","Short answer","The point of a nail has an area of 1.5 mm2 and needs a pressure of 50 N/mm2 to enter wood. Explain how to find the force the hammer must supply.","3.5","Rearranging pressure = force divided by area gives force = pressure x area, so force = 50 x 1.5 = 75 N. pressure area 75"],[11,"y8c03-r026","Short answer","Water escapes faster from a low hole in a dam than from a high one. Explain why.","3.6","The pressure in the water increases with depth, so the water at the lower hole is under greater pressure and comes out with more force. pressure depth more force"],[11,"y8c03-r027","Short answer","Explain, in terms of particles, why the pressure in a liquid increases with depth.","3.6","Deeper down there is more liquid above you, and the weight of that liquid, caused by gravity, pushes on the particles so that they move with more force. more liquid above weight force"],[11,"y8c03-r028","Short answer","Explain why the pressure at one depth in a liquid is the same in every direction.","3.6","The particles in a liquid are moving randomly in all directions, so they push equally in all directions. random all directions equal"],[11,"y8c03-r029","Short answer","Explain, in terms of particles, why blowing more air into a balloon increases the pressure inside it.","3.6","Each gas particle exerts a small force when it collides with the wall; adding more particles means more collisions each second, so the total force, and therefore the pressure, increases. collide more force"],[11,"y8c03-r030","Short answer","Explain why a metal container is crushed when the air is pumped out of it.","3.6","With the air removed the pressure inside falls close to zero while the atmospheric pressure outside is unchanged, so the outside pressure is no longer balanced and crushes the container. inside outside unbalanced"],[11,"y8c03-r031","Short answer","Explain why the pressure inside a football is higher on a hot day than on a cold day.","3.6","As the temperature rises the particles move faster, so their collisions with the inside of the ball exert more force and the pressure increases. faster collisions more force"],[11,"y8c03-r032","Short answer","Explain why atmospheric pressure decreases as you climb higher.","3.6","Higher up there are fewer gas particles in each cubic metre of air, and there is less air above you whose weight can push down. fewer particles less air above weight"],[11,"y8c03-r033","Short answer","Explain what is meant by diffusion.","3.7","Diffusion is the overall random movement of particles from an area where they are at higher concentration to an area where they are at lower concentration. random higher lower"],[11,"y8c03-r034","Short answer","Explain why the movement of particles does not stop when diffusion stops.","3.7","Diffusion stops when the concentrations are equal, but the individual particles are still moving randomly; there is simply no longer an overall movement in one direction. equal still moving overall"],[11,"y8c03-r035","Short answer","Explain why a smell gets stronger as you walk towards the food.","3.7","The concentration of the gas particles from the food is higher the closer you get to the source, so more of them reach your nose. concentration closer source"],[11,"y8c03-r036","Short answer","Explain why tea diffuses faster in hot water than in cold water.","3.7","A higher temperature makes the particles move faster, so they spread out more quickly. higher temperature faster spread"],[11,"y8c03-r037","Short answer","Explain how the difference in concentration affects how fast diffusion happens.","3.7","The bigger the difference in the concentrations of the particles, the faster the diffusion. bigger difference faster"],[11,"y8c03-r038","Short answer","Arun says that when the orange colour has stopped spreading, the particles in the liquid have stopped moving. Explain whether he is correct.","3.7","He is not correct: the colour stops spreading because the concentrations have become equal, but the particles go on moving randomly all the time. not correct equal still moving"],[11,"y8c03-r039","Short answer","Explain why equal volumes of water were used in all the beakers in the diffusion experiment.","3.7","The volume of water is a control variable; if it were different the ink would have different distances to spread, so you could not tell whether the temperature caused the difference in time. control same temperature"],[11,"y8c03-r040","Short answer","Suggest two improvements to the ink diffusion method that would give better results.","3.7","Use the same volume of ink each time, added the same way; repeat each temperature and take a mean; and agree a clear rule for deciding when the ink has fully spread. same volume repeat mean"],[11,"y8c03-r041","Short answer","One rod is the length of the left feet of 16 people chosen at random. Explain one disadvantage of the rod as a unit of length.","3.2","People's feet are different sizes, so a different group of 16 people would give a different rod, and the unit would not be the same from place to place. different not the same people"],[11,"y8c03-r2001","Short answer","A bicycle's driving force is balanced with the force of friction. Explain what this tells you about its motion.","3.1","Balanced forces do not change motion, so the bicycle is not speeding up or slowing down. It is either stationary or travelling at a constant speed in a straight line, and the rider must keep pedalling simply to keep the driving force equal to friction. balanced no change in motion constant speed straight line"],[11,"y8c03-r2002","Short answer","Describe the effect on a bird's movement of the four forces acting on it while flying.","3.1","If the upward force from the wings balances the bird's weight it stays at the same height, and if the forward force balances air resistance it flies at a constant speed. The bird only rises, falls, speeds up or slows down when one of these pairs becomes unbalanced. upward balances weight same height forward balances air resistance constant speed unbalanced"],[11,"y8c03-r2003","Short answer","Describe two changes to the forces that would make a flying bird go slower.","3.1","Either reduce the forward force produced by the wings, or increase the backward air resistance, for example by spreading the wings and tail wider. Either change leaves a resultant force pointing backwards, which slows the bird down. reduce forward force increase air resistance resultant backwards slows down"],[11,"y8c03-r2004","Short answer","A ball on a string swings in a circle. Explain the direction it moves when the string breaks.","3.1","While the string is intact it pulls the ball towards the pole, and that unbalanced force is what keeps bending the ball's path into a circle. When the string breaks that force disappears, so with no unbalanced force the ball carries straight on in the direction it was moving at that instant. string pulls inwards unbalanced force force disappears straight line"],[11,"y8c03-r2006","Short answer","Arun's journey to school gives one value for speed. Explain why this is an average speed.","3.2","He would not have travelled at exactly the same speed for the whole journey — he starts from rest, slows at corners and crossings, and speeds up in between. The single value is the total distance divided by the total time, which spreads all that variation evenly and gives an average. not constant slows and speeds up total distance total time average"],[11,"y8c03-r2007","Short answer","A car must cover 50 km in 2 hours. Explain why it must go faster than the average speed for part of the journey.","3.2","The average of 25 km/h must be made up of the whole journey, but the car starts from rest and has to stop or slow down at junctions, where its speed is below 25 km/h. To make up for that time it must travel above 25 km/h elsewhere, otherwise the total time would be longer than 2 hours. starts from rest slows at junctions below average must go above make up time"],[11,"y8c03-r2008","Short answer","Explain why the same speed can be written as a number of km/h or a number of m/s.","3.2","Speed is a distance divided by a time, so its unit depends on which units of distance and time you use. Changing from kilometres and hours to metres and seconds changes the number but not the actual speed, which is why the unit must always be given with the answer. distance divided by time depends on the units number changes speed is the same"],[11,"y8c03-r2009","Short answer","A spacecraft reached 260 000 km/h. Explain how you would find how far it travels in 2 hours, and why the unit matters.","3.2","Distance equals speed multiplied by time, so 260 000 × 2 = 520 000 km. It works directly because the speed is in kilometres per hour and the time is in hours; if the time had been in minutes it would have to be converted first, or the answer would be wrong. distance equals speed times time 520 000 km units must match convert"],[11,"y8c03-r2010","Short answer","Explain which of speed, force, mass and weight can be worked out from a distance–time graph.","3.3","Only the speed. The graph shows how far the object has travelled at each time, and the steepness of the line gives the distance covered per unit of time, which is the speed. Nothing on the graph tells you how heavy the object is or what forces act on it. speed steepness distance per time not mass not force"],[11,"y8c03-r2011","Short answer","Explain what a sketch graph is and why the axes still have to be labelled.","3.3","When sketching a graph you only label the axes with quantities; you do not need to scale them or put numbers on them. The labels are still essential because the shape of the line only means something once the reader knows which quantity each axis represents. label the quantities no numbers no scale shape is meaningless without labels"],[11,"y8c03-r2012","Short answer","Sofia walks, stops at a crossing, then continues at a higher speed. Explain how each part appears on a distance–time graph.","3.3","The first part is a straight sloping line. The stop is a horizontal line, because time passes but the distance does not change. Continuing at a higher speed gives a straight line that is steeper than the first, because more distance is covered in the same time. sloping line horizontal when stopped steeper more distance in the same time"],[11,"y8c03-r2013","Short answer","Marcus rides to a friend's house and back at the same constant speed. Explain why the two sloping lines on his graph differ.","3.3","If distance is measured from the park, the outward line slopes upward as the distance from the park increases and the return line slopes downward as it decreases. Both have the same steepness, because he travels at the same constant speed, but in opposite directions. upward downward same steepness same speed opposite direction"],[11,"y8c03-r2014","Short answer","Explain why a longer spanner makes a tight nut easier to turn.","3.4","The moment of a force equals the force multiplied by the distance from the pivot. A longer spanner increases that distance, so the same effort from your hand produces a larger moment, and a larger moment is more likely to be enough to turn the nut. moment force times distance longer distance larger moment"],[11,"y8c03-r2015","Short answer","Explain the condition for a seesaw to balance, and why the heavier person must sit closer to the pivot.","3.4","The seesaw balances when the moments on both sides are equal. Moment is force times distance from the pivot, so a larger weight needs a smaller distance to give the same moment as a smaller weight further out. moments equal force times distance larger weight smaller distance"],[11,"y8c03-r2016","Short answer","An engine exerts a moment of 350 Nm at a wheel of radius 0.35 m. Explain how to find the force at the rim and what the answer means.","3.4","Force equals moment divided by distance, so 350 ÷ 0.35 = 1000 N. That is the force the wheel pushes with at its outside edge, and it shows that a small moment at the centre becomes a large force when the radius is small. moment divided by distance 1000 N at the rim small radius gives large force"],[11,"y8c03-r2017","Short answer","Explain why scientists insist on standard international units when calculating moments.","3.4","The moment equation only gives the right answer if the force is in newtons and the distance in metres, giving newton metres. If people use their own units the numbers cannot be combined or compared, and results from different places would be meaningless to each other. newtons metres newton metres cannot compare meaningless"],[11,"y8c03-r2018","Short answer","Explain why touching the thorns on a rose stem is more dangerous than touching the rest of the stem.","3.5","Pressure is force divided by area. A thorn has a very small area at its point, so even a gentle push produces a very large pressure at that point — large enough to break the skin — while the same push spread over the wide, smooth stem produces a pressure far too small to do any harm. small area force divided by area large pressure breaks the skin"],[11,"y8c03-r2019","Short answer","Explain why putting a large piece of wood under a wheel stops it sinking into sand.","3.5","The weight pressing down is unchanged, but the wood spreads that force over a much larger area of sand. Pressure is force divided by area, so a larger area gives a much smaller pressure, and the sand is no longer pushed aside. same force larger area smaller pressure does not sink"],[11,"y8c03-r2020","Short answer","Explain why the two ends of a drawing pin have such different shapes.","3.5","The flat end is wide so that the pressure on your thumb is small and does not hurt, and the point is extremely narrow so that the same force produces a very large pressure on the board and pushes into it. One object uses both ends of the pressure equation at once. wide end small pressure thumb sharp point large pressure same force"],[11,"y8c03-r2021","Short answer","Some countries measure pressure in pounds per square inch. Explain what this tells you about the unit of pressure in general.","3.5","Pressure is always a force divided by an area, so its unit is always a unit of force divided by a unit of area — newtons per square metre in science, pounds per square inch elsewhere. The quantity is the same; only the units chosen to express it differ. force divided by area unit of force per unit of area same quantity different units"],[11,"y8c03-r2022","Short answer","Pressure acts on a fish in deep water from three different directions. Explain what this shows.","3.6","It shows that pressure in a liquid acts equally in all directions at a given depth, not just downwards. The water particles are moving in all directions and collide with every surface of the fish, so the fish is pushed on from above, below and the sides alike. all directions equally same depth particles collide every surface"],[11,"y8c03-r2023","Short answer","Water comes out of three holes at different heights in a bottle. Explain the conclusion that can be drawn.","3.6","The water from the lowest hole travels furthest, showing it is pushed out hardest. Since the only difference between the holes is their depth below the surface, the conclusion is that the pressure in a liquid increases with depth. lowest travels furthest pushed hardest only depth differs pressure increases with depth"],[11,"y8c03-r2024","Short answer","A sealed balloon is cooled. Explain what happens to the pressure inside it.","3.6","The pressure decreases. Cooling means the air particles have less energy and move more slowly, so they hit the inside of the balloon less often and with less force, and the pressure they produce falls — which is why the balloon also shrinks. less energy move slower hit less often less force pressure falls"],[11,"y8c03-r2025","Short answer","Explain why the pressure in a car tyre rises as the tyre warms up while driving.","3.6","The volume of the tyre barely changes, but the higher temperature gives the air particles inside more energy. They move faster and collide with the tyre wall more often and harder, so the pressure increases even though no more air has been added. higher temperature more energy faster more collisions pressure increases"],[11,"y8c03-r2027","Short answer","Blue ink is added to the bottom of a beaker of water. Explain why all the water eventually turns blue.","3.7","The ink and water particles are all moving randomly. Ink particles move from where there are many of them, at the bottom, to where there are fewer, spreading out through the water by diffusion until they are evenly distributed and the whole beaker looks blue. moving randomly many to fewer diffusion evenly spread"],[11,"y8c03-r2028","Short answer","Explain why the brown bromine gas looks less intense the further it is from the container.","3.7","The bromine particles spread out from the container by diffusion. Further away there are fewer bromine particles in the same volume of air, so less brown colour is seen — the intensity is a measure of how concentrated the particles are. spread out fewer particles same volume less intense"],[11,"y8c03-r2029","Short answer","When a green gas has spread through a bottle, explain whether the movement of particles has stopped.","3.7","It has not. The particles are still moving randomly and still spreading in every direction — what has stopped is any overall change, because as many particles move one way as the other. Diffusion has finished, but movement has not. still moving randomly as many each way no overall change"],[11,"y8c03-o047","Short answer","Give two examples of non-standard units used to measure speed.","3.2","Knots (for ships) and Mach (for aeroplanes). knots Mach"],[11,"y8c03-o048","Short answer","Why do scientists use standard units for measurement?","3.2","To avoid confusion, because the same units are then used in all countries. avoid confusion all countries"],[11,"y8c03-o049","Short answer","What is the standard unit for speed?","3.2","Metres per second. metres per second"],[11,"y8c03-o089","Short answer","What do scientists use graphs for?","3.3","To describe how two variables are related. two variables related"],[11,"y8c03-o090","Short answer","Which quantity is plotted on the vertical axis of a distance–time graph?","3.3","Distance travelled. distance"],[11,"y8c03-o091","Short answer","Which quantity is plotted on the horizontal axis of a distance–time graph?","3.3","Time. time"],[11,"y8c03-o125","Short answer","Give an everyday example of a force causing something to turn.","3.4","Pushing down on a door handle makes the handle turn. door handle turn"],[11,"y8c03-o126","Short answer","What happens when you push down on a bicycle pedal?","3.4","The pedal turns. pedal turns"],[11,"y8c03-o127","Short answer","What name is given to an object that turns when a force is applied to it?","3.4","A lever. lever"],[11,"y8c03-o128","Short answer","What is a pivot?","3.4","The point around which a lever turns. point lever turns"],[11,"y8c03-o158","Short answer","Why does a knife cut modelling clay?","3.5","Because the force pushing down on the clay causes pressure on the clay. force pressure"],[11,"y8c03-o159","Short answer","What can you think of pressure as?","3.5","The pushing effect of a force. pushing effect force"],[11,"y8c03-o160","Short answer","Give one way to increase the pressure when cutting with a knife.","3.5","Increase the force pushing down on the knife. increase force"],[11,"y8c03-o161","Short answer","Give a second way to increase the pressure when cutting with a knife.","3.5","Use a sharper knife, because a sharp knife has a smaller surface area in contact with the clay. sharper smaller surface area"],[11,"y8c03-w020","Multiple choice","What happens to the pressure in a liquid as the depth increases?","3.6","The pressure increases as the depth increases. The pressure decreases as the depth increases. The pressure is the same at every depth. The pressure becomes zero at the bottom."],[11,"y8c03-w024","Multiple choice","What is meant by diffusion?","3.7","The spreading out of particles from where they are more crowded to where they are less crowded. The change of a liquid into a gas at its boiling point. The sinking of heavy particles to the bottom of a liquid. The breaking of particles into smaller pieces."],[11,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the force that pulls an object toward the centre of the Earth?","3.1","Weight Friction Contact force Air resistance"],[11,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","When the ground pushes up on a rock to stop it falling, this force is called the ____ force.","3.1","contact"],[11,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","The unit used to measure the speed of ships is called ____.","3.2","knots"],[11,"qwen-01-11","Multiple choice","Which unit of speed is often used for aeroplanes?","3.2","Mach Knots Metres per second Newtons"],[11,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","On a distance-time graph, what does a horizontal straight line represent?","3.3","Constant speed Increasing speed Stationary or at rest Decreasing distance"],[11,"qwen-02-10","Gap fill","A steeper upward sloping line on a distance-time graph indicates that the object is moving ____ than before.","3.3","faster"],[11,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","The object that turns when a force is applied to it is called a ____.","3.4","lever"],[11,"qwen-04-07","Multiple choice","Why does a camel have large feet?","3.5","To decrease pressure on the sand To increase speed while running To make it harder to see To increase the force on the ground"],[11,"qwen-04-08","Gap fill","Pressure is calculated by dividing force by ____.","3.5","area"],[11,"qwen-05-02","Gap fill","In a liquid, pressure increases as you go deeper because there is more ____ above your position.","3.6","liquid"],[11,"qwen-05-03","Multiple choice","What causes pressure in a gas inside a container like a balloon?","3.6","Gas particles colliding with the walls of the container The weight of the gas pushing down on the bottom The temperature of the air outside the balloon The elastic nature of the rubber stretching"],[11,"qwen-06-02","Gap fill","Concentration is defined as the number of particles in a particular ____.","3.7","volume"],[11,"qwen-07-01","Multiple choice","In the earliest balances, how was the weight of an unknown object measured?","3.4","By adding known weights to the other side until it balanced By moving a single weight along a ruler By comparing the height of two objects By using a spring to stretch under the load"],[11,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","Which of these is a result of unbalanced forces?","3.1","A car moving at constant speed A boat slowing down A book not moving on a desk An object staying still"],[11,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","What does a speed of 60 km/h mean?","3.2","Travels 60 m every second Travels 60 km every hour Travels 60 m every hour Travels 60 km every second"],[11,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","In a distance-time graph, a line that shows no change in distance with time represents an object that is ____.","3.3","stationary"],[11,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the name given to the turning effect of a force?","3.4","moment minute rotate revolve"],[11,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","What happens to the pressure in a liquid as depth increases?","3.6","It decreases It stays the same It increases It becomes zero"],[11,"qwenw-02-10","Gap fill","A sharp knife is better for cutting bread than a dull one because it has a smaller ____.","3.5","area"],[11,"qwenw-03-03","Multiple choice","Why does blue ink spread through water faster when the water is hotter?","3.7","The water particles move slower The ink particles are heavier in hot water The particles have more energy and move faster The water becomes less dense"],[12,"y8c04-f42h1","Click the diagram","Click on the apparatus you would set into the ground to catch beetles that crawl over the surface.","4.2",""],[12,"y8c04-f42h2","Click the diagram","Click on the apparatus you would sweep through long grass to catch insects.","4.2",""],[12,"y8c04-f42h3","Click the diagram","Click on the apparatus you would hold under a branch while a friend hits the branch with a stick.","4.2",""],[12,"y8c04-f42h4","Click the diagram","Click on the apparatus you would use to look closely at a very small organism.","4.2",""],[12,"y8c04-f42h5","Click the diagram","Click on the apparatus you would use to record an organism whose name you do not know, so that you can identify it later.","4.2",""],[12,"y8c04-f42h6","Click the diagram","Click on what you would use to identify the animals and plants that you find.","4.2",""],[12,"y8c04-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the red-tailed hawk in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the rattlesnake in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the collared lizard in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the kangaroo rat in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on the grasshopper in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h6","Click the diagram","Click on the prickly pear cactus in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f01h7","Click the diagram","Click on the brittlebrush in the diagram.","4.1",""],[12,"y8c04-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the cormorants in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the minnows in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the protoctists in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f02h5","Click the diagram","Click on the shrimps in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the bar that shows the mean number of microplastic particles in 2008.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the bar that shows the mean number of microplastic particles in 1996.","4.4",""],[12,"y8c04-m001","Multiple choice","What is an ecosystem?","4.1","A network of interactions between all the living organisms and the non-living things around them The place where one organism naturally lives All the animals that eat the same kind of food The non-living parts of an environment only"],[12,"y8c04-m002","Multiple choice","What is a habitat?","4.1","The place where an organism naturally lives A network of interactions between organisms The food that an organism eats The study of ecosystems"],[12,"y8c04-m011","Multiple choice","A saguaro cactus has no leaves. Which part does it use to make food by photosynthesis?","4.1","Its green stems Its roots Its spines Its flowers"],[12,"y8c04-m012","Multiple choice","Why can saguaro cacti not grow where the temperature falls below 0 degrees Celsius?","4.1","Their cells are killed if they freeze Their roots cannot absorb frozen rain Their spines fall off in the cold They cannot flower in the cold"],[12,"y8c04-m013","Multiple choice","What is unusual about where mangroves can grow?","4.2","They are trees that can grow with their roots in sea water They are trees that grow with no roots at all They are trees that grow only in fresh water They are trees that grow only on dry sand"],[12,"y8c04-m014","Multiple choice","Why do young fish live among the mangrove roots?","4.2","They are safe from larger fish that might eat them The water there is much warmer There is more light among the roots The roots are their only food"],[12,"y8c04-m021","Multiple choice","At a deep-sea hydrothermal vent, which organisms are the producers in the food chain?","4.2","Tiny bacteria Green plants Giant tube worms Shrimps"],[12,"y8c04-m022","Multiple choice","Where do the producers at a hydrothermal vent get their energy from?","4.2","From the chemicals dissolved in the hot water From sunlight reaching the sea bed From the heat of the sea water alone From eating zooplankton"],[12,"y8c04-m024","Multiple choice","What was true about New Zealand before humans arrived?","4.3","There were no predatory mammals there There were no birds there There were no plants there There were no insects there"],[12,"y8c04-m025","Multiple choice","Why were the eggs and young of New Zealand's ground-nesting birds safe before humans arrived?","4.3","There were no predators to eat their eggs The eggs were hidden high in trees The adult birds could fly away with them The eggs had very thick shells"],[12,"y8c04-m033","Multiple choice","Water hyacinth forms such a thick covering on a lake that native plants and fish die. Why?","4.3","Light and oxygen cannot reach the water below The plants poison the water with a toxin The plants eat the fish directly The water becomes too cold for fish"],[12,"y8c04-m037","Multiple choice","DDT does not break down in the environment. What word describes a chemical like this?","4.4","Persistent Soluble Nutritious Temporary"],[12,"y8c04-m038","Multiple choice","Why does DDT stay in the environment for many years?","4.4","It is not broken down by decomposers It is washed away by rain each year It is absorbed and destroyed by plants It evaporates within a few days"],[12,"y8c04-m048","Multiple choice","Why may the concentration of microplastics in a seal's body be greater than in a fish's body?","4.4","The seal takes in the microplastics from all the fish it eats, and they cannot be broken down The seal drinks more sea water than the fish Microplastics are made inside the seal's body The seal lives in colder water than the fish"],[12,"y8c04-m049","Gap fill","An ecosystem is a network of ____ between all the living organisms and the non-living things around them.","4.1","interactions"],[12,"y8c04-m054","Gap fill","There are sharp ____ on the cactus stems, which protect them from being eaten by animals.","4.1","spines"],[12,"y8c04-m055","Gap fill","Mangroves are trees that can grow with their roots in ____ water.","4.2","sea"],[12,"y8c04-m057","Gap fill","At a hydrothermal vent, tiny ____ are the producers in the food chain instead of plants.","4.2","bacteria"],[12,"y8c04-m060","Gap fill","People in New Zealand are trying to ____ stoats, which means to completely get rid of them.","4.3","eradicate"],[12,"y8c04-m063","Gap fill","Water hyacinth makes such a thick covering that light and ____ cannot reach the water below.","4.3","oxygen"],[12,"y8c04-m065","Gap fill","A chemical that does not break down, such as DDT, is described as a ____ chemical.","4.4","persistent"],[12,"y8c04-r001","Short answer","Explain why the plants of the Sonoran Desert must be adapted to survive with very little water.","4.1","Deserts get very little rainfall, so there is hardly any water in the soil for most of the year. An organism with no adaptation for saving or storing water would dry out and die, so only species with such adaptations survive there. little rainfall adaptations survive water"],[12,"y8c04-r002","Short answer","Explain the advantage to a saguaro cactus of having roots that spread out widely just under the soil surface.","4.1","Rain in a desert is rare and soaks only the top of the soil before it evaporates. Wide, shallow roots cover a large area of that thin wet layer, so the cactus can absorb a lot of water quickly in the short time it is available. wide shallow absorb large area rain"],[12,"y8c04-r003","Short answer","Explain how nesting in a teddy bear cholla protects a cactus wren's eggs and young.","4.1","The cholla is so spiky that very few other animals will go near it. Predators that would eat the eggs or the chicks cannot reach the nest, so more of the young survive. spiky predators cannot reach protected"],[12,"y8c04-r004","Short answer","Explain why many desert animals rest in the shade or burrow into the soil during the hot day.","4.1","The air temperature in the open is very high during the day. Shade and soil are cooler, so hiding there stops the animal overheating and losing too much water by evaporation. cooler overheating water loss shade burrow"],[12,"y8c04-r005","Short answer","Kangaroo rats come out to feed at night. Suggest two advantages of being active at night in a desert.","4.1","It is much cooler at night, so the rat loses less water and does not overheat. Many daytime predators are not hunting then, although rattlesnakes and coyotes still are, so the risk of being eaten is lower than in daylight. cooler less water predators night nocturnal"],[12,"y8c04-r006","Short answer","Explain how insects feeding on nectar benefit the desert plants.","4.1","While the insect feeds it picks up pollen and carries it to another flower. This pollinates the plants, so they can reproduce and make seeds. Both organisms gain, so it is an interaction that benefits each of them. pollen pollinate reproduce nectar"],[12,"y8c04-r007","Short answer","Ants collect seeds and store them in their nests. Explain how this can still lead to new plants growing.","4.1","The ants do not eat every seed they store. Some are left uneaten in the nest, and months or years later those seeds germinate and grow into new plants. uneaten germinate new plants store"],[12,"y8c04-r008","Short answer","Explain how being nocturnal helps some desert animals to avoid overheating or drying out.","4.1","The temperature is often very high during the day but much lower at night. An animal that is active only at night is never exposed to the highest temperatures, so it does not overheat and loses less water by evaporation. temperature night overheat drying out"],[12,"y8c04-r009","Short answer","Explain why the air is an important non-living part of the desert ecosystem.","4.1","The air supplies carbon dioxide, which the plants need for photosynthesis to make food, and oxygen, which all the organisms need for respiration to release energy. carbon dioxide photosynthesis oxygen respiration"],[12,"y8c04-r010","Short answer","Explain how kangaroo rats affect the non-living part of their environment.","4.1","Their droppings break down and become part of the soil, adding minerals to it. The gases they take in and give out also change the composition of the air, so the organisms change the environment as well as being affected by it. droppings soil gases air affect"],[12,"y8c04-r011","Short answer","Explain why a food web on its own is not a full description of an ecosystem.","4.1","An ecosystem is the whole network of interactions between all the organisms and the non-living things around them. A food web shows only the feeding interactions, and leaves out things such as nesting sites, pollination, roots holding the soil, and the effects of light, temperature and water. network interactions non-living feeding only"],[12,"y8c04-r012","Short answer","Explain how plant roots help to protect the desert soil.","4.1","The roots hold the soil particles together. When heavy rain falls the soil is not washed away, so the minerals and the habitat for soil organisms stay in place. roots hold soil washed away"],[12,"y8c04-r013","Short answer","Explain why a desert food web could not exist without the producers.","4.1","Producers are the only organisms that make their own food, using energy from sunlight. Every other organism in the web gets its energy by eating plants or by eating animals that ate plants, so with no producers there would be no energy entering the web at all. producers make their own food energy sunlight"],[12,"y8c04-r014","Short answer","Explain what the arrows in a food web represent, and why they point the way they do.","4.1","Each arrow shows the transfer of energy and nutrients from the organism being eaten to the organism that eats it. The arrow points in the direction the energy travels, so it points towards the consumer, not towards the food. energy transfer eaten direction"],[12,"y8c04-r015","Short answer","Explain the difference between an ecosystem and a habitat.","4.1","A habitat is the place where a particular organism naturally lives, such as the inside of a saguaro flower for a sap beetle. An ecosystem is much larger: it is the whole network of interactions between all the organisms in an area and the non-living things around them, and it contains many different habitats. habitat place ecosystem network interactions"],[12,"y8c04-r016","Short answer","Explain how mangrove roots help young fish to survive.","4.2","The tangle of roots is too narrow and cluttered for larger fish to swim through. Young fish can shelter among them, so they are safe from the predators that would otherwise eat them. roots shelter safe larger fish predators"],[12,"y8c04-r017","Short answer","Explain the role of bacteria in the mangrove ecosystem.","4.2","Bacteria decompose the mangrove leaves that fall onto the mud. This breaks the dead material down so that prawns and crabs can feed on it, and returns minerals to the mud for the trees to use again. decompose bacteria dead leaves minerals"],[12,"y8c04-r018","Short answer","Explain how tiny algae can grow on the underside of Arctic sea ice.","4.2","Enough light passes through the ice to reach them, so they can photosynthesise. They are the producers of that ecosystem, even though they are living under the ice. light through the ice photosynthesise producers"],[12,"y8c04-r019","Short answer","Suggest why being a good swimmer is an important adaptation for a polar bear.","4.2","The sea ice is broken into floes with water between them. A bear that can swim well can move from one floe to another to reach new hunting grounds, so it can keep finding seals as the ice shifts. swim ice floe hunting seals"],[12,"y8c04-r020","Short answer","Explain why the water in a rice paddy heats up quickly during the day and cools quickly at night.","4.2","The water is very shallow, so there is only a small mass of water to be warmed or cooled. A small amount of water needs much less energy to change its temperature than a deep body of water, so it changes temperature quickly in both directions. shallow small mass temperature quickly"],[12,"y8c04-r021","Short answer","Explain how adding fertiliser to a paddy field affects the animals that live there.","4.2","The fertiliser makes not only the rice but also the algae grow faster. The algae are producers, so more of them means more food for the animals that eat them, and the numbers of those animals can increase. fertiliser algae producers more food"],[12,"y8c04-r022","Short answer","Explain why plants cannot live at a hydrothermal vent.","4.2","Vents are in the deep ocean where no sunlight reaches. Plants need light for photosynthesis to make their food, so without light they cannot survive there. deep ocean no light photosynthesis cannot"],[12,"y8c04-r023","Short answer","Explain how the producers at a hydrothermal vent obtain their energy, and why this is unusual.","4.2","The bacteria use energy in the chemicals dissolved in the hot water instead of energy from light. Almost every other food chain on Earth starts with a producer that uses sunlight, so this is a food chain that does not depend on the Sun at all. bacteria chemicals instead of light energy"],[12,"y8c04-r024","Short answer","Explain how the giant tube worms and the bacteria that live inside them both benefit.","4.2","The worm provides a habitat for the bacteria, a safe place to live with a supply of the chemicals they need. In return the worm uses some of the food that the bacteria make, so each organism gains from the interaction. habitat food both benefit bacteria"],[12,"y8c04-r025","Short answer","Ecologists compared coral reefs with mangroves on the shore and reefs without. Explain what this comparison was designed to find out.","4.2","It tests whether the presence of mangroves affects the numbers of fish on the nearby reef. The reefs without mangroves act as the comparison, so any difference in fish mass can be linked to the mangroves rather than to the reef itself. compare mangroves numbers of fish control"],[12,"y8c04-r026","Short answer","Suggest why coral reefs near mangroves may have a greater mass of fish than reefs with no mangroves.","4.2","Baby fish spend their early lives among the mangrove roots, safe from larger fish that would eat them. More of them survive to adulthood and then move out onto the reef, so the reef next to mangroves has more and larger fish. baby fish roots safe survive reef"],[12,"y8c04-r027","Short answer","Explain why the species that developed in New Zealand are different from those elsewhere on Earth.","4.3","New Zealand became separated from all other land about 66 million years ago. After that, organisms could not move between New Zealand and other places, so the species there changed on their own and became different from species anywhere else. separated 66 million isolated different"],[12,"y8c04-r028","Short answer","Explain why so many New Zealand birds nest on the ground and some cannot fly.","4.3","Before humans arrived there were no predatory mammals in New Zealand, so eggs and young birds on the ground were safe and there was no advantage in being able to fly away. Birds without those costly abilities survived just as well, so ground nesting and flightlessness became common. no predators safe ground cannot fly"],[12,"y8c04-r029","Short answer","Explain why the arrival of rats was so damaging to New Zealand's native birds.","4.3","The rats eat birds' eggs and defenceless young birds. The native birds had evolved with no predatory mammals, so they had no adaptations or behaviour to protect their nests, and huge numbers of eggs and chicks were lost. rats eggs young birds no defences"],[12,"y8c04-r030","Short answer","People brought stoats to New Zealand to control rabbits. Explain why this was not a good solution.","4.3","The stoats did not stay with the rabbits. They are fierce hunters that breed rapidly, so they spread all over New Zealand and killed native birds as well, making species such as the laughing owl and the New Zealand thrush extinct. stoats spread breed rapidly native birds extinct"],[12,"y8c04-r031","Short answer","Suggest why it is very difficult to eradicate an introduced species once it has settled into a new place.","4.3","It has usually spread over a very large area and breeds rapidly, so any survivors quickly build the numbers back up. There are also few predators or diseases in the new place to keep it under control, and any method used to kill it may harm native species too. spread breed rapidly few predators survivors"],[12,"y8c04-r032","Short answer","Explain how buffelgrass could harm the native plants of the Sonoran Desert.","4.3","Buffelgrass takes water and nutrients from the soil. In a desert both are already in short supply, so there is less left for the native plants, and slow-growing species such as the saguaro cactus may not survive. water nutrients soil compete native"],[12,"y8c04-r033","Short answer","Buffelgrass could reduce the number of saguaro cacti. Suggest how this would affect other desert species.","4.3","Many animals depend on the cactus: woodpeckers make nest holes in the stems, elf owls use the old holes, hawks nest where the arms branch, bats feed on the nectar and many animals eat the fruits. Fewer cacti means fewer nest sites and less food, so the numbers of all those species would fall. nest holes food fruits numbers fall depend"],[12,"y8c04-r034","Short answer","Explain how beavers change the habitat they live in.","4.3","They cut down trees with their sharp teeth and use them to build dams. The dams slow the river water and form deep, wide pools, so fast shallow river is replaced by still deep water and the trees along the bank are lost. dams slow pools cut down trees"],[12,"y8c04-r035","Short answer","Some people suggest introducing brown bears to control the beavers in Tierra del Fuego. Suggest why this might not be a good idea.","4.3","The bear would itself be an introduced species with no predators there. It may not eat only beavers, and could attack native animals or livestock, so the plan risks repeating exactly the problem it is meant to solve. introduced species no predators eat native repeat the problem"],[12,"y8c04-r036","Short answer","Explain why water hyacinths are not an invasive species in South America.","4.3","South America is where they live naturally, and several herbivores there feed on them. Being eaten keeps the numbers down, so the plants never multiply enough to take over the water. native herbivores feed on them numbers controlled"],[12,"y8c04-r037","Short answer","Explain why a thick covering of water hyacinth kills the organisms living in the water below.","4.3","The covering stops light and oxygen reaching the water underneath. Without light the water plants and algae cannot photosynthesise, and without oxygen the animals cannot respire, so the organisms below die. light oxygen photosynthesise respire"],[12,"y8c04-r038","Short answer","Explain how water hyacinth causes problems for the fishing communities on Lake Victoria.","4.3","There is so much of the plant that the fishing boats cannot move through it, and the nets fill with water hyacinth instead of fish. People who depend on fishing lose both their food and their income. boats cannot move nets not fish"],[12,"y8c04-r039","Short answer","Suggest why cane toad numbers have increased rapidly in Australia while staying fairly constant in South America.","4.3","In South America they have many predators that keep the numbers under control. In Australia the predators that eat cane toads often die, so almost nothing kills the toads and their numbers rise quickly. predators control Australia die increase"],[12,"y8c04-r040","Short answer","Scientists feed northern quolls small sausages of cane toad meat that make them vomit. Explain how this is meant to save the quolls.","4.3","The sausage is small enough to make the quoll sick without killing it. The scientists hope the quoll will learn to connect the taste of cane toad with feeling ill and avoid eating them, so it will not take a fatal dose from a real toad. sick not kill learn avoid"],[12,"y8c04-r041","Short answer","When planning an experiment to test whether trained quolls survive better, name the variables the scientists should change, measure and keep the same.","4.3","They should change whether the quoll has been fed the toad sausages or not. They should measure how many quolls in each group are still alive after a set time, or how many eat a cane toad. They should keep the same the area the quolls are released into, the number and age of quolls in each group, and the length of the study. change measure keep the same variables"],[12,"y8c04-r042","Short answer","Explain why DDT was thought to be safe when it was first used.","4.4","It was used to kill insects that spread diseases such as malaria and typhus, and insects that eat crops, and it worked very well. No one had tested what it did to other animals, so no one thought it could harm organisms other than insects. insects disease no one thought other organisms"],[12,"y8c04-r043","Short answer","Explain why Rachel Carson's book Silent Spring was important.","4.4","She described how DDT was killing not only mosquitoes but also birds. The book made many people realise that insecticides can be very harmful to the environment, which changed public opinion and led scientists to investigate how the harm happens. Silent Spring birds harmful environment"],[12,"y8c04-r044","Short answer","Explain why the fact that DDT stays in the environment for many years makes it so harmful.","4.4","Because it does not break down, every amount ever sprayed is still present and keeps building up. It can also be blown long distances in the air, so it reaches organisms far from where it was used, and once it is inside an animal it stays there for the animal's whole life. does not break down builds up long distances whole life"],[12,"y8c04-r045","Short answer","Explain what bioaccumulation means, using DDT as your example.","4.4","Bioaccumulation is the build-up of a substance inside the body of a single organism over its lifetime. DDT never breaks down, so every bit an animal takes in stays in its body, and the longer it lives and the more it takes in, the more DDT it has. build-up one organism lifetime never breaks down"],[12,"y8c04-r046","Short answer","Explain why the concentration of DDT increases as you go up a food chain.","4.4","Each shrimp eats a large quantity of algae and keeps all the DDT from every one of them. A fish then eats many shrimps and keeps all of that DDT, and a cormorant eats many fish. At each step the DDT from a large mass of food is concentrated into a much smaller mass of predator, so the concentration rises along the chain. all the DDT many concentrated up the chain"],[12,"y8c04-r047","Short answer","Explain why a cormorant has a much higher concentration of DDT in its body than a minnow.","4.4","The cormorant is further up the food chain. It eats many fish over its life, and each of those fish had already accumulated the DDT from many shrimps, which had accumulated it from many protoctists. All of that DDT ends up in the cormorant and none of it breaks down. further up eats many accumulated none breaks down"],[12,"y8c04-r048","Short answer","Explain how DDT in a female bird's body can stop her eggs hatching.","4.4","DDT makes the shells of the eggs very thin and easy to break. The eggs crack or are crushed before the chicks are ready, so they do not hatch, and the number of young birds falls. thin shells break do not hatch fewer young"],[12,"y8c04-r049","Short answer","Explain why some microplastics end up in the mud at the bottom of the sea.","4.4","Some microplastics are denser than sea water, so instead of floating they slowly sink and settle on the sea bed, where they become part of the mud. denser sink sea bed mud"],[12,"y8c04-r050","Short answer","Explain why fish that filter zooplankton out of sea water may end up with microplastics in their bodies.","4.4","Microplastics float in the water at the same size as the zooplankton. The fish sucks in sea water and filters out everything of that size, so it takes in the plastic particles accidentally along with its food. filter float accidentally with their food"],[12,"y8c04-r051","Short answer","Explain why the concentration of microplastics in a seal's body may be greater than in a fish's body.","4.4","Microplastics cannot be broken down by living things, so they stay in the body. A seal eats many fish over its life and keeps the microplastics from every one of them, so the plastic from a large mass of fish is concentrated in one seal. cannot be broken down eats many fish concentrated stays"],[12,"y8c04-r052","Short answer","The farmers think the chemical company's information may be biased. Explain why.","4.4","The company makes money from selling the spray, so it gains if the farmers believe it is safe and effective. Information from someone with something to gain may leave out unfavourable results, so it should be checked against an independent source. makes money something to gain leave out independent"],[12,"y8c04-r053","Short answer","The predators being sold are said to eat only one species of insect. Suggest what problems could arise if they in fact eat other insects too.","4.4","They would reduce the numbers of harmless insects as well, including pollinators and the prey of other animals. That would disturb the local food webs, and the predators themselves could become an invasive species with no natural control. other insects pollinators food webs invasive"],[12,"y8c04-r054","Short answer","Suggest why animals higher up the food chain are at more risk from a chemical insect spray.","4.4","The spray does not break down quickly, so it stays in the bodies of the insects that take it in. Each predator eats many of those insects and keeps all the chemical, so the concentration is higher at every step and the animals at the top receive the largest dose. does not break down eats many concentration highest top"],[12,"y8c04-r055","Short answer","The government agriculture department also gives the farmers advice. Explain whether the farmers can be sure this advice is unbiased.","4.4","They cannot be certain. A government department has no product to sell, so it has less reason to be biased than the chemical company, but it may still have its own policies or interests. The safest approach is to compare advice from several independent sources, such as the university developing the resistant maize. cannot be certain no product to sell less reason compare sources"],[12,"y8c04-o001","Short answer","Why is it difficult for animals and plants to live in a desert?","4.1","Deserts get very little rainfall, so organisms must survive with very little water. little rainfall water"],[12,"y8c04-o002","Short answer","What must desert organisms have in order to survive with little water?","4.1","Adaptations that help them to survive with little water. adaptations"],[12,"y8c04-o003","Short answer","In which country is the Sonoran Desert that you studied?","4.1","The USA - it is in Arizona. USA Arizona"],[12,"y8c04-o004","Short answer","Name the tall cacti that grow in the Sonoran Desert.","4.1","Saguaro cacti. saguaro"],[12,"y8c04-o037","Short answer","Sofia says an ecosystem is more than just a place. Is she right? Explain.","4.1","Yes. An ecosystem is the network of interactions between all the living organisms and the non-living things around them, not simply the place itself. network interactions not just a place"],[12,"y8c04-o055","Short answer","Explain the difference between a habitat and an ecosystem.","4.1","A habitat is the place where an organism naturally lives. An ecosystem is the network of interactions between all the organisms and the non-living things around them. place network interactions"],[12,"y8c04-o058","Short answer","Give two ways in which the saguaro cactus is adapted to live in the desert.","4.1","Its roots spread out widely just below the surface so it can capture a lot of water when it rains, and it has no leaves, so less water evaporates. roots spread out no leaves water"],[12,"y8c04-o069","Short answer","Name three ecosystems other than the desert that you have studied.","4.2","Mangrove forests, sea ice in the Arctic Ocean, and a rice paddy. mangrove sea ice rice paddy"],[12,"y8c04-o070","Short answer","What is unusual about where mangrove trees grow?","4.2","They can grow with their roots in sea water. roots sea water"],[12,"y8c04-o071","Short answer","Where in the world are mangrove forests found?","4.2","Along the coasts of many tropical countries. coasts tropical"],[12,"y8c04-o073","Short answer","What do mud skippers do when the tide is out?","4.2","They climb out onto the mud and feed on whatever they can find. climb out mud feed"],[12,"y8c04-o074","Short answer","What happens to the mangrove leaves that fall onto the mud?","4.2","They are decomposed by bacteria. decomposed bacteria"],[12,"y8c04-o075","Short answer","Which animals eat the partly decomposed mangrove leaves?","4.2","Prawns and crabs. prawns crabs"],[12,"y8c04-o076","Short answer","What do crab-eating macaques feed on in a mangrove forest?","4.2","Crabs, which they catch on the tree roots and mud. crabs roots mud"],[12,"y8c04-o077","Short answer","Why does some of the sea water in the Arctic Ocean freeze in winter?","4.2","Because it gets so cold there. so cold freezes"],[12,"y8c04-o078","Short answer","Why do seals have to come to the surface of the Arctic Ocean?","4.2","To breathe air. breathe air"],[12,"y8c04-o079","Short answer","What do polar bears look for when they patrol the sea ice?","4.2","Seals to kill and eat. seals"],[12,"y8c04-o129","Short answer","When a new species arrives in an ecosystem, what two things do ecologists need to work out?","4.3","How the new species fits into the network of interactions, and how this affects the species already there. fits into network affects"],[12,"y8c04-o130","Short answer","How long ago did New Zealand become separated from other areas of land?","4.3","About 66 million years ago. 66 million years"],[12,"y8c04-o160","Short answer","Why are stoats a problem in New Zealand but not in Europe?","4.3","In Europe there are predators, diseases and competitors that keep their numbers down, and the prey species have adaptations to escape them. In New Zealand these controls are missing. predators numbers adaptations"],[12,"y8c04-o165","Short answer","What has happened to the beavers in Tierra del Fuego since 1946?","4.3","No beavers are used for fur now, but they still live there, their numbers have increased, and their dams have caused a lot of damage to local ecosystems. numbers increased dams damage"],[12,"y8c04-o167","Short answer","Suggest why introducing brown bears to Tierra del Fuego might be a bad idea.","4.3","The bears would themselves be an introduced species, and they might eat native animals or cause other damage rather than only controlling the beavers. introduced species native animals damage"],[12,"y8c04-o179","Short answer","How are scientists trying to save the northern quoll?","4.3","They feed quolls little sausages of cane toad meat, which make the quolls vomit but do not kill them, so the quolls learn to avoid cane toads. sausages vomit learn to avoid"],[12,"y8c04-o184","Short answer","What is an insecticide?","4.4","A chemical that kills insects. kills insects"],[12,"y8c04-o185","Short answer","When was DDT first produced?","4.4","In the 1940s. 1940s"],[12,"y8c04-o186","Short answer","What was DDT originally used for?","4.4","To kill insects that transmit diseases, and insects that eat crops. transmit diseases eat crops"],[12,"y8c04-o187","Short answer","Which two diseases were controlled by killing insects with DDT?","4.4","Malaria, transmitted by mosquitoes, and typhus, transmitted by fleas. malaria mosquitoes typhus fleas"],[12,"y8c04-o209","Short answer","Explain the difference between bioaccumulation and biomagnification.","4.4","Bioaccumulation is the build-up of a substance inside one organism during its lifetime. Biomagnification is the increase in concentration from one level of a food chain to the next. one organism food chain concentration"],[12,"y8c04-o222","Short answer","Where do microplastics come from?","4.4","From big pieces of thrown-away plastic that slowly break apart, and from healthcare and beauty products such as toothpastes and face creams. break apart toothpastes face creams"],[12,"y8c04-o227","Short answer","Explain why the concentration of microplastics in a seal may be greater than in a fish.","4.4","The seal eats many fish over its lifetime and keeps all the microplastics from all of them, so the concentration increases up the food chain. eats many fish increases food chain"],[12,"y8c04-w038","Gap fill","Microplastics cannot be broken down by living things, so they build up inside one animal's body over its lifetime. This build-up is called ____.","4.4","bioaccumulation"],[12,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the main reason organisms in a desert need special adaptations?","4.1","To find enough food Because there is little water To avoid all predators The soil is too rocky"],[12,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Saguaro cacti grow very slowly and their roots spread out widely just underneath the soil to absorb ____.","4.1","rain"],[12,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which bird makes holes in cacti to create its nest?","4.1","Cactus wren Gila woodpecker Red-tailed hawk Barn owl"],[12,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Mangroves are trees that can grow with their roots in ____.","4.2","sea water"],[12,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What do young fish use mangrove roots for?","4.2","To hide from larger fish To find food To build nests To breathe air"],[12,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","Why does the water in rice paddies heat up quickly during the day?","4.2","Because it is shallow Because it is deep Because of the fertiliser Because of the birds"],[12,"qwen-05-01","Multiple choice","Why were many native birds in New Zealand unable to protect their eggs before humans arrived?","4.3","They did not have feathers There were no predatory mammals to eat the eggs The birds lived in trees only The eggs were too small for predators"],[12,"qwen-05-02","Gap fill","Rats stowed away on boats and now eat birds' eggs and ____ young birds.","4.3","defenceless"],[12,"qwen-05-05","Multiple choice","What is the main reason scientists think 53 native bird species in New Zealand became extinct?","4.3","People hunted them for sport Introduced invasive species caused the extinctions The climate changed too quickly They ran out of food sources"],[12,"qwen-05-06","Gap fill","DDT is a ____ chemical, which means it does not break down and stays in the environment for many years.","4.4","persistent"],[12,"qwen-05-07","Multiple choice","How does DDT affect the eggs of birds like the ibis?","4.4","It makes them change colour It makes the shells very thin and easy to break It prevents the eggs from being laid It makes the eggs taste bad"],[12,"qwen-06-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the process where DDT builds up in an organism's body over its lifetime?","4.4","Biomagnification Bioaccumulation Photosynthesis Decomposition"],[12,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Why do saguaro cacti not have leaves?","4.1","To reduce water loss through evaporation Because they do not need to make food To protect them from freezing temperatures So that birds can build nests in the stems"],[12,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Saguaro cacti use their green stems to make food by the process of ____.","4.1","photosynthesis"],[12,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","In the tropical rainforest, fungi on the forest floor are known as ____.","4.2","decomposers"],[12,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Where do bacteria at hydrothermal vents get their energy from?","4.2","Chemicals dissolved in the hot water Sunlight shining through the ocean Eating giant tube worms Decomposing dead fish"],[12,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","Beavers use their sharp teeth to cut down trees to build ____.","4.3","dams"],[12,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","Why are water hyacinths not an invasive species in South America?","4.3","Herbivores feed on them and control their numbers They do not grow in fresh water there The temperature is too cold for them to survive They are eaten by all the local fish"],[12,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","Farmers need to check if the advice they receive is ____.","4.4","unbiased"],[12,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","The university is developing a new strain of ____ that resists insect pests.","4.4","maize"],[13,"y8c05-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the proton in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the nucleus in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the neutron in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the electron in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the positively charged matter in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the electrons in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the particle source in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the gold foil in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the particle detector in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f03h4","Click the diagram","Click on the particles that have passed through the foil in the diagram.","5.1",""],[13,"y8c05-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the pure diamond.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the yellow diamond.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the blue diamond.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the sodium chloride in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the burette in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the conical flask in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f07a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the delivery tube in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f07h7","Click the diagram","Click on the rubber bung in the diagram.","5.2",""],[13,"y8c05-f08a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph taken in Scotland.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph taken in Namibia.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f08h3","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph taken in New Zealand.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f08h4","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph taken in Canada.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f08h5","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph taken in Republic of Ireland.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f08h6","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph taken in Bangladesh.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f09a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.3",""],[13,"y8c05-f10a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.4",""],[13,"y8c05-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the Depth column of the pollen diagram.","5.4",""],[13,"y8c05-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the Core column of the pollen diagram.","5.4",""],[13,"y8c05-f10h3","Click the diagram","Click on the Years ago column of the pollen diagram.","5.4",""],[13,"y8c05-f10h4","Click the diagram","Click on the Environment column of the pollen diagram.","5.4",""],[13,"y8c05-f10h5","Click the diagram","Click on the Climate cycle column of the pollen diagram.","5.4",""],[13,"y8c05-f11a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f11h3","Click the diagram","Click on the carbon dioxide in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f12a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f12h2","Click the diagram","Click on the oxygen in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f12h3","Click the diagram","Click on the nitrogen in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f13a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f13h1","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph of a container ship burning diesel.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f13h2","Click the diagram","Click on the photograph of cars burning petrol.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f14a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f14h1","Click the diagram","Click on the atmosphere in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f14h2","Click the diagram","Click on the Earth in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f15a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-f15h1","Click the diagram","Click on the thermometer in the diagram.","5.5",""],[13,"y8c05-m001","Multiple choice","The word 'atom' comes from a Greek word. What does that Greek word mean?","5.1","Cannot be split Very small Cannot be seen Made of charge"],[13,"y8c05-m027","Multiple choice","What does it mean if an element is pure?","5.2","Every one of its atoms is exactly the same and made of just one type of atom It contains at least two different types of atom It has been heated until all the liquid has evaporated It has no electrical charge"],[13,"y8c05-m035","Multiple choice","In the marking of gold, what does one carat stand for?","5.2","One twenty-fourth One hundredth One thousandth One twelfth"],[13,"y8c05-m047","Multiple choice","What do people mean by weather?","5.3","The state of the atmosphere and its changes from hour to hour or day to day The average conditions of a place over more than 30 years The study of the atmosphere The main climate zones on Earth"],[13,"y8c05-m055","Multiple choice","What is a period when so much of the Earth is frozen called?","5.4","A glacial period An interglacial period An ice age zone A temperate period"],[13,"y8c05-m072","Multiple choice","What is the atmosphere?","5.5","A layer of gas above the Earth's surface The solid crust of the Earth The water in the Earth's oceans The molten rock inside the Earth"],[13,"y8c05-m099","Gap fill","The protons and neutrons grouped closely together in the centre of an atom form the ____ of the atom.","5.1","nucleus"],[13,"y8c05-m109","Gap fill","The carat number of a piece of gold is a measure of its ____.","5.2","purity"],[13,"y8c05-m116","Gap fill","Climate means the weather of a place over a much longer time, usually more than ____ years.","5.3","30 thirty"],[13,"y8c05-m120","Gap fill","The word 'glacial' means ____.","5.4","frozen"],[13,"y8c05-m126","Gap fill","The atmosphere is a layer of ____ above the Earth's surface.","5.5","gas"],[13,"y8c05-r001","Short answer","The word 'atom' comes from a Greek word meaning 'cannot be split'. Explain why that name is now misleading.","5.1","Scientists have since discovered that atoms are made up of even smaller particles — protons, neutrons and electrons. The atom can be split into these sub-atomic particles, so the original name no longer describes what an atom really is. sub-atomic particles protons neutrons electrons can be split"],[13,"y8c05-r002","Short answer","Explain why almost all the mass of an atom is in the nucleus.","5.1","Protons and neutrons have much more mass than electrons, and both of them are grouped closely together in the centre of the atom. The electrons outside contribute very little mass, so nearly all of the atom's mass is in the nucleus. protons neutrons more mass centre nucleus"],[13,"y8c05-r003","Short answer","Explain what holds an atom together.","5.1","The protons in the nucleus carry a positive charge and the electrons carry a negative charge. Opposite charges attract, so this electrostatic attraction pulls the electrons towards the nucleus and holds the atom together. positive negative attract electrostatic holds"],[13,"y8c05-r004","Short answer","Explain why a helium atom with 2 protons and 2 electrons has no overall charge.","5.1","The negative charge on one electron is exactly the same size as the positive charge on one proton. With two of each, the total positive charge and the total negative charge are equal, so they cancel out and the atom is neutral. same size equal cancel neutral no overall charge"],[13,"y8c05-r005","Short answer","An atom contains a lot of completely empty space. Explain how Rutherford's gold foil experiment supports this.","5.1","Most of the fast-moving particles passed straight through the thin gold foil without being deflected at all. If the atoms had been solid throughout, the particles would have been stopped or deflected, so the fact that they passed through shows that most of an atom is empty space. passed straight through not deflected empty space gold foil"],[13,"y8c05-r006","Short answer","Only about 1 in 8000 particles was deflected in Rutherford's experiment. Explain what this told him about the nucleus.","5.1","Something small, dense and positively charged must be present to push those few particles off course. Because so few were deflected, that region has to be very tiny compared with the whole atom, so the atom has a very small nucleus with the mass concentrated in it. very few small dense positive nucleus"],[13,"y8c05-r007","Short answer","Explain how Thomson's 'plum pudding' model of the atom differs from the model scientists use today.","5.1","In Thompson's model the electrons were scattered randomly throughout the whole structure of the atom, like fruit in a pudding, and there was no nucleus. In the modern model the protons and neutrons are grouped together in a tiny central nucleus and the electrons move around outside it, with empty space in between. scattered throughout no nucleus modern nucleus in the centre electrons outside"],[13,"y8c05-r008","Short answer","Explain why other scientists repeat an experiment after a new discovery is published.","5.1","The discovery is only trusted once it can be checked independently. Other scientists look closely at the findings to see whether they can repeat the experiment and get the same results, and some actively look for mistakes or wrong conclusions, so errors are found before the idea becomes accepted. repeat check mistakes trusted same results"],[13,"y8c05-r009","Short answer","Explain what is meant by saying that each scientist 'builds on the ideas and discoveries of others'.","5.1","No one scientist produced the modern model of the atom. Thompson found the electron, Rutherford then found the proton and the nucleus, and Chadwick found the neutron. Each one used the previous model as a starting point and changed the part that the new evidence showed was wrong. Thomson Rutherford Chadwick starting point new evidence"],[13,"y8c05-r010","Short answer","Scientists still work to improve our understanding of the atom. Explain why a scientific model is never simply 'finished'.","5.1","A model is our best current explanation of the evidence we have. New experiments, such as those using the Large Hadron Collider, can produce evidence the old model cannot explain, so the model has to be changed. There is still a lot we do not know about atoms. best current new evidence changed do not know"],[13,"y8c05-r011","Short answer","A student writes 'Electrons are found in the nucleus of the atom.' Explain why this statement is wrong.","5.1","The nucleus contains only the protons and the neutrons, which are grouped closely together in the centre. The electrons are outside the nucleus, moving in the space around it, and they are attracted to it by the opposite charges. nucleus protons and neutrons outside electrons"],[13,"y8c05-r012","Short answer","Explain what a scientist means by a 'pure element'.","5.2","Every single atom in the sample is exactly the same and is of just one type of atom. If atoms of any other element are present, even in tiny numbers, the sample is a mixture and not a pure element. every atom the same one type no other"],[13,"y8c05-r013","Short answer","Explain why 18 carat gold is not pure gold.","5.2","Carats measure how many parts out of 24 are gold. In 18 carat gold only 18 of the 24 parts are gold; the other 6 parts are other metals such as silver or copper, so it is an alloy rather than a pure element. 18 out of 24 other metals alloy not pure"],[13,"y8c05-r014","Short answer","Explain why samples of gold of different carats have slightly different colours.","5.2","The lower the carat, the more of the sample is made of other metals such as copper or silver. Those metals have their own colours, so as their proportion rises the colour shifts from yellow-gold towards a coppery tone. lower carat other metals copper colour changes"],[13,"y8c05-r015","Short answer","Explain why silver marked 900 is less pure than silver marked 925.","5.2","The mark gives the number of parts per thousand that are silver. In 925 silver, 925 atoms out of every 1000 are silver, but in 900 silver only 900 are, so 900 silver contains more atoms of other metals and is less pure. parts per thousand 925 900 more other metals"],[13,"y8c05-r016","Short answer","Explain why a diamond can be yellow even though diamond is made of carbon.","5.2","The colour comes from atoms of other elements mixed in with the carbon. If just a few carbon atoms per million are replaced by nitrogen, the diamond looks yellow; a pure diamond with no other elements is colourless. other elements nitrogen few per million pure is colourless"],[13,"y8c05-r017","Short answer","Explain why green diamonds are the rarest kind.","5.2","A green diamond forms only when about one carbon atom in every thousand is replaced by nitrogen, nickel or hydrogen. That particular combination and proportion of impurities happens very seldom in nature, so green diamonds are rare. one in 1000 nitrogen nickel hydrogen rare"],[13,"y8c05-r018","Short answer","Explain why evaporating seawater does not give pure sodium chloride.","5.2","Sodium chloride is not the only salt dissolved in seawater. When the water evaporates, all the dissolved salts are left behind together, so the solid contains magnesium chloride, sodium sulfate, calcium chloride and others as well. not the only salt all left behind magnesium chloride mixture"],[13,"y8c05-r019","Short answer","1000 g of seawater contains about 350 g of salts, of which 68% is sodium chloride. Explain how to find the mass of sodium chloride, and why the answer is not 350 g.","5.2","You take 68% of the 350 g of salts, which gives about 238 g. It is not 350 g because the other 32% of the salts is made up of magnesium chloride, sodium sulfate, calcium chloride and other salts, which are also left behind when the water evaporates. 68% 350 g 238 other salts"],[13,"y8c05-r020","Short answer","Explain how the name of a salt tells you which acid it was made from.","5.2","The second part of the name comes from the acid. A salt whose name ends in chloride was made using hydrochloric acid, and one ending in sulfate was made using sulfuric acid, so sodium chloride and sodium sulfate come from different acids. name chloride hydrochloric sulfate sulfuric"],[13,"y8c05-r021","Short answer","Explain why the product of a chemical reaction may still be impure even after the reaction is complete.","5.2","If there is more than one product, they are mixed together and have to be separated. The products may also be mixed with leftover reactants if not all of them were used up, so extra purification work is needed to get one pure substance. more than one product mixed leftover reactants separate"],[13,"y8c05-r022","Short answer","Explain why the reactants must be measured carefully in a neutralisation reaction.","5.2","If too much acid or too much alkali is added, the extra reactant is left over in the mixture with the salt. Measuring carefully, and using an indicator to see the end point, means both reactants are used up and the product is not contaminated. measure left over contaminated indicator"],[13,"y8c05-r023","Short answer","A shop assistant says a lower-carat gold ring 'looks almost the same but is harder'. Explain how you would decide whether this is science or opinion.","5.2","The claim can be tested with data. Hardness values for gold of different purities can be measured and plotted against the percentage of gold, and if the graph shows lower-carat gold really is harder then the advice is based on science. 'Looks almost the same' is a judgement, so that part is opinion. tested data hardness opinion judgement"],[13,"y8c05-r024","Short answer","9 carat gold is not always harder than 14 or 18 carat gold. Suggest a variable, other than the percentage of gold, that could explain this.","5.2","The alloys do not all contain the same other metals. Copper and silver have different hardnesses, so an alloy with more copper could be harder than one with less gold but more silver. The type of metal mixed in is a second variable affecting hardness. which metals copper silver second variable"],[13,"y8c05-r025","Short answer","Explain the difference between weather and climate.","5.3","Weather is the state of the atmosphere and how it changes from minute to minute, hour to hour or day to day. Climate is the average weather of a place over a much longer time, usually more than 30 years, so weather can change in minutes but climate takes several years to change. state of the atmosphere changes quickly average 30 years long time"],[13,"y8c05-r026","Short answer","Explain why climate information includes the range of extreme weather as well as the normal weather.","5.3","The average alone does not tell you what a place is really like to live in. Two places with the same average temperature could have very different extremes, so knowing the range of extremes shows what conditions people and organisms there must be able to survive. average not enough extremes range"],[13,"y8c05-r027","Short answer","Explain why scientists take careful measurements of the weather.","5.3","Only by recording temperature, precipitation and the other factors regularly and accurately can they build up the long record needed to describe the climate, spot how it is changing, and make useful forecasts. A single casual observation would not show any pattern. record regularly long record pattern forecast"],[13,"y8c05-r028","Short answer","In a study of temperature in Reykjavik the temperature was taken in the same place every six hours. Explain why this matters.","5.3","Keeping the place and the timing the same means those variables cannot affect the results. Any change in the readings must then be a real change in the weather rather than a change in where or when the measurement was made, so the readings can fairly be compared. same place same times fair comparison real change"],[13,"y8c05-r029","Short answer","Heavy rain fell all day on 28 July. Suggest how this could have affected the temperature readings for that day.","5.3","Rain usually comes with thick cloud, which blocks some of the Sun's energy from reaching the ground. Less energy arriving, and water evaporating from wet surfaces, would tend to make the daytime temperature lower than on a clear day. cloud blocks the Sun evaporating lower"],[13,"y8c05-r030","Short answer","One week of weather data was collected in Reykjavik. Explain why this is not enough to describe Iceland's climate.","5.3","Climate is the average weather over more than 30 years. One week can easily be unusually warm, cold or wet, so it shows the weather at that moment rather than the long-term pattern. Many years of data are needed before a climate can be described. one week 30 years unusual long-term"],[13,"y8c05-r031","Short answer","Explain why the period when Yuka was alive is called a glacial period.","5.4","So much of the Earth's surface was frozen then. Ice sheets covered far more land than today, spreading much further from the poles, and that spread of ice is what defines a glacial period. frozen ice sheets covered further from the poles"],[13,"y8c05-r032","Short answer","Explain the difference between a glacial period and an interglacial period.","5.4","In an interglacial period there is permanent ice only close to the North and South Poles. In a glacial period the ice spreads much further south from the North Pole and further north from the South Pole, so much more of the Earth is covered. interglacial only near the poles glacial spreads further"],[13,"y8c05-r033","Short answer","Explain the difference between a glacial period and an ice age.","5.4","An ice age is a very long stretch of time during which the Earth has permanent ice on its surface and its climate cycles between colder and warmer stages. A glacial period is one of those colder stages within an ice age, with interglacial periods in between. ice age long cycles glacial one colder stage"],[13,"y8c05-r034","Short answer","Scientists have found long periods when there was no permanent ice anywhere on Earth. Explain what this shows about the Earth's climate.","5.4","It shows that the climate has changed enormously over very long timescales, not just cycled between glacial and interglacial periods. The Earth has at times been warm enough that even the poles had no permanent ice, so today's conditions are not the only ones possible. changed long timescales no ice at the poles not permanent"],[13,"y8c05-r035","Short answer","Explain how boulders left in strange positions are evidence that glaciers were once present.","5.4","Glaciers are rivers of ice that move slowly downhill carrying rocks with them. When the ice melts the rocks are dropped where they lie, far from where they came from. Scratches on rock surfaces, made where moving ice dragged other rocks across them, support the same explanation. carried glacier melts left behind scratches"],[13,"y8c05-r036","Short answer","Fossils of cold-adapted animals and plants are found in places that are now warm. Explain what this tells scientists.","5.4","Those organisms could only have lived there if the climate at the time was much colder than it is now. The fossils are therefore independent evidence, separate from the boulders, that the climate of those places has changed. cold-adapted climate was colder changed independent evidence"],[13,"y8c05-r037","Short answer","Explain why the deeper layers of a peat bog are older than the layers above them.","5.4","New plant material is added on top of the bog year after year, and the older material is buried beneath it. So each layer is older than the one above, and the deepest peat formed first. added on top buried deeper older"],[13,"y8c05-r038","Short answer","Explain why scientists must keep a peat core in the correct order when they remove it.","5.4","The depth of each part of the core is what tells them its age. If the order is mixed up they can no longer say which sample is oldest, so the pollen they find could not be matched to a date and the record would be useless. order depth age cannot match useless"],[13,"y8c05-r039","Short answer","Explain how pollen in a peat core can tell scientists what the climate was like in the past.","5.4","The pollen is identified to find out which plants were growing at that time. Because each kind of plant can only live in a certain type of climate, the plants present at a given depth show what the climate must have been when that layer formed. identify plants each plant certain climate depth"],[13,"y8c05-r040","Short answer","Explain why plant material, including pollen, has not rotted away in a peat bog.","5.4","Decay is carried out by micro-organisms that need oxygen and do not thrive in acid conditions. A peat bog is waterlogged, so there is almost no oxygen, and it is slightly acid, so decay is extremely slow and the plant material is preserved. no oxygen acid decay slow preserved"],[13,"y8c05-r041","Short answer","Suggest what happens to living organisms in a region where the temperature stays well below freezing for a long time.","5.4","Plants cannot grow, so the food supply fails and the water is frozen and unavailable. Organisms that are not adapted to the cold die or must move to warmer regions, so the number of species living there falls sharply. cannot grow food frozen die move"],[13,"y8c05-r042","Short answer","Explain where the Earth's early atmosphere came from.","5.5","There was far more volcanic activity then than there is now, and the volcanoes produced gases. Those gases collected above the surface and formed the early atmosphere. volcanoes gases volcanic activity early atmosphere"],[13,"y8c05-r043","Short answer","Explain how the first lakes and oceans formed.","5.5","The volcanoes gave out water vapour. As the Earth cooled, this water vapour condensed into liquid water, which fell as rain and collected in low places to form the first lakes and oceans. water vapour cooled condensed rain collected"],[13,"y8c05-r044","Short answer","Explain why the Earth's early atmosphere was not suitable for animals.","5.5","It was made mainly of carbon dioxide, with only traces of other gases, and there was little or no oxygen. Animals need oxygen for respiration to release energy, so nothing that respires like an animal could have survived in it. carbon dioxide no oxygen respiration could not survive"],[13,"y8c05-r045","Short answer","Explain how oxygen first appeared in the Earth's atmosphere.","5.5","Micro-organisms developed in the oceans about 3500 million years ago and used carbon dioxide from the atmosphere to make food. Oxygen was produced as a waste product of this process, and as plants later developed on land, the level of oxygen kept rising. micro-organisms carbon dioxide make food waste product oxygen"],[13,"y8c05-r046","Short answer","Bands of red iron oxide are found in rocks 2.1 to 2.0 billion years old. Explain how these are evidence that oxygen levels were rising.","5.5","Iron in the rocks can only combine to form iron oxide if there is oxygen available. The appearance of these iron oxide bands shows that by that time there was enough oxygen in the surroundings for the reaction to happen, so oxygen levels must have risen. iron oxide combine enough oxygen evidence"],[13,"y8c05-r047","Short answer","Explain how the carbon dioxide in the early atmosphere was used up.","5.5","Plants and micro-organisms took it in and used the carbon to make the chemicals in their bodies. Some organisms did not rot when they died and became fossil fuels such as oil and coal, while animals with shells locked carbon into calcium carbonate. In each case the carbon was removed from the air and stored. taken in living things fossil fuels shells calcium carbonate locked up"],[13,"y8c05-r048","Short answer","Explain why the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere began to rise about 200 years ago.","5.5","Humans started to release carbon that had been locked up in the Earth for millions of years by burning fossil fuels such as oil and coal for heat, industry, transport and electricity. Burning turns that stored carbon back into carbon dioxide in the air. burning fossil fuels locked up released rise"],[13,"y8c05-r049","Short answer","Explain how cutting down forests increases the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.","5.5","Large established trees use a lot of carbon dioxide in photosynthesis. When they are cut down there are fewer trees, so less carbon dioxide is being taken out of the air, and the level in the atmosphere rises even if no extra is added. trees photosynthesis fewer less taken out rises"],[13,"y8c05-r050","Short answer","Explain why the greenhouse analogy is a useful way to describe what carbon dioxide does.","5.5","A greenhouse lets in light and heat from the Sun but traps the heat energy inside so it cannot escape. Carbon dioxide and methane behave in a similar way in the atmosphere, letting energy in but stopping some of it escaping, so the comparison makes the effect easier to picture. lets in traps cannot escape similar easier to picture"],[13,"y8c05-r051","Short answer","The greenhouse effect is described as a natural effect. Explain why it is still a problem today.","5.5","Without any greenhouse effect the Earth would be much colder, so the effect itself is not harmful. The problem is that increasing the levels of carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour strengthens it, so less heat escapes and the climate becomes warmer than before. natural much colder increasing less heat escapes warmer"],[13,"y8c05-r052","Short answer","Explain the difference between a renewable and a non-renewable energy resource.","5.5","A renewable resource does not run out, or is replenished within a human lifetime, such as wind, tides and sunlight. A non-renewable resource cannot be replaced once it has been used, such as coal, petroleum and natural gas. renewable replenished non-renewable cannot be replaced coal"],[13,"y8c05-r053","Short answer","Explain two advantages and one disadvantage of generating electricity with wind turbines.","5.5","Wind is renewable so it will not run out, and no harmful waste product such as carbon dioxide is produced when the electricity is generated. The disadvantage is that electricity is only produced when the wind blows, so the supply is not reliable and many turbines are needed to match a power station. renewable no carbon dioxide only when the wind blows not reliable"],[13,"y8c05-r054","Short answer","Explain the disadvantages of generating electricity from the tides.","5.5","The tides rise and fall at fixed times, so electricity can only be generated at certain times each day and not when demand is highest. The barrier built across the water can also interfere with wildlife habitats. certain times cannot control barrier wildlife habitats"],[13,"y8c05-o001","Short answer","What does the Greek word that gives us the word 'atom' mean?","5.1","It means 'cannot be split'. cannot be split"],[13,"y8c05-o002","Short answer","How do the atoms of one particular element compare with each other?","5.1","They are all the same. all the same"],[13,"y8c05-o090","Short answer","What do people mean by 'weather'?","5.3","The state of the atmosphere and its changes from minute to minute, hour to hour, day to day, or week to week. state of the atmosphere changes"],[13,"y8c05-o091","Short answer","Does the weather change by the same amount everywhere?","5.3","No — in some places the weather is very similar each day, but in others it changes frequently. similar changes frequently"],[13,"y8c05-o092","Short answer","Name the six things people usually combine when they think about the weather.","5.3","Temperature, humidity, precipitation, cloudiness, visibility and wind. temperature humidity precipitation cloudiness visibility wind"],[13,"y8c05-o093","Short answer","Give three reasons why countries take careful measurements of the weather.","5.3","To help predict what will happen next, to see patterns in the weather, and to provide information about the weather over time. predict patterns over time"],[13,"y8c05-o094","Short answer","What do people mean by 'climate'?","5.3","The weather of a place over a much longer time, usually more than 30 years. longer time 30 years"],[13,"y8c05-o096","Short answer","Climate is an average, so how quickly does it change compared with the weather?","5.3","Much more slowly. Weather can change in minutes or hours, but a climate takes several years to change. several years"],[13,"y8c05-o097","Short answer","What does climate information include as well as the normal weather?","5.3","The statistics that tell us about the range of extreme weather at that place. statistics extreme weather"],[13,"y8c05-o110","Short answer","How much colder was the Earth when Yuka the mammoth was alive?","5.4","The Earth was much colder than it is now. much colder"],[13,"y8c05-o111","Short answer","How long ago were the ice sheets shown on the map?","5.4","25 000 years ago. 25 000"],[13,"y8c05-w001","Multiple choice","Which statement about the particles in an atom is correct?","5.1","Electrons have less mass than protons do. Electrons have more mass than protons do. Electrons and protons have exactly the same mass. Electrons have no mass at all."],[13,"y8c05-w018","Multiple choice","What is the difference between weather and climate?","5.3","Weather is the day-to-day conditions; climate is the average pattern over many years. Climate is the day-to-day conditions; weather is the long-term average. Weather happens on land and climate happens at sea. They mean exactly the same thing."],[13,"y8c05-w025","Multiple choice","A core of peat is taken from a bog. Is the oldest peat at the top or the bottom?","5.4","At the top, because it has been exposed the longest. At the bottom of the bog, because new peat forms on top of the old. In the middle of the core. It is mixed evenly through the core."],[13,"y8c05-w032","Multiple choice","What produced the gases that made up the Earth's earliest atmosphere?","5.5","Volcanoes, which poured out gases in the first billion years. Plants growing on the land. Animals breathing out. Rain falling on the rocks."],[13,"y8c05-w040","Gap fill","The small, dense centre of an atom, which contains the protons and the neutrons, is called the ____.","5.1","nucleus"],[13,"y8c05-w042","Gap fill","Alloys are ____ than the pure metal they are made from.","5.2","harder"],[13,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The protons and neutrons are grouped closely together in the centre of the atom to form the ____.","5.1","nucleus"],[13,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What is the purity percentage of 18 carat gold?","5.2","50% 75% 92.5% 100%"],[13,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","Pure diamonds are colourless and ____.","5.2","translucent"],[13,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","What is the electrical charge of a neutron?","5.1","Positive Negative No charge Double positive"],[13,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which two key factors affect climate?","5.3","Temperature and wind speed Humidity and cloudiness Temperature and precipitation Visibility and pressure"],[13,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","Climate is defined as the weather of a place over a long time, usually more than ____ years.","5.3","30"],[13,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","Today, the Earth is in a(n) ____ period.","5.4","interglacial"],[13,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","What is the difference between a glacial period and an interglacial period?","5.4","Glacials are warmer, interglacials are colder Glacials have more ice spread out, interglacials have less ice Interglacials are frozen solid, glacials are warm There is no difference"],[13,"qwen-05-04","Gap fill","A change in the ____ can affect the climate.","5.5","atmosphere"],[13,"qwen-06-01","Multiple choice","What was the main source of gases that formed Earth's early atmosphere?","5.5","Volcanic activity Decaying plants Ocean evaporation Meteor impacts"],[13,"qwen-06-03","Multiple choice","Which gas was present in very small amounts or not at all in Earth's early atmosphere?","5.5","Oxygen Carbon dioxide Methane Water vapour"],[13,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Which particle in an atom has a negative electrical charge?","5.1","Proton Neutron Electron Nucleus"],[13,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","What is the percentage of gold in 18 carat gold?","5.2","50% 75% 90% 100%"],[13,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","Which of these is a type of precipitation?","5.3","Temperature Rain, hail or snow Visibility Atmosphere"],[13,"qwenw-03-01","Multiple choice","In a peat bog core, where is the oldest material found?","5.4","At the top of the core In the middle of the core At the bottom of the core Only in the wettest part"],[14,"y8c06-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the normal in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the incident ray in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the reflected ray in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the mirror in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the mirror in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the modelling clay in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the white paper in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the pencil in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f02h5","Click the diagram","Click on the protractor in the diagram.","6.1",""],[14,"y8c06-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the incident ray in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the normal in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the refracted ray in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the normal in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the view from here in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the cup in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the glass in the diagram.","6.2",""],[14,"y8c06-f07a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the white light in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the glass prism in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f07h3","Click the diagram","Click on the screen in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f08a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the incident ray in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the glass prism in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f08h3","Click the diagram","Click on the ray box in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f08h4","Click the diagram","Click on the screen in the diagram.","6.3",""],[14,"y8c06-f09a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the red in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the magenta in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f09h3","Click the diagram","Click on the yellow in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f10a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the white light in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the light between the two filters, after it has passed through the green filter.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f10h4","Click the diagram","Click on the green filter in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f11a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f11h1","Click the diagram","Click on the white light coming in.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-f11h2","Click the diagram","Click on the yellow light in the diagram.","6.4",""],[14,"y8c06-m001","Multiple choice","What kind of mirror are most of the mirrors you use every day?","6.1","Plane mirrors Curved mirrors Spoon-shaped mirrors Coloured mirrors"],[14,"y8c06-m014","Multiple choice","What is the material that light passes through called?","6.2","A medium A normal A prism A spectrum"],[14,"y8c06-m028","Multiple choice","When light passes into a medium where it slows down, which way does it bend?","6.2","Towards the normal Away from the normal Along the normal It does not bend at all"],[14,"y8c06-m029","Multiple choice","In which year did Newton show that white light could be split into different colours?","6.3","1666 1801 1905 1500"],[14,"y8c06-m042","Multiple choice","How many colours of light can all other colours be made from?","6.4","Three Seven Two Six"],[14,"y8c06-m059","Multiple choice","Which three colours are the primary colours of light?","6.4","Red, green and blue Red, yellow and blue Cyan, magenta and yellow Red, white and blue"],[14,"y8c06-m060","Multiple choice","Where does the word 'galaxy' come from?","6.5","A Greek word for 'milky' A Latin word for 'star' An Arabic word for 'cloud' A Greek word for 'spiral'"],[14,"y8c06-m072","Multiple choice","What is an asteroid?","6.6","An object made from rock that orbits the Sun A ball of hot gas that gives out light A moon that orbits a planet A cloud of dust between the galaxies"],[14,"y8c06-m088","Gap fill","The line drawn perpendicular to the mirror in a ray diagram is called the ____.","6.1","normal"],[14,"y8c06-m093","Gap fill","The material that light passes through is called a ____.","6.2","medium"],[14,"y8c06-m098","Gap fill","The range of colours that can be seen in white light is called a ____.","6.3","spectrum"],[14,"y8c06-m103","Gap fill","The three colours of light from which all other colours can be made are called the ____ colours.","6.4","primary"],[14,"y8c06-m109","Gap fill","The Milky Way is a ____ galaxy.","6.5","spiral"],[14,"y8c06-m114","Gap fill","____ are objects made from rock that orbit the Sun.","6.6","Asteroids"],[14,"y8c06-r001","Short answer","Explain why the angle of incidence is measured from the normal and not from the mirror surface.","6.1","The normal is a line perpendicular to the mirror at the point where the ray strikes it, and it is defined the same way for every surface. Measuring from the normal means the same rule works for curved surfaces and for refraction as well, and it is what makes the law of reflection come out as a simple equality. normal perpendicular same rule law of reflection"],[14,"y8c06-r002","Short answer","State the law of reflection and explain why it is called a law rather than a theory.","6.1","The law of reflection says that the angle of reflection is equal to the angle of incidence. In physics a law is something that always applies — it has been tested many times and no exception has been found — whereas a theory is an explanation of why something happens. angle of reflection equal angle of incidence always applies"],[14,"y8c06-r003","Short answer","Explain how a driver can use a mirror to see a cyclist who is not in front of the car.","6.1","Light from the Sun is reflected off the cyclist and travels to the mirror as the incident ray. It reflects off the mirror with the angle of reflection equal to the angle of incidence, and the reflected ray travels to the driver's eye, so the driver sees the cyclist even though the light did not come straight from them. reflected from the cyclist mirror angle of reflection driver's eye"],[14,"y8c06-r004","Short answer","Explain why your reflection in a spoon looks different from your reflection in a plane mirror.","6.1","A plane mirror is flat, so all the normals point the same way and the reflected rays keep their arrangement. A spoon is curved, so the normal points in a different direction at each point on the surface, and the reflected rays are spread out or brought together, which distorts the image. flat curved normal different direction distorted"],[14,"y8c06-r005","Short answer","Explain why an arrowhead should always be drawn on a light ray in a ray diagram.","6.1","The arrowhead shows which way the light is travelling. Without it the diagram cannot tell you which ray is the incident ray arriving at the surface and which is the reflected ray leaving it, so the angles could be labelled the wrong way round. direction incident reflected which way"],[14,"y8c06-r006","Short answer","A moving ball bouncing off a wall is used as an analogy for reflection of light. Explain why this analogy is useful and where it breaks down.","6.1","It is useful because the ball bounces off at the same angle it arrived at, just as the angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence. It breaks down because the ball is a solid object that touches the wall and loses energy, whereas light is not a material object and is not slowed by reflecting. bounces same angle solid object loses energy light is not"],[14,"y8c06-r007","Short answer","Explain why the background seen through a glass of water appears distorted.","6.2","Light from the background passes from air into the glass and the water and then back out into the air. At each boundary it changes speed and so changes direction. The rays reaching your eye no longer come from the direction the object really is in, so the image looks bent out of shape. refraction changes speed changes direction boundary distorted"],[14,"y8c06-r008","Short answer","Define refraction and explain what causes it.","6.2","Refraction is the change in direction of light when it passes from one medium to another, caused by a change in speed. Light travels more slowly in glass or water than in air, and one side of the ray slows down before the other, which swings the ray round into a new direction. change in direction one medium to another change in speed one side first"],[14,"y8c06-r009","Short answer","Explain how a person on roller skates moving onto a soft surface at an angle is an analogy for refraction.","6.2","One skate reaches the soft surface first and slows down while the other is still moving quickly on the hard surface. The difference in speed between the two sides turns the skater. In the same way, one side of a light ray enters the glass and slows first, which turns the ray. one skate first slows difference in speed turns"],[14,"y8c06-r010","Short answer","Explain why light bends towards the normal when it passes from air into glass.","6.2","Light travels more slowly in glass than in air. The side of the ray that enters the glass first is slowed first, so the ray swings round towards the normal, and the angle of refraction is smaller than the angle of incidence. slows down towards the normal angle of refraction smaller"],[14,"y8c06-r011","Short answer","Explain why light bends away from the normal when it passes from glass into air.","6.2","Light travels faster in air than in glass, so the ray speeds up as it leaves. The side that reaches the air first speeds up first, so the ray swings away from the normal and the angle of refraction is greater than the angle of incidence. speeds up away from the normal angle of refraction greater"],[14,"y8c06-r012","Short answer","In the glass block experiment you are told to include an angle of incidence of zero. Explain what happens to the ray in that case and why it is worth testing.","6.2","At an angle of incidence of zero the ray travels along the normal, so both sides of the ray reach the glass at the same moment and slow down together. There is nothing to turn the ray, so it passes straight through without bending. It is worth testing because it shows that it is the angle, not the change of medium alone, that produces the bending. zero along the normal both sides together no bending"],[14,"y8c06-r013","Short answer","Explain why it is hard to see clearly through a window covered in raindrops.","6.2","Each individual drop is a curved piece of water with its own surfaces, so each one refracts the light passing through it in a different direction. The light arriving at your eye comes from many directions at once and no clear image is formed. each drop refracts different direction no clear image"],[14,"y8c06-r014","Short answer","Explain why using windscreen wipers makes it easier to see through a wet windscreen.","6.2","The wipers remove the separate drops and leave a flat film of water or clear glass. All the light is then refracted in the same direction at the same flat surface, so the image is not broken up and you can see clearly. removes the drops flat same direction see clearly"],[14,"y8c06-r015","Short answer","Two transparent substances are listed with the speed of light in each. Explain how you would decide between which pair there would be the most refraction.","6.2","The amount of bending depends on how much the speed changes at the boundary. The greatest refraction happens between the two substances whose speeds of light are the furthest apart, and there would be no refraction at all between two substances in which light travels at the same speed. speed changes furthest apart greatest same speed no refraction"],[14,"y8c06-r016","Short answer","Explain why dispersion happens when white light passes through a prism.","6.3","White light is made of several colours, and each colour is refracted through a slightly different angle. Because they are bent by different amounts they leave the prism travelling in slightly different directions, so they spread out and can be seen separately. different colours refracted different angle spread out"],[14,"y8c06-r017","Short answer","Violet light is refracted through the largest angle and red through the smallest. Explain what this tells you about the speed of each colour in the glass.","6.3","The more a colour is bent, the more its speed changed on entering the glass. Violet is bent the most, so violet light is slowed the most and travels most slowly in the glass, while red is bent least and travels fastest in the glass. bent the most slowed the most violet slowest red fastest"],[14,"y8c06-r018","Short answer","Explain why the colours in a spectrum are not separated by gaps.","6.3","The amount of refraction changes gradually as you go through the colours rather than jumping from one to the next. Each colour therefore merges into the one beside it, so the spectrum is a continuous band rather than seven separate stripes. gradually merge continuous no gaps"],[14,"y8c06-r019","Short answer","Newton is said to have seen five or six colours but most people now say seven. Explain how the same spectrum can be described differently.","6.3","The colours merge into each other, so where one ends and the next begins is a judgement, not a measurement. Different observers draw the boundaries in different places, which is why some people see indigo and violet as one colour and count six. merge judgement boundaries different observers"],[14,"y8c06-r020","Short answer","Explain the three conditions needed to see a rainbow.","6.3","The Sun must be shining to provide bright light. There must be rain or small drops of water in the air to cause the dispersion. The Sun must be behind you, because the light is reflected inside the drops and comes back towards you rather than carrying on away from you. Sun shining drops of water Sun behind you reflected inside"],[14,"y8c06-r021","Short answer","Explain the part played by reflection, as well as refraction, in forming a rainbow.","6.3","Light is refracted as it enters each drop and the colours begin to separate. The light is then reflected from the inside of the back of the drop, which sends it back towards the observer, and it is refracted again on leaving. Without the reflection the colours would carry on away from you and you would not see them. refracted entering reflected inside back towards refracted leaving"],[14,"y8c06-r022","Short answer","Explain why a hosepipe on a sunny day can make a rainbow.","6.3","The hosepipe produces small drops of water in the air, which do the same job as raindrops. With the Sun shining and behind the camera, light is dispersed and reflected inside those drops, so the colours are seen exactly as in a natural rainbow. drops of water same as rain Sun behind dispersed"],[14,"y8c06-r023","Short answer","Explain the safety instruction not to put your eye closer than about half a metre to the prism.","6.3","Close to the prism the light has not spread out, so all the energy is concentrated in a small bright beam. Looking into it could damage the retina of your eye, so you keep back to let the light spread and dim before it reaches you. concentrated very bright damage keep back"],[14,"y8c06-r024","Short answer","A prism made of plastic separates white light into colours. Explain how you can tell from the ray diagram how the speeds of the different colours in the plastic compare.","6.3","The colour bent through the largest angle changed speed the most on entering the plastic, so it travels most slowly inside it. The colour bent least changed speed least and travels fastest inside it. Reading off the order of the colours in the diagram therefore gives the order of their speeds. bent most slowest bent least fastest order"],[14,"y8c06-r025","Short answer","Explain what is meant by the primary colours of light.","6.4","They are red, green and blue: the three colours that cannot be made by mixing any other colours of light, and from which all other colours of light can be made by adding them in different proportions. red green blue cannot be made all others adding"],[14,"y8c06-r026","Short answer","Explain why the primary colours of light are different from the primary colours of paint.","6.4","Light adds: when beams overlap, the colours reaching your eye are added together and the result is brighter. Paint subtracts: each pigment absorbs some colours from the white light falling on it, so mixing paints removes more colours and the result is darker. Since the two processes work in opposite directions, they need different primaries. adding subtracting absorb brighter darker"],[14,"y8c06-r027","Short answer","Explain how a screen can show orange when it only has red, green and blue sources.","6.4","Orange is made by adding red and green light, but with the red brighter than the green. By changing the brightness of each primary colour independently, the screen can produce all the different shades, so only three sources are needed. red and green brightness brighter different shades"],[14,"y8c06-r028","Short answer","Explain how a coloured filter works.","6.4","A filter is a transparent piece of coloured glass or plastic that only lets light of its own colour through. It absorbs the other colours in the white light falling on it, so the light that is transmitted is only the colour of the filter. transmits its own colour absorbs other colours"],[14,"y8c06-r029","Short answer","Explain why a filter is described as subtracting light.","6.4","White light is made of seven colours. A red filter absorbs six of them and transmits only red, so six colours have been taken away from what arrived. Nothing is added, so the process is subtraction and the transmitted light is always dimmer than the light going in. seven colours absorbs six taken away dimmer"],[14,"y8c06-r030","Short answer","Traffic lights use three identical white lamps. Explain how they show three different colours.","6.4","Each lamp has a different coloured filter in front of it — red, yellow or green. Each filter absorbs the colours that are different from its own and transmits only one, so three identical lamps produce three different colours. identical lamps coloured filter absorbs transmits one"],[14,"y8c06-r031","Short answer","Explain why no light gets through when green light shines on a red filter.","6.4","A red filter transmits only red light and absorbs all the other colours, and green is one of the colours it absorbs. As the light arriving is entirely green, all of it is absorbed and nothing is left to come through. only red absorbs green absorbed no light"],[14,"y8c06-r032","Short answer","Explain why a flower looks yellow in white light.","6.4","White light containing all seven colours falls on the flower. The flower reflects only the yellow light and absorbs the other six colours, so the only light reaching your eye from it is yellow. reflects yellow absorbs the others reaches your eye"],[14,"y8c06-r033","Short answer","Explain, in terms of reflection and absorption, why one object looks white and another looks black.","6.4","A white object reflects all the colours in white light equally, so all of them reach your eye together and you see white. A black object absorbs all the colours and reflects none, so almost no light reaches your eye from it. reflects all equally absorbs all reflects none"],[14,"y8c06-r034","Short answer","Explain why a red cube appears black when it is seen in green light.","6.4","A red object reflects only red light and absorbs every other colour. In green light there is no red light for it to reflect, so it absorbs the green and reflects nothing, and with no light coming from it the cube looks black. only reflects red no red present absorbs green reflects nothing"],[14,"y8c06-r035","Short answer","Explain why light from the Sun appears white.","6.4","The Sun gives out all the colours of the spectrum at once and at roughly equal brightness. When all of them enter your eye together they are added and the result is seen as white, which is why the Sun's light can be split into a full rainbow. all the colours at once added white"],[14,"y8c06-r036","Short answer","Suggest why scientists do not consider black to be a colour.","6.4","Black is what you see when no light is reaching your eye from an object, because every colour has been absorbed. It is the absence of light rather than a particular kind of light, so there is no black in the spectrum. no light absorbed absence not in the spectrum"],[14,"y8c06-r038","Short answer","Explain why the Milky Way appears as a band of light across the night sky.","6.5","Our galaxy is a spiral, and we live inside one of its arms. Looking along the plane of the disc we see a huge number of stars stacked behind one another, which merge into a bright band, while looking out of the disc in other directions we see far fewer stars. spiral we are inside along the arm many stars band"],[14,"y8c06-r039","Short answer","Explain why most of the stars we see at night belong to our own galaxy.","6.5","Other galaxies are enormously further away than the stars in the Milky Way. Light from their individual stars is far too faint to be picked out separately from Earth, so the points of light we can see are nearly all nearby stars in our own galaxy. other galaxies much further too faint own galaxy"],[14,"y8c06-r040","Short answer","Explain what holds a galaxy together.","6.5","Gravity. A galaxy contains stellar dust, gas, stars and solar systems, and every part of it attracts every other part. Because a galaxy is so large and has such an enormous total mass, the gravitational force is very strong even though the individual dust particles are tiny. gravity very large mass attracts strong"],[14,"y8c06-r041","Short answer","Explain how scientists estimated the number of galaxies in the universe.","6.5","They counted the galaxies in one part of space, then multiplied that number up to cover the whole universe. This assumes the part they counted is typical, so the answer is an estimate rather than an exact count. counted one part multiplied up estimate"],[14,"y8c06-r042","Short answer","Explain two reasons why the estimated number of galaxies may not be accurate.","6.5","The part of space they counted may contain more or fewer galaxies than the rest of the universe, so scaling it up would give too high or too low an answer. They may also not know the total volume of the universe accurately, so the factor they multiply by may itself be wrong. not typical more or fewer total volume not known accurately"],[14,"y8c06-r043","Short answer","Explain how estimating the number of grains of sand in a container is an analogy for estimating the number of stars in a galaxy.","6.5","In both cases there are far too many to count individually. You count a small quantity carefully, find how much of the whole that represents, and multiply up. The accuracy of the answer depends on whether the small sample really is typical of the whole. too many to count count a small amount multiply up typical"],[14,"y8c06-r044","Short answer","Particles of stellar dust can have a mass as small as 0.000 000 000 000 001 g. Suggest how clouds of such dust can still produce strong gravitational forces.","6.5","Gravity depends on the total mass, and a cloud contains an unimaginably large number of these particles spread over an enormous volume. Adding up all those tiny masses gives a very large total, so the force it produces is strong. total mass enormous number add up large total"],[14,"y8c06-r045","Short answer","Black holes are solid objects, not holes. Suggest two reasons why they appear black.","6.5","They give out no light of their own, so there is nothing to see from them directly. Their gravity is so strong that any light falling on them is pulled in and not reflected back out, so no light reaches us from that direction at all. no light of their own gravity light pulled in not reflected"],[14,"y8c06-r046","Short answer","Most galaxies are moving further apart. Predict two ways this will change how other galaxies are seen from Earth in the future.","6.5","They will appear smaller, because they are further away. They will also appear fainter, because the light spreads out more over the greater distance, so fewer of them will be visible at all. further away smaller fainter fewer visible"],[14,"y8c06-r047","Short answer","Explain why most asteroids are not regular in shape.","6.6","They are lumps of rock that were never large enough for their own gravity to pull them into a sphere. Just like rocks on Earth, their shape is whatever the breaking and collisions left behind, which is why scientists describe them as potato-shaped. lumps of rock gravity too weak not a sphere potato"],[14,"y8c06-r048","Short answer","Ceres was first thought to be a planet. Explain why the term 'asteroid' was introduced instead.","6.6","Ceres looked much smaller than the planets already known. Rather than call such a small body a planet, scientists introduced a new term for objects of that size that orbit the Sun, and Ceres became the largest of them. much smaller new term orbits the Sun largest"],[14,"y8c06-r049","Short answer","Explain why Ceres is described as an unusual asteroid.","6.6","Unlike other asteroids it is round, with a diameter of 975 km, and it has a core, a mantle and a crust like some planets. Scientists think it would have become a planet if it had continued to grow while the Solar System was forming. round core mantle crust like a planet largest"],[14,"y8c06-r050","Short answer","Itokawa appears to be made of separate lumps of rock. Explain why they are not pressed together into a single solid body.","6.6","The asteroid is a relatively small object, so its total mass is small and the force of gravity holding the lumps together is weak. There is not enough force to crush them into one solid piece, which is also why tidal forces from a passing planet can change its shape. small weak gravity not enough force tidal forces"],[14,"y8c06-r051","Short answer","Some asteroids are small and spin quickly. Explain how this tells scientists they are made of a single piece of rock.","6.6","In such a small object the force of gravity is too weak to hold separate pieces together, and spinning quickly would throw loose pieces off. Since the asteroid stays intact, it must be one solid piece of rock rather than a pile of rubble. gravity too weak spin quickly thrown off single piece"],[14,"y8c06-r052","Short answer","Explain how studying samples collected from Itokawa gives evidence about how the Solar System formed.","6.6","The samples are made of rock that has not been changed by weather, water or life as rock on Earth has been. They appear to come from small planets or moons broken up by impacts, so they preserve material from the time the Solar System was forming and can be analysed directly. samples unchanged impacts preserve evidence"],[14,"y8c06-r053","Short answer","Explain why many small asteroids that hit the Earth never reach the surface.","6.6","They are less than a few metres across, and as they travel through the atmosphere friction heats them so strongly that they break apart before they get down to the ground. Only larger objects survive the journey through the atmosphere. small atmosphere break apart never reach the surface"],[14,"y8c06-r054","Short answer","Explain the two reasons why asteroids sometimes collide with the Earth.","6.6","Many asteroids follow orbits that pass relatively close to the Earth, so they come near enough to be affected by it. The Earth also exerts a strong force of gravity on passing objects, which can pull an asteroid out of its path and onto a collision course. orbits pass close gravity pulled collision"],[14,"y8c06-r055","Short answer","All the planets have names but most asteroids do not. Suggest one reason for this.","6.6","There are vastly more asteroids than planets and most of them are very small, so many have only been detected and not studied. Names are given to the ones that have been observed in enough detail, which is a small fraction of the total. far more very small not studied small fraction"],[14,"y8c06-r056","Short answer","The total mass of all the asteroids in the belt is less than that of any planet. Explain how this supports the idea that asteroids are rocks left over from the formation of the Solar System.","6.6","There is not enough material in the whole belt to have made a planet, so it cannot be the remains of a planet that broke apart. It fits much better with material that never managed to gather into a planet in the first place and was left over. not enough material never formed left over not a broken planet"],[14,"y8c06-r057","Short answer","One website claims a building made by aliens can be seen on the asteroid Eros; a university website says it is light reflecting off a crater. Discuss which is more reliable.","6.6","The university website is more reliable. It is written by scientists who study these images, it gives an explanation based on something already known to happen, and its work can be checked by others. The alien website is independent with no stated expertise, and it makes an extraordinary claim without extraordinary evidence, so it should not be accepted without support. university scientists checked explanation no evidence"],[14,"y8c06-o001","Short answer","What name is given to a flat mirror?","6.1","A plane mirror. plane"],[14,"y8c06-o002","Short answer","What happens to your reflection when you look at a surface that is not plane, such as a spoon?","6.1","It is distorted. distorted"],[14,"y8c06-o003","Short answer","What are the straight lines that light travels in called?","6.1","Rays. rays"],[14,"y8c06-o004","Short answer","What two things should you always do when you draw a light ray?","6.1","Use a ruler, and put an arrowhead on the ray to show its direction. ruler arrowhead"],[14,"y8c06-o005","Short answer","What is the ray of light arriving at a mirror called?","6.1","The incident ray. incident ray"],[14,"y8c06-o006","Short answer","What is the name of the line drawn perpendicular to a mirror?","6.1","The normal. normal"],[14,"y8c06-o014","Short answer","What is the name for a material that light passes through?","6.2","A medium. medium"],[14,"y8c06-o016","Short answer","What happens to the speed of light when it passes from air into water or glass?","6.2","It travels more slowly. slowly slower"],[14,"y8c06-o017","Short answer","Define refraction of light.","6.2","The change in direction of light on passing from one medium to another because of a change in speed. change in direction change in speed"],[14,"y8c06-o030","Short answer","What shape of glass block did Newton use to split light into its colours?","6.3","A block in the shape of a triangle. triangle"],[14,"y8c06-o031","Short answer","What is the range of colours seen in white light called?","6.3","A spectrum. spectrum"],[14,"y8c06-o032","Short answer","Are the colours in a spectrum separate from each other?","6.3","No - they merge from one to another. merge"],[14,"y8c06-o065","Short answer","Why do we see the Milky Way as a band of light across the sky?","6.5","Because the Milky Way is a flat spiral galaxy and we are inside its disc. Looking along the disc we see a huge number of stars stacked up behind one another, which merge into a band. arm spiral"],[14,"y8c06-o066","Short answer","About how many stars are there in the Milky Way?","6.5","Hundreds of billions. Modern estimates are around 100-400 billion stars, including our Sun. (Older books print 250 million, which is far too low.) 250 000 000 stars Milky Way"],[14,"y8c06-o067","Short answer","Where are most of the stars we see at night?","6.5","In our own galaxy. own galaxy"],[14,"y8c06-o077","Short answer","What is the largest size that asteroids reach?","6.6","Up to 975 km across. 975"],[14,"y8c06-w001","Multiple choice","Which of these means the same as 'plane mirror'?","6.1","A mirror with a perfectly flat, smooth surface. A mirror that makes things look larger. A mirror that is curved inwards. A mirror used only on aeroplanes."],[14,"y8c06-w015","Multiple choice","Which word describes the range of colours in a rainbow?","6.3","spectrum section vacuum reflection"],[14,"y8c06-w035","Multiple choice","What is the name of the galaxy where we live?","6.5","The Milky Way galaxy. The Solar System. The Andromeda galaxy. The Universe."],[14,"y8c06-w045","Multiple choice","Which statement describes asteroids?","6.6","All asteroids are larger than planets. They are made from rock and have irregular shapes. All asteroids orbit the Earth. Asteroids are made of gas and are perfectly round."],[14,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the line that is perpendicular to the mirror surface where the light ray hits?","6.1","Normal Incident ray Reflected ray Boundary"],[14,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","A light ray arriving at a mirror is called an ____ ray.","6.1","incident"],[14,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","When light passes from air into glass or water, it is ____ at the boundary.","6.2","refracted"],[14,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the name for the material that light passes through?","6.2","medium lens prism spectrum"],[14,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","What is the name for the range of colours seen in white light?","6.3","spectrum prism dispersion reflection"],[14,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","Dispersion means splitting light into different ____.","6.3","colours"],[14,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","When red light and green light are added together, they produce the colour ____.","6.4","yellow"],[14,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","What colour is produced when red light and blue light are added together?","6.4","Magenta Cyan Yellow White"],[14,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","The word 'galaxy' comes from a Greek word for ____.","6.5","milky"],[14,"qwen-04-03","Multiple choice","What shape is the Milky Way galaxy?","6.5","Elliptical Irregular Spiral Round"],[14,"qwen-04-07","Multiple choice","What are asteroids made from?","6.6","Ice and gas Rock Metal only Plasma"],[14,"qwen-04-08","Gap fill","Most asteroids in the Solar System orbit between Mars and ____.","6.6","Jupiter"],[14,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What is another name for a plane mirror?","6.1","curved mirror flat mirror magnifying mirror bathroom mirror"],[14,"qwenw-01-04","Multiple choice","What is the name for any transparent object that light can pass through?","6.2","medium regular normal average"],[14,"qwenw-01-07","Gap fill","The range of colours in a rainbow is called a ____.","6.3","spectrum"],[14,"qwenw-01-09","Gap fill","The primary colours of light are red, green and ____.","6.4","blue"],[14,"qwenw-01-12","Multiple choice","Which of these describes the number of stars in our galaxy?","6.5","fewer than 100 between 100 and 1000 between 1000 and 1 000 000 more than 1 000 000"],[14,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","Where is the asteroid belt located?","6.6","Between the orbits of Venus and Earth Between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter Closer to the Sun than Mercury Between the orbits of Uranus and Neptune"],[15,"y8c07-f72h1","Click the diagram","Click on the part of the food triangle showing the foods you should eat the smallest amount of.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f72h2","Click the diagram","Click on the band of the food triangle holding the foods eaten mainly for protein.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f72h3","Click the diagram","Click on the band of the food triangle showing fresh fruit and vegetables.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f72h4","Click the diagram","Click on the band of the food triangle showing the foods you should eat the most of.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f72h5","Click the diagram","Click on the bar for the person who needs the most energy each day.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f72h6","Click the diagram","Click on the bar for the person who needs the least energy each day.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f72h7","Click the diagram","Click on the bar for the man aged 50 years.","7.2",""],[15,"y8c07-f73h1","Click the diagram","Click on the bar for mothers who smoked 15 or more cigarettes each day during pregnancy.","7.3",""],[15,"y8c07-f73h2","Click the diagram","Click on the bar showing the smallest percentage of babies born with a low birthweight.","7.3",""],[15,"y8c07-f73h3","Click the diagram","Click on the single cell that this growth sequence starts with.","7.3",""],[15,"y8c07-f73h4","Click the diagram","Click on the stage where the single cell has just divided into two.","7.3",""],[15,"y8c07-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the cranium in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the vertebral column in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the clavicle in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the scapula in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on the sternum in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the scapula (shoulder blade) in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the ball-and-socket joint in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the humerus in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the radius in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the two tendons in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the scapula in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the biceps muscle in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-f03h4","Click the diagram","Click on the three tendons in the diagram.","7.4",""],[15,"y8c07-m001","Multiple choice","Which nutrient does rice contain a lot of?","7.1","Starch Protein Fat Vitamin C"],[15,"y8c07-m002","Multiple choice","Starch is a type of which nutrient?","7.1","Carbohydrate Protein Fat Mineral"],[15,"y8c07-m043","Multiple choice","What is your diet?","7.2","The food that you eat each day The energy you use each day The exercise you take each day A plan to lose weight"],[15,"y8c07-m044","Multiple choice","What is a balanced diet?","7.2","A diet that provides all the different kinds of nutrients, and the right amount of energy A diet that contains no fat or sugar at all A diet that contains only carbohydrate and protein A diet that is the same for every person"],[15,"y8c07-m063","Multiple choice","How did every person on Earth begin their life?","7.3","As a single cell As a ball of cells called an embryo As a miniature human being As a baby"],[15,"y8c07-m064","Multiple choice","What do cells do as growth happens?","7.3","Each cell gets bigger, then divides again Each cell gets smaller and smaller Cells join together to make one large cell Cells stop dividing once the embryo forms"],[15,"y8c07-m090","Multiple choice","Where is the skeleton of an insect or other arthropod?","7.4","On the outside of its body Inside its body Only in its legs They have no skeleton"],[15,"y8c07-m091","Multiple choice","What is a skeleton on the outside of the body called?","7.4","An exoskeleton An endoskeleton A tendon A socket"],[15,"y8c07-m124","Multiple choice","What kind of joint is the knee joint?","7.4","A hinge joint A ball-and-socket joint A fixed joint A sliding joint"],[15,"y8c07-m125","Gap fill","Starch is a type of ____.","7.1","carbohydrate"],[15,"y8c07-m137","Gap fill","Your ____ is the food that you eat each day.","7.2","diet"],[15,"y8c07-m145","Gap fill","A little ball of cells produced when a cell divides again and again is called an ____.","7.3","embryo"],[15,"y8c07-m153","Gap fill","A skeleton on the outside of the body is called an ____.","7.4","exoskeleton"],[15,"y8c07-r001","Short answer","Explain the difference between the words 'food' and 'nutrient'.","7.1","A food is something you eat, such as chicken or rice. A nutrient is one of the substances inside that food which the body uses, such as protein, carbohydrate, fat, vitamins, minerals or water. One food usually contains several different nutrients. food what you eat nutrient substance several"],[15,"y8c07-r002","Short answer","Explain why you need to eat carbohydrates.","7.1","The body breaks starch down into glucose, and glucose is the fuel your cells use for respiration. Respiration releases the energy the cells need, so without carbohydrate there would be no easy supply of fuel for that process. starch glucose respiration release energy fuel"],[15,"y8c07-r003","Short answer","Explain why a person who has been injured needs plenty of protein.","7.1","Protein is used to make new cells. Repairing damaged tissue means building new cells to replace the ones that were destroyed, so more protein is needed than usual. Protein is also needed to make haemoglobin and antibodies. new cells repair replace haemoglobin antibodies"],[15,"y8c07-r004","Short answer","Explain why the body can use protein for energy but this is not what protein is for.","7.1","You get almost all of your energy from carbohydrates and fats, and protein is only used for energy if you run out of those. Protein's real job is making new cells, haemoglobin and antibodies, so using it as fuel means it is no longer available for growth and repair. run out carbohydrates and fats new cells not available"],[15,"y8c07-r005","Short answer","Explain the difference between the body's carbohydrate store and its fat store.","7.1","A small amount of carbohydrate is stored in the cells, especially in the liver and muscles, and it is used up quickly. Fat is stored in special cells underneath the skin and around some organs, and there is much more of it, so it is the long-term store. small amount liver and muscles fat under the skin long-term"],[15,"y8c07-r006","Short answer","Explain why animals living in cold places, such as seals, have thick fat stores under the skin.","7.1","Fat provides heat insulation as well as an energy store. A thick layer under the skin slows the loss of heat from the body to the cold surroundings, so the animal can keep its body temperature up without using so much energy. insulation slows heat loss cold body temperature"],[15,"y8c07-r007","Short answer","Explain why a person short of vitamin A may not be able to see at night.","7.1","Vitamin A is needed to help the eyes work well, and it is particularly important for seeing when it is quite dark. Without enough of it the eyes cannot respond to the very small amount of light available at night, even though daytime vision may seem normal. vitamin A eyes dark small amount of light"],[15,"y8c07-r008","Short answer","Explain why sailors on long sea voyages in the past often developed scurvy.","7.1","Scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C, which comes mainly from fresh fruit and vegetables. On a long voyage there was no way to keep fresh food, so the sailors ate none for months and their supply of vitamin C ran out. vitamin C fresh fruit and vegetables none on board long voyage"],[15,"y8c07-r009","Short answer","Explain why most of your vitamin D does not come from your food.","7.1","There are not many kinds of food that contain vitamin D. Instead it is made in the skin when sunlight falls on it, so for most people sunlight rather than diet is the main source. few foods made in the skin sunlight main source"],[15,"y8c07-r010","Short answer","Explain why people living far from the equator are more likely to be short of vitamin D.","7.1","Vitamin D is made in the skin when sunlight falls on it. Far from the equator there is less strong sunshine, especially in winter, and people spend more time indoors and covered up, so their skin makes less vitamin D. less sunshine skin indoors covered up"],[15,"y8c07-r011","Short answer","Explain the link between vitamin D and calcium in building strong bones.","7.1","Calcium is the mineral that makes bones and teeth strong, but the body cannot absorb it properly from food without vitamin D. So a child could eat plenty of calcium and still have bones that do not grow normally if they are short of vitamin D. absorb calcium vitamin D bones not absorbed"],[15,"y8c07-r012","Short answer","Explain why a shortage of iron in the diet leads to anaemia.","7.1","Iron is needed to make haemoglobin. Without enough iron the body makes too little haemoglobin, so not enough oxygen can be transported around the body, and this shortage of oxygen is what causes anaemia. iron haemoglobin oxygen transported anaemia"],[15,"y8c07-r013","Short answer","Use your knowledge of respiration to explain why a person with anaemia feels very tired.","7.1","Anaemia means not enough oxygen is being carried to the cells. Cells need oxygen for respiration to release energy from glucose, so with less oxygen they carry out less respiration and release less energy, which makes the person feel tired. not enough oxygen respiration less energy tired"],[15,"y8c07-r014","Short answer","Explain why water is essential inside your cells.","7.1","Water in the cells allows all the different chemicals inside them to dissolve. Chemicals can only react together when they are dissolved and able to move, so without water the reactions that keep the cell alive could not happen. dissolve chemicals react together cells"],[15,"y8c07-r015","Short answer","Explain why water is essential in the blood.","7.1","Water allows blood to flow easily, so it can be pumped all round the body carrying dissolved substances such as glucose, oxygen and waste. If there is too little water the blood cannot transport these substances properly. flow transport dissolved round the body"],[15,"y8c07-r016","Short answer","Marcus says Brazil nuts contain more calcium than all their other nutrients put together, and Sofia says it is more fat. Explain how you would use a nutrient table to decide who is right.","7.1","You read the mass of each nutrient in 100 g of Brazil nuts, add together all the nutrients except the one being tested, and compare. Calcium is a mineral needed only in small amounts, so its mass will be tiny, while fat is present in large amounts, which is why Sofia is right. read the table add together compare fat calcium small"],[15,"y8c07-r017","Short answer","A child has weak teeth and bones. Explain which nutrients they most need and why.","7.1","They need calcium, because bones and teeth contain calcium and it is what makes them strong, and vitamin D, because without it the calcium cannot be absorbed from the food. Protein is also needed, since bones contain living cells that have to be built. calcium vitamin D absorb protein living cells"],[15,"y8c07-r018","Short answer","Explain what is meant by a balanced diet.","7.2","A balanced diet provides some of all the different kinds of nutrient — carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins, minerals and water — and also gives the right amount of energy for that person. Too much or too little energy, or a missing nutrient, means the diet is not balanced. all the nutrients right amount of energy not too much not too little"],[15,"y8c07-r019","Short answer","Explain why some eight-year-old children need more energy than the average for their age.","7.2","The average hides a lot of variation. A child who is bigger than average, or who is very active and plays a lot of sport, uses more energy each day, so they need to take in more energy in their food to match what they use. bigger more active uses more energy average"],[15,"y8c07-r020","Short answer","Suggest why a man aged 50 needs less energy in his diet than a boy aged 15.","7.2","The 15-year-old is still growing, so energy is needed to build new cells as well as for everyday activity, and teenagers are often more physically active. The 50-year-old has stopped growing and may be less active, so he uses less energy each day. still growing new cells more active stopped growing"],[15,"y8c07-r021","Short answer","Explain why a person with an office job needs less carbohydrate and fat than someone who does heavy physical work.","7.2","Carbohydrate and fat are the main energy supplies. Someone who sits down for most of the day uses less energy than someone moving around or playing sport, so they need to take in less. Eating the same amount as an active person would mean storing the extra as fat. sitting uses less energy active stored as fat"],[15,"y8c07-r022","Short answer","Explain why a pregnant woman needs extra protein, iron and calcium.","7.2","She needs plenty of protein to build her growing baby's new cells, extra iron to make haemoglobin for both her own blood and her baby's, and plenty of calcium for building the baby's bones. All three are being used to build a second body as well as maintain her own. protein new cells iron haemoglobin calcium bones"],[15,"y8c07-r023","Short answer","Fibre cannot be digested. Explain why it is still an important part of the diet.","7.2","Because it is not digested it does not go into the blood or the cells, but it travels all the way through the digestive system. It keeps the digestive system healthy and helps to prevent constipation, when the digestive system slows down and faeces collect inside it. not digested travels through digestive system healthy constipation"],[15,"y8c07-r024","Short answer","Explain why foods made from plants are good sources of fibre.","7.2","Plant cell walls are made of cellulose, which humans cannot digest. Foods such as cereal grains, seeds and fresh fruit and vegetables are full of plant cell walls, so they carry a lot of fibre, and they usually supply plenty of vitamins and minerals too. cell walls cellulose cannot digest plants"],[15,"y8c07-r025","Short answer","Explain what the area of each layer in the food triangle is meant to show.","7.2","The bigger the area of a kind of food in the triangle, the greater the proportion of your diet it should make up. So the foods in the wide bottom layers should be eaten most often and those in the narrow top should be eaten least. bigger area greater proportion most least"],[15,"y8c07-r026","Short answer","Explain why sweets and fried foods are placed at the top of the food triangle.","7.2","They are high in sugar and fat, which should make up only a small proportion of the diet. Too much sugar causes tooth decay and raises the risk of diabetes, and too much fat can lead to weight gain and heart disease, so they belong in the narrowest part. sugar fat small proportion tooth decay heart disease"],[15,"y8c07-r027","Short answer","Explain why whole-grain bread or brown rice is a better choice than white bread or white rice.","7.2","The whole grain keeps the outer layers of the seed, which are made of plant cell walls and so supply much more fibre. Fibre keeps the digestive system healthy and helps prevent constipation, and these foods usually contain more vitamins and minerals as well. whole grain fibre digestive system vitamins and minerals"],[15,"y8c07-r028","Short answer","Suggest how a person who does not eat meat or fish can still get enough protein.","7.2","They can eat plant foods that are rich in protein, such as beans, lentils, nuts and seeds, and foods made from milk or eggs if they eat those. Eating a variety of these ensures the body gets the protein it needs to make new cells. beans nuts seeds milk variety"],[15,"y8c07-r029","Short answer","Explain why a burger, fries and a sweet milky drink should not be eaten too often.","7.2","This meal is high in fat and sugar and low in fibre, vitamins and minerals. Too much sugar causes tooth decay and increases the risk of diabetes, and too much fat and carbohydrate leads to weight gain, which strains the joints, heart and other organs. Eating it occasionally is fine; eating it often makes the whole diet unbalanced. high in fat and sugar low in fibre tooth decay weight gain occasionally"],[15,"y8c07-r030","Short answer","A man regularly eats food containing more energy than he uses each day. Explain what happens to the extra energy.","7.2","The energy that is not used is stored, first as a small amount of carbohydrate in the liver and muscles and then as fat under the skin and around the organs. Over time the fat stores grow, so he puts on weight, which puts a strain on his joints, heart and other organs. not used stored fat puts on weight strain"],[15,"y8c07-r031","Short answer","Suggest why, on average, an adult woman needs less energy each day than an adult man.","7.2","On average women have a smaller body mass and a smaller proportion of muscle. A smaller body with less muscle uses less energy both at rest and when active, so less energy is needed in the food each day. smaller body less muscle uses less energy on average"],[15,"y8c07-r032","Short answer","Explain what happens inside the body, as a person grows, to make it get bigger.","7.3","The cells divide to make more cells, and each new cell then grows bigger before dividing again. Growth is an increase in the number and size of cells, which is why plenty of protein is needed to build them and enough energy for the dividing. cells divide more cells grow bigger protein energy"],[15,"y8c07-r033","Short answer","Explain the difference between growth and development.","7.3","Growth is getting bigger — more cells and larger cells. Development is the change from a simple structure into one that can do more: tissues and organs forming, leg muscles and bones becoming strong enough to walk and run, and the brain developing so the child learns to talk and play. growth bigger development new abilities tissues and organs"],[15,"y8c07-r034","Short answer","Everyone passes through the same stages of development. Explain why the stages have no sharp divisions between them.","7.3","Each stage blends gradually into the next because the changes happen continuously rather than all at once. The stages are names we give to parts of a smooth process, and each person goes through them at slightly different rates and in slightly different ways. blends gradually continuous names we give different rates"],[15,"y8c07-r035","Short answer","Explain how regular exercise helps to stop a person storing too much fat.","7.3","Exercise uses up some of the energy in the food eaten each day. If the energy taken in is used rather than left over, there is less surplus to be stored as fat, so the fat stores do not keep growing. uses energy less surplus not stored fat"],[15,"y8c07-r036","Short answer","Explain two ways, other than weight, in which exercise improves health.","7.3","It makes the heart and muscles work hard, so they become stronger and able to do more. It also makes many people feel more cheerful and positive about life, so it benefits mental as well as physical health. heart muscles stronger cheerful mental health"],[15,"y8c07-r037","Short answer","Explain why smokers find it so difficult to stop smoking.","7.3","Tobacco contains nicotine, which is addictive. Once the body is used to it, going without makes the person feel unwell and craving, so it is difficult to manage without it even when they know the damage it is doing. nicotine addictive used to it difficult to manage"],[15,"y8c07-r038","Short answer","Explain how nicotine increases a smoker's risk of heart disease.","7.3","Nicotine damages the blood vessels and makes them get narrower, so it is harder for blood to get through them. The heart has to work harder to push blood through the narrowed vessels, and smokers are more likely than non-smokers to develop heart disease. narrower blood vessels harder to get through heart disease"],[15,"y8c07-r039","Short answer","Explain why carbon monoxide in tobacco smoke leaves a smoker's cells short of energy.","7.3","Carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin in the red blood cells and stops it doing its normal job of combining with oxygen. Less oxygen reaches the cells, so they cannot carry out enough respiration and cannot release as much energy from glucose. carbon monoxide haemoglobin less oxygen respiration less energy"],[15,"y8c07-r040","Short answer","Explain how damage to the alveoli makes it hard for a smoker to get enough oxygen.","7.3","Particulates in the smoke make the walls of the alveoli break down, so instead of millions of tiny alveoli the smoker has a few large spaces. That greatly reduces the total surface area available for oxygen to diffuse into the blood, so less oxygen gets through. alveoli break down large spaces surface area less oxygen"],[15,"y8c07-r041","Short answer","Explain what cancer is and how smoking increases the risk of it.","7.3","Cancer happens when cells start dividing out of control and spread to other parts of the body. Tobacco smoke contains tar, a mixture of dark sticky substances, and many other harmful chemicals which damage cells, so smoking increases the risk of many kinds of cancer including lung cancer. dividing out of control spread tar harmful substances risk"],[15,"y8c07-r042","Short answer","Explain why smoking harms people who do not smoke.","7.3","People near a smoker accidentally breathe in the cigarette smoke, which contains the same nicotine, carbon monoxide, tar and particulates. Those substances do the same damage in their bodies, so the harm is not limited to the smoker. breathe in same substances same damage not only the smoker"],[15,"y8c07-r043","Short answer","Suggest why tobacco companies might have tried to hide the dangers of smoking.","7.3","They made their money from selling cigarettes. If people understood the harm they would buy fewer, so the companies had a strong financial reason to play down or dispute the evidence — which is exactly why information from them about their own product should be treated as possibly biased. money sell fewer financial reason biased"],[15,"y8c07-r044","Short answer","Suggest why it took so long for people to realise that smoking was harmful.","7.3","The diseases caused by smoking take many years to appear, so the link between the habit and the illness is not obvious. Until the 1960s no research had shown the connection clearly, and the tobacco companies advertised cigarettes as harmless or even good for you. many years not obvious no research advertised"],[15,"y8c07-r045","Short answer","E-cigarettes provide nicotine but are less likely to make the smoker ill. Suggest how they might help more people to give up smoking.","7.3","The main reason people find it so hard to stop is that nicotine is addictive. An e-cigarette supplies the nicotine without the tar, carbon monoxide and particulates, so the craving is satisfied while the most damaging substances are avoided, and the dose can then be reduced gradually. nicotine addictive without the tar reduce gradually"],[15,"y8c07-r046","Short answer","Describe and explain how smoking during pregnancy affects the chance of a baby having a low birthweight.","7.3","The more cigarettes a mother smokes each day, the greater the percentage of babies born with a low birthweight. Carbon monoxide and nicotine reduce the oxygen and nutrients reaching the baby through the mother's blood, so the baby's cells cannot grow as fast. more cigarettes greater percentage less oxygen cannot grow"],[15,"y8c07-r047","Short answer","Explain why both calcium and protein are needed for strong bones.","7.4","Calcium is the mineral that makes bone hard, so without enough of it the bones may not grow properly. Bones also contain living cells, and protein is needed to build those cells, so both nutrients are required. calcium hard living cells protein"],[15,"y8c07-r048","Short answer","Explain why movement of the skeleton can only happen at a joint.","7.4","Bones themselves are rigid and cannot bend. Movement can only take place where two bones meet one another, so a joint is needed wherever the body has to change shape. bones are rigid cannot bend two bones meet joint"],[15,"y8c07-r049","Short answer","Explain the difference between a hinge joint and a ball-and-socket joint.","7.4","A hinge joint lets the bones move back and forth in one direction only, like a door opening and closing — the elbow and knee are examples. In a ball-and-socket joint one bone has a ball-shaped end that fits into a cup in the other, which lets the bones move in a complete circle, as at the shoulder and hip. hinge one direction ball and socket complete circle elbow shoulder"],[15,"y8c07-r050","Short answer","Explain where a muscle gets the energy it needs to contract.","7.4","Muscle cells release the energy from glucose by respiration, like all other cells. The harder and more often the muscle contracts, the more energy it uses and so the more glucose it needs, which is why an active person must eat plenty of carbohydrate. glucose respiration more contraction more glucose"],[15,"y8c07-r051","Short answer","Explain how the contraction of a muscle moves a bone.","7.4","Muscles are attached to bones by tough cords called tendons. When the muscle contracts it gets shorter and pulls on the tendon, and the tendon pulls on the bone, so the bone moves at the joint. contracts shorter tendon pulls bone"],[15,"y8c07-r052","Short answer","Explain why one muscle on its own cannot both bend and straighten the arm.","7.4","Muscles can only pull. They generate a force by getting shorter, but they cannot generate a force by getting longer, so the biceps cannot push the arm straight again. A second muscle is needed on the other side of the joint to pull it back. can only pull shorter cannot push second muscle"],[15,"y8c07-r053","Short answer","Explain what is meant by antagonistic muscles, using the biceps and triceps as your example.","7.4","Antagonistic muscles are two muscles that work as a team at a joint, each pulling the bones in the opposite direction. To bend the arm the biceps contracts and the triceps relaxes; to straighten it the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes. opposite directions biceps contracts triceps relaxes team"],[15,"y8c07-r054","Short answer","Explain what happens between deciding to bend your arm and the arm actually moving.","7.4","The brain sends an electrical impulse along a neurone to the biceps muscle. The cells in the biceps respond by contracting, the contracting biceps pulls on its tendon, and the radius is pulled upwards, so the arm bends at the elbow. brain electrical impulse neurone contract tendon bends"],[15,"y8c07-r055","Short answer","The biceps is fixed to the scapula at one end and the radius at the other. Explain why this arrangement makes the arm bend.","7.4","Because both ends are firmly fixed, shortening the muscle must pull those two bones closer together. The scapula is held in place by the rest of the skeleton, so it is the radius that moves upwards, and the arm bends at the elbow. both ends fixed pulled closer scapula held radius moves"],[15,"y8c07-r056","Short answer","Explain why two muscles are needed to move a bone in one direction and then back again.","7.4","A muscle can only produce a force by contracting, that is by getting shorter. It cannot push, so it cannot return the bone to its original position. A second muscle attached on the other side of the joint contracts to pull the bone back, while the first one relaxes. only pull cannot push other side pull back relaxes"],[15,"y8c07-r057","Short answer","In an experiment to measure the force needed to break a drinking straw, explain which variables should be kept the same and why.","7.4","The length and diameter of the straw, the material it is made from and the way it is supported should all be kept the same, except for the one variable being tested. If more than one thing changes, any difference in the breaking force cannot be linked to the variable you are investigating. keep the same one variable cannot be linked fair test"],[15,"y8c07-r058","Short answer","A student wants to test the hypothesis 'long bones break more easily than short bones' using drinking straws. Explain why a straw is a reasonable model and what its limits are.","7.4","A straw is a hollow tube like a long bone, so its length can be varied while everything else is kept the same, and the breaking force can be measured with a newton meter. The limits are that a straw is made of a single uniform material, whereas real bone is living tissue containing calcium and cells and can repair itself, so the values would not apply directly to a real bone. hollow tube vary length measure force living tissue not the same material"],[15,"y8c07-o001","Short answer","Give two reasons why the body needs protein.","7.1","Protein is needed to make new cells so the body can grow, and to repair the body if it is damaged. It is also needed to make haemoglobin and antibodies. grow growth repair new cells haemoglobin antibodies"],[15,"y8c07-o002","Short answer","What happens to starch after you have eaten it?","7.1","The body breaks the starch down to make a sugar called glucose. broken down glucose sugar digested"],[15,"y8c07-o003","Short answer","What is the difference between a fat and an oil?","7.1","Fats and oils are very similar, but fats are solid at normal temperatures and oils are liquid. fat solid oil liquid solid liquid normal temperature"],[15,"y8c07-o010","Short answer","Explain why a person who does not eat enough iron feels very tired.","7.1","Without enough iron they do not make enough haemoglobin, so not enough oxygen is transported around the body. The cells cannot carry out enough respiration, so they release less energy. haemoglobin oxygen respiration energy transported"],[15,"y8c07-o016","Short answer","Why does a pregnant woman need extra iron in her diet?","7.2","She needs iron to make haemoglobin in her own blood and in her baby's blood. haemoglobin baby blood iron"],[15,"y8c07-o017","Short answer","Why are foods made from plants good sources of fibre?","7.2","Plant cell walls are made of cellulose, and fibre is mostly cellulose. cell walls cellulose plant cells"],[15,"y8c07-o018","Short answer","What is constipation, and how does fibre help to prevent it?","7.2","Constipation is when the digestive system slows down and faeces collect inside it instead of being passed out. Fibre keeps the digestive system working, so it helps to prevent constipation. digestive system slows down faeces prevent"],[15,"y8c07-o019","Short answer","Give two reasons why you should not eat too much sugar.","7.2","Too much sugar can make your teeth decay, and it increases the risk of developing an illness called diabetes. teeth decay tooth decay diabetes"],[15,"y8c07-o020","Short answer","Why is putting on too much weight bad for the body?","7.2","Extra weight puts a strain on the joints, the heart and other body organs. strain joints heart organs"],[15,"y8c07-o021","Short answer","What is the risk of eating too many fats that come from animals?","7.2","It can increase the risk of developing heart disease. heart disease heart"],[15,"y8c07-o022","Short answer","What is an embryo?","7.3","An embryo is the little ball of cells produced when the first cell divides over and over again, which eventually becomes a baby. ball of cells cells divides early stage baby"],[15,"y8c07-o024","Short answer","Why does a growing child need plenty of protein and energy in the diet?","7.3","Cells contain a lot of protein, and energy is needed to make cells divide. Growth happens by cells dividing and growing, so both are needed. protein cells divide energy growth"],[15,"y8c07-o025","Short answer","Give two ways in which regular exercise helps to keep a person healthy.","7.3","Exercise uses up some of the energy in food so that less is stored as fat, and it makes the heart and muscles work hard so that they become strong. It can also make people feel more cheerful. uses energy less fat heart muscles strong cheerful"],[15,"y8c07-o026","Short answer","How does nicotine increase a smoker's risk of heart disease?","7.3","Nicotine damages the blood vessels and makes them narrower, so it is harder for blood to get through them. blood vessels narrower harder for blood damages"],[15,"y8c07-o027","Short answer","Explain why the cells of a smoker may not release enough energy.","7.3","Carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin and stops it carrying oxygen to the body cells. With less oxygen the cells cannot carry out enough respiration, so they release less energy. carbon monoxide haemoglobin oxygen respiration energy"],[15,"y8c07-o029","Short answer","How do particulates in tobacco smoke make it hard for a smoker to get enough oxygen?","7.3","The particulates get trapped in the lungs and make the walls of the alveoli break down, so instead of millions of tiny alveoli the smoker has a few large spaces. This gives a much smaller surface area for oxygen to pass into the blood. alveoli break down big spaces surface area oxygen"],[15,"y8c07-o030","Short answer","What is an exoskeleton, and which animals have one?","7.4","An exoskeleton is a skeleton on the outside of the body. Insects and other arthropods have one. outside insects arthropods exoskeleton"],[15,"y8c07-o031","Short answer","What is a joint?","7.4","A joint is a place where two bones meet one another. Movement in the skeleton can only take place at a joint, because bones cannot bend. two bones meet movement bones cannot bend"],[15,"y8c07-o032","Short answer","Describe how the bones move at a hinge joint.","7.4","They move back and forth in one direction only, in the same way that a door opens and closes. back and forth one direction door hinge"],[15,"y8c07-o033","Short answer","Describe the shape of the two bones at a ball-and-socket joint.","7.4","One bone has an end shaped like a ball, and the other bone has a cup, or socket, that the ball fits into. ball socket cup fits into"],[15,"y8c07-o034","Short answer","What is meant by contraction of a muscle?","7.4","Contraction is when the specialised cells in a muscle make themselves shorter, so the whole muscle gets shorter. shorter muscle cells contract"],[15,"y8c07-o036","Short answer","What is a tendon, and what does it do?","7.4","A tendon is a tough cord that attaches a muscle to a bone. When the muscle contracts it pulls on the tendon, which pulls on the bone and makes it move at a joint. tough cord attaches muscle to bone pulls"],[15,"y8c07-o039","Short answer","Explain why the biceps cannot push the arm straight again.","7.4","Muscles can only pull. They generate a force by getting shorter, but they cannot generate a force by getting longer, so another muscle, the triceps, is needed to pull the arm straight. only pull cannot push getting longer triceps"],[15,"y8c07-o040","Short answer","What are antagonistic muscles?","7.4","Two muscles that work together as a pair, so that when one of them contracts it moves the bones at a joint in one direction, and when the other contracts it moves them in the other direction. pair one contracts other relaxes opposite direction"],[15,"y8c07-w001","Multiple choice","A table gives the nutrients contained in 100 g of each food. How do you work out how much fat there is in 200 g of milk?","7.1","Read the figure straight from the table, because the table already covers any mass. Double the figure in the table, because the table is for 100 g and you have 200 g. Halve the figure in the table. Add 200 to the figure in the table."],[15,"y8c07-w002","Multiple choice","Marcus says brazil nuts contain more calcium than all the other nutrients put together. Sofia says they contain more fat than all the other nutrients put together. Who is right?","7.1","Marcus, because nuts are a very good source of calcium. Sofia, because brazil nuts contain more fat than all the other nutrients put together. Both of them, because the two statements can both be true. Neither of them, because protein is the largest part of a brazil nut."],[15,"y8c07-w006","Multiple choice","Which two nutrients provide most of the energy for the cells in the body?","7.2","Carbohydrates and fats provide most of the energy. Vitamins and minerals provide most of the energy. Fibre and water provide most of the energy. Protein and vitamins provide most of the energy."],[15,"y8c07-w011","Multiple choice","On the bar chart, what do the bars actually show?","7.3","The total number of people who died from each disease. The deaths caused by smoking, as a percentage of all deaths from that disease. The number of people who smoke in that country. The percentage of people who recovered from each disease."],[15,"y8c07-w028","Gap fill","The nutrient that is needed for growth and for repairing the body is ____.","7.1","protein"],[15,"y8c07-w030","Gap fill","The bar chart shows the ____ daily energy needs of six different groups of people.","7.2","mean average"],[15,"y8c07-w032","Gap fill","The bars on the chart show the deaths caused by smoking as a ____ of all deaths from that disease.","7.3","percentage per cent percent"],[15,"y8c07-w034","Gap fill","The knee is an example of a ____ joint, which allows movement in one direction only.","7.4","hinge"],[15,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the main job of protein in the body?","7.1","To give you energy for respiration To make new cells and repair damage To help your eyes see in the dark To keep your blood vessels healthy"],[15,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Starch is broken down by the body to make a sugar called ____.","7.1","glucose"],[15,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which of these is true about fats and oils?","7.1","Fats are liquid at normal temperatures Oils are solid at normal temperatures Fats are solid and oils are liquid at normal temperatures Both fats and oils are gases"],[15,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the main reason fibre is not considered a nutrient?","7.2","It does not provide energy You cannot digest it It is only found in meat It is made of protein"],[15,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","Fibre is mostly made of a substance called ____.","7.2","cellulose"],[15,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which food group should make up the largest proportion of your diet according to the triangle?","7.2","Sweets and fried foods Meat, fish, eggs or dairy Rice, bread, pasta and whole grains Fresh fruit and vegetables"],[15,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","The tiny ball of cells formed after a single cell divides and grows is called an ____.","7.3","embryo"],[15,"qwen-04-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the skeleton found on the outside of an insect's body?","7.4","Endoskeleton Exoskeleton Cartilage frame Bone shell"],[15,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","Bones are hard and strong because they contain a lot of ____.","7.4","calcium"],[15,"qwen-04-03","Multiple choice","What is the name for the place where two bones meet in the skeleton?","7.4","A joint A tendon A ligament A muscle"],[15,"qwen-05-01","Multiple choice","Which space agency hopes to send people to Mars by the 2030s?","7.3","NASA ESA CERN WHO"],[15,"qwen-05-03","Multiple choice","What happens to the bones and muscles of astronauts when they are in space for a long time?","7.3","They get smaller and weaker They grow larger and stronger They stay exactly the same size They turn into boneless tissue"],[15,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Vitamin A is needed in the diet to help with ____.","7.1","night vision"],[15,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","Which nutrient is the body's long-term energy store, kept in special cells under the skin?","7.1","fat and oil carbohydrate protein vitamin C"],[15,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","If you eat too much food, your body turns the extra nutrients into ____ which is stored below the skin.","7.2","fat"],[15,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Which of these foods contains no dietary fibre?","7.2","chicken apples beans spinach"],[15,"qwenw-02-08","Gap fill","Electronic cigarettes provide the smoker with ____ but are less likely to make them ill.","7.3","nicotine"],[15,"qwenw-02-10","Gap fill","In the China survey, ____ percent of women who had been smokers successfully gave up smoking.","7.3","3.2"],[15,"qwenw-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the instrument used to measure force in newtons?","7.4","Balance Newton meter Thermometer Ruler"],[15,"qwenw-03-02","Gap fill","The unit of force measured by a newton meter is the ____.","7.4","newton"],[16,"y8c08-f84h1","Click the diagram","Click on the test tube holding the least reactive of the five metals.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f84h2","Click the diagram","Click on the piece of sodium reacting on the surface of the water.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f84h3","Click the diagram","Click on the magnesium in this apparatus.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f84h4","Click the diagram","Click on the part of the apparatus that supplies the steam for the reaction.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f84h5","Click the diagram","Click on the flame where the gas made by the reaction is burning.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the oxygen in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the heat in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the fuel in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the stopper in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the plastic bottle in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the to spark generator in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the bent paperclips in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f02h5","Click the diagram","Click on the string in the diagram.","8.1",""],[16,"y8c08-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the thermometer in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the glass rod in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the thermometer in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the opening system in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the heat insulator in the diagram.","8.2",""],[16,"y8c08-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 1 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 2 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 3 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f06h4","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 4 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f07a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 1 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 2 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f07h3","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 3 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f07h4","Click the diagram","Click on the tube 4 in the diagram.","8.3",""],[16,"y8c08-f08a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the bubbles of gas in the diagram.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the metal (calcium) in the diagram.","8.4",""],[16,"y8c08-f09a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.5",""],[16,"y8c08-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the thermometer in the diagram.","8.5",""],[16,"y8c08-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the water in the diagram.","8.5",""],[16,"y8c08-f09h3","Click the diagram","Click on the spirit burner in the diagram.","8.5",""],[16,"y8c08-m001","Multiple choice","What is burning?","8.1","A chemical reaction in which a substance combines with oxygen A physical change in which a substance melts A reaction in which a substance combines with water A reaction that absorbs thermal energy"],[16,"y8c08-m027","Multiple choice","What do some chemical reactions do with thermal energy from their surroundings?","8.2","They absorb it and change it to chemical energy stored in the chemical bonds They give it out as light They destroy it completely They change it into new atoms"],[16,"y8c08-m056","Multiple choice","What do many metals do if they get hot enough?","8.3","They react with oxygen They melt without reacting They absorb water They turn into acids"],[16,"y8c08-m079","Multiple choice","Why is sandpaper used on the metal before placing it in water?","8.4","To clean the surface of the metal To make the metal thinner To make the metal hotter To weigh the metal"],[16,"y8c08-m097","Multiple choice","Why is copper used for the roofs of some buildings and magnesium is not?","8.4","Copper does not react with water but magnesium does Copper is lighter than magnesium Magnesium is more expensive than copper Copper conducts electricity better"],[16,"y8c08-m099","Multiple choice","What are the products when magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid?","8.5","Magnesium chloride and hydrogen Magnesium oxide and water Magnesium hydroxide and oxygen Magnesium and chlorine"],[16,"y8c08-m103","Multiple choice","What did Arun and Marcus watch for to compare the reactivity of the metals with acid?","8.5","How many bubbles each metal produces How hot each test tube became How much the metal changed colour How long each metal took to dissolve completely"],[16,"y8c08-m109","Gap fill","Burning is a chemical reaction in which a substance combines with ____.","8.1","oxygen"],[16,"y8c08-m117","Gap fill","Reactions that absorb thermal energy from their surroundings are called ____ reactions.","8.2","endothermic"],[16,"y8c08-m125","Gap fill","Sodium, potassium and calcium are so reactive that they have to be stored under ____.","8.3","oil"],[16,"y8c08-m132","Gap fill","The general word equation for a metal reacting with water is metal + water -> metal ____ + hydrogen.","8.4","hydroxide"],[16,"y8c08-m138","Gap fill","Magnesium chloride is an example of a ____.","8.5","salt"],[16,"y8c08-m141","Gap fill","The metals produce ____ when they are added to dilute hydrochloric acid.","8.5","hydrogen"],[16,"y8c08-r001","Short answer","Explain why burning is described as a chemical reaction and not just a physical change.","8.1","In burning, the substance combines with oxygen and the atoms rearrange to form a completely new substance, an oxide. A physical change would leave the same substance behind, but after burning the original substance is gone, so it is a chemical reaction. combines with oxygen atoms rearrange new substance oxide"],[16,"y8c08-r002","Short answer","Trace the energy changes that happen when a fuel such as wood burns.","8.1","The chemical energy stored in the fuel is changed into thermal, light and sound energy. That energy then dissipates, which means it spreads out into the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings rises. chemical energy thermal light dissipate surroundings"],[16,"y8c08-r003","Short answer","Explain why a fire goes out if you smother it with a damp blanket.","8.1","Burning requires three things together: oxygen, fuel and heat. A blanket cuts off the supply of oxygen from the air, so even though fuel and heat are still there the reaction cannot continue. oxygen fuel heat cuts off cannot continue"],[16,"y8c08-r004","Short answer","Explain what makes a reaction exothermic, using burning as your example.","8.1","In an exothermic reaction thermal energy is given out. When a fuel burns, the atoms of carbon and oxygen join together in new ways, chemical energy is changed to thermal energy and the temperature rises, so energy is leaving the reaction and going into the surroundings. given out thermal energy temperature rises surroundings"],[16,"y8c08-r005","Short answer","Describe the evidence you would look for to show that burning is exothermic.","8.1","You would look for a rise in temperature of the surroundings: the flame feels hot, a thermometer near it reads higher, and light and sound are also given out. All of these show energy leaving the reaction rather than being taken in. temperature rises feels hot energy given out surroundings"],[16,"y8c08-r006","Short answer","Explain, in terms of atoms, where the energy comes from when hydrogen burns.","8.1","The hydrogen and oxygen combine to form water. When the atoms of hydrogen and oxygen rearrange themselves and combine together, energy is given out, so the energy was stored in the chemical bonds of the original substances and is released when those bonds are rearranged. rearrange combine energy given out stored bonds"],[16,"y8c08-r007","Short answer","Explain how the burning of hydrogen inside a bottle makes the bottle shoot along a string.","8.1","The reaction gives out a lot of energy very quickly, which pushes the stopper out of the bottle. The chemical energy is changed into kinetic energy as well as thermal, sound and light, and the kinetic energy makes the bottle move very quickly along the string. gives out energy stopper pushed out kinetic energy moves"],[16,"y8c08-r008","Short answer","Explain why a spark is needed to start the hydrogen reaction, even though the reaction gives out energy.","8.1","The hot spark provides the energy needed to start the reaction. A reaction can be exothermic overall and still need a small input of energy to get going; once started, the energy it gives out keeps it going. spark energy to start still exothermic keeps going"],[16,"y8c08-r009","Short answer","Explain why burning magnesium is called an oxidation reaction as well as an exothermic reaction.","8.1","It is an oxidation reaction because the magnesium combines with oxygen and a new substance, magnesium oxide, is formed. It is also exothermic because energy is given out as heat and light as the magnesium and oxygen atoms rearrange themselves. The two names describe different things: what reacts, and which way the energy goes. combines with oxygen oxide formed energy given out heat and light"],[16,"y8c08-r010","Short answer","Explain why the temperature of the surroundings rises when potassium reacts with water.","8.1","The bonds between the atoms of oxygen and hydrogen in the water break and the atoms rearrange to form potassium hydroxide and hydrogen. Stored chemical energy is changed to thermal energy, which dissipates into the environment, so the surroundings get hotter. bonds break rearrange chemical energy thermal energy dissipates"],[16,"y8c08-r011","Short answer","A test tube gets hot when magnesium is added to dilute hydrochloric acid. Explain what this tells you about the reaction.","8.1","It tells you the reaction is exothermic. Thermal energy is being given out by the reaction and transferred to the test tube and the surroundings, so their temperature rises. If the reaction were endothermic the test tube would feel cold instead. exothermic given out transferred temperature rises"],[16,"y8c08-r012","Short answer","Marcus got a higher final temperature than Sofia in the same reaction. Explain why that does not prove more chemical energy was changed to thermal energy in his experiment.","8.1","The final temperature depends on the starting temperature as well as the energy released, so what matters is the temperature rise, not the final reading. They also may have lost different amounts of heat to the surroundings, so the two final temperatures are not a fair comparison on their own. temperature rise starting temperature heat loss not a fair comparison"],[16,"y8c08-r013","Short answer","Sofia and Marcus want a larger temperature change. Explain how you would turn 'use a different metal' into an investigation they could actually carry out.","8.1","You would write it as a question with one variable to change and one to measure, such as 'Which metal gives the largest temperature rise when added to dilute hydrochloric acid?' Everything else — the volume and concentration of acid, the mass of metal, the type of container and the starting temperature — must be kept the same so the temperature change can be linked to the metal alone. one variable temperature rise keep the same volume mass"],[16,"y8c08-r014","Short answer","Explain why safety glasses must be worn when magnesium is added to dilute hydrochloric acid.","8.1","The reaction gives out thermal energy and produces hydrogen gas, so the mixture bubbles and can spit. Acid splashed into the eye would cause serious damage, and the eye cannot be washed as easily as skin, so eye protection is essential. acid spit bubbles eye damage"],[16,"y8c08-r015","Short answer","Explain what happens to energy in an endothermic reaction.","8.2","Thermal energy is absorbed from the surroundings and changed to chemical energy stored in the chemical bonds of the products. Because energy is being taken out of the surroundings, the surroundings get colder. absorb surroundings chemical energy bonds colder"],[16,"y8c08-r016","Short answer","Explain how you can tell from a thermometer that a reaction is endothermic.","8.2","The temperature at the end of the reaction is lower than that at the start. Because the reaction is taking thermal energy out of the mixture and its surroundings, the reading falls rather than rises. lower end start takes energy falls"],[16,"y8c08-r017","Short answer","Explain why a test tube feels cooler when sodium hydrogen carbonate reacts with citric acid.","8.2","During this reaction thermal energy is absorbed from the surroundings and stored in the form of chemical bonds. The test tube and the air around it are the surroundings, so they lose thermal energy and their temperature falls, making the tube feel cool. absorbed surroundings chemical bonds temperature falls"],[16,"y8c08-r018","Short answer","Explain why sherbet sweets give a cool, fizzy feeling in your mouth.","8.2","The sherbet is a mixture of dry citric acid and sodium hydrogen carbonate. These do not react while dry, but in your mouth they dissolve in your saliva and react together. The reaction is endothermic, absorbing thermal energy from your mouth, and the carbon dioxide produced gives the fizz. dissolve saliva react endothermic absorbs energy carbon dioxide"],[16,"y8c08-r019","Short answer","Explain why sherbet can be stored dry in a packet without reacting.","8.2","The citric acid and sodium hydrogen carbonate are both solids and cannot mix properly at the particle level while dry. They only react once they dissolve in a liquid such as saliva, which lets their particles move and meet, so the packet stays unreacted. dry solids dissolve particles move meet saliva"],[16,"y8c08-r020","Short answer","Stirring potassium chloride into water makes the beaker feel cold, yet no reaction has happened. Explain this.","8.2","No new substances are formed — the potassium chloride simply dissolves, giving a solution in which potassium chloride is the solute and water the solvent. Dissolving still absorbs thermal energy from the surroundings, so it is an endothermic process rather than an endothermic reaction. no new substances dissolves solute solvent endothermic process"],[16,"y8c08-r021","Short answer","Explain why melting ice is called an endothermic process and not an endothermic reaction.","8.2","Thermal energy is absorbed from the surroundings as the solid ice changes to liquid water, which makes it endothermic. But no new substance is formed — it is still water, just in a different state — so it is a process and not a reaction. absorbs energy change of state no new substance still water"],[16,"y8c08-r022","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain what the absorbed energy does when ice melts.","8.2","The particles in the ice are lined up in rows and can only vibrate about fixed positions. As they absorb thermal energy from the surroundings they vibrate more and more, and when they have enough energy they can move and overcome the forces holding them in place, so the solid becomes a liquid. vibrate fixed positions absorb energy overcome forces move"],[16,"y8c08-r023","Short answer","Explain why you feel cold when you come out of a swimming pool, even on a warm day.","8.2","The water on your skin evaporates, and evaporation is an endothermic process — the particles need to absorb energy to escape from the liquid. That energy comes from your skin, which therefore loses thermal energy and feels cold. evaporates endothermic absorbs energy from your skin cools"],[16,"y8c08-r024","Short answer","Suggest whether water freezing is endothermic or exothermic, and explain your reasoning.","8.2","It is exothermic. Melting absorbs energy to let the particles break free, so the reverse change must give that energy back out as the particles settle into fixed positions again. Energy is released into the surroundings, so freezing is exothermic. exothermic reverse gives out surroundings fixed positions"],[16,"y8c08-r025","Short answer","Explain how a self-heating can of food works.","8.2","The can holds calcium oxide and water in two separate sealed compartments. Opening the can breaks the seal between them so they mix and react. That reaction is exothermic, so thermal energy is given out and transferred to the food, heating it without any power supply. two compartments calcium oxide water mix and react exothermic heats the food"],[16,"y8c08-r026","Short answer","Explain why self-heating cans are expensive to produce.","8.2","The compartments must be sealed from one another and from the food, so that the food does not become contaminated by the chemicals. Making and testing those seals costs far more than making an ordinary can. sealed from one another from the food contaminated costs more"],[16,"y8c08-r027","Short answer","Explain why a self-heating food container can only be used once.","8.2","Once the seal is broken the calcium oxide and water mix and react, and the reaction changes them into a new substance. The original reactants no longer exist, so there is nothing left to react a second time, and the compartments cannot be re-sealed. reactants used up new substance cannot be re-sealed once"],[16,"y8c08-r028","Short answer","Explain how a chemical ice pack works and why it needs no freezer.","8.2","The pack has two compartments, one containing ammonium nitrate and one water. Pushing on the pack breaks the inner compartment so the water mixes with the ammonium nitrate and it begins to dissolve. Dissolving is an endothermic process, so the temperature drops and the pack cools the injury without ever being chilled beforehand. two compartments ammonium nitrate dissolves endothermic temperature drops"],[16,"y8c08-r029","Short answer","Compare the advantages and disadvantages of a frozen ice pack and a chemical ice pack.","8.2","A frozen pack can be refrozen and used again and again, which is cheaper, but it must be stored in a fridge or freezer so it is no use where there is no power. A chemical pack can be used anywhere and needs no freezer, but once the compartment is broken the substances have mixed, so it can only be used once. reusable needs a freezer used anywhere once only"],[16,"y8c08-r032","Short answer","Explain how you can tell from these experiments that magnesium is more reactive than iron.","8.3","Magnesium reacts more quickly with oxygen than iron does, and it needs less encouragement to start. Comparing how fast and how vigorously two metals react with the same substance is how you place them in order of reactivity. reacts more quickly vigorously same substance order of reactivity"],[16,"y8c08-r033","Short answer","Suggest why learners are not given sodium or potassium to heat in a Bunsen flame.","8.3","These metals are so reactive that they react very quickly with oxygen even without being heated. Heating them would produce a violent reaction that could not be controlled safely in a school laboratory. very reactive without heating violent not safe"],[16,"y8c08-r034","Short answer","Suggest why learners are not given gold or silver to heat.","8.3","Gold does not react with oxygen at all and silver reacts only very slowly, so there would be nothing to observe. They are also expensive, so using them in a class experiment would be wasteful for no result. do not react very slowly nothing to observe expensive"],[16,"y8c08-r035","Short answer","Explain why a freshly cut piece of sodium is shiny but soon becomes dull.","8.3","Cutting exposes clean metal, which is shiny. The shiny surface soon becomes dull because the metal reacts with the oxygen in the air, and the surface becomes covered with a new substance, the oxide of the metal. freshly cut shiny reacts with oxygen oxide dull"],[16,"y8c08-r036","Short answer","Explain why sodium and potassium are stored under oil.","8.3","They are so reactive that they react with the oxygen and water vapour in the air as soon as they are exposed to it. A layer of oil keeps the air away from the metal surface, so the metal stays unreacted until it is needed. so reactive air kept away oxygen water vapour unreacted"],[16,"y8c08-r037","Short answer","The layer of oxide on some metals stops further reaction. Explain why this is useful.","8.3","The oxide layer covers the surface and prevents any more of the metal from reacting with the air or water vapour. The metal underneath is protected, so the object keeps its strength instead of being eaten away. layer prevents protects the metal underneath keeps its strength"],[16,"y8c08-r038","Short answer","Explain why the rusting of iron is described as a reaction that is not useful.","8.3","The iron reacts with oxygen to form iron oxide, so the iron changes and no longer has the same properties. A strong iron girder can become rusted and fall apart, so the reaction destroys exactly the property that made the metal worth using. iron oxide no longer the same properties girder fall apart"],[16,"y8c08-r039","Short answer","Explain what the four test tubes in the rusting experiment are designed to show.","8.3","Each tube removes a different condition: tube 3 has calcium chloride to keep the air dry, tube 4 has boiled water with the dissolved gas removed, and the others have both water and air. Comparing them shows that the reaction between iron and oxygen only takes place when both water and oxygen are present. removes a condition dry air boiled water both water and oxygen compare"],[16,"y8c08-r040","Short answer","Explain why the same type of nail must be used in all four test tubes.","8.3","The nail is a control variable. If the nails were different — different metal, different size, different coating — a difference in rusting could be caused by the nail instead of by the conditions being tested, so no conclusion about water and oxygen could be drawn. control variable same difference caused by conclusion"],[16,"y8c08-r041","Short answer","Using the results of the rusting experiment, explain two ways of preventing iron from rusting.","8.3","Rusting needs both water and oxygen, so removing either one stops it. You can keep the air away by covering the iron with paint, plastic or oil, or you can galvanise it by covering it with another metal, and keeping the iron dry also prevents the reaction. needs water and oxygen remove one coat galvanise keep dry"],[16,"y8c08-r042","Short answer","Explain why rusting takes weeks while burning magnesium takes seconds, even though both are oxidation.","8.3","Both reactions are a metal combining with oxygen, but iron is not very reactive with oxygen, so the reaction takes a long time to happen. Magnesium is much more reactive, so once it is hot enough it reacts very quickly and gives out energy as heat and light. both oxidation iron not very reactive long time magnesium more reactive quickly"],[16,"y8c08-r043","Short answer","Explain why a silver object that is not cleaned goes black over time.","8.3","Silver reacts slowly with the air, and a thin layer of a new black compound builds up on the surface, so the black colour is a different substance rather than dirt on the silver. Your course calls this compound silver oxide; chemists have since shown that most of the black tarnish is silver sulfide, formed with sulfur compounds in the air. Either way, it is a new substance made by a slow reaction with the air. reacts slowly silver oxide new substance surface"],[16,"y8c08-r044","Short answer","Explain why the hydrogen produced when sodium reacts with water often catches fire.","8.4","So much thermal energy is generated by the reaction that the hydrogen gas produced in the reaction catches fire. The reaction is strongly exothermic, and the heat it releases is enough to ignite the hydrogen it is making at the same time. thermal energy exothermic hydrogen ignites"],[16,"y8c08-r045","Short answer","Write the general pattern for a metal reacting with water and explain what it tells you.","8.4","The metal reacts with water to produce hydrogen and the metal hydroxide. It tells you that whichever reactive metal you use, the same two kinds of product appear, so you can predict the products of a reaction you have not yet seen. metal hydroxide hydrogen same pattern predict"],[16,"y8c08-r046","Short answer","Explain how you could tell whether calcium or magnesium is more reactive with water.","8.4","You would compare how vigorously each reacts under the same conditions — how quickly bubbles of hydrogen appear, or how long it takes to collect a fixed volume of gas. The metal that produces gas faster is the more reactive, provided the mass of metal, the volume and temperature of the water and the apparatus are kept the same. compare how quickly bubbles same conditions fair test"],[16,"y8c08-r047","Short answer","Explain which factors must be controlled to make a comparison of calcium and magnesium a fair test.","8.4","The mass and surface area of the metal, the volume and temperature of the water, and the apparatus used must all be the same. Only the metal should differ, otherwise any difference in the rate of bubbling might be caused by one of the other factors instead. mass surface area volume temperature only the metal differs"],[16,"y8c08-r048","Short answer","Explain how a filter funnel and test tube let you collect the gas from a metal reacting with water.","8.4","The upside-down funnel over the metal channels all the bubbles upwards into one place instead of letting them escape across the surface. The test tube full of water above it collects the gas by the displacement of water, so the gas pushes the water out and you can see how much has been made. funnel channels bubbles displacement of water collects measure"],[16,"y8c08-r049","Short answer","Explain why magnesium reacts more rapidly with steam than with cold water.","8.4","The particles in steam have much more energy and move much faster than those in cold water, so they collide with the magnesium surface more often and with more energy. More of those collisions have enough energy to react, so the reaction goes much faster. particles more energy collide more often more energy collisions faster"],[16,"y8c08-r050","Short answer","Explain the difference between steam and water vapour.","8.4","Steam is formed by boiling water and is very hot, whereas water vapour is made up of water particles in the air at lower temperatures. They are the same substance in the same state, but the particles in steam carry far more energy, which is why steam reacts with metals that water vapour does not. boiling very hot lower temperature same substance more energy"],[16,"y8c08-r051","Short answer","Suggest why some metals react with hot water but not with cold water.","8.4","Reactions only happen when particles collide with enough energy. In hot water the particles have more energy and collide more often, so a metal that is only moderately reactive can now react, while in cold water almost no collision has enough energy to start the reaction. more energy collide enough energy moderately reactive"],[16,"y8c08-r052","Short answer","Explain why the surface of the metal is cleaned with sandpaper before the experiment.","8.4","Many metals are already covered with a layer of oxide formed by reaction with the air. That layer prevents the water from reaching the metal underneath, so the reaction would look slower than it really is. Sandpapering exposes fresh metal and makes the comparison fair. oxide layer prevents reaching the metal fresh surface fair"],[16,"y8c08-r053","Short answer","Explain what it means to say an element is inert, and give an example.","8.4","An inert element is one that does not react, or reacts only very reluctantly, with other substances. Gold is an example: it does not react with oxygen and does not react with water at all, which is why gold jewellery keeps its shine for centuries. does not react gold oxygen water keeps its shine"],[16,"y8c08-r054","Short answer","Explain why copper is used for the roofs of some buildings but magnesium is not.","8.4","Copper is very low in the reactivity order: it does not react with water and reacts only very slowly with air, so a copper roof lasts for many years in the rain. Magnesium is much more reactive and would react with water and oxygen, so the roof would corrode away. copper unreactive does not react with water magnesium reactive corrode"],[16,"y8c08-r055","Short answer","Explain why water pipes are made of copper, and what would happen if iron were used instead.","8.4","Copper does not react with water, so the pipes stay intact and do not contaminate the water. Iron reacts with water and oxygen to form rust, so iron pipes would gradually weaken, leak and discolour the water with iron oxide. copper does not react iron rusts weaken leak discolour"],[16,"y8c08-r056","Short answer","State the general pattern for a metal reacting with a dilute acid and explain why it is useful.","8.5","When a metal reacts with an acid, the products are a salt and hydrogen. This is useful because it lets you predict the products of a reaction you have never seen: you only need to know which metal and which acid were used to name the salt. salt hydrogen predict general equation"],[16,"y8c08-r057","Short answer","Describe and explain what you would observe if magnesium were added to dilute hydrochloric acid in a test tube.","8.5","You would see bubbles of gas rising from the metal as hydrogen is produced, the magnesium getting smaller and eventually disappearing as it is used up, and the test tube getting warm because the reaction is exothermic. bubbles hydrogen metal disappears warm exothermic"],[16,"y8c08-r058","Short answer","Counting bubbles is used to compare how reactive metals are with acid. Explain the weakness of this method.","8.5","Bubbles are hard to count once the reaction is fast, they vary in size so equal numbers do not mean equal volumes of gas, and it is easy to lose count. A better method is to time how long it takes to collect a fixed volume of hydrogen, which gives a number that can be compared directly. hard to count vary in size not equal volumes time to collect a fixed volume"],[16,"y8c08-r059","Short answer","Marcus used dilute hydrochloric acid and Arun used very dilute sulfuric acid. Explain why their results cannot be compared.","8.5","They changed two things at once: the metal and the acid. A slower reaction in Arun's tubes could be caused by the metal being less reactive or by his acid being more dilute, and there is no way to tell which, so no conclusion about the metals can be drawn. two variables acid different cannot tell which no conclusion"],[16,"y8c08-r060","Short answer","Write the key features of a plan that would let Marcus and Arun compare four metals fairly.","8.5","Use the same acid at the same concentration and the same volume in every tube, the same mass and surface area of each metal, and the same starting temperature. Measure the same thing each time — for example the time to collect a fixed volume of hydrogen — and repeat each test so a mean can be calculated. same acid same volume same mass same measurement repeat mean"],[16,"y8c08-r061","Short answer","Explain the practical difficulties of timing how long it takes to collect a test tube of hydrogen.","8.5","Some gas escapes before the tube is in position, the reaction may be so fast that the timing is inaccurate or so slow that it takes very long, the tube is not a precise volume, and it is hard to judge exactly when it is full. Each of these makes the measured time less accurate. gas escapes too fast too slow judging when full less accurate"],[16,"y8c08-r062","Short answer","Suggest how Zara could improve the accuracy of her results when comparing six metals.","8.5","She could collect the gas in a measuring cylinder or gas syringe so the volume is known exactly, start the timer at the moment the metal touches the acid, use a bung so no gas escapes, and repeat each measurement to calculate a mean. Using the same mass and surface area of metal each time makes the comparison fair as well as accurate. gas syringe known volume bung repeat mean same mass"],[16,"y8c08-r063","Short answer","One of Zara's six results does not fit the pattern. Suggest how such an odd result could arise.","8.5","The metal may have been covered with an oxide layer that was not cleaned off, a different mass or surface area may have been used, gas may have leaked from the apparatus, or the timing may have started late. Any one of these would change the measured time without the metal's reactivity being different. oxide layer different surface area leak timing error"],[16,"y8c08-r064","Short answer","Explain how you can tell that a reaction has taken place when a metal is put into acid.","8.5","You would see bubbles of hydrogen gas being given off, the piece of metal getting smaller as it is used up, and the test tube getting warmer because the reaction is exothermic. Any of these shows a new substance is being made. bubbles metal gets smaller warmer new substance"],[16,"y8c08-o002","Short answer","What name is given to the substance that reacts with oxygen when something burns?","8.1","It is called a fuel. fuel"],[16,"y8c08-o003","Short answer","Where is the energy stored in a fuel before it burns?","8.1","Fuels have a store of chemical energy. chemical energy"],[16,"y8c08-o051","Short answer","Do all metals react with oxygen?","8.3","Many metals react with oxygen if they get hot enough. many metals hot enough"],[16,"y8c08-o052","Short answer","How do you know that magnesium is more reactive than iron?","8.3","Because magnesium reacts more quickly than iron. more quickly"],[16,"y8c08-o053","Short answer","What type of reaction takes place between a metal and oxygen?","8.3","It is an oxidation reaction. oxidation"],[16,"y8c08-o068","Short answer","Describe how sodium reacts with water.","8.4","Sodium reacts vigorously with water. vigorously"],[16,"y8c08-o069","Short answer","Which is more reactive with water, sodium or potassium?","8.4","Potassium is even more reactive than sodium. potassium more reactive"],[16,"y8c08-o070","Short answer","Why does the hydrogen catch fire when potassium reacts with water?","8.4","Because so much thermal energy is generated by the reaction. thermal energy"],[16,"y8c08-o081","Short answer","Write the word equation for magnesium reacting with hydrochloric acid.","8.5","magnesium + hydrochloric acid -> magnesium chloride + hydrogen magnesium chloride hydrogen"],[16,"y8c08-o082","Short answer","What type of substance is magnesium chloride?","8.5","It is an example of a salt. salt"],[16,"y8c08-o084","Short answer","Write the general word equation for a metal reacting with an acid.","8.5","metal + acid -> salt + hydrogen salt hydrogen"],[16,"y8c08-w001","Multiple choice","Sofia and Marcus want to find out whether a longer piece of magnesium ribbon gives a bigger temperature rise. What is the independent variable?","8.1","The length of the magnesium ribbon. The temperature rise of the acid. The volume of hydrochloric acid. The type of test tube used."],[16,"y8c08-w014","Multiple choice","Sherbet sweets react in your mouth and give a cool, fizzy feeling. What kind of reaction is this?","8.2","Exothermic, because energy is given out. Endothermic, because the reaction takes energy in and leaves your mouth feeling cool. Neither, because no new substance is made. Exothermic, because it is happening inside your warm mouth."],[16,"y8c08-w020","Multiple choice","What is the chemical name for rust?","8.3","Iron oxide, formed when iron reacts with water and air. Iron chloride. Iron sulfate. Iron hydroxide only."],[16,"y8c08-w045","Gap fill","The variable that you deliberately change in an investigation is called the ____ variable.","8.1","independent"],[16,"y8c08-w047","Gap fill","A reaction that takes energy in from its surroundings, so that the temperature falls, is called ____.","8.2","endothermic"],[16,"y8c08-w049","Gap fill","The chemical name for rust is hydrated iron ____.","8.3","oxide"],[16,"y8c08-w050","Gap fill","The gas given off when a metal reacts with water or with steam is ____.","8.4","hydrogen"],[16,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of a chemical reaction that gives out thermal energy?","8.1","Endothermic reaction Exothermic reaction Oxidation reaction Combustion"],[16,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The substance that reacts with oxygen during burning is called a ____.","8.1","fuel"],[16,"qwen-10-01","Multiple choice","What are the products when sodium hydrogen carbonate reacts with citric acid?","8.2","sodium citrate, water and carbon dioxide sodium hydrogen carbonate, water and oxygen citric acid, sodium citrate and carbon monoxide water, carbon dioxide and hydrogen gas"],[16,"qwen-10-02","Gap fill","Chemical reactions that absorb thermal energy from their surroundings are called ____ reactions.","8.2","endothermic"],[16,"qwen-12-02","Gap fill","Metals that do not react with oxygen, such as gold, are described as ____.","8.3","inert"],[16,"qwen-12-03","Multiple choice","Why do sodium and potassium have to be stored under oil?","8.3","To keep them shiny To prevent them reacting with air or water vapour To make them dissolve faster To increase their temperature"],[16,"qwen-14-01","Multiple choice","Why do you need to use sandpaper on the metal before putting it in water?","8.4","To make the metal smaller To remove a layer of metal oxide so the water can touch the metal To heat up the metal quickly To make the metal shiny for better observation"],[16,"qwen-14-02","Gap fill","Metals may be covered in a surface layer of ____ if they have reacted with oxygen in the air.","8.4","metal oxide"],[16,"qwen-14-09","Multiple choice","Why might it be difficult to see how some metals react with water?","8.4","The water is too clear The metal pieces are too small They may be covered in a surface layer of metal oxide The test tubes are dirty"],[16,"qwen-24-01","Multiple choice","What are the two products formed when a metal reacts with an acid?","8.5","salt and hydrogen metal oxide and water salt and oxygen hydrogen and carbon dioxide"],[16,"qwen-24-04","Gap fill","The reaction between magnesium and dilute hydrochloric acid produces magnesium chloride and ____.","8.5","hydrogen"],[16,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The students are measuring the temperature rise in a reaction between magnesium ribbon and ____ acid.","8.1","hydrochloric"],[16,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Who are the two people conducting the experiment?","8.1","Sofi a and Marcus Alice and Bob James and Sarah Tom and Jerry"],[16,"qwenw-04-03","Multiple choice","Which of these reactions is endothermic?","8.2","A reaction where the temperature goes from 21°C to 45°C A reaction where the temperature goes from 19°C to 16°C A reaction where the temperature goes from 18°C to 20°C A reaction where the temperature goes from 18°C to 22°C"],[16,"qwenw-04-04","Gap fill","Rusting is a chemical reaction that is not ____.","8.3","useful"],[16,"qwenw-05-01","Multiple choice","What gas is produced when metals react with dilute hydrochloric acid?","8.5","Oxygen Hydrogen Carbon dioxide Nitrogen"],[17,"y8c09-f92h1","Click the diagram","Click on the Earth in this diagram.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f92h2","Click the diagram","Click on the north pole of the bar magnet on the right of the diagram.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f92h3","Click the diagram","Click on the south pole of the bar magnet on the right of the diagram.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f92h4","Click the diagram","Click on the magnetic compass on the bridge of this ship.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f92h5","Click the diagram","Click on the end of the compass needle that is pointing towards magnetic north.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f92h6","Click the diagram","Click on the number painted on the runway.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f92h7","Click the diagram","Click on the part of this ancient compass that is free to turn.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the magnet in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the paperclip in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f02a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the bar magnet in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the ruler in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the paperclip in the diagram.","9.1",""],[17,"y8c09-f03a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the spin axis in the diagram.","9.2",""],[17,"y8c09-f04a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the iron nail in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the coiled wire in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the cell in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f05a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the iron nail in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the cell in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the insulated wire in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f05h4","Click the diagram","Click on the switch in the diagram.","9.3",""],[17,"y8c09-f06a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.4",""],[17,"y8c09-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the rubber in the diagram.","9.4",""],[17,"y8c09-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the wood in the diagram.","9.4",""],[17,"y8c09-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the nickel in the diagram.","9.4",""],[17,"y8c09-f06h4","Click the diagram","Click on the aluminium in the diagram.","9.4",""],[17,"y8c09-m001","Multiple choice","What is a magnet?","9.1","Something that will attract magnetic materials Something that repels all metals Something that conducts electricity only Something that can only be made from plastic"],[17,"y8c09-m002","Multiple choice","Which list contains only magnetic materials?","9.1","Iron, steel, nickel and cobalt Copper, aluminium and zinc Gold, silver and tin Plastic, wood and glass"],[17,"y8c09-m030","Multiple choice","Two poles are brought together and the field lines between them point in opposite directions. What is the result?","9.1","The two poles repel The two poles attract Nothing happens at all The magnets lose their poles"],[17,"y8c09-m031","Multiple choice","Two poles are brought together and the field lines between them point in the same direction. What is the result?","9.1","The two poles attract The two poles repel Nothing happens at all The field lines cross"],[17,"y8c09-m032","Multiple choice","Who, according to the story, first noticed magnetic rock about 4000 years ago?","9.2","A Greek shepherd called Magnes A Chinese sailor called Magnes A Roman soldier called Magnes An Egyptian farmer called Magnes"],[17,"y8c09-m033","Multiple choice","What happened to the iron nails in the shepherd's shoes?","9.2","They stuck to one particular type of rock They rusted away in the field They turned into lodestone They became too hot to wear"],[17,"y8c09-m059","Multiple choice","Where does the Earth's magnetic field occur?","9.2","All around the Earth Only at the poles Only at the equator Only at certain times"],[17,"y8c09-m061","Multiple choice","When is a material described as magnetic?","9.3","When it is attracted to a magnet When it conducts electricity When it is made of any metal When it is heated strongly"],[17,"y8c09-m062","Multiple choice","What is steel?","9.3","A mixture that contains a large proportion of iron A pure element found in rock A non-magnetic metal A mixture of copper and zinc"],[17,"y8c09-m088","Multiple choice","What do the three things needed to make an electromagnet also do?","9.4","They are the factors that will affect the strength of an electromagnet They decide the colour of the coil They decide how long the cell lasts only They have no effect on the strength"],[17,"y8c09-m089","Multiple choice","How does the number of turns affect an electromagnet?","9.4","The more turns in the coil, the stronger the electromagnet The more turns in the coil, the weaker the electromagnet The number of turns makes no difference More turns reverse the poles"],[17,"y8c09-m104","Multiple choice","As the number of turns on the coil increases, what happens to the number of paperclips lifted?","9.4","The number of paperclips lifted increases The number of paperclips lifted decreases The number of paperclips lifted stays the same The paperclips fall off straight away"],[17,"y8c09-m109","Gap fill","Magnetic materials include the metals iron, steel, nickel and ____.","9.1","cobalt"],[17,"y8c09-m119","Gap fill","Lodestone contains a substance later named ____, which is a naturally occurring magnet.","9.2","magnetite"],[17,"y8c09-m128","Gap fill","Magnetised means turned into a ____.","9.3","magnet"],[17,"y8c09-m136","Gap fill","The more ____ in the coil, the stronger the electromagnet.","9.4","turns"],[17,"y8c09-r001","Short answer","Explain what is meant by a magnetic field.","9.1","A magnetic field is the area around a magnet where the effects of the magnet can be detected. It is not something you can see, so it is defined by what happens in it: a magnetic material placed there is attracted, and a compass needle placed there turns. area around a magnet effects can be detected attracted compass turns"],[17,"y8c09-r002","Short answer","A paperclip is attracted to a magnet but stops being attracted as you move it away. Explain why.","9.1","The paperclip is only attracted while it is inside the magnetic field of the magnet. As you move it further away it leaves the field, and outside the field the effects of the magnet cannot be detected, so there is no force on it. inside the field further away leaves the field no force"],[17,"y8c09-r003","Short answer","Explain why a paperclip is attracted to a magnet but a copper coin is not.","9.1","Magnets attract magnetic materials, and magnetic materials are the metals iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. Paperclips are made from steel, which is magnetic, but copper is not a magnetic metal, so the coin is not attracted no matter how close it is. magnetic materials iron steel nickel cobalt copper is not"],[17,"y8c09-r004","Short answer","Explain how a compass can be used to detect a magnetic field.","9.1","A compass contains a magnetised needle that is free to turn. When it is placed in a magnetic field the needle turns and points in the direction of the field, so a change in where the needle points shows that a field is present and which way it runs. magnetised needle free to turn points in the direction shows the field"],[17,"y8c09-r005","Short answer","Explain how you can tell from a field-line diagram where the magnetic field is strongest.","9.1","The magnetic field is strongest where the lines are closest together. Near the poles the lines are crowded, which is why the field is strongest at the poles, and further out the lines spread apart and the field is weaker. lines closest together strongest poles spread apart weaker"],[17,"y8c09-r006","Short answer","Two magnets are drawn with field lines. Explain the two ways you could tell which magnet is stronger.","9.1","The stronger magnet has field lines that are closer together, showing a stronger field, and the field lines extend further away from the magnet, showing that its effects can be detected at a greater distance. closer together extend further greater distance stronger"],[17,"y8c09-r007","Short answer","Explain why magnetic field lines must never cross or touch each other.","9.1","A compass placed at any point in the field points in one single direction. If two lines crossed, the field would be pointing in two different directions at the same point, which is impossible, so the lines are drawn so they never meet. one direction at a point two directions impossible never cross"],[17,"y8c09-r008","Short answer","Explain, in terms of field lines, why two opposite poles attract each other.","9.1","When opposite poles face each other the magnetic field lines between the two magnets are all pointing in the same direction, so the fields join up smoothly. This means there is a force of attraction and the magnets are pulled together. same direction join up force of attraction pulled together"],[17,"y8c09-r009","Short answer","Explain, in terms of field lines, why two like poles repel each other.","9.1","When like poles are together, the magnetic field lines in the space directly between the two magnets are all pointing in opposite directions. The fields push against each other instead of joining, so the magnets repel and try to move away from each other. opposite directions push against repel move away"],[17,"y8c09-r010","Short answer","The field of magnet A extends further than the field of magnet B. Explain what this tells you.","9.1","It tells you that magnet A is the stronger magnet. Its effects can still be detected at distances where magnet B's field has become too weak to detect, which is exactly what a stronger field means. A is stronger detected further away weaker field extends"],[17,"y8c09-r011","Short answer","Explain how the pattern of iron filings around a bar magnet shows where the field is strongest.","9.1","Each iron filing becomes magnetised and lines up along the field, so the filings map out the field lines. Where the filings are packed closest together and pile up thickest — at the poles — the field lines are closest together, so that is where the field is strongest. filings line up map the field lines closest together poles strongest"],[17,"y8c09-r012","Short answer","Explain why the iron-filing pattern alone cannot tell you which pole is north and which is south.","9.1","The filings only line up along the field lines; they do not show the direction of the arrows on the lines. The pattern is the same at both ends, so you need a compass, which points from north to south, to identify the poles. pattern is the same no direction compass needed north to south"],[17,"y8c09-r013","Short answer","Explain why iron filings must be kept off the magnet itself during this experiment.","9.1","Filings that land on the magnet are held at the poles where the field is strongest, and they are very difficult to remove. They then come off on other people's hands, and filings on the skin are unpleasant and hard to wash away, so the card and paper keep them separated from the magnet. held at the poles difficult to remove skin card keeps them off"],[17,"y8c09-r014","Short answer","In the paperclip-and-ruler experiment the measurement is repeated three times. Explain why.","9.1","It is hard to judge the exact moment the paperclip starts to move, so a single reading may be too large or too small. Repeating and taking a mean reduces the effect of these random errors and shows up any reading that is clearly anomalous. hard to judge random error mean anomalous more reliable"],[17,"y8c09-r015","Short answer","The magnet is turned round so the other pole faces the paperclip. Explain what this tests.","9.1","It tests whether the two poles of the same magnet are equally strong. If the paperclip is attracted at the same distance d from both ends, the field is equally strong at both poles; a different distance would show that one end has a stronger field. both poles same distance equally strong compare"],[17,"y8c09-r016","Short answer","Explain how a floating needle of lodestone became the first compass.","9.2","Lodestone contains magnetite, a naturally occurring magnet, so the needle is a magnet free to turn. Floating it on water let it turn without friction, and it always came to rest pointing the same way — one end towards the north and the other towards the south — because it lined up with the Earth's magnetic field. magnetite free to turn no friction same direction Earth's field"],[17,"y8c09-r017","Short answer","Explain why the invention of the compass was so important for travel.","9.2","It allowed people to navigate in places such as oceans and deserts, where there are no landmarks to steer by, with less chance of getting lost. With a compass you always know what direction you are facing, even out of sight of land and under cloud. oceans deserts no landmarks know the direction less chance of getting lost"],[17,"y8c09-r018","Short answer","Satellite navigation exists, yet ships and aeroplanes still carry magnetic compasses. Suggest why.","9.2","Satnav systems do not use the Earth's magnetic field; they depend on electrical power and on signals from satellites. A magnetic compass needs neither, so it still works if the power fails or the signal is lost, which makes it a reliable backup. does not need power no signal needed backup if satnav fails"],[17,"y8c09-r019","Short answer","Explain why the Earth's geographic north pole is actually a magnetic south pole.","9.2","Magnetic field lines always point from north to south, and the Earth's field lines point towards the Earth's north pole. A field line arriving at a pole means that pole behaves as a magnetic south, which is also why the north pole of a compass needle is attracted to it. field lines point north to south towards the north pole behaves as magnetic south attracts the needle's north"],[17,"y8c09-r020","Short answer","Explain the difference between the geographic north pole and magnetic north.","9.2","The geographic poles are the parts of the Earth through which the spin axis passes, so they are fixed by the Earth's rotation. Magnetic north is the magnetic pole that is close to the geographic north pole, and it is not in the same place — it is currently in the Arctic Ocean, north of Alaska, and it moves. spin axis fixed magnetic pole nearby not the same place moves"],[17,"y8c09-r021","Short answer","Explain what causes the natural light displays seen in the sky near the poles.","9.2","They are caused by particles coming from the Sun arriving in the stronger parts of the Earth's magnetic field. The field is strongest near the magnetic poles, so the particles are funnelled there, which is why the displays are seen close to the north and south poles and not at the equator. particles from the Sun stronger parts field near the poles"],[17,"y8c09-r022","Short answer","Explain why the Earth's magnetic field cannot be caused by magnetic rocks, as people once thought.","9.2","The temperatures deep inside the Earth are so high that rocks there would lose any magnetism that they had. A permanent magnet cannot survive those conditions, so the field must be produced by something else — the movement in the hot, mostly iron core. high temperatures lose magnetism cannot survive core"],[17,"y8c09-r023","Short answer","Explain how the liquid outer core accounts for the fact that magnetic north moves.","9.2","The core is liquid and moving, so whatever produces the field is itself not fixed in place. Because the source moves, the position of the magnetic poles drifts slowly — about 60 km per year — and has occasionally reversed altogether, which a solid permanent magnet could not do. liquid moving source not fixed poles drift reversed"],[17,"y8c09-r024","Short answer","Airport runway numbers are based on direction from magnetic north. Explain why the numbers sometimes have to be changed.","9.2","Magnetic north moves at a speed of about 60 km per year. Over decades this changes the compass direction of a fixed runway enough to alter its number, so the painted numbers must be repainted to stay correct for pilots using compasses. magnetic north moves 60 km per year direction changes repainted"],[17,"y8c09-r025","Short answer","Magnetic north keeps moving. Explain how its position can still be known accurately.","9.2","It is measured regularly rather than assumed. Scientists survey the field in many places and update the published position, so navigation charts and compass corrections are revised as the pole drifts. measured regularly surveyed updated charts revised"],[17,"y8c09-r026","Short answer","A bar magnet hangs freely on a string. Explain which pole ends up pointing towards geographic north.","9.2","The north pole of the magnet points towards geographic north. Opposite poles attract, and the Earth's magnetic pole that lies near geographic north behaves as a magnetic south pole, so it attracts the north pole of the hanging magnet. north pole of the magnet opposite poles attract magnetic south geographic north"],[17,"y8c09-r027","Short answer","Explain why the hanging-magnet experiment must be done away from other magnets or large magnetic objects.","9.2","The Earth's magnetic field is very weak, so a nearby magnet or a large iron object would produce a stronger field at the magnet and turn it. The magnet would then line up with that object instead of with the Earth, and the result would be wrong. Earth's field is weak nearby magnet stronger lines up with it wrong result"],[17,"y8c09-r028","Short answer","Explain why the hanging magnet is moved to a different part of the room and the direction recorded again.","9.2","If it points the same way in both places, the field turning it is the same everywhere in the room, which is what you would expect from the Earth. If it points differently, something local in one position is affecting it, so the second reading is a check on the first. same direction field is the same everywhere local object check"],[17,"y8c09-r029","Short answer","Explain how stroking a steel needle with a bar magnet turns it into a magnet.","9.2","Stroking the needle several times in the same direction with the same pole lines up the magnetism inside the steel. Once it is lined up the needle has its own two poles and behaves as a magnet, and because steel keeps its magnetism it stays magnetised after the magnet is taken away. stroke same direction same pole lines up steel keeps its magnetism"],[17,"y8c09-r030","Short answer","Explain why the magnetised needle is floated on a cork disc in a non-magnetic dish.","9.2","Floating removes almost all friction so the needle is free to turn until it lines up with the Earth's weak magnetic field. The dish must be non-magnetic, otherwise the dish itself would produce a field and the needle would point at the dish instead of north. free to turn no friction weak field non-magnetic dish"],[17,"y8c09-r031","Short answer","A map shows the Earth's magnetic field strength with lines joining places of equal strength. Explain how such a map is like a field-line diagram.","9.2","Both use the spacing of lines to show how the field changes: where the lines on the map are packed close together, the field strength changes quickly over a short distance, and the labelled values let you read the strength at any place directly rather than only comparing. lines join equal values spacing changes quickly read the strength"],[17,"y8c09-r032","Short answer","Explain the difference between the words 'magnetic' and 'magnetised'.","9.3","A material is described as magnetic if it is attracted to a magnet — iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are magnetic. A material is magnetised when it has been turned into a magnet itself, so it has its own poles and its own field. All magnetised objects are made from magnetic materials, but a magnetic material is not necessarily magnetised. magnetic attracted to a magnet magnetised made into a magnet own poles"],[17,"y8c09-r033","Short answer","Explain what makes a magnet a permanent magnet.","9.3","A permanent magnet has a magnetic field that is always there — you cannot switch the field off and on again. It was made by magnetising a magnetic material, and it keeps that magnetism without needing any power supply. always there cannot switch off magnetised no power needed"],[17,"y8c09-r034","Short answer","Explain how an electromagnet is made and how it works.","9.3","A wire is wrapped around a magnetic material such as iron: the wrapped wire is the coil and the material in the middle is the core. When current passes through the coil the core becomes magnetised, so the whole thing behaves as a magnet with two poles. When the current is switched off, the core loses most of its magnetism. coil core current becomes magnetised switched off loses magnetism"],[17,"y8c09-r035","Short answer","State and explain the main difference between an electromagnet and a permanent magnet.","9.3","An electromagnet can be switched on and off, because its magnetism only exists while current flows through the coil. A permanent magnet's field is always there and cannot be switched off. This is why electromagnets are used wherever the magnetism has to be controlled. switched on and off current always there controlled"],[17,"y8c09-r036","Short answer","Explain two ways of reversing the poles of an electromagnet.","9.3","You can wrap the coil around in the opposite direction, or reverse the connections to the cell or power supply. Both change the direction the current travels around the core, and it is that direction which decides which end is north. wrap the other way reverse the cell direction of the current decides the poles"],[17,"y8c09-r037","Short answer","Explain how you could use a magnetic compass to find out which end of an electromagnet is the north pole.","9.3","Bring a compass near one end while the current is on. A compass points along magnetic field lines, and field lines run from north to south, so the compass needle's north end will point towards the south pole of the electromagnet. The end it points away from is the north pole. compass points along field lines north to south identify the pole"],[17,"y8c09-r038","Short answer","Explain how a bar magnet could be used instead of a compass to identify the poles of an electromagnet.","9.3","Bring a known pole of the bar magnet close to one end of the electromagnet. Opposite poles attract and like poles repel, so if the known north pole is repelled, that end of the electromagnet is also north; if it is attracted, that end is south. known pole attract repel identify"],[17,"y8c09-r039","Short answer","Explain why a fire door is held open by an electromagnet rather than a permanent magnet.","9.3","The electromagnet is connected to the fire alarm. When the alarm goes off the current is switched off, so the magnet loses its magnetism and releases the door, which closes to stop the fire spreading. A permanent magnet could not be switched off, so the door could never be released automatically. connected to the alarm current off releases door closes permanent cannot"],[17,"y8c09-r040","Short answer","Explain how an electromagnet is used to sort scrap metal.","9.3","Switched on, the electromagnet attracts iron and steel and lifts them, leaving the non-magnetic metals such as copper, aluminium and zinc behind. The magnetic metals are then moved away and dropped by switching the current off, so the two groups end up in separate places. attracts iron and steel leaves copper aluminium zinc moved switched off to drop"],[17,"y8c09-r041","Short answer","Explain how an electric bell keeps ringing while the switch is held down.","9.3","When the electromagnet is on, the hammer is pulled onto the bell. That movement breaks the circuit, so the electromagnet switches off and the hammer moves away. The circuit becomes complete again and the hammer is pulled back. This repeats over and over until the power supply is turned off. pulled onto the bell breaks the circuit moves away complete again repeats"],[17,"y8c09-r042","Short answer","Explain why a toaster uses an electromagnet to hold the handle down.","9.3","While the current is on, the electromagnet holds the handle down and the toast stays inside. When the timer switches the electromagnet off it loses its magnetism at once and releases the handle, so the toast pops up. Only a magnet that can be switched off can release at a chosen time. holds the handle current on switched off releases pops up"],[17,"y8c09-r043","Short answer","Explain why only the plastic coating of the wire should touch the nail when making an electromagnet.","9.3","The plastic is an insulator, so it keeps the current flowing all the way round each turn of the coil. If bare wire touched the nail or another turn, the current could take a short cut through the metal instead of going round the coil, and the electromagnet would be much weaker or would not work. plastic insulator short cut current goes round the coil weaker"],[17,"y8c09-r044","Short answer","Explain why the electromagnet is tested by hanging paperclips on the end of the nail rather than the middle.","9.3","The whole nail becomes magnetised, and like any magnet its field is strongest at the poles, which are at the ends. Testing at the end therefore gives the biggest and most consistent effect, while the middle is where the field is weakest and few paperclips would be held. poles at the ends strongest middle weakest consistent"],[17,"y8c09-r045","Short answer","Explain why an electromagnet should be switched on only for the shortest possible time.","9.3","The coil has a very low resistance, so a large current flows and drains the cell quickly. The current also heats the wire, which can become hot enough to be dangerous, so keeping the time short protects both the cell and the person using it. drains the cell large current wire gets hot safety"],[17,"y8c09-r046","Short answer","State the three factors that affect the strength of an electromagnet and explain the effect of each.","9.4","The number of turns in the coil — the more turns, the stronger the electromagnet. The current — the greater the current, the stronger the electromagnet. The material of the core — iron and some types of steel make the strongest electromagnets. Each one can be changed independently of the others. number of turns current core material stronger"],[17,"y8c09-r047","Short answer","Explain how you would investigate the effect of current on the strength of an electromagnet fairly.","9.4","Change only the current, and keep the number of turns on the coil and the core the same throughout. Measure the strength the same way each time, for example by counting how many paperclips the electromagnet can hold, and repeat each reading so a mean can be taken. change the current only same turns same core same measurement repeat"],[17,"y8c09-r048","Short answer","Explain how you would investigate the effect of the core material fairly.","9.4","Change only the material in the core, keeping the number of turns on the coil and the current the same. Use cores of the same size and shape, and measure the strength in the same way each time, so any difference in strength can only be due to the material. change the core only same turns same current same size only the material"],[17,"y8c09-r049","Short answer","Explain what 'soft iron' means and why it is used for electromagnet cores.","9.4","Soft iron is not soft in the way modelling clay is; it is the term for iron that is easily magnetised and also easily demagnetised. That is exactly what an electromagnet needs: it becomes strongly magnetic the instant the current flows and loses nearly all its magnetism the instant the current stops. easily magnetised easily demagnetised not physically soft switches quickly"],[17,"y8c09-r050","Short answer","Explain why a steel core would be a poor choice for an electromagnet that must switch on and off.","9.4","Steel keeps its magnetism once it has been magnetised, which is why steel is used for permanent magnets. In an electromagnet that would mean the core stayed magnetic after the current was switched off, so the device could not release whatever it was holding. steel keeps its magnetism permanent magnets stays magnetic cannot release"],[17,"y8c09-r051","Short answer","Explain how the strength of an electromagnet can be measured in a school laboratory.","9.4","The strength can be measured by the force the electromagnet exerts on a magnetic material. The easiest way is to see how many magnetic objects, such as paperclips or steel pins, it can lift and hold — the more it holds, the stronger it is. force on a magnetic material count paperclips lift and hold stronger"],[17,"y8c09-r052","Short answer","Explain why an electromagnet for sorting scrap metal must be much stronger than the one in a toaster.","9.4","The scrap magnet must lift heavy pieces of iron and steel against gravity, so it needs a large force acting over a distance. The toaster magnet only has to hold a light handle in place against a weak spring, so a much smaller force is enough. lift heavy scrap large force against gravity light handle small force"],[17,"y8c09-r053","Short answer","Marcus finds that more turns on the coil lifts more paperclips. Explain what this shows and what he must keep constant.","9.4","It shows that the electromagnet gets stronger as the number of turns on the coil increases. For this conclusion to be valid he must keep constant the current in the coil, the material in the core, and the size of the paperclips, since any of those would otherwise change the number lifted. stronger more turns current constant same core same paperclips"],[17,"y8c09-r054","Short answer","Marcus thinks one of his results is anomalous. Explain what he should do about it.","9.4","He should repeat that measurement with the same conditions. If the repeat agrees with the trend, the first reading was a one-off error and can be replaced; if the repeat gives the same odd value, the result is real and he must look for a cause, such as paperclips sticking to each other. repeat same conditions compare with the trend look for a cause"],[17,"y8c09-r055","Short answer","Arun can use large or small paperclips to measure the strength of an electromagnet. Explain which choice gives better results.","9.4","Small paperclips are better because each one weighs less, so the number lifted changes by a larger number of clips for the same change in strength, giving finer detail in the results. Whichever he chooses, he must use the same size throughout, since a change in clip size would change the count without the magnet changing at all. small clips finer detail more clips same size throughout"],[17,"y8c09-r056","Short answer","Sofia can vary the current with an adjustable power supply instead of adding cells. Explain two advantages of the power supply.","9.4","The power supply lets her set many different values of current, including values between those the cells would give, so she gets more points for her graph. It also keeps its output steady, whereas cells run down as the experiment goes on and would make later readings lower than they should be. many values in between more points steady output cells run down"],[17,"y8c09-r057","Short answer","Explain the safety risk Sofia must watch for as the current in her electromagnet gets larger.","9.4","A larger current heats the wire in the coil more. The wire can become hot enough to burn her fingers or melt its plastic insulation, so she must check whether the coil is becoming hot and switch off immediately if it is. larger current wire gets hot burn insulation switch off"],[17,"y8c09-r058","Short answer","An electromagnet is held above an iron block on a digital balance. Explain what happens to the reading when it is switched on.","9.4","The electromagnet attracts the iron block upwards. That upward pull partly supports the block's weight, so the block presses down on the balance with less force and the reading falls. The larger the current, the stronger the pull and the lower the reading. attracts upwards supports the weight presses down less reading falls"],[17,"y8c09-r059","Short answer","A record electromagnet was 4500 times stronger than a school bar magnet and used more electricity than three million lamps. Explain what this tells you about the current needed.","9.4","Strength increases with current, so making a field thousands of times stronger requires an enormous current. Since the electrical energy used depends on that current, the machine consumes as much electricity as millions of lamps — the energy cost is the price of the field strength. strength increases with current enormous current energy cost"],[17,"y8c09-o002","Short answer","Name four metals that are magnetic materials.","9.1","Iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. iron steel nickel cobalt"],[17,"y8c09-o003","Short answer","What is meant by the term 'magnetic field'?","9.1","A magnetic field is the area around a magnet where the effects of the magnet can be detected. area around a magnet effects detected"],[17,"y8c09-o005","Short answer","Explain why a paperclip stops being attracted when you move it away from a magnet.","9.1","The paperclip has moved outside the magnetic field of the magnet, so the magnet has no effect on it. outside magnetic field no effect"],[17,"y8c09-o006","Short answer","Describe one way of detecting a magnetic field without using a compass.","9.1","See whether a magnetic object, such as a paperclip, moves because it is attracted. magnetic object moves attracted"],[17,"y8c09-o014","Short answer","Two magnets are placed with like poles facing each other. Describe the field lines between them and the force produced.","9.1","The field lines in the space directly between the two magnets point in opposite directions, so the magnets repel and try to move away from each other. opposite directions repel move away"],[17,"y8c09-o020","Short answer","Do satellite navigation systems use the Earth's magnetic field?","9.2","No. Satellite navigation, or satnav, systems do not use the Earth's magnetic field. Ships and aeroplanes still carry magnetic compasses as well. do not satnav satellite"],[17,"y8c09-o023","Short answer","Explain why the geographic north pole of the Earth is really a magnetic south pole.","9.2","Magnetic field lines point from north to south, and the Earth's field lines point towards the geographic north pole. So the pole they point into must be a magnetic south pole. north to south field lines magnetic south pole"],[17,"y8c09-o028","Short answer","What is thought to be the origin of the Earth's magnetic field?","9.2","The Earth's core. Scientists think the heat generated in the core, which is mostly made from iron, causes it to continually create a magnetic field. core iron heat"],[17,"y8c09-o030","Short answer","Why do the numbers painted on airport runways sometimes have to be changed?","9.2","Airport runways are numbered according to their direction from magnetic north, and magnetic north moves, so the numbers eventually become wrong. direction magnetic north moves"],[17,"y8c09-o032","Short answer","Steel is not a pure metal. Why is steel magnetic?","9.3","Steel is a mixture that contains a large proportion of iron, and iron is magnetic. mixture large proportion iron"],[17,"y8c09-o034","Short answer","What is a permanent magnet?","9.3","A permanent magnet is a magnet that has a magnetic field that is always there. You cannot switch the magnetic field off and on again. always there cannot switch off"],[17,"y8c09-o035","Short answer","Describe how an electromagnet is made.","9.3","An electromagnet is made by wrapping a wire around a magnetic material, such as iron, and passing a current through the wire. wrapping wire iron current"],[17,"y8c09-o036","Short answer","What are the coil and the core of an electromagnet?","9.3","The coil is the wire that is wrapped around. The core is the material in the middle of the coil. coil wire core middle"],[17,"y8c09-o043","Short answer","Explain how an electromagnet sorts scrap metal.","9.3","The electromagnet will attract iron and steel, leaving other non-magnetic metals behind. Switching the magnet off then drops the metal that has been lifted. attract iron steel non-magnetic switching off"],[17,"y8c09-o045","Short answer","Explain why the hammer in an electric bell keeps moving instead of staying still.","9.3","When the electromagnet is on, the hammer is pulled onto the bell. The movement breaks the circuit and the hammer moves away from the bell. The circuit becomes complete again and the hammer is pulled back, and this repeats. breaks the circuit moves away complete again repeats"],[17,"y8c09-o048","Short answer","State the three factors that affect the strength of an electromagnet.","9.4","The number of turns in the coil, the material of the core, and the current flowing in the coil. turns coil core current"],[17,"y8c09-o049","Short answer","What is meant by a 'factor' in an investigation?","9.4","A factor is another word for a variable that will affect something. variable affect"],[17,"y8c09-o050","Short answer","How does the number of turns on the coil affect the strength of an electromagnet?","9.4","The more turns in the coil, the stronger the electromagnet. more turns stronger"],[17,"y8c09-o051","Short answer","How does the current affect the strength of an electromagnet?","9.4","The greater the current, the stronger the electromagnet. greater current stronger"],[17,"y8c09-o053","Short answer","What is meant by 'soft iron'?","9.4","Soft iron is the term used for iron that is easily magnetised and also easily demagnetised. easily magnetised easily demagnetised"],[17,"y8c09-o055","Short answer","Describe the world record electromagnet made in 2019.","9.4","Scientists made an electromagnet 4500 times stronger than a school bar magnet. It uses more electricity than three million electric lamps. 4500 stronger electricity three million"],[17,"y8c09-w020","Multiple choice","Elsa has connectors for use in a circuit. What two more things does she need to make an electromagnet?","9.3","A length of wire to wind into a coil, and a cell or power supply. A bar magnet and a compass. A plastic rod and some paper. A thermometer and a stopwatch."],[17,"y8c09-w039","Gap fill","The region around a magnet in which a magnetic force can be detected is called a magnetic ____.","9.1","field"],[17,"y8c09-w041","Gap fill","The two magnetic metals in the Earth's core are iron and ____.","9.2","nickel"],[17,"y8c09-w043","Gap fill","An electromagnet is made by wrapping a ____ of wire around a metal core and passing a current through it.","9.3","coil"],[17,"y8c09-w045","Gap fill","A result that does not fit the pattern of the other results is described as ____.","9.4","anomalous"],[17,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Magnetic field lines have arrows that point from the north pole to the ____ pole.","9.1","south"],[17,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What happens when two north poles of magnets are brought close together?","9.1","They attract They repel Nothing happens They stick together"],[17,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","A compass contains a magnetised needle that is free to ____.","9.1","turn"],[17,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","If the magnetic field lines of one magnet are closer together than another, what does this mean?","9.1","It is weaker It is stronger It has no field It is a different shape"],[17,"qwen-01-08","Gap fill","The rock called lodestone contains a substance named ____ which is a naturally occurring magnet.","9.2","magnetite"],[17,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","How were the Chinese able to see that a lodestone needle always points in one particular direction?","9.2","It was heavy It floated on water It was made of gold It was hot"],[17,"qwen-01-11","Multiple choice","Why is the Earth's geographic North Pole actually a magnetic South Pole?","9.2","Because it is cold Because field lines point towards it Because it spins fast Because of the sun"],[17,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","In an electromagnet, the wire wrapped around the magnetic material is called a ____.","9.3","coil"],[17,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What happens to the magnetism of an electromagnet when the current is switched off?","9.3","It loses most of its magnetism It becomes a permanent magnet Its strength doubles It changes direction"],[17,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","Which of these metals is NOT magnetic?","9.3","Copper Iron Nickel Cobalt"],[17,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","The main difference between an electromagnet and a permanent magnet is that the strength of an electromagnet can be changed by changing the ____.","9.4","current"],[17,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","What material is used for the core in an electromagnet to make it strongest?","9.4","rubber wood soft iron plastic"],[17,"qwen-03-05","Multiple choice","How can you increase the strength of an electromagnet by changing the coil?","9.4","Decrease the number of turns Increase the number of turns Use a thicker wire only Remove the coil"],[17,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Which two metals can be magnetised when used as the core of an electromagnet?","9.3","copper and aluminium steel and iron gold and copper aluminium and gold"],[17,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","A coil of wire is wrapped around a metal core. Current is passed through the wire to make an ____.","9.3","electromagnet"],[17,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which statement is true about the Earth's magnetic field?","9.2","It occurs only at the poles It occurs all around the Earth It occurs only at the equator It occurs only at certain times"],[17,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","As the number of turns on the coil increases, the electromagnet gets ____.","9.4","stronger"],[17,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Marcus is investigating how the number of turns on a coil affects the strength of an electromagnet. Which of these must he keep the same?","9.4","The current in the coil The number of turns in the coil The number of paperclips lifted The strength of the electromagnet"],[17,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","A steel needle can be magnetised by using a ____.","9.2","bar magnet"],[18,"ch01-f01a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus is used to collect the gas that a water plant gives off in the light. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.1",""],[18,"ch01-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the container that holds the pond water.","1.1",""],[18,"ch01-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the liquid that the plant and the apparatus are standing in.","1.1",""],[18,"ch01-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that guides the rising bubbles into the tube above it.","1.1",""],[18,"ch01-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the organism that is producing the gas.","1.1",""],[18,"ch01-f02a","Diagram labelling","This is a plant cell. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tough outer layer that gives the plant cell its shape.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the thin layer, just inside the outer wall, that controls what enters and leaves the cell.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the jelly-like material in which most of the chemical reactions of the cell take place.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the large fluid-filled space that helps to keep the cell firm.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f02h5","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that controls the cell.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f02h6","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part in which photosynthesis takes place.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f03a","Diagram labelling","This is a cross-section through a leaf, seen under a microscope. Name the layer or part labelled {n}.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the covering on the leaf surface that stops the cells from drying out.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the single layer of cells that covers the top of the leaf.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the layer in which most of the photosynthesis happens.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f03h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the layer of cells at the bottom of the leaf, which contains most of the stomata.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f03h5","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that carries water to the cells of the leaf.","1.2",""],[18,"ch01-f04a","Diagram labelling","These are the tubes used in the hydrogencarbonate indicator investigation. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the living things in these tubes that respire but cannot photosynthesise.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that holds the animals above the liquid, and which is put into every tube including the ones with no animals.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the solution that changes colour according to the concentration of carbon dioxide.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f05a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows the carbon cycle. Name the process or the store of carbon labelled {n}.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the store of carbon that every arrow in this cycle either takes from or returns to.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the process that returns carbon locked in fossil fuels to the air.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the only process in this cycle that removes carbon dioxide from the air.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f05h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the store of carbon that is made directly by photosynthesis.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f05h5","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the store of carbon inside the organisms that break down dead bodies and waste.","1.3",""],[18,"ch01-f06a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus is used to show what happens to water when it is heated. Name the part labelled {n}.","1.4",""],[18,"ch01-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the instrument that measures the temperature of the water.","1.4",""],[18,"ch01-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the curved top surface of the water, whose position you read to see whether the water has expanded.","1.4",""],[18,"ch01-r001","Short answer","Explain what the word photosynthesis means and why the name is a good description of the process.","1.1","Synthesis means making, so photosynthesis means making with light. The name is a good description because plants use the energy they get from sunlight to build a new substance, glucose, out of water and carbon dioxide, so light really is what drives the making. synthesis means making making with light energy from sunlight builds glucose"],[18,"ch01-r002","Short answer","List the three things a plant needs for photosynthesis and state where each one comes from.","1.1","In photosynthesis plants use water, which they get from the soil, carbon dioxide, which they get from the air, and energy, which they get from sunlight. Two of these are raw materials that are used up in the reaction, while the sunlight supplies the energy that makes the reaction happen. water from the soil carbon dioxide from the air energy from sunlight two raw materials plus energy"],[18,"ch01-r003","Short answer","Explain the role of chlorophyll in photosynthesis.","1.1","The energy in sunlight is captured by a green pigment called chlorophyll, which is inside some of the cells in the leaves of plants. Without chlorophyll the light energy would simply pass through or be reflected, so nothing would be available to drive the reaction between water and carbon dioxide. green pigment captures energy from sunlight inside cells in leaves energy drives the reaction"],[18,"ch01-r004","Short answer","Name the products of photosynthesis and explain what happens to the water and carbon dioxide.","1.1","The plants use the energy from sunlight to make the water and carbon dioxide combine together in a chemical reaction. Two new substances are made in the reaction, and these are glucose and oxygen. The water and carbon dioxide are therefore the reactants, which are used up as the new substances are formed. water and carbon dioxide combine chemical reaction glucose and oxygen made reactants used up"],[18,"ch01-r005","Short answer","In the water plant experiment the gas collected is tested with a glowing splint. Explain what result is expected and why.","1.1","If the gas contains a lot of oxygen, the splint will relight. Oxygen is one of the two products of photosynthesis, so a water plant left in sunlight should release oxygen into the tube, and the relighting splint is the test that identifies that gas. glowing splint relights gas is oxygen oxygen is a product of photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-r006","Short answer","Explain why a water plant is used rather than a land plant when collecting the gas produced in photosynthesis.","1.1","A water plant releases the oxygen it produces as bubbles that rise through the water, so the gas can be collected in an upturned tube and its volume or rate of production can be measured. A land plant releases its oxygen into the air, where it mixes with the other gases and cannot easily be captured or counted. gas released as bubbles in water can be collected in a tube land plant releases into air cannot be captured"],[18,"ch01-r007","Short answer","Explain why it was important to leave the apparatus where it would get plenty of sunlight.","1.1","Sunlight supplies the energy that chlorophyll captures, and this energy is what makes water and carbon dioxide combine. Without plenty of sunlight the plant would photosynthesise only slowly or not at all, so very little oxygen would be produced and there would be too little gas collected to test with the glowing splint. sunlight supplies the energy captured by chlorophyll little light means little photosynthesis not enough gas to test"],[18,"ch01-r008","Short answer","In the bubble-counting experiment, the apparatus is left for about five minutes before readings begin. Explain why this is done.","1.1","The apparatus is left for about 5 minutes to give the plant time to settle down and start to photosynthesise. When you can see bubbles coming from the cut end of the plant stem, the plant has reached a steady rate, so counts taken after that reflect the light conditions being tested rather than the plant still adjusting. time to settle down start to photosynthesise steady bubbling first readings reflect the conditions"],[18,"ch01-r009","Short answer","Explain why the bubble count is repeated two more times at each distance.","1.1","Counting bubbles is easy to get slightly wrong, and the plant does not release bubbles at a perfectly even rate, so single counts vary. Repeating the count two more times at the same distance allows a mean to be calculated, which is closer to the true rate, and it also makes an unusual count easy to spot. counts vary repeat and take a mean closer to true rate spot unusual results"],[18,"ch01-r010","Short answer","Explain the first reason why photosynthesis is important for all life on Earth.","1.1","Firstly, photosynthesis provides energy, in the form of chemical energy in nutrients, for all other organisms. Plants use the energy in sunlight to make glucose and other carbohydrates, and these carbohydrates contain some of the energy that was originally in the sunlight, which is then passed to animals when they eat. provides energy as chemical energy in nutrients sunlight energy stored in carbohydrates passed on when eaten"],[18,"ch01-r011","Short answer","Explain why it is true that all the energy in all the food in the world comes from plants.","1.1","Plants capture the energy in sunlight and store it as chemical energy in carbohydrates. When animals eat food, they get some of the energy that was captured by plants, and a food chain shows us how this energy is passed along from one organism to another. Even an animal that only eats other animals is therefore relying on energy that a plant captured earlier in the chain. plants capture sunlight energy animals get it by eating food chain passes it along meat eaters rely on it indirectly"],[18,"ch01-r012","Short answer","A lizard eats a grasshopper. Explain how the lizard relies on photosynthesis for its energy.","1.1","The grasshopper eats plants, and those plants used photosynthesis to capture energy from sunlight and store it as chemical energy in carbohydrates. When the grasshopper ate the plants it took in some of that energy, and when the lizard eats the grasshopper it takes in some of the energy in turn. The lizard's energy therefore came originally from sunlight by way of photosynthesis. plants capture sunlight energy grasshopper eats plants lizard eats grasshopper energy traced back to photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-r013","Short answer","Explain why oxygen being a waste product of photosynthesis matters so much to animals.","1.1","Oxygen is a waste product of photosynthesis, and it is released from the leaves of plants and mixes with the other gases in the atmosphere. Animals and plants need oxygen for respiration, so what is waste to the plant is essential to every organism that respires, and about 20 per cent of the air around us is oxygen because of this. oxygen is a waste product released into the atmosphere needed for respiration about 20 per cent of air"],[18,"ch01-r014","Short answer","Explain how the Earth's atmosphere came to contain oxygen.","1.1","When the Earth was first formed, about 4.6 billion years ago, there was almost no oxygen in the Earth's atmosphere. Scientists think that oxygen first began to collect in the air when bacteria first began to photosynthesise, so the oxygen we breathe today is the accumulated waste product of billions of years of photosynthesis. almost no oxygen at first 4.6 billion years ago photosynthesising bacteria oxygen accumulated"],[18,"ch01-r015","Short answer","Apart from food, explain one other reason why a lizard could not survive if there were no plants on Earth.","1.1","Animals need oxygen for respiration, and oxygen is a waste product of photosynthesis that is released from the leaves of plants into the atmosphere. With no plants photosynthesising, oxygen would no longer be added to the air, so the lizard would have no oxygen for respiration and could not release the energy from its food. oxygen needed for respiration oxygen comes from photosynthesis no plants means no oxygen added"],[18,"ch01-r016","Short answer","Explain why photosynthesis happens inside chloroplasts and why not every plant cell can photosynthesise.","1.2","Chlorophyll is essential for photosynthesis because it captures the energy from sunlight, and chlorophyll is kept inside chloroplasts, inside plant cells. This means that photosynthesis happens inside chloroplasts. Not all cells have chloroplasts, so not all plant cells can photosynthesise, and in most plants the cells in the leaves have the most chloroplasts. chlorophyll is inside chloroplasts so photosynthesis happens there not all cells have chloroplasts leaf cells have the most"],[18,"ch01-r017","Short answer","Explain why plants store carbohydrate and when the stored carbohydrate is used.","1.2","On warm, sunny days plants can make more carbohydrate than they need to use immediately, so they store some of it for use later on, perhaps at night, or at a time of year when there is less sunlight. Storing the surplus means the plant still has a supply of energy at times when photosynthesis cannot keep up with its needs. make more than needed on sunny days store the surplus used at night used when there is less sunlight"],[18,"ch01-r018","Short answer","State the form in which plants store carbohydrate and explain how this allows a leaf to be tested.","1.2","Plants store carbohydrates as starch, and they store the starch inside the chloroplasts in their cells. Because starch only builds up where photosynthesis has been happening, one way to check whether a leaf has been photosynthesising is to test it for starch. stored as starch stored inside chloroplasts starch test shows photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-r019","Short answer","In the starch test, the flame must be turned off before ethanol is used. Explain why.","1.2","Ethanol is very flammable, which means it catches light easily. If the burner were still alight when the ethanol was brought out, the ethanol vapour could catch fire, so the flame must be turned off before any ethanol is used. ethanol is very flammable catches light easily turn off the flame first"],[18,"ch01-r020","Short answer","Explain why leaves are thin and why this suits their function.","1.2","Chloroplasts are mostly inside the cells in the middle layers of the leaf, and leaves are very thin, so it is easy for sunlight to reach these cells. A thick leaf would leave its inner cells in shade, and it would also make it harder for gases to reach them, so thinness helps the leaf do the job it is for. chloroplasts in the middle layers leaves are very thin sunlight reaches those cells easily"],[18,"ch01-r021","Short answer","Explain what stomata are and what they are for.","1.2","There are tiny holes in the leaf, which allow gases to diffuse in and out, and these holes are called stomata, with a single one called a stoma. They let carbon dioxide from the air get into the leaf for photosynthesis, and they also let the oxygen that is produced diffuse back out. tiny holes in the leaf stomata, singular stoma carbon dioxide in oxygen out"],[18,"ch01-r022","Short answer","Use your knowledge of diffusion to explain how carbon dioxide gets into a leaf.","1.2","The cells inside a plant leaf use up carbon dioxide when they photosynthesise, so the concentration of carbon dioxide inside the leaf becomes lower than in the air outside. Substances diffuse from where they are more concentrated to where they are less concentrated, so carbon dioxide diffuses in through the stomata and then through the air spaces between the cells. cells use up carbon dioxide lower concentration inside diffuses from high to low in through stomata and air spaces"],[18,"ch01-r023","Short answer","Explain why the air spaces inside a leaf are important.","1.2","The gases can easily diffuse through the air spaces between the cells inside the leaf. Without these spaces, carbon dioxide entering through a stoma would have to pass through cell after cell to reach the photosynthesising cells in the middle, which would be far slower, so the air spaces let every inner cell be supplied quickly. gases diffuse through air spaces between the cells supply reaches inner cells quickly"],[18,"ch01-r024","Short answer","Explain the purpose of the waxy layer on the surface of a leaf.","1.2","A waxy layer on the leaf surface stops the leaf cells from drying out. A leaf is thin and has a large surface area, so without a waterproof covering it would lose water quickly to the air, and the cells inside would not have the water they need for photosynthesis. waxy layer is waterproof stops cells drying out thin leaf would lose water quickly"],[18,"ch01-r025","Short answer","Compare the amount of photosynthesis done by the palisade layer and the spongy layer.","1.2","The palisade layer contains cells that do most of the photosynthesis, because these cells are near the top of the leaf and contain the most chloroplasts. The cells in the spongy layer do a small amount of photosynthesis, since they are further from the light and their layer is mostly given over to air spaces for gas movement. palisade does most photosynthesis near the top with most chloroplasts spongy layer does a small amount air spaces for gases"],[18,"ch01-r026","Short answer","Explain how water reaches the photosynthesising cells in a leaf.","1.2","A vein carries water to the cells in the leaf. The water is taken up from the soil by the roots and is brought to the cells in the leaf along the veins, so that the chloroplasts have the water they need as one of the two raw materials for photosynthesis. veins carry water to leaf cells water taken from the soil by roots raw material for photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-r027","Short answer","The cells inside a leaf produce oxygen when they photosynthesise. Suggest what happens to this oxygen.","1.2","Some of the oxygen is used by the plant's own cells for respiration. The rest builds up to a higher concentration inside the leaf than in the air outside, so it diffuses through the air spaces between the cells and out through the stomata into the air, where it mixes with the other gases in the atmosphere. some used in respiration higher concentration inside the leaf diffuses out through stomata into the atmosphere"],[18,"ch01-r028","Short answer","Explain what yield means and why farmers add fertiliser.","1.2","Yield is the quantity of crop that the farmer harvests. Farmers add fertiliser to their fields because it makes the crops grow larger and produce a higher yield, since fertilisers contain the minerals that plants need and that the soil may not supply in large enough quantities. yield is the quantity harvested fertiliser makes crops grow larger higher yield supplies missing minerals"],[18,"ch01-r029","Short answer","Explain why a plant may grow poorly even when it has plenty of light, water and carbon dioxide.","1.2","Plants need only quite small quantities of minerals, and they get these minerals from the soil through their roots. But sometimes the soil does not contain enough of certain minerals, and this stops the plants growing to their full potential, no matter how good the other conditions are. minerals needed in small quantities taken from soil through roots soil may lack certain minerals limits growth"],[18,"ch01-r030","Short answer","Explain why a plant short of magnesium has yellow leaves and grows badly.","1.2","Magnesium is needed to make the green pigment chlorophyll, so if a plant does not have enough magnesium its leaves look yellow instead of green. It cannot grow well, because it does not have much chlorophyll to absorb energy from sunlight, and so it cannot photosynthesise as much as it should. magnesium needed for chlorophyll leaves look yellow less chlorophyll absorbs less energy less photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-r031","Short answer","Explain why plants need nitrate.","1.2","Nitrate contains nitrogen atoms, and these are needed so that the plant can convert carbohydrates to proteins. Proteins are essential for making new cells, so that the plant can grow well. Nitrogen is also needed to make chlorophyll, so a shortage affects photosynthesis as well as growth. nitrate contains nitrogen carbohydrates converted to proteins proteins make new cells also needed for chlorophyll"],[18,"ch01-r032","Short answer","Describe what happens to a plant that does not have enough nitrogen.","1.2","Without enough nitrogen, leaves die and the plant stays small. The plant cannot convert enough of its carbohydrates into proteins, and proteins are essential for making new cells, so growth is limited, and because nitrogen is also needed to make chlorophyll the plant photosynthesises less as well. leaves die plant stays small cannot make enough protein fewer new cells less chlorophyll"],[18,"ch01-r033","Short answer","Explain why farmers test the soil in different parts of a field before adding fertiliser.","1.2","Farmers can test the soil in their fields to find out exactly which minerals are lacking in each part of the field. This tells the farmer where they need to add fertiliser, and where it is not needed, so fertiliser is not wasted on ground that already has enough, which saves money and avoids adding more minerals than the crop can use. test to find which minerals are lacking shows where fertiliser is needed avoids waste saves money"],[18,"ch01-r034","Short answer","Explain how GPS technology helps a farmer use fertiliser more precisely.","1.2","The screen in the tractor cab shows the farmer exactly where he is in the field, as well as the results of the soil tests, so the farmer can easily control how much fertiliser is added in each part of the field. Knowing the exact position means the right quantity can be matched to the right patch of ground as the machine moves. screen shows position in the field shows soil test results control fertiliser in each part match quantity to location"],[18,"ch01-r035","Short answer","Compare how plants obtain protein with how animals obtain protein.","1.2","Plants make their own protein: they use nitrate taken from the soil through their roots to convert the carbohydrates they made in photosynthesis into proteins. Animals cannot do this, so they must take in protein that is already made, by eating plants or by eating other animals that ate plants. plants make their own protein nitrate from the soil from carbohydrates they made animals must eat protein"],[18,"ch01-r036","Short answer","Explain why living organisms need carbon but cannot use diamond or graphite.","1.3","Living organisms do not need diamonds or pencil leads, but they do need carbon. Organisms cannot use carbon in the form of an element; they can only use it when it is part of a compound. Diamond and graphite are both forms of the element carbon, so although they are pure carbon, no organism can take the carbon from them. cannot use carbon as an element only as part of a compound diamond and graphite are elements"],[18,"ch01-r037","Short answer","Name three carbon-containing compounds found in cells and explain where they come from.","1.3","Carbohydrates, proteins and fats are all compounds that contain carbon. We rely on plants to make these substances: plants take carbon dioxide from the air and use it in photosynthesis to make carbohydrates, and they then use those carbohydrates to make proteins and fats. carbohydrates proteins and fats plants make them carbon dioxide from the air photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-r038","Short answer","Explain why the carbohydrates in a plant contain carbon atoms that were once part of the air.","1.3","Plants take carbon dioxide from the air and use it in photosynthesis to make carbohydrates. Carbon dioxide is a compound that contains carbon atoms combined with oxygen atoms, so when the plant uses it as a raw material, those carbon atoms end up built into the carbohydrate molecules the plant makes. carbon dioxide taken from the air used in photosynthesis carbon atoms built into carbohydrates"],[18,"ch01-r039","Short answer","Explain how decomposers obtain their carbon.","1.3","Decomposers get their carbon when they break down waste products from plants and animals. Those waste products and dead remains contain carbohydrates, proteins and fats, which are all carbon compounds, so breaking them down gives the decomposers both the carbon and the energy they need. break down waste from plants and animals waste contains carbon compounds carbon and energy obtained"],[18,"ch01-r040","Short answer","The arrows in a food chain represent energy passing along. Explain whether they also show carbon atoms passing along.","1.3","Yes, they do. Animals get their carbon-containing nutrients when they eat plants or other animals, so the same arrow that shows energy moving from one organism to the next also shows carbon compounds moving from one organism to the next. Energy and carbon travel together along the chain because they are both carried in the same nutrients. arrows show carbon as well as energy nutrients contain both carbon passes when one organism eats another"],[18,"ch01-r041","Short answer","Explain how carbon dioxide taken in by plants gets back into the air.","1.3","A lot of the carbon dioxide that plants take from the air eventually goes back into the air again, and this happens when plants and animals respire. The respiration equation is glucose plus oxygen goes to carbon dioxide plus water, so respiration breaks down the carbon compounds and releases carbon dioxide. returned by respiration plants and animals respire glucose plus oxygen gives carbon dioxide and water"],[18,"ch01-r042","Short answer","Explain why plants release carbon dioxide at night.","1.3","Plants respire all the time. At night, when they cannot photosynthesise, they give out carbon dioxide, just as we do. During the day photosynthesis takes in more carbon dioxide than respiration releases, so the release is hidden, but at night only respiration is happening, so carbon dioxide is given out. plants respire all the time cannot photosynthesise at night give out carbon dioxide photosynthesis masks it by day"],[18,"ch01-r043","Short answer","Explain the role of decomposers in returning carbon to the air.","1.3","Decomposers respire too. As they break down waste products from plants and animals, they release carbon dioxide into the air. Without decomposers, the carbon locked up in dead remains would stay out of circulation, so decomposers are an essential return route in the carbon cycle. decomposers respire break down dead remains and waste release carbon dioxide return route in the cycle"],[18,"ch01-r044","Short answer","Explain why the bodies of some dead organisms are not broken down by decomposers.","1.3","Sometimes their bodies fall into places where there is no oxygen, such as a peat bog, or deep in the ocean. In these places, the decomposers cannot respire, because there is not enough oxygen for them, so the remains are not broken down and instead get gradually buried as more and more sediment builds up on top of them. fall into places with no oxygen peat bog or deep ocean decomposers cannot respire remains buried by sediment"],[18,"ch01-r045","Short answer","Explain how buried remains become fossil fuels and how long this takes.","1.3","High pressure and heat change the buried remains into fossil fuels, including coal, oil or natural gas. Changing dead organisms to fossil fuels takes a very long time, and most of the fossil fuels that we use on Earth today were formed hundreds of millions of years ago. high pressure and heat coal oil or natural gas takes a very long time formed hundreds of millions of years ago"],[18,"ch01-r046","Short answer","Compare how oil and natural gas formed with how coal formed.","1.3","Oil and natural gas formed when tiny marine organisms died and fell to the sea bed, which is why oil rigs drill into the sea bed to reach them. Coal was formed from the remains of plants that grew in huge swamps, whose remains were buried over millions of years, slowly turning into coal. oil and gas from marine organisms fell to the sea bed coal from swamp plants buried over millions of years"],[18,"ch01-r047","Short answer","Explain what happens to the carbon in a fossil fuel when it is burned.","1.3","Fossil fuels contain carbon, which came from the carbohydrates, fats and proteins in the dead organisms. When we burn a fossil fuel, the carbon in it combines with oxygen from the air and forms carbon dioxide, and this is called combustion. Carbon that had been locked away underground is therefore returned to the atmosphere. carbon came from dead organisms combines with oxygen forms carbon dioxide called combustion"],[18,"ch01-r048","Short answer","Explain the difference between a fossil and a fossil fuel.","1.3","A fossil is the remains of an organism, or traces of it such as its burrows, that have turned to rock, and we can still see the shape of the organism in a fossil. Fossil fuels do not look like organisms at all, and oil and gas are not even rocks. Fossil fuels are given this name because, like fossils, they were formed a very long time ago and buried underground. fossil keeps the shape of the organism turned to rock fossil fuels do not look like organisms named for being old and buried"],[18,"ch01-r049","Short answer","Describe how a carbon cycle drawn before humans existed would differ from the one drawn today.","1.3","The main difference is that there would be no combustion of fossil fuels, because there would be no one extracting and burning them. Photosynthesis, respiration by plants, animals and decomposers, and the very slow formation of fossil fuels would all still be there, so carbon would still cycle, but the fast return route from buried fuels to the air would be missing. no burning of fossil fuels photosynthesis and respiration unchanged fossil fuel formation still happens fast return route missing"],[18,"ch01-r050","Short answer","Explain why fossil fuels are described as non-renewable resources.","1.3","Changing dead organisms to fossil fuels takes a very long time, and most of the fossil fuels we use today were formed hundreds of millions of years ago. We are using them far faster than new ones can form, so once a deposit has been extracted and burned it is not replaced on any timescale useful to us, which is what non-renewable means. take hundreds of millions of years to form used far faster than they form not replaced in useful time non-renewable"],[18,"ch01-r051","Short answer","Explain why carbon dioxide in the atmosphere is both necessary and a problem.","1.4","Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas that helps to keep the Earth warm, and without any carbon dioxide in the atmosphere at all the Earth would be a frozen place, unable to support life. But at the moment we have too much carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, so more heat is trapped than before and the climate is changing. greenhouse gas keeps Earth warm none at all would mean frozen too much now traps more heat"],[18,"ch01-r052","Short answer","Explain how burning fossil fuels changes the carbon cycle.","1.4","It takes a long time to form fossil fuels, and they can then stay buried in the ground for millions of years. But if we extract them and burn them, we release the carbon in them back into the air in the form of carbon dioxide. Carbon that was taking millions of years to be locked away is therefore returned to the atmosphere in a very short time. fossil fuels stay buried for millions of years extracting and burning releases the carbon returned as carbon dioxide fast release of a slow store"],[18,"ch01-r053","Short answer","Define climate and explain how it differs from the weather on a single day.","1.4","Climate is the long-term pattern of temperatures, wind and rainfall on Earth. It describes what is usual over many years in a place, whereas the weather on one day is a single instance that may be unusually hot, cold, wet or dry without the climate having changed at all. long-term pattern temperature wind and rainfall weather is a single day one unusual day is not climate change"],[18,"ch01-r054","Short answer","Describe how the Earth's climate has varied over its history.","1.4","About 2 billion years ago the Earth experienced the first ice age that we know about. Since then, the Earth has cycled between relatively warm periods and relatively cold ones. In the warm periods there was no ice at all, even at the poles, and in the colder periods, called ice ages, there was ice at the poles. first known ice age 2 billion years ago cycles of warm and cold warm periods had no polar ice ice ages had polar ice"],[18,"ch01-r055","Short answer","Explain what snowball Earth was and why scientists also call it slushball Earth.","1.4","On at least one occasion, perhaps about 650 million years ago, the whole Earth was covered with ice and snow. Scientists call this snowball Earth, or sometimes slushball Earth, because they are not sure whether everything was completely frozen, since slush is melting ice. Scientists still do not completely understand what caused this to happen. whole Earth covered in ice about 650 million years ago slushball because not certain it was fully frozen cause still not understood"],[18,"ch01-r056","Short answer","Explain how dust in the air after an asteroid impact affected plants and animals.","1.4","The dust in the air meant that less light reached the Earth's surface. Plants could not photosynthesise, so animals had less food. As well as the disruption to food chains, the Earth became much colder, because less heat from the Sun could reach the surface, so organisms faced both starvation and cold. dust blocked light plants could not photosynthesise animals had less food Earth became much colder"],[18,"ch01-r057","Short answer","Explain what a mass extinction is, using the asteroid impact as an example.","1.4","A mass extinction is when a very large proportion of the species alive at one time die out together. The asteroid caused a mass extinction: up to 75 per cent of all the species on Earth that were alive at that time are thought to have become extinct because of the asteroid collision, including the dinosaurs. very large proportion of species die out up to 75 per cent caused by the asteroid collision dinosaurs included"],[18,"ch01-r058","Short answer","Explain why most scientists link the extinction of the dinosaurs to an asteroid collision rather than to the impact itself.","1.4","Most scientists think that the changes in climate, caused by the asteroid impact, explain why dinosaurs became extinct around this time. The impact itself only destroyed one region, but the dust it threw into the air blocked sunlight worldwide, cooling the Earth and collapsing food chains, so the climate change killed far more species than the collision did. climate changes caused by the impact impact itself was local dust blocked sunlight worldwide food chains collapsed"],[18,"ch01-r059","Short answer","Explain the difference between a meteoroid, a meteor and a meteorite.","1.4","Objects in space that are smaller than an asteroid are called meteoroids. When meteoroids enter Earth's atmosphere they are called meteors, and some people call meteors shooting stars as they appear like stars moving very fast across the sky. The parts of meteoroids that do collide with Earth are called meteorites. meteoroid in space meteor in the atmosphere meteorite reaches the surface shooting stars"],[18,"ch01-r060","Short answer","Explain why most meteors present little risk to people on the ground.","1.4","They usually present little risk as they burn in the atmosphere due to heat from friction. Most are small, so friction with the air heats them enough to destroy them completely before they can reach the surface, and only around 500 meteorites reach Earth's surface each year. burn up in the atmosphere heat from friction most are small about 500 meteorites reach the surface each year"],[18,"ch01-r061","Short answer","Explain why an asteroid passing further from Earth can be a greater risk than one passing closer.","1.4","When assessing the possible risk to Earth, scientists look at both the mass or diameter of the asteroid and the closest possible approach to Earth. An asteroid passing further from Earth may be a greater risk than one passing closer if its mass is greater, because a much larger object would do far more damage in the event of a collision. risk depends on mass and on closest approach greater mass means more damage distance alone does not decide risk"],[18,"ch01-r062","Short answer","Explain what is causing mean temperatures on Earth to increase now.","1.4","The mean temperatures on Earth are increasing, and this is caused by an increase in carbon dioxide concentration in the atmosphere. Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas, which traps heat energy close to the Earth's surface, so when carbon dioxide concentrations increase, more heat is trapped. carbon dioxide concentration increasing greenhouse gas traps heat near the surface more heat trapped"],[18,"ch01-r063","Short answer","The y-axis of the temperature graph shows difference from the temperature in 1880 rather than actual temperature. Explain why this is useful.","1.4","Notice that the y-axis on the graph does not show the actual temperature; it shows how much it differs from the temperature in 1880. Plotting the difference makes the change easy to read directly off the axis, and it avoids the problem that the actual mean temperature of the whole Earth is hard to state as a single reliable figure. axis shows difference from 1880 change can be read directly avoids quoting an absolute global mean"],[18,"ch01-r064","Short answer","Explain why higher temperatures increase the chance of extreme weather events.","1.4","The higher temperatures mean that there is more energy in the atmosphere. This increases the chance of extreme weather events, such as hurricanes and typhoons, because storms are driven by the energy available to them, so a warmer atmosphere can produce more of them and more severe ones. more energy in the atmosphere storms driven by available energy more and more severe storms hurricanes and typhoons"],[18,"ch01-r065","Short answer","Explain why it is difficult to be certain that storms are becoming more frequent.","1.4","Many scientists think that we are already seeing an increase in the number of storms and in their severity, but it is difficult to be certain, because there has always been a lot of variation each year in the number of big storms. A run of stormy years could be part of that ordinary variation rather than a real change in the underlying pattern. large natural variation year to year hard to separate trend from variation a stormy run may be ordinary variation"],[18,"ch01-r066","Short answer","Cyclone Idai hit southern Africa in 2019. Explain why scientists will not say it was caused by climate change.","1.4","We cannot say, because storms like this can happen anyway. A single storm is one event, and severe storms occurred before the climate began to change, so no individual storm can be pointed to as the result of climate change. However, there seem to be more of them now, and they are more violent, so the pattern rather than the single event is what carries the evidence. single storms can happen anyway no one event can be attributed the pattern carries the evidence more and more violent storms"],[18,"ch01-r067","Short answer","Explain how a changing climate makes growing crops harder, even in places where total rainfall does not fall.","1.4","Rains may come late, or might not come at all, or rain may fall when it does not normally fall, or fall much more heavily, causing flooding. Farmers choose crops and decide when to sow and harvest based on the usual pattern, so rain arriving at the wrong time or all at once damages crops even if the yearly total is unchanged. rains come late or at the wrong time may fall much more heavily flooding farmers rely on the usual pattern"],[18,"ch01-r068","Short answer","Explain why a stable climate over the last 2000 years has been valuable to people.","1.4","For the last 2000 years or so, our climate has been fairly stable. Knowing this helps people to choose the best crops to grow, and to know when they should sow seed and collect the harvest. Farming depends on being able to predict the coming season from past ones, and that only works while the pattern stays the same. climate fairly stable for 2000 years helps choose crops when to sow and harvest farming relies on predictability"],[18,"ch01-r069","Short answer","Explain the two reasons why sea level rises as the Earth's mean temperature increases.","1.4","First, water expands as it is heated, so if the sea temperature increases, sea level rises even though no new water has been added. Second, melting ice caps and glaciers add extra water to the oceans. The two effects act together, which is why warming raises sea level in more than one way. water expands when heated so level rises with no new water melting ice caps and glaciers add water two effects together"],[18,"ch01-r070","Short answer","Sea level has been rising at about 3 mm per year. Explain why this small yearly figure still matters.","1.4","A rise of 3 mm per year seems tiny, but it accumulates: over the rest of this century it adds up to a substantial rise, and scientists estimate that more than 600 million people are at risk from flooding caused by sea level rise by the end of this century. Many megacities are built on the coast, such as Shanghai, Mumbai and Los Angeles, and these are especially vulnerable. 3 mm per year accumulates over 600 million people at risk coastal megacities vulnerable Shanghai Mumbai Los Angeles"],[18,"ch01-o001","Short answer","Explain why it was best to use a water plant in the experiment to collect the gas produced in photosynthesis.","1.1","A water plant releases the gas as bubbles under the water, so the gas rises into the funnel and can be collected in the test tube instead of escaping into the air. bubbles collect under water"],[18,"ch01-o002","Short answer","Explain why it was important to leave the gas-collecting apparatus where it would get plenty of sunlight.","1.1","Light provides the energy for photosynthesis. Without enough light the plant would photosynthesise very slowly and hardly any oxygen would be collected. light energy photosynthesis"],[18,"ch01-o004","Short answer","In the light intensity experiment, the lamp is moved further from the test tube each time. Describe what happens to the number of bubbles per minute, and explain why.","1.1","The number of bubbles per minute falls. Moving the lamp further away lowers the light intensity, so less energy reaches the chlorophyll and the rate of photosynthesis decreases. decreases light intensity rate"],[18,"ch01-o005","Short answer","In the light intensity experiment the bubble count is repeated three times at each distance. Explain why.","1.1","Repeating and taking a mean reduces the effect of random variation, so the result for each distance is more reliable and any anomalous count can be spotted. mean reliable repeat"],[18,"ch01-o006","Multiple choice","Which pair are the reactants in photosynthesis?","1.1","Glucose and oxygen Water and carbon dioxide Water and oxygen Glucose and carbon dioxide"],[18,"ch01-o007","Multiple choice","Where does a plant get the carbon dioxide it uses in photosynthesis?","1.1","From the soil From the air From the water in its cells From the sunlight"],[18,"ch01-o009","Gap fill","About ____ % of the air around us is oxygen.","1.1","20 twenty"],[18,"ch01-o010","Short answer","Explain how a lizard that eats grasshoppers relies on photosynthesis for its energy.","1.1","Grass photosynthesises and stores energy from sunlight in carbohydrates. The grasshopper eats the grass and gains some of that energy, and the lizard gains some again when it eats the grasshopper. food chain energy carbohydrates"],[18,"ch01-o012","Multiple choice","In which part of a plant cell does photosynthesis take place?","1.2","The nucleus The vacuole The chloroplasts The cell wall"],[18,"ch01-o013","Gap fill","Plants store extra carbohydrate as ____ inside the chloroplasts in their cells.","1.2","starch"],[18,"ch01-o018","Multiple choice","Which layer of a leaf carries out most of the photosynthesis?","1.2","The upper epidermis The palisade layer The spongy layer The lower epidermis"],[18,"ch01-o020","Gap fill","Tiny holes in the lower epidermis that let gases diffuse in and out of a leaf are called ____.","1.2","stomata stoma"],[18,"ch01-o025","Gap fill","The quantity of a crop that a farmer harvests is called the ____.","1.2","yield"],[18,"ch01-o031","Multiple choice","Which of these is the word equation for respiration?","1.3","water + carbon dioxide -> glucose + oxygen glucose + oxygen -> carbon dioxide + water carbon dioxide + oxygen -> glucose + water glucose + water -> carbon dioxide + oxygen"],[18,"ch01-o032","Short answer","Explain why plants give out carbon dioxide at night but not usually during the day.","1.3","Plants respire all the time, which releases carbon dioxide. In daylight they also photosynthesise, and photosynthesis uses carbon dioxide faster than respiration makes it, so overall they take it in. At night they cannot photosynthesise, so only respiration happens and carbon dioxide is released. respire photosynthesise night"],[18,"ch01-o033","Short answer","In the hydrogencarbonate indicator investigation, predict the colour in a tube holding a water plant in the light, and explain your prediction.","1.3","It turns purple. In the light the plant photosynthesises faster than it respires, so it removes carbon dioxide from the solution and the indicator shows no carbon dioxide. purple photosynthesis used up"],[18,"ch01-o034","Short answer","In the hydrogencarbonate indicator investigation, why was a small platform put into every tube, even the tubes with no animals?","1.3","So that all six tubes were identical apart from what was being tested. If only some tubes had platforms, the platform itself could be the reason for a difference in the results. same control fair"],[18,"ch01-o035","Multiple choice","Hydrogencarbonate indicator turns yellow in a tube. What does this show?","1.3","There is no carbon dioxide There is a low concentration of carbon dioxide There is a higher concentration of carbon dioxide There is no oxygen"],[18,"ch01-o036","Short answer","Explain how the bodies of dead organisms become fossil fuels.","1.3","They fall into places with no oxygen, such as a peat bog or the deep ocean, so decomposers cannot respire and break them down. Sediment buries the remains, and high pressure and heat gradually change them into coal, oil or natural gas. no oxygen buried pressure"],[18,"ch01-o039","Gap fill","When we burn a fossil fuel, the carbon in it combines with oxygen from the air and forms carbon dioxide. This process is called ____.","1.3","combustion burning"],[18,"ch01-o040","Short answer","In a food chain, the arrows show energy passing between organisms. Explain whether they also show carbon atoms passing along.","1.3","Yes. The energy is stored in carbohydrates, fats and proteins, and all of these compounds contain carbon atoms. So when one organism eats another, carbon atoms move along the arrow with the energy. carbon atoms compounds feeding"],[18,"ch01-o042","Multiple choice","Which two gases are named in the topic as greenhouse gases?","1.4","Oxygen and nitrogen Carbon dioxide and methane Carbon dioxide and oxygen Methane and nitrogen"],[18,"ch01-o048","Multiple choice","What is the correct name for a meteoroid that has entered the Earth's atmosphere but has not reached the ground?","1.4","An asteroid A meteor A meteorite A comet"],[18,"ch01-o049","Gap fill","The parts of meteoroids that do collide with Earth are called ____.","1.4","meteorites meteorite"],[18,"ch01-o055","Multiple choice","Arun collects 18.3 cm3 of gas next to a window, 7.2 cm3 in a shady corner and almost none in a dark cupboard. What conclusion can he make?","1.1","Plants need chlorophyll for photosynthesis Plants photosynthesise faster when they have more light Plants use water for photosynthesis Water plants photosynthesise more slowly than land plants"],[18,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What are the two products made during photosynthesis?","1.1","Glucose and oxygen Carbon dioxide and water Starch and light Chlorophyll and glucose"],[18,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Plants use a green pigment called ____ to capture energy from sunlight.","1.1","chlorophyll"],[18,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Where do plants get the carbon dioxide needed for photosynthesis?","1.1","From the soil From the air From their roots From other plants"],[18,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Plants store excess carbohydrates as ____ inside their cells.","1.2","starch"],[18,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the main reason a plant with low magnesium levels cannot grow well?","1.2","It cannot make enough chlorophyll to absorb sunlight It lacks nitrogen for making proteins Its roots cannot take in water from the soil The leaves become too heavy to support"],[18,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","How do farmers use GPS technology when adding fertiliser to a field?","1.2","To measure the temperature of the soil To control how much fertiliser is added in each part of the field To predict the weather for the next week To count the number of crops in the field"],[18,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Organisms cannot use carbon in the form of an element; they can only use it when it is part of a ____.","1.3","compound"],[18,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which of the following is a compound that contains carbon atoms and is made by plants during photosynthesis?","1.3","Oxygen Water Carbohydrates Nitrogen"],[18,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","When we burn a fossil fuel, the carbon in it combines with oxygen to form ____, a process called combustion.","1.3","carbon dioxide"],[18,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","Why do decomposers not break down organisms quickly in places like peat bogs or deep oceans?","1.3","The temperature is too high There is not enough oxygen for them to respire The pressure is too low The light levels are too bright"],[18,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","Fossil fuels are formed from the remains of dead organisms that were buried and changed by high ____ and heat.","1.3","pressure"],[18,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","What is the difference between a fossil and a fossil fuel?","1.3","Fossils are made of carbon, but fossil fuels are not A fossil is the remains or traces of an organism turned to rock, while a fossil fuel does not look like an organism Fossil fuels are found only in the ocean, while fossils are found on land Fossils form quickly, but fossil fuels take millions of years to form"],[18,"qwen-02-11","Multiple choice","A plant respires all the time, but at night it cannot photosynthesise. What happens to the carbon dioxide the plant produces at night?","1.3","It is used up again straight away in photosynthesis It is given out into the air, because respiration continues but photosynthesis has stopped It is stored in the roots until the morning It is turned into oxygen inside the leaf"],[18,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What happens to the Earth if there is no carbon dioxide in the atmosphere?","1.4","It becomes a frozen place It becomes too hot to live on The oceans evaporate completely Plants grow faster"],[18,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","Carbon dioxide and methane are known as ____ gases.","1.4","greenhouse"],[18,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","What is the name given to the period when the whole Earth was covered in ice?","1.4","Ice Age Snowball Earth Slushball Period Frozen Era"],[18,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","The asteroid collision that killed the dinosaurs caused a mass ____.","1.4","extinction"],[18,"qwen-03-05","Multiple choice","What are objects in space called when they enter Earth's atmosphere?","1.4","Meteorites Asteroids Meteors Comets"],[18,"qwen-03-06","Gap fill","The parts of meteoroids that actually collide with Earth are called ____.","1.4","meteorites"],[18,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","In Arun's experiment, the volume of gas collected in Apparatus C was ____ cm3.","1.1","0.5"],[18,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","What conclusion can Arun make from his results?","1.1","Plants need chlorophyll for photosynthesis. Plants photosynthesise faster when they have more light. Plants use water for photosynthesis. Aquatic plants photosynthesise slower than land plants."],[18,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which colour of light produced the highest number of bubbles in Marcus's experiment?","1.1","Green Blue Red Colourless"],[18,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","In the duckweed experiment, Sofia added one grain of fertiliser to dish B and ____ grains to dish C.","1.2","two"],[18,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Which variable did Sofia change in her duckweed experiment?","1.2","Number of duckweed plants Volume of water Quantity of fertiliser Light intensity"],[19,"ch02-f01a","Diagram labelling","This is the Bohr model of a lithium atom. Name the part labelled {n}.","2.1",""],[19,"ch02-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the shell that fills first, and which is full when it holds only two electrons.","2.1",""],[19,"ch02-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the central part of the atom that holds the protons and the neutrons.","2.1",""],[19,"ch02-f02a","Diagram labelling","This is a card model of an atom. Name the part labelled {n}.","2.1",""],[19,"ch02-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the particle that carries a negative charge.","2.1",""],[19,"ch02-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the positively charged particle inside the nucleus.","2.1",""],[19,"ch02-f03a","Diagram labelling","These are atoms of the first three metals in Group 1. Name the atom labelled {n}, or read off the numbers of protons and neutrons shown.","2.2",""],[19,"ch02-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the Group 1 atom that has the fewest electron shells.","2.2",""],[19,"ch02-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the Group 1 atom that reacts most violently with water.","2.2",""],[19,"ch02-f04a","Diagram labelling","These are atoms of the first three noble gases. Name the gas labelled {n}, or read off the numbers of protons and neutrons shown.","2.2",""],[19,"ch02-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the noble gas whose only shell is full with just two electrons.","2.2",""],[19,"ch02-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the noble gas of these three that has the most electron shells.","2.2",""],[19,"ch02-f05a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows sodium reacting with chlorine. Name the atom, the ion or the change labelled {n}.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the change that turns a sodium atom into a sodium ion.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the particle that has a charge of +1.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f06a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows magnesium reacting with oxygen. Name the atom, the ion or the change labelled {n}.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the change that magnesium goes through when it reacts with oxygen.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the particle that has a charge of +2.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f07a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows magnesium reacting with chlorine. Name the atom, the ion or the change labelled {n}.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the change that magnesium goes through when it reacts with chlorine.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the particle that is left when a magnesium atom loses both of its outer electrons.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f08a","Diagram labelling","Each of these diagrams shows a covalent molecule, drawn as shared pairs of electrons. Name the molecule labelled {n}.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the molecule in which one nitrogen atom is joined to three hydrogen atoms.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the molecule whose formula is H2.","2.3",""],[19,"ch02-f09a","Diagram labelling","This is the giant lattice of sodium chloride. Name the ion labelled {n}.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the positive ion in this lattice.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the negative ion in this lattice.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f10a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows carbon dioxide molecules. Name the type of bond or force labelled {n}.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the weak attractions between one carbon dioxide molecule and the next.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the strong bonds that hold the atoms inside one carbon dioxide molecule together.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f11a","Diagram labelling","This is the structure of graphite. Name the type of bond labelled {n}.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f11h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the weak bonds that let the layers of graphite slide over one another.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f11h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the strong bonds that join the carbon atoms inside a single layer.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f12a","Diagram labelling","This circuit is used to test whether a substance conducts electricity. Name the part labelled {n}.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f12h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the parts that carry the current into and out of the substance being tested.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-f12h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that supplies the voltage for this circuit.","2.4",""],[19,"ch02-r001","Short answer","Explain what the atomic number of an element tells you and how it changes across the Periodic Table.","2.1","The atomic number tells you how many protons an atom of that element contains. The atomic number increases by one with every element as you move through the Periodic Table, so each element in turn has exactly one more proton in its nucleus than the element before it. number of protons increases by one every element"],[19,"ch02-r002","Short answer","Explain the difference between the atomic number and the mass number of an element.","2.1","The atomic number tells you how many protons an atom contains. The mass number tells you how many protons and neutrons each atom in the element has in total. So the mass number always includes the neutrons as well as the protons, which is why it is the larger of the two numbers. atomic number counts protons mass number counts protons and neutrons mass number is larger"],[19,"ch02-r003","Short answer","Explain why an atom has no overall electrical charge even though it contains charged particles.","2.1","Protons have a positive charge and electrons have a negative charge. An atom has no overall charge because the number of protons is the same as the number of electrons, so the positive and negative charges are equal in size and cancel each other out exactly. protons positive electrons negative equal numbers charges cancel"],[19,"ch02-r004","Short answer","A lithium atom has an atomic number of 3 and a mass number of 7. Calculate the number of neutrons and explain your method.","2.1","The atomic number tells you there are three protons. The mass number tells you that, for lithium, the number of protons and neutrons is seven. Subtracting the protons from the mass number gives seven minus three, which is four, so a lithium atom has four neutrons. atomic number gives 3 protons mass number 7 is protons plus neutrons 7 minus 3 4 neutrons"],[19,"ch02-r005","Short answer","Hydrogen has an atomic mass of one and carbon has an atomic mass of 12. Explain what this comparison tells you about the two atoms.","2.1","The higher the mass number, the more mass the atom has, so one atom of carbon has 12 times more mass than one atom of hydrogen. The comparison shows that the mass of an atom depends on how many protons and neutrons are packed into its nucleus, not on the element's chemical behaviour. higher mass number means more mass carbon 12 times hydrogen mass comes from the nucleus"],[19,"ch02-r006","Short answer","Describe the pattern in the mass of atoms across the Periodic Table and give an example.","2.1","The atoms of the elements increase in mass as you progress from left to right, starting with hydrogen, and downwards in the Periodic Table. For example, an atom of hydrogen has less mass than an atom of sodium, because sodium is further to the right and further down and so has more protons and neutrons in its nucleus. increase left to right increase downwards hydrogen less mass than sodium"],[19,"ch02-r007","Short answer","Explain what density means and why iron and sodium have such different densities.","2.1","Density is the measure of the mass for a fixed volume of a substance, given in units of g/cm3. A piece of sodium measuring 1 cm3 has a mass of 0.97 g while a piece of iron of the same volume has a mass of 7.87 g, so the same volume of iron holds far more mass. That is why iron is more dense than sodium. mass for a fixed volume g/cm3 0.97 g and 7.87 g iron more dense"],[19,"ch02-r008","Short answer","Describe how Niels Bohr changed the model of the atom and why his contribution mattered.","2.1","In 1913 the Danish scientist Niels Bohr developed Rutherford's model of the atom further. He had the idea and the evidence that the electrons move in different electron shells, also called energy levels, around the nucleus. His idea changed the model of the structure of the atom that we use today, which is why he was awarded the Nobel prize for his work. Bohr 1913 developed Rutherford's model electrons in shells or energy levels model used today"],[19,"ch02-r009","Short answer","State how many electrons the first three electron shells can hold and use this to explain the arrangement 2,8,3.","2.1","The first electron shell only has room for two electrons, and the second and third electron shells have room for up to eight electrons each. The first shell always fills up before electrons go into the second shell. So 2,8,3 means the first shell is full with two electrons, the second shell is full with eight, and three electrons are in the third shell. first shell holds 2 second and third hold up to 8 fill from the inside out 2,8,3 explained"],[19,"ch02-r010","Short answer","Explain why the electronic arrangement for a boron atom is written as 2,3 rather than 3,2.","2.1","The first electron shell only has room for two electrons and the first shell always fills up before electrons go into the second shell. Boron has five electrons, so two go into the first shell to fill it and the remaining three go into the next shell, which is written as 2,3. first shell holds only two fills before the second five electrons 2,3"],[19,"ch02-r011","Short answer","Explain what holds the electrons in their shells around the nucleus.","2.1","The electrons are held in place by electrostatic forces. Electrons have a negative charge and protons in the nucleus have a positive charge, and opposite charges attract, so the attraction between the nucleus and the electrons keeps the electrons in their electron shells around it. electrostatic forces electrons negative protons positive opposite charges attract"],[19,"ch02-r012","Short answer","Explain what a group in the Periodic Table is and name the elements in the first group.","2.2","The columns in the Periodic Table are called groups. The first group, also known as the alkali metals, includes the elements lithium, sodium and potassium. Elements in the same group have some properties in common, which is why they are placed together in the same column. columns are groups Group 1 alkali metals lithium sodium potassium shared properties"],[19,"ch02-r013","Short answer","Explain how the increasing atomic number and mass number down Group 1 relate to the size of the atoms.","2.2","As you go down the group the atomic number increases and the mass number also increases. These increasing numbers tell you that the size of the atom is increasing, because more protons and neutrons in the nucleus means more electrons, which have to occupy further electron shells further from the nucleus. atomic number increases mass number increases size of the atom increases more shells"],[19,"ch02-r014","Short answer","Melting points fall down Group 1 and the element below potassium is rubidium. Explain what prediction can be made and why prediction is possible here.","2.2","When you look at the melting points you can see that they go down as you go down the group, so for the next element down, the metal rubidium, we can predict it would have a melting point lower than 63 degrees Celsius. Prediction is possible because elements in the same group follow a trend, so an unmeasured member of the group can be placed by extending the pattern. melting points go down the group rubidium below 63 C trend allows prediction"],[19,"ch02-r015","Short answer","Lithium is 2,1, sodium is 2,8,1 and potassium is 2,8,8,1. Explain what this shows about why they behave alike.","2.2","Each of these atoms has one electron in the outermost electron shell, with all the shells below it full. Because the number of electrons in the outermost shell is what decides how an element reacts, all three metals react in a similar way, which is why they are placed together in Group 1. one outer electron each inner shells full outer electrons decide reactions so similar behaviour"],[19,"ch02-r016","Short answer","Suggest why the alkali metals are placed in a group numbered 1.","2.2","The group number matches the number of electrons in the outermost electron shell. Lithium is 2,1, sodium is 2,8,1 and potassium is 2,8,8,1, so every member of this group has exactly one electron in its outer shell, and the group is therefore numbered 1. group number equals outer electrons all have one outer electron lithium sodium potassium"],[19,"ch02-r017","Short answer","Describe the trend in reactivity among fluorine, chlorine and bromine.","2.2","These three halogens are in Group 7 and their reactivity falls as you go down the group. The most reactive is fluorine, then chlorine, and bromine is the least reactive of the three. This is the opposite of the trend in Group 1, where reactivity increases as you go down. Group 7 halogens fluorine most reactive bromine least reactive opposite of Group 1"],[19,"ch02-r018","Short answer","Chlorine has the electronic structure 2,8,7. Use this to predict the number of outer electrons in bromine and explain your reasoning.","2.2","In chlorine the first shell has two electrons and is full, the second shell has eight electrons and is full, and the third electron shell has seven electrons. Bromine is in the same group as chlorine, and elements in a group all have the same number of electrons in the outermost shell, so bromine will also have seven electrons in its outer shell. chlorine 2,8,7 seven in outer shell same group means same outer electrons bromine has seven"],[19,"ch02-r019","Short answer","Explain why the noble gases do not form compounds.","2.2","Group 8 includes the elements helium, neon and argon, and they are inert, meaning unreactive, and do not form compounds. This is because their outermost energy level of electrons is already full, so they have no reason to lose, gain or share electrons in order to become more stable. Group 8 noble gases inert and unreactive outermost shell already full no need to react"],[19,"ch02-r020","Short answer","Neon has an atomic number of 10 and a mass number of 20. Work out its electronic structure and explain why neon is unreactive.","2.2","The atomic number of 10 means there are 10 protons and therefore 10 electrons. The electrons are arranged with two in the first shell and eight in the second shell, which is written 2,8. Both shells are full, and atoms are more stable when the outermost shell is completely full, so neon does not need to react. 10 electrons 2,8 arrangement both shells full stable so unreactive"],[19,"ch02-r021","Short answer","Argon has the arrangement 2,8,8. Explain what this tells you about argon's chemical behaviour.","2.2","All three of argon's shells are full, with two in the first shell and eight in the second and third shells. Atoms are more stable when the outermost electron shell is completely full of electrons, so argon has no tendency to lose, gain or share electrons and is inert like the other noble gases. 2,8,8 all shells full stable inert"],[19,"ch02-r022","Short answer","Describe what John Newlands noticed about the elements and explain the limitation of his arrangement.","2.2","In 1864 there were 63 known elements, and John Newlands arranged them in order of the mass of their atoms, starting with the lightest. He noticed that every eighth element in this arrangement had similar properties, so a similar element appeared periodically. The limitation was that this pattern did not continue through all the elements. 1864, 63 elements ordered by atomic mass every eighth element similar pattern did not continue"],[19,"ch02-r023","Short answer","Explain how Mendeleev improved on Newlands' arrangement in 1869.","2.2","In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev used John Newlands' ideas but left gaps in the arrangement of elements, so that the pattern of eights continued and the elements with similar properties were grouped together. Leaving gaps meant that undiscovered elements did not break the pattern, and the properties of those missing elements could then be predicted from their position. Mendeleev 1869 left gaps pattern of eights continued similar properties grouped"],[19,"ch02-r024","Short answer","Describe Johann Dobereiner's approach to organising the elements.","2.2","Johann Dobereiner grouped elements in threes based on their properties and their appearance, which he called his Law of Triads. At about the same time other scientists began to find out the mass of the atoms of the elements, and it was that mass data that later allowed the elements to be placed in a single ordered sequence. grouped in threes based on properties and appearance Law of Triads atomic masses found at the same time"],[19,"ch02-r025","Short answer","Explain why most elements react but the noble gases do not.","2.3","Atoms are more stable when the outermost electron shell, the highest energy level, is completely full of electrons. The elements in Group 8, the noble gases, already have their outermost energy level full, so they do not react to form compounds. All the other groups do not have full outer shells, so they can react to form compounds and in doing so they fill their outermost electron shells with electrons. full outer shell is stable noble gases already full others not full react to fill the outer shell"],[19,"ch02-r026","Short answer","State the two ways in which chemical bonds can be formed.","2.3","The elements in compounds are held together by chemical bonds, and these bonds can be formed in two ways. Either the atoms can lose or gain electrons, which produces charged ions attracted to each other, or the atoms can share electrons, which holds them together through the shared pair. lose or gain electrons share electrons two ways to bond"],[19,"ch02-r027","Short answer","Explain why a sodium ion is more stable than a sodium atom.","2.3","When a sodium atom loses the electron from its outer shell, which is the outermost electron shell, the next shell becomes the outermost electron shell, and this electron shell is full. Since atoms are more stable when their outermost shell is full, the sodium ion is more stable than the sodium atom. loses outer electron next shell becomes outermost that shell is full more stable"],[19,"ch02-r028","Short answer","Explain why the symbol for a sodium ion is written as Na+ rather than Na.","2.3","When a sodium ion is formed it has one less electron than the atom, so there is now one more positively charged proton than negatively charged electrons. The ion therefore has an overall charge of plus one, and this is shown by writing the symbol as Na+. one less electron one more proton than electrons overall charge plus one written Na+"],[19,"ch02-r029","Short answer","Explain why a chlorine atom gains an electron and what this does to its stability.","2.3","In Group 7, elements such as chlorine have seven electrons in the outermost electron shell, which has room for eight. To fill its outermost electron shell, a chlorine atom gains an electron and forms a chlorine ion. The outermost electron shell in the chlorine ion is now full, so the chlorine ion is more stable than the chlorine atom. seven outer electrons gains one electron outer shell now full more stable than the atom"],[19,"ch02-r030","Short answer","Explain why a chloride ion carries a negative charge.","2.3","When a chlorine ion is formed, it has one more negatively charged electron than the atom, so there is now one more electron than the positively charged protons. The extra negative charge is not balanced, so the ion has an overall charge of minus one and its symbol is written as Cl with a minus sign. gains an electron more electrons than protons overall negative charge Cl minus"],[19,"ch02-r031","Short answer","Explain what holds a sodium ion and a chloride ion together in sodium chloride.","2.3","In an ionic bond there is an attraction between the positively charged ion, sodium, and the negatively charged ion, chlorine. The two ions have opposite charges, so they are attracted to each other, and this attraction holds the two elements together to form the compound sodium chloride, NaCl. ionic bond positive sodium ion negative chloride ion opposite charges attract NaCl"],[19,"ch02-r032","Short answer","A sodium ion has 10 electrons and 11 protons. Explain what charge it has and why.","2.3","There are 11 positive charges from the protons but only 10 negative charges from the electrons in the ion. One positive charge is therefore left unbalanced, which means that the sodium ion has a positive electrical charge of plus one, written as Na+ with the electronic structure [2,8]+. 11 protons and 10 electrons one unbalanced positive charge plus one [2,8]+"],[19,"ch02-r033","Short answer","Explain why potassium is more reactive than lithium.","2.3","Potassium loses the electron in the outermost electron shell more easily than sodium or lithium can lose theirs. The electrons are held in their shells by electrostatic forces between the positive protons and the negative electrons, and the electron in the outermost shell of potassium is furthest away from the protons, so it is easier to overcome these forces. loses outer electron more easily held by electrostatic forces outer electron furthest from protons easier to overcome"],[19,"ch02-r034","Short answer","Explain why fluorine is more reactive than chlorine.","2.3","Fluorine is more reactive than chlorine because fluorine gains an electron in the outermost electron shell more easily than chlorine does. The outermost shell of a fluorine atom is closer to the nucleus, so an incoming electron is attracted more strongly, making the change to an ion happen more readily. gains an electron more easily outer shell closer to the nucleus stronger attraction"],[19,"ch02-r035","Short answer","State what an ionic compound is and when ionic compounds form.","2.3","Ionic compounds are those that are made from ions. They form when a metal reacts with a non-metal, because the metal atom loses electrons to become a positive ion and the non-metal atom gains those electrons to become a negative ion, and the two are then attracted to each other. made from ions metal reacts with a non-metal metal loses non-metal gains"],[19,"ch02-r036","Short answer","Explain how magnesium oxide forms, including why two electrons are transferred.","2.3","A magnesium atom has two electrons in its outermost electron shell, with the structure 2,8,2, and an oxygen atom has six electrons in its outermost shell, 2,6. The magnesium atom loses the two electrons and forms a magnesium ion, Mg2+, and the oxygen atom gains those two electrons and becomes an oxygen ion, O2-. The two ions are attracted to one another and form an ionic bond to make magnesium oxide, MgO. magnesium 2,8,2 loses two oxygen 2,6 gains two Mg2+ and O2- attract to form MgO"],[19,"ch02-r037","Short answer","Explain why the formula for magnesium chloride is MgCl2.","2.3","The magnesium atom loses two electrons and forms a magnesium ion, Mg2+. Each chlorine atom can only gain one electron, so two chlorine atoms are needed to take both electrons and they become two chloride ions. The two chloride ions are attracted to the one magnesium ion, so the compound contains two chlorines for every magnesium and its formula is MgCl2. magnesium loses two electrons each chlorine gains only one two chlorines needed MgCl2"],[19,"ch02-r038","Short answer","Explain how a covalent bond in hydrogen chloride satisfies both atoms.","2.3","A hydrogen atom has one electron in its outermost shell, where there is room for two, and a chlorine atom has seven electrons in its outermost shell, where there is room for eight. The atoms share a pair of electrons, so both hydrogen and chlorine have their outermost electron shells full of electrons and a molecule of hydrogen chloride is formed, with the formula HCl. hydrogen needs one more chlorine needs one more share a pair both shells full HCl"],[19,"ch02-r039","Short answer","Define a covalent bond and state when this type of bonding occurs.","2.3","The type of chemical bond where electrons are shared is called a covalent bond. Covalent molecules are formed when atoms of different non-metals join together to form compounds, and also when atoms of the same non-metal join together, such as two hydrogen atoms forming a hydrogen molecule. electrons are shared covalent bond non-metals with non-metals same element also possible"],[19,"ch02-r040","Short answer","Explain why two hydrogen atoms join together to form H2.","2.3","An atom of hydrogen has one electron in its outermost electron shell and that shell has room for only two electrons. The hydrogen atoms share a pair of electrons, so both atoms have two electrons in the outermost electron shell, meaning the shell is full and the arrangement is more stable than two separate atoms. A molecule of hydrogen is formed with the formula H2. one electron each shell holds two share a pair both full and more stable H2"],[19,"ch02-r041","Short answer","Explain what a dot and cross diagram shows and why the two symbols are used.","2.3","In a dot and cross diagram the electron from one atom is shown as a dot and the electrons from the other atom are shown as crosses. Using two different symbols makes it possible to see which atom each electron came from, even though the shared electrons in the bond belong to both atoms once the molecule has formed. dots for one atom crosses for the other shows where electrons came from shared pair belongs to both"],[19,"ch02-r042","Short answer","Explain what a lattice is and why sodium chloride forms one.","2.4","The ions of sodium, Na+, and chlorine, Cl-, have equal and opposite electrical charges so they are strongly attracted to one another. These electrostatic forces act in all directions and form ionic bonds, so each ion pulls on ions all around it rather than on just one partner. The ions therefore build up into a giant structure known as a lattice. equal and opposite charges electrostatic forces act in all directions giant structure lattice"],[19,"ch02-r043","Short answer","Explain why sodium chloride forms crystals with a regular shape.","2.4","Sodium chloride forms crystals with a regular shape because the ions are arranged in a regular pattern inside the lattice, with each sodium ion surrounded by six chloride ions. The regularity of the arrangement at the level of the ions shows up as the regular shape of the crystal you can see. ions in a regular pattern each Na+ surrounded by six Cl- regular crystal shape"],[19,"ch02-r044","Short answer","Explain why many covalent substances such as oxygen and carbon dioxide exist as simple molecules.","2.4","The forces holding the atoms together inside each molecule are very strong covalent bonds, but the forces between the molecules, called intermolecular forces, are weak. Because the molecules are only weakly attracted to one another, they do not build up into a giant structure and the substance exists as separate simple molecules. strong bonds inside molecules weak forces between molecules intermolecular forces separate simple molecules"],[19,"ch02-r045","Short answer","Explain why diamond is the hardest material on Earth.","2.4","The carbon atoms in diamond form a giant structure in which each carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds. It is so hard because of the strong, rigid, three-dimensional structure of the lattice, which means that scratching or cutting diamond would require breaking many strong covalent bonds at once. giant structure four strong covalent bonds per carbon rigid three-dimensional lattice hardest material"],[19,"ch02-r046","Short answer","Explain why diamond is used for cutting and drilling tools as well as jewellery.","2.4","Diamond is the hardest material on Earth, so it can scratch and cut through materials that are softer than it, which is almost everything. That is why it is not just used for jewellery but is used for cutting and drilling tools, with diamond drill bits having small pieces of diamond powder embedded in them. hardest material cuts softer materials cutting and drilling tools diamond powder in drill bits"],[19,"ch02-r047","Short answer","Explain why graphite is soft even though it is made of the same element as diamond.","2.4","In graphite the carbon atoms each make bonds with three other atoms, which forms layers. The covalent bonds between the carbon atoms within the layers are strong, but the bonds between the layers are weak, so the layers slide over each other very easily. This makes the surface very soft and it easily comes away, which is what happens when you make a pencil mark on paper. three bonds per carbon forms layers strong within layers weak between layers layers slide"],[19,"ch02-r048","Short answer","Explain why graphite is suitable both for pencil lead and for lubricating machinery.","2.4","Graphite is used for the lead in pencils and for lubricating moving parts in machines because its layers slide over one another very easily. In a pencil this means layers come away onto the paper to leave a mark, and in a machine it means the surfaces can move past each other with less friction. layers slide easily layers come away on paper reduces friction between moving parts"],[19,"ch02-r049","Short answer","Explain what a macromolecule is and give an example.","2.4","Some covalent substances such as silicon dioxide have giant covalent structures rather than simple molecules, and these large structures are called macromolecules. Diamond and graphite are also examples, since in each of them the carbon atoms are joined by covalent bonds into one continuous giant structure instead of separate small molecules. giant covalent structure macromolecule silicon dioxide diamond and graphite"],[19,"ch02-r050","Short answer","Explain why ionic substances have very high melting points and boiling points.","2.4","Ionic substances have very high melting points and boiling points because there are very strong electrostatic forces holding the ions together throughout the whole lattice. Melting or boiling means separating those ions, so a very large amount of energy has to be supplied to overcome the attractions between them. very strong electrostatic forces throughout the lattice large energy needed to separate ions"],[19,"ch02-r051","Short answer","Explain why methane and chlorine have low melting points.","2.4","Methane and chlorine are made of simple molecules. The covalent bonds inside each molecule are strong, but the intermolecular forces between the molecules are weak, and melting only has to separate the molecules from one another, not break the bonds inside them. Only a small amount of energy is therefore needed, so their melting points are low. simple molecules weak intermolecular forces melting separates molecules not bonds little energy needed"],[19,"ch02-r052","Short answer","Silicon dioxide is hard and melts at 1610 degrees Celsius. Explain which properties suggest it has a giant covalent structure.","2.4","Silicon dioxide is a compound formed from two non-metals, silicon and oxygen, so its bonding is covalent. A simple molecular covalent substance would melt at a low temperature, but silicon dioxide is a hard substance with a melting point of 1610 degrees Celsius, and that combination of hardness and a very high melting point shows that many strong covalent bonds must be broken to melt it, which means a giant covalent structure. two non-metals so covalent hard melting point 1610 C simple molecules would melt low giant covalent"],[19,"ch02-r053","Short answer","Explain the conditions under which an ionic compound will conduct electricity.","2.4","Ionic compounds will conduct electricity if they are dissolved in water or if they are melted to form a liquid. They can conduct because the ions have an electrical charge, but the ions must also be free to move about and carry the electrical charge, which they cannot do while they are locked in position in a solid lattice. conducts when dissolved or melted ions carry charge ions must be free to move not in the solid"],[19,"ch02-r054","Short answer","Explain why covalent substances made from simple molecules do not conduct electricity.","2.4","Covalent substances made from simple molecules do not conduct electricity because their molecules have no overall electrical charge. Conduction requires charged particles that are free to move and carry the charge, and in a simple molecular substance the electrons are all held in shared pairs within the molecules, so there are no free charged particles. molecules have no charge conduction needs free charged particles electrons held in shared pairs"],[19,"ch02-r055","Short answer","In the conductivity practical you set up the apparatus without the solution and test that the bulb works first. Explain why.","2.4","Testing that the bulb is working before adding any solution shows that the circuit itself is complete and the lamp is sound. If this check is skipped and the bulb fails to light later, you cannot tell whether the substance is a non-conductor or the circuit itself is faulty, so the result would mean nothing. check circuit works first confirms bulb and connections otherwise cannot interpret a result distinguish non-conductor from faulty circuit"],[19,"ch02-r056","Short answer","Predict what would happen if a covalent substance were used in place of an ionic crystal or solution in the conductivity test, and give reasons.","2.4","The bulb would not light, because covalent substances made from simple molecules do not conduct electricity. Their molecules carry no electrical charge, so there are no charged particles free to move through the circuit and carry the current, unlike the ions in a melted or dissolved ionic compound. bulb does not light covalent simple molecules do not conduct no charged particles contrast with ions"],[19,"ch02-r057","Short answer","Compare an ionic bond with a covalent bond.","2.4","In an ionic bond electrons are transferred, so one atom becomes a positive ion and the other a negative ion, and the ions of sodium, Na+, and chlorine, Cl-, have equal and opposite electrical charges so they are strongly attracted to one another. In a covalent bond no electrons are transferred at all; instead the atoms share a pair of electrons so that both outer shells are full. ionic transfers electrons forms oppositely charged ions covalent shares electrons both fill outer shells"],[19,"ch02-r058","Short answer","Explain what the word molecule means, using oxygen as an example.","2.4","A molecule is a group of atoms joined together by covalent bonds, in which the atoms share electrons so that their outermost shells are full. Oxygen atoms join together to form an oxygen molecule, which shows that a molecule can be made from atoms of the same element as well as from atoms of different elements. atoms joined by covalent bonds shared electrons can be the same element oxygen molecule"],[19,"ch02-o001","Short answer","An atom of lithium has a mass number of 7 and an atomic number of 3. Work out how many neutrons it has, and explain the calculation.","2.1","It has 4 neutrons. The atomic number gives the 3 protons, and the mass number counts protons plus neutrons, so the neutrons are 7 - 3 = 4. 4 mass number protons"],[19,"ch02-o002","Short answer","Explain why an atom has no overall electrical charge.","2.1","An atom contains the same number of protons as electrons. Each proton carries +1 and each electron carries -1, so the positive and negative charges cancel out exactly. same number protons electrons cancel"],[19,"ch02-o003","Gap fill","The atomic number of an element tells you how many ____ its atoms contain.","2.1","protons"],[19,"ch02-o004","Gap fill","The mass number tells you how many ____ and ____ each atom has in total.","2.1","protons neutrons"],[19,"ch02-o005","Multiple choice","What is the charge on a proton and on an electron?","2.1","Proton negative, electron positive Proton positive, electron negative Both positive Both negative"],[19,"ch02-o006","Short answer","One atom of carbon has an atomic mass of 12 and one atom of hydrogen has an atomic mass of 1. How many times more mass does the carbon atom have, and what general rule does this show?","2.1","Twelve times more mass. It shows that the higher the mass number, the more mass the atom has. 12 higher mass number"],[19,"ch02-o007","Short answer","1 cm3 of sodium has a mass of 0.97 g and 1 cm3 of iron has a mass of 7.87 g. What quantity is being compared here, and which metal is more dense?","2.1","Density, which is the mass of a fixed volume of a substance, measured in g/cm3. Iron is more dense than sodium. density iron fixed volume"],[19,"ch02-o008","Short answer","What was Niels Bohr's contribution to the model of the atom?","2.1","He had the idea, with evidence, that the electrons move in separate electron shells, also called energy levels, around the nucleus, rather than in a single cloud. electron shells energy levels nucleus"],[19,"ch02-o009","Gap fill","The first electron shell has room for only ____ electrons, while the second and third shells each have room for up to ____ electrons.","2.1","two eight"],[19,"ch02-o010","Short answer","What holds the electrons in place in their shells?","2.1","Electrostatic forces - the attraction between the positively charged protons in the nucleus and the negatively charged electrons. electrostatic forces attraction charge"],[19,"ch02-o011","Short answer","A boron atom has 5 electrons. Explain why its electronic structure is written 2,3 and not 3,2.","2.1","The first shell always fills up before any electrons go into the second shell. The first shell holds two, so the remaining three go into the second shell, giving 2,3. first shell fills up before"],[19,"ch02-o012","Short answer","In the card model of an atom in Activity 2.1.1, what do the concentric rings of card represent, and what do the small green circles stuck on them represent?","2.1","The concentric rings represent the electron shells, and the small green circles stuck onto them represent the electrons. electron shells electrons"],[19,"ch02-o014","Multiple choice","Which three of these elements are all in Group 1?","2.2","Helium, neon and argon Fluorine, chlorine and bromine Lithium, sodium and potassium Carbon, silicon and nitrogen"],[19,"ch02-o015","Short answer","Both the atomic number and the mass number rise as you go down Group 1. What does that tell you about the atoms themselves?","2.2","It tells you that the size of the atom is increasing as you go down the group. size increasing bigger"],[19,"ch02-o016","Short answer","Potassium melts at 63 degrees Celsius. Rubidium is the next element down in Group 1. Predict its melting point and explain your prediction.","2.2","Lower than 63 degrees Celsius, because the melting points go down as you go down the group. lower 63 down the group"],[19,"ch02-o017","Short answer","Lithium, sodium and potassium have very similar chemical properties. Which feature of their atomic structure explains this?","2.2","Every one of them has exactly one electron in its outermost electron shell, with the shells below it full. Because they all react by losing that single outer electron, they behave in the same way. one electron outermost outer shell"],[19,"ch02-o018","Short answer","Sodium has an atomic number of 11 and a mass number of 23. State the number of protons, electrons and neutrons in one sodium atom, and give its electronic structure.","2.2","11 protons, 11 electrons and 12 neutrons. The electronic structure is 2,8,1. 11 12 2,8,1"],[19,"ch02-o019","Gap fill","The electronic structure of potassium is 2,8,8,1, so a potassium atom has ____ electron in its outermost shell.","2.2","one 1"],[19,"ch02-o020","Short answer","What is the name given to the elements of Group 7, and name three of them.","2.2","The halogens are Group 7. The three named are fluorine, chlorine and bromine. Group 7 fluorine chlorine bromine"],[19,"ch02-o022","Multiple choice","At room temperature, which statement about the first three halogens is correct?","2.2","All three are gases Fluorine and chlorine are gases and bromine is a liquid All three are liquids Fluorine is a solid and the other two are gases"],[19,"ch02-o024","Short answer","Why are the elements of Group 8 called the noble gases, and what does 'inert' mean?","2.2","They are all gases, and they are inert - unreactive - so they do not form compounds. Their outer electron shells are already full, so they have no reason to react. gases inert unreactive do not form compounds"],[19,"ch02-o027","Short answer","State the single rule that explains why most elements react at all.","2.3","Atoms are more stable when their outermost electron shell is completely full of electrons, so atoms with incomplete outer shells react in order to fill them. stable outermost full"],[19,"ch02-o028","Short answer","Explain why the noble gases do not react to form compounds, using the idea of electron shells.","2.3","Their outermost energy level of electrons is already full, so they are already stable and have nothing to gain by losing, gaining or sharing electrons. full outermost stable"],[19,"ch02-o029","Multiple choice","What is formed when a sodium atom loses an electron?","2.3","A sodium molecule An ion A neutron A new element"],[19,"ch02-o036","Gap fill","Ionic compounds are made from ions. They form when a ____ reacts with a ____.","2.3","metal non-metal"],[19,"ch02-o041","Gap fill","A diagram that draws one atom's electrons as dots and the other atom's as crosses is called a ____ diagram.","2.3","dot and cross"],[19,"ch02-o042","Multiple choice","What name is given to a chemical bond in which electrons are shared?","2.3","An ionic bond A covalent bond An electrostatic bond A metallic bond"],[19,"ch02-o045","Short answer","Explain why sodium chloride forms a giant lattice rather than separate NaCl molecules.","2.4","The Na+ and Cl- ions have equal and opposite charges, so the electrostatic forces between them are strong and act in all directions. Every ion therefore attracts ions all around it, and the structure repeats outwards as a giant lattice. all directions electrostatic lattice"],[19,"ch02-o046","Gap fill","In the sodium chloride lattice, each sodium ion is surrounded by ____ chloride ions.","2.4","six 6"],[19,"ch02-o048","Short answer","In a substance made of simple molecules, which forces are strong and which are weak?","2.4","The covalent bonds holding the atoms together inside each molecule are very strong, but the intermolecular forces between one molecule and the next are weak. inside strong between weak intermolecular"],[19,"ch02-o051","Gap fill","Giant covalent structures such as diamond, graphite and silicon dioxide are also called ____.","2.4","macromolecules"],[19,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What does the atomic number of an element tell you?","2.1","The total number of protons and neutrons The number of protons in the atom The number of electrons in the outer shell The mass of a single atom"],[19,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The mass number of an atom is the total count of protons and ____.","2.1","neutrons"],[19,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Why does an atom have no overall charge?","2.1","Protons and neutrons cancel each other out The number of protons equals the number of electrons Electrons are neutral particles Neutrons have a positive charge"],[19,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","The first electron shell can hold a maximum of ____ electrons.","2.1","two"],[19,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","What is the trend in melting points as you go down Group 1 (alkali metals)?","2.2","They increase They stay the same They decrease They become zero"],[19,"qwen-01-08","Gap fill","The elements in Group 1 are also known as the ____ metals.","2.2","alkali"],[19,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","What is the electronic structure of a sodium atom?","2.2","2,1 2,8,1 2,8,8,1 2,7"],[19,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","Fluorine and chlorine are gases at room temperature, while bromine is a ____.","2.2","liquid"],[19,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Why is the chloride ion written as Cl-?","2.3","It has one more proton than electrons It has one more electron than protons It has lost a neutron It has gained a proton"],[19,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","The bond formed between a sodium ion and a chlorine ion is called an ____ bond.","2.3","ionic"],[19,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which element is more reactive: potassium or lithium?","2.3","Lithium Potassium They are equally reactive Neither reacts"],[19,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","Electrons are held in their shells by ____ forces.","2.3","electrostatic"],[19,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the giant structure formed by ions in sodium chloride?","2.4","lattice molecule layer chain"],[19,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","The forces between the ions in an ionic compound are called ____ forces.","2.4","electrostatic"],[19,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","How many chloride ions surround each sodium ion in the lattice structure of sodium chloride?","2.4","four six eight two"],[19,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","The weak forces that hold simple covalent molecules together are called ____ forces.","2.4","intermolecular"],[19,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The mass number of an atom is the sum of its protons and ____.","2.1","neutrons"],[19,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","In a neutral atom, the number of electrons is always equal to the number of ____.","2.1","neutrons protons shells groups"],[19,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","Lithium, sodium, and potassium are all elements found in Group ____ of the Periodic Table.","2.2","1"],[19,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","The most reactive element in Group 7 is ____.","2.2","fluorine"],[19,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","Diamond and graphite are both giant structures formed from the element ____.","2.4","carbon"],[19,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Why is graphite a very soft material?","2.4","It has strong bonds between layers Its structure allows layers to slide over each other It is made of metal atoms It has a high melting point"],[19,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","The formula for calcium chloride is CaCl____.","2.3","2"],[19,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","Which of these substances is a liquid at room temperature?","2.4","Potassium chloride Bromine Magnesium oxide Ammonia"],[19,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","Of lithium, sodium and potassium, the element with the highest melting point is ____.","2.2","lithium"],[20,"ch03-f01a","Diagram labelling","Five liquids of different density have been poured into the same jar and have settled into layers. Name the liquid labelled {n}.","3.1",""],[20,"ch03-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the liquid with the greatest density.","3.1",""],[20,"ch03-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the liquid with the lowest density.","3.1",""],[20,"ch03-f02a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus heats water with an immersion heater and measures the energy transferred. Name the part labelled {n}.","3.2",""],[20,"ch03-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that transfers energy into the water.","3.2",""],[20,"ch03-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the instrument that measures how hot the water has become.","3.2",""],[20,"ch03-f03a","Diagram labelling","This is the energy diagram for an electric lamp. Name the energy labelled {n}.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the energy that goes into the lamp.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the wasted energy output of this lamp.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f04a","Diagram labelling","This is the energy diagram for a car engine. Write down the label at position {n}.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the useful energy output of a car engine.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the largest single energy output of this engine.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f05a","Diagram labelling","Two bottles of water hang from the same supporting string. Name the part labelled {n}.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part that has to hold the whole weight of both bottles.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the objects that swing and pass energy from one to the other.","3.3",""],[20,"ch03-f06a","Diagram labelling","Water carries thermal energy away from a car engine. Name the part labelled {n}.","3.4",""],[20,"ch03-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part where the thermal energy is produced.","3.4",""],[20,"ch03-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the part where the thermal energy is dissipated into the air.","3.4",""],[20,"ch03-f07a","Diagram labelling","A convection current carries thermal energy from a heater around a room. Write down the label at position {n}.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that explains why the air directly above the heater moves upwards.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label that explains why the air on the far side of the room moves downwards.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f08a","Diagram labelling","A pan of water is heated only at the bottom. Write down the label at position {n}.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that says what the water directly above the flame does.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f09a","Diagram labelling","This is a section through a vacuum flask. Name the part labelled {n}.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the empty gap that stops conduction and convection.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f10a","Diagram labelling","This is a hot water tank with an electric heater near the bottom. Name the part labelled {n}.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the pipe that takes the hottest water away, at the top of the tank.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f11a","Diagram labelling","Coloured ink has been dropped into one side of a beaker of water that is being heated. Name the substance labelled {n}.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f11h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the substance added so that the movement of the water can be seen.","3.5",""],[20,"ch03-f12a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows particles at the surface of a liquid. Write down the label at position {n}.","3.6",""],[20,"ch03-f12h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that describes the particles that leave the liquid altogether.","3.6",""],[20,"ch03-f12h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label that describes the particles that stay in the liquid.","3.6",""],[20,"ch03-o001","Short answer","Marcus says that metal is always heavier than polystyrene. Explain why this statement is not always true.","3.1","A very large block of polystyrene can have a larger mass than a small piece of metal, because mass depends on volume as well as material. What is always true is that the same volume of iron has a larger mass than the same volume of polystyrene. same volume larger mass polystyrene"],[20,"ch03-o002","Gap fill","A container that has a space filled with air inside it is described as ____, and the word ____ is used as its opposite.","3.1","hollow solid"],[20,"ch03-o003","Short answer","State what the word regular means when it is used to describe a block of material, and explain how you find the volume of such a block.","3.1","Regular means that the faces of the object meet at right angles to each other. You measure the lengths of the sides and multiply them together. right angles multiply sides"],[20,"ch03-o004","Short answer","A block of wood measures 10 mm by 6 mm by 4 mm. Calculate its volume and state the unit.","3.1","10 x 6 x 4 = 240 mm3. The unit is mm3 because the lengths of the sides are given in millimetres. 240 mm3"],[20,"ch03-o005","Short answer","Explain why the volume of an irregular object cannot be found by measuring its sides, and describe the method that is used instead.","3.1","Irregular means the sides or angles of the shape are not equal, so there is no set of side lengths to multiply. Instead the displacement method is used: the object is placed into a measuring cylinder of water and the increase in the volume of the water is the volume of the object. not equal displacement increase in volume"],[20,"ch03-o006","Short answer","A rock is lowered into a measuring cylinder containing 40 cm3 of water. The water level rises to the 56 cm3 mark. Calculate the volume of the rock and explain your working.","3.1","The volume of the rock is 56 - 40 = 16 cm3, because the increase in the volume of the water is equal to the volume of the object placed in it. 16 cm3 increase"],[20,"ch03-o007","Gap fill","Density means mass per unit ____, and is calculated using the equation density = mass divided by ____.","3.1","volume volume"],[20,"ch03-o008","Short answer","A block of iron measuring 1 cm x 1 cm x 1 cm has a mass of 7.9 g. Calculate its density and give the unit.","3.1","The volume is 1 x 1 x 1 = 1 cm3, so density = 7.9 / 1 = 7.9 g/cm3. The unit is g/cm3 because the mass is in grams and the volume is in cm3. 7.9 g/cm3"],[20,"ch03-o009","Short answer","Copper has a density of 8.96 g/cm3. Calculate the mass of a piece of copper with a volume of 20 cm3.","3.1","mass = density x volume = 8.96 x 20 = 179.2 g. 179.2 g density × volume"],[20,"ch03-o010","Short answer","Explain, using particles, why gases have lower densities than liquids or solids.","3.1","The particles in a gas are far apart, so a given volume of gas contains far fewer particles and therefore has a much smaller mass than the same volume of a liquid or solid. far apart fewer particles volume"],[20,"ch03-o011","Gap fill","As particles in a material get packed closer together, the density of the material ____. So the materials with the highest densities are all ____.","3.1","increases solids"],[20,"ch03-o012","Short answer","Osmium has atomic number 76 and iron has atomic number 26. Use this information to compare their densities and state the general pattern for the elements.","3.1","Osmium is more dense than iron. For the elements, the density usually increases as the atomic number increases. osmium more dense atomic number"],[20,"ch03-o013","Multiple choice","An object has a density of 1.2 g/cm3. Water has a density of 1.0 g/cm3. What will happen when the object is placed in water?","3.1","It will float, because it is less dense than water It will sink, because it is more dense than water It will float, because it is more dense than water It will stay exactly halfway down, because the densities are close"],[20,"ch03-o014","Short answer","Swimming pool floats are made from polystyrene, or are hollow with only air inside. Explain why both designs work.","3.1","Both designs give the float a very low density. Polystyrene has a very low density itself, and making an object hollow gives it a large volume for a small mass, so its density is much less than the density of water and it floats. low density air less dense than water"],[20,"ch03-o015","Short answer","Steel has a density of 8-9 g/cm3, which is much greater than the density of water. Explain how a ship built from steel can float.","3.1","Ships are not solid blocks of steel. There are large spaces inside ships that contain only air, so the ship has a very large volume. The average density, which is the mass of all the materials in the ship including the air divided by the total volume, is less than the density of water. large spaces air average density less than"],[20,"ch03-o016","Short answer","Explain why loading a ship with too much cargo can make it sink.","3.1","Cargo, passengers and fuel all add mass to a ship, which increases its density. If the density of the ship becomes higher than the density of water, the ship will no longer float. add mass increases its density higher than"],[20,"ch03-o017","Short answer","Three liquids are poured carefully into the same container and form separate layers. Explain how you can work out which liquid has the highest density.","3.1","When liquids are added together carefully, the less dense liquids float on the more dense liquids, so the layers arrange themselves in order of density. The liquid in the bottom layer has the highest density and the liquid in the top layer has the lowest. less dense float bottom highest density"],[20,"ch03-o018","Short answer","Explain why oil spilled from a ship is washed up on beaches rather than sinking to the sea bed.","3.1","Crude oil is less dense than water, so when it spills out of ships the oil floats on the water. Waves then push the floating oil onto the beach. Oil spilling from ships is a major source of water pollution. less dense floats pollution"],[20,"ch03-o019","Short answer","Carbon dioxide has a density of 0.002 g/cm3 and a fizzy drink has a density close to 1.0 g/cm3. Explain why the bubbles rise to the top of the drink.","3.1","The gas in the bubbles is far less dense than the liquid around it, and less dense substances float in more dense substances, so the bubbles rise to the surface. less dense float rise"],[20,"ch03-o020","Short answer","Hydrogen is less dense than helium, yet party balloons are filled with helium and not hydrogen. Explain why.","3.1","Hydrogen forms an explosive mixture with air, so it would be dangerous to use in balloons. Helium is almost as light but does not react in this way. explosive mixture air dangerous"],[20,"ch03-o021","Short answer","Explain, in terms of particles, why a gas can be compressed but a solid cannot, and state what happens to the density of the gas when it is compressed.","3.1","Solids are very difficult to compress because their particles are already in contact, while gases are easy to compress because their particles are far apart. When a gas is compressed the same number of particles is in a smaller volume, so the density of the gas increases. already in contact far apart smaller volume increases"],[20,"ch03-o022","Short answer","Explain why a hot air balloon rises through the air.","3.1","Heat causes a gas to expand, so the hot air inside the balloon has the same mass in a larger volume and is less dense than the colder air outside. A less dense substance floats in a more dense substance, so the balloon rises. expand less dense colder air outside"],[20,"ch03-o023","Short answer","State the unit in which thermal energy is measured, and describe what happens to the particles in an object when its thermal energy increases.","3.2","Thermal energy is measured in joules. When the thermal energy of an object increases, the particles in the object start to vibrate faster, so the energy of the particles increases. joules vibrate faster"],[20,"ch03-o024","Gap fill","Heat is the ____ thermal energy of the vibrating particles in an object, while temperature is the ____ energy of the particles.","3.2","total average"],[20,"ch03-o025","Short answer","Two glasses hold the same volume of water, but one is at a higher temperature than the other. Compare the heat of the two glasses of water and explain your answer.","3.2","The water at the higher temperature has the greater heat. The number of particles in both glasses is the same, but the particles in the hotter water are moving faster, so the total thermal energy of all the particles is larger. higher temperature same number moving faster total"],[20,"ch03-o026","Short answer","Two glasses of water are at the same temperature, but one contains a much larger volume of water. Compare the heat of the two, and explain your answer.","3.2","The larger volume of water has the greater heat. It contains more particles, and although the average energy of each particle is the same, the total thermal energy of all the particles is greater. larger volume more particles total greater"],[20,"ch03-o027","Short answer","State the two things that the temperature of an object tells you.","3.2","It tells you the direction that thermal energy will be transferred, and the average energy of the particles in the object. direction average energy"],[20,"ch03-o028","Short answer","Ice cream at -20 degrees Celsius is taken out into a room at 24 degrees Celsius. State the direction in which thermal energy is transferred, and explain how the size of the temperature difference affects the transfer.","3.2","Thermal energy is transferred from the air to the ice cream, because thermal energy is transferred from areas of higher temperature to areas of lower temperature. The temperature difference is 44 degrees, and the larger the temperature difference between two objects, the faster the thermal energy transfer. from the air higher to lower larger faster"],[20,"ch03-o029","Short answer","Explain why temperature, rather than heat, is a useful way of comparing objects of different sizes made from different materials.","3.2","Temperature tells us the average energy of the particles, so it does not depend on how many particles there are. That lets you compare, for example, 100 particles of soup with 100 particles of water even though the objects differ in size and material. average energy does not depend number of particles"],[20,"ch03-o030","Short answer","A sparkler burns at about 1000 degrees Celsius, but a spark landing on your hand does not cause serious burns. Explain why.","3.2","The mass of the spark is very small, so it contains very few particles and its total thermal energy, or heat, is very small. The temperature difference between the spark and the air is very large, so thermal energy is transferred from the spark to the air quickly, and by the time the spark reaches your skin both its temperature and its heat have decreased significantly. very small few particles heat transferred quickly"],[20,"ch03-o031","Short answer","Zara says that if object X has a higher temperature than object Y, then X must always have more heat than Y. Explain whether she is correct.","3.2","She is not correct. Temperature is the average energy of the particles while heat is the total thermal energy of all the particles, so an object at a high temperature with very few particles, such as a spark, can have less heat than a much larger cooler object. not correct average total number of particles"],[20,"ch03-o032","Short answer","Explain what Kelvin predicted about the lowest possible temperature, and state the value of absolute zero.","3.2","As the temperature of an object decreases the particles move more slowly, and Kelvin predicted that particles would eventually stop moving if the temperature were low enough, and that this would be the lowest possible temperature. This temperature is called absolute zero and is -273 degrees Celsius. stop moving lowest possible absolute zero 273"],[20,"ch03-o033","Short answer","Explain the difference between what people usually mean by conserving energy and what a scientist means by saying that energy is conserved.","3.3","People often talk about conserving energy when they mean using less energy, such as switching off a lamp. In physics, saying that energy is conserved means that the total quantity of energy stays the same, whether the energy is stored, changed, transferred or dissipated. using less total quantity stays the same"],[20,"ch03-o034","Short answer","An electric lamp is supplied with 100 J of electrical energy. 10 J is changed to light energy. State how much is changed to thermal energy, name the useful and wasted outputs, and explain how this shows energy is conserved.","3.3","90 J is changed to thermal energy. The light energy is useful and the thermal energy is wasted. The total energy input of 100 J is the same as the total energy output of 10 J + 90 J = 100 J, so energy is conserved. 90 useful wasted input equals output"],[20,"ch03-o035","Gap fill","In an energy diagram, the electrical energy supplied is called the energy ____ and the light and thermal energy together are called the energy ____.","3.3","input output"],[20,"ch03-o036","Short answer","In physics, what is meant by a system? Give one example.","3.3","A system is something that has been chosen to study, especially in terms of energy changes. An electric lamp or a car engine is an example of a system. chosen to study energy changes"],[20,"ch03-o037","Short answer","Explain why the total energy output of a system can never be greater than the total energy input.","3.3","Energy cannot be created. Created means to make something, or to bring something into existence, so no system can produce energy that was not supplied to it. cannot be created input"],[20,"ch03-o038","Short answer","Wasted thermal energy from a lamp is said to be dissipated. Explain what dissipated means, and explain why this does not break the law of conservation of energy.","3.3","Dissipated means the energy spreads out into the surroundings and becomes less useful. The energy is not destroyed, only spread out, so the total quantity of energy is still the same and the law of conservation of energy still holds. spreads out less useful not destroyed"],[20,"ch03-o039","Multiple choice","Which of these is the law of conservation of energy?","3.3","Energy can be created but not destroyed Energy can be destroyed but not created Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another Energy is used up when it is transferred"],[20,"ch03-o040","Short answer","An electric motor is supplied with 1000 J of electrical energy and produces 500 J of kinetic energy. Calculate the thermal energy produced and explain your working.","3.3","1000 - 500 = 500 J of thermal energy. The total energy output must equal the total energy input, so any input energy that does not become kinetic energy must appear as thermal energy. 500 input output equal"],[20,"ch03-o041","Short answer","In a wood fire, 70% of the chemical energy in the wood is changed to thermal energy and the rest is changed to light and waste chemicals. Calculate the percentage changed to light and waste chemicals.","3.3","100% - 70% = 30% is changed to light and waste chemicals. 30 100"],[20,"ch03-o042","Gap fill","Thermal energy always moves from ____ places to ____ places.","3.4","hotter colder"],[20,"ch03-o043","Short answer","Explain why you feel heat in your hands when you hold a hot drink, and state the condition that must be met for this to happen.","3.4","Thermal energy moves from the drink, through the container and into your hands. This will only happen if the drink is hotter than your hands, because thermal energy always moves from hotter places to colder places. hotter than moves hands"],[20,"ch03-o044","Short answer","Explain why food placed in a refrigerator becomes colder.","3.4","The food is hotter than the inside of the refrigerator, so thermal energy transfers out of the food into the colder surroundings, and the food becomes colder. transfers out colder thermal energy"],[20,"ch03-o045","Short answer","Explain the purpose of the water inside a car engine.","3.4","There is water inside a car engine to remove thermal energy from the metal engine parts. This stops the engine overheating. remove thermal energy metal engine parts overheating"],[20,"ch03-o046","Short answer","Sofia says that when she holds a piece of ice, cold moves from the ice into her hand. Explain why she is wrong and describe what really happens.","3.4","Cold is not a type of energy; cold means there is less thermal energy. When you hold the ice, thermal energy transfers away from your hand and into the ice, and you feel cold because thermal energy has been transferred away from your hand. cold is not less thermal energy away from your hand"],[20,"ch03-o047","Short answer","Explain why holding ice for too long can damage your skin.","3.4","Thermal energy keeps transferring from your hand into the ice, and your skin needs the correct quantity of thermal energy to function, so losing too much thermal energy damages it. thermal energy correct quantity function"],[20,"ch03-o048","Short answer","Explain what happens in terms of thermal energy when a window is opened in a hot room on a colder day.","3.4","Thermal energy from the hotter inside moves to the colder outside, because thermal energy always moves from hotter places to colder places, so the room cools down. hotter inside colder outside moves"],[20,"ch03-o049","Short answer","State what is meant by dissipation, and describe what determines how fast thermal energy is dissipated.","3.4","Dissipation is used to describe energy that spreads out and becomes less useful. The rate, or speed, of thermal energy transfer increases when the temperature difference between the hot place and the cold place increases. spreads out less useful temperature difference increases"],[20,"ch03-o050","Short answer","Marcus says that dissipated thermal energy has disappeared. Explain whether he is correct.","3.4","He is not correct. Energy is always conserved, so the thermal energy has not disappeared or been destroyed; it has just spread out and moved to a colder place, where it is less useful. not correct conserved spread out colder place"],[20,"ch03-o051","Short answer","Describe what happens to the particles in a solid when it is heated, and explain why the solid expands. Use the word vigorously in your answer.","3.5","In solids, the particles move by vibrating around fixed positions, and this vibration gets faster when the solid is heated. As the particles vibrate more vigorously they take up more space, so the solid expands. Vigorously means with more energy, more speed and more force. vibrating fixed positions more space expands"],[20,"ch03-o052","Short answer","Describe how the particles in a gas move, and explain what changes when the gas is heated.","3.5","In gases, the particles are far apart and move in straight lines until they collide with another particle or the walls of the container. When the gas is heated the particles move faster and collide with more force and more frequently, and they take up more space, so the gas expands. far apart straight lines collide expands"],[20,"ch03-o053","Short answer","Explain how thermal energy is transferred through a solid by conduction.","3.5","When particles are vibrating they push against the particles beside them. The faster and more vigorous the vibration, the greater the push, and this causes the particles beside them to vibrate more vigorously as well, passing the thermal energy along the solid. push beside them vibrate more vigorously"],[20,"ch03-o054","Short answer","Explain why conduction works best in solids.","3.5","Conduction works best in solids because the particles are close together and can only move by vibrating, so vibrations are passed on easily from one particle to the next. close together only vibrating"],[20,"ch03-o055","Short answer","Explain why metals are better conductors of thermal energy than fabrics such as wool and cotton.","3.5","Metals are the best conductors of heat because of the way their particles are arranged, and the electrons that are free to move in a metal help to pass along the vibrations. Fabrics such as wool and cotton, and plastics, are poor conductors, and poor conductors of thermal energy are good thermal insulators. free to move electrons poor conductors insulators"],[20,"ch03-o056","Short answer","A cooking pan is made from metal but its handle is made from plastic and the spoon from wood. Explain these choices.","3.5","Metal is a good conductor of thermal energy, which helps the food to heat faster. Plastic and wood are good thermal insulators, so the handle and the spoon stay comfortable to touch instead of conducting thermal energy to the hand. good conductor insulator comfortable to touch"],[20,"ch03-o057","Short answer","Explain why conduction cannot happen in a vacuum.","3.5","There are no particles to vibrate in a vacuum, and conduction depends on vibrating particles pushing against their neighbours, so conduction cannot happen there. no particles vibrate vacuum"],[20,"ch03-o058","Short answer","Explain, using the density equation, why a heated part of a liquid or gas rises.","3.5","When a liquid or gas is heated the particles take up more space, so the volume increases. The number of particles does not change, so the mass stays the same. Since density is mass divided by volume, the density decreases, and less dense substances float in more dense substances, so the heated part floats upwards. volume increases mass stays the same density decreases float"],[20,"ch03-o059","Short answer","Describe how a convection current forms when a liquid is heated from the bottom, and explain what it achieves.","3.5","The part of the liquid at the bottom becomes less dense and starts to float up through the colder, more dense parts, which is convection. Colder, more dense parts then sink down to occupy the space, get heated in turn and rise. This movement is called a convection current, and it causes all of the liquid to eventually become heated. less dense float up sink down all of the liquid"],[20,"ch03-o060","Short answer","Explain why convection can happen in liquids and gases but not in solids or in a vacuum.","3.5","Convection can happen in liquids and gases because the particles are free to move. It cannot happen in a solid because the particles are not free to move and can only vibrate about fixed positions, and it cannot happen in a vacuum because there are no particles to move. free to move fixed positions no particles"],[20,"ch03-o061","Short answer","There is a vacuum between the Sun and the Earth. Explain how thermal energy from the Sun still reaches us, and state how long it takes.","3.5","Conduction and convection cannot transfer thermal energy through a vacuum, so the energy is transferred by radiation. Radiation is a type of wave that we cannot see and does not need particles, so it can pass through a vacuum. The thermal energy from the Sun takes about nine minutes to reach Earth. radiation wave does not need particles nine minutes"],[20,"ch03-o062","Gap fill","All objects give out thermal energy through radiation. The ____ the object, the more radiation it will emit, and cooler objects ____ thermal energy by radiation from hotter ones.","3.5","hotter absorb"],[20,"ch03-o063","Short answer","Describe the surfaces that are the best emitters and absorbers of radiation, and those that are the worst.","3.5","The best emitters and absorbers of radiation are dull, are black, and have a large surface area. The worst emitters and absorbers are shiny, are white or silver, and have a small surface area. Shiny, white or silver surfaces reflect radiation away. dull black large surface area shiny reflect"],[20,"ch03-o064","Short answer","Explain why the radiator in a car engine is painted black and has many thin metal fins attached to it.","3.5","The radiator is designed to radiate thermal energy away. Black surfaces are the best emitters of radiation, and the thin metal fins give the radiator a large surface area, which also increases the radiation emitted. black best emitter large surface area"],[20,"ch03-o065","Short answer","Explain why buildings in many hot countries are painted white, and why penguins have black feathers on their backs.","3.5","White surfaces reflect radiation away and are poor absorbers, so buildings painted white absorb less thermal energy from radiation and stay cooler. Black surfaces are the best absorbers, so penguins have black feathers on their backs to absorb as much thermal energy from radiation as possible. white reflect absorb less black absorb"],[20,"ch03-o066","Short answer","A room heater has hot water flowing through the inside. Describe how all three ways of transferring thermal energy are involved in heating the room.","3.5","Thermal energy from the hot water is transferred to the metal of the heater by conduction, and from the metal to the air also by conduction. The warm air expands and rises, which heats the air above by convection. The metal surface of the heater also emits thermal energy by radiation. conduction convection radiation rises"],[20,"ch03-o067","Short answer","Explain how the vacuum and the silver surface inside a vacuum flask each slow down the transfer of thermal energy.","3.5","The vacuum surrounding the bottle prevents conduction of thermal energy from the drink to the outside, because there are no particles to pass on vibrations. The silver surface of the bottle reflects thermal energy in the form of radiation back into the hot drink. vacuum prevents conduction silver reflects radiation"],[20,"ch03-o068","Short answer","Explain how double glazed windows and foam-filled cavity walls reduce thermal energy transfer out of a building.","3.5","Double glazed windows have two layers of glass separated by a thin layer of argon gas, and argon is a poor conductor of heat. The cavity in the wall is filled with foam, which is a poor conductor and also fills the space to prevent convection currents from forming inside the wall. argon poor conductor foam prevent convection"],[20,"ch03-o069","Short answer","Explain why covering car windows with silver material stops the inside of a parked car becoming too hot.","3.5","Silver surfaces are poor absorbers and reflect radiation away, so the silver material reflects radiation from the Sun away from the inside of the car instead of letting it be absorbed. silver reflect radiation Sun"],[20,"ch03-o070","Multiple choice","Which two ways of transferring thermal energy cannot occur in a vacuum?","3.5","Conduction and radiation Convection and radiation Conduction and convection Radiation only"],[20,"ch03-o071","Short answer","State what evaporation is, and explain how it can happen at 22 degrees Celsius when the boiling point of water is 100 degrees Celsius.","3.6","Evaporation is the change of state from liquid to gas. The particles in a liquid move at random speeds, and some of them are moving fast enough to have sufficient energy to escape from the surface and become particles of a gas, even though the liquid as a whole is far below its boiling point. change of state liquid to gas escape surface"],[20,"ch03-o072","Gap fill","The particles in a liquid move in random directions at random speeds. The word random means ____.","3.6","unpredictable"],[20,"ch03-o073","Short answer","Explain why evaporation causes cooling. Use the idea of average particle energy in your answer.","3.6","Temperature is a measure of the average energy of the particles. When the particles with the highest energy escape from the liquid, this lowers the average energy of the particles that remain, so the temperature of the liquid falls and evaporation causes cooling. highest energy escape average energy lower"],[20,"ch03-o074","Short answer","Explain why you feel cold when you get out of a swimming pool.","3.6","The water on your skin evaporates. The particles with the highest energy escape, and the energy provided to change the state of the water from liquid to gas comes from your skin, so your skin loses thermal energy and cools. evaporates energy comes from your skin loses"],[20,"ch03-o075","Short answer","Many people think that the liquid on the skin alone causes cooling. Explain the correct sequence by which sweat cools the skin.","3.6","Since the sweat was produced in the skin, it starts at the same temperature as the skin. When the sweat evaporates, its temperature drops. Thermal energy from the skin is then transferred to the cooler sweat, and this continues to lower the skin temperature. same temperature evaporates temperature drops transferred"],[20,"ch03-o076","Short answer","Explain why very humid conditions can be dangerous for someone working hard in the heat.","3.6","In very humid conditions sweat may not be able to evaporate, so the cooling effect of evaporation is lost. Without this cooling effect the body temperature can increase, which can be dangerous. humid cannot evaporate body temperature increase"],[20,"ch03-o077","Short answer","A water cooler is made from porous clay. Explain what porous means and how the cooler works.","3.6","Porous means that water can slowly soak through the clay. Some water soaks through to the outside of the cooler and evaporates into the surrounding air, and this evaporation cools the water still inside the cooler. soak through evaporates cooling"],[20,"ch03-o078","Short answer","Explain how an air cooler containing a sponge soaked in water cools the air blown through it.","3.6","A fan blows warm air from the room through the wet sponge. The warm air provides the thermal energy needed to evaporate the water, so the thermal energy of the air decreases and the air is cooled. warm air provides evaporate decreases"],[20,"ch03-o079","Short answer","Equal volumes of perfume and water at the same temperature are put on the skin. Explain why the perfume feels colder.","3.6","Perfumes have weaker forces between the particles, so perfume evaporates faster than water. Faster evaporation removes thermal energy from the skin faster, so the perfume feels colder. weaker forces evaporates faster removes thermal energy"],[20,"ch03-o080","Short answer","Equal volumes of liquid soap and water at the same temperature are put on the skin. Predict which will feel colder and explain why.","3.6","The water will feel colder. Liquid soap has stronger forces between the particles and evaporates much more slowly than water, so water removes thermal energy from the skin faster. water stronger forces more slowly faster"],[20,"ch03-o081","Multiple choice","Hot water at 80 degrees Celsius is left in an open beaker in a room at 20 degrees Celsius. Which change of state takes place at the surface of the water?","3.6","Freezing Condensation Evaporation Boiling"],[20,"ch03-o082","Multiple choice","Two blocks of copper are both at 25 degrees Celsius. Block A has a mass of 10 g and block B has a mass of 80 g. Which statement is correct?","3.2","A and B have the same temperature and the same heat A and B have the same temperature, but B has more heat B has a higher temperature and more heat than A A has more heat because it heats up faster"],[20,"ch03-o083","Gap fill","Energy is always ____, meaning that energy cannot be ____ or ____.","3.3","conserved created destroyed"],[20,"ch03-o084","Short answer","A small metal ball at 200 degrees Celsius is dropped into water at 4 degrees Celsius. Describe what happens to the temperature of the ball and of the water, and state what is true once they stop changing.","3.2","Thermal energy is transferred from the hotter ball to the colder water, so the temperature of the ball falls and the temperature of the water rises. The transfer is fastest at the start when the temperature difference is largest. Once the temperatures stop changing, the temperature of the ball and the temperature of the water are the same. ball falls water rises same"],[20,"ch03-o085","Gap fill","When a solid is heated the particles take up more space, so the solid ____. This word means to increase in ____.","3.5","expands size"],[20,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the scientific definition of density?","3.1","mass per unit volume volume per unit mass weight per unit area force per unit time"],[20,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","To find the volume of an irregular object, you place it in water and measure the increase in water level. This method is called the ____ method.","3.1","displacement"],[20,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the unit used to measure heat?","3.2","Joules Degrees Celsius Newtons Cubic metres"],[20,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","Heat is defined as the ____ thermal energy of the vibrating particles in an object.","3.2","total"],[20,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","What does temperature tell us about the particles in an object?","3.2","The total energy of all particles The average energy of the particles The number of particles present The mass of each particle"],[20,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","Thermal energy is transferred from areas of higher temperature to areas of lower ____.","3.2","temperature"],[20,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What does it mean when we say energy is conserved in physics?","3.3","The total quantity of energy stays the same Less energy is being used by the device Energy is created to replace what is lost Energy disappears into the surroundings"],[20,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","In an electric lamp, the electrical energy is called the energy input and the light and thermal energy are called the energy ____.","3.3","output"],[20,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","Why can the total energy output never be greater than the total energy input?","3.3","Because energy cannot be created Because energy is always destroyed Because some energy is lost to sound Because the system runs out of fuel"],[20,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","Dissipated energy spreads out into the surroundings and becomes less ____.","3.3","useful"],[20,"qwen-03-08","Gap fill","When thermal energy spreads out into the surroundings so that it is too spread out to be useful, we say it has ____.","3.4","dissipated"],[20,"qwen-03-09","Multiple choice","Why do you feel cold when holding ice?","3.4","Thermal energy transfers away from your hand into the ice Cold energy moves from the ice into your hand Your hands create less thermal energy than usual The ice destroys the thermal energy in your skin"],[20,"qwen-03-10","Gap fill","The rate of thermal energy transfer increases when the temperature difference between the hot place and the cold place ____.","3.4","increases"],[20,"qwen-03-11","Multiple choice","What happens to thermal energy when it dissipates?","3.4","It spreads out and moves to a colder place It is destroyed and disappears It turns into light energy It stays in the same place but gets hotter"],[20,"qwen-03-12","Gap fill","Cold is not a type of energy; it means there is less thermal ____.","3.4","energy"],[20,"qwen-04-01","Multiple choice","What is the name for the spreading out of thermal energy?","3.4","Conduction Dissipation Radiation Insulation"],[20,"qwen-04-02","Gap fill","In solids, particles move by ____ around fixed positions.","3.5","vibrating"],[20,"qwen-04-03","Multiple choice","Why do solids expand when they are heated?","3.5","The particles break apart The particles vibrate more vigorously and take up more space The mass of the solid increases New particles are created"],[20,"qwen-04-04","Gap fill","Metals are the best conductors of heat because free ____ help pass along vibrations.","3.5","electrons"],[20,"qwen-04-05","Multiple choice","Which material is described as a good thermal insulator?","3.5","Copper Wool Iron Aluminium"],[20,"qwen-05-01","Multiple choice","What is the change of state called when a liquid turns into a gas?","3.6","Condensation Evaporation Freezing Boiling"],[20,"qwen-05-02","Gap fill","Particles in a liquid move in random directions, and the word random means ____.","3.6","unpredictable"],[20,"qwen-05-03","Multiple choice","Which particles in a liquid are able to escape from the surface?","3.6","Particles with the lowest energy Particles with medium energy that get pulled back Particles with the highest energy All particles equally"],[20,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Which state of matter has the lowest density?","3.1","solid liquid gas they all have the same density"],[20,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Density is defined as the ____ of a certain volume of substance.","3.1","mass"],[20,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","Thermal energy is transferred from a place of higher temperature to a place of ____ temperature.","3.4","lower"],[20,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which method transfers thermal energy from the Sun to Earth?","3.5","conduction convection radiation insulation"],[20,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","In a solid, particles vibrate about fixed ____.","3.2","positions"],[20,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","What happens to the particles in a solid when it is heated?","3.2","they vibrate more they stop moving they move further apart and flow they change into gas"],[20,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","Evaporation occurs when a liquid changes to a ____.","3.6","gas"],[20,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Which particles cause evaporation?","3.6","the particles with the most energy the particles with the least energy all particles equally only the coldest particles"],[20,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","The law of conservation of energy states that the quantity of energy will stay the ____.","3.3","same"],[20,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","What is humidity a measure of?","3.6","The temperature of the air A measure of the quantity of water vapour in air The speed at which wind blows The amount of liquid water on the ground"],[21,"ch04-f01a","Diagram labelling","This is a root hair cell growing out between moist soil particles. Name the part labelled {n}.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the cell with the long, thin extension that reaches out between the soil particles.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the layer that controls which substances enter and leave the cell.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the large space that is filled with cell sap.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on the cell wall of the root hair cell.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on the cytoplasm of the root hair cell.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f02a","Diagram labelling","This is a root cut across, seen under a microscope. Write down the label at position {n}.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the cross-shaped area where the xylem tissue is found.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on a root hair on this root.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the root cap.","4.1",""],[21,"ch04-f03a","Diagram labelling","This is a section through part of a leaf. Name the part labelled {n}.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the hole in the leaf surface through which water vapour escapes.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the gap inside the leaf where water evaporates from the cell surfaces.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the gas that is diffusing out of the leaf.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-f03h4","Click the diagram","Click on the xylem vessel in this leaf.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-f04a","Diagram labelling","This is the human excretory system. Name the part labelled {n}.","4.3",""],[21,"ch04-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tube that carries urine from a kidney down to the bladder.","4.3",""],[21,"ch04-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the organ that stores urine until it is released.","4.3",""],[21,"ch04-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the single tube that carries urine out of the body.","4.3",""],[21,"ch04-f04h4","Click the diagram","Click on the blood vessel that brings blood to the kidneys.","4.3",""],[21,"ch04-f04h5","Click the diagram","Click on the blood vessel that carries blood away from the kidneys.","4.3",""],[21,"ch04-f05h1","Click the diagram","Sofia kept plant A at 4 degrees Celsius, plant B at 20 degrees Celsius and plant C at 30 degrees Celsius. Click on the label of the plant that lost the least mass over the eight days.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-f05h2","Click the diagram","Sofia kept plant A at 4 degrees Celsius, plant B at 20 degrees Celsius and plant C at 30 degrees Celsius. Click on the label of the plant whose leaves transpired fastest.","4.2",""],[21,"ch04-o001","Short answer","Give one reason why every living thing needs water inside its cells.","4.1","Water dissolves the chemicals inside a cell, and the chemicals have to be dissolved before chemical reactions such as respiration can happen. dissolve chemical reactions respiration"],[21,"ch04-o002","Short answer","Describe what soil is made of, and explain why there are spaces inside it.","4.1","Soil is made of tiny particles of rock. The particles are irregularly shaped and are all sorts of different sizes, so they do not pack together tightly and there are spaces between them. particles of rock irregularly shaped do not pack spaces"],[21,"ch04-o003","Gap fill","The spaces between the soil particles are filled with ____ and ____.","4.1","air water"],[21,"ch04-o004","Short answer","Explain how the shape of a root hair cell helps it to do its job.","4.1","The cell has a long extension growing out into the soil. This gives it a really big surface area, so water and mineral ions can be absorbed into the plant faster. long extension surface area absorb faster"],[21,"ch04-o005","Multiple choice","How much of a plant cell does one root hair make up?","4.1","It is a whole cell on its own. It is part of a single cell. It is made of many cells joined together. It is a group of dead cells."],[21,"ch04-o006","Short answer","Water moves from the soil into the cytoplasm of a root hair cell. Name, in order, the two structures it passes through on the way.","4.1","It passes through the cell wall first, then the cell membrane, and then it is in the cytoplasm. cell wall cell membrane cytoplasm"],[21,"ch04-o007","Gap fill","Nitrate ions are needed to make ____, and magnesium ions are needed to make ____.","4.1","proteins chlorophyll"],[21,"ch04-o008","Multiple choice","Which two things fill the spaces between the particles of soil?","4.1","air and water oil and water air and salt water only"],[21,"ch04-o009","Short answer","Explain how mineral ions get from the soil into a root hair cell.","4.1","The mineral ions are dissolved in the water in the spaces between the soil particles, and they move into the root hair cell along with that water. dissolved along with the water move in"],[21,"ch04-o010","Short answer","Describe the structure of a xylem vessel.","4.1","Xylem vessels are long, tube-like cells. They are dead, so all their contents such as cytoplasm and a nucleus have disappeared. Only the cell walls are left, with an empty space inside, and the end walls have gone too, so many vessels stack up into long empty tubes. dead no contents end walls long empty tubes"],[21,"ch04-o011","Multiple choice","Which statement about xylem vessels is correct?","4.1","They are living cells full of cytoplasm. They are dead cells with an empty space inside. They each have a large nucleus. They are single cells that never join up."],[21,"ch04-o012","Gap fill","In a xylem vessel the end walls have completely ____, so many vessels stack on top of one another to make long, empty ____.","4.1","disappeared tubes"],[21,"ch04-o013","Short answer","Explain why the structure of xylem vessels makes them good at carrying water.","4.1","They are empty tubes, so there is nothing inside to get in the way of the water, and because their end walls have disappeared the stacked vessels form one continuous tube that runs all the way from the roots to the highest parts of the plant. empty no end walls continuous roots to the top"],[21,"ch04-o014","Short answer","What is the wood in a tree trunk made of, and what does it do?","4.1","The wood is made up of xylem vessels. They carry water from the roots all the way to the top of the tree. xylem vessels carry water top of the tree"],[21,"ch04-o016","Short answer","Describe where the xylem is found when you look at a root that has been cut across.","4.1","The xylem tissue is in the centre of the root, in a cross-shaped area. The root hairs are on the outside of the root. centre cross-shaped root hairs outside"],[21,"ch04-o017","Short answer","Describe the whole path taken by a water molecule from the soil to a leaf at the top of a tree.","4.1","The water moves from the soil into a root hair cell, then from the outside of the root towards the inside, into the xylem vessels in the centre of the root. The xylem vessels then carry it up through the root and stem to the leaves. root hair across the root xylem vessels up"],[21,"ch04-o018","Multiple choice","A celery stalk is stood in blue dye. When the stalk is cut across, blue spots appear. Which kind of cell is found where the blue spots are?","4.1","palisade cells root hair cells xylem cells red blood cells"],[21,"ch04-o019","Short answer","In the celery experiment, explain why it is important that the cut end of the stalk is clean and not blocked or damaged.","4.1","The dye can only travel up the stalk if the open ends of the xylem vessels are clear. If the cut end is blocked or crushed, the vessels are closed off and little or no dye will move up. xylem blocked dye cannot travel"],[21,"ch04-o020","Short answer","In the celery experiment, suggest why you should put no more than 2 cm of coloured water in the container.","4.1","So that you can be sure the coloured areas appeared because the dye was carried up through the stalk, and not because it leaked in from the sides of a deeply submerged stalk. carried up not leaked in sides"],[21,"ch04-o021","Short answer","In the celery experiment, suggest why the stalk is rinsed with tap water before it is cut across.","4.1","To remove any dye from the outside of the stalk, so you can be sure the coloured spots inside were caused only by dye that had been carried up the stalk. remove dye outside only carried up"],[21,"ch04-o022","Short answer","Predict what happens to the speed at which water moves up a stalk when the temperature increases, and explain why.","4.1","The water moves up faster, because higher temperatures give more kinetic energy to the particles. faster kinetic energy particles"],[21,"ch04-o023","Short answer","A student plans to test how temperature affects the speed at which dye moves up a celery stalk. State the independent variable, the dependent variable, and two variables that must be kept the same.","4.1","The independent variable is the temperature. The dependent variable is the rate at which the coloured water moves up the stalk, found by measuring a distance and a time. Variables to keep the same include the depth of coloured water, the size of the stalk and the light intensity. temperature rate distance and time same"],[21,"ch04-o024","Short answer","Give the risk in the celery experiment and say how it should be controlled.","4.1","Cutting the stalk carries a risk of harm from the sharp blade. Put the stalk on a firm, non-slip surface and cut away from your body so that a slipping blade does not hit your hand. sharp blade non-slip cut away"],[21,"ch04-o027","Short answer","What is transpiration?","4.2","Transpiration is the loss of water vapour from leaves. loss water vapour leaves"],[21,"ch04-o028","Short answer","Describe what happens to water after it arrives at a leaf in the xylem vessels.","4.2","It moves out of the xylem vessels into the leaf cells. Cells with chloroplasts use a little of it for photosynthesis, but most of it soaks into the cellulose cell wall, evaporates to water vapour, diffuses into the air spaces between the cells and then diffuses out through the stomata. leaf cells cell wall evaporates air spaces stomata"],[21,"ch04-o029","Gap fill","Inside a leaf, liquid water soaks into the cellulose ____ ____ and then ____ to become water vapour.","4.2","cell wall evaporates"],[21,"ch04-o030","Short answer","Name the exact place inside a leaf where liquid water changes into water vapour.","4.2","In the cellulose cell wall of a leaf cell. cellulose cell wall leaf cell"],[21,"ch04-o031","Multiple choice","What are the stomata?","4.2","Tiny holes in the underside of the leaf. The tubes that carry water up the stem. The cells that hold the chloroplasts. The waxy layer on top of the leaf."],[21,"ch04-o032","Short answer","Explain why most transpiration happens from the underside of a leaf rather than the top.","4.2","The upper surface is covered with a layer of wax that stops water passing through. The underside has stomata, which are openings leading to the air spaces inside the leaf, so water vapour can diffuse out from the air spaces through them. wax upper surface stomata underside diffuse"],[21,"ch04-o033","Short answer","Water vapour is in the air spaces inside a leaf. Explain how it gets out into the air outside.","4.2","The air spaces connect with the air outside the leaf through the stomata, tiny holes in the underside, and the water vapour diffuses out through these holes. air spaces stomata diffuses out"],[21,"ch04-o034","Short answer","Give the four reasons why a plant needs water.","4.2","Support, because cells full of water are firm and hold the plant up; transport, because water carries dissolved mineral salts through the xylem; cooling, because evaporating water takes heat away; and photosynthesis, because water is one of the reactants. support transport cooling photosynthesis"],[21,"ch04-o035","Short answer","Explain, in terms of cells, what happens when a plant wilts.","4.2","Plant cells contain a lot of water, especially in their vacuoles, and a cell with plenty of water is strong and firm. When a cell does not contain enough water it becomes soft and floppy, and when all the cells in a plant are like this the plant collapses. vacuoles firm soft and floppy collapses"],[21,"ch04-o036","Gap fill","A plant cell that has plenty of water is strong and ____. When all the cells in a plant are like this, they press on one another and make the whole plant firm and well ____.","4.2","firm supported"],[21,"ch04-o037","Multiple choice","A plant has not been watered for several days and has collapsed. What word describes it?","4.2","wilted excreted transpired irregular"],[21,"ch04-o038","Short answer","Explain how water is used for transport inside a plant.","4.2","As water moves through the xylem vessels it carries dissolved mineral salts with it, and this is how the mineral salts are transported from the roots to all the other parts of the plant. xylem dissolved mineral salts roots to all parts"],[21,"ch04-o039","Short answer","Explain how water cools a plant, and say why this matters most in hot places.","4.2","When water evaporates it takes heat away with it, so when water evaporates from the cell wall of a cell in a leaf it cools the cell. This is important for plants that live in very hot places, where they would otherwise overheat. evaporates takes heat away cools the cell hot places"],[21,"ch04-o040","Short answer","Explain why a plant that cannot get water also cannot photosynthesise.","4.2","Water is one of the reactants in photosynthesis. In photosynthesis plants change water and carbon dioxide to glucose and oxygen, so without water there is nothing for the carbon dioxide to react with. reactant carbon dioxide glucose oxygen"],[21,"ch04-o041","Short answer","Suggest two ways a desert plant may be adapted to get as much water as possible from the soil, and two ways it may reduce the water it loses.","4.2","To get water it may have very deep roots to reach water far below the surface, or very wide-spreading roots to catch rain over a large area. To lose less it may have small leaves, which have a smaller surface area, and a thick layer of wax on the leaves to reduce transpiration. deep roots wide-spreading small leaves thick wax"],[21,"ch04-o042","Short answer","In the experiment with two potted plants, one is completely covered by a bag and the other has only its pot and soil covered. State the independent variable.","4.2","Whether the leaves of the plant were exposed to the air or not. leaves exposed to the air"],[21,"ch04-o043","Short answer","In the two potted plants experiment, list the variables that should be kept the same.","4.2","The soil is covered in both cases. The two plants should be the same size and the same kind, the pots the same size with the same quantity of water in the soil, and both plants kept at the same temperature and light intensity. soil covered same size same quantity of water temperature light"],[21,"ch04-o044","Short answer","In the two potted plants experiment, explain why the plant with uncovered leaves lost mass much faster. Use the word transpiration in your answer.","4.2","Water vapour was lost from the leaves of the uncovered plant by transpiration and escaped into the air, so the pot and plant became lighter. The water vapour lost from the other plant was trapped inside the bag and could not escape, so the mass of that whole pot and bag barely changed. transpiration water vapour trapped could not escape"],[21,"ch04-o045","Short answer","Drops of water appear on the inside of the bag that covers a plant. Explain how they got there.","4.2","Water travelled up the plant in the xylem, evaporated from the cell walls inside the leaves and diffused out through the stomata as water vapour. Trapped inside the bag, the vapour condensed back to liquid water on the cool inside surface of the bag. evaporated stomata water vapour condensed"],[21,"ch04-o046","Short answer","Eight leaves are hung up to dry. Some have petroleum jelly on the top surface, some on the bottom, some on both and some have none. Put the treatments in order, starting with the one that loses the most water.","4.2","Most water is lost by the leaf with no petroleum jelly, then the leaf with jelly on the upper surface only, then the leaf with jelly on the lower surface only, and least of all by the leaf with jelly on both surfaces. no jelly upper surface lower surface both"],[21,"ch04-o047","Short answer","Explain why putting petroleum jelly on the lower surface of a leaf conserves far more water than putting it on the upper surface.","4.2","The upper surface has few or no stomata, so blocking it changes little. The underside has the stomata, through which water vapour diffuses out from the air spaces, so blocking the underside closes the main escape route. few or no stomata underside stomata diffuse out"],[21,"ch04-o048","Short answer","In the hanging leaves experiment, two leaves were given each treatment. Explain why, and say whether using more leaves would be better.","4.2","Using two leaves gives more reliable results, because a single leaf might be unusual. Using even more leaves would be better still, because then anomalous results could be identified and the data would be more reliable. more reliable unusual anomalous more leaves"],[21,"ch04-o049","Short answer","Two leaves were given exactly the same treatment but did not lose exactly the same mass. Suggest why.","4.2","The two leaves probably had different masses to start with and contained different quantities of water, and one may have been hanging in a slightly draughtier place than the other. different starting mass different water draughtier"],[21,"ch04-o050","Gap fill","In liquid water the particles are randomly arranged but touching. In water as a gas the particles are ____ apart and are ____ touching at all.","4.2","far not"],[21,"ch04-o051","Short answer","Sofia investigated how temperature affects water loss from plants. Explain why she covered the pot and soil of each plant with a plastic bag.","4.2","So that any water lost could only have come from the plants themselves, and not by evaporation from the soil. Without the bag she could not tell which of the two had caused the mass to fall. only from the plants not from the soil evaporation"],[21,"ch04-o052","Short answer","Sofia recorded the mass of one plant and its pot as 893, 873, 856, 837, 861, 792, 779 and 761 grams over eight days. Identify the anomalous result and explain how you know.","4.2","The value of 861 g on day 5 is anomalous. The mass had been falling steadily as water was lost, and it cannot rise again, because the plant only loses water during the experiment. 861 anomalous mass only falls"],[21,"ch04-o053","Short answer","A plant and pot had a mass of 893 g on day 1 and 761 g on day 8. Calculate the mean loss of mass per day, showing your working.","4.2","Loss of mass = 893 minus 761 = 132 g. There are seven days between day 1 and day 8, so the mean loss per day = 132 divided by 7 = 19 g per day. 132 divided by 7 19"],[21,"ch04-o054","Short answer","Three identical plants were kept at 4, 20 and 30 degrees Celsius. The one at 30 degrees lost the most mass and the one at 4 degrees the least. Explain this result.","4.2","The higher the temperature, the more energy the water particles have, so water evaporates faster from the cell walls inside the leaves and transpiration is faster. Faster transpiration means more water vapour lost and a greater fall in mass. more energy evaporates faster transpiration more mass lost"],[21,"ch04-o055","Short answer","What does excretion mean?","4.3","Excretion means getting rid of waste materials. It only applies to waste substances that organisms make in their cells, plus any substances they have too much of that have been part of their body. getting rid waste made in cells too much"],[21,"ch04-o056","Short answer","Explain why getting rid of faeces does not count as excretion.","4.3","Food travels along the tube from the mouth to the anus without ever really being part of the body. Only things that move out of the digestive system into the blood or the body cells are part of the body, so the waste that leaves as faeces has never been part of the body. never been part of the body digestive system blood or cells"],[21,"ch04-o057","Short answer","Name the three things that humans excrete, and say where each comes from.","4.3","Carbon dioxide, which body cells make in respiration; urea, a waste substance made in liver cells; and excess water that is not needed by the body. carbon dioxide respiration urea liver excess water"],[21,"ch04-o058","Multiple choice","In which organ is urea made?","4.3","the kidney the liver the bladder the lung"],[21,"ch04-o059","Short answer","Explain how excess protein in the diet leads to urea being made.","4.3","Proteins in food are broken down to smaller molecules in the digestive system, and these go into the blood, which transports them to the liver. If there is more protein than the body needs, the liver changes the smaller molecules into urea. broken down blood liver more than we need urea"],[21,"ch04-o060","Short answer","Explain why urea must be removed from the body quickly.","4.3","Urea is a poisonous substance. If it builds up in the body it makes a person ill, so as soon as it is made in a liver cell it is taken away from the liver in the blood and removed by the kidneys. poisonous builds up ill kidneys"],[21,"ch04-o061","Short answer","Describe what the kidneys do to the blood that flows through them.","4.3","The kidneys filter the blood. They remove all of the urea from it and they also remove excess water from it. filter all of the urea excess water"],[21,"ch04-o062","Gap fill","The solution made of ____ dissolved in ____ is called urine.","4.3","urea water"],[21,"ch04-o063","Short answer","Describe the path taken by urine from the kidney to the outside of the body, naming each part.","4.3","The urine made in each kidney flows down a tube called a ureter, which carries it to the bladder. The bladder stores it for a while. The urine then flows out of the bladder to the outside through another tube, the urethra. ureter bladder urethra"],[21,"ch04-o064","Multiple choice","Which organ filters the blood?","4.3","the bladder the kidney the ureter the urethra"],[21,"ch04-o065","Gap fill","The word renal means ____ ____ ____ ____.","4.3","to do with kidneys"],[21,"ch04-o066","Short answer","What is another name for the excretory system, and why is it given that name?","4.3","The renal system. It is named after the kidneys, because renal means to do with kidneys and the kidneys are the organs that do the filtering. renal system kidneys"],[21,"ch04-o067","Short answer","Describe how carbon dioxide is excreted from the body.","4.3","Carbon dioxide made by body cells in respiration diffuses into the blood and is carried to the lungs. There it diffuses from the blood capillaries into the alveoli, and it is removed from the body in expired air. diffuses into the blood lungs alveoli expired air"],[21,"ch04-o068","Short answer","Suggest why plants do not produce urea that they have to excrete.","4.3","Plants make their own proteins, using carbohydrates that they have made in photosynthesis and nitrates that they absorb from the soil. They make only as much as they need, so they are unlikely to have excess protein to break down into urea. make their own proteins photosynthesis nitrates no excess"],[21,"ch04-o069","Multiple choice","Which of these is NOT excretion?","4.3","breathing out carbon dioxide passing urine containing urea losing excess water in urine getting rid of faeces"],[21,"ch04-o070","Short answer","Explain why food inside the digestive system is not really inside the body.","4.3","The digestive system is a long tube from the mouth to the anus, and food travelling through it has never crossed into the body. Only things that move out of the digestive system into a person's blood or body cells are really part of their body. long tube never part of the body into the blood or cells"],[21,"ch04-o071","Short answer","Give two reasons why humans need water that are also true for plants, and one reason that is true only for humans.","4.3","Both humans and plants need water for transport and for cooling. Humans also need water for getting rid of waste materials in urine, which plants do not do. transport cooling urine waste"],[21,"ch04-o072","Short answer","Name two uses of water in a plant that a human body does not have.","4.3","A plant uses water for support, because cells full of water keep the plant firm, and as a reactant in photosynthesis. Humans have a skeleton for support and do not photosynthesise. support photosynthesis"],[21,"ch04-o073","Gap fill","Blood is brought to the kidneys in an ____ and taken away from the kidneys in a ____.","4.3","artery vein"],[21,"ch04-o074","Short answer","A person eats far more protein than they need for several weeks. Explain what their body does with the excess.","4.3","The proteins are broken down to smaller molecules in the digestive system and carried in the blood to the liver. Because there is more than the body needs, the liver changes them into urea, which the blood carries to the kidneys, where it is filtered out and leaves the body dissolved in urine. liver urea kidneys urine"],[21,"ch04-o075","Short answer","What is a fetus?","4.4","A fetus is a baby before it is born. For the first nine months of its life a new human being grows inside its mother, and during that time it is called a fetus. baby before it is born nine months inside its mother"],[21,"ch04-o076","Short answer","Explain how substances get from a mother to her fetus if their blood never mixes.","4.4","The two blood systems come very close together but do not touch. The substances that the fetus needs diffuse from the mother's blood into the fetus's blood, and the substances the fetus needs to excrete diffuse in the opposite direction. do not mix very close diffuse opposite direction"],[21,"ch04-o077","Short answer","List three substances that move from the mother's blood into her fetus's blood.","4.4","Oxygen, and nutrients such as glucose, water, vitamins and minerals. oxygen glucose nutrients"],[21,"ch04-o078","Short answer","List two substances that the fetus excretes, which are removed by the mother's blood.","4.4","Carbon dioxide and urea. carbon dioxide urea"],[21,"ch04-o079","Short answer","Explain why a pregnant woman needs to eat more protein than usual.","4.4","Protein is needed to help the fetus produce new cells and grow. The mother also needs protein to keep her own muscles strong, and she needs extra protein to make extra haemoglobin, because her blood has to transport oxygen for the fetus as well as for herself. new cells grow extra haemoglobin oxygen"],[21,"ch04-o080","Multiple choice","Which mineral does a pregnant woman need more of in order to make extra haemoglobin?","4.4","calcium iron magnesium nitrate"],[21,"ch04-o081","Short answer","Explain why a pregnant woman needs plenty of calcium.","4.4","Calcium is essential for helping the baby to grow strong bones, and for keeping the mother's own bones and teeth in good health. strong bones mother's bones and teeth"],[21,"ch04-o082","Short answer","Explain why a pregnant woman needs carbohydrate, and why she should not eat too much of it.","4.4","Carbohydrate supplies energy. Glucose is a carbohydrate, and cells get their energy by combining glucose with oxygen in respiration, which both the mother and the fetus need to do. But if she eats too much, the extra could be changed to fat and make her put on too much weight, which is not healthy. energy glucose respiration changed to fat weight"],[21,"ch04-o083","Short answer","Explain why a pregnant woman needs plenty of vitamins A, C and D.","4.4","Vitamin A gives good vision for the mother and the fetus, and lets their white blood cells fight pathogens. Vitamin C gives strong skin and blood vessels for both. Vitamin D gives strong bones and teeth for both. vision white blood cells skin and blood vessels bones and teeth"],[21,"ch04-o084","Short answer","Name one food that is a good source of each of protein, iron and calcium.","4.4","Protein: meat, fish, pulses, milk or other dairy products. Iron: meat, dark green vegetables, fish and shellfish, nuts and seeds. Calcium: dairy products, nuts and seeds. fish dark green vegetables dairy"],[21,"ch04-o085","Short answer","What does it mean to say a pregnant woman should eat a balanced diet, and why does she need slightly more than usual?","4.4","A balanced diet means eating some of all the different nutrients that are needed to stay healthy. She needs a little more than usual because some of the nutrients she eats are passed to the growing fetus. all the different nutrients a little more passed to the fetus"],[21,"ch04-o086","Short answer","Name the three harmful substances in tobacco smoke mentioned in this topic.","4.4","Carbon monoxide, nicotine and tar. carbon monoxide nicotine tar"],[21,"ch04-o087","Short answer","Explain in full why carbon monoxide from cigarettes harms a developing fetus.","4.4","When a pregnant woman smokes, carbon monoxide diffuses from her blood into the fetus's blood. Carbon monoxide reduces the amount of oxygen that haemoglobin can transport, so the fetus's cells get less oxygen. They cannot respire as much and cannot release as much energy as normal, which can affect the development of the fetus. diffuses haemoglobin less oxygen respire energy"],[21,"ch04-o088","Short answer","Describe one measurable effect on babies born to mothers who smoked during pregnancy.","4.4","Babies born to mothers who smoked during pregnancy tend to be smaller than babies born to non-smoking mothers. smaller non-smoking mothers"],[21,"ch04-o089","Short answer","Explain why nicotine is bad for a developing fetus.","4.4","Nicotine is an addictive drug and it can damage blood vessels, so it is not good for a developing fetus to have nicotine in its blood. It reaches the fetus because it diffuses from the mother's blood into the fetus's blood. addictive damage blood vessels diffuses"],[21,"ch04-o090","Gap fill","A ____ is something that is taken into the body, and that affects the way the ____ works.","4.4","drug body"],[21,"ch04-o091","Short answer","Explain what a medicinal drug is and give two examples.","4.4","A medicinal drug is a drug that is good for health. Antibiotics kill bacteria in the body and are used to cure the infections that bacteria cause. Some painkillers, such as aspirin, are also medicinal drugs. good for health antibiotics bacteria aspirin"],[21,"ch04-o092","Multiple choice","What do antibiotics do?","4.4","They kill bacteria in the body. They reduce pain in the muscles. They increase the amount of oxygen in the blood. They break down excess protein into urea."],[21,"ch04-o093","Short answer","A pregnant woman regularly takes a drug prescribed for her own health. What should she do, and what should she not do?","4.4","She should check with her doctor or a pharmacist to see whether it is safe for her to keep on taking it. She should not stop taking it without checking first. check with a doctor pharmacist not stop without checking"],[21,"ch04-o094","Short answer","Explain why a drug taken by a pregnant woman is a problem for her fetus.","4.4","Most drugs that a pregnant woman takes will go into her fetus's blood, so the fetus is taking the drug too. Any drug that is not needed can cause harm, and these drugs can be even more harmful to a fetus than to an adult person. go into the fetus's blood the fetus is taking the drug more harmful"],[21,"ch04-o095","Short answer","State what the recommendations say about alcohol, illegal drugs and caffeine during pregnancy.","4.4","She should avoid alcohol. She should never take illegal drugs at all, and it is especially important to avoid them during pregnancy. There is not much evidence that caffeine harms a developing fetus, but most doctors recommend that a pregnant woman should not drink a lot of coffee or cola. avoid alcohol never illegal drugs not much evidence not a lot of coffee"],[21,"ch04-o096","Short answer","The advice about caffeine is worded differently from the advice about smoking. Explain what this tells you about the evidence behind each.","4.4","The advice on smoking is definite because there is strong evidence that carbon monoxide and nicotine reach the fetus and harm it. There is not much evidence that caffeine harms a developing fetus, so doctors only recommend not drinking a lot of coffee or cola rather than banning it. strong evidence not much evidence recommend rather than ban"],[21,"ch04-o097","Short answer","Explain why scientists cannot do a controlled experiment to find out how smoking affects fetal health.","4.4","It is not easy to do experiments on human health. We cannot ask some women to smoke during pregnancy and others not to, and then compare the health of their babies, because that would deliberately harm people. cannot ask some women to smoke compare harm"],[21,"ch04-o098","Short answer","Describe how scientists collect evidence about smoking and health when they cannot do an experiment.","4.4","They collect information from as many people as possible and look for correlations between smoking and fetal health. They publish details of how they did their study and their results, so that other scientists can look at the information and decide for themselves what the results show. as many people as possible correlations publish other scientists decide"],[21,"ch04-o099","Gap fill","For the first ____ months of its life a new human being grows inside its mother, and during this time it is called a ____.","4.4","nine fetus"],[21,"ch04-o100","Short answer","A pregnant woman takes iron pills. Explain why a doctor might have suggested this.","4.4","Iron is needed to make haemoglobin, and a pregnant woman needs more haemoglobin because her blood has to carry oxygen for her fetus as well as for herself. If she cannot get enough iron in her diet, iron pills can help make up the difference. haemoglobin oxygen for the fetus cannot get enough in her diet"],[21,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the main function of root hair cells?","4.1","To make food for the plant To absorb water and mineral ions from the soil To transport water up to the leaves To protect the tip of the root"],[21,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Water moves into the root hair cell by passing through the cell wall and the ____.","4.1","cell membrane"],[21,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Which ions are dissolved in the water between the soil particles?","4.1","Oxygen and carbon dioxide Nitrate and magnesium ions Calcium and iron ions Sodium and potassium ions"],[21,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","Where do the air spaces in a leaf connect to the outside air?","4.2","Through the upper epidermis Through tiny holes called stomata on the underside of the leaf Through the xylem vessels in the stem Through the root hairs"],[21,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","What happens to liquid water in leaf cells before it becomes vapour?","4.2","It freezes into ice It soaks into the cellulose cell wall and evaporates It is used entirely for photosynthesis It moves back down into the roots"],[21,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the other name for the human excretory system?","4.3","The renal system The digestive system The circulatory system The respiratory system"],[21,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","The waste substance made in liver cells from excess protein is called ____.","4.3","urea"],[21,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Which tube carries urine from the kidneys to the bladder?","4.3","The ureter The urethra The vein The artery"],[21,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","The solution made of urea dissolved in excess water is called ____.","4.3","urine"],[21,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Why does a plant wilt when it has not been watered for several days?","4.2","Its cells become soft and floppy Its roots stop absorbing minerals Its leaves turn yellow Its xylem vessels block up"],[21,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","Water evaporating from the cell wall of a leaf helps to ____ the plant.","4.2","cool"],[21,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","How do substances move from the mother's blood to the fetus's blood?","4.4","They diffuse across They are pumped by the heart The blood systems mix together They travel through the ureter"],[21,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","A baby before it is born is called a ____.","4.4","fetus"],[21,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","The fetus needs protein to make new cells and grow. The mother needs extra protein for a second reason of her own. What is it?","4.4","To make extra haemoglobin To produce more fat To increase her body temperature To make extra urea"],[21,"qwen-02-10","Gap fill","The gas in tobacco smoke that reduces the amount of oxygen haemoglobin can transport is ____.","4.4","carbon monoxide"],[21,"qwen-02-11","Multiple choice","Which mineral is essential for helping the baby grow strong bones?","4.4","Calcium Iron Sodium Potassium"],[21,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Zara used a red dye to track the movement of ____ up the celery stalk.","4.1","water"],[21,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","According to Zara's experiment, what happens to the rate of water transport in celery stalks as temperature increases?","4.1","It decreases It stays the same It increases It stops completely"],[21,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Why did the scientists measure water uptake per gram of wheat plant instead of total volume?","4.1","To compare plants of different sizes fairly Because the scale was broken To save time on calculations Because water is measured in grams"],[21,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","What was the hypothesis Sofia tested in her experiment?","4.2","Plants lose more water when it is cold Plants lose more water from their leaves when the temperature increases Plants grow faster at 30 degrees Celsius Plants need less water when the temperature is high"],[21,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","Sofia's experiment was designed to investigate how temperature affects water loss from plant ____.","4.2","leaves"],[21,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","What is the name of the process where plants lose water from their leaves?","4.2","Photosynthesis Transpiration Respiration Germination"],[21,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","Sofia's experiment involved keeping three plants at different ____.","4.2","temperatures"],[21,"qwenw-05-02","Gap fill","In the excretory system, urea dissolves in ____ to form a liquid called urine.","4.3","water"],[21,"qwenw-05-03","Multiple choice","What is the other name for the excretory system?","4.3","renal digestive circulatory respiratory"],[21,"qwenw-05-07","Multiple choice","Which blood vessel brings blood into the kidney?","4.3","artery vein ureter urethra"],[21,"qwenw-05-10","Gap fill","For each mammal species, the mean time for which ____ lasts is compared with the mean mass of an adult female.","4.4","pregnancy"],[21,"qwenw-06-02","Gap fill","A baby is recorded as low birthweight if its mass at birth is less than ____ g.","4.4","2500"],[21,"qwenw-06-04","Gap fill","The Swedish study investigated the idea that women who drink a lot of ____ during pregnancy might have smaller babies.","4.4","coffee"],[21,"qwenw-06-06","Gap fill","Scientists in ____ studied how the birthweight of a baby is affected if the mother smokes while she is pregnant.","4.4","Japan"],[22,"ch05-f01a","Diagram labelling","Name the label at position {n} in this reactivity series.","5.1",""],[22,"ch05-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on potassium in the reactivity series.","5.1",""],[22,"ch05-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on gold in the reactivity series.","5.1",""],[22,"ch05-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on iron in the reactivity series.","5.1",""],[22,"ch05-f01h4","Click the diagram","Click on magnesium in the reactivity series.","5.1",""],[22,"ch05-f01h5","Click the diagram","Click on copper in the reactivity series.","5.1",""],[22,"ch05-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that says where the iron ore and the coke are put into the furnace.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label that says where the iron is being displaced from its ore.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label that says where air is blown into the furnace.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f02h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label that says where the molten iron collects.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f03a","Diagram labelling","Copper oxide powder is being heated under a layer of charcoal powder. Name the substance labelled {n}.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the layer of charcoal powder.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the copper oxide powder.","5.2",""],[22,"ch05-f04a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus is used to make the salt copper sulfate from a metal oxide and an acid. Name the substance labelled {n}.","5.3",""],[22,"ch05-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the dilute sulfuric acid in the beaker.","5.3",""],[22,"ch05-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the copper oxide being added to the acid.","5.3",""],[22,"ch05-f05a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus is used to make the salt copper chloride from a metal carbonate and an acid. Name the substance labelled {n}.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the copper carbonate on the spatula.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the dilute hydrochloric acid in the beaker.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-f06a","Diagram labelling","A salt is being prepared by neutralisation, adding acid from a burette to an alkali. Name the substance labelled {n}.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the dilute hydrochloric acid in the burette.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the universal indicator.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the dilute sodium hydroxide in the flask.","5.4",""],[22,"ch05-r001","Short answer","Explain what the reactivity series is and how it was worked out.","5.1","The reactivity series is a list of metals placed in the order of their reactivity, with the most reactive metals at the top and the least reactive at the bottom. It was worked out by looking at the reaction of the metals with oxygen, water (or steam) and dilute acid, and using the results of all the investigations to place the metals in order. So it is a summary of experimental evidence, not a property that can be read off a single metal on its own. a list of metals in order of reactivity most reactive at the top, least reactive at the bottom built from reactions with oxygen, water or steam, and dilute acid a summary of evidence, not a single measurement"],[22,"ch05-r002","Short answer","Explain why three different tests are used to build the reactivity series rather than one.","5.1","Metals are placed in order using the results of all the investigations, with oxygen, with water or steam and with dilute acid, and one test on its own does not separate every metal. Lead has no reaction with steam or water, so the water test cannot rank it against copper, but the acid test can. Using several tests means every pair of metals is separated by at least one of them, and agreement between the tests is what makes the order trustworthy. one test cannot separate every pair of metals some metals do not react with water at all different tests separate different pairs agreement between tests makes the order trustworthy"],[22,"ch05-r003","Short answer","A metal reacts slowly with dilute acid and not at all with cold water. Explain where in the series it belongs.","5.1","It belongs somewhere in the middle to lower part of the list, because the series has the most reactive metals at the top and the least reactive at the bottom, and metals near the top show a very vigorous reaction in cold water. A metal that does not react with cold water at all must sit below those, but it cannot be at the very bottom either, since it still reacts with dilute acid where the least reactive metals do not. The two observations together fix a band rather than a single position. most reactive at the top, least at the bottom no reaction with cold water rules out the top reacting with acid rules out the very bottom the two results fix a band, not an exact place"],[22,"ch05-r004","Short answer","Lithium is missing from the reactivity series. Suggest where it should be placed and give your reasons.","5.1","It should be placed near the top, with potassium and sodium, because lithium is in the same group of the periodic table as those two metals and behaves in the same way, reacting vigorously with cold water to form a hydroxide. The series has the most reactive metals at the top, and a metal that reacts with cold water rather than needing steam or acid belongs there. Its reaction is a little less vigorous than sodium's, so just below sodium is the best estimate. place it near the top with potassium and sodium it reacts vigorously with cold water to form a hydroxide cold-water reaction places a metal near the top slightly less vigorous than sodium, so just below it"],[22,"ch05-r005","Short answer","Platinum stays shiny for a long time. Explain where in the reactivity series it belongs.","5.1","It belongs at or near the bottom, with silver and gold, because the series has the least reactive metals at the bottom. A metal that stays shiny for a long time is not reacting with the oxygen in the air, since a reactive metal forms an oxide on its surface and becomes dull. That is also why platinum is a precious metal that is used for jewellery: the jewellery keeps its appearance. place it at or near the bottom with silver and gold staying shiny means it does not react with oxygen in air reactive metals form a dull oxide layer this is why it is used for jewellery"],[22,"ch05-r006","Short answer","Three metals are added to dilute hydrochloric acid. Explain how to tell which is most reactive.","5.1","The most reactive metal is the one that reacts most vigorously: it fizzes fastest, produces bubbles of hydrogen most quickly and may make the tube warmest. The reactivity series is built by comparing exactly this kind of reaction, and the rate of the reaction becomes less vigorous as you go down the list. The samples must be the same size and the acid the same concentration, or the comparison would be measuring those differences instead. the most vigorous reaction indicates the most reactive metal compare the speed of bubbling and the warmth reactions become less vigorous down the series sample size and acid concentration must be kept the same"],[22,"ch05-r007","Short answer","Explain what happens when a clean iron nail is placed in copper sulfate solution.","5.1","The blue copper sulfate solution changes to a slightly paler colour and the nail becomes copper coloured, because the iron nail has become coated with copper. Iron is more reactive than the copper that it has pushed out from the copper sulfate and has reacted to form iron sulfate. Both observations come from the same event: the copper leaving the solution makes the blue fade, and the copper landing on the nail changes its colour. the blue solution becomes paler the nail becomes coated with copper iron is more reactive and pushes the copper out iron sulfate is formed in solution"],[22,"ch05-r008","Short answer","Explain what a displacement reaction is and state the rule that governs it.","5.1","The pushing out of one metal by another is called displacement, so this type of reaction is called a displacement reaction. The rule is that a more reactive metal can replace a less reactive one in a salt, which is why iron displaces copper from copper sulfate. The rule works in one direction only, so the reaction happens if the added metal is higher in the reactivity series than the metal in the salt. displacement is one metal pushing another out of a salt a more reactive metal replaces a less reactive one iron displaces copper from copper sulfate the rule works in one direction only"],[22,"ch05-r009","Short answer","Explain why a copper nail placed in iron sulfate solution shows no reaction.","5.1","If a copper nail was placed in a solution of iron sulfate there would be no reaction because copper is less reactive than iron, so copper cannot displace the iron in the iron sulfate. Displacement only happens when a more reactive metal can replace a less reactive one in a salt, so the rule fails in this direction. This is why displacement reactions can be used to rank metals: the absence of a reaction is evidence too. copper is less reactive than iron a less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive one displacement works in one direction only no reaction is itself evidence about the order"],[22,"ch05-r010","Short answer","Metal X displaces lead from lead chloride but not zinc from zinc chloride. Explain what this tells you.","5.1","Because a more reactive metal can replace a less reactive one in a salt, displacing lead shows that metal X is more reactive than lead. Failing to displace zinc shows that metal X is less reactive than zinc, since it cannot push zinc out of its salt. So metal X lies between zinc and lead in the reactivity series, which makes iron the obvious candidate. displacing lead means X is more reactive than lead failing to displace zinc means X is less reactive than zinc X lies between zinc and lead in the series iron fits that position"],[22,"ch05-r011","Short answer","Explain how a table of displacement results can be used to put metals in order of reactivity.","5.1","More reactive metals can displace less reactive ones from solutions of salts, so every tick in the table is a statement that the metal added is above the metal in the salt, and every blank says it is below. Working through the ticks pairs the metals up one at a time until each metal's position relative to all the others is fixed. The metal that displaces all the others is the most reactive, and the one displaced by all of them is the least. each reaction means the added metal is more reactive each non-reaction means it is less reactive the pairs together fix the whole order the metal that displaces all others is most reactive"],[22,"ch05-r012","Short answer","Sofia must identify a metal that is zinc, iron, copper or silver, using solutions of their salts. Explain how.","5.1","She adds a piece of the unknown metal to each of the four solutions in turn and records which ones react, using the rule that a more reactive metal can replace a less reactive one in a salt. Zinc displaces all three of the others, iron displaces copper and silver but not zinc, copper displaces only silver, and silver displaces none of them. The pattern of which solutions react is different for each metal, so the results identify it. add the unknown metal to each salt solution a reaction means the unknown is the more reactive metal each candidate gives a different pattern of results the pattern identifies the metal"],[22,"ch05-r013","Short answer","Explain why displacement reactions cannot be carried out in a school laboratory using potassium.","5.1","Potassium is at the top of the reactivity series and has a very vigorous reaction in cold water, so it would react violently with the water in any salt solution before it could displace the metal in the salt. The reaction is dangerously fast and releases hydrogen, so it is not something a student can do safely. This is a limit on the method, not on the rule: potassium would displace every other metal if the reaction could be done. potassium is at the top of the reactivity series it reacts violently with the water in the solution the reaction is too dangerous to do in a school laboratory a limit on the method, not on the rule"],[22,"ch05-r014","Short answer","Explain why aluminium can displace iron from iron oxide.","5.2","Aluminium is more reactive than iron, and a more reactive metal can replace a less reactive one in a compound, so aluminium will displace iron from solid iron oxide if it is heated. The products are aluminium oxide and iron, because the oxygen ends up combined with the more reactive metal. Heating is needed to start the reaction, but it is the difference in reactivity that decides that it can happen at all. aluminium is more reactive than iron a more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one the products are aluminium oxide and iron heating starts it, reactivity decides it can happen"],[22,"ch05-r015","Short answer","Explain why the thermite reaction produces molten iron.","5.2","The reaction releases a lot of energy, so it is an exothermic reaction, and the temperature gets so high that the iron that is produced is molten, in a liquid state. The melting point of iron is 1535 °C, so the reaction must raise the temperature above that figure. The energy comes from the displacement itself, not from the flame used to start it. the reaction is exothermic and releases a lot of energy the temperature rises above iron's melting point of 1535 °C the iron produced is molten the energy comes from the reaction, not the igniter"],[22,"ch05-r016","Short answer","Explain why the thermite reaction is used to weld railway rails rather than ordinary welding.","5.2","Often the rails need to be welded in situ, on the railway lines and not in a workshop where all the welding equipment is available, so the method has to be portable. The iron oxide and aluminium powder react in a container placed on the rails, and the molten iron produced in the reaction is shaped and used to join the rails together. The reaction supplies both the heat and the iron itself, so no electricity supply or heavy machinery has to be brought to the track. rails must be welded in place, not in a workshop the reaction is portable and runs in a container on the rails it supplies both the heat and the molten iron no power supply or heavy equipment is needed"],[22,"ch05-r017","Short answer","Explain why a second reaction is needed before the thermite reaction can happen.","5.2","In order for the thermite reaction to take place, the iron oxide and aluminium mixture has to be ignited, and this is done using another exothermic reaction, between magnesium powder and barium nitrate. That reaction provides the energy to start the displacement reaction between the aluminium and iron oxide. A reaction can release a great deal of energy overall and still need energy put in first to get it going. the mixture must be ignited before it will react magnesium powder and barium nitrate supply the energy that starts the aluminium and iron oxide reaction an exothermic reaction can still need energy to begin"],[22,"ch05-r018","Short answer","Carbon is not a metal. Explain how it can still be used to displace metals.","5.2","Carbon is not a metal, but it can be used to displace some metals from their compounds, and it will displace zinc, iron, tin and lead from their ores. What matters is that carbon is more reactive than those metals, so it takes the oxygen from their oxides, which is the same rule that governs displacement between metals. Being a metal is not the requirement; being higher in reactivity than the element in the compound is. carbon displaces zinc, iron, tin and lead from their ores carbon is more reactive than those metals it takes the oxygen from their oxides reactivity, not being a metal, is what matters"],[22,"ch05-r019","Short answer","Explain why air is blown into a blast furnace.","5.2","Air is blown into the furnace to burn the coke and create the high temperatures needed for the reaction. The displacement itself is between carbon and iron oxide, which reacts with carbon to form iron and carbon dioxide, but it will only proceed at very high temperature. So some of the coke is used as fuel to make the conditions and the rest is used as the reactant that displaces the iron. air burns the coke to create high temperatures the displacement needs those high temperatures carbon reacts with iron oxide to give iron and carbon dioxide coke acts as both fuel and reactant"],[22,"ch05-r020","Short answer","Early blast furnaces were built where there was iron ore and coal mining. Explain why.","5.2","The furnace needs iron ore, which is mainly iron oxide, and coke, a form of carbon made from coal, both in very large quantities. Moving thousands of tonnes of rock and coal was slow and expensive before modern transport, so it was far cheaper to build the furnace where both raw materials came out of the ground. The product, iron, is much more valuable per tonne than either raw material, so it was worth transporting instead. the furnace needs large quantities of ore and coke both were expensive to transport in bulk building on the source avoided moving them the iron produced is worth more per tonne to transport"],[22,"ch05-r021","Short answer","Explain why the discovery that carbon displaces iron was so important.","5.2","People discovered that iron ore heated with charcoal, a form of carbon, at very high temperatures produced molten iron, around 3500 years ago. Iron does not occur as the metal in the ground; it is locked in an ore, a rock that contains a metal compound, so without a way of displacing it there is no iron to use. Today this displacement reaction is still carried out, but on a large scale in a blast furnace, so the same chemistry underpins iron production now. iron is locked up in its ore as a compound heating with charcoal displaced the iron and gave molten iron without this there is no usable iron the same reaction is still used in the blast furnace"],[22,"ch05-r022","Short answer","Explain whether iron can displace aluminium from aluminium oxide.","5.2","It cannot, because aluminium is more reactive than iron, and only a more reactive element can displace a less reactive one from its compound. Iron is below aluminium in the reactivity series, so it cannot take the oxygen away from aluminium. The reaction runs the other way instead, which is exactly the thermite reaction used to weld rails. aluminium is more reactive than iron a less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive one iron cannot take oxygen from aluminium the reaction runs the other way, as in thermite"],[22,"ch05-r023","Short answer","In the copper oxide and charcoal experiment, explain why the powders are layered and not mixed.","5.2","The instruction is to place a spatula of copper oxide in a tube, add a spatula of charcoal powder on top, and not mix the powders together, then heat the two layers strongly and look carefully at where the layers meet. Keeping them separate means any change can only have happened at the boundary, so a copper-coloured layer appearing there is clear evidence that the two reacted. Mixed powders would make it impossible to tell a new product from the unchanged starting materials. the powders are layered and heated without mixing any change can only occur where the layers meet a copper colour there is evidence of reaction mixed powders would hide the product among the reactants"],[22,"ch05-r024","Short answer","Explain why carbon can be used to extract lead from lead oxide but not aluminium from aluminium oxide.","5.2","Carbon can displace lead because carbon is more reactive than lead, so heating lead oxide with carbon gives lead and carbon dioxide. Aluminium is much more reactive than many metals and sits above carbon, so carbon cannot take the oxygen away from aluminium oxide and the reaction does not happen. The position of carbon in the reactivity series is what divides the metals that can be extracted this way from those that cannot. carbon is more reactive than lead, so it displaces it aluminium is more reactive than carbon carbon cannot take oxygen from aluminium oxide carbon's position divides which metals can be extracted this way"],[22,"ch05-r025","Short answer","Copper is less reactive than lead. Predict whether carbon can extract copper from copper oxide.","5.2","It can, because carbon already displaces lead from lead oxide, and copper is less reactive than lead, so copper is even easier to displace. A more reactive element can replace a less reactive one in a compound, and carbon is above both metals. The prediction follows from the order alone, without needing a new experiment. carbon already displaces lead copper is less reactive than lead so copper is even easier to displace the prediction follows from the order alone"],[22,"ch05-r026","Short answer","A metal displaces iron and copper from their sulfates but not magnesium. Explain what this shows.","5.2","Displacing iron and copper shows the unknown metal is more reactive than both of them, since a more reactive metal replaces a less reactive one in a salt. Failing to displace magnesium shows it is less reactive than magnesium. So it lies between magnesium and iron in the series, which leaves aluminium and zinc as the candidates, and one further test against zinc sulfate would separate them. more reactive than iron and copper less reactive than magnesium so it lies between magnesium and iron aluminium and zinc remain, separated by one more test"],[22,"ch05-r027","Short answer","Explain what all salts have in common with the acids they are made from.","5.3","Many of the methods for making salts start with acids, and all acids contain hydrogen, so a salt is what is left when the hydrogen of the acid is replaced by a metal. That is why hydrochloric acid gives chlorides, sulfuric acid gives sulfates and nitric acid gives nitrates: the rest of the acid stays in the salt. Knowing the acid therefore tells you the second half of the salt's name. all acids contain hydrogen a salt is the acid with its hydrogen replaced by a metal the rest of the acid stays in the salt the acid tells you the second half of the salt's name"],[22,"ch05-r028","Short answer","Explain why carbonic acid gives carbonates and citric acid gives citrates.","5.3","Carbonic acid is a weak acid formed when carbon dioxide reacts with water, and salts made from carbonic acid are called carbonates. Citric acid is found in fruits such as oranges and lemons, and salts formed using citric acid are called citrates. The pattern is the same as for the laboratory acids: the salt keeps the name of the part of the acid that is not the hydrogen. carbonic acid forms when carbon dioxide dissolves in water its salts are called carbonates citric acid is found in fruits and gives citrates the salt is named after the non-hydrogen part of the acid"],[22,"ch05-r029","Short answer","Explain why salts are so important in everyday life, using two examples.","5.3","Sodium chloride is used to preserve food and, as table salt, to flavour food, so one salt does two different jobs in the kitchen alone. Gymnasts use the salt magnesium carbonate to keep their hands dry so that they do not slip on the apparatus, and ammonium nitrate is used as a fertiliser to help crops to grow well. Each use depends on a particular property of that salt, which is why the chemical industry makes hundreds of thousands of tonnes of different salts every day. sodium chloride preserves and flavours food magnesium carbonate keeps gymnasts' hands dry ammonium nitrate is used as a fertiliser each use depends on that salt's own properties"],[22,"ch05-r030","Short answer","Soya seeds are coated with copper sulfate before planting. Explain why.","5.3","The coating stops fungi growing on the seeds when they are planted, so the seed is protected during the period before it germinates, when it is sitting in damp soil where fungi thrive. Copper sulfate is used because it is poisonous to the fungi at the small quantity present in a coating. The salt is chosen for a specific biological effect, not simply because it is a salt. the coating stops fungi growing on the seeds seeds sit in damp soil where fungi thrive copper sulfate is poisonous to the fungi chosen for a specific effect, not at random"],[22,"ch05-r031","Short answer","Explain the general equation for the reaction of a metal with an acid and why hydrogen appears.","5.3","The general equation is acid plus metal gives salt plus hydrogen, so zinc and hydrochloric acid give zinc chloride and hydrogen. Hydrogen appears because all acids contain hydrogen, and a metal that is reactive enough displaces that hydrogen from the acid, taking its place in the compound. What is left, the metal joined to the rest of the acid, is the salt. acid + metal gives salt + hydrogen all acids contain hydrogen the metal displaces the hydrogen and takes its place the metal joined to the rest of the acid is the salt"],[22,"ch05-r032","Short answer","Explain why it would be dangerous to prepare sodium chloride by reacting sodium with hydrochloric acid.","5.3","Sodium is at the top of the reactivity series and has a very vigorous reaction even in cold water, so with an acid the reaction would be violent rather than controllable. It would release hydrogen very quickly and a great deal of energy, so the mixture could spit acid or ignite the hydrogen. Sodium chloride is made by a safer route instead, such as neutralising hydrochloric acid with sodium hydroxide. sodium is at the top of the reactivity series the reaction would be violent, not controllable hydrogen and a lot of energy are released very fast a safer route such as neutralisation is used instead"],[22,"ch05-r033","Short answer","Explain why silver and copper cannot be used to make salts by reacting them with an acid.","5.3","Some metals will not react with acids to make salts: silver and copper are too unreactive to displace hydrogen from an acid. Since all acids contain hydrogen and the metal has to push that hydrogen out to take its place, a metal below hydrogen in reactivity simply does not react. We have to find another way of making salts from unreactive metals, which is to start with a metal oxide. silver and copper are too unreactive they cannot displace hydrogen from the acid a metal must push the hydrogen out to take its place so a metal oxide is used as the starting point instead"],[22,"ch05-r034","Short answer","Explain how copper sulfate can be made even though copper does not react with acid.","5.3","We start with a metal oxide instead of the metal: the general equation is metal oxide plus acid gives salt plus water, so copper sulfate is made by heating copper oxide with sulfuric acid. Nothing has to displace hydrogen here, because the oxide already contains the copper in a combined form that the acid can react with, and the hydrogen ends up in the water. The method therefore works for unreactive metals where the direct route fails. start with the metal oxide, not the metal metal oxide + acid gives salt + water copper oxide and sulfuric acid give copper sulfate no displacement of hydrogen is needed, so it works for unreactive metals"],[22,"ch05-r035","Short answer","Explain why the mixture is left to fizz completely before it is poured into the evaporating dish.","5.3","Once the mixture stops fizzing, the acid has stopped reacting, which means all the acid that can react has been used up by the metal. Pouring earlier would carry unreacted acid into the evaporating dish, so the crystals would be contaminated and the heating would drive off acid fumes. Waiting for the fizzing to stop is therefore how you know the reaction has finished. fizzing stops when the acid has finished reacting pouring early carries unreacted acid over the crystals would be contaminated and acid fumes driven off the end of fizzing shows the reaction is complete"],[22,"ch05-r036","Short answer","Explain why the evaporating dish is heated gently and only until crystals start to form at the edge.","5.3","Heating gently avoids the solution spitting, which is a hazard since the solution may spit and burn you. Stopping once crystals appear at the edge leaves a saturated solution that is then removed from the heat and left for a few days to form crystals, which is the process of crystallisation. Slow crystallisation gives larger, better-formed crystals, whereas boiling the solution dry gives a powder and can decompose the salt. gentle heating prevents spitting and burns stop when crystals appear at the edge the rest crystallises slowly over a few days slow crystallisation gives better crystals than boiling dry"],[22,"ch05-r037","Short answer","Explain how you could make zinc nitrate using zinc metal, and why silver sulfate cannot be made that way.","5.3","Zinc nitrate is made by adding zinc metal to dilute nitric acid, following acid plus metal gives salt plus hydrogen, and then crystallising the solution once the fizzing stops. Silver sulfate cannot be made this way because silver is too unreactive to displace hydrogen from an acid, so no reaction takes place at all. A metal oxide would have to be used as the starting material instead. add zinc to dilute nitric acid, then crystallise acid + metal gives salt + hydrogen silver is too unreactive to displace hydrogen a metal oxide must be used for silver instead"],[22,"ch05-r038","Short answer","Explain why the acid mixture must not be allowed to boil when making copper sulfate from copper oxide.","5.3","Boiling makes the acid mixture spit, which is dangerous since hot acid can burn skin and eyes, and it also drives off acid vapour into the room. It would also evaporate the water too quickly, so the salt would be left as a crust rather than growing into crystals. Gentle heating gives the reaction time to finish and the solution time to crystallise properly. boiling makes hot acid spit and gives off vapour hot acid can burn skin and eyes the water evaporates too fast for crystals to grow gentle heating lets the reaction finish and crystals form"],[22,"ch05-r039","Short answer","Explain the general equation for an acid reacting with a carbonate and how it differs from acid with a metal.","5.4","The general equation is acid plus carbonate gives salt plus water plus carbon dioxide, so sulfuric acid and calcium carbonate give calcium sulfate, water and carbon dioxide. It differs from acid plus metal, which gives salt plus hydrogen, because the gas produced is carbon dioxide rather than hydrogen, and water is produced as well. The carbon and oxygen of the carbonate leave together as carbon dioxide, which is why fizzing is seen in both reactions but the gases are different. acid + carbonate gives salt + water + carbon dioxide acid + metal gives salt + hydrogen the gas is carbon dioxide, not hydrogen water is produced as well with a carbonate"],[22,"ch05-r040","Short answer","Explain how you would check that the gas given off by an acid and a carbonate is carbon dioxide.","5.4","Bubble the gas through limewater: carbon dioxide turns limewater milky, and no other gas produced in these reactions does. A lighted splint would not distinguish it usefully, since carbon dioxide simply puts the splint out, and that is also what happens with several other gases. The limewater test is chosen because a positive result points to one gas only. bubble the gas through limewater carbon dioxide turns limewater milky a splint test does not identify it uniquely the test is chosen because the positive result is specific"],[22,"ch05-r041","Short answer","Explain why limestone buildings are damaged by acid rain.","5.4","The rock limestone is made of calcium carbonate, and it is damaged when it reacts with acid rain and erodes. The reaction is acid plus carbonate gives salt plus water plus carbon dioxide, so the solid carbonate is converted into a soluble salt and a gas, and the stone is carried away rather than staying in place. The damage is therefore chemical: the rock is being turned into other substances, not simply worn down. limestone is calcium carbonate acid rain reacts with it as acid plus carbonate the solid becomes a soluble salt, water and carbon dioxide the damage is chemical, not simple wearing away"],[22,"ch05-r042","Short answer","Explain why coral is damaged when the oceans become more acidic.","5.4","The skeletons of coral are made from calcium carbonate and react with acids, and this happens when the oceans become slightly more acidic as more carbon dioxide dissolves in the water. Carbon dioxide dissolving in water forms carbonic acid, and acid plus carbonate gives salt plus water plus carbon dioxide, so the skeleton is slowly dissolved away. The same chemistry that erodes limestone buildings attacks the coral. coral skeletons are calcium carbonate dissolved carbon dioxide makes the water more acidic acid reacts with carbonate and dissolves the skeleton the same reaction that erodes limestone"],[22,"ch05-r043","Short answer","Explain what neutralisation is and what it produces.","5.4","Alkalis react with acids to neutralise them, and when an acid is neutralised by an alkali a salt is produced, with water as the other product. For example, sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid to form the salt sodium chloride and water. The general equation is acid plus alkali gives salt plus water, and no gas is produced, which is one way this route differs from using a metal or a carbonate. an alkali neutralises an acid the products are a salt and water sodium hydroxide and hydrochloric acid give sodium chloride and water no gas is produced by this route"],[22,"ch05-r044","Short answer","Explain the difference between a base and an alkali.","5.4","Metal oxides are called bases, and soluble metal bases form alkalis when they dissolve in water, since when a metal oxide dissolves in water it forms an alkaline solution. So every alkali is a base that happens to dissolve, but not every base is an alkali: some metal oxides are not soluble in water, for example iron and copper oxide, so they do not form alkalis. Those insoluble bases can still react with acids to form salts, which is how copper sulfate is made. metal oxides are bases a soluble base dissolves to form an alkali iron and copper oxide are insoluble, so form no alkali insoluble bases still react with acids to make salts"],[22,"ch05-r045","Short answer","Suggest how you could use iron oxide to make iron chloride.","5.4","Iron oxide is a base, and although it is not soluble in water so it forms no alkali, it can still react with an acid to form a salt. Heating iron oxide gently with dilute hydrochloric acid gives iron chloride and water, following metal oxide plus acid gives salt plus water. The solution is then filtered to remove any unreacted oxide and crystallised to obtain the salt. iron oxide is an insoluble base it still reacts with an acid to give a salt warm it with dilute hydrochloric acid to give iron chloride and water filter off unreacted oxide, then crystallise"],[22,"ch05-r046","Short answer","Explain why excess copper carbonate is added to the acid and then filtered off.","5.4","More and more copper carbonate is added until there is no more reaction, which shows that all the acid has been used up, so no acid is left to contaminate the salt or to spit when the solution is heated. The unreacted copper carbonate that is left is a solid, so filtering removes it and the filtrate passing into the flask is the salt solution alone. Using an excess is deliberate: it guarantees the acid is fully reacted without having to measure quantities precisely. excess is added until no more reaction occurs this shows all the acid has been used up filtering removes the unreacted solid an excess guarantees completion without precise measurement"],[22,"ch05-r047","Short answer","Zara and Marcus neutralise potassium hydroxide with acid. Explain how they know when it is neutralised.","5.4","They add an indicator to the alkali first and add the acid a little at a time until the indicator shows the solution is no longer alkaline, since the point of neutralisation cannot be seen otherwise: acid plus alkali gives a salt and water, and both are colourless. This is why they then have a coloured solution and have to remove the colour so that the crystals of potassium chloride are pure. Using a burette to add the acid makes it possible to stop at the right volume. neutralisation cannot be seen without an indicator the salt and water are both colourless add acid gradually until the indicator changes the indicator colour must then be removed for pure crystals"],[22,"ch05-r048","Short answer","Explain how the colour of the indicator can be removed so that the potassium chloride crystals are pure.","5.4","They repeat the neutralisation without the indicator, using exactly the volume of acid that they measured the first time, so the reaction is complete but nothing coloured has been added. The first run is used only to find the volume; the second produces the salt. The crystals are then obtained by crystallising that colourless solution. repeat the neutralisation without indicator use the volume of acid found in the first run the first run measures, the second produces the salt crystallise the colourless solution"],[22,"ch05-r049","Short answer","Substance A gives a gas that turns limewater milky; substance C gives a gas that pops with a lighted splint. Explain what each tells you.","5.4","A gas that turns limewater milky is carbon dioxide, and carbon dioxide is produced when an acid reacts with a carbonate, so substance A must be a carbonate. A squeaky pop with a lighted splint identifies hydrogen, and hydrogen is produced when an acid reacts with a metal, so substance C must be a metal. Since A produced zinc sulfate it was zinc carbonate with sulfuric acid, and since C produced magnesium chloride it was magnesium with hydrochloric acid. limewater turning milky identifies carbon dioxide, so A is a carbonate a squeaky pop identifies hydrogen, so C is a metal A was zinc carbonate with sulfuric acid C was magnesium with hydrochloric acid"],[22,"ch05-r050","Short answer","Substance B produced no bubbles but gave iron chloride. Explain what B must have been.","5.4","No bubbles means no gas was produced, which rules out both a metal, which would give hydrogen, and a carbonate, which would give carbon dioxide. The remaining route to a salt is metal oxide plus acid gives salt plus water, and water is not seen as bubbles. Since the salt formed was iron chloride, substance B was iron oxide and the liquid added was hydrochloric acid. no gas rules out a metal and a carbonate the remaining route is metal oxide plus acid that route gives water, not a gas B was iron oxide and the liquid hydrochloric acid"],[22,"ch05-r051","Short answer","Explain what happens to atoms in a chemical reaction.","5.5","In chemical reactions atoms form new combinations: atoms that are on their own may join together with other atoms, and atoms that are bonded with other atoms may separate, forming new combinations with other atoms. In the reaction between iron and sulfur, the iron and sulfur atoms that were there at the start are still there at the end; they have just rearranged themselves. No atoms are lost and no new atoms are produced. atoms form new combinations bonded atoms may separate and rejoin differently the same atoms are present at the start and the end no atoms are lost and none are made"],[22,"ch05-r052","Short answer","Explain why no element present in the reactants can be missing from the products.","5.5","No element that is present in the reactants disappears from the products and no new element appears in the products, because the atoms are simply rearranged into new combinations rather than being destroyed or created. In magnesium and hydrochloric acid, the magnesium ends up in the salt magnesium chloride, the hydrogen that was part of the acid leaves as hydrogen gas, and the chlorine that was part of the acid ends up in the salt. Every element can be traced from one side of the equation to the other. no element disappears and no new element appears atoms are rearranged, not created or destroyed magnesium ends up in the salt, hydrogen leaves as a gas every element can be traced across the equation"],[22,"ch05-r053","Short answer","Explain what a symbol equation tells you that a word equation does not.","5.5","A symbol equation tells you not only which elements are present in the reactants and products but how many of each atom is present. In the magnesium and hydrochloric acid equation, there is one atom of magnesium on each side, two atoms of hydrogen on each side and two atoms of chlorine on each side. A word equation names the substances but cannot show that the atoms balance, which is the evidence that nothing has been gained or lost. a symbol equation gives the number of each atom one magnesium, two hydrogen, two chlorine on each side a word equation names substances only the counts show that nothing is gained or lost"],[22,"ch05-r054","Short answer","Explain why the mass of the flask does not change in the calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid experiment.","5.5","If no atoms are gained or lost during a chemical reaction, then no mass is gained or lost either, and the stopper in the top of the flask means no atoms can enter or leave. The carbon dioxide produced is still inside the flask, so although a gas has been made, it is still being weighed. In chemical reactions the elements you begin with are the ones you end the reaction with, so the mass you begin with is the mass you end with. no atoms are gained or lost, so no mass is the stopper means nothing can enter or leave the carbon dioxide stays in the flask and is still weighed the mass at the end equals the mass at the start"],[22,"ch05-r055","Short answer","Explain why the idea that the mass will decrease because a gas is light is wrong.","5.5","Gases have mass just like solids and liquids; being light means having a low density, not having no mass at all. The carbon dioxide produced is sealed in the flask, so its mass is still on the balance, and no atoms can enter or leave. The mistake is to treat a gas as nothing rather than as matter that happens to occupy a large volume. gases have mass; low density is not zero mass the carbon dioxide stays inside the sealed flask its mass is still on the balance a gas is matter that occupies a large volume"],[22,"ch05-r056","Short answer","Explain why the idea that mass increases because there are more products than reactants is wrong.","5.5","Counting substances is not the same as counting atoms: two reactants can rearrange into three products without a single extra atom appearing, since the atoms are simply rearranged into new combinations. Mass depends on how many atoms of each kind there are, and no atoms are lost and no new atoms are produced. So the number of names on each side of an equation says nothing about the total mass. counting substances is not counting atoms two reactants can rearrange into three products no new atoms are produced mass depends on the atoms, not the number of substances"],[22,"ch05-r057","Short answer","In Arun's open flask the mass appears to decrease. Explain why, and what he should have done.","5.5","The law of conservation of mass tells you that there must be the same mass at the end of the reaction as at the start, so the mass has not really been lost. The carbon dioxide gas escapes into the air because the flask is open, so you cannot measure its mass, and it appears as if the mass decreases as the reaction continues. He should have closed the flask with a stopper so that the gas was kept inside and included in every reading. mass cannot really be lost carbon dioxide escapes from the open flask the escaped gas is no longer weighed, so the reading falls he should have stoppered the flask to keep the gas in"],[22,"ch05-r058","Short answer","Explain why the ashes weigh more than the magnesium at the start when magnesium is heated in a crucible.","5.5","There is an increase in mass because oxygen from the air has combined with the magnesium, following magnesium plus oxygen gives magnesium oxide. The lid of the crucible is lifted from time to time during the heating to allow the air in, so the oxygen needed is supplied from outside the crucible and its mass is added to what was there at the start. Mass is still conserved overall; it is only the contents of the crucible that have gained. oxygen from the air combines with the magnesium magnesium + oxygen gives magnesium oxide the lid is lifted to let air in, so oxygen is added mass is conserved overall; the crucible's contents gain"],[22,"ch05-r059","Short answer","Explain why some people expect the ashes to be lighter, and why they are wrong.","5.5","Some people think that the ashes will be lighter because the ashes look smaller than the magnesium, and they see the flames escaping from the crucible, so they think something has been lost. Both are judgements about appearance rather than mass: a powder can occupy less space than a ribbon while containing more matter, and light is not a substance leaving. The word equation explains what happens, since oxygen from the air has combined with the magnesium. they judge by volume and by the visible flames a smaller-looking powder can still have more mass light leaving is not matter leaving oxygen from the air has combined with the magnesium"],[22,"ch05-r060","Short answer","Explain what Lavoisier did and why his work mattered.","5.5","Antoine Lavoisier carried out the magnesium heating experiment in 1772 and repeated it many times, finding an increase in mass every time, which is what made the result something to explain rather than an error. He could not explain it at first, but finally came up with the idea that when something burns it combines with a gas from the air. He also found that the gas from the air involved in burning is involved in respiration as well, linking two processes that had seemed unrelated. he repeated the experiment many times and always found a gain repetition made the result something to explain, not an error he concluded that burning combines a substance with a gas from the air he linked that same gas to respiration"],[22,"ch05-r061","Short answer","Explain, in terms of bonds, what makes a reaction exothermic or endothermic.","5.5","All chemical reactions involve energy: energy is used to break bonds in the reactants and energy is released when new bonds are formed in the products. If less energy is needed to break bonds in the reactants than is released when bonds form in the products, energy is released overall and the reaction is exothermic. If it takes more energy to break the bonds in the reactants than is released when the new bonds form, the reaction takes in energy and is endothermic. breaking bonds takes in energy forming bonds releases energy releasing more than is taken in gives an exothermic reaction taking in more than is released gives an endothermic reaction"],[22,"ch05-r062","Short answer","Explain why sherbet sweets feel cool in your mouth.","5.5","The reaction between sodium hydrogencarbonate and citric acid happens when we eat sherbet sweets, and it is endothermic, so it takes in energy. When this reaction takes place, energy is transferred from the environment, which is your mouth, to chemical energy stored in the bonds of the products. Your mouth loses energy, so it feels cool; nothing has actually been added to cool it. sodium hydrogencarbonate reacts with citric acid in the mouth the reaction is endothermic and takes in energy energy is transferred from your mouth to the products' bonds your mouth loses energy, so it feels cool"],[22,"ch05-r063","Short answer","Explain why energy is conserved even in a reaction that releases a lot of energy.","5.5","Whether a chemical reaction takes in energy or releases energy, there is no overall change in the amount of energy during the reaction, because energy cannot be created or destroyed, which is the law of conservation of energy. Energy may be transferred from one form to another, but the same amount of energy remains after the reaction as before. In an exothermic reaction, energy stored in bonds has been transferred to the surroundings, not made. energy cannot be created or destroyed the total amount is unchanged by the reaction energy is transferred from one form to another an exothermic reaction moves stored energy to the surroundings"],[22,"ch05-r064","Short answer","Arun starts with 100 g zinc and 150 g acid but measures 247 g at the end, twice. Explain the result.","5.5","Conservation of mass says the products should total 250 g, so 3 g appears to be missing, and because he repeats the experiment and gets the same result it is not a random error. The reaction of zinc with sulfuric acid produces hydrogen, and the beakers are open, so the hydrogen escaped into the air and was not weighed. Repeating the experiment in a closed container would show the full 250 g. the products should total 250 g the same result twice means it is not a random error hydrogen gas escaped from the open beaker a closed container would show the full mass"],[22,"ch05-r065","Short answer","Explain what a scientist should do when an experiment gives an unexpected result.","5.5","They should first repeat the experiment to see whether the result is reproducible, since a result that happens once may be a mistake but one that happens every time needs explaining. Then they should check the method for anything that could let matter or energy in or out unnoticed, such as an open container. If the result survives all of that, the explanation has to change, which is exactly what Lavoisier did after finding an increase in mass every time. repeat it to see whether it is reproducible a repeatable result needs explaining, not discarding check the method for unnoticed losses or gains if it survives, the explanation must change"],[22,"ch05-r066","Short answer","In the investigation burning magnesium in oxygen, explain which variables must be controlled.","5.5","The question is how the mass of the compound formed depends on the mass of the magnesium burned, so the mass of magnesium is the variable that is changed and the mass of compound is the one measured. Everything else must be kept the same: the volume of oxygen in the gas jar, the purity of the magnesium, the heating time and temperature, and the same balance used for every measurement. If those changed as well, a difference in the product mass could not be attributed to the mass of magnesium. mass of magnesium is changed, mass of compound measured keep the volume of oxygen the same keep purity, heating time and temperature the same use the same balance throughout otherwise the cause of a difference is unknown"],[22,"ch05-r067","Short answer","Explain why the mass of compound stops increasing when the mass of magnesium goes from 2.0 g to 3.0 g.","5.5","The magnesium is burned in a limited volume of pure oxygen, so there is only a fixed amount of oxygen available to combine with it. Once all the oxygen has reacted, adding more magnesium cannot form any more magnesium oxide, so the mass of compound stays the same and the extra magnesium is left unreacted. The graph levels off at the point where oxygen, not magnesium, became the limiting factor. the volume of oxygen is limited once the oxygen is used up no more oxide can form extra magnesium is left unreacted the graph levels off where oxygen becomes limiting"],[22,"ch05-o002","Short answer","What is the reactivity series, and which end of it holds the most reactive metals?","5.1","It is a list of the metals in order of how reactive they are, with the most reactive metals at the top and the least reactive at the bottom. list order most reactive at the top least reactive at the bottom"],[22,"ch05-o003","Gap fill","In the reactivity series the two metals right at the top are ____ and ____, and the metal right at the bottom is ____.","5.1","potassium sodium gold"],[22,"ch05-o007","Multiple choice","How does magnesium react with cold water?","5.1","There is a slow reaction in cold water to form the hydroxide. There is a violent reaction and it is very dangerous. There is no reaction with steam or water. It only reacts if an acid is added first."],[22,"ch05-o008","Short answer","What happens when aluminium, zinc or iron meets water, and what does that tell you about their position in the series?","5.1","They react with steam but not with water, so they form an oxide only when the water is hot enough to be steam. That places them below the metals that react with cold water. steam not water oxide below"],[22,"ch05-o009","Gap fill","Lead shows ____ reaction with steam or water.","5.1","no"],[22,"ch05-o010","Short answer","What is a mnemonic, and how would you use one to learn the reactivity series?","5.1","A mnemonic is a sentence in which the first letter of each word is the same as the first letter of the things you want to remember. You would make a sentence whose words begin with K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, Cu, Ag, Au, in that order. sentence first letter order"],[22,"ch05-o013","Short answer","Explain why the iron nail becomes coated with copper, using the word 'displacement'.","5.1","Iron is more reactive than copper, so it pushes the copper out of the copper sulfate and takes its place, forming iron sulfate. That pushing out is called displacement. iron is more reactive pushed out displacement"],[22,"ch05-o019","Multiple choice","Why is the iron made in the thermite reaction molten?","5.2","Because the reaction releases so much energy that the temperature goes above iron's melting point of 1535 degrees C. Because iron has an unusually low melting point. Because the reaction takes in energy from the surroundings. Because the iron is dissolved in the aluminium oxide."],[22,"ch05-o021","Short answer","Explain why the thermite reaction is used for welding railway rails rather than an electric welder.","5.2","The rails often have to be welded in situ, out on the line and not in a workshop with all the welding equipment. The thermite reaction can be carried out anywhere, and the molten iron it makes is shaped and used to join the rails together. in situ not in a workshop molten iron join the rails"],[22,"ch05-o022","Short answer","How is the thermite reaction started, and why is another reaction needed to do it?","5.2","The iron oxide and aluminium mixture has to be ignited, using another exothermic reaction between magnesium powder and barium nitrate. That reaction provides the energy needed to start the displacement reaction. ignited magnesium powder barium nitrate provides the energy"],[22,"ch05-o023","Gap fill","An ore is a ____ that contains a metal ____.","5.2","rock compound"],[22,"ch05-o026","Short answer","How long ago did people first use carbon to get iron from its ore, and how did they do it?","5.2","About 3500 years ago. They heated iron ore with charcoal, which is a form of carbon, at very high temperatures, and this produced molten iron. 3500 years charcoal very high temperatures molten iron"],[22,"ch05-o030","Short answer","In the experiment where copper oxide is heated with charcoal, why are the two powders left in separate layers instead of being mixed?","5.2","So that any new substance can only appear where the two layers meet. That shows the copper was made by the reaction and was not there at the start. separate layers where they meet made by the reaction"],[22,"ch05-o031","Short answer","Apart from sodium chloride, name three salts you have already met in your science course.","5.3","Copper sulfate, silver nitrate and calcium carbonate. copper sulfate silver nitrate calcium carbonate"],[22,"ch05-o032","Short answer","Give two everyday uses of sodium chloride.","5.3","It is used to preserve food, and as table salt to flavour food. preserve flavour table salt"],[22,"ch05-o033","Short answer","Why do gymnasts use magnesium carbonate?","5.3","To keep their hands dry so that they do not slip on the apparatus. hands dry do not slip"],[22,"ch05-o034","Gap fill","The salt used to make blackboard chalk is ____ ____.","5.3","calcium sulfate"],[22,"ch05-o035","Short answer","What is aluminium sulfate added to dyes for?","5.3","To help the dyes stick to fibres. help stick fibres"],[22,"ch05-o039","Gap fill","Hydrochloric acid makes salts called ____, sulfuric acid makes ____, and nitric acid makes ____.","5.3","chlorides sulfates nitrates"],[22,"ch05-o040","Multiple choice","Which acid would you need in order to make the salt potassium nitrate?","5.3","nitric acid hydrochloric acid sulfuric acid carbonic acid"],[22,"ch05-o045","Short answer","Why can copper and silver not be used to make salts by adding them to an acid?","5.3","They are too unreactive to displace hydrogen from an acid, so no reaction happens. Another route, starting from the metal oxide, has to be used instead. too unreactive displace hydrogen metal oxide"],[22,"ch05-o049","Short answer","Carbonates are themselves salts. Which acid are they formed from?","5.4","Carbonic acid. Carbonates can be formed by the reaction of a metal with carbonic acid. carbonic acid metal"],[22,"ch05-o050","Short answer","Write the general equation for the reaction between an acid and a carbonate.","5.4","acid + carbonate -> salt + water + carbon dioxide acid carbonate salt water carbon dioxide"],[22,"ch05-o054","Short answer","Explain why coral is at risk as more carbon dioxide dissolves in the oceans.","5.4","The skeletons of coral are made from calcium carbonate, which reacts with acids. As more carbon dioxide dissolves in the water the oceans become slightly more acidic, so the coral reacts and is damaged. calcium carbonate more acidic carbon dioxide react"],[22,"ch05-o059","Gap fill","In the neutralisation practical, ____ cm3 of sodium hydroxide is measured into a small flask, and a few drops of ____ ____ solution are added.","5.4","20 universal indicator"],[22,"ch05-o060","Short answer","What is a base, and when is a base also an alkali?","5.4","Metal oxides are called bases. A base is also an alkali when it is soluble, because soluble metal bases form alkalis when they dissolve in water. metal oxides bases soluble alkalis"],[22,"ch05-o062","Short answer","Iron oxide and copper oxide do not form alkalis. Are they still bases, and can they still be used to make salts?","5.4","Yes. They are metal oxides, so they are bases. They are not soluble in water so they do not form alkalis, but they can still react with acids to form salts. not soluble still bases react with acids salts"],[22,"ch05-o069","Multiple choice","Calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid react in a stoppered flask on a balance. What happens to the reading?","5.5","It stays the same, because no atoms can enter or leave the flask. It decreases, because one of the products is a gas and gases are very light. It increases, because there are two reactants and three products. It decreases, because the acid is used up."],[22,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","Which metal is listed as the most reactive in the reactivity series?","5.1","Gold Potassium Copper Iron"],[22,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The list of metals arranged by their reactivity is called the ____ series.","5.1","reactivity"],[22,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","Where are the least reactive metals placed in the reactivity series?","5.1","At the top In the middle At the bottom On the left"],[22,"qwen-04-04","Gap fill","The reaction between aluminium and iron oxide is called the ____ reaction.","5.2","thermite"],[22,"qwen-04-05","Multiple choice","What state is the iron in after the thermite reaction?","5.2","Solid Gas Molten (liquid) Plasma"],[22,"qwen-04-06","Gap fill","Carbon can displace zinc, iron, tin and ____ from their ores.","5.2","lead"],[22,"qwen-04-07","Multiple choice","What is an ore?","5.2","A pure metal element A rock that contains a metal compound A liquid mixture of metals A gas produced by heating"],[22,"qwen-05-01","Multiple choice","Which salt is used by gymnasts to keep their hands dry?","5.3","Sodium chloride Magnesium carbonate Calcium sulfate Ammonium nitrate"],[22,"qwen-05-02","Gap fill","Calcium sulfate is a salt that is used to make ____.","5.3","blackboard chalk"],[22,"qwen-05-03","Multiple choice","What are salts made from carbonic acid called?","5.3","Chlorides Carbonates Nitrates Sulfates"],[22,"qwen-05-09","Multiple choice","Which gas is produced when an acid reacts with a carbonate?","5.4","Hydrogen Oxygen Carbon dioxide Nitrogen"],[22,"qwen-05-10","Gap fill","The rock limestone is made of calcium carbonate and it ____ when it reacts with acid rain.","5.4","erodes"],[22,"qwen-06-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the bright blue-green mineral found in rocks that contains copper salts?","5.4","Malachite Quartz Calcite Gypsum"],[22,"qwen-06-02","Gap fill","When an acid reacts with an alkali, the two products formed are a salt and ____.","5.4","water"],[22,"qwen-06-05","Multiple choice","Why does the mass of a sealed flask containing reacting chemicals stay constant?","5.5","Because no atoms can enter or leave the flask Because the chemicals turn into gas Because the heat makes the mass disappear Because the stopper adds weight"],[22,"qwen-06-06","Gap fill","The principle that states no mass is gained or lost in a chemical reaction is called the law of conservation of ____.","5.5","mass"],[22,"qwen-06-07","Multiple choice","In Arun's experiment, why did the mass of the flask and contents appear to decrease?","5.5","The carbon dioxide gas escaped into the air The flask broke and lost liquid The balance was not working correctly The chemicals turned into light"],[22,"qwen-06-08","Gap fill","When magnesium is heated in a crucible, it reacts with oxygen from the air to form ____.","5.5","magnesium oxide"],[22,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","Which metal is the most reactive in the reactivity series?","5.1","Gold Potassium Copper Iron"],[22,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","In a displacement reaction, the more reactive metal pushes out or ____ the less reactive metal from its salt solution.","5.1","displaces"],[22,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","If you put a copper nail in iron sulfate solution, will a reaction happen?","5.1","Yes, because copper is more reactive No, because copper is less reactive than iron Yes, because iron is less reactive No, because both are unreactive"],[22,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","Salts made from sulfuric acid are called ____ salts.","5.3","sulfate"],[22,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Why can't carbon be used to extract aluminium from its oxide?","5.2","Aluminium is less reactive than carbon Aluminium is more reactive than carbon Carbon does not react with oxides The reaction produces no gas"],[22,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","When making copper chloride, unreacted copper carbonate is removed by ____ the mixture.","5.4","filtering"],[22,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","Which acid is used to make magnesium nitrate?","5.3","Hydrochloric acid Sulfuric acid Nitric acid Citric acid"],[22,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","The most common ore of lead is called lead ____.","5.2","sulfide"],[22,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","In a blast furnace, iron is produced from its ore by using ____ to displace it.","5.2","carbon"],[22,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","When the gas from substance C is tested with a lighted splint, it makes a squeaky pop. This gas is ____.","5.4","hydrogen"],[22,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","In the reaction between magnesium and oxygen to form magnesium oxide, one atom of ____ bonds with one atom of oxygen.","5.5","magnesium"],[22,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","What happens to the atoms when chemicals react together?","5.5","They are lost They rearrange to make other chemicals They disappear into the air They turn into energy"],[22,"qwenw-02-07","Multiple choice","Marcus mixes 10 g of iron filings and 6 g of sulfur. After heating, the mass of the product is 16 g. What does this show about mass in a chemical reaction?","5.5","Mass is created Mass is destroyed The mass does not change Only half the mass remains"],[22,"qwenw-02-09","Multiple choice","Which acid should Zara and Marcus use to prepare potassium chloride from potassium hydroxide?","5.4","Hydrochloric acid Sulfuric acid Nitric acid Citric acid"],[23,"ch06-f001","Click the diagram","Click on the label that names the direction the sound wave is travelling.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f002","Click the diagram","Click on the label that describes how the air particles move.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f003","Diagram labelling","Name the part of the graph labelled {n}.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f004","Click the diagram","Click on the amplitude label that is above the centre line of the wave.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f005","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the horizontal axis.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f006","Diagram labelling","These two oscilloscope traces differ in loudness. Name label {n}.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f007","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the louder sound.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f008","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the sound with the higher pitch.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f009","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the sound with the lower pitch.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f010","Diagram labelling","Name the part of the apparatus labelled {n}.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f011","Click the diagram","Click on the blocks of wood.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f012","Click the diagram","Click on the mass taped to the end of the metre rule.","6.1",""],[23,"ch06-f013","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} on this diagram of two waves cancelling.","6.2",""],[23,"ch06-f014","Click the diagram","Click on the peak of the upper wave.","6.2",""],[23,"ch06-f015","Click the diagram","Click on the trough of the lower wave.","6.2",""],[23,"ch06-f016","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in this diagram of noise-cancelling headphones.","6.2",""],[23,"ch06-f017","Click the diagram","Click on the part that detects the sound coming from outside.","6.2",""],[23,"ch06-f018","Diagram labelling","Name the stage or object labelled {n} in the collision theory diagram.","6.3",""],[23,"ch06-f019","Click the diagram","Click on Theia.","6.3",""],[23,"ch06-f020","Click the diagram","Click on the ring of rocks and dust.","6.3",""],[23,"ch06-f021","Diagram labelling","Name the part of the Earth labelled {n}.","6.5",""],[23,"ch06-f022","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the convection current on the left-hand side of this diagram.","6.5",""],[23,"ch06-f023","Click the diagram","Click on the mantle.","6.5",""],[23,"ch06-f024","Click the diagram","Click on the African Plate.","6.5",""],[23,"ch06-f025","Diagram labelling","Name the continent labelled {n} on the jigsaw fit.","6.5",""],[23,"ch06-r001","Short answer","A bell is rung. A student standing next to it hears a loud sound, but a student on the far side of the field hears the same bell as a faint sound. The bell vibrated in exactly the same way both times. Explain the difference.","6.1","Loudness depends on two things: how much the object vibrates, and how far away you are. The bell's vibration was identical for both students, so the difference must come from distance. As the sound wave spreads out from the bell, its energy is dissipated into the surroundings, so less energy arrives at the distant student's ear each second and the sound is heard as quieter. distance energy dissipation spreads out quieter same vibration"],[23,"ch06-r002","Short answer","Explain, in terms of the air particles, why making a drum skin vibrate through a bigger range makes a louder sound.","6.1","The drum skin pushes and pulls the air particles next to it. The greater the distance of each vibration of the skin, the greater the distance that the air particles are pushed and pulled. That larger particle movement is a larger amplitude in the sound wave, and a larger amplitude is heard as a louder sound. range of vibration particles pushed larger amplitude louder"],[23,"ch06-r003","Short answer","Why do scientists draw a graph of a sound wave instead of drawing the moving air particles themselves?","6.1","In a sound wave the particles move backwards and forwards along the same direction as the wave travels, which is very difficult to show clearly in a drawing. A graph of the distance the particles move against time shows the same information but is much easier to draw and to read, so amplitude and frequency can be measured from it. difficult to draw graph distance against time easier to measure"],[23,"ch06-r004","Short answer","Define the amplitude of a sound wave, and say what is being measured.","6.1","The amplitude is the maximum distance that the particles move, either forward or backward, from their resting position in the sound wave. It is a distance, so it is measured in units such as millimetres, and it is the property of the wave that determines how loud the sound is. maximum distance particles forward or backward distance unit"],[23,"ch06-r005","Short answer","A student measures the distance from zero to the peak of a wave and gets 4 mm. Without measuring again, state the distance from zero to the trough and explain how you know.","6.1","The distance from zero to the trough is also 4 mm. Amplitude can be measured either from zero up to the peak or from zero down to the trough, and both of these distances are equal in a graph of the same sound wave, because the particles move the same distance forward as they move backward. 4 mm equal peak trough same wave"],[23,"ch06-r006","Short answer","On an oscilloscope trace, what is meant by a peak and what is meant by a trough?","6.1","The peak is the highest point of the graph and the trough is the lowest point. The peak shows the moment when the air particles have moved their maximum distance one way, and the trough shows the moment when they have moved their maximum distance the other way. peak highest trough lowest maximum distance"],[23,"ch06-r007","Short answer","Two sounds are made by the same loudspeaker. Sound A has a larger amplitude than sound B. State which sound is louder and explain why.","6.1","Sound A is louder. As the amplitude of a sound wave increases, the loudness of the sound increases, because a larger amplitude means the air particles are being moved a greater distance, which carries more energy to the ear. A larger amplitude louder more energy"],[23,"ch06-r008","Short answer","Explain how an oscilloscope is able to show a picture of a sound that you can only hear.","6.1","An oscilloscope uses a microphone to detect the sound. The microphone converts the vibrations of the air into an electrical signal, and the oscilloscope then produces a graph of that signal on a screen. The trace on the screen is a graph of how the particles move with time, so it can be used to compare amplitude and frequency. microphone detects sound graph on screen compare waves"],[23,"ch06-r009","Short answer","A ruler is held over the edge of a desk and twanged twice. The second time it makes a higher pitch sound. Explain what must have been different about the vibration.","6.1","The second time, the ruler vibrated faster. The pitch of the sound from a vibrating ruler changes because the speed of the vibrations changes, and the faster the vibrations, the higher the pitch of the sound. A shorter length of ruler over the edge of the desk would vibrate faster and give the higher pitch. vibrated faster speed of vibration higher pitch shorter length"],[23,"ch06-r010","Short answer","Explain why frequency is described as a number per second, and state what it tells you about the sound.","6.1","Frequency is the number of vibrations in each second, and 'per' means 'in each'. It is therefore a count of complete vibrations divided by the time taken. Frequency tells you the pitch of the sound: as the frequency of a sound wave increases, the pitch of the sound also increases. number of vibrations in each second per means in each pitch"],[23,"ch06-r011","Short answer","A tuning fork is labelled 500 Hz. Explain what this number means in terms of the movement of the fork.","6.1","A frequency of 500 Hz means that 500 vibrations happen every second. Each vibration is one complete backward and forward movement of the prongs of the fork, so the prongs complete 500 of those full movements in a single second, which is far too fast to see. 500 vibrations every second one complete vibration too fast to see"],[23,"ch06-r012","Short answer","Two oscilloscope traces are recorded over the same time. Trace A shows six complete waves and trace B shows three. Explain which sound has the higher pitch.","6.1","Trace A has the higher pitch. The horizontal axis of the graph is time, so a wave with a higher frequency will have more waves in the same time. Trace A fits twice as many complete waves into the same time interval, so its frequency is twice as high, and higher frequency means higher pitch. A horizontal axis is time more waves in same time higher frequency higher pitch"],[23,"ch06-r013","Short answer","Two oscilloscope traces show waves of different frequency but the same amplitude. What can you say about how the two sounds compare to a listener?","6.1","The two sounds have different pitches, because their frequencies are different, but the loudness of both these sounds is the same, because their amplitudes are the same. Amplitude and frequency are independent properties, so one can be changed while the other stays fixed. different pitch same loudness same amplitude independent"],[23,"ch06-r014","Short answer","A xylophone has bars of different lengths. Predict which bars make the highest pitch notes and justify your prediction.","6.1","The shortest bars make the highest pitch notes. Usually, vibrating objects that are shorter make higher pitch sounds than those that are longer, because a shorter object vibrates faster, and a faster vibration means a higher frequency and therefore a higher pitch. shortest bars shorter vibrates faster higher frequency higher pitch"],[23,"ch06-r015","Short answer","On a gayageum, the player can move the bridges under the strings. Explain how moving a bridge changes the note that string plays.","6.1","The bridges can be moved to change the length of each string that vibrates. Moving a bridge to make the vibrating length shorter makes the string vibrate faster, which raises the frequency and so raises the pitch. Moving it the other way lengthens the vibrating part and lowers the pitch. change vibrating length shorter string higher pitch longer lower"],[23,"ch06-r016","Short answer","When a ruler is twanged at the edge of a desk you cannot count the vibrations. Explain the problem and how adding a mass to the end solves it.","6.1","When you make a ruler vibrate at the end of a desk, the vibrations are too fast to measure, so no timing can be taken. Taping a mass to the end makes the ruler vibrate more slowly, so the vibrations can be counted and timed, which allows measurements to be made and a trend to be found. too fast to measure mass slows vibration can be counted measurements possible"],[23,"ch06-r017","Short answer","In the ruler investigation you keep the length the same and change the mass. Explain why the length must be kept the same.","6.1","Length also affects how fast a ruler vibrates, so if both the length and the mass were changed at once you would not know which of them caused any change in the frequency. Keeping the length the same makes it a controlled variable, so the only thing that changes is the mass and any change in the result can be blamed on the mass. controlled variable only one variable changed length also affects frequency fair test"],[23,"ch06-r020","Short answer","Water waves are used as an analogy for sound waves. Explain why this is useful and state one way the analogy is not accurate.","6.2","The analogy is useful because water waves can be seen, so the pattern made when two sets of waves meet can be watched directly, and the same behaviour happens with sound. It is not accurate because particles in water waves do not move in the same way as particles in a sound wave, so the analogy explains the behaviour of the waves but not the motion of the particles. can be seen same behaviour particles move differently analogy has limits"],[23,"ch06-r021","Short answer","Define interference, and state the condition that must be met for it to happen.","6.2","Interference is the effect that is produced when waves meet each other. It can only happen when the waves are of the same type, so two sound waves can interfere with each other, but a sound wave cannot interfere with a water wave. effect when waves meet same type of wave sound with sound"],[23,"ch06-r022","Short answer","Two sound waves of very different frequencies meet. Explain why a clear interference effect is unlikely to be heard.","6.2","Interference is easiest to detect when the waves have the same frequency and the same amplitude. If the frequencies are very different, the peaks and troughs of the two waves do not stay lined up with each other, so there is no steady place where they always reinforce or always cancel, and no clear effect is heard. same frequency same amplitude not lined up no steady effect"],[23,"ch06-r023","Short answer","State the condition under which two waves reinforce, and describe what happens to the amplitude and the frequency of the result.","6.2","Waves reinforce when they meet with the peaks together and the troughs together. When this happens, the amplitudes of the two waves that interfere are added together, so the resulting wave has a larger amplitude, but the frequency of the two waves that interfere does not change, so the pitch of the sound stays the same. peaks together troughs together amplitudes added frequency unchanged"],[23,"ch06-r024","Short answer","Explain the full chain of reasoning that links two sound waves reinforcing to what a person actually hears.","6.2","When the two waves meet with peaks together, their amplitudes add, so the amplitude of the sound wave increases. The loudness of a sound wave depends on its amplitude, so when two sound waves reinforce, the sound becomes louder. The person therefore hears the same note, at the same pitch, but louder than either wave alone. amplitudes add amplitude increases loudness depends on amplitude louder same pitch"],[23,"ch06-r025","Short answer","At a concert with a loudspeaker on each side of the stage, some people hear a particular note much louder than others. Explain why.","6.2","The two loudspeakers produce the same sound, so two sets of sound waves travel out and meet in the audience. At certain positions the waves arrive with their peaks together and reinforce, so their amplitudes add and the sound of that pitch is heard louder than usual. At other positions the waves do not meet peak to peak, so no extra loudness is heard there. two sources of same sound waves meet reinforce louder at certain positions"],[23,"ch06-r026","Short answer","In a diagram of two loudspeakers, curved lines represent peaks of the sound waves. Explain what a person standing where two lines cross would hear.","6.2","The curved lines represent peaks of amplitude, so where two of these lines cross, a peak from one loudspeaker arrives at the same moment as a peak from the other. The waves therefore reinforce, their amplitudes add, and a person at that position will hear a louder sound. two peaks arrive together reinforce amplitudes add louder"],[23,"ch06-r027","Short answer","Sound waves also reinforce where two troughs meet, but this is hard to show on the loudspeaker diagram. Explain why two troughs meeting still gives a louder sound.","6.2","A trough is the maximum movement of the particles in the other direction, so two troughs arriving together add in exactly the same way as two peaks: the particle is pushed to a larger maximum distance, the amplitude is increased, and the sound is louder. The diagram only draws the peaks, so the trough positions are simply not marked. troughs add too larger amplitude louder diagram only shows peaks"],[23,"ch06-r028","Short answer","State the condition under which two waves cancel, and explain why the result is no sound.","6.2","Waves cancel when they meet with the peaks of one together with the troughs of the other. A peak is the wave's maximum positive amplitude and a trough is its maximum negative amplitude, so adding them gives zero, in the same way that 2 + (-2) = 0. The amplitude of the resulting sound wave becomes zero, and with no amplitude there is no sound. peak meets trough positive and negative add to zero no sound"],[23,"ch06-r029","Short answer","Two sound waves of the same frequency but different amplitudes meet peak to trough. Explain why the sound is not completely silenced.","6.2","For two sound waves to cancel completely, their frequencies must be the same and their amplitudes must be the same. Here the amplitudes are different, so when the positive and negative amplitudes are added they do not give exactly zero. A smaller wave is left over, so a quieter sound is still heard rather than silence. amplitudes must match do not add to zero smaller wave left quieter not silent"],[23,"ch06-r030","Short answer","Explain why people at a concert almost never find a position where they hear nothing at all.","6.2","Complete cancelling needs the two waves to arrive exactly peak to trough with equal amplitudes. In a real hall the sound waves are also reflected off objects, including off people in the audience, so extra waves arrive at every position from many directions. These extra waves do not all cancel, so it is not likely that the sound waves will ever completely cancel and some sound is always heard. reflection off objects and people extra waves arrive never complete cancelling"],[23,"ch06-r031","Short answer","Explain how noise cancelling headphones use interference to make a quiet sound.","6.2","A small microphone picks up the sound wave from the surroundings. The headphones then create a sound wave that will cancel with the sound wave from the surroundings, that is, one with the same frequency and amplitude but arranged so its troughs meet the peaks of the unwanted sound. The two waves add to nearly zero amplitude inside the headphone, so the wearer hears very little of the outside noise. microphone detects noise headphones create opposite wave peaks meet troughs amplitude near zero"],[23,"ch06-r032","Short answer","Zara and Sofia play the same note on two pianos in the same room at the same time. Explain why the note sounds louder than when either plays alone.","6.2","The two pianos are two sources of the same sound, so two sound waves of the same frequency travel out and meet. Where they arrive with peaks together they reinforce, and the amplitudes of the two waves are added. Since loudness depends on amplitude, the note is heard louder than when only one piano plays. two sources same frequency reinforce amplitudes added louder"],[23,"ch06-r033","Short answer","Waves A and B have the same pitch, meet and reinforce, and the resulting wave C has double the amplitude of one of them. Deduce how the amplitudes and frequencies of A and B compare, and state the frequency of C.","6.2","Since the amplitudes of A and B add to give double the amplitude of one of them, A and B must have equal amplitudes. They have the same pitch, so their frequencies are equal as well. Reinforcement does not change frequency, so the frequency of wave C is the same as the frequency of wave A. equal amplitudes equal frequencies frequency unchanged C same as A"],[23,"ch06-r034","Short answer","An early theory said the Moon split away from the Earth. State the prediction this theory made and describe the measurement that was later made.","6.3","The calculations showed that, if this theory was correct, the Moon would still be slowly moving away from Earth. Accurate measurements made more recently have shown that the Moon is indeed still moving away from Earth, at a rate of about 4 cm each year, which is the result the theory predicted. prediction moving away 4 cm each year measurement agrees"],[23,"ch06-r035","Short answer","The measured movement of the Moon matched the splitting theory's prediction. Explain why scientists still did not accept that theory as confirmed.","6.3","These calculations could not completely confirm the theory that the Moon had formed by splitting away from the Earth. A measurement agreeing with a prediction shows that the theory is possible, but other theories can predict the same movement, so agreement alone does not prove that this particular explanation is the correct one. agreement is not proof other theories predict the same not confirmed"],[23,"ch06-r036","Short answer","State what the collision theory is and give its other common name.","6.3","The collision theory, more commonly called the giant impact hypothesis, is a theory of how the Moon was formed. It was suggested in 1974 and says that a newly formed planet about the same size as Mars collided with the Earth relatively soon after the formation of the Solar System, and that the Moon formed from the material thrown out. giant impact hypothesis 1974 Mars sized planet collided with Earth"],[23,"ch06-r037","Short answer","Explain why scientists currently prefer the collision theory over other theories of the Moon's formation.","6.3","There is more evidence that supports the collision theory than any other current theory. Scientists choose between competing explanations by comparing how much evidence each one accounts for, so the theory backed by the most evidence is accepted for now. This does not make it certain, and it could be replaced if better evidence appeared. most evidence compare theories accepted for now could change"],[23,"ch06-r038","Short answer","The colliding planet in the collision theory has been given a name. State the name and explain why a name was needed for an object nobody has ever seen.","6.3","Scientists have called the colliding planet Theia. The object no longer exists as a separate body, because the theory states that Earth was formed from the two planets joined together, so it can never be observed. A name is still needed so that scientists can discuss the object, its size and its composition clearly when testing the theory. Theia no longer exists joined with Earth name allows discussion"],[23,"ch06-r039","Short answer","Describe how the Moon formed according to the collision theory, and name the force responsible.","6.3","The collision would have caused rocks and dust to break away from both planets, forming a ring around the Earth. The Moon was formed when the rocks and dust were pulled together by gravity. Gravity is the force that pulls the scattered material into one body, and the Earth itself was formed from the two planets joined together. rocks and dust break away ring pulled together by gravity Earth from both planets"],[23,"ch06-r040","Short answer","The composition of rocks on the Moon is very similar to that on Earth. Explain why this single fact does not decide between the collision theory and the fission theory.","6.3","The fission theory suggests that the Earth and Moon were once the same object which then split into two parts, so it predicts similar rock composition. The collision theory says Earth and the Moon both formed from mixed material of Earth and Theia, so it predicts similar composition too. Because both theories predict the same observation, this evidence cannot be used to choose between them. both theories predict similarity fission split one object collision mixed material cannot choose"],[23,"ch06-r041","Short answer","The capture theory says the Moon formed separately far from Earth and was later captured by Earth's gravity. State one observation that supports the collision theory but contradicts the capture theory, and explain why.","6.3","The composition of the Moon's rocks being very similar to that of Earth's rocks supports the collision theory but contradicts the capture theory. The collision theory says the Moon formed from material from Earth and Theia, so a close match is expected. The capture theory says the Moon formed separately and far away from Earth, from different material, so it would predict a clearly different composition. similar rock composition collision predicts match capture predicts difference formed far away"],[23,"ch06-r042","Short answer","Mars has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos. Predict how their rock composition would compare with the rocks of Mars if they were formed by a collision with Mars, and if they were captured asteroids.","6.3","If they were formed by a collision of another object with Mars, they would be made from material thrown off Mars mixed with material from the colliding object, so their rocks would be similar in composition to the rocks of Mars. If they were captured asteroids that formed elsewhere, their composition would be different from the rocks of Mars because they formed from different material in a different place. collision gives similar rocks capture gives different rocks formed elsewhere test by composition"],[23,"ch06-r043","Short answer","State what nebulae are and name the gases they mostly contain.","6.4","Nebulae are clouds of dust and gas in space. The gases found in nebulae are mostly hydrogen, with a smaller quantity of helium. The word nebulae is plural, so a single one of these clouds is called a nebula. clouds of dust and gas hydrogen helium nebulae is plural"],[23,"ch06-r044","Short answer","A nebula the same size as the Earth would have a mass of only a few kilograms. Explain what this tells you about the material in a nebula.","6.4","It tells you that the particles of gas and dust are very far apart in nebulae. A huge volume contains only a tiny amount of matter, so the density is extremely low, far lower than the air around us. A nebula looks solid in photographs only because it is enormous and the light from stars is scattered through it. particles very far apart extremely low density huge volume tiny mass looks solid but is not"],[23,"ch06-r045","Short answer","Many nebulae are more than 10 000 times bigger than the Solar System. Explain why nebulae can still be difficult to see from Earth even though they are so large.","6.4","Although they are enormous, the particles of gas and dust in nebulae are very far apart, so the material is extremely thinly spread and gives out or reflects only a small amount of light from each part of it. Nebulae are also very far away, so the light that does reach Earth is spread out and faint, and many can only be seen clearly through a powerful telescope. very low density little light very far away faint"],[23,"ch06-r046","Short answer","Explain how the death of a giant star can produce a nebula.","6.4","Some nebulae form when giant stars reach the end of their life. These giant stars then explode, sending dust and gas over a wide area of space. The material that made up the star is scattered outwards and spreads thinly, and that spread-out cloud of dust and gas is a nebula. giant star ends its life explodes dust and gas spread wide area"],[23,"ch06-r047","Short answer","The Orion nebula can be seen without a telescope from the northern hemisphere, but the Tarantula nebula cannot. Explain why an observer's position on Earth matters.","6.4","The northern hemisphere is the part of the Earth that is north of the equator and the southern hemisphere is the part south of it. The solid Earth blocks the view of part of the sky, so an observer in the northern hemisphere can see the Orion nebula easily, while the Tarantula nebula is the easiest one to see for someone in the southern hemisphere. northern hemisphere southern hemisphere Earth blocks part of sky different objects visible"],[23,"ch06-r048","Short answer","Explain what is meant by a stellar nursery and why that name was chosen.","6.4","A stellar nursery is an area in space where stars are formed. The word stellar can be used to describe anything about stars, and a nursery is a place to care for young people, animals or plants, so in this case the word nursery applies to young stars. The name is a comparison, not a scientific description of what happens. area where stars form stellar means about stars nursery for young name is a comparison"],[23,"ch06-r049","Short answer","A student writes 'all nebulae are stellar nurseries'. Correct this statement and explain the correction.","6.4","The statement is wrong. Only some types of nebulae act as stellar nurseries. All nebulae are clouds of dust and gas, but star formation needs the dust and gas to be able to collapse together under gravity, which does not happen in every nebula, so being a nebula does not guarantee that new stars are forming there. only some nebulae all are clouds of dust and gas star formation needs collapse not guaranteed"],[23,"ch06-r050","Short answer","Explain why the force of gravity in a stellar nursery gets stronger as a star begins to form.","6.4","In a stellar nursery, the dust and gas start to collapse together under the force of gravity. When the mass of dust and gas collects together and becomes larger, the force of gravity pulling inward increases. So the more material that gathers, the stronger the pull, which gathers more material still, and the process speeds itself up. mass collects together larger mass gravity increases process speeds up"],[23,"ch06-r051","Short answer","Describe the steps by which a collapsing cloud of gas and dust starts to give out heat and light.","6.4","The dust and gas collapse together under gravity, and as the mass collects the inward force of gravity increases. When this force gets very large, the pressure inside the new star also gets very large, and the high pressure causes heat. The heat can cause atoms to react with each other, and these reactions cause the new star to give out heat and light. gravity collapse pressure increases pressure causes heat atoms react gives out heat and light"],[23,"ch06-r052","Short answer","A photograph of a stellar nursery shows young stars that are dimmer than expected. Give two reasons why newly formed stars are hard to see.","6.4","First, some of these stars are only half the mass of the Sun and have not yet reached their full brightness, so they are giving out less light than they eventually will. Second, they are still inside the cloud, and the light from the stars has to pass through the surrounding dust and gas, which blocks and scatters some of it before it can reach us. not yet full brightness lower mass surrounded by dust and gas light blocked and scattered"],[23,"ch06-r053","Short answer","The diameter of the Solar System is 0.001 light years and the diameter of the Orion nebula is 24 light years. Calculate how many times bigger the nebula is, and comment on the result.","6.4","Dividing 24 by 0.001 gives 24 000, so the Orion nebula is 24 000 times bigger in diameter than the Solar System. This shows that nebulae are on a completely different scale from planetary systems, and it is consistent with the statement that many nebulae are more than 10 000 times bigger than the Solar System. 24 divided by 0.001 24 000 times much larger scale consistent with text"],[23,"ch06-r054","Short answer","Describe the outer layers of the Earth and explain why their difference matters for tectonic plates.","6.5","The outer layers of the Earth are the solid, rocky crust which rests on the more fluid mantle. The difference matters because a fluid can flow and carry convection currents, while the solid crust cannot. The crust is broken into tectonic plates that sit on top of the moving fluid mantle and are carried along by it. solid rocky crust more fluid mantle fluid can flow crust carried along"],[23,"ch06-r055","Short answer","The inner core is estimated to be at a temperature of over 5000 degrees Celsius. Explain the role this plays in moving the continents.","6.5","The mantle is heated from the innermost part of the Earth, the inner core, and the inner part of the mantle gets thermal energy from the core. This heating is what drives convection currents in the mantle, and those currents drag the tectonic plates of the crust along with them, so the heat of the core is ultimately the energy source for continental movement. core heats mantle thermal energy drives convection plates dragged along"],[23,"ch06-r056","Short answer","Explain, step by step, how a convection current forms in the mantle.","6.5","The inner part of the mantle gets thermal energy from the core. The fluid in the mantle then expands when heated and becomes less dense than the fluid surrounding it. This hotter, less dense fluid in the mantle rises towards the crust, then cools near the crust and sinks again, and this circular movement is a convection current. heated by core expands less dense rises cools and sinks"],[23,"ch06-r057","Short answer","Convection currents in a beaker of water can be seen in seconds, but mantle convection takes millions of years. Explain the difference.","6.5","The mantle is a very thick fluid and does not flow easily like water, so the convection currents move very slowly. The same physical process is happening in both cases, but the resistance to flow in the mantle is enormously greater, so the circulation takes an extremely long time to complete. mantle is very thick fluid does not flow easily same process very slow"],[23,"ch06-r058","Short answer","Tectonic plates move at between 0.6 and 10 cm per year. Explain why this movement was not noticed for most of human history.","6.5","At a few centimetres each year the movement is far too small to see or feel over a human lifetime, and there was no way to measure positions on Earth accurately enough to detect it. Just as the convection currents are slow, the movement of the tectonic plates is also slow, so its effects only become obvious over millions of years. a few cm per year too small to notice no accurate measurement obvious only over millions of years"],[23,"ch06-r059","Short answer","Explain how the shapes of the present-day continents provide evidence for tectonic plates.","6.5","When you look at a map of the world it looks like the continents could fit together like pieces of a giant jigsaw puzzle, and scientists have shown that the continental coasts can fit together. Scientists say that this continental jigsaw appearance is evidence for tectonic plates, because the simplest explanation for the matching shapes is that the land was once joined and has since been carried apart. coasts fit together jigsaw appearance once joined carried apart"],[23,"ch06-r060","Short answer","State the hypothesis about a single ancient continent and explain what caused it to break apart.","6.5","There is a hypothesis that there was once only one large continent that eventually separated. The separate parts became some of the tectonic plates, and the convection currents from the mantle drove their movement, carrying the pieces away from each other over millions of years to their present positions. one large continent separated became tectonic plates convection currents drove movement"],[23,"ch06-r061","Short answer","Explain what the fossil record is and why it can tell us about a time before there were any people.","6.5","The fossil record is the name given to the collection of thousands of fossils that provide us with information about the time before humans were on Earth. Fossils are the remains of dead animals and plants that have turned to stone over millions of years, so they preserve physical evidence of living things from long before anyone could write anything down. collection of fossils before humans remains turned to stone millions of years"],[23,"ch06-r062","Short answer","Fossils of Mesosaurus, a crocodile-like animal that lived in shallow coastal water, have been found in both South America and Africa. Explain how this supports tectonic plate movement.","6.5","Mesosaurus lived at the coast and in shallow water, so it is not likely that they would have crossed the 5000 km wide Atlantic Ocean that now separates these continents. The best remaining explanation is that South America and Africa were joined when Mesosaurus was alive, and that the plates carrying them have since moved apart, splitting the population in two. shallow water animal could not cross 5000 km continents once joined moved apart"],[23,"ch06-r063","Short answer","Fossils of the plant Glossopteris have been found in Antarctica, India, Australia, Africa and South America. Explain why finding the same plant on five separate continents is strong evidence.","6.5","A plant cannot travel across thousands of kilometres of open ocean, and it is very unlikely that the same species would appear independently on five different continents. This gives more evidence to the hypothesis that these continents were once joined, so that a single population of Glossopteris was spread across one landmass which later broke apart. plants cannot cross oceans five continents once joined single landmass split"],[23,"ch06-r064","Short answer","Scientists know that the Earth's magnetic poles have swapped positions almost 200 times in the last 100 million years. Explain how they can know this about the distant past.","6.5","Magnetic crystals in molten rocks line up to point north in the same way as a compass needle. When the rock solidifies, the crystals are locked in place, so scientists can use these crystals to tell the direction of the Earth's magnetic field at the time when the rock became solid. By examining rocks of different ages they can read off when the field pointed the other way. magnetic crystals align locked when rock solidifies record field direction rocks of different ages"],[23,"ch06-r065","Short answer","Explain what is meant by the alignment of magnetic crystals in rocks.","6.5","The direction that the crystals are pointing is called alignment, because the word alignment means to line up in a particular way. While the rock is molten the crystals are free to turn and line up with the Earth's magnetic field, and once the rock solidifies that direction is fixed permanently. direction crystals point line up free while molten fixed when solid"],[23,"ch06-r066","Short answer","Explain what happens at a mid-oceanic ridge and what effect it has on the continents.","6.5","At mid-oceanic ridges, magma is coming up from the mantle and solidifying to form new rocks on the ocean floor. This action pushes the continents away from each other, because new sea floor is constantly being added in the middle, forcing the older rock and the land attached to it outwards on both sides. magma rises solidifies into new rock new sea floor added pushes continents apart"],[23,"ch06-r067","Short answer","At a mid-oceanic ridge the magnetic crystals all point north, but rocks further from the ridge show the opposite alignment. Explain what this tells you.","6.5","The magnetic crystals in the mid-oceanic ridges always have an alignment to north because the Earth's magnetic field is currently in that direction, so those rocks formed recently. Rocks further away from these ridges contain magnetic crystals with the opposite alignment, which suggests that these rocks are much older and solidified at a time when the Earth's field pointed the other way. The pattern therefore shows that new rock forms at the ridge and older rock has been pushed outwards. ridge rocks point north formed recently distant rocks reversed older pushed outwards"],[23,"ch06-r068","Short answer","State the prediction the tectonic plate hypothesis makes about earthquakes and volcanoes, and say whether the evidence supports it.","6.5","If the hypothesis of moving tectonic plates was correct, then we would expect there to be more earthquakes and volcanoes at the tectonic plate boundaries, because that is where the plates push against or pull away from each other. This is indeed the case: most of the volcanoes and earthquakes that have been recorded occur close to the tectonic plate boundaries, so the evidence supports the hypothesis. prediction more at boundaries plates push or pull apart map supports it"],[23,"ch06-r069","Short answer","A student writes 'earthquakes and volcanoes always occur at tectonic plate boundaries'. Use the world map evidence to discuss whether this statement is correct.","6.5","The statement is too strong. The map shows that most of the volcanoes and earthquakes that have been recorded occur close to the tectonic plate boundaries, which supports the idea of plate movement. However, some are marked away from the boundaries, so the correct statement is that they mostly occur at boundaries, not that they always do. too strong most occur at boundaries some away from boundaries mostly not always"],[23,"ch06-o028","Short answer","Two loudspeakers give out the same sound, and the waves spreading out from them are drawn as sets of curved lines. What do the curved lines represent, and what happens where two of them cross?","6.2","The curved lines represent peaks of amplitude in the sound waves. Where two lines cross, the waves reinforce and a listener there hears a louder sound. peaks cross reinforce louder"],[23,"ch06-o029","Short answer","Explain why it is very unlikely that anyone in a concert audience hears complete silence.","6.2","Sound waves reflect off objects and off people in the audience, adding many more waves to the pattern, so it is very unlikely that only two identical waves meet to cancel. reflect objects people many more waves"],[23,"ch06-o033","Short answer","Explain how noise cancelling headphones work.","6.2","A small microphone picks up the sound from the surroundings. The headphones analyse it, then create a sound wave of the same amplitude and frequency but out of phase with it, so the two waves cancel. microphone same amplitude frequency out of phase cancel"],[23,"ch06-o035","Short answer","What did scientists in the early twentieth century think about how the Moon formed?","6.3","That the Moon was formed by splitting away from the Earth, soon after the Earth was formed. splitting away Earth soon after"],[23,"ch06-o040","Short answer","According to the collision theory, how were the Earth and the Moon formed after the impact?","6.3","The Earth was formed from the two planets joined together, and the Moon was formed when the rocks and dust broken off by the collision were pulled together by gravity. two planets joined rocks and dust pulled together gravity"],[23,"ch06-o042","Short answer","Give two further pieces of evidence supporting the collision theory, one about the Moon's core and one from outside the Solar System.","6.3","The Moon has a small iron core, similar to the Earth's, and there is evidence outside the Solar System of similar collisions causing rings of rock and dust. small iron core outside the Solar System rings of rock and dust"],[23,"ch06-o044","Short answer","Explain why Venus having no moon is treated as evidence against the collision theory.","6.3","Collisions in the early years of the Solar System would have been common, so scientists would have expected Venus to have gained a moon in the same way. Venus no moon common expected"],[23,"ch06-o045","Short answer","Explain why the composition of the Moon's rocks is treated as evidence against the collision theory.","6.3","If the Moon formed mainly from Theia, its rocks would be expected to resemble Theia's rather than Earth's. In fact the composition of the Moon is more similar to Earth. Theia expected more similar to Earth"],[23,"ch06-o046","Short answer","Phobos and Deimos were captured asteroids. Suggest how their rock composition compares with the rock of Mars, and how it would differ if they had formed by a collision with Mars.","6.3","As captured asteroids their composition would be different from the rock of Mars. If they had formed in a collision with Mars, their composition would be similar to Mars. different captured similar collision"],[23,"ch06-o047","Short answer","Collisions between planet-sized objects were common soon after the Solar System formed. Suggest why they are no longer common.","6.3","Most of the loose planet-sized objects have already collided and joined together into the planets, so far fewer are left to collide, and those that remain are spread out in stable orbits. already collided joined together fewer left stable orbits"],[23,"ch06-o048","Short answer","When is the word moon written with a capital M, and when with a small m?","6.3","With a capital, Moon means the large natural object that orbits the Earth. With a small letter, moon means any natural object orbiting another planet. capital orbits the Earth small letter any natural object"],[23,"ch06-o049","Short answer","What is a nebula?","6.4","A cloud of dust and gas in space. cloud dust gas space"],[23,"ch06-o050","Short answer","Name the two gases that nebulae are mostly made of, and say which is more plentiful.","6.4","Mostly hydrogen, with a smaller quantity of helium. hydrogen helium smaller quantity"],[23,"ch06-o051","Short answer","A nebula the same size as the Earth would have a mass of only a few kilograms. Explain what this tells you about a nebula.","6.4","That the particles of gas and dust in a nebula are very far apart, so its density is extremely low. particles very far apart low density"],[23,"ch06-o052","Short answer","How large are most nebulae compared with the Solar System?","6.4","Most nebulae are very large - many are more than 10 000 times bigger than the Solar System. 10 000 times bigger Solar System"],[23,"ch06-o057","Short answer","Outline the process by which a new star forms in a stellar nursery.","6.4","Dust and gas start to collapse together under gravity. As more mass collects, the inward force of gravity increases, so the pressure inside increases. The high pressure causes heat, and the heat makes atoms react, so the new star gives out heat and light. collapse gravity mass pressure heat react light"],[23,"ch06-o059","Short answer","Young stars in a stellar nursery are harder to see than older stars the same distance away. Suggest two reasons why.","6.4","Some young stars are only about half the mass of the Sun and have not yet reached their full brightness, and the dust and gas of the cloud around them blocks some of their light. half the mass not yet full brightness dust and gas blocks"],[23,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the maximum distance that particles move from their position in a sound wave called?","6.1","amplitude frequency pitch loudness"],[23,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The number of vibrations that happen in each second is called ____.","6.1","frequency"],[23,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","If the amplitude of a sound wave increases, what happens to the loudness?","6.1","The loudness decreases The loudness stays the same The loudness increases The pitch gets higher"],[23,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","The highest point on a graph of a sound wave is called the ____.","6.1","peak"],[23,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What unit is used to measure frequency?","6.1","hertz (Hz) metres (m) seconds (s) decibels (dB)"],[23,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","A piece of equipment that displays graphs of sound waves is called an ____.","6.1","oscilloscope"],[23,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","The effect produced when two sound waves meet each other is called ____.","6.2","interference"],[23,"qwen-01-11","Multiple choice","Which condition makes it easiest to detect interference between sound waves?","6.2","Different frequencies and amplitudes Same frequency and amplitude One wave is much louder The waves are different types"],[23,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","When two sound waves meet and make a louder sound, they are said to ____ each other.","6.2","reinforce"],[23,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What happens to the amplitude of a sound wave when two waves reinforce?","6.2","It becomes zero It increases It stays the same It decreases"],[23,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","What is the result when two sound waves cancel completely?","6.2","A louder sound No sound A higher pitch A lower pitch"],[23,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What is the name of the planet that collided with Earth in the collision theory?","6.3","Venus Mars Theia Jupiter"],[23,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","The collision theory for the formation of the Moon is more commonly called the ____ impact hypothesis.","6.3","giant"],[23,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","According to the collision theory, what formed the Moon?","6.3","A piece of Earth that split off Rocks and dust pulled together by gravity Gas from the Sun Ice from space"],[23,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","Accurate measurements have shown that the Moon is still moving away from Earth at a rate of about ____ cm each year.","6.3","4"],[23,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","Which piece of evidence supports the collision theory?","6.3","The Moon is denser than Earth The surface of Earth was never molten Venus has no moon The composition of rocks on the Earth and Moon are the same"],[23,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What are the two main gases found in nebulae?","6.4","Hydrogen and helium Oxygen and nitrogen Carbon dioxide and hydrogen Helium and oxygen"],[23,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","A stellar nursery is an area in space where ____ are formed.","6.4","stars"],[23,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","What force causes dust and gas in a stellar nursery to collapse together?","6.4","Gravity Magnetism Friction Pressure"],[23,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","The part of the Earth that is north of the equator is called the ____ hemisphere.","6.4","northern"],[23,"qwen-03-05","Multiple choice","Which nebula is easiest to see from the southern hemisphere?","6.4","Tarantula nebula Orion nebula Andromeda nebula Vela nebula"],[23,"qwen-03-06","Gap fill","The outer layers of the Earth are made up of solid, rocky ____.","6.5","crust"],[23,"qwen-03-07","Multiple choice","What causes convection currents in the Earth's mantle?","6.5","Thermal energy from the core The rotation of the Earth Magnetic fields Solar radiation"],[23,"qwen-03-08","Gap fill","Fossils are the remains of dead animals and plants that have turned to ____ over millions of years.","6.5","stone"],[23,"qwen-03-09","Multiple choice","Why is the Mesosaurus fossil evidence for tectonic plates?","6.5","It was found in Africa and South America It lived on land only It is a plant fossil It was found in Antarctica"],[23,"qwen-03-10","Gap fill","The direction that magnetic crystals in rocks point is called ____.","6.5","alignment"],[23,"qwen-03-11","Multiple choice","Where do most earthquakes and volcanoes occur?","6.5","Close to tectonic plate boundaries In the middle of continents Only in oceans At the poles"],[23,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What happens to the loudness of a sound when it is reinforced?","6.2","It becomes louder It becomes quieter The pitch gets higher The pitch gets lower"],[23,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","When two identical sound waves meet and reinforce, the resulting wave has double the ____.","6.2","amplitude"],[23,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","What must be true for two sound waves to cancel each other completely?","6.2","They have the same amplitude and frequency They have different amplitudes but the same frequency They have the same amplitude but different frequencies They have double the amplitude and half the frequency"],[23,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","The unit used to measure the frequency of a sound wave is the ____.","6.1","hertz"],[23,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","When a sound wave travels through air, it causes the particles in the air to ____.","6.1","vibrate"],[23,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","The number of complete vibrations a sound wave makes in one second is called its ____.","6.1","frequency"],[23,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","When two sound waves meet and cancel each other, the result is that the sound becomes ____.","6.2","quieter"],[23,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","Which statement is true about all nebulae?","6.4","They contain dust and gas They are all bigger than the Milky Way They all act as stellar nurseries They are all the same shape"],[23,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","The Orion Nebula is an example of a ____ nebula.","6.4","emission"],[23,"qwenw-02-07","Multiple choice","Why do dark nebulae appear almost black?","6.4","They block the light from stars behind them They emit very little energy They reflect light from nearby stars They are made of hot gas"],[23,"qwenw-02-08","Gap fill","The Crab Nebula is an example of a ____ remnant.","6.4","supernova"],[23,"qwenw-02-10","Gap fill","Convection currents inside the Earth cause the movement of ____.","6.5","tectonic plates"],[23,"qwenw-02-11","Multiple choice","What evidence supports the idea that continents have moved?","6.5","Similar fossils found on different continents The number of moons each planet has The temperature of the Sun The shape of nebulae"],[23,"qwenw-02-12","Gap fill","Alfred Wegener was the first person to suggest that Earth's continents were slowly ____.","6.5","moving"],[24,"ch07-f01a","Diagram labelling","This plant cell has been stained and is just beginning to divide. Name the part labelled {n}.","7.1",""],[24,"ch07-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label that shows where the cell wall of this cell runs.","7.1",""],[24,"ch07-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label pointing to one of the dark red chromosomes.","7.1",""],[24,"ch07-f02a","Diagram labelling","This is a diagram of a human sperm cell, the male gamete. Name the part labelled {n}.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the part that makes this cell able to swim.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the part that carries the 23 chromosomes of the sperm cell.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the layer that surrounds the whole sperm cell.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f03a","Diagram labelling","This is a diagram of a human egg cell, the female gamete. Name the part labelled {n}.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the layer that surrounds the whole egg cell.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the part of the egg cell that holds its food reserves.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the part of the egg cell that carries its 23 chromosomes.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f04a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows fertilisation in humans. Describe what is happening at the stage labelled {n}.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the first step of fertilisation.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label for the step that gives the new cell 46 chromosomes.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f05a","Diagram labelling","This diagram shows how the sex of a baby is determined. Write down the statement given at label {n}.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the statement about the sex chromosome carried by every egg cell.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the statement that explains why a father can have either a son or a daughter.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the statement that describes the fertilisation that produces a girl.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f05h4","Click the diagram","Click on the statement that describes the fertilisation that produces a boy.","7.2",""],[24,"ch07-f06a","Diagram labelling","This is Zara's bar chart of the flower colours she counted. Write down the axis title or the category shown at label {n}.","7.3",""],[24,"ch07-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the title of the vertical axis of this bar chart.","7.3",""],[24,"ch07-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the colour with the tallest bar.","7.3",""],[24,"ch07-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the colour Zara found the fewest of.","7.3",""],[24,"ch07-f06h4","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the colour of which Zara found 5 plants.","7.3",""],[24,"ch07-f07a","Diagram labelling","This diagram explains how a population of bacteria becomes resistant to an antibiotic. Describe the stage labelled {n}.","7.4",""],[24,"ch07-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the caption for the stage that describes variation already present in the population.","7.4",""],[24,"ch07-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the caption for the stage in which the antibiotic decides which bacteria survive.","7.4",""],[24,"ch07-f07h3","Click the diagram","Click on the caption for the stage in which the gene for resistance becomes common.","7.4",""],[24,"ch07-r001","Short answer","Explain why you did not see any chromosomes when you looked at cells under a microscope.","7.1","Chromosomes only become visible with a light microscope when a cell is dividing, and most of the cells on a slide are not dividing at the moment you look at them. Chromosomes also need to be coloured using special stains in order to be able to see them, so even a dividing cell stays invisible in an unstained preparation. Both conditions have to be met at once, which is why chromosomes are rarely seen by accident. chromosomes are only visible while a cell is dividing most cells on a slide are not dividing they also need special stains both conditions must be met at once"],[24,"ch07-r002","Short answer","Explain why chromosomes were not discovered until the nineteenth century.","7.1","Chromosomes were discovered in the nineteenth century because that was when scientists first had sufficiently powerful microscopes and were first able to see very small things, such as the structure of cells. Before that, no one could see inside a cell well enough for chromosomes to be noticed at all. A discovery like this depends on the instruments available, not only on someone thinking to look. powerful enough microscopes only existed from the nineteenth century before that the structure of cells could not be seen the discovery depended on the instrument not only on someone deciding to look"],[24,"ch07-r003","Short answer","Explain why fruit fly chromosomes are often used to teach students about chromosome structure.","7.1","Fruit flies have especially thick chromosomes, called giant chromosomes, which are much easier to see than most chromosomes, so a student can actually make out the long, striped thread rather than a blur. Fruit fly cells also have only eight chromosomes, so there are fewer of them to separate in the field of view. The organism is chosen to make the structure visible, not because its chromosomes are different in kind. fruit flies have giant chromosomes that are thicker much easier to see than most chromosomes only eight chromosomes, so less crowded chosen for visibility, not because they differ in kind"],[24,"ch07-r004","Short answer","Explain how scientists decide which chromosome is called chromosome 1.","7.1","Scientists number the chromosomes in a cell according to how long the chromosomes are, so in a human cell the longest chromosome is chromosome 1, the next longest is chromosome 2, and so on. The numbering is therefore a convention based on a measurable feature, not a statement about which chromosome is most important. It gives everyone the same name for the same chromosome, which is what makes it possible to say that two genes for eye colour are on chromosome 15. numbered by length longest is chromosome 1, next longest 2 a convention based on a measurable feature gives everyone the same name for the same chromosome"],[24,"ch07-r005","Short answer","Explain why each chromosome in a photograph of a dividing cell looks like a cross shape.","7.1","Each chromosome looks like a cross shape because, just before a cell divides, each chromosome makes a copy of itself, and the two copies stay joined together part way along their length. So the cross is not one chromosome bent over but two identical copies still attached at one point. The copying happens first so that each of the two new cells can receive a complete set. each chromosome copies itself before division the two copies stay joined part way along the cross is two copies, not one bent thread copying first lets each new cell get a full set"],[24,"ch07-r006","Short answer","Explain why the picture of lined-up human chromosomes is not a photograph of what a cell really looks like.","7.1","The picture has been made by cutting and pasting pictures of the individual chromosomes, which have then been lined up in size order, because they do not really line themselves up like this. The arrangement is imposed afterwards to make the chromosomes easy to count and to pair up. It is a way of presenting real data clearly, so the chromosomes are genuine even though the layout is not. the individual chromosomes were cut out and pasted in order they do not line themselves up like this in a cell the layout makes them easy to count and pair the chromosomes are real even though the arrangement is not"],[24,"ch07-r007","Short answer","Red blood cells do not contain a nucleus. Explain whether they contain chromosomes.","7.1","They do not, because the nucleus of every cell contains threads called chromosomes, so a cell with no nucleus has no place for them. This also means a red blood cell has no genes and no DNA to control what it does, which is part of why it cannot divide or repair itself. It can still carry oxygen because the proteins it needs were made before the nucleus was lost. chromosomes are found in the nucleus no nucleus means no chromosomes so no genes or DNA to control the cell it cannot divide or repair itself"],[24,"ch07-r008","Short answer","Explain the difference between a chromosome and a gene.","7.1","Each chromosome is made up of hundreds of different genes, arranged in a particular sequence along the chromosome, so a gene is a part of a chromosome rather than a different kind of object. Each gene helps to control a particular characteristic in the organism, while the chromosome is the whole thread that carries them all. Both are made of DNA, since each chromosome is one enormously long molecule of DNA. a chromosome is made of hundreds of genes in sequence a gene is part of a chromosome each gene helps control one characteristic both are made of DNA"],[24,"ch07-r009","Short answer","Chromosome 1 has about 2000 genes and chromosome 15 about 600. Explain why.","7.1","Chromosome 15 is a much shorter chromosome than chromosome 1, and the genes are arranged in a particular sequence along the chromosome, so a shorter thread has room for fewer of them. Since chromosomes are numbered by length, the numbering and the gene count fall roughly in the same order. The difference is one of length, not of importance. chromosome 15 is much shorter than chromosome 1 genes lie in sequence along the chromosome a shorter chromosome holds fewer genes a difference of length, not importance"],[24,"ch07-r010","Short answer","Two sisters have different eye colours even though everyone has the eye-colour genes in the same place. Explain how.","7.1","Everyone has genes for eye colour in the same place on their chromosome 15s, so the position is not what differs between the sisters. What differs is the version of the gene each sister inherited, since there are several versions of a gene, each made of slightly different DNA. Each sister received a different combination from her parents, so the same gene in the same place gives a different eye colour. the position of the gene is the same in everyone the versions of the gene differ versions are made of slightly different DNA each sister inherited a different combination"],[24,"ch07-r011","Short answer","Explain the relationship between chromosomes, genes and DNA.","7.1","Chromosomes are made of a chemical substance called DNA, and each chromosome is one enormously long molecule of DNA. Genes are lengths of that molecule, since each chromosome is made up of hundreds of different genes, which means that genes are also made of DNA. So the three words describe the same material at three different scales: the whole thread, a working section of it, and the chemical it is built from. each chromosome is one long DNA molecule genes are lengths of that molecule so genes are also made of DNA three scales of the same material"],[24,"ch07-r012","Short answer","Describe the shape of a DNA molecule and explain why the twists cannot be seen under a microscope.","7.1","A DNA molecule has a shape like a twisted ladder, and this shape is called a double helix. We cannot see these twists when we use microscopes to look at chromosomes because DNA molecules are much too small to see, even though a single gene could be a length of DNA with about 2500 of these twists. The chromosome is visible because it is an enormously long DNA molecule packed up, not because the helix itself is large. a twisted ladder shape called a double helix DNA molecules are far too small to see one gene may have about 2500 twists the chromosome is visible as packed-up DNA, not as a helix"],[24,"ch07-r013","Short answer","Explain what is meant by saying that the DNA in a cell determines what the cell does.","7.1","The DNA in a cell contains a complete set of instructions to make a functioning cell, and a whole organism, and since each gene helps to control a particular characteristic, the DNA controls how the cell is built and what it produces. Every cell of an organism carries the same instructions, which is why a cell can be replaced by a copy of itself. What differs between a muscle cell and a leaf cell is which of the instructions are being used. DNA is a complete set of instructions for the cell and organism each gene controls a particular characteristic every cell carries the same instructions cells differ in which instructions are used"],[24,"ch07-r014","Short answer","All fruit flies have a wing-shape gene in the same place, yet some cannot fly. Explain this.","7.1","All fruit flies have a gene for wing shape in the same place on their chromosome 2, but some fruit flies have normal wings and some have very tiny wings, so small that they cannot fly. The difference comes from having different versions of that gene, each made of slightly different DNA, rather than from having the gene in a different place or not having it at all. Same gene, same position, different version, different characteristic. the gene is in the same place in every fruit fly some have normal wings and some tiny wings the versions of the gene differ in their DNA same position, different version, different characteristic"],[24,"ch07-r015","Short answer","Explain why all the DNA in a cell is copied before the cell divides.","7.1","When a plant or animal grows, or when it needs to repair damage to its body, some of its cells divide to form two new cells, and before this happens all the DNA in the original cell is copied so that each new cell then gets a complete copy of all the DNA in the original cell. Without the copying, each new cell would receive only half the instructions and could not work properly. This is the same event that makes each chromosome look like a cross just before division. cells divide for growth and repair all the DNA is copied first each new cell gets a complete copy otherwise each cell would have only half the instructions"],[24,"ch07-r016","Short answer","Explain what a gamete is and name the two human gametes.","7.2","Sperm cells and egg cells are specialised cells known as gametes: a sperm cell is a male gamete and an egg cell is a female gamete. They are specialised because their job is to join at fertilisation, which is how every human being began life as a single cell formed when a sperm cell joined with an egg cell. Unlike other body cells, gametes have only one set of chromosomes. gametes are specialised sex cells sperm is the male gamete, egg the female gamete they join at fertilisation to start a new life gametes have only one set of chromosomes"],[24,"ch07-r017","Short answer","Explain why egg cells are much bigger than sperm cells.","7.2","Egg cells are much bigger than sperm cells because they contain food reserves in their cytoplasm, and those reserves supply the zygote while it divides over and over again before it can obtain food any other way. A sperm cell does not need reserves because it only has to reach the egg, so it is very small and its cytoplasm is a small amount. The size difference follows from the different jobs the two gametes do. egg cells contain food reserves in the cytoplasm the reserves supply the dividing zygote sperm only has to reach the egg size follows from the different jobs"],[24,"ch07-r018","Short answer","Explain how a sperm cell is adapted for its function.","7.2","A sperm cell has a tail, which produces swimming movements, and sperm cells are very active, using their tails to swim vigorously, so the cell can travel to reach an egg cell. It is a very small cell, which makes it easier to move, and its nucleus contains 23 chromosomes, the one set it must deliver. Each feature serves the single job of getting one set of chromosomes to the egg. a tail produces swimming movements the cell is very active and swims vigorously very small, so easier to move the nucleus carries the 23 chromosomes it must deliver"],[24,"ch07-r019","Short answer","Egg cells cannot move by themselves. Explain why this does not prevent fertilisation.","7.2","Fertilisation happens because the sperm cell moves to the egg, not the other way round: sperm cells are very active, using their tails to swim vigorously, while another difference from sperm cells is that egg cells cannot move by themselves. Only one of the two gametes has to travel for them to meet. The egg instead invests in food reserves, which is why it is much bigger. the sperm swims to the egg only one gamete needs to travel the egg cannot move by itself the egg invests in food reserves instead"],[24,"ch07-r020","Short answer","Explain why gametes have 23 chromosomes when body cells have 46.","7.2","Human cells each have 46 chromosomes in their nucleus, in two sets each containing 23, but gametes have only one set of chromosomes. This means that when a sperm cell joins with an egg cell, the new cell that is produced has two sets and will have 46 chromosomes. If gametes had 46 each, the zygote would have 92 and the number would double in every generation. body cells have two sets of 23, making 46 gametes have only one set sperm plus egg restores 46 in the zygote otherwise the number would double each generation"],[24,"ch07-r021","Short answer","Define fertilisation and explain what a zygote is.","7.2","The joining of a sperm cell with an egg cell is called fertilisation: the head of the sperm cell enters the egg cell and the nucleus of the sperm cell and nucleus of the egg cell fuse together. The new cell that is formed when the nucleus of the sperm cell and the egg cell join together is called a zygote. All humans began their life as a single cell like this, which then divides over and over again. fertilisation is the joining of sperm and egg the two nuclei fuse together the new cell is a zygote the zygote divides over and over to make the body"],[24,"ch07-r022","Short answer","Explain where all the chromosomes in your body cells came from.","7.2","All of the chromosomes in the cells in a human body came from the person's mother and father: there is one set from the mother and one set from the father. Those sets arrived in the egg cell and the sperm cell, each carrying one set of 23, and fused at fertilisation to make a zygote with 46. Every cell in the body is descended from that zygote, so every cell carries copies of the same two sets. one set from the mother and one from the father carried in the egg and the sperm they fused at fertilisation to give 46 every body cell is descended from that zygote"],[24,"ch07-r023","Short answer","Explain how the sex chromosomes determine whether a person is male or female.","7.2","A person with two X chromosomes, XX, is female, and a person with one X chromosome and one Y chromosome, XY, is male. These two chromosomes are sex chromosomes and they determine whether a person is male or female, which is why in a photograph of a woman's chromosomes one pair is labelled X and X. A Y chromosome is much smaller than an X chromosome, so the pair looks unequal in a male. XX is female, XY is male the sex chromosomes determine male or female a woman's set shows a pair of X chromosomes the Y is much smaller than the X"],[24,"ch07-r024","Short answer","Explain why a baby's sex depends on the sperm cell rather than the egg cell.","7.2","All egg cells contain one X chromosome, but sperm cells can have either one X chromosome or one Y chromosome, so the egg always contributes the same thing and the sperm is the variable. Each time fertilisation happens, either an X-containing sperm cell or a Y-containing sperm cell could join with an egg, and that decides whether the zygote is XX or XY. It is not the father choosing anything, only that his gametes are the ones that come in two kinds. all eggs carry an X chromosome sperm carry either an X or a Y the sperm is therefore the variable which sperm arrives decides XX or XY"],[24,"ch07-r025","Short answer","Explain why approximately equal numbers of boys and girls are born.","7.2","Half of all sperm cells contain an X chromosome and half contain a Y chromosome, so the chance of either of these events happening is about equal each time fertilisation occurs. An X-containing sperm gives an XX zygote which develops into a girl, and a Y-containing sperm gives an XY zygote which develops into a boy. Because the two kinds of sperm are equally common, over very large numbers of births the two outcomes come out almost equal. half of sperm carry X and half carry Y the two outcomes are equally likely XX develops into a girl, XY into a boy over many births the numbers come out almost equal"],[24,"ch07-r026","Short answer","Explain what inheritance means and how sex inheritance is an example of it.","7.2","Inheritance means passing on DNA, as chromosomes containing genes, from parents to offspring. A baby's sex is determined because a baby inherits X or Y chromosomes from its parents, so we can use the term sex inheritance to describe this. It is one example among many, since all organisms also inherit other features from their parents in exactly the same way, through the chromosomes carried in the gametes. inheritance is passing DNA from parents to offspring DNA travels as chromosomes containing genes a baby inherits X or Y chromosomes other features are inherited the same way"],[24,"ch07-r027","Short answer","In the modelling activity, explain why one container holds 30 X chromosomes and the other 15 X and 15 Y.","7.2","The first container represents the chromosomes in 30 egg cells, and all egg cells contain one X chromosome, so all 30 must be X. The second represents the chromosomes in 30 sperm cells, and half of all sperm cells contain an X chromosome and half contain a Y chromosome, so 15 of each is the right mixture. The model only gives sensible results if the counts match the biology it is standing in for. the first pot represents 30 egg cells, all carrying X the second represents 30 sperm, half X and half Y 15 and 15 matches the real proportions the model only works if the counts match the biology"],[24,"ch07-r028","Short answer","Explain why the pieces must be taken out without looking.","7.2","Taking a piece out without looking makes each draw random, so an X-containing and a Y-containing sperm are equally likely to be chosen, which is the feature of real fertilisation the model is meant to reproduce. If you could see and choose, you would decide the result yourself and the totals would show your choices rather than chance. The point of the activity is to see what chance alone produces over 30 fertilisations. drawing blind makes each pick random X and Y sperm are then equally likely choosing would decide the result yourself the activity is about what chance alone produces"],[24,"ch07-r029","Short answer","Explain what a class's results from the chromosome activity can and cannot tell you.","7.2","They can show that the chance of a baby being a boy or a girl is about equal, because half the sperm carry X and half carry Y. They cannot be expected to give exactly 15 and 15, since each draw is random and a small number of trials will scatter around the expected value. Combining results from the whole class gives a larger sample, so the proportion should sit closer to a half. they show the chance is about equal an exact 15/15 split is not expected small samples scatter around the expected value combining the class gives a closer proportion"],[24,"ch07-r030","Short answer","Explain what makes one results table better than another.","7.2","A better table can be understood by someone who was not there: it has clear headings that say what was recorded, units where they are needed, and one row or column for each category so nothing has to be worked out from the layout. In this activity it should show the chromosome combination of every zygote produced and the totals of female and male zygotes. A table that needs its author standing beside it to be explained has failed at its job. it can be understood by someone who was not there clear headings saying what was recorded units and one row or column per category totals shown, not left to be worked out"],[24,"ch07-r031","Short answer","Choosing two differences between a sperm cell and an egg cell, explain how each helps the gamete do its job.","7.2","The sperm cell has a tail, which produces swimming movements, so it can travel to reach the egg cell, which the egg cannot do since egg cells cannot move by themselves. The egg cell has cytoplasm containing food reserves, so it can supply the zygote as that single cell divides over and over again, while the sperm only has a small amount of cytoplasm because it needs to stay light and mobile. Each difference matches a different part of what happens at and after fertilisation. the sperm's tail lets it swim to the egg the egg cannot move by itself the egg's food reserves supply the dividing zygote the sperm stays small and light to move"],[24,"ch07-r032","Short answer","Explain what variation means and give an example.","7.3","The differences between individuals belonging to the same species are called variation, so it is about differences within a species and not between species. A group of goats all belonging to the same species will still differ in coat colour, size and horn shape, and the people in a class all belong to the same species but are all very different from one another. Differences between horses and donkeys are not variation, because they belong to different species. variation is differences between individuals of the same species goats of one species differ in colour, size and horns people in a class differ though they are one species differences between species are not variation"],[24,"ch07-r033","Short answer","Explain why a zygote has a combination of DNA that is not the same as either parent's.","7.3","Different sperm cells and different egg cells probably contain different versions of these genes, each made of slightly different DNA, so no two gametes carry the same selection. When a sperm cell and egg cell fuse together at fertilisation, the zygote that is produced has a new combination of DNA, and the combination is not exactly the same as in its parents, or in its brothers and sisters. That is why brothers and sisters resemble each other without being identical. different gametes carry different versions of genes fertilisation combines two of them at random the zygote has a new combination of DNA not the same as its parents or its siblings"],[24,"ch07-r034","Short answer","Explain what is meant by genetic differences.","7.3","Differences in the DNA of organisms within a species are called genetic differences, and genetic means to do with genes. They arise because there are several versions of a gene, each made of slightly different DNA, and each individual inherits its own combination at fertilisation. They are the cause of variation that can be passed on to offspring, which separates them from differences caused by the environment. genetic differences are differences in DNA within a species genetic means to do with genes several versions of each gene exist these differences can be passed on to offspring"],[24,"ch07-r035","Short answer","In a family photograph of three generations, explain why there are both similarities and differences.","7.3","Some of the DNA from the grandparents has been passed on to their children and grandchildren, and you can probably see some similarities between them because some of their DNA is the same. But everyone in the family has a slightly different combination of DNA from everyone else, since each person received a different selection through the gametes that formed them. So shared DNA gives the family resemblance and the different combinations give the differences. DNA passed down from the grandparents is shared shared DNA gives the family resemblance each person has a slightly different combination the different combinations give the differences"],[24,"ch07-r036","Short answer","Two goats have different body masses. Explain why this is not purely genetic.","7.3","The size of the goat could be affected by its DNA, but its environment will also affect size: one goat might eat more than another, so it would grow fatter and have a greater body mass, and this is not caused by its genes. Coat colour is different, because variation in coat colour is caused by genetic differences alone. So not all variation within a species is caused by differences in DNA, and body mass is a feature where both causes act together. DNA affects size, but so does the environment a goat that eats more grows fatter that difference is not caused by its genes coat colour, by contrast, is genetic"],[24,"ch07-r037","Short answer","Explain how you would decide whether a feature's variation is genetic, environmental, or both.","7.3","Ask whether the feature could be changed during the organism's life by how it lives, since an organism's environment also affects it: body mass changes with how much an animal eats, so it has an environmental component. Ask also whether individuals raised in the same conditions still differ, because a difference that survives identical conditions must come from their DNA. Features such as eye colour stay the same whatever the conditions, so their variation is caused by genetic differences. ask whether living conditions could change the feature body mass changes with food, so it is partly environmental differences that persist in identical conditions are genetic eye colour does not change with conditions"],[24,"ch07-r038","Short answer","Leaves on one tree have different numbers of leaflets. Explain whether genes could have caused this.","7.3","All the leaves on one individual tree grew from the same DNA, so genetic differences cannot explain differences between them. The variation must come from the environment instead, for example how much light, water or space each leaf had while it was developing. This is a clean test, because keeping the genes identical leaves the environment as the only possible cause. all leaves on one tree share the same DNA so genes cannot explain differences between them the environment must be the cause identical genes leave the environment as the only variable"],[24,"ch07-r039","Short answer","Zara recorded her flower-colour results in a table and in a bar chart. Explain what each shows better.","7.3","The table keeps the exact numbers, so you can read off that she found nine yellow plants and check the total, and the tally row shows how the counts were built up as she worked. The bar chart shows the pattern at a glance, so it is immediately obvious which was the most common flower colour and how far ahead of the others it was. The two are not rivals; the table is for retrieving values and the chart is for seeing the shape of the data. the table keeps the exact numbers and the tally the chart shows the pattern at a glance the most common colour is obvious in the chart one is for reading values, the other for seeing the shape"],[24,"ch07-r040","Short answer","Explain why a tally is used while collecting data rather than writing numbers straight down.","7.3","For each plant that she found, she put a stroke in the tally row, and when she had recorded the flower colour of each plant she added up all the tally strokes and wrote the number in the last row. A stroke can be added without losing your place or stopping to re-count, which matters when you are recording one item at a time in the field. The totals are worked out once at the end, so there is a single arithmetic step to check rather than a running count that could drift. a stroke is added for each item as it is found no need to stop and re-count each time the totals are added up once at the end one arithmetic step to check instead of a running count"],[24,"ch07-r041","Short answer","Explain the difference between the median, the mode and the mean of a set of leaflet counts.","7.3","The median is the middle value for the number of leaflets when the results are put in order, the mode is the most common value, and the mean is the average, found by adding up all of the numbers of leaflets and dividing by the number of leaves. They answer different questions: the median says what a typical leaf in the middle of the range has, the mode says what happens most often, and the mean uses every value including the extremes. They can all be different numbers for the same set of results. median is the middle value in order mode is the most common value mean is the total divided by the number of leaves they answer different questions and can differ"],[24,"ch07-r042","Short answer","Explain why the bars in a frequency diagram of wrist circumference touch, while the bars in the flower-colour bar chart do not.","7.3","The bars in your frequency diagram should touch one another because wrist circumference is a measurement that can take any value, and the categories are neighbouring ranges with no gaps between them. Flower colour is not a measurement: red, pink and orange are separate named groups with nothing in between, so their bars are drawn with spaces between them. The spacing of the bars tells the reader which kind of data is being shown. wrist circumference is a continuous measurement the categories are neighbouring ranges with no gaps flower colour gives separate named groups the spacing tells the reader which kind of data it is"],[24,"ch07-r043","Short answer","Explain why every person's wrist must be measured in exactly the same way.","7.3","If some wrists are measured tightly and others loosely, or at different places along the arm, then the differences in the results would partly record how the measuring was done rather than real variation between the people. The investigation is about variation in wrist circumference, so the method has to be the one thing that does not vary. Keeping it identical is what allows a difference in the results to be read as a difference in the people. inconsistent measuring adds differences of its own the results would record method, not variation the method must be the one thing kept constant only then does a difference mean a real difference"],[24,"ch07-r044","Short answer","Explain how to choose sensible categories when grouping measurements for a frequency diagram.","7.3","Decide on categories that you can group your results into, trying to have at least four categories but no more than ten. Too few categories hide the shape of the distribution, because almost everything falls into one or two bars, and too many leave most bars holding one item or none, so the pattern disappears into noise. The categories should also be equal in width and not overlap, so that each result belongs in exactly one of them. use at least four and no more than ten categories too few hide the shape of the distribution too many leave the pattern lost in noise equal widths, no overlap, each result in exactly one"],[24,"ch07-r045","Short answer","Summarise the theory of natural selection.","7.4","In every species there is variation among individual organisms, and some of this variation is caused by differences in their genes. Some individuals have features that make it more likely that they will survive than individuals that do not have these features. Those individuals live to reproduce and pass on their genes to their offspring, so over generations the helpful versions of the genes become more common in the population. This process is called natural selection. variation exists in every species some of it is caused by differences in genes some features make survival more likely survivors reproduce and pass on those genes the helpful genes become more common over generations"],[24,"ch07-r046","Short answer","In the giraffe story, explain why the offspring mostly had long necks.","7.4","When all the lower leaves had been eaten, only the long-necked animals could get food, so most of the animals with short necks died of starvation. When the animals reproduced, they passed on their genes to their offspring, and because only the long-necked animals had survived it was only their genes that passed to the next generation. The necks of the survivors did not grow; what changed was which genes were still present to be inherited. only long-necked animals could reach food most short-necked animals died of starvation only the survivors' genes passed on no neck grew; the gene pool changed"],[24,"ch07-r047","Short answer","Explain why the giraffe story is described as imaginary and what it is still useful for.","7.4","We do not know if this is what happened, and we can never really know, because the events would have taken place long ago and we have no evidence that any of this ever happened. It is still useful because the theory does help to explain how almost every species is so well adapted to its environment, and the story shows how the five steps of natural selection fit together. The evidence for natural selection itself comes from cases we can observe, such as antibiotic resistance and the peppered moth. we cannot know what happened long ago the story is illustrative, not evidence it shows how the five steps fit together the real evidence comes from observable cases"],[24,"ch07-r048","Short answer","Explain how a population of bacteria becomes resistant to an antibiotic.","7.4","In a population of bacteria, not every one is alike, and by chance one may have a gene that makes it resistant to an antibiotic. When antibiotic is added, it kills the bacteria that are not resistant, and the resistant one can now multiply and form a population of resistant bacteria just like itself. The antibiotic did not create the resistance; the resistant bacterium was already there and the antibiotic removed its competitors. variation exists in the bacterial population by chance one carries a resistance gene the antibiotic kills the non-resistant ones the survivor multiplies into a resistant population the antibiotic selects rather than creates resistance"],[24,"ch07-r049","Short answer","Explain what is meant by saying bacteria have become resistant to an antibiotic.","7.4","It means the antibiotic no longer kills those bacteria, so the drug does not work anymore against the disease they cause. It does not mean individual bacteria have toughened up during treatment: resistance comes from a gene that makes a bacterium resistant, and the resistant ones survive and multiply while the others are killed. Resistance is a property of the population's genes, which is why it persists in the next generation. the antibiotic no longer kills those bacteria the drug stops working against the disease resistance comes from a gene, not from toughening up it persists because it is inherited"],[24,"ch07-r050","Short answer","Explain why doctors advise that antibiotics should be saved for serious illnesses.","7.4","Every time an antibiotic is used, it kills the bacteria that are not resistant and leaves any resistant ones to multiply and form a population of resistant bacteria, so each unnecessary course makes resistant bacteria more common. Using antibiotics only when they are really needed slows that selection down, so the drugs still work when people have serious illnesses. Doctors are protecting a resource that natural selection is steadily destroying. each use selects for resistant bacteria unnecessary use makes resistance more common restraint slows the selection down keeps the drugs working for serious illness"],[24,"ch07-r051","Short answer","Explain why pale peppered moths were at an advantage before 1849.","7.4","The pale colour of peppered moths camouflages them perfectly against lichen-covered tree bark, and peppered moths spend the daylight hours resting on tree trunks where they are hunted and eaten by birds. Scientists have shown that birds can see dark moths more easily on lichen-covered tree trunks than on dark ones, so a dark moth resting in daylight was far more likely to be found. Being harder to see meant living long enough to reproduce, which is why almost all peppered moths were pale. pale moths were camouflaged on lichen-covered bark they rest on tree trunks in daylight birds find dark moths easily on pale bark harder to see meant surviving to reproduce"],[24,"ch07-r052","Short answer","Explain why the dark variety of the peppered moth became common near English cities by 1900.","7.4","Many factories burnt coal, which polluted the air with smoke, and the smoke killed lichens and made the tree trunks dark. When the tree trunks got darker, the pale moths were not camouflaged and were more likely than the dark ones to be eaten by birds, so the dark moths were more likely to survive and reproduce. They passed on their genes for dark colour to their offspring, and over several generations more and more of the offspring inherited these advantageous genes, so the dark colour became more and more common. smoke killed the lichens and darkened the tree trunks pale moths lost their camouflage and were eaten dark moths survived and reproduced more often they passed on the genes for dark colour the dark form became more common over generations"],[24,"ch07-r053","Short answer","Explain why most peppered moths in England are pale again today.","7.4","Today pollution in England is much less, so lichens grow on tree trunks again and the bark is pale once more. That reverses which moth is camouflaged: birds can see dark moths more easily on lichen-covered tree trunks, so the pale moths now survive and reproduce more often and pass on their genes for pale colour. Natural selection has no fixed direction, it simply follows whichever feature helps survival in the conditions of the time. less pollution, so lichens grow and bark is pale again pale moths are camouflaged once more pale moths now survive and reproduce more often natural selection follows the conditions, it has no fixed direction"],[24,"ch07-r054","Short answer","On an island with no snakes, some lizards have brown tails and some blue. Explain why brown-tailed lizards are more likely to be eaten once snakes arrive.","7.4","When a lizard is attacked by a predator, the lizard's tail falls off and squirms violently, attracting the attention of the predator while the lizard runs off and hides. Snakes are especially good at seeing the colour blue, so a blue tail is noticed first and the snake attacks the discarded tail rather than the lizard. A brown tail is less likely to draw the snake away from the lizard itself, so the brown-tailed lizards are more likely to be caught. a shed tail squirms and distracts the predator snakes see blue especially well a blue tail draws the snake to the tail, not the lizard a brown tail is less effective as a decoy"],[24,"ch07-r055","Short answer","More lizards in the next generation have blue tails. Explain why 'the lizards changed their tail colour to avoid being eaten' is wrong.","7.4","The lizard's genes determine the colour of its tail, so an individual cannot change it during its life, and the lizards cannot learn to have offspring with blue tails either. What happens is that more parent lizards with blue tails survive, so they are the ones that reproduce, and they pass on their genes for blue tails to their offspring. The population changes because of which individuals survived to breed, not because any individual changed. genes determine tail colour, so an individual cannot change it lizards cannot choose their offspring's colour more blue-tailed parents survive and reproduce they pass on their genes for blue tails the population changes, not the individual"],[24,"ch07-r056","Short answer","In the pasta-caterpillar investigation, explain which measurement tests the girls' idea.","7.4","Their idea is that in a grassy area, green caterpillars are less likely to be found by a predator than yellow caterpillars, so the measurement must be how many green caterpillars and how many yellow caterpillars Marcus picks up. The time taken to find 20 tells you nothing about colour, and which hand he uses is irrelevant to the idea being tested. A measurement only tests an idea if the idea predicts what it should show. count how many green and how many yellow are picked up the idea is about which colour is found more easily time taken says nothing about colour a measurement tests an idea only if the idea predicts it"],[24,"ch07-r057","Short answer","The girls want a more reliable conclusion. Explain which repeat is the right one.","7.4","They should repeat the experiment five times with the same caterpillars, using five different students to collect them, because that keeps everything the same except the person collecting and gives more results to compare. Repeating with 25 green and 75 yellow changes the starting proportions, so any difference in what is picked up could come from the numbers put out rather than from the colour. Repeating with blue and red caterpillars tests different colours altogether and cannot answer the original question. repeat five times with the same caterpillars and different collectors everything stays the same except the collector changing the proportions confounds the result changing the colours tests a different question"],[24,"ch07-r058","Short answer","Explain why coloured pasta is used instead of real caterpillars, and what the model cannot show.","7.4","Pasta pieces can be made in known numbers and exact colours, 50 green and 50 yellow, and spread over a grassy area, so the only difference between them is the colour being tested, which would be impossible to arrange with real caterpillars. Using a person to pick them up also makes the searching safe and repeatable. The model cannot show how real birds hunt, since caterpillars move, smell and hide, and a human searching deliberately is not the same as a predator feeding. pasta can be made in known numbers and exact colours colour becomes the only difference between the two groups safe and repeatable to search cannot show how real predators hunt moving, hiding prey"],[24,"ch07-r059","Short answer","A graph shows the percentage of penicillin-resistant pneumonia cases rising over time. Explain how natural selection caused this trend.","7.4","In a population of the bacteria, not every one is alike, and by chance some carry a gene that makes them resistant to penicillin. Each time penicillin is used it kills the bacteria that are not resistant, and the resistant ones survive and multiply to form a population of resistant bacteria just like themselves. As penicillin continued to be used, the resistant bacteria made up a larger and larger share of the cases, which is the rising trend the graph shows. variation exists in the bacterial population some carry a resistance gene by chance penicillin kills the non-resistant bacteria the resistant ones survive and multiply their share of cases rises over time"],[24,"ch07-r060","Short answer","Explain why natural selection cannot work in a species with no genetic variation.","7.4","Natural selection begins with the fact that in every species there is variation among individual organisms and some of this variation is caused by differences in their genes. If every individual had identical DNA, no individual would have features that make it more likely to survive, so nothing would be selected for. Even if many died, the survivors would pass on the same genes as everyone else, and the population would not change over generations. selection needs variation caused by differences in genes identical DNA means no individual has an advantage nothing would be selected for survivors would pass on the same genes, so nothing changes"],[24,"ch07-r061","Short answer","Explain why natural selection accounts for organisms being well adapted to their environment.","7.4","In every species there is variation, and some individuals have features that make it more likely that they will survive than individuals that do not have these features. Those individuals reproduce and pass on the genes for those features, so each generation contains a larger share of the individuals whose features suit the conditions they live in. Repeated over very many generations, this theory does help to explain how almost every species is so well adapted to its environment, without anything having to aim at that result. variation means some individuals suit the conditions better those individuals survive and reproduce the useful genes increase in each generation repeated over many generations this produces adaptation no aim or intention is needed"],[24,"ch07-o001","Short answer","State where in a cell the chromosomes are found.","7.1","In the nucleus. nucleus"],[24,"ch07-o002","Short answer","Name the chemical substance that chromosomes are made of.","7.1","DNA. DNA"],[24,"ch07-o003","Short answer","State how many chromosomes there are in a human cell.","7.1","46. 46"],[24,"ch07-o004","Short answer","State the number of chromosomes in a fruit fly cell and in a mango tree cell.","7.1","A fruit fly cell has eight chromosomes and a mango tree cell has 40. eight 40"],[24,"ch07-o005","Short answer","Explain what a gene is.","7.1","A gene is a part of a chromosome that helps to control a particular characteristic of the organism. part of a chromosome controls a characteristic"],[24,"ch07-o006","Short answer","Explain how scientists decide which chromosome in a human cell is called chromosome 1.","7.1","Chromosomes are numbered according to their length. The longest one is chromosome 1, the next longest chromosome 2, and so on. length longest"],[24,"ch07-o015","Short answer","State what a gamete is.","7.2","A sex cell - in humans, a sperm cell or an egg cell. sex cell"],[24,"ch07-o016","Short answer","Name the male gamete and the female gamete in humans.","7.2","The male gamete is the sperm cell and the female gamete is the egg cell. sperm cell egg cell"],[24,"ch07-o017","Short answer","Describe the function of the tail of a sperm cell.","7.2","It produces swimming movements, so the sperm cell can swim to the egg cell. swim"],[24,"ch07-o028","Short answer","Explain why approximately equal numbers of boys and girls are born each year.","7.2","Half of all sperm cells carry an X chromosome and half carry a Y, so the chance of making an XX zygote and the chance of making an XY zygote are about equal. half X and half Y chance is equal"],[24,"ch07-o029","Short answer","Explain why the sex of a baby is determined by its father and not by its mother.","7.2","Every egg cell carries an X chromosome, but a sperm cell can carry either an X or a Y, so it is the sperm cell that decides whether the zygote is XX or XY. sperm cell X or Y"],[24,"ch07-o031","Short answer","Goats of one species differ from one another in size, coat colour and horns. State two features that all of them share.","7.3","For example, they all have four legs and two ears. four legs two ears"],[24,"ch07-o032","Short answer","Zara counted 5 red, 2 pink, 3 orange, 9 yellow and 3 cream kidney vetch plants. Calculate how many plants she found altogether.","7.3","5 + 2 + 3 + 9 + 3 = 22 plants. 22"],[24,"ch07-o033","Short answer","Zara found 5 red, 2 pink, 3 orange, 9 yellow and 3 cream flowered plants. State the most common flower colour.","7.3","Yellow. yellow"],[24,"ch07-o034","Short answer","Describe how you use a tally row when you are recording how many of each kind you find.","7.3","Put one stroke in the tally row for each individual you find, then add up all the strokes at the end and write the total in the number row. one stroke each add up the strokes"],[24,"ch07-o035","Short answer","The bars of a flower colour bar chart have gaps between them, but the bars of a wrist circumference frequency diagram touch. Explain why.","7.3","Flower colours are separate categories, so the bars are drawn apart. Wrist circumferences are ranges of numbers that follow on from each other, so the bars touch. separate categories ranges of numbers"],[24,"ch07-o036","Short answer","State what is meant by genetic differences.","7.3","Differences in the DNA of organisms within a species. DNA within a species"],[24,"ch07-o041","Short answer","Explain why the members of one family look alike but are not identical.","7.3","Some of their DNA is the same, which produces the similarities, but everyone in the family has a slightly different combination of DNA from everyone else. some DNA is the same different combination"],[24,"ch07-o045","Short answer","In the imaginary story about the long-necked herbivores, explain why most of the short-necked animals died.","7.4","In the years when the rains did not come the animals ran short of food, and once the lower leaves had been eaten only the long-necked animals could reach food, so the short-necked ones starved. only long necks could reach food starvation"],[24,"ch07-o049","Short answer","Explain how a whole population of resistant bacteria can grow from a single bacterium.","7.4","The one resistant bacterium is not killed by the antibiotic, so it survives and multiplies, and every offspring inherits the gene for resistance. multiply offspring inherit the gene"],[24,"ch07-o050","Short answer","Suggest why doctors advise that antibiotics should be saved for serious illnesses.","7.4","Every time an antibiotic is used it gives an advantage to the bacteria that are resistant to it, so the gene for resistance becomes more common. Using antibiotics less often slows this down and keeps them working for when they are really needed. advantage to resistant bacteria keeps the antibiotic working"],[24,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","When are chromosomes visible under a light microscope?","7.1","Only when the cell is dividing At all times in every cell Only in red blood cells Only when the cell is dead"],[24,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","Chromosomes need to be coloured using special ____ in order to be seen.","7.1","stains"],[24,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","How many chromosomes are there in a human body cell?","7.1","46 23 8 40"],[24,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","Each chromosome is made up of hundreds of different ____.","7.1","genes"],[24,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What is the shape of a DNA molecule called?","7.1","Double helix Single strand Cross shape Chain of beads"],[24,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","Sperm cells and egg cells are specialised cells known as ____.","7.2","gametes"],[24,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","How many chromosomes does a human sperm cell contain?","7.2","23 46 12 8"],[24,"qwen-01-08","Gap fill","The joining of a sperm cell with an egg cell is called ____.","7.2","fertilisation"],[24,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","What is the name of the new cell formed when a sperm and egg join?","7.2","Zygote Gamete Nucleus Chromosome"],[24,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","A person with two X chromosomes is ____.","7.2","female"],[24,"qwen-01-11","Multiple choice","Which sex chromosome is much smaller than the X chromosome?","7.2","Y chromosome X chromosome Chromosome 1 Chromosome 2"],[24,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","Inheritance means passing on ____ from parents to offspring.","7.2","DNA"],[24,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","What is the term for differences between individuals of the same species?","7.3","Mutation Variation Selection Fertilisation"],[24,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","The differences in DNA between organisms within a species are called ____ differences.","7.3","genetic"],[24,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","Which factor can cause a goat to have a greater body mass?","7.3","Its genes only Eating more food The colour of its coat The shape of its horns"],[24,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","A sperm cell and an egg cell each contain chromosomes made of many ____.","7.3","genes"],[24,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What happens when a sperm cell and an egg cell fuse together?","7.3","They form a zygote with a new combination of DNA They create two identical clones The DNA is destroyed completely Only the mother's genes are kept"],[24,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","In the story about the long-necked herbivores of Africa, the long-necked animals survived because they could still reach ____ when the lower leaves had been eaten.","7.4","food"],[24,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","Why did dark peppered moths become more common in English cities?","7.4","Birds could see pale moths more easily on dark tree trunks Dark moths flew faster than pale ones The air pollution made the moths change colour instantly Pale moths stopped reproducing completely"],[24,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","The process where advantageous features become more common over generations is called natural ____.","7.4","selection"],[24,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","How do bacteria become resistant to antibiotics?","7.4","They eat the antibiotic and get stronger A gene for resistance becomes more common in the population The antibiotic changes their DNA directly They hide from the medicine in the soil"],[24,"qwen-02-10","Gap fill","Lichen is a plant-like organism that grows on rocks and ____.","7.4","trees"],[24,"qwen-02-11","Multiple choice","What is the 'mode' in a set of data?","7.3","The middle value The average value The most common value The largest value"],[24,"qwen-02-12","Gap fill","In a frequency diagram, the bars should ____ one another.","7.3","touch"],[24,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","Why did dark peppered moths become more common than pale ones when tree trunks got darker?","7.4","Dark moths were faster at flying away from birds Pale moths were not camouflaged and were eaten by birds more often Dark moths laid more eggs than pale moths Birds preferred to eat dark moths because they tasted better"],[24,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","Which variety of peppered moth is the most common in England today, and why?","7.4","Most are now dark because pollution is still high Most are now pale because lichens grow on tree trunks again Both dark and pale moths have disappeared Only dark moths survive in England now"],[24,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What are egg cells and sperm cells called?","7.2","gametes chromosomes genes nuclei"],[24,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","Egg cells and sperm cells are specialised cells called ____.","7.2","gametes"],[24,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","A sperm cell can join with an egg cell in a process called ____.","7.2","fertilisation"],[24,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","How many chromosomes are found in the nucleus of a sperm cell?","7.2","23 46 12 50"],[24,"qwenw-01-06","Gap fill","The nucleus of a sperm cell contains ____ chromosomes.","7.2","23"],[24,"qwenw-01-07","Multiple choice","Which structure in an egg cell contains food reserves?","7.2","cytoplasm nucleus tail membrane"],[24,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","What word describes differences between individuals of the same species?","7.3","variation adaptation frequency feature"],[24,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","Differences between individuals that belong to the same species are called ____.","7.3","variation"],[24,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","What happens to a lizard's tail when it is attacked by a predator?","7.4","It falls off and squirms to attract the predator It hardens to protect the body It changes colour to match the ground It grows longer to fight back"],[24,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","While the predator is attacking the tail, the lizard runs off and hides. It then grows a new ____.","7.4","tail"],[24,"qwenw-02-03","Multiple choice","Why are snakes especially good at seeing lizards with blue tails?","7.4","Snakes have eyes that detect the colour blue well Blue is the only colour snakes can see Snakes are attracted to the smell of blue tails Blue tails make a sound that snakes hear"],[24,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","The part of a lizard's cell that contains its genes is the ____.","7.4","nucleus"],[24,"qwenw-02-05","Multiple choice","What is the term for passing on genes from parents to offspring?","7.4","Inheritance Variation Mutation Selection"],[24,"qwenw-02-06","Gap fill","In a grassy area, green caterpillars are less likely to be found by predators than ____ caterpillars.","7.4","yellow"],[25,"ch08-f01a","Diagram labelling","Magnesium ribbon is reacting with dilute sulfuric acid and the hydrogen given off is being collected. Name the part labelled {n}.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the piece of apparatus whose scale is read to find the volume of hydrogen produced.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on the scale in the diagram.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the liquid reactant in this apparatus.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f02a","Diagram labelling","This graph was drawn while calcium carbonate reacted with hydrochloric acid. Name the quantity plotted on the axis labelled {n}.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the annotation that describes the part of the curve where the line is becoming less steep.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the annotation that describes the part of the curve where the line has become horizontal.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f02h3","Click the diagram","Click on the Mass of carbon dioxide produced in the diagram.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f03a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus is used for collecting a gas over water. Name the part labelled {n}.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the tube that carries the gas from the flask across to the measuring cylinder.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the piece of apparatus that is filled with water at the start and has the gas volume read off it.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f03h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the funnel used to add the acid to the flask without letting gas escape.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f04a","Diagram labelling","These two pictures show the same reaction mixture early on and later on. Name the substance labelled {n}.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the hydrochloric acid in the diagram.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the calcium carbonate in the diagram.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f04h3","Click the diagram","Click on the caption that describes the mixture at the very start of the reaction, when the rate is fastest.","8.1",""],[25,"ch08-f05a","Diagram labelling","These drawings compare a block of magnesium with magnesium ribbon of the same mass. Name the type of atom labelled {n}.","8.2",""],[25,"ch08-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the atom at the surface in the diagram.","8.2",""],[25,"ch08-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the atom inside in the diagram.","8.2",""],[25,"ch08-f05h3","Click the diagram","Click on the caption that explains why a solid block of magnesium reacts only slowly with the air.","8.2",""],[25,"ch08-f06a","Diagram labelling","This graph compares the same reaction carried out at two different temperatures. Name the quantity plotted on the axis labelled {n}.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the line that reaches its final volume of gas in the shorter time.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the line with the smaller gradient at the start of the reaction.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the Volume of carbon dioxide produced in the diagram.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f07a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the caption of the box in which the particles are moving more slowly and colliding less often.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the caption of the box in which the particles have the most energy.","8.3",""],[25,"ch08-f08a","Diagram labelling","This apparatus is used to time how long it takes to collect 25 cm3 of carbon dioxide. Name the part labelled {n}.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the measuring cylinder in the diagram.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the reactant whose concentration is deliberately changed in this investigation.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f08h3","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the solid reactant, which is kept at the same mass in every run.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f09a","Diagram labelling","This graph compares the same reaction using a high and a low concentration of acid. Name the quantity plotted on the axis labelled {n}.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the line that reaches the final volume of carbon dioxide in the shorter time.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the line with the gentler slope at the beginning.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f09h3","Click the diagram","Click on the Volume of carbon dioxide produced in the diagram.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f10a","Diagram labelling","These two beakers show the particles in dilute acid and in acid twice as concentrated. Name the part labelled {n}.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the particle that there are twice as many of in the right-hand beaker.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the label of the particle that the acid has to collide with for a reaction to happen.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-f10h3","Click the diagram","Click on the beaker containing acid in the diagram.","8.4",""],[25,"ch08-r001","Short answer","Magnesium ribbon is dropped into dilute sulfuric acid. Describe how the bubbling changes over time and say what this tells you about the reaction.","8.1","At the start of the reaction a lot of bubbles are given off quickly, then as the reaction comes to an end fewer bubbles are produced, and eventually no more bubbles are produced. This shows that the reaction started quickly, then slowed down, and eventually stopped, so the rate of reaction is not constant but falls as the reaction proceeds. many bubbles at start fewer later none at the end rate falls not constant"],[25,"ch08-r002","Short answer","In the magnesium and sulfuric acid experiment, explain why the rate is measured by following the hydrogen gas rather than the magnesium or the magnesium sulfate.","8.1","It is difficult to measure how quickly the reactants are used up or how quickly the magnesium sulfate is formed, because the magnesium sulfate stays dissolved in the acid and the ribbon is hard to weigh while it is reacting. The easiest way to measure the rate is to measure how quickly the hydrogen gas is produced, because the gas leaves the mixture and its volume can be measured directly. hard to measure reactants used up product stays in solution gas leaves mixture volume easy to measure"],[25,"ch08-r003","Short answer","Explain why the syringe must be attached to the flask so that no hydrogen can escape.","8.1","The rate is worked out from the volume of gas produced in a particular length of time, so every bubble of hydrogen made must end up in the syringe. If any hydrogen escaped, the measured volume would be less than the volume actually produced, so the calculated rate would be too low and the results would be wrong. all gas must be collected no escape measured volume too low rate too low"],[25,"ch08-r004","Short answer","Explain why the rate of reaction must be described as a volume in a length of time rather than just a volume.","8.1","Rate means how quickly something happens, so a time is always needed. You measure the volume of gas produced in a particular length of time, and dividing that volume by the time gives a rate such as cubic centimetres per second. A volume on its own tells you how much reacted but not how fast. rate needs a time volume divided by time cm3 per second volume alone is not a rate"],[25,"ch08-r005","Short answer","A table of results shows the total volume of hydrogen collected at each time. Explain how you can tell from such a table that the reaction has finished.","8.1","The reaction has finished when the total volume of hydrogen stops increasing, so the same volume is recorded at that time and at every later time. No more gas is being produced, which means no more reaction is taking place, so the moment when the readings first stop changing is the time the reaction ended. volume stops increasing same reading repeats no more gas produced reaction ended"],[25,"ch08-r006","Short answer","The syringe in the experiment holds 100 cm3. Suggest what would happen if a lot more magnesium and acid were used, and how the risk could be reduced.","8.1","More magnesium and acid would produce more than 100 cm3 of hydrogen, so the syringe would fill completely and the pressure inside would push the plunger out of the barrel, letting hydrogen escape. The risk could be reduced by using a larger syringe or a gas syringe with a stop, or by using smaller amounts of magnesium and acid so that less than 100 cm3 of gas is formed. more than 100 cm3 plunger pushed out gas escapes larger syringe or smaller amounts"],[25,"ch08-r007","Short answer","Explain what an anomalous result is and how one can be recognised on a graph.","8.1","An anomalous result is a result that does not fit the pattern made by the others. When a graph of the results is plotted it is easier to see the pattern that they make, so an anomalous point stands out clearly as one that lies well away from the smooth curve or line that the other points follow. does not fit the pattern graph shows pattern point lies away from curve"],[25,"ch08-r008","Short answer","You obtain an anomalous result in a rate of reaction experiment. Describe what you should do about it and explain how that helps.","8.1","You should repeat the measurement, ideally repeating the whole experiment more than once. If the repeats follow the pattern and the odd value does not appear again, the result really was anomalous and was caused by a mistake such as a misread syringe or a leak. If the same value appears again, it is not anomalous and the pattern itself needs rethinking. repeat the measurement compare repeats if not repeated it was anomalous if repeated pattern is wrong"],[25,"ch08-r009","Short answer","In the reaction between calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid the rate can be followed by weighing the flask. Explain why the mass falls.","8.1","The reaction produces carbon dioxide gas. As carbon dioxide gas is lost from the flask into the air, the mass of the flask decreases. The mass lost each minute is a measure of how much gas has been produced, so following the falling mass follows the rate of the reaction. carbon dioxide produced gas escapes from flask mass decreases mass loss measures rate"],[25,"ch08-r010","Short answer","Explain how the slope of a line on a volume against time graph tells you the rate of reaction.","8.1","The slope or gradient of the line tells you how quickly the reaction is taking place, because a steep slope means a large volume of product is made in a short time. The steeper the slope, the faster the reaction, and where the line is less steep the reaction is slower. slope or gradient volume made in a short time steeper means faster"],[25,"ch08-r011","Short answer","On a graph of carbon dioxide produced against time, the line is steepest at the start and then levels out. Explain what each part of the shape shows.","8.1","The line is steepest at the start of the reaction, and this is when the reaction is fastest. As the slope of the line becomes less steep, the reaction is slowing down. When the line levels out, it shows that no more carbon dioxide is being produced, which means that the reaction has ended. steepest at start fastest less steep means slowing level means ended"],[25,"ch08-r012","Short answer","A graph shows that 20 cm3 of carbon dioxide is produced between 10 seconds and 30 seconds. Calculate the average rate over that period and explain the method.","8.1","Read the volume at 10 seconds and the volume at 30 seconds from the graph and subtract them to find the volume produced, here 20 cm3. Subtract the times to find how long it took, here 20 seconds. Dividing the volume by the time gives 20 cm3 in 20 seconds, so on average the rate of reaction over this period of time is 1 cm3 per second. read two points subtract volumes subtract times divide 1 cm3 per second"],[25,"ch08-r013","Short answer","Using the same graph, the average rate between 60 and 80 seconds is much smaller than between 10 and 30 seconds. Explain why.","8.1","Both periods last 20 seconds, but less gas is produced in the later period because the line is much less steep there. The reaction is fastest at the start when there are plenty of unreacted particles, and by 60 to 80 seconds many of the reactant particles have already been used up, so the rate has fallen. same time interval less gas produced line less steep reactants used up slower"],[25,"ch08-r014","Short answer","In a rate experiment you take a reading every thirty seconds until you have three readings the same. Explain why three identical readings are used as the stopping point.","8.1","Three readings the same show that the measured quantity has stopped changing, so no more product is being formed and the reaction has finished. One unchanged reading might happen by chance or by misreading the scale, but three in a row make it very unlikely that the reaction is still going on slowly. quantity stopped changing reaction finished one reading could be chance three confirms"],[25,"ch08-r015","Short answer","State the condition that particles must meet for a chemical reaction to take place.","8.1","For a chemical reaction to take place, the particles of the reactants involved have to collide with one another with enough energy to react together. Both parts matter: particles that never meet cannot react, and particles that meet too gently simply bounce apart without reacting. particles must collide with enough energy both conditions needed"],[25,"ch08-r016","Short answer","Use collision theory to explain why the reaction is fastest at the start.","8.1","At the start of the reaction there are a lot of particles that have not reacted, so collisions happen frequently. More frequent collisions mean more successful collisions each second, so a lot of product, such as carbon dioxide, is produced in the first 30 second period and the rate is high. many unreacted particles frequent collisions more successful collisions per second high rate"],[25,"ch08-r017","Short answer","Use collision theory to explain why the rate of a reaction falls as it goes on.","8.1","As the particles react, the number of particles that have not reacted gets lower and lower, so the chance of two unreacted particles colliding with each other decreases. Fewer successful collisions happen each second, which means less product is formed in the later 30 second periods, so the rate of reaction is slower. fewer unreacted particles chance of collision decreases less product per period slower"],[25,"ch08-r018","Short answer","Use collision theory to explain why a reaction eventually stops completely.","8.1","Eventually all the particles of at least one reactant have reacted, so there are no more collisions that result in the production of the product. With no successful collisions, no more product is formed, the graph line levels out and the reaction has finished. all particles reacted no successful collisions no more product line levels out"],[25,"ch08-r019","Short answer","Two sections of a rate graph are compared. Explain how to decide from the graph alone which section shows the greater rate of reaction.","8.1","The slope of the line on the graph tells you the rate of reaction, and the steeper the line the faster the reaction. So you compare how steeply the line rises in each section over the same time interval; the section where the line climbs more for the same time along the horizontal axis has the greater rate. compare slopes steeper is faster same time interval"],[25,"ch08-r022","Short answer","A large piece of magnesium does not burn in a Bunsen flame, magnesium ribbon burns brightly, and magnesium powder burns even faster. Identify the variable being changed.","8.2","The variable being changed is the surface area of the magnesium. All three are the same metal reacting with oxygen in the same flame, so the element, the temperature and the other reactant are the same. The only difference is how much of the magnesium is exposed at the surface, which is the total surface area. surface area same metal same flame only difference is exposure"],[25,"ch08-r023","Short answer","Explain, in terms of atoms, why a block of magnesium reacts so slowly with oxygen.","8.2","Only the magnesium atoms on the surface can make contact with the oxygen and react with it. In the block of magnesium, most of the atoms are inside the block, away from the oxygen, so they cannot take part in any collisions. With so few atoms available to react, very few successful collisions happen each second and the reaction is very slow. only surface atoms react most atoms inside away from oxygen few collisions slow"],[25,"ch08-r024","Short answer","Magnesium ribbon and a magnesium block of the same mass are compared. Explain why the ribbon reacts faster.","8.2","Magnesium ribbon has a larger total surface area than a magnesium block of the same mass, because the metal is spread out thinly instead of packed into a lump. In the magnesium ribbon, more of the atoms are on the surface and can react with the air, so collisions with oxygen happen more frequently and the rate of reaction is greater. same mass larger total surface area more atoms on surface more collisions faster"],[25,"ch08-r025","Short answer","Explain why magnesium powder reacts faster than magnesium ribbon of the same mass.","8.2","An equivalent mass of magnesium powder has an even larger total surface area than the ribbon, because it is divided into many tiny pieces each with its own surface. It therefore has the most atoms available to react with the oxygen, so successful collisions happen most frequently and the reaction is even quicker. powder largest surface area many tiny pieces most atoms available quickest"],[25,"ch08-r026","Short answer","Explain why cutting a solid into smaller pieces increases its total surface area, even though no material is added.","8.2","Each time you cut a solid into smaller pieces, you create two new faces where the cut was made. Those faces were previously inside the solid, so they were not part of the surface. Nothing has been added, but material that was hidden inside is now exposed, so the total surface area increases every time the solid is divided further. cutting creates new faces previously inside now exposed total surface area increases"],[25,"ch08-r027","Short answer","Use the example of a loaf of bread to explain the idea of total surface area.","8.2","A whole loaf has only its outside crust as its surface. When it is cut into slices, each slice has two new flat faces that were inside the loaf before, so the slices of bread have a total surface area greater than the loaf of bread even though the amount of bread is unchanged. The same idea applies to breaking a solid reactant into smaller pieces. loaf has only its outside slices have new faces greater total surface area same amount"],[25,"ch08-r028","Short answer","Explain why the statement 'small pieces of solids always react faster than larger pieces' is true in terms of collisions.","8.2","Smaller pieces of the same mass have a larger total surface area, so more of the particles of the solid are exposed where the other reactant can reach them. That means collisions between the reactant particles happen more frequently, more of them are successful each second, and so the rate of reaction is greater. larger total surface area more particles exposed more frequent collisions greater rate"],[25,"ch08-r029","Short answer","In the surface area investigation you use two sizes of calcium carbonate chips. Explain why the same method must be used for both experiments.","8.2","Using the same method keeps everything except the chip size the same, so the volume and concentration of acid, the mass of calcium carbonate, the temperature and the way the gas is collected are all controlled variables. If the method changed between the two runs, any difference in rate could have been caused by the method rather than by the surface area, and no valid conclusion could be drawn. controlled variables only chip size changes fair test otherwise conclusion invalid"],[25,"ch08-r030","Short answer","Two beakers contain the same mass of calcium carbonate in the same acid, one as small chips and one as large chips. Predict and explain which fizzes more quickly.","8.2","The beaker with the small chips fizzes more quickly. For the same mass, small chips have a larger total surface area than large chips, so more calcium carbonate particles are exposed to the acid. Collisions between the acid particles and the calcium carbonate particles happen more frequently, so carbon dioxide is produced faster. small chips faster larger surface area more particles exposed more frequent collisions"],[25,"ch08-r031","Short answer","In a surface area experiment using equal masses of flat zinc and lumpy zinc, compare the total volume of gas produced by each and explain your answer.","8.2","The total volume of gas produced is the same in both reactions. Surface area changes how quickly the reaction happens, not how much can react. Equal masses of zinc in enough acid contain the same number of zinc particles, so the same amount of hydrogen is eventually produced, but the flat piece produces it in less time. same total volume same mass of zinc surface area changes speed not amount flat piece faster"],[25,"ch08-r032","Short answer","Describe how the line of best fit for powdered zinc would differ from the line for a lump of zinc of the same mass, and explain why.","8.2","The line for the powder would be steeper at the start and would level out sooner, but it would level out at the same height as the line for the lump. The powder has the largest total surface area, so collisions happen most frequently and the reaction is fastest, but the same mass of zinc contains the same number of particles so the final volume of gas is unchanged. steeper at start levels out sooner same final height largest surface area same number of particles"],[25,"ch08-r033","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why the rate of reaction is high at the start and slows down after a time.","8.2","When there are many particles, the particles collide with each other more often. At the start of a reaction the reactants are all present, so collisions are frequent and many of them are successful, giving a high rate. As the reaction proceeds the reactant particles are used up, so there are fewer left, collisions become less frequent and the rate slows down. many particles collide more often high rate at start particles used up fewer collisions slows"],[25,"ch08-r034","Short answer","When hydrochloric acid is mixed with sodium thiosulfate solution the mixture becomes cloudy. Explain why.","8.3","The reaction produces sulfur. Sulfur is insoluble in water, so instead of dissolving it forms a precipitate of tiny solid particles spread through the liquid. These particles block light, so the mixture turns cloudy, and the more sulfur that forms the more opaque the mixture becomes. sulfur produced insoluble precipitate tiny solid particles cloudy"],[25,"ch08-r035","Short answer","Explain why the reaction between hydrochloric acid and sodium thiosulfate is a good one for investigating reaction rate.","8.3","The precipitate of sulfur is opaque, so when you can no longer see through the solution you know the sulfur has been formed. This gives a clear end point that everyone can judge, so you can easily time how long it takes for the sulfur precipitate to be formed, and that time can be used to compare rates at different temperatures. opaque precipitate clear end point easy to time compare rates"],[25,"ch08-r036","Short answer","In the thiosulfate investigation, suggest how you could keep the test tube at the same temperature while the reaction takes place, and explain why this matters.","8.3","The test tube can be stood in a water bath held at the chosen temperature while the reaction runs, rather than being taken out after heating. This matters because temperature is the independent variable being investigated, so if the mixture cooled during the reaction the actual temperature would not be the one recorded and the times measured would not fairly compare the temperatures. water bath keep in bath during reaction temperature is the variable otherwise recorded value is wrong"],[25,"ch08-r037","Short answer","Explain what preliminary work is and how it improves a rate of reaction investigation.","8.3","Preliminary work is a trial run carried out before the real investigation. It shows whether the chosen concentrations and temperatures give times that are long enough to measure but not so long that the experiment cannot be completed, and it reveals practical problems such as how to judge the end point. The method and the range of values can then be adjusted before the real results are collected. trial run check range of values times measurable find practical problems improve method"],[25,"ch08-r038","Short answer","A graph compares the same reaction at two temperatures. Explain why the higher temperature line rises more steeply but finishes at the same height.","8.3","You do not get any more of the product, carbon dioxide gas, at the higher temperature. You get the same volume of gas but in less time. The amounts of reactant are unchanged, so the same number of particles react and the same volume of gas is finally produced, but the reaction happens faster so the line is steeper and levels out sooner. same volume of gas in less time same amount of reactant steeper line levels out sooner"],[25,"ch08-r039","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain in detail why increasing the temperature increases the rate of reaction.","8.3","Particles move all the time. When the temperature of the reaction is increased, the particles move faster. Because they are moving faster they collide more often, and each collision also happens with more energy, so a greater proportion of collisions have enough energy to react. More frequent collisions and a higher proportion of successful ones both increase the rate, so the higher the temperature the faster the rate of reaction. particles move faster collide more often collide with more energy more successful collisions faster rate"],[25,"ch08-r040","Short answer","A reaction is carried out at 30 degrees Celsius and at 40 degrees Celsius. Predict the shape of the line if the same experiment were done at 50 degrees Celsius.","8.3","The line for 50 degrees Celsius would be steeper than both of the others at the start and would level out earliest, because the particles move fastest and collide most often and with most energy. It would level out at the same height as the other two lines, because the same amounts of reactant are used so the same total volume of gas is finally produced. steepest line levels out earliest same final height same amounts of reactant"],[25,"ch08-r041","Short answer","Students plan to investigate temperature from 20 to 70 degrees Celsius with an interval of 10 degrees. Explain why a range of values with regular intervals is used rather than just two temperatures.","8.3","Six evenly spaced values give enough points to plot a graph and see the shape of the relationship between temperature and rate, not just whether one is faster than the other. Regular intervals spread the points evenly across the range, so any trend or curve can be seen clearly and an anomalous point stands out from the pattern. enough points for a graph see the trend evenly spread spot anomalies"],[25,"ch08-r042","Short answer","In the temperature investigation, state which variables must be kept the same and explain why.","8.3","The mass and surface area of the metal, the volume and concentration of the acid, and the way the end point is judged must all be kept the same. Each of these also affects the rate of reaction, so if any of them changed between runs you would not know whether a change in the time taken was caused by the temperature or by that other variable, and the test would not be fair. same mass and surface area of metal same volume and concentration of acid same end point fair test"],[25,"ch08-r043","Short answer","The students stop the stopwatch when the reaction stops. Explain how they can tell that the reaction has stopped.","8.3","The reaction has stopped when no more bubbles of gas are given off and the piece of metal has completely disappeared into the acid. Until then gas is still being produced, so the reaction is still going on, and stopping the watch early would give a time that is too short and a rate that is too high. no more bubbles metal completely gone stopping early gives wrong time"],[25,"ch08-r044","Short answer","Students repeat the temperature investigation using the same mass of metal in powdered form. Describe how the graph would change and explain why.","8.3","Every line would be steeper and each reaction would finish sooner, because the powder has a much larger total surface area so more metal particles are exposed to the acid and collisions are more frequent. The lines would still level out at the same heights as before, because the same mass of metal contains the same number of particles and so produces the same volume of gas. all lines steeper finish sooner larger surface area same final volumes same mass"],[25,"ch08-r045","Short answer","Students are given one bottle of acid but need five different concentrations. Explain how they can make them and how accuracy is ensured.","8.4","They can make up the different concentrations by diluting the acid they have been given, mixing measured volumes of acid with measured volumes of water so that the total is the same each time, for example making up 50 cm3 each time. The liquids must be measured with a measuring cylinder or pipette rather than poured by eye, so that each concentration is known and the comparison between them is valid. dilute with water same total volume each time measure with measuring cylinder known concentrations"],[25,"ch08-r046","Short answer","In the concentration investigation the time taken to collect 25 cm3 of carbon dioxide is measured. Explain why a fixed volume of gas is used rather than a fixed time.","8.4","Timing to a fixed volume gives one clear number for each concentration that can be compared directly, and a shorter time means a faster reaction. It also means every run measures the same part of the reaction, the early fast stage, so the concentrations are compared under the same conditions rather than at different stages of their reactions. fixed volume gives one comparable number shorter time means faster same stage of reaction compared"],[25,"ch08-r047","Short answer","In the concentration investigation, explain how the test is made fair.","8.4","Everything except the concentration of the acid is kept the same: the same mass and size of marble chips, the same volume of acid, the same temperature, the same apparatus and the same volume of gas collected. Each of these would change the rate on its own, so holding them constant means any difference in the time taken must have been caused by the concentration. only concentration changed same mass and size of chips same volume and temperature difference caused by concentration"],[25,"ch08-r048","Short answer","A graph compares a high concentration and a low concentration of acid reacting with marble chips. Explain why both lines level out at the same height.","8.4","You do not get any more product, carbon dioxide gas, at the higher concentration; you get the same volume of gas but more quickly. The marble chips are the limiting reactant and the same mass is used each time, so the same number of carbonate particles react in both experiments and the same total volume of carbon dioxide is finally produced. same volume of gas more quickly same mass of marble same number of particles react"],[25,"ch08-r049","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why increasing the concentration of hydrochloric acid increases the rate of its reaction with calcium carbonate.","8.4","The higher the concentration of hydrochloric acid, the more hydrochloric acid particles there are in a given amount of space. This means that there will be more frequent collisions between hydrochloric acid particles and calcium carbonate particles, so more successful collisions happen each second and the rate of reaction is greater. more particles in a given space more frequent collisions more successful collisions per second greater rate"],[25,"ch08-r050","Short answer","Predict the shape of the line on the graph if the marble chip experiment were repeated with an even less concentrated acid, and justify your prediction.","8.4","The line would be less steep than both existing lines and would take longer to level out, because fewer acid particles in a given space mean less frequent collisions and a slower rate. Provided there is still enough acid to react with all the marble, it would level out at the same height as the others, because the same mass of marble produces the same volume of carbon dioxide. less steep takes longer to level out fewer acid particles same final height if enough acid"],[25,"ch08-r051","Short answer","A manufacturer wants to speed up a chemical process. State three variables that could be changed and explain the limitation the manufacturer faces.","8.4","To speed up a reaction you could increase the temperature, the concentration or the surface area, and all three could be increased at once. The limitation is cost: increasing the temperature can be very expensive because the cost of energy is high, increasing the concentration of the reactants can also be expensive, and increasing the surface area may involve crushing solids, which adds to the costs of production. Manufacturers have to balance the costs against the increase in speed. temperature concentration surface area all cost money balance cost against speed"],[25,"ch08-r052","Short answer","A manufacturer raises the temperature of a reaction and gets the product sooner. Explain why this does not increase the total amount of product made.","8.4","Changing the conditions does not make any more of the product, it just makes it more quickly. The amount of product is fixed by the amounts of reactants used, because each reactant particle can only react once. Raising the temperature makes the collisions more frequent and more energetic so the same reaction finishes sooner, but the same number of particles react. does not make more product just more quickly amount fixed by reactants same number of particles react"],[25,"ch08-r053","Short answer","Define a catalyst and explain why this definition makes catalysts attractive to industry.","8.4","A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a reaction, is unchanged by the reaction, and so the same catalyst can be used over and over again. Because it is not used up, only a small amount is needed and it does not have to be replaced for each batch, so it is usually much cheaper than increasing the temperature of the reaction. speeds up reaction unchanged used over and over cheaper than heating"],[25,"ch08-r054","Short answer","Manganese oxide is insoluble in water. Explain how this property lets you recover it from the test tube and what recovering it demonstrates.","8.4","Because manganese oxide is insoluble it stays as a solid in the mixture, so it can be separated by filtering and then dried. Adding the recovered manganese oxide to another tube of hydrogen peroxide and seeing it work again demonstrates that it was not used up in the first reaction, which is exactly what the definition of a catalyst requires. insoluble so can be filtered recovered and dried works again not used up proves it is a catalyst"],[25,"ch08-r055","Short answer","Hydrogen peroxide breaks down on its own, but manganese oxide is still called a catalyst for the reaction. Explain why.","8.4","Bubbles of oxygen gas are given off as the hydrogen peroxide breaks down slowly on its own, so the reaction can happen without any help. The manganese oxide made the reaction happen faster than if the hydrogen peroxide was left to break down on its own, and at the end of the reaction the manganese oxide was still there and could be used again. Speeding up a reaction that already happens, without being used up, is what a catalyst does. reaction happens anyway manganese oxide makes it faster still there at the end can be used again"],[25,"ch08-r056","Short answer","State what enzymes are and give one example of their use in the human body.","8.4","Enzymes are biological catalysts, that is, catalysts made by living things to increase the rate of the reactions that take place inside their cells. For example, enzymes are used to speed up the breakdown of food in your digestive system so that the small molecules produced can be absorbed into your blood. biological catalysts reactions inside cells digestion small molecules absorbed into blood"],[25,"ch08-r057","Short answer","Explain why all living cells contain the enzyme catalase.","8.4","Hydrogen peroxide is formed as a waste product in many reactions in the cells of plants and animals. It is poisonous, and if it is not broken down into harmless water and oxygen quickly it will kill living cells. Catalase speeds up that breakdown, so the hydrogen peroxide is removed fast enough to keep the cell alive. hydrogen peroxide is a waste product poisonous must be broken down quickly catalase speeds up breakdown"],[25,"ch08-r058","Short answer","In the hydrogen peroxide experiment, pieces of fruit, vegetable and meat all made the reaction much faster. Explain this result.","8.4","Fruit, vegetables and meat are all made of living cells, and all living cells contain the enzyme catalase, which speeds up the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide. Adding any of these tissues therefore adds catalase to the hydrogen peroxide, so the breakdown into water and oxygen happens very quickly and bubbles of oxygen appear rapidly. all made of cells all cells contain catalase catalase added rapid breakdown oxygen bubbles"],[25,"ch08-r059","Short answer","Explain how the enzymes in biological washing powders help to clean clothes.","8.4","The enzymes they contain help to break down stains such as food and blood. These stains are made of large molecules that are not easily washed out, and the enzymes speed up reactions that split them into smaller, more soluble molecules which can then be rinsed away by the water. enzymes break down stains food and blood large molecules split more soluble rinsed away"],[25,"ch08-r060","Short answer","Explain why many cars are fitted with catalytic converters in their exhaust systems.","8.4","The engine produces harmful gases as it burns fuel. Catalytic converters have catalysts inside that speed up reactions that help to get rid of these harmful gases, converting them into less harmful ones before they leave the exhaust. The catalyst is not used up, so it keeps working for the life of the converter without needing to be replaced each journey. harmful gases from engine catalyst speeds up reactions converts to less harmful gases not used up"],[25,"ch08-r061","Short answer","Students may use only a fixed mass of marble chips and a fixed volume of standard acid. State two variables they could still change to get a faster reaction and explain each.","8.4","They could raise the temperature of the acid, because heating makes the particles move faster so they collide more often and with more energy, giving more successful collisions each second. They could also crush the marble chips into smaller pieces, because for the same mass this gives a larger total surface area, so more marble particles are exposed to the acid and collisions are more frequent. increase temperature particles move faster and collide more crush the chips larger surface area more frequent collisions"],[25,"ch08-r062","Short answer","Suggest a practical difficulty that could make the time to collect a fixed volume of gas longer than it should be.","8.4","Gas can leak from the joints of the apparatus, so some carbon dioxide never reaches the measuring device and it takes longer for the reading to reach the required volume. There is also a delay between adding the acid and sealing the flask, during which gas escapes. Both make the measured time too long and the calculated rate too low, and both are reduced by sealing the apparatus well and starting the timer as the bung is fitted. gas leaks at joints delay before sealing time too long rate too low seal well and time from bung"],[25,"ch08-o016","Short answer","Describe what happens when you put magnesium ribbon into a Bunsen flame.","8.2","It reacts very quickly and burns with a bright white flame to form magnesium oxide. bright white flame magnesium oxide quickly"],[25,"ch08-o017","Short answer","A large block of magnesium reacts only very slowly when it is heated in air, but magnesium powder burns straight away. Explain the difference.","8.2","Only the atoms on the surface can react with the oxygen in the air. Almost all the atoms in the large piece are buried inside it, while the powder has a very large total surface area for the same mass, so far more of its atoms are exposed and can react. surface area atoms on the surface inside same mass"],[25,"ch08-o019","Short answer","Magnesium ribbon and a magnesium block have exactly the same mass. Which has the larger total surface area, and why does that matter?","8.2","The ribbon has the larger total surface area. That means more of its atoms are on the surface where they can react, so the ribbon reacts faster. ribbon larger total surface area more atoms react faster"],[25,"ch08-o023","Short answer","An iron nail, iron wool and iron filings are each heated in a Bunsen flame. Put them in order from slowest to fastest reaction, and explain the order.","8.2","The nail reacts slowest, then the iron wool, and the filings react fastest. The filings are the smallest pieces, so for the same mass they have the largest total surface area and the most iron atoms in contact with the oxygen in the air. nail slowest filings fastest largest total surface area in contact with oxygen"],[25,"ch08-o024","Short answer","In the marble chip investigation, what is the independent variable and what is kept the same about the calcium carbonate?","8.2","The independent variable is the size of the pieces of calcium carbonate, and so their total surface area. The total mass of the pieces is kept the same. size of the pieces surface area total mass kept the same"],[25,"ch08-o025","Short answer","Why must the volume, type and concentration of the acid be kept the same in the marble chip investigation?","8.2","Because each of those factors could change the rate of reaction on its own. Surface area is the variable being tested, so it must be the only one that changes. could affect the rate only variable that changes fair test"],[25,"ch08-o027","Short answer","The same mass of calcium carbonate is used with large chips and then with small chips, with the same acid each time. Does the quicker reaction make more carbon dioxide? Explain.","8.2","No. Both experiments produce the same total volume of carbon dioxide, because the amount of reactant is the same. The smaller chips simply produce it more quickly. no same volume more quickly same amount of reactant"],[25,"ch08-o028","Short answer","What happens to the rate of a reaction if you change the temperature of the reactants?","8.3","The rate of reaction changes. Raising the temperature makes the reaction faster and lowering it makes the reaction slower. rate of reaction will change higher temperature faster"],[25,"ch08-o030","Short answer","Why is a black cross drawn on a card and placed under the test tube in the sodium thiosulfate experiment?","8.3","The sulfur precipitate is opaque, so it hides the cross. You time how long it takes until you can no longer see the cross through the solution, which tells you when enough sulfur has formed. opaque can no longer see time end point"],[25,"ch08-o031","Short answer","Why is the sodium thiosulfate reaction a good one for investigating reaction rates?","8.3","Because you can easily time how long it takes for the sulfur precipitate to be formed, which gives a clear and simple measurement of how fast the reaction went. easily time sulfur precipitate clear end point"],[25,"ch08-o032","Short answer","Give two safety precautions needed for the sodium thiosulfate and hydrochloric acid experiment, and say why each is needed.","8.3","The room must be well ventilated because sulfur dioxide gas is produced. Used solutions are put into a container of solid sodium hydrogen carbonate, which reacts with the sulfur dioxide, for the teacher to remove later. well ventilated sulfur dioxide sodium hydrogen carbonate"],[25,"ch08-o033","Short answer","What is a trial run, and what is it for?","8.3","A trial run is a practice experiment carried out to make sure you can do the experiment safely and effectively, so you find and solve the problems before the real investigation. practice experiment safely and effectively find problems"],[25,"ch08-o034","Short answer","What is the preliminary work for in this investigation?","8.3","It is done to decide which temperatures to use, including how big a range and how large an interval is needed to give a difference in reaction time that can actually be measured. decide which temperatures range interval"],[25,"ch08-o038","Short answer","Use particle theory to explain why a reaction goes faster at a higher temperature.","8.3","Particles move all the time, and when the temperature is increased they move faster. They therefore collide more often, and with more energy, so more of the collisions lead to a reaction and the rate goes up. move faster collide more often more energy"],[25,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","In the reaction between magnesium ribbon and dilute sulfuric acid, which gas is produced?","8.1","Hydrogen Oxygen Carbon dioxide Nitrogen"],[25,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","The rate of reaction can be measured by working out how much product is made or reactant is used up in a given ____.","8.1","time"],[25,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","What does the slope or gradient of a line on a rate of reaction graph tell you?","8.1","How quickly the reaction is taking place The total mass of the reactants The temperature of the flask The color change of the solution"],[25,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","A result that does not fit the pattern of other results is called an anomalous ____.","8.1","result"],[25,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","Why does the rate of reaction slow down as a chemical reaction proceeds?","8.1","The temperature decreases There are fewer unreacted particles to collide The gas escapes too quickly The reactants turn into solids"],[25,"qwen-02-01","Multiple choice","Why does magnesium powder burn faster than a large block of magnesium?","8.2","The powder has more atoms on the surface available to react The powder is hotter than the block The block has more oxygen inside it The powder reacts with nitrogen instead of oxygen"],[25,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","When you cut a solid into smaller pieces, the total ____ increases.","8.2","surface area"],[25,"qwen-02-03","Multiple choice","In the reaction between hydrochloric acid and sodium thiosulfate, what substance forms a cloudy precipitate?","8.3","Sulfur Carbon dioxide Hydrogen gas Sodium chloride"],[25,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","In the temperature experiment, you know sulfur has formed when you can no longer see through the ____.","8.3","solution"],[25,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","What happens to the particles in a reaction when the temperature is increased?","8.3","They move faster and collide more often They stop moving completely They become larger in size They move slower and collide less often"],[25,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","If you increase the temperature of a reaction, the rate of reaction becomes ____.","8.3","faster"],[25,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","In the experiment with marble chips and acid, why must you keep the volume and concentration of the acid the same?","8.2","To make sure only the size of the chips is changed Because the acid changes color at different volumes To make the reaction produce more gas Because larger chips need less acid"],[25,"qwen-02-08","Gap fill","A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up is called a ____.","8.4","catalyst"],[25,"qwen-02-11","Multiple choice","Why is a trial run important before doing the final investigation?","8.3","To make sure you can do the experiment safely and effectively To use up all your chemicals To get a higher grade on the test To change the temperature of the room"],[25,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the chemical name for marble?","8.4","Calcium carbonate Sodium chloride Magnesium oxide Hydrogen peroxide"],[25,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","A substance that speeds up a reaction but is unchanged by it is called a ____.","8.4","catalyst"],[25,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","Why do manufacturers often prefer using a catalyst over increasing the temperature?","8.4","It is usually much cheaper It produces more product It makes the reaction stop faster It changes the colour of the gas"],[25,"qwen-03-05","Multiple choice","What gas is produced when hydrogen peroxide decomposes?","8.4","Oxygen Carbon dioxide Hydrogen Nitrogen"],[25,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What gas is produced when magnesium ribbon reacts with hydrochloric acid?","8.1","Hydrogen Oxygen Carbon dioxide Nitrogen"],[25,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","In the reaction between magnesium and hydrochloric acid, the gas that is collected in a syringe is called ____.","8.1","hydrogen"],[25,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","What does the slope of the line on a rate of reaction graph tell you?","8.1","The temperature of the acid The rate of reaction The mass of magnesium used The volume of the container"],[25,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","If the line on a graph is steeper, it means the reaction is happening ____.","8.1","faster"],[25,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","Which piece of equipment is used to measure the volume of gas collected in the experiment?","8.1","A syringe A beaker A thermometer A balance"],[25,"qwenw-02-01","Multiple choice","In Zara's investigation, what was the independent variable?","8.2","The mass of the zinc The surface area of the zinc The volume of gas collected The type of acid used"],[25,"qwenw-02-02","Gap fill","Zara placed the zinc in ____ to carry out the reaction.","8.2","hydrochloric acid"],[25,"qwenw-02-03","Multiple choice","How did Zara collect the gas produced in the reaction?","8.2","In a test tube Over water in a measuring cylinder Using a gas syringe In a balloon"],[25,"qwenw-02-04","Gap fill","The two pieces of zinc used in the experiment had the same ____.","8.2","mass"],[25,"qwenw-02-07","Multiple choice","Which two forms of zinc did Zara compare in her investigation?","8.2","A powder and a lump A flat piece and a lump Two lumps of different sizes A wire and a sheet"],[25,"qwenw-02-08","Gap fill","The gas collected in the experiment was produced by reacting zinc with ____.","8.2","hydrochloric acid"],[25,"qwenw-03-01","Multiple choice","What must happen for a chemical reaction to take place?","8.3","Particles of the reactants must collide with enough energy The temperature must be exactly 20 degrees Celsius The products must collide with the reactants The particles must stop moving completely"],[25,"qwenw-03-02","Gap fill","The chemicals you have at the start of a reaction are called ____.","8.3","reactants"],[25,"qwenw-03-03","Multiple choice","What are the products of a reaction?","8.3","The chemicals formed after the reaction has taken place The chemicals you have at the start of the reaction The energy released during the collision The container used to hold the chemicals"],[25,"qwenw-03-04","Gap fill","When there are many particles, they collide with each other ____ often.","8.3","more"],[25,"qwenw-05-01","Multiple choice","In the investigation by Zara and Sofia, which substance is mixed with zinc to test the effect of concentration?","8.4","Dilute sulfuric acid Dilute hydrochloric acid Water only Concentrated sulfuric acid"],[25,"qwenw-05-02","Gap fill","Zara and Sofia keep the volume of acid, mass of zinc, and temperature the same to ensure a fair test. The variable they are changing is the ____ of the acid.","8.4","concentration"],[25,"qwenw-05-03","Multiple choice","Arun collected hydrogen gas from a reaction between magnesium and which acid?","8.4","Sulfuric acid Hydrochloric acid Nitric acid Citric acid"],[25,"qwenw-05-04","Gap fill","In Arun's investigation, the volume of hydrogen gas produced was measured every ____ seconds.","8.4","30"],[26,"ch09-f01a","Diagram labelling","This parallel circuit has four ammeters. Give the label of the ammeter at position {n}.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f01h1","Click the diagram","Click on the A4 in the diagram.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f01h2","Click the diagram","Click on ammeter A1.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f01h3","Click the diagram","Click on ammeter A2.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f02a","Diagram labelling","State the ammeter reading shown at position {n} in this parallel circuit.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f02h1","Click the diagram","Click on the ammeter reading that gives the current through the cell.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f02h2","Click the diagram","Click on the 1.0 A in the diagram.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f03a","Diagram labelling","State the ammeter reading shown at position {n}. (The unknown branch current X is not asked for here.)","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f03h1","Click the diagram","Click on the reading that shows the total current in this circuit.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f03h2","Click the diagram","Click on the unknown branch current.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f04a","Diagram labelling","Give the label of the component at position {n} in this two-branch parallel circuit.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f04h1","Click the diagram","Click on the L1 in the diagram.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f04h2","Click the diagram","Click on the switch that controls lamp L1.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f05a","Diagram labelling","Give the label of the component at position {n} in this three-branch parallel circuit.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f05h1","Click the diagram","Click on the switch that must be closed before any lamp in this circuit can light.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f05h2","Click the diagram","Click on the L1 in the diagram.","9.1",""],[26,"ch09-f06a","Diagram labelling","State what is shown at position {n} in this series circuit.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f06h1","Click the diagram","Click on the 12 V in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f06h2","Click the diagram","Click on the supply for this series circuit.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f06h3","Click the diagram","Click on the voltmeter reading for the left-hand lamp.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f07a","Diagram labelling","State the voltage shown at position {n} in this circuit.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f07h1","Click the diagram","Click on the 12 V in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f07h2","Click the diagram","Click on the voltage across the left-hand lamp.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f08a","Diagram labelling","State the meter reading shown at position {n}.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f08h1","Click the diagram","Click on the 6 V in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f08h2","Click the diagram","Click on the 2 A in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f09a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f09h1","Click the diagram","Click on the lamp 1 in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f09h2","Click the diagram","Click on lamp 2.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f10a","Diagram labelling","Give the label printed at position {n} in this series circuit.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f10h1","Click the diagram","Click on the 9 V in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f10h2","Click the diagram","Click on the L1 in the diagram.","9.2",""],[26,"ch09-f11a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f11h1","Click the diagram","Click on the V in the diagram.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f11h2","Click the diagram","Click on the symbol for current in the Ohm's law triangle.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f11h3","Click the diagram","Click on the symbol for resistance in the Ohm's law triangle.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f12a","Diagram labelling","State the value printed at position {n}.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f12h1","Click the diagram","Click on the 2 A in the diagram.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f12h2","Click the diagram","Click on the resistance value in the upper circuit.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f12h3","Click the diagram","Click on the voltage across the 100 ohm resistor.","9.3",""],[26,"ch09-f13a","Diagram labelling","Name the part labelled {n} in the diagram.","9.4",""],[26,"ch09-f13h1","Click the diagram","Click on the To rest of circuit in the diagram.","9.4",""],[26,"ch09-f13h2","Click the diagram","Click on the 12 V in the diagram.","9.4",""],[26,"ch09-f13h3","Click the diagram","Click on the lamp that runs at half the battery voltage.","9.4",""],[26,"ch09-r001","Short answer","Explain why the current is the same all the way around a series circuit.","9.1","In a series circuit all the components are connected end-to-end, one after the other, so there is only one path for the current to flow. The current has nowhere else to go, so every charge that passes one point must pass every other point, which means the current is the same all the way around a series circuit. connected end to end only one path nowhere else to go current same everywhere"],[26,"ch09-r002","Short answer","A lamp and a buzzer are in series with one switch. Explain why opening the switch stops both of them at the same instant.","9.1","There is only one path for the current in a series circuit, and the switch is part of that path. Opening the switch breaks the path, so the current in the whole circuit stops flowing. Since both components need that same current to work, they both stop operating at the same time. one path switch breaks the path current stops everywhere both stop together"],[26,"ch09-r003","Short answer","Define a parallel circuit and explain where the name comes from.","9.1","In a parallel circuit there is more than one path for the current to flow, and the paths where the current can flow are called branches. The name parallel comes from the circuit diagram, because the branches are drawn parallel to each other, and the components in each of the branches are said to be connected in parallel. more than one path branches drawn parallel in diagram"],[26,"ch09-r004","Short answer","Describe what happens to the current at a branch in a parallel circuit and where it goes afterwards.","9.1","Current from the cell flows to the branch in the circuit, and at the branch the current is divided between the paths. If the two lamps are the same, the current will be divided equally between them. When the current comes to the other side where the branches join again, the current combines and adds together again before returning to the cell. current divided at branch equal if components identical combines where branches join returns to cell"],[26,"ch09-r005","Short answer","State the rule for current in a parallel circuit and explain why it must be true.","9.1","The rule is that the current through the cell is equal to the total of the current in all the branches. It must be true because the current divides at the branch and recombines where the branches meet again, so all the charge that leaves the cell travels through one branch or another and all of it returns. None of it is used up or created along the way. current through cell total of branch currents divides and recombines nothing used up"],[26,"ch09-r006","Short answer","In a circuit with ammeters A1 and A4 outside the branches and A2 and A3 in the two branches, state how the four readings are related.","9.1","The readings on ammeters A1 and A4 are equal, because both are outside the branches on the single path to and from the cell. The readings on ammeters A2 and A3 will add up to the reading on A1, and therefore also to the reading on A4, because the current divides between the two branches and recombines afterwards. A1 equals A4 A2 plus A3 equals A1 divides and recombines"],[26,"ch09-r007","Short answer","A parallel circuit has three branches, each carrying a different current. Explain whether the parallel current rule still applies.","9.1","The rule still works even when the current in the branches is different. The current through the cell is equal to the total of the currents through each of the branches, whatever those individual values are. Equal division only happens when the components are identical; the rule about the total does not depend on that. rule still works total of all branches equal division only if identical"],[26,"ch09-r008","Short answer","The current through the cell is 5.5 A. Two branches carry 3.0 A and 1.5 A, and the current through a buzzer in the third branch is unknown. Calculate the missing current and explain the method.","9.1","The currents through each of the branches must add up to the total current of 5.5 A. Calling the missing current X gives 3.0 A plus 1.5 A plus X equals 5.5 A, so X equals 5.5 A minus 4.5 A, which is 1.0 A. The missing current through the buzzer is 1.0 A, and the unit must always be given with the answer. branches add to total 5.5 minus 4.5 1.0 A give the unit"],[26,"ch09-r009","Short answer","State the main practical advantage of a parallel circuit and explain how it arises.","9.1","In a parallel circuit the current through a branch can keep flowing even if the current stops flowing in the other branches, because each branch is a separate path back to the cell. This means components in the same circuit can be switched on and off independently, and if a component in one branch stops working, the other branches are not affected. branches are separate paths independent switching one failure does not affect others"],[26,"ch09-r010","Short answer","Two lamps L1 and L2 are on separate branches, each with its own switch. Explain why closing switch S1 lights L1 without affecting L2.","9.1","When switch S1 is closed, a complete path is made through lamp L1 and current flows, so L1 lights. This will not affect lamp L2 because L2 is on a different branch, which has its own separate path to the cell and its own switch. The state of one branch does not change whether the other branch is complete. closing S1 completes L1 branch L2 on a different branch separate path unaffected"],[26,"ch09-r011","Short answer","Both lamps in a two-branch parallel circuit are lit, and then lamp L1 breaks. Explain what happens to lamp L2.","9.1","Lamp L2 will not be affected and will stay lit. The break in L1 only breaks the path through its own branch, so no current flows in that branch, but the branch containing L2 is still a complete path from one terminal of the cell to the other, so current continues to flow through L2. L2 stays lit break only affects its own branch other branch still complete"],[26,"ch09-r012","Short answer","Explain the difference between putting a switch on one branch of a parallel circuit and putting it between the cell and the branches.","9.1","A switch on one branch only breaks that branch, so components in a parallel circuit can be switched on and off separately by having switches on each branch. A switch between the cell and the branches is on the single path that all the current must take, so opening it stops the current to every branch and the components are all switched on or off together. switch on a branch controls that branch switch before branches controls all single path to cell"],[26,"ch09-r013","Short answer","In a circuit with lamps controlled by switches S1, S2 and S3, and a switch S4 between the cell and the branches, explain what S4 does.","9.1","For any lamp to light in this circuit, switch S4 must be closed, because it lies on the single path that all the current takes on its way to the branches. Switches S1, S2 and S3 can then be used to control each lamp separately. If all the lamps are on, opening S4 will cause all of the lamps to go off at once. S4 must be closed for any lamp on the single path S1 to S3 control lamps separately S4 switches all off"],[26,"ch09-r014","Short answer","Car headlamps and a string of decorative lamps are both wired in parallel. Explain the advantage this gives in each case.","9.1","Car headlamps are connected in parallel so they can be switched on and off together by one switch placed before the branches, and if one headlamp fails the other still works. The string of lamps is connected in parallel so that, if one lamp fails, the others will still light, and they can also be switched on and off together. headlamps switched together one failure does not stop the other string keeps lighting switched together"],[26,"ch09-r015","Short answer","Four identical lamps are connected in parallel with a cell, and the current through the cell is 2.0 A. Calculate the current through each lamp and explain your reasoning.","9.1","The current through the cell is equal to the total of the currents in all the branches, and because the four lamps are identical the current will be divided equally between them. Dividing 2.0 A by 4 gives 0.5 A, so the current through each lamp is 0.5 A. total divided between branches identical so equal shares 2.0 divided by 4 0.5 A"],[26,"ch09-r016","Short answer","Give two advantages of connecting four lamps in parallel rather than in series.","9.1","First, each lamp can be switched on and off separately by putting a switch in its own branch, whereas in series one switch controls them all. Second, if one lamp stops working the others are not affected, because each branch is a separate path, whereas in a series circuit a single break stops the current everywhere and all four lamps go out. separate switching one failure does not affect others series break stops everything"],[26,"ch09-r017","Short answer","In the parallel circuit practical you are told to check that all the lamps work correctly before including the ammeter. Explain why this order is sensible.","9.1","If the ammeter is added first and a lamp does not light, you cannot tell whether the fault is in the lamp, the wiring or the ammeter itself. Checking that all the lamps are working correctly first means the circuit is known to be sound, so any later problem can be traced to the ammeter or its connections, and the readings taken can be trusted. test circuit first isolate the fault known working circuit readings can be trusted"],[26,"ch09-r018","Short answer","In a parallel circuit with two lamps and a buzzer, the buzzer stops working so ammeter A3 in its branch reads zero. State the effect on the readings of the ammeters in the other branches and on the total.","9.1","The readings on the ammeters in the lamp branches are unchanged, because those branches are still complete paths across the same supply and nothing about them has altered. The reading on the ammeter measuring the total current falls, because the total current through the cell is the sum of the branch currents and one of those has become zero. branch ammeters unchanged branches independent total current falls sum of branches"],[26,"ch09-r019","Short answer","State what voltage measures and give its unit.","9.2","Voltage is a measure of the electrical energy in a circuit. It can measure the electrical energy supplied to a circuit by the cell, battery or power supply, and it can also measure the energy changed by components in the circuit. Voltage is measured in units called volts, whose symbol is V. measure of electrical energy energy supplied or changed volts symbol V"],[26,"ch09-r020","Short answer","Explain the difference between a cell and a battery.","9.2","A battery is two or more cells connected in series. A single cell supplies one fixed voltage, such as 1.5 V, while connecting cells in series adds their voltages together, so batteries commonly supply 6 V, 9 V or 12 V. A 12 V battery, for example, contains eight cells each of 1.5 V. battery is cells in series voltages add 6 V 9 V 12 V eight 1.5 V cells make 12 V"],[26,"ch09-r021","Short answer","Explain what it means to say that a lamp has a voltage across it.","9.2","Voltage can measure the energy changed by components in a circuit. Lamps change electrical energy into light and thermal energy, and the energy that a lamp changes can be measured in volts. So the voltage across a lamp is a measure of how much energy each bit of charge gives up as it passes through that lamp. energy changed by the component electrical to light and thermal measured in volts"],[26,"ch09-r022","Short answer","Lamps used in school experiments have a voltage rating. Explain what the rating tells you and why it matters.","9.2","The rating tells us the maximum voltage that can be used across that component. If a higher voltage is applied the component changes more energy than it was designed for, so it will overheat and be damaged or burn out. Checking the rating before connecting a component is therefore a safety and equipment precaution. maximum voltage more energy changed than designed component damaged check before connecting"],[26,"ch09-r023","Short answer","Explain why an ammeter is connected in series with a component but a voltmeter is connected in parallel with it.","9.2","An ammeter measures the current flowing through a component, so it must be placed in the same path as that component in order for the same current to pass through it, which means connecting it in series. A voltmeter measures the energy difference either side of a component, so it must be connected to both sides at once, which means connecting it in parallel with the component. ammeter measures current through same path so in series voltmeter measures across both sides so in parallel"],[26,"ch09-r024","Short answer","Explain why the voltages across the components in a series circuit must add up to the voltage of the supply.","9.2","Energy is always conserved, so the energy changed by the components in a circuit must be equal to the energy supplied by the cell, battery or power supply. Since voltage is a measure of energy, this means the voltages across each component in a series circuit must add up to the voltage of the supply. No energy can appear or vanish along the way. energy is conserved energy changed equals energy supplied voltage measures energy voltages add to supply"],[26,"ch09-r025","Short answer","Three identical lamps are connected in series with a battery. Explain how the supply voltage is shared between them.","9.2","The lamps are identical, so they change the same quantity of energy and therefore have the same voltage across each of them. The voltage across all the lamps adds up to the voltage from the battery, so each lamp has one third of the supply voltage across it. identical lamps same energy changed same voltage each voltages add to supply one third each"],[26,"ch09-r026","Short answer","A lamp and a buzzer are in series. The lamp changes more energy than the buzzer. Compare the voltages across them and the currents through them.","9.2","The lamp is changing more energy than the buzzer, so the voltage across the lamp is higher than across the buzzer, and the two voltages add up to the voltage of the battery. The current, however, is the same through both, because in a series circuit there is only one path and the current is the same all the way around. lamp has higher voltage voltages add to battery current the same in both one path"],[26,"ch09-r027","Short answer","Explain what happens to the voltage across each lamp when more lamps are added to a series circuit.","9.2","The voltage from the supply in a series circuit is shared between each of the components, and the total is fixed by the supply. Adding components such as lamps or buzzers means the same supply voltage has to be divided among more of them, so each component gets a smaller share of the voltage and the lamps are dimmer. supply voltage is shared total fixed more components smaller share each dimmer"],[26,"ch09-r028","Short answer","Explain why adding more components in series decreases the current in the circuit.","9.2","As components are added, it becomes more difficult for the power supply to push the electrons around the circuit, because each extra component adds resistance to the single path. With the supply voltage unchanged and more opposition to the flow, the current falls, so the circuit with more components has a smaller current. more components add resistance harder to push electrons supply voltage unchanged smaller current"],[26,"ch09-r029","Short answer","Explain how adding cells in series changes a circuit, and calculate the supply voltage of two 1.5 V cells in series.","9.2","Adding more cells in a series circuit increases the voltage of the supply, because the cells push in the same direction and their voltages add. Two 1.5 V cells give a supply voltage of 2 times 1.5 V, which is 3 V. Increasing the number of cells will also increase the current in the circuit and increase the voltage across each component. cell voltages add 2 times 1.5 V equals 3 V increases current increases voltage across components"],[26,"ch09-r030","Short answer","In a parallel circuit, lamp 2 is not connected directly to the cell terminals on the diagram. Explain why the voltage across it is still equal to the cell voltage.","9.2","If you follow the wires from lamp 2 you can get to the terminals of the cell without going through lamp 1, so there is nothing else in that path to change any energy. That means lamp 2 can be thought of as being connected directly across the terminals of the cell, so the voltage across lamp 2 is the same as the voltage of the cell. trace the wires no other component in the path effectively across the terminals same as cell voltage"],[26,"ch09-r031","Short answer","State the rule for voltage in a parallel circuit and give an example.","9.2","The voltages across each of the branches of a parallel circuit are equal to the voltage of the supply. For example, with a 9 V battery, the voltage across each lamp in a two-branch parallel circuit is also 9 V. This holds even when the branches contain different components, so a lamp and a buzzer on separate branches both have 9 V across them. each branch equals supply voltage 9 V battery gives 9 V per branch true even for different components"],[26,"ch09-r032","Short answer","Compare how current and voltage behave in the branches of a parallel circuit.","9.2","The voltage in all branches of a parallel circuit is the same and equal to the supply, because each branch connects across the same two terminals. The current, however, can be different in the branches, because the current can flow in different paths and each branch takes a share that depends on what it contains. voltage same in all branches current can differ different paths share depends on the branch"],[26,"ch09-r033","Short answer","Explain why adding more branches to a parallel circuit increases the current through the cell.","9.2","Adding more branches to a parallel circuit gives more paths for the current to flow through. The more paths there are for current to flow through, the easier it becomes for the supply to drive charge around, so the total current through the cell increases even though the current in each existing branch is unchanged. more paths easier for current to flow total current through cell increases existing branches unchanged"],[26,"ch09-r034","Short answer","Explain why adding more components to one branch of a parallel circuit decreases the current in that branch.","9.2","The voltage across any branch stays the same, because it is fixed by the supply. Adding more components in that branch makes it harder for current to flow in that branch, because each component adds resistance. With the same voltage pushing against greater opposition, the current in that branch decreases, while the other branches are unaffected. branch voltage unchanged more components add resistance harder for current current in that branch falls others unaffected"],[26,"ch09-r035","Short answer","State three effects of adding cells to a parallel circuit and explain why they happen.","9.2","Adding cells increases the supply voltage, because the cell voltages add together. This increases the voltage across each branch, since every branch has the supply voltage across it. The larger voltage pushes a larger current through each branch, and since the current through the cell is the total of the branch currents, the current through the cell increases as well. supply voltage increases voltage across each branch increases current in each branch increases total current increases"],[26,"ch09-r036","Short answer","Six identical lamps are connected in parallel across a 220 V mains supply. State the voltage across one lamp and explain your answer.","9.2","The voltage across one lamp is 220 V. Each lamp is in its own branch of a parallel circuit, and the voltages across each of the branches of a parallel circuit are equal to the voltage of the supply, so every lamp has the full 220 V across it regardless of how many lamps there are. 220 V each branch equals supply number of lamps does not matter"],[26,"ch09-r046","Short answer","Define resistance and compare the resistance of conductors and insulators.","9.3","Resistance is a measure of how easy or difficult it is for electrons to move through a material. Conductors, such as copper, have very low resistance, so electrons move through them easily. Insulators, such as most plastics, have very high resistance, so electrons can hardly move through them at all. how easy electrons move conductors low resistance insulators high resistance copper and plastic"],[26,"ch09-r047","Short answer","A 100 m length of copper wire has a resistance of about 1 ohm while 1 cm of some plastics has a resistance of over a million million ohms. Explain what this comparison shows.","9.3","It shows how enormous the difference between conductors and insulators is. A huge length of copper still barely opposes the flow of electrons, while a tiny thickness of plastic opposes it almost completely. This is why copper is used for the wires and plastic is used to cover them, and why the insulation does not allow current to flow at all under most circumstances. huge difference long copper still low resistance thin plastic enormous resistance wire and insulation"],[26,"ch09-r048","Short answer","Explain why the connecting wires in a circuit are made of copper.","9.3","Copper wire is designed to have very low resistance so that current is affected as little as possible between the components of a circuit. If the wires had a high resistance they would reduce the current and change energy themselves, so the readings would not reflect what the components are doing and the components would receive less voltage than intended. very low resistance current barely affected wires should not change energy components work as intended"],[26,"ch09-r049","Short answer","Explain how a filament lamp produces light, and why the filament is designed to have a high resistance.","9.3","Inside the lamp there is a small wire called a filament which has a high resistance. When current flows through the filament, thermal energy is transferred, and because the resistance is high there is so much thermal energy that the filament glows and emits light. A low resistance filament would not get hot enough to glow. filament has high resistance current flows thermal energy transferred glows and emits light"],[26,"ch09-r050","Short answer","State the relationship between resistance and current in a circuit, and explain it.","9.3","The greater the resistance in a circuit, the smaller the current in the circuit. Resistance slows the flow of electrons, so for the same voltage pushing them, fewer electrons pass each point every second when the resistance is higher, which means a smaller current. greater resistance smaller current resistance slows electrons same voltage"],[26,"ch09-r051","Short answer","State Ohm's law in words and in symbols, and say what each symbol means.","9.3","Ohm's law states that voltage equals current multiplied by resistance, written as V equals I times R. In this equation V is the voltage in volts, I is the current in amps and R is the resistance in ohms. The letter I is used for current because in French current was originally called intensite du courant. voltage = current x resistance V = I x R V volts I amps R ohms"],[26,"ch09-r052","Short answer","Ohm studied resistance in wires. Explain why his law is useful far beyond the wires he studied.","9.3","The link between voltage, current and resistance applies to many electrical components and not only to wires. That means the same equation can be used to calculate the resistance of a lamp, a buzzer or a resistor from measurements of the voltage across it and the current through it, which makes it one equation for a whole range of components. applies to many components not only wires one equation for many cases calculate resistance from V and I"],[26,"ch09-r053","Short answer","The voltage across resistor R1 is 6 V and the current through it is 3 A. Calculate its resistance, showing the method.","9.3","Ohm's law says voltage equals current multiplied by resistance, so resistance equals voltage divided by current. Dividing 6 V by 3 A gives a resistance of 2 ohms. The unit must always be given with the answer. R = V divided by I 6 divided by 3 2 ohms give the unit"],[26,"ch09-r054","Short answer","A circuit contains a 6 ohm resistor with a current of 2 A through it. Calculate the voltage across the resistor and explain the method.","9.3","Ohm's law in the form voltage equals current multiplied by resistance can be used directly here, because both the current and the resistance are known. Multiplying 2 A by 6 ohms gives a voltage of 12 V across the resistor. V = I x R 2 times 6 12 V no rearranging needed"],[26,"ch09-r055","Short answer","A 100 ohm resistor has 12 V across it. Calculate the current through it.","9.3","Ohm's law says voltage equals current multiplied by resistance, so current equals voltage divided by resistance. Dividing 12 V by 100 ohms gives a current of 0.12 A. The answer is much less than one amp because the resistance is large. I = V divided by R 12 divided by 100 0.12 A large resistance small current"],[26,"ch09-r056","Short answer","In the practical you measure the current through and voltage across each component, then calculate its resistance. Explain how the resistance is obtained and why both measurements are needed.","9.3","Resistance is calculated by dividing the voltage across the component by the current through it, using Ohm's law rearranged. Both measurements are needed because resistance is not measured directly by either meter: the same current can occur with different voltages depending on the component, so only the pair of values identifies the resistance. divide voltage by current Ohm's law rearranged neither meter measures resistance need both values"],[26,"ch09-r057","Short answer","In the experiment where cells are added one at a time, you plot voltage on the horizontal axis and current on the vertical axis. Describe the expected trend and explain it.","9.3","As more cells are added the supply voltage increases and the current increases with it, so the points rise steadily and the most appropriate line is a straight line through the origin. This happens because the resistance of the circuit is unchanged, so by Ohm's law the current is proportional to the voltage pushing the electrons around. current increases with voltage straight line through origin resistance unchanged current proportional to voltage"],[26,"ch09-r058","Short answer","Sketch, in words, how the current against voltage graph would differ if a resistor with a higher resistance were used, and explain why.","9.3","The line would still be straight and pass through the origin, but it would be less steep, because for each value of voltage the current would be smaller. This follows from Ohm's law: current equals voltage divided by resistance, so a larger resistance gives a smaller current at every voltage. A lower resistance would give a steeper line. still straight through origin less steep smaller current at each voltage lower resistance steeper"],[26,"ch09-r059","Short answer","A resistor is connected in series with a cell, and an identical resistor is added in series. State how the current changes and explain using Ohm's law.","9.3","The current decreases, and for two identical resistors it falls to half its original value. Adding a resistor in series doubles the total resistance of the circuit while the supply voltage stays the same, and current equals voltage divided by resistance, so doubling the resistance halves the current. current decreases halves resistance doubled voltage unchanged I = V divided by R"],[26,"ch09-r060","Short answer","Explain what a variable resistor is and give two everyday uses.","9.4","A variable resistor is the component used to change the resistance in a circuit, rather than having a fixed value. It is used in a dimmer switch for a lamp, where turning the control changes the brightness, and in the volume control of a music player, where sliding or turning it changes the loudness from the loudspeakers or headphones. changes resistance dimmer switch volume control brightness and loudness"],[26,"ch09-r061","Short answer","Describe the circuit symbol for a variable resistor and explain why the term fixed resistor is sometimes used.","9.4","The circuit symbol for a variable resistor is similar to that for a fixed resistor, a rectangle, but with an arrow drawn through the symbol to show that its value can be changed. The term fixed resistor is sometimes used for the ordinary type of resistor to avoid confusion with variable resistors when both appear in the same circuit. rectangle with arrow through it arrow shows it can be changed fixed resistor avoids confusion"],[26,"ch09-r062","Short answer","Explain the full chain of reasoning from turning up a variable resistor to the lamp becoming dimmer.","9.4","As the resistance of the variable resistor is increased, it becomes more difficult for the electrons to move, so the current in the circuit decreases. A smaller current flowing through the lamp means less energy is transferred in the filament each second, so the lamp glows less brightly and becomes dimmer. resistance increased current decreases smaller current through lamp dimmer"],[26,"ch09-r063","Short answer","Explain what happens to a lamp when the resistance of a variable resistor in series with it is decreased.","9.4","As the resistance of the variable resistor is decreased, it becomes easier for current to flow, so the current in the circuit increases. A larger current flowing through the lamp transfers more energy in the filament each second, so the lamp becomes brighter. resistance decreased current increases larger current brighter"],[26,"ch09-r064","Short answer","Describe how the switch in a fridge light circuit is operated and explain why it behaves the way it does.","9.4","The switch is opened and closed by the movement of the fridge door. When the door is open, the switch is closed, so the circuit is complete and the light comes on. When the door is closed, the switch is open, so the circuit is broken and the light goes off. The switch is arranged this way so the light only uses energy when someone can actually see inside. door operates the switch door open means switch closed door closed means switch open light only on when needed"],[26,"ch09-r065","Short answer","In a car, a buzzer sounds when the door is opened while the headlamps are on. Explain how the circuit achieves this.","9.4","Switch S1 controls the headlamps and switch S2 is controlled by the door, and the buzzer is on the same branch of the parallel circuit as the headlamps. Current cannot flow to switch S2 unless switch S1 for the headlamps is closed, so the buzzer can only sound when the headlamps are already on and the door is then opened. S1 controls headlamps S2 controlled by door buzzer on headlamp branch both switches must be closed"],[26,"ch09-r066","Short answer","Explain how a battery voltage indicator in a vehicle is wired and why a switch is included with it.","9.4","A voltmeter is connected in parallel across the battery, because a voltmeter must be connected across whatever it measures, and a switch is included in this branch of the parallel circuit. The switch lets the driver connect the voltmeter only when a reading is wanted, so the voltmeter branch does not draw current from the battery all the time and slowly discharge it. voltmeter in parallel across battery switch in that branch connect only when needed avoid draining the battery"],[26,"ch09-r067","Short answer","Explain how two equal resistors allow a 6 V lamp to be run safely from a 12 V battery.","9.4","If two resistors of equal resistance are connected in series with a battery, the battery voltage will be shared equally across each resistor, so each has 6 V across it. A 6 V lamp can then be connected in parallel with one of the resistors. Because every branch of a parallel connection has the same voltage across it, the lamp receives a 6 V supply and works properly instead of being damaged by 12 V. equal resistors share voltage equally 6 V across each lamp in parallel with one resistor lamp gets 6 V"],[26,"ch09-r068","Short answer","A circuit uses one cell and two lamps. One switch must turn both lamps on and off together, but the brightness of each lamp must be controlled separately. Describe how to arrange the components.","9.4","The two lamps must be on separate parallel branches, with a variable resistor in series with each lamp inside its own branch, so that adjusting one variable resistor changes the current in that branch only and so controls that lamp's brightness alone. The single switch must be placed between the cell and the branches, so that opening it stops the current to both branches and turns both lamps off together. lamps in parallel branches variable resistor in each branch switch between cell and branches controls both together"],[26,"ch09-r069","Short answer","Explain where a variable resistor must be placed so that it changes the brightness of a lamp without affecting a buzzer in the same circuit.","9.4","The lamp and the buzzer must be on separate parallel branches, and the variable resistor must be placed in series with the lamp inside the lamp's branch. Changing its resistance then changes the current in that branch only, so the lamp's brightness changes while the buzzer's branch still has the full supply voltage across it and its current is unchanged. separate branches variable resistor in the lamp branch changes that branch current only buzzer unaffected"],[26,"ch09-r070","Short answer","Explain how one variable resistor can control the brightness of two lamps that are connected in series.","9.4","The variable resistor is placed in series with the two lamps, anywhere in the single path of the circuit. Because a series circuit has only one path, the current is the same all the way around, so changing the resistance changes the current through both lamps by the same amount and both lamps dim or brighten together. variable resistor in series one path same current everywhere both lamps change together"],[26,"ch09-r071","Short answer","Explain how one variable resistor can control the brightness of two lamps that are connected in parallel.","9.4","The variable resistor must be placed between the cell and the point where the branches divide, so that all the current passes through it. Increasing its resistance reduces the total current from the cell and so reduces the current in both branches, dimming both lamps together. Placing it inside one branch would only dim the lamp in that branch. placed before the branches all current passes through it reduces total current both lamps dim together"],[26,"ch09-r072","Short answer","A circuit contains a component X in series with an ammeter and a cell. Explain what happens to the ammeter reading when the value of X is increased, where X is a variable resistor.","9.4","The ammeter reading decreases. Increasing the resistance of X increases the total resistance of the circuit while the supply voltage stays the same, and the greater the resistance in a circuit the smaller the current, so less current flows through the ammeter in the single path of the series circuit. reading decreases total resistance increases voltage unchanged greater resistance smaller current"],[26,"ch09-o053","Short answer","What is resistance?","9.3","Resistance is a measure of how easy or difficult it is for electrons to move through a material. how easy or difficult electrons to move material"],[26,"ch09-o054","Short answer","Compare the resistance of conductors and insulators, giving one example of each.","9.3","Conductors, such as copper, have very low resistance. Insulators, such as most plastics, have very high resistance. conductors copper very low insulators plastics very high"],[26,"ch09-o056","Short answer","Why is copper wire used to connect the components of a circuit together?","9.3","Copper wire is designed to have very low resistance, so the current is affected as little as possible between the components of the circuit. very low resistance current is affected as little as possible"],[26,"ch09-o059","Short answer","Explain why the filament of a lamp gives out light when current flows through it.","9.3","The filament has a high resistance, so when current flows through it thermal energy is transferred. There is so much thermal energy that the filament glows and emits light. thermal energy is transferred glows emits light"],[26,"ch09-o080","Short answer","Describe how the switch in a fridge light circuit works as the door is opened and closed.","9.4","The switch is opened and closed by the movement of the fridge door. When the door is open the switch is closed, so the lamp lights. When the door is closed the switch is open, so the lamp goes out. door open switch closed door closed switch open movement of the door"],[26,"ch09-o082","Short answer","In the car warning circuit, S1 controls the headlamps and S2 is controlled by the door. Explain why the buzzer only sounds when the door is opened AND the headlamps are on.","9.4","Current cannot reach switch S2 unless switch S1 for the headlamps is closed. Opening the door closes S2, so the buzzer only sounds when both S1 and S2 are closed. cannot flow to S2 unless S1 is closed both switches closed door open closes S2"],[26,"ch09-o083","Short answer","Describe how a voltmeter and a switch are arranged so that the voltage of a 12 V battery can be checked when needed.","9.4","The voltmeter is connected in parallel across the battery, with a switch included in that branch of the parallel circuit, so the reading is only taken when the switch is closed. voltmeter in parallel across the battery switch in that branch"],[26,"ch09-o085","Short answer","Explain how two equal resistors let a 6 V lamp be run safely from a 12 V battery.","9.4","Two resistors of equal resistance in series with the battery share the 12 V equally, so 6 V is across each of them. The 6 V lamp is connected in parallel with one resistor, so it receives 6 V and works properly. equal resistance shared equally 6 V across each in parallel with one of the resistors"],[26,"qwen-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the component used to measure current in a circuit?","9.2","Voltmeter Ammeter Switch Cell"],[26,"qwen-01-02","Gap fill","In a series circuit, the current is ____ all the way around.","9.1","the same"],[26,"qwen-01-03","Multiple choice","What happens to the current in a parallel circuit when it reaches a branch?","9.1","It stops flowing It is divided between the branches It doubles in size It changes direction"],[26,"qwen-01-04","Gap fill","The paths where current can flow in a parallel circuit are called ____.","9.1","branches"],[26,"qwen-01-05","Multiple choice","What is the rule for current in a parallel circuit?","9.1","The current through the cell equals the current in one branch The current through the cell is the total of the currents in all branches The current is the same in every branch The current through the cell is half the total of the branches"],[26,"qwen-01-06","Gap fill","If a component in one branch of a parallel circuit stops working, the other branches are not ____.","9.1","affected"],[26,"qwen-01-07","Multiple choice","What is the unit of voltage?","9.2","Amperes (A) Volts (V) Ohms (Ω) Watts (W)"],[26,"qwen-01-08","Gap fill","A voltmeter is connected ____ with the component it is measuring.","9.2","in parallel"],[26,"qwen-01-09","Multiple choice","How does an ammeter connect to a component?","9.2","In parallel In series Across the cell only It does not connect to components"],[26,"qwen-01-10","Gap fill","In a series circuit, the voltages across each component add up to the voltage of the ____.","9.2","supply"],[26,"qwen-01-12","Gap fill","Most cells supply a voltage of ____ V.","9.2","1.5"],[26,"qwen-02-02","Gap fill","Resistance is measured in units called ____.","9.3","ohms"],[26,"qwen-02-04","Gap fill","A component designed to have a known resistance is called a ____.","9.3","resistor"],[26,"qwen-02-05","Multiple choice","Which material has very low resistance?","9.3","Plastic Copper Rubber Glass"],[26,"qwen-02-06","Gap fill","The letter I in Ohm's law represents ____.","9.3","current"],[26,"qwen-02-07","Multiple choice","What is the word equation for Ohm's law?","9.3","Voltage = Current + Resistance Voltage = Current × Resistance Current = Voltage × Resistance Resistance = Voltage + Current"],[26,"qwen-02-09","Multiple choice","Why does a lamp filament glow?","9.3","It has very low resistance Thermal energy is transferred when current flows through it It is made of plastic The voltage across it is zero"],[26,"qwen-03-01","Multiple choice","What is the name of the component used to change resistance in a circuit?","9.4","Fixed resistor Variable resistor Ammeter Voltmeter"],[26,"qwen-03-02","Gap fill","In audio equipment, the word ____ is used to mean loudness from loudspeakers or headphones.","9.4","volume"],[26,"qwen-03-03","Multiple choice","What happens to the current in a circuit when the resistance of a variable resistor is increased?","9.4","It increases It decreases It stays the same It becomes zero"],[26,"qwen-03-04","Gap fill","A smaller current flowing through a lamp makes the lamp ____.","9.4","dimmer"],[26,"qwen-03-05","Multiple choice","In a refrigerator circuit, what happens to the switch when the door is open?","9.4","The switch opens The switch closes The switch breaks The switch turns off"],[26,"qwen-03-06","Gap fill","The circuit symbol for a variable resistor has an ____ through the symbol.","9.4","arrow"],[26,"qwen-03-07","Multiple choice","How are two resistors of equal resistance connected to share battery voltage equally?","9.4","In parallel In series Across the lamp With a switch"],[26,"qwen-03-08","Gap fill","A voltmeter is connected in ____ across the battery to measure voltage.","9.4","parallel"],[26,"qwenw-01-01","Multiple choice","What is the unit used to measure voltage?","9.2","ampere volt ohm joule"],[26,"qwenw-01-02","Gap fill","The component used to measure the current in a circuit is called an ____.","9.1","ammeter"],[26,"qwenw-01-03","Multiple choice","In a parallel circuit, what happens to the current as it flows through different branches?","9.1","It stays the same in every branch It divides between the branches It doubles in each branch It stops flowing"],[26,"qwenw-01-04","Gap fill","The unit used to measure resistance is the ____.","9.3","ohm"],[26,"qwenw-01-05","Multiple choice","What happens to the current in a circuit when the resistance increases?","9.3","The current increases The current decreases The current stays the same The voltage increases"],[26,"qwenw-01-08","Gap fill","In a parallel circuit, if one component fails, the other components ____ working.","9.1","continue"],[26,"qwenw-01-09","Multiple choice","What does the voltage of a battery show?","9.2","The current from the battery The energy that the battery can supply The length and width of the battery The resistance inside the battery"],[26,"qwenw-01-10","Gap fill","A resistor whose resistance can be changed, drawn as a rectangle with an arrow through it, is called a ____ resistor.","9.4","variable"],[26,"qwenw-01-11","Multiple choice","Two identical lamps are connected in series with one cell. How does the voltage across each lamp compare with the voltage of the cell?","9.2","It is equal to the cell voltage It is half the cell voltage It is double the cell voltage It is zero"],[26,"qwenw-01-12","Gap fill","A resistor with a voltage of 12 V and a current of 4 A has a resistance of ____ ohms.","9.3","3"]]};
